        StaticBook("1st Malinor Text", "Galdin", "An Introduction:\n\nBy Peurith\nKarin'ayla, welcome to Peurith’s Archive. I am our lord Forefather’s trusted scribe, whose duty is to document the goings on of our blessed Kingdom. If you are reading this, you yourself are a trusted advisor to Malin. Be warned, the punishment for\nexposing anything you are about to read is banishment.", ""),
        StaticBook("2", "Ehier", "Much like this chest\nYour fate will burn\nHurry up dear child\nAt the back of the shop's return", ""),
        StaticBook("2nd Malinor Text", "Galdin", "Veluluai:\n\nTranscribed by Peurith, Scribe of Malin\nVeluluai - wife of Uradras - advisor of Malin, leader by mandate of the Scholarly Order of Maehr.\nAppointed to the study of deific powers following the defeat of the Archdaemon, Veluluai spends many years at sea, returning only to speak in private\nconference with our King. As of now, she has been gone from the capital for a decade. Perhaps in time we will discover the secret she uncovers for our people.", ""),
        StaticBook("3", "Ehier", "The first word holds the most weight\nThe city falls under this family's gaze\nFirst letter is the middle of 18th\n\nThe Second word is a mix of a heavy rock and street\n\nThe 2nd number of the 3rd Pascal row", ""),
        StaticBook("30 Scriptures", "Walther Hawthorne", "Thirty strict rules, ser in place by the Aengul to aid bortals in being content with their lives, are known in Rthulan faith as The Strictures. Their true origins, where and when they were written, are unknown but it is thought that Yeu Rthulu wrote them\nherself back when Aenguls and Daemons freely roamed the land.\n\n[=================]\nThe Complete Strictures\n[=================]\n\nStricture 1: One must never find joy in the death of any man, woman, or child.\nStricture 2: It is right for one to be bound in matrimony as to create lifemates, but one must never force such a sacred act on another.\n\nStricture 3: One must never make love outside of marriage.\nStricture 4: One must restrict themselves from gluttony and drunkenness.\n\nStricture 5: One must restrict themselves fron un-marital lust and intercourse.\n\nStricture 6: One must restrict themselves from taking what is not theirs.\nStricture 7: One must restrict their tongue from lashing words, for words are made to build up, not harm.\n\nStricture 8: One must restrict themselves from needlessly striking out against someone.\nStricture 9: One must not allow one's list for revenge overtake them. Just retribution may be needed, but vengeance is reserved for the Gods.\n\nStricture 10: One must not deceive or harm another with words.\nStricture 11: One must not abandon those whom trust them for support.\n\nStricture 12: One must not allow their hearts to be apathetic to their brother's burdens.\n\nStricture 13: One must respect their family for they're of the same blood.\nStricture 14: One must not show hatred to anyone due to their race, their age, or gender.\n\nStricture 15: One must restrict the use of substances which muffle and cloud emotions as to use to deal with their pain.\nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves.\n\nStricture 17: One must not harm any man or woman without the cause of just retribution.\nStricture 18: One must be submit under the discernment of others as to have an understanding of what is brought by justice and what is brought by anger.\nStricture 19: One must keep themselves accountable to their spouses and likewise hold their spouses accountable.\n\nStricture 20: One must not allow one's anger to provoke trouble and pain.\nStricture 21: One must allow themselves to be counted as equal unless proven otherwise by content of character.\n\nStricture 22: One must not challenge or disobey those whom are appointed to authority.\nStricture 23: One must find mercy within themselves to be patient with the mistakes of others.\n\nStricture 24: One must find joy in whatever he has, whether little or great.\nStricture 25: One must find the purest of joys from their spouse.\n\nStricture 26: It is in due right, and in way of nature, that those bound in matrimony should enjoy the privileges of sexual interocurse and relations. Yet, it is to be kept between the\ntwo lifemates, not extending to others and creating such things as orgies or brothels, for they are detestable and disgusting.\n\nStricture 27: One support and nurture their spouse as they would themselves.\nStricture 28: One must love their spouse more than any worldly thing or person.\n\nStricture 29: One must not hide their true nature from their spouse. For it is the true nature of lifemates that must be bound together.\nStricture 30: One must honor and follow the strictures if they wish to live a pure life, contented and compassionately.", ""),
        StaticBook("30 Strictures", "Quavinir Tinuvial", "The 30 strictures of Yeu Rthulu\n\n\nYeu Rthulu is the aengul of kindness and compassion, and her strictures are meant to assist in the joy and selflessness of one's life.\nStricture 1: One must never find joy in the death of any man, woman, or child. \n\nStricture 2: It is right for one to be bound in matrimony as to create lifemates, but one must never force such a sacred act on another.\nStricture 3: One must never make love outside of marriage.\n\nStricture 4: One must restrict themselves from gluttony and drunkenness.\n\nStricture 5: One must restrict themselves from un-marital lust and intercourse.\nStricture 6: One must restrict themselves from taking what is not theirs.\n\nStricture 7: One must restrict their tongue from lashing words, for words are made to build up, not harm.\n\nStricture 8: One must restrict themselves from needlessly striking out against someone.\n\nStricture 9: One must not allow one's lust for revenge overtake them. Just retribution may be needed, but vengeance is reserved for the Gods.\nStricture 10: One must not deceive or harm one another with words.\n\nStricture 11: One must not abandon those whom trust one for support.\n\nStricture 12: One must allow their hearts to not be apathetic to their brother's burdens.\n\nStricture 13: One must respect their family, for they're of same blood.\nStricture 14: One must not show hatred to anyone due to their race, their age, or gender.\n\n Stricture 15: One must restrict from the use of substances which muffle and cloud the emotions as to use to deal with their pain.\nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves.\n\nStricture 17: One must not harm any man, or woman without the cause of just retribution.\n\nStricture 18: One must be submit under the discernment of others as to have a understanding of what is brought by justice, and what is brought by anger.\n\nStricture 19: One must keep themselves accountable to their spouses. And likewise hold their spouses accountable.\n\nStricture 20: One must not allow one's anger to provoke trouble, and pain.\nStricture 21: One must allow themselves to be counted as equal unless proven otherwise by content of character.\n\nStricture 22: One must not challenge or disobey those whom are appointed to authority.\nStricture 23: One must find mercy within themselves to be patient with the mistakes of others.\n\nStricture 24: One must find joy in whatever he has, whether little, or great.\nStricture 25: One must find the purest of joys from their spouse.\n\nStricture 26: It is in due right, and in way of nature that those bound in marriage should enjoy the privileges of sexual intercourse and relations. Yet, it is to be kept between the 2 lifemates,-\nnot extending to others and creating such things as orgies or brothels, for they are detestable and disgusting.\n\nStricture 27: One must support and nurture their spouse as they would themselves.\nStricture 28: One must love their spouse more than any worldly thing, or person.\n\nStricture 29: One must not hide their true nature from their spouse. For it is the true nature of lifemates that must be bound together.\nStricture 30: One must honor and follow the strictures if they wish to live a pure life, contented, and compassionately.", ""),
        StaticBook("30 Strictures", "Ypres Signus", ";-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;\n        The Holy\n     Strictures of\n      Yeu Rthulu\n\n    As according to\nthe cannon which has been confirmed and past down by all servants of Yeu Rthulu. Blessings upon you.\nStricture 1: One must never find joy in the death of any man, woman, or child. \n\nStricture 2: It is right for one to be bound in matrimony as to create lifemates, but one must never force such a sacred act on another.\nStricture 3: One must never make love outside of marriage.\n\nStricture 4: One must restrict themselves from gluttony and drunkenness.\nStricture 5: One must restrict themselves from un-marital lust and intercourse.\n\nStricture 6: One must restrict themselves from taking what is not theirs.\nStricture 7: One must find peace and tolerance to deal with those whom're not enlightened, for they follow their own path.\n\nStricture 8: One must restrict themselves from needlessly striking out against someone.\nStricture 9: One must not allow one's lust for revenge overtake them. Just retribution may be needed, but vengeance is reserved for the Gods.\nStricture 10: One must not deceive or harm one another with words.\n\n \nStricture 11: One must not abandon those whom trust one for support.\nStricture 12: One must allow their hearts to not be apathetic to their brother's burdens.\n \nStricture 13: One must respect their family, for they're of same blood.\nStricture 14: One must not show hatred to anyone due to their race, their age, or gender.\n \nStricture 15: One must restrict from the use of substances which muffle and cloud the emotions as to use to deal with their pain.\nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves. \n\n \nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves.\nStricture 18: One must be submit under the discernment of others as to have a understanding of what is brought by justice, and what is brought by anger.\nStricture 19: One must keep themselves accountable to their spouses. And likewise hold their spouses accountable.\n\nStricture 20: One must not allow one's anger to provoke trouble, and pain.\nStricture 21: One must allow themselves to be counted as equal unless proven otherwise by content of character.\n\nStricture 22: One must not challenge or disobey those whom are appointed to authority.\nStricture 23: One must find mercy within themselves to be patient with the mistakes of others.\n\nStricture 24: One must find joy in whatever he has, whether little, or great.\nStricture 25: One must find the purest of joys from their spouse.\nStricture 26: It is in due right, and in way of nature that those bound in marriage should enjoy the privileges of sexual intercourse and relations. Yet, it is to be kept between the 2 lifemates,-\nnot extending to others and creating such things as orgies or brothels, for they are detestable and disgusting.\n\nStricture 27: One must support and nurture their spouse as they would themselves.\nStricture 28: One must love their spouse more than any worldly thing, or person.\n\nStricture 29: One must not hide their true nature from their spouse. For it is the true nature of lifemates that must be bound together.\nStricture 30: One must honor and follow the strictures if they wish to live a pure life, contented, and compassionately.\n\n\n-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;", ""),
        StaticBook("3rd Malinor Text", "Galdin", "Eulogy to Taynei’Hiylu:\n\nTranscribed by Peurith, Scribe of Malin\nThe loss of the green one was hard for King. He spent two years following in his chambers, murmuring of the deep earth. [Faded] -ply in mourning. Our Dragon is not dead, but the truth-..[Faded]", ""),
        StaticBook("4", "Ehier", "The colour between black and white\n\nThe first word is the feeling of happiness \n\nThe second word is the part of --- Orenia", ""),
        StaticBook("4 In A Row Rules", "Jaya Jayamen", "4 In A Row\n           Rules\n4 in a row is a 2 player game where the objective is you are trying to get 4 of your color tokens in a row. You begin by each player rolling a 20 sided dice to decide who goes first (( use /roll 20 )).\nThe player with the higher number rolled goes first. also decide who gets which color tokens. Then whoever goes first places one of his color token on the bottom of the grid. (( the game is played with 7 squares horizontally by 6 squares vertically.\nThe 1st and 9th column in a double chest are not in play.))\n\nEach color token you place must be at the bottom of the grid or directly on top of one color token already placed.\nYou alternate turns placing one color token each turn. When someone gets 4 of their color tokens in a row either diagonally, vertically, or horizontally, the game is over and the one who got 4 of their color tokens in a row wins.", ""),
        StaticBook("4th Malinor Text", "Galdin", "Council Meeting:\n\nOfficial Record of the Meeting of Malin's Princely Council\n[Much of this test is faded or burnt. Only this part remains.]\nPrincess Irrin: “How could you have forgotten your faith? Our beloved Taynei was their child, and you spit on her memory by forsaking the mother and father? Even our King holds true to the\nright worship!\"\nPrincess Veluluai: “You forget yourself, Irrin. You are only an upjumped soldier. Do not speak for our King.”\nPrincess Larihei: “If my liege has something to say of my teachings, then he may speak it through his own lips.”\n[The rest is faded]", ""),
