        StaticBook("Blessed", "Guyden Aureon", "       Ah'mayilu\n\nThy devout grace, thy enchanting beauty, thy presense like ambrosia which permits me bliss.            -[O]-\nI dare remain, I swear to entertain, for the rest of thy final reign.           -[O]-\nFor come thy end, with a salvation so deserved, I cry for thou I'll solemnly miss. \n\n     Ker kae'eth.", ""),
        StaticBook("Blood Magic", "Imfry", "Magic type of\n       blood magic\n-------------------      \n\n\nOOC note:\nOnly signed this as its annoying to type somthing in it howeverI did not creat it. idk who did.\nBlood Magic is a malevolent form of magic, classified as a Dark Art, though much like Shades, bearing a lot of resemblance to Alteration. Modern day Blood Magic, the second generation of the art, is a weaker bastardization of the\noriginal magic used by the original Order of Blood. Due to recent research into mana and life force, discoveries have been made that make possible the reinvention of the magic.\nBlood Magic is, at it’s core, the magical art of drawing power (mana) from the blood of any living being, be it yourself or any other. Use of the magic sacrifices vitality and “life”in exchange for increased power and a temporarily deeper mana pool.\nIt can be used to cast or enhance spells as well as cast some spells of it’s own at later stages, although it’s used most efficiently in the performance of powerful rituals that would otherwise be far too draining and taxing for even some\nof the most powerful Arcane mages.\n\nDuring the process of drawing the power from the blood, the natural energy is twisted and morphed,creating an unnatural and crude form of it’s original state. It is a violation of\nnature and an abominable magic, thus classified as a Dark Arts magic.\nPractice.\n-------------------\nBlood Magic in Combat\nBlood Magic is not designed for combat, being an almost purely complementary and support magic, howeverthere are a number of ways Blood Magic can be used in combat, both in the heat of the moment as well as prepared Blood Magic.\nAt it’s core, Blood Magic is simply the art of drawing power from the blood in order to enhance spells. In combat, a Blood Mage would be able to create a wound on either themselves or on another if they were powerful enough\nand draw the mana from the bloodstream and fuel their spells. This is rather useful as mages can cast combat spells for much longer han a mage normally would, though at a heavy price. There are different ways a Blood Mage can increase the efficiency\nof which they draw power in the heat of battle, but these secrets are unknown to the majority of people.\nSometimes, in the heat of battle, a Blood Mage may become overwhelmed. In this situation, the mage may see no other way out and may be willing to weaken themselves to near death, in order to escape. Doing this is extremely dangerous and not a viable\ntactic for standard combat or escaping any situation requiring a mage to be active in their escape. To do this, the mage would draw a hefty amount of power from their blood in order to overcharge a spell in order to cast cataclysmic tier spells,\nputting their life at risk.\nPractice\n-------------------Blood Magic in Rituals.\n\nBlood Magic is not designed for combat, however useful it may be. Blood Mages can reach the full height of their potential during rituals. To put it simply,\nrituals are premeditated and focused events, where a Blood Mage will use many different means o gain as much power as possible to achieve some form of magical goal. These goals are as endless as the form of magic can take.\nThey can be literally anything, however they can usually be classified into one of the three types below.\nHowever there are a number of things that all these rituals have in common. First and foremost, they all require at least one Blood Mage. The amount of power required would be too much for a normal mage to summon and handle. Second of all, they will more\noften than not need blood sacrifices. Those blood sacrifices can come from either the casters, volunteers or prisoners, it matters not. But drawing power from oneself, like a Blood Mage might do in the heat of battle\nwill not nearly be enough to cast such monstrous spells. \n\nFinally, the Blood Mage rituals rely heavily on the use of ancient symbols and markings, first used by the Order of Blood in Asulon. he nature of these symbols and the mechanics\nbehind them is unknown, however it is known that these marks create some form of loosening in the veil between us and the power we are attempting to draw upon, making the draining process so much more  effciebt. These symbols bat be marked on the ground,\non sacrifices or on the casters. There are many symbols used, many of them are incorrectly written and discovered from memory of the few that have seen them, thus they have a lesser effect than that of the originals.\nPractise\n-------------------Blood magic in Rithuals\n\nRitual Types \nOne, Two & three\nOne.\n\nThere are Elemental Rituals which is the most simple of the lot. This ritual will usually be done at the behest of a Blood Mage in collaboration with one or more evocationist in order to summon a mass amount of this element in a cerain\nway. For example a fire storm or blizzard etc. To put it simply, Elemental Rituals are simply huge enhancements to standard evocation.\nTwo.\n\nSummoning Rituals involve the summoning of beings, be them ethereal or corporeal, from the other worldly planes.  is possible that summoning rituals do not summon anything at all, however what links\nthem all is the opening of gateways or tears in the planes, be it the void, the ancestral plane amongst many many others. These rituals are quite dangerous as history has proven that more often than not, something will escape and it is not guaranteed that\nit can be controlled.\n\nSummoning Rituals are very much the wild card rituals. They require at least one Blood Mage, as per usual, numerous blood sacrifices as well as the appropriate mages, Including many symbols marked.\nThree.\nFinally there is what is called Creation Rituals, which involve a number of different magics in order to form twisted and crude forms of life. This is by far the most difficult type of ritual to perform and requires a lot of research and knowledge\ninto what it is you are attempting to do. These rituals require a number of different mages, alchemy and a huge amount of power, an amount that can't be reached by blood magic alone. The nature of these rituals will vary quite a lot but they will require\nthe most preparation and will be most strenuous on the casters.\nRestrictions\n-------------------Draining from others\nBlood Mages require blood from a living host for their magic to work, however you needn’t always drain from yourself. However what will always remain the same, is the need for an open wound and physical access to the blood in order to drain from it.\nDraining from others is far less rewarding than draining from yourself and also far more difficult. \n\nAs you climb the Tiers, it becomes increasingly easier to drain from others, you don’t have to be as close to your victim in order to drain them of the\nower tiers, more power can be absorbed and drained, not simply wasted. Though when draining from others, less of the carrier mana can be “caught” and thus more is dissipated and less is absorbed for spell usage.\nhe act of draining power itself is a fairly painful one and can cause stinging and burning feelings within the wounds you are drawing from, akin to pouring lemon juice onto a cut. The more skilled a Blood mage becomes, the less of this pain they will feel\nhen drawing power from within themselves, their ability to draw power from the blood becoming more and more refined.\nRestrictions\n-------------------Blood Bending\nYou might imagine blood magic as manipulating the blood itself to extremely overpowered lengths, actually managing to fully control people’s bodies and puppeteer them. This form of Blood Magic, true Blood Magic, does not encompass that whatsoever.\nThere are a few spells that are for the higher tiers (Tier 4 upwards) that do resemble the control of blood, but nothing nearly to that extent.\nTier 4 spells will be able to, for example, magically latch on to the carrier mana within their “opponents” blood and in a very crude form of blood control, causing blood clots and, if enough blood clots the arteries, hemorrhages.\nThis is because the bond between the blood and the mana that is attached to it is so strong, it is extremely difficult to rip them apart manually, even through magic. High Tiers can manipulate the mana in blood  in a similar crude ways, however it is\nextremely difficult as well as physically draining. \n\nThis type of spell however, is difficult to cast, requiring a lot of necessary conditions in order to cast. For example, the blood vessels must be skin level and fairly visible.\nShould blood vessels burst and the skin become ruptured, the wounds would be rather small. The purpose of this type of spell is create a wound so that the Blood Mage may proceed to drain, not as a kill move.\nin order to latch on to the carrier mana and the blood in your opponent, you will be draining a large amount of your own mana pool in the process, likely having to drain from your own blood beforehand.", ""),
        StaticBook("Bloodsoaked Book", "Anonymous", "VAEY* CODEX\n      VOLUME IV\n___________________\n\nA recruit must know that the ***** Order is led in life *** death by *** ***** Grandmaster, annointed by ***** himself. A Grandmaster, guided by the **** of Grandmasters ****,\n**** the safety of Lasthope, Yatl, *** *** Atlas. They speak with *****'* fire, rule with *****'s vigour, and strike with *****'s fury. They are the ****** of the Oathstone, *** the ****** ** Atlas.\n__________________", ""),
        StaticBook("Bloody Diary", "Targoth Glottgut'Raguk", "Tidwald's diary.\n\nStarted on;\n11th of Snow's Maiden 1655.\n\n\nARTWINKLE PUDD IS NOT TO PEEP INSIDE THIS BOOK!!!!!! NO NO NO\n11th of Snow's Maiden 1655.\n\nHello Haense, it's Tidwald. It's chilly enough again this year, but that's to be expected. I found a load of damp books in a soaked crate by the lake, handy. Artwinkle still hasn't had any luck with finding magic. I feel sorry\nfor the clumsy mess. THE CLUMSY MESS.\n               _____\n\n12th Snow's Maiden 1655\n\nWas up at the home of the Warhawks earlier, out near that Ascended HQ and shor what only did I see, A BATTLE! Some lich, Orsul, and 2 bands\nof folk. Very exciting, saw some new magnetic earth magic as well, very interesting stuff. Flaming swords, crazy disintigrating magic, springy elves pulling tunics over peoples heads. I'd never be able for such galavanting. \n8th of First Seed 1655\n\nI CAN NOT BELIEVE THIS. MY SWEET BELLE LEFT ME FOR THAT HUNKY SMITH. I CAN NOT COMPETE WITH THE YOUNG AND HEALTHY IN MY OLD AND SHAKEY STATE. I MUST FIND THE FOUNTAIN OF YOUTH.\nArtwinkle's Magic Log\n\nI've had several lessons, now, all spanning months and months, and years. Lythian has cut me loose as a student, now, after an incident in Holm wherein an explosion caused perhaps by me unsettled the peace.\nBut this wasn't the reason - He merely wasn't able to take me further as a student. As discussed at the start, now that I'm a Tier-Four Fire Evocationist, he's taught me all he can. Very happy with where I am today. Bet I could show them High Elves now!\nWould serve 'em right fer doubting me!\n\nNow. The hunt for an Arcanist lives on...\n---\nSixteen Eighty Nine.\n-\nThis blasted blizzard. The house is freezin'. I do my best to keep the place heated at all times, with my Heat Evocation,\nbut I am unable to cover all hours of the day, and I can only keep it so when I'm here. I worry for Tid. He spends most of his days in bed, now. He's gettin' old, for certain. He's always been old! But I fear that age is finally draggin' him down, now.\nI will look after him as I can. He's even stopped using this journal! He loved this journal!\n\nI head to Aberrang now to seek out that Alchemist, whom I bought a potion of wizard's wisdom from at that fayre in Markev. Perhaps he can help.\nBegan to use my middle name as my proper surname. 'Pudd', kept tricking folk to believing I'm a Halfling, til they met me in person. Only an issue on official forms and letters, but I suppose then that those are the most important times of all.\n[!] The page would be smeared with blood, and clearly messy writing would make up the pages[!]\n\n-=FOR KRUGMAR=-", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of Alchemy", "Jhazeem the Flowerboy", "Alchemy is not a magic, but a form of trade that uses realworld objects of important energy, and transfers that energy into an equal substance of the same amount of energy.Our world is a river, and alchemy allows its users to drink from the river whenever\nwe choose, as long as we return the favor by forming the rivers water that is taken into a substance of greater purpose. Alchemists use ingredients of each symbol(Air, Fire, Earth, Water) to transform it into an equal energy form of substance\nthat benefits Alchemists in their journey to achieve Takwin. The Most common way to achieve Takwin is to focus one element into a body, living or dead, to create a Homunculi. Takwin is something that all Alchemists try to achieve, but have never achieved.\nTakwin can never truly create life, only change life. Alchemists who have tried to achieve Takwin have ended up creating Homunculis, creatures of Chaotic Alchemy; or Chimeras, the  combination of living beings.There are other things Alchemists can create,\nwith the combination of magic: Salamandras, Vithquars, and Cakemen. The commonly known but rarely created 'Golden Apple' is a form of Alchemy, which steals the memory of Elves. Alchemy could never create new materials, only combine many to balance its\neffects which either adds properties, or removes properties.\nTo become a professional Alchemist, you must master the art of Equivalent Exchange.", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of Endings", "Unknown", "[!] The ink in this tome appears to be fresh; the handwriting, neat.\n\n\nCopy of the book\n\"Book of Endings\"\nWritten by\nDoomsayer\nThe Book\n        Of Endings\n-------------------\n\nOnce shall he come, with fire and brimstone.  His name shall we never forget, for he is the ultimate enemy with a fate entwined with mortals.  He will show us his hate, and from it we will\nlearn hate.\nTwice, shall he come.  To show us he is undying.  The eternal enemy of life beyong life, undeath most soalce.  We will be arrogant to him, and he will be driven back again.  We inherit his resistance, but it is a quality that shall expire.\nThrice, shall he come.  The lord of endings is he.  The king of fire with his ever patient eye, to be unknown by many names but one name above all.  Iblees will return again, and an ending he will bring.  One fit for the twisted souls of Mortals.\nLet them know of his coming, and all will be at an end.  To forget the enemy is the only way to defeat it.  Let the knowledge die, or only memories shall remain.  Karvad, Abysyyl, Daemon, Curser, Iblees.\n\n          ____\n\n       /         \\\n       |    [ ]    |\n       \\         /\n\n          ----\n*The Eye of Iblees is sketched onto the last page*", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of herbs 1", "Ruffus Boffin", "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1gAdwM44PS86KTPulQHo6w5CfmKLOJz6-JKzLnElYVO4/edit?usp=sharing", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of Shí - I", "Yíng Zhéng", "The Book of Shí I\n Fashion and Culture\n\nGarments\nFashion in the Shíguó is quite uniform, with both commoners and government officials wearing similar styles of clothing, with the only distinguishable features being the colour and quality -\nof the garments. Silk is considered the material of choice by high ranking individuals, chosen for its smooth and slippery texture as well as its ability to stay cool to the touch in the warm Shí climate. Vividity and variety of colours used in a piece -\nof garment were used as indicators of wealth, as they showed that the wearer was able to afford for the greater array of dyes. Both male and female garments were adorned with emroidery, with wealthier individuals being able to afford for more detailed -\nand larger works on their garments.\n\nMale\nTraditional Shí clothing is referred to as ‘shífú’, for men it consists of a ‘shēnyī’ (a long coat or robe) and a ‘zhóngyí' (shirts and trousers). The Shényí is a robe-like garment that covers the entirety of the-\nwearer's arms, and reaches to their feet, it is generally worn in more formal confrontations. The zhōngyī is worn underneath the shifu and is mostly made of cotton or silk.\n\nFemale\nThe most common garment worn by women is a 'rúqún', -\nconsisting of a long skirt and an upper garment, decorations such as braids or ornaments may be worn with these.\nHairstyles and Headwear\nBoth male and females refrain from cutting their hair once they have reached adulthood, between the ages of 15 to 20, allowing their hair to grow long. This follows the ideas and teachings that every inch o an individual is a -\ndebt to their parents and to God, that they are of their parent's flesh and blood; thus to cut your hair is to dishonour your family. The shaving of hair is a common punishment considered equivalent or worse than death.\n\nMale\nMen are allowed to tie their-\nhair into a knot (jí) - that is all. The fútóu, a cloth wrap around the hair is the most common headwear for men.\n\nFemale\nWith the changing of fashion throughout the ages, many different styles of hair have come and go, such as -\nthe xiáfá, sánfá, and yuánfá. These hairstyles would be accomodated with various hair accessories as ornaments for the hair, such as hair pins (jiá, which were also used by males) in order to hold their hair in place. It is also common for females -\nto leave their hair down before marriage.\nNames\nAn individual’s name consists of their family name (xìng) and personal name (míng), in some circumstances it is seen as being disrespectful to use personal names. Family names originate from clan names and are patrilineal, passed from father to-\nchild. Sub-surnames are given to government officers in order to distinguish between ranks and seniority, this is known as a 'shi'. Personal names are given by parents to have a meaning, they are used as a blessing on their child.\nIn formal consultations, the xìng comes before the míng, which is followed by their shì and title.\n\nExample:\nYíng (xìng) Zhēng (míng) Meng (shì) Taishi (grand historian), meaning 'to bring success'.\nLanguage\nThe language of the Shí is known as 'Shíhuà', its can be found in two written forms: New Shíwén and Old Shíwén. New Shíwén was introduced 200 years ago bby linguist Qian Jiegang in order to improve communication with the outside world -\nand vastly improve literacy rates amongst the Shí people, it is a flexiosisation of the Shíhuà (Shí tongue). Despite Shíhuà being the official language of the Shí, the 'Common' tongue has become more widespread among the Shí people, and Old Shíwén has -\nvastly died out.", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of Things", "Aluatis", "", ""),
        StaticBook("Bounty", "Jack Inkstone", "*A roughly scribbled note, the letters are sloppy as if written by an untrained hand* \n\"A bounty is placed on Jack Inkstones head. Four-hundred Mina's for his corpse. Payment on delivering his body to Marshal Rhy's of the Saint Karl Brotherhood.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Bow-order", "George Gaia", "I would also like a more lighter-colored bow, wooden with some rims and edges of any light metal (your choice.) I want it to be effective and light, but at the same time able to deflect a blade.", ""),
        StaticBook("Braille - Vol. 1", "Nora", "*The pages would be filled with dots and bumps along the paper. Each page seems freshly printed and rather thick, preventing the bumps from bending easily. \n\nThe first few pages introduce the alphabet, and with the assistance of a non-blind person th\nreader will be able to decipher the characters.\n\nLater in the book's pages are basic phrases such as \"Hello\" and \"Goodbye\", along with other fundamental parts of reading.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Brathmordakin", "Miscellus", "THE BRATHMORDAKIN: A GUIDE\n\nThe Dwarven Gods are diverse and plentiful. This book is an attempt to catalogue their various powers, domains, and personalities for all to read. \n\nBrathmordakin, roughly\ntranslated, means House of the Gods. The exact translation is unknown.\n\nAt the head of this pantheon is Yemekar, the creator. He smithed the Dwarves in his image, created the other races, and is the patron of smithing and creation. Very few living\nDwedmar (Dwarves), if any, have spoken to him. It seems that they are mostly intangible figures that only the greatest of clergymen can truly speak to.\n\nWyrvun is a god of frost, virtue, and righteousness. He takes the form of a frost drake when on\nthe mortal plane, and is one of the only examples of a god with a mortal avatar. He is also what the other races know as an Aeungul, though the Dwarven clergy does not seem to believe that all Aenguls are gods or vice versa. \n\nThe third of the\nBrathmordakin is Anabella. She is the mother figure of the gods, with domains of peace, nature, and nurturing. She values the home and comforting and brings rest to restless dwarves. \n\nDungrimm is the \"Guardian of the Dead.\" He is what\nmost men would call a Daemon, though he is still good: his domains are of battle, valor, bravery, courage, defense, the dead, and the moon, and he never waivers in the face of adversity. He believes in the power of life and all that it brings, and,\nbeing a god of death, disdains the needless throwing away of life. He is a good man, and I believe in his tenets. \n\nBelka is the capricious Lady of Passion. A loving and romantic god, she believes in mercy, safety, passionate emotion, and full\nembracement of life through courtship and marriage. \n\nOgradhad is a master of knowledge: god of scholars, invention, discovery, creativity, and magic, he displays the industrial mind the Dwarves are so well-known for. He is the god of\nmages and tinkers alike, admiring a diverse mental portfolio and the preservation of history.\n\nArmakak is the merchant. He is a god easily seen within the roots of the Dwedmar: the patron of merchants and thieves alike,\nhe embodies avarice, greed, ambition, and a need for more. He both represents strength and stealth, and will do anything to get what he wants. Wealth, to Grimdugan, is paramount. Always pay your debts back to a Dwarf.\nFor more, read Da Ord Nar Kadrin, found in the Dwarven cities.\n\n(( https://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/131576-da-kirkja-dverga-the-dwarven-clergy/\n))", ""),
        StaticBook("Brathmordakin", "Hamlin Frostbeard", "The Brathmordakin\n-------------------The Brathmordakin are a pantheon of gods, the divine rulers of the dwarven race. There are seven dwarven gods with Yemekar, the creator, as the strongest and the de facto leader.\nThe other gods are a collection of six aenguls and daemons. The reason dwarves worship a god is to secure a place for their souls in the afterlife. When a dwarf dies, his soul travels to Dungrimm’s keep on the moon\nwhere it is auctioned off to the god who bids the highest for it. A dwarf that has faithfully served their god in life will have a much greater chance of bought by and serving that god in the afterlife.\nYemekar, The Maker\n-------------------The symbol of Yemekar is representative as a Hammer & Anvil which is fitting since Yemekar created the whole dwarven race he is know as lawful to his creation and depicted as good. \nThose who follow The Maker are\naccomplished smiths and anything which includes creations.\nAnbella, Hearth Mother\n-------------------The Symbol of Anbella is depict as two linked silver rings worshipping Anbella would make her worshippers tend to there land and home it also mean to join clan or friends and in a common purpose.\nDungrimm, Guardian of the Dead\n-------------------The symbol of Dungrimm is an iron mask worshipping him would mean to always seize the opportunity to defend your kin and ensure victory when in conflict Dungrimm is normal worshipped upon times of war\nBelka, Lady of Passion\n-------------------The symbol of Belka is a lighting bolt worshippers of her believe in love and romance and to cheerish there youth she also teaches her followers to be merciful in action and speech.\nOgradhad, The Lore Master\n-------------------The symbol of Ogradhad is an open book worshippers of this deitie are those who seek knowledge and prefer to tinker magic and most of those who follow this deitie will follow this path of knowledge always.\nArmakak, Merchant Father\n-------------------The symbol of Armakak is a golden coin followers of this deitie believe in wealth and luck they most use there wit and merchant abilities to gain wealth as Armakak helps those who seek this.\nGrimdugan, Lord of Avarice\n-------------------The symbol of Grimdugan is dipicted as an Obsidian Dagger mostly know as followers of greed shadows and thieves. Grimdugan teaches his followers to steal most wealth but not of those who are children of the\nBrathmordakin nor conspire against the favored of Grimdugan", ""),
        StaticBook("Brathmordakinism", "Kardel Irongut", "___________________THE BRATHMORDAKIN: The Dwarven Pantheon of Gods\n___________________Overview:\n(1)Yemekar\n(2)Belka\n(3)Dungrimm\n(4)Anbella\n(5)Armakak\n(6)Grimdugan\n(7)Ogradhad\n(8) On Khorvad\nCHAPTER 1: YEMEKAR\n___________________YEMEKAR, whose name means 'The Maker' in Old Dwarvish, is the dwarven aedifex of the universe, the supreme and benevolent demiurge. He appeared from nothing and, at that moment, the concept of existance was born.\nYemekar had a Plan, with which he forged all of Creation, and his plan was good. At his forge within the sun, Yemekar created six* helpers (*Khorvad's nature as a former Brathmordakin is disputed) to aid him with the entirety of the cosmos.\nYemekar's symbol is a Hammer striking an Anvil.\n\nYemekar represents all craftsmanship and art, and is considered the highest divine in the Dwarven pantheon. His name is often written with each letter capitalized.\nCHAPTER 2: BELKA\n___________________SYMBOL: A lightning bolt\n___________________The Lady of Passion is said to have been the first Brathmordakin Yemekar created to help him with his forge. Thus, Belka is considered the most beautiful creation of Yemekar,\nbeing the first of any creation.\n\nBelka represents emotion, love, dance, weather, and lust. She is seen as the lady of carnal pleasures, and is worshipped by lovers far and wide. Priests invoke her during marriage ceremonies for her blessings.\nTraditionally, it is Belka who is held to have been the first woman in the world, who laid with Urguan to bear his sons. She lived in the form of Yudora, wife of Urguan, who embodied her features and is worshipped as the only female dwarven paragon.\nCHAPTER 3: DUNGRIMM\n___________________SYMBOL: A silver mask\n___________________Dungrimm is the lord of death, disease, and war. He is the guardian of the gates of Khaz'a'Dentrumm, and after Yemekar is the most powerful Brathmordakin. Dungrimm is prayed\nto by warriors and is seen as the defender of souls, keeping Khorvad's evil clutch away from them as they ascend to the silver halls on the Moon.\nCHAPTER 4: ANBELLA\n___________________SYMBOL: Two linked rings\n___________________Anbella, the Hearth Mother, represents those qualities of motherliness, warm love, a good harvest, and fertility. She is prayed to by all dwarves, but particularily by\nfarmers, druids, and especially forest dwarves.\n\nAnbella's helpers are Cerunnos and Cerridwen, who function as her two avatars in the material planes. Her domain is nature, which she guards dearly and fiercely as a mother would her child.\nCHAPTER 5: ARMAKAK\n___________________SYMBOL: A golden coin\n___________________The Lord of Wealth, Armakak, is a powerful Brathmordakin charged with maintaining not only values, profit, and luck, but also with weaving destiny alongside his brother\nGrimdugan. Armakak famously is remembered in the proverb of the two brothers, showing that hard, honest work is always better than greed and thievery.\n\nArmakak is an umbrella patron for all merchants, and is especially famous among the Goldhands.\nCHAPTER 6: GRIMDUGAN\n___________________SYMBOL: A Black Dagger\n___________________Grimdugan is the Lord of Avarice, the lord of greed, thievery, and co-weaver of destiny with his brother Armakak. Grimdugan features in the proverb of two brothers as\none who encourages thievery and malice, incurring consequence in exchange for the temporary wealth such practices yield.\n\nGrimdugan is often a trickster and is prayed to only by the mad or those who truly have business with him. He rules the Kal'Varak.\nCHAPTER 7: OGRADHAD\n___________________SYMBOL: The Open Book\n___________________Ogradhad, Lord of Lore, is the Brathmordakin of knowledge, runesmithing, and magic. All dwarven practitioners of the high arts revere him, but especially academics.\nOgradhad is said to have given the Dwarven speech, sentience, intellect, and fire. He is revered as the one who ordered the universe and ascribed the things of the world their qualities. His protectors are the dragons, who shield his lore from thievery.\nPART 8: ON KHORVAD\n___________________The Demonic Khorvad is not a Brathmordakin, but he is a creature terribly powerful and a hater of the dwarves. Khorvad is the Lord of Chaos, who dwells beyond the material veil and rules in the dark hell of Khor'Vuur.\nKhorvad hates all of creation and particularily hates the Brathmordakin, who are infinitely more powerful than him. He plots to steal their place in the ruling of the world, and corrupts Yemekar's creations for his own vices.\nKhorvad created Ibal from his blood to destroy Aegis and curse the descendants, and Khorvad made Dormmar from his spleen to cause pain and hatred in the lands of Urguan. The Ironborn worship him as a patron and in turn accept his horrifying powers.\nThe vast majority of dwarven realms prohibit Khorvad worship, and evoking his name is seen as unlucky. Some cities consider affiliation with Khorvad a high crime, and will routinely execute dwarves suspected of Khorvad worship.", ""),
        StaticBook("Brewing", "Vitalius Maarsen", "-Brewing-\n   \n             by\n    Vitalius Maarsen\n\n\nIn this book you will find basic instructions and procedures for brewing in any couldron or alembic.\n-Alchemist's tools\n\nAn alchemist isn't one if he doesn't equip himself with a few tools:\nCaludron, Iron Laddle, Alembic, Golden or silver knife, Flasks, Jars and mortar and pestle.\n-Cauldron\n\nIt's a really useful for experimenting as it leaves more room for error.\n\nA costant source of hear must be under the cauldron, it must be hot enough until a   drop of water beads when touching its surface\nThen fill the cauldron with base of choice and let it simmer until a continuos stream of small bubbles is visible rising from the bottom.\nhen add your reagents of choice, starting with the weakest and finishing with the strongest, the more reagents are\nadded, the more volatite the potion will become.\nIf the potion becomes volatile carefully but virorously stir with an iron ladle stir until the brew settles.\nGreater potions can be only brewed in a cauldron, an alembic does not have sufficent capacity.\n-Alembic\n\nAn Alembic is used for replicating exact recipes of potions, it leaves really little room for innovatin.\nit can can brew only weak and moderate potions.\n\nPrepare a base of distilled water where\nthe reagents will be used\nThe extracts will be poured into the upper chamber and they are left to simmer and  distill, as the chamber maintains a costant pressure failure is negligible.", ""),
        StaticBook("Briar's Diary 1", "Vendel", "BRIAR'S\n              JOURNAL\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNo peeking!\nCame up with a marvelous new dye base today. Nim's something? My memory is a little fuzzy right now.\n\nIt's been a few years since Tay dug me out of the floor. Sometimes I think I should have stayed down there.\n\nNIM'S BEARD, THAT WAS IT.\nTHE LIST\nDwyn      Muildir      Riv'su\nTaynuel   Skale         Renn\nViltaren  Hareven    Calius\nHarlan     Melgarth   Sena\nDamien   Anthron    Veidan\nTaletheus  Alakagh  Irawyn\nGilliaen   Var'yan   Ashwen\nTHE LIST CONTINUED\nCame up with a new alcohol today. Mainly potatoes, with a touch of murex cactus and a sprinkle of flash fruit and drilltongue sap. Calling it Blue Fire for now, because this shit burns going down. It's also blue.\n\nIt makes great aniseptic.\nthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyesthetreesarelinedwitheyes [This mantra continues for the entire page.]\nDon't try to save me\nI promise it won't work.\n\nDon't try to help me\nTo say it'll be alright.\n\nAll I need from you\nis to place it back\nin my shaking hands\nand hold me while I bleed.\nThey stood and watched without saying a word\nWas this a guppy or was this a bird?\nShe had a beak and feathers and other things.\nBut two things she didn't have.\nShe didn't have wings.\n[!] A bright orange feather is pasted down on this page, with sketches of wings and wingspan measurements.\nKNIVES DON'T HAVE YOUR BACK!\n\n[!] This page is torn and stained with blood.\n[!] This page is covered in happy little crow drawings.\nI know the order is keeping more secrets. I suppose it isn't my place to go digging. I probably already know more than I'm supposed to. For now, I should keep my nose clean. Don't need a witchhunt on my feathery ass.\nI'm really really happy today. Dwyn said yes! I'm going to have a family, a real family that cares about me, and wants to keep me save, and doesn't want to hurt me. It's almost too good to be true.\nQuavinir still dislikes me. But I think we can work on that. I don't THINK I'm that bad a person. And I know he isn't. Maybe we just need to spend some time around eachother?\nOw. Writing this after getting my Seed Tattoos. I'm officially Briar Tinuvial.\n....why do so many pretty people want to kill me? Damien and I use to be so close. Maybe it's just a bird-cat thing.\nOh Aspects what's wrong with me? Did I drink too much? I had a dream that I was with Dwyn. He was quite literally biting a pillow, so responsive, so lewd. I should not be having these sorts of thoughts about my family. But the way he responded under me...\nI'm the broken one\n I'm the broken one\n  I'm the broken one\n   I'm the broken one\n    I'm the broken one\n     I'm the broken one\n      I'm the broken one\n       I'm the broken one\n        I'm the broken one\n         I'm the broken one\nWho lives\n\n\n\n\n\nWho dies\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWho tells my story?\nHare and Damien seem to be getting along really well. That's good. I know that Hareven and Dwyn told me to stop hiding and start living....but I don't know if I can take that sort of gamble. Maybe I can just pair off other people?\nMy day\nis gray ash.\nThe night is too long\nThese words are wrong\nThere's nothing good to say\n\nAs I lay down to rest\nno dreams come, its for the best.\n\nDaylight comes, boredom too\nWhy'd I bother to get up so soon?\n[!] This page is covered with scribbles and swears.\nI think\nI'd do better\nOn my own\n\nNo friends\nNo fights\nJust me, alone.\nI whipped up a potion for Hareven. I hope that I got the dosages right. If I'm correct, with this potion he'll be able to enjoy himself without hurting anyone. He deserves to be happy.\nHmmm I need to adjust the dosage a bit. Maybe I can just dilute the potion stock that I already have?\n\n[!] There are sketches of plants.\nI think I'm going to tell Muildir. Maybe. He and I have gotten a bit closer. He's so distant though. He may hold no affection for me, but I could probably confide in him. If not, this journal will catalogue my last days.\nI didn't end up telling Muildir. But I do have a new trusted friend: Riv'su. He even knows about my attempted suicide. Even though Hare is the one that found me, he still says I should try to become a guide. He says I have a lot to offer the Order.\nI haven't written in here in almost a week. I went to visit Tay to get his advice on being a guide. He was delirious, and now I'm bedbound for a few months. Hare has been taking good care of me, but Muildir knows about my wings. I'll prepare for the worst\nI'm finally able to be up and about. Why does everything have to be so complicated? Dwyn doesn't understand why dating is so terrifying for me. It's dangerous. I could end up unattuned, dead, banished, any number of things. I don't want to lose everything\nI'm moving into Dwyn's house. I'm not sure I should maintain a home in Tay's clan hall anymore. I don't want to be a burden, and he has more important things to worry about than me. I'm just going to try to pull back, and stay out of everyones ways.\nI feel like I should get rid of people. Not in the gross killing sense, of course. But in the sense of being unavailable for them, and making them unavailable for me. Damien already hates me, and Muildir doesn't want me around. How hard can it be?\nWell, I've been crashing at Tale's place lately. He's a total sweetheart. I've been doing a lot of traveling, a lot of hiding. Gotta keep a low profile. Had another run-in with Tay. Told him about trying to hang myself. We're actually good now.\nI stopped by the Tinuvial house to pick up some of my things and drop off others...and met Dwyn's cousin Irawyn. Why are all ame so muscular and handsome? Not that it matters. He's family. Speaking of family, Dwyn got divorced.\nGoing to open up a shop in Caras Elder. I don't really use money often, but I need something to occupy more time.\n\nI'm lonely....\n\n[!] There's an ink blob at the bottom of the page.\nWell, I went exploring today, and found a meteor! I have a really good idea, just gotta get Hareven and the others on board.\n\n....I gave Damien some cactus juice, and he passed out on top of me. I was stuck laying on the floor for hours.\nSo much has happened since my last entry...and it all seems so unimportant.\n\nI guess I just ran out of time. Thought I had more. I'm moving to the Silk Grove, to see if I can fix things.\n\nIf I survive this...there are going to be changes.\nThe Aspects are a sham. I've spent my life dedicated to, and serving, dieties that couldn't care less if I live or die. If ANY druid lives or dies. We spend our entire lives slaving at their feet, and death only rewards us with MORE WORK.\nThe Aspects take and take and take and never give back. The life of a druid requires constant personal sacrifice, and reaps no reward. Well I'm done giving. From now on, I won't sacrfice anything. I'll find my own path.\nNot going to lie, I would like to light Muildir on fire. Or maybe just his house. With him inside. I can't believe I ever thought he was a decent person.\nNot going to lie. Things have been difficult lately. It feels like I'm always angry now. No luck on a cure. Not sure how many years I have left. I struck a deal with that thing, Tichar....I just hope it pans out.\nOne sacrifice left. Oddly, I don't even feel guilty. I'm finally doing something for me. And you know what they say. Can't make an omlet without breaking some eggs.\n....never thought people could taste good before. Now...it's strange. To think that people are meat too. And good meat, at that. Damien has been by my side this long, trying to tend to me and keep me alive. He's the only friend I have left.\nMuildir and I have reconsiled. It has come to my attention that my mind is not entirely to be trusted, even by  myself.\nGot Damien a present\nFUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK\nFUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK FUCK\n[This page is stained with ink, and has a hole in it]\nToo late to turn back now. No regrets. No anchors.", ""),
        StaticBook("Bridge Ballista", "Nona", "THE BRIDGE BALLISTA\n\n\nSeveral months before war was officially declared in Norland, the Dwarves knew that armed conflict was inevitable. They had the resources and numbers to bring the fight to their enemies, but a seemingly unsurmountable\nproblem stood in their path. The island fortress of The Krag. In preparation of attacking this seemingly impregnable bastille, scores of Dwarven engineers were assigned to develop an efficient means of entry.\n\nExperiments ranged from explosives,\nto catapults, to highly dangerous prototypes of flying machines, but all seemed too expensive, or too inefficient to meet the needs of their current dilemma. That was until, the suggestion was made to think of a redesign of an old favorite, tried and true\nfor hundreds of years. Old blueprints created by Nalro Grayhammer, the greatest ever dwarven engineer where dug up from engineering books within the vault of the great library of Kal’Omith. These blueprints, the first concept of the bridge ballista, were\noriginally made to be used in the siege of the Shadow Castle during the dwarves attack on Oren, however were scrapped for a more simple approach through the use of just bombarding the walls.  These blueprints were eventually put into use by the current\nMaster Engineer Ogdan Frostbeard, in preparation for the attack on Norland.\n\nThus the Vath-Kahrkrumm (Wall Attacker) otherwise known as the bridge ballista, was put into production. An incredibly useful, yet fickle machine,\nit utilizes the same mechanics of an ordinary ballista, but instead of bolts it launches two powerful anchors made of black steel meant to be driven into the stone walls of a mighty fortress. In the wake of these anchors follows a bridge, made of rope and\ndubiously unsafe and flimsy wooden planks. Although strong enough for several armored dwarves to walk across at a time, the fragile bridge is prone to swaying in high winds, and can be easily broken through by a prepared defender. \n\nThe reliability of\nthis war machine is low at best, and it is often prone to misfires, or simply not reaching it’s intended target. However, by time constraints and the expertise of Dwarven engineering inspectors, it was deemed ready for battle. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Bringing of Life", "Edwyn Lynderly", "The Bringing\n             of Life.\n\n\n\n  The Notes of Edwyn\n               Lynderly\n6th of Malin's Welcome.\n\nToday begins the endeavour to grow that of a tree, from nothing more than a sapling. I travelled  to the cloud temple, I came across a small store in which to purchase a sapling during my prior task.\nI settled on a spruce sapling, I've always admired how despite being present in the coldest of enviroments they can still flourish into something magnificent. Tomorow I shall attempt to plant my newly obtained sapling.\n7th of Malin's Welcome.\n\nWith sapling in hand, I left the druids grove in search of the perfect location. Wondering into the nearby woods, I soon settled on a small clearing that would be perfect for my tree. Grasping my shovel I dug a small hole, the\nperfect size for my small sapling. Bringing it to settle within it's new home, I brushed the dirt back towards it's original resting place.  It's oddly therapeutic this planting thing. Allowing it a drizzle of water I collected from the nearby river, I\nleft the sapling.\n14th of Malin's Welcome.\n\nA few days have passed since the planting of my sapling, it's growing well. Along with allowing it daily watering, I've been applying bonemeal to help fertilize it's growth. I used a small stick to support the\ngrowing tree. I was concerned it's size would soon cause it to collapse, better it be secure at the end of the day. I'll continue to monitor it's growth over the coming month, hopefully it'll grow into something sufficient soon.. The risk is holds against\nthat of natures  prey makes me anxious.. Once it grows more, that threat will hopefully disperse. I'd rather not have to buy another sapling.\n21st of Malin's Welcome.\n\n§II nearly lost the damned sapling today. I was returning from the river bank, collecting water within my pail when I saw the small critter edging closer towards it. It had a nibble, but dropping my bucket I managed\nto shoo it away from the plant.. It'll need some defense against this, cant sit here day in day out looking after the bloody thing.\n29th Malin's Welcome.\n\nAfter several days of heaving rocks back and forth, I've built a hefty wall around my sapling. It's nothing major but should keep those small bastards at bay, moreso thanks to the lighting.. Or does that\nattract more critters towards it.. Well, a splash late now. I've considered giving my tree a name, oddly. Never really held a 'connection' to anything other than another person.. For now however, I shall name it 'Edwyn's tree.' Very original, I know.\n3rd of The First Seed.\n\nA new month, never really noticed the months pass by until I actually went to record this journal. It's now grown to reach the height of my chest, quite interesting that it grows so quickly.. However, I have been\nfeeding it hefty amounts of bonemeal on a regular basis. Those creatures that lurk the shadows of the grove are awfully useful in obtaining the bones. I've considered stopping the bonemeal, allowing it to go naturally for some time.. Yet, I must admit my\ncuriosity only grasps at my conscious to see just how far I can push this plant.\n12th of The First Seed.\n\nAnother spurt of growth from the plant! It's spectacular to watch, every day I awake to a few new branchs.. It's almost become consuming of my life, keeping me awake as I continplate what I may\nfind in the morning.. Yet, along with this I also cannot concern over it.. A large bear, happy lumberjack or even just that of a strong wind can ruin my efforts. I've still not slowed my bonemeal additions, I figure the large my tree can grow the greater\nchance it stands against these threats.\n15th of The First Seed.\n\nIt's the early hours of the morning, I couldnt sleep. Had this gut feeling something was wrong with the tree, yet whilst trudging from the warmth of my bed I found little to be concerned about. Odd.\n24th of The First Seed.\n\nI can't even maintain it's growth at this point, it's gone far beyond what I even expected. It's already above my height I believe, and yet I cannot stop with the bonemeal. It's practically an\nobsession at this point, an addiction. Even though I admit it I still ignore my own pleas, having this burning desire for it to stretch far above it's surrounding brothers.\n30th of The First Seed.\n\nNearing the end of the second month.. The growth has halted slightly, I believe the earth it has rooted itself into fails to address it's constant need for water. I need a better means of relieving it's constant\nthirst.. Perhaps if I extend the river to run alongside it? However thats an awful amount of work, I'll have to consider it over the coming days.\n4th of The Grand Harvest.\n\nYet another month, and with it my small pond has been completed.. After heaving multiple buckets of water up the small incline leading towards my tree it began to rain.. A days work, completed in minutes by that of\nnature.. Perhaps the aspects noticed by struggle and gave me aid, or just merely a coincidence. Whatever it is, the tree should be able to feed from it's new source for some time.. I've already noticed a hint of growth again, which makes me happy.. I was\nconcerned it had reached its limit.. Yet no, my little sapling has only continued to grow.\n12th of The Grand Harvest.\n\nI woke today to be greeted by a slither of growth once again, and yet, despite such I only feel disappointment. A few local brethren to my tree had given in to the drought of water caused by the massive thirst of my own.\nTheir leaves went brown, and within a few days their trunks failed to sustain themselves as rot caused them to fall.  Perhaps the excess bonemeal has only came to punish me, yet now I fail to grant it such a privlidge and even so it grows..\n19th of The Grand Harvest.\n\nSoon will come the time to show my efforts to Arin. The tree continues to grow substainally, however it's yet to poison any more of the surrounding nature with thirst thankfully.  It's been  an interesting experiance, from\nthe small sapling which could hardly survive.. Requiring a wall for protection, which thanks to my shoddy building has since fallen into ruin.. Makes quite the pretty border however, I must admit.  Now the tree remains large, I doubt I shall leave it come\nthe end of my trial. It's almost like a child at this point, even though it's constantly threatening to destroy more of it's brethren for the precious supply of water, I cannot hate it nor bring the guts to save the others.. It's my tree, and with my care\nit has far outgrown the others.\n\nI suppose that also means this will be the last time I write within this journal, and probably see it. Oh well, fun whilst it lasted.", ""),
        StaticBook("Bronzesmithing 1", "Quavinir", "Bronzesmithing:\n      Volume 1\n\n\nby Quavinir Twiceborn\nBronzesmithing, or 'bronze casting' is the art of creating objects made of the metallic alloy made of bronze. Hopefully, this book series should give the reader enough knowledge on the subject of bronzesmithing that the reader, if they so chose, would be\nable to bear enough knowledge as to be able to take up the trade themselves. The writer of this book has been a bronzesmith for decades, and due to consistant requests to be a mentor or take on a apprentice as to share his knowledge on the subject,\nhas chosen to instead write a series of books containing all the prerequisite knowledge of bronzesmithing. The writer of this book does not claim that this book will give the reader all the needed skills and abilities to be a bronzesmith, but rather\nthat this book contains all the knowledge needed in which for one to begin practicing bronzesmithing themselves.\nWith that out of the way, lets begin.\nChapter 1:\n   What The Heck is\n         BRONZE\n\n\nThe alloy known as bronze is a mixture of two somewhat common metals, copper, and tin. Copper is a brownish red metal, that is notably weak, yet light. It is considered by many\nto be a trash metal, incapable of making anything of value aside from ornaments. And for the most part, that is a correct judgement. Copper is a relatively soft, and light metal. Things that are made of pure copper tend to bend excessively easy, though\nthey do not rust, rather taking on a light greenish coating called a 'patena' (though there will be more on that later.) \n\nCopper for the most part is not very useful on it's own (aside from the claims of 'conductivity' that many redstone workers\nclaim it bears) and alone is not very useful.\n\nTin is a light, silvery metal that can bear a notable shine. Tin, like bronze is not a exceptionally useful metal either. Though it is somewhat harder than copper, it still is not hard enough to be very\nuseful in the construction of tools and armor. Tin unlike bronze can rust, and it's rust can corrode whatever it infects. \n\nBoth copper and tin alone are not very useful. But together, they're fantastic.\nMuch as a touch of yeast can cause dough to rise, a small amount of tin mixed with copper causes its potential to explode. When mixed together, the alloy formed is extremely durable, yet malleable. The standard allocation for the creation of bronze is\n1 part tin, for every 9 parts of copper. Though, this allocation can be tweaked as to allow higher quality bronze. \n\nThe standard I myself use, is 12% tin and 88% copper. This allocation in my opinion is the most suited for the casting of tools and\nweapons, and the bronze produced from such is notably more hardy than the standard allocation. \n\nFor armor, which must be beat into shape, the most advantageous allocation is 6% tin, and 94% copper. Any less copper and the metal shall be notably\nweaker, any more tin and the bronze shall be too rigid to effectively beat into shape, and upon eing beat into shape will form corners and ridges that will ruin the consistancy of the metal, and lead to imperfections and weaknesses.\nThe standard I use for the allocation of metals is by weight. I weigh each of the metals as equal and allocate them as if they weighed the same for the parts. If I were to make 100 pounds of casting bronze, I would allocate 88 pounds of copper and\n12 pounds of tin. For sizing however, many may see this as odd. Tin is lighter than bronze by a small margin, and I have observed other smiths attempting to allocate the amounts of tin and bronze by total area of said metal. This as far as I can tell is\nfolly, and I advise the reader to allocate their bronze mixtures strictly by weight.\n\nThough the mixture of tin and copper is what is widely considered to be 'bronze', there are various other copper based alloys that are considered a type of 'bronze'.\nArsenical Bronze is a alloy made between copper, and the dangerous stone known as 'arsenic'. It produces a black metal that is quite durable, and able to be cast efficiently. However, the smelting and mixing of these two metals is liable to cause toxic\nfumes that are able to poison, and kill those who inhale such. The Kharajyr who used such bronze in times past claimed it as some form of judgement from their god Metzli, viewing any who caught the sickness from the fumes or died from such judged as\nunworthy to work the metal by Metzli.\n\nAnother alloy with copper is that whhich is known as 'brass'. Brass is a alloy between copper and the mineral known as zinc. It is notably less hardy than standard bronze, though it notably presents very\nlittle friction with other pieces of brass, making it a commonly used material for those who make machinery or contraptions. Brass likewise bears a notably acoustic property to it, making it one of the more ideal choices for the use of musical instruments\nthat one blows into to make sound. Brass however is somewhat weaker than bronze, and the mineral zinc is far more difficult to properly refine, hence the smithing of brass weapons and tools is not exceptionally viable.\nChapter 2:\n    What You'll Need\n\n         /---\\\n      _/   0   \\_\n  \n =]      <====[____]\n ||   i^i__________\n ||   = ----------\n ||   VV\n===================\nThe art of bronzesmithing, despite ones initial impressions can be somewhat complex, and there are many tools and skills needed in which to do such effectively. \nThe most important 2 items you shall need in which to start casting bronze is a\nfurnace, or kiln, and than a smelting bucket.\n\nA smelting kiln, or furnace, is what you shall use to to heat the metal that you shall be working with. Given the standard means of making things out of bronze is casting, the process of melting the\nmetal so you may be able to pour it into a mold is essential, and the tool used in doing such is your most valuable tool.\n\nThe most effective means of making a furnace is to make one out of clay.\nA clay furnace is somewhat simple, yet its construction may well not be. I advise speaking with an accomplished builder, (due to the construction of a clay furnace not being too far away from that of a small building)\nOne must first bear a suitably large area, a small clay furnace will usually be a bit over a meter wide, and around 2 meters tall if done properly. You will start with making a roughly 15-20 centimeter thick-§\nwall of clay, around a\n45-to-60 centimeter circle. You will begin building up, keeping the wall consistant for roughly 60 centimeters, taking a break after about every 10 centimeters to light a fire in the center as to bake the clay to being hard.\nDepending on the make up of the clay in your area this may take as little as an hour, though much clay may take longer. I advise keeping a fire lit for roughly 4 or so hours, as to ensure that each section of the wall is fully baked.\nAfter the first 10 centimeters of construction, you will need to make a small arch shaped wooden frame, roughly 28 centimeters high, and place that onto the clay wall as for where your opening for the furnace may be. Likewise, I advide making 2 small\ncircular wooden frames, (no larger than 5 centimeters in diameter) and placing them at the sides of the furnace walls, right as you begin contruction, as for holes for a billows, or furnace blower. (Though, I know a few others who prefer to drill holes\nin the kiln at a later time for the forge blowers, though I prefer to have them built into the furnace.)\n\nAt about the 60 centimeter mark, you will want to lower the thickness of the walls gradually, lowering the thickness by roughly 1 centimeter\nfor every 10 centimeters you build up. You shall do this as you build up the clay walls for roughly 50 centimeters (making so upon completion of this step, your furnace is 110 centimeters tall, and the walls between 15-10 centimeters thick.)\nAfter that, you will need to start building inward slightly, this is diffcult, and I advise one to do such carefully. You will need to build with the walls curved inward,  creating an internal dome. Moving roughly 3 centimeters inward for every 10 you\nbuild upward. This will take time, and many choose to use a wooden lattice, or frame to aid in this, though it is well possible without.You will do this for an additional 60 centimeters upward, going 10 centimeters at a time.\nUpon completing this, you should bear a 160 centimeter tall kiln, with a 24 centimeter large opening at the top. Before continueing, coat the inside of the  kiln with roughly one centimeter of clay (or less, it is difficult to measure such at this\njuncture) and ensure that the inside of the kiln is consistant and smooth, with as few ridges or abnormalities as possible. Afer doing the inside, do the same with the outside, ensuring the kiln is smooth.\nAfter this, you will need to fasion a cone of clay separately, being atleast 7 centimeters thick at the bottom, with a bottom opening at about 24 centimeters in diameter. Make the cone atleast 30 centimeters tall, with atop opening atleast 16 centimeters\nwide. Attach the cone to the top of the kiln with wet clay, again bake the clay into place. Remove the wooden frame for the door, and the frames (if you chose to make them) for the holes for the forge blowers. Take the frame you made for the door, and\nmake a flat clay door, of atleast 6 centimeters in thickness in the dimensions of the door frame, as to fit in the entrance. Cut an L shaped wooden stand for the door, and press it into the wet clay before baking. The L shaped stand should\nbe roughly as tall as the door, and the L shaped wooden stand should be placed into the clay --> L flat side first.\n\nFire up the kiln again, using any forge blowers, or billows you're able to puchase. Coals should be sufficient in which\nto gain the heat capable of melting bronze.\n\nPlace in the center of the kiln a small clay circle, (probably only about 5 centimeters in height, and about 18 centimeters in diameter) as for you to place your smelting bucket for your bronze.\nWhen firing the kiln for use of smelting bronze, ensure that you do not place the coals on the stand for the smelting bucket, as accidentally leaving such on the stand when placing the bucket may cause the bucket to tip over, and fall down, spilling\nthe contained alloy all over the hot coals.\n\nAs for the smelting bucket, one can fashion one out of clay much in a similar way the kiln was made out of. The size of the bucket can vary madly, and I advise the smith to experiment with different sizes.\nIf one has the availability of such, I would advise having a smelting bucket carved from soapstone. Soapstone is an exceptionally good material for bronze casting, as it is both resistant to melting, and capable of transmiting heat through itself.\nSoapstone is a blackish grey stone, with a decent hardness. It bears veins of black and white through it. Most miners worth their pickaxe should be able to identify, and procure soapstone for you, should it be available in the area of their work.\nCasting molds, like the bucket, can likewise be made of clay, or more preferably soapstone. However, most clay molds, even if well made, will not be able to be used more than once, given that once solidified, bronze tends to stick to the clay, requiring-\none to break the mold the item is cast in after the bronze has cooled.\n\nSoapstone, on the other hand, may have molds carved out of them, and when cooled, the bronze does not stick to the stone as much, if at all. Allowing the bronze casts\nto be removed easillY (Especilly if in a two part mold that can be opened up.).\n\n\n\n\nCONTINUED IN VOLUME                2", ""),
        StaticBook("Bronzesmithing 2", "Quavinir", "Bronzesmithing:\n       Volume 2\n\nBy Quavinir Twiceborn\nThe next tools you shall need shall be sanding implements. The art of making anything presentable out of bronze is mostly tied to how much time one is willing to devote to sanding an item. Various items can be used for sanding bronze.\nSeashell paper is among one of the most effective tools for sanding and polishing metal, which is made from finely crushed seashells being attached to parchement, or leather with pitch, or another adhesive. The rough texture of the seashells is able to be\nused quite effectively as a polishing implement.\n\nAnother useful sanding implemented is the dried stalks of what is referred to as 'scouring rush'. Scouring rush is a reed that grows in sectioned stalks similar to bamboo.\nThese stalks can be baked and dried, and gain a exceptionally resilient, and rough texture. Like the seashell paper, it is well useable for polishing and sanding metal.\nFor larger pieces of bronze, such as swords, or larger tools, the most effective means for the bulk of the shining of bronze shall be files. \nI would severely suggest commissioning a ironsmith, or a smith of any kind to make one a file, as starting out,\ntrying to make a file oneself would be an absolute pain.\nA bronzesmith can never have to many kinds of files. Even the files one uses for nails can be sufficient. Though, the most efficient kind f file is that which bears two handles, one on the top,\nand one on the bottom, with the rough section being inbetween the two handles. \nFiles are extremely useful in both shining. and refining the shape of a bronze tool. Any tool with an edge will require being filed down as to give it the proper shape.\nThe next tool one will need will be a pair of tongs. I again advise commissioning such frmo a established smith, as trying to start smithing without them would be a pain.\nI advise getting tongs tailored to the bucket you shall be using for your bronze-\ncasting, as a fitted pair of tongs can be invaluable in securely holding onto a bucket of molten bronze, as the bronze caster both needs to withdraw the bucket, and empty its contents gradually. Tongs are relatively easy to make, so once you've\ngotted yourself one pair, making yourself a pair of tongs is a possibility.\n\nA later tool you yourself should be able to make is a hammer. Though one comes to mind as the quitessential smithing tool, one can be a bronzesmith\nand use such sparingly. Hammers come in many different shapes and sizes, despite what many may think. A hammer's force is not directly proportional to it's weight, as some may thing. A hammer may be socketed onto a haft, it may have a hole through it as\nto be put on a handle, or it may be attached simply with pitch or tied to a haft via string.\nThere are various different types of hammers, and I advise the reader to choose which sort've hammers would best fit the job they're doing. Some jobs may require\nsmall hammers, only about the size of a tooth-cleaning-brush,while other jobs may require large mallets. Be discerning, and try things out.\nThe only jobs a bronzesmith will use a hammer for (aside from alongside a chisel as to carve a mold\nfor use in casting) would be for bending metal or armor into shape, fastening something onto another (whether through literally beating bronze around something, or using nails or rivets) or beating bronze into shape for use as armor, as most other\nitems can be made better if cast.\n\nThe next item a bronzesmith shall need will be pliers, which are surprisingly more useful than the hammer in the daily life of a bronzesmith.\nPliers again, I advise for the discerning bronzesmith\nto get these from already established smith, before attepting to create on one's own. Pliers can be use to both pry a bronze cast from it's mold, and likewise be used to pry and twist off any overcast bronze from a newcast edged tool.\nGet a Whetstone to.\nNext, you will need a chisel. A chisel is more useful in the creation of molds, though molds for one's brozeworks are extremely important. I again advise that the reader commission or buy some chisels from an established smith.\nIf you wish to make the hafts one uses for tools, one will need a varyance of woodworking tools. Tools such as carving knives, more sandpaper, a vice, a pencil (whether metal or charcoal) some form of finishing oil, (I use linseed personally) a saw\na rasp, a wood turner's table, and a wedge.\nThis book is mainly about bronzesmithing, so do not expect me to be going into the proper use of these wood worker tools. Mayhaps in a future series, but not today.\nChapter 3:\n     Lets Get Started\n\nOnce you have the majority of your tools and a proper smelting furnace made, now comes the fun part. /Making things./\nFor a beginner, I would suggest a simple hammer head, that one would be able to use\nin the future. Follow these instructs as closely as possible.\n\nGet some good clay again, and make a 15 centimeter by 15 centimeter block. Using some form of precise tool such as a small stick, or toothpicker, carefully fashion a square hole into the\ncenter of the clay, 6 centimeters wide and tall, than dig out the small hole in the clay to roughly 18 centimeters deep, aiming to keep the square as straight as possible. I advise using a small carved wooden tab to ensure such.\nAfter carving out the mold, bake it near a fire for a night, checking on it every so often to ensure the hole maintains the same shape.\nAfter baking the clay mold, take some clay, and make a shallow clay wall around the hole, just as wide as the hole,\nthough extending above, and below the hole on the mold roughly 10 centimeters. The stall frame around the hole should be no higher than 2 centimeters. Repeat the baking process, though this should not take nearly as long.\nAfter finishing the mold, procure for youself some refined copper, and tin. Weigh out 119 grams of copper, and 16 grams of tin. Procure a forge blower if possible, and some coals. Place the coals into the kiln and light a fire. Keep the fire stocked and\nblowed untill the fire gets white hot. Place the allocated tin and copper into your bucket, and place it into the kiln. Close the kiln off and keep it heated for roughly half an hour. After half an hour has elapsed check on the bucket. If the bucket\nappears white hot, than you're ready to remove it. If it appears red, orange, or yellow, it still need more time, and will need to be heated for longer. Once the bucket is white hot, withdraw it with your tongs, and slowly pour the molten bronze inside\ninto the clay mold, being careful not to spill it outside of the frame. The bronze should fill up both the mold, and the small frame around the hole. Set your bucket aside and wait.\nAfter the bronze has cooled, it will appear slightly blackish and brown.\nUse your plies to remove the cast hammer head from the mold. If it is to diffucult, you may break the mold apart from around the bronze cast.\nThe resulting hammer head should look something like this:\n___\n           |    |\n           |    |\n       __ |    |__\n       --------\n\nUse your sanding tools and begin sanding the hammer head. Keep sanding untill is begins to take a dullish brown luster.\nAfter sanding it, procure a useable haft, whether through making it yourself, or hacing it made by another. Near the end of the haft, carve a flat rectangle into the wood, 30 centimeters long and 10 centimeters wide. The base of the hammer head should fit\nperfectly into the carved square. Take a small chord, and tie it around the haft, and the two thinner fins of metal. And there you go, you've now made a hammer.\n\nYou have now made your first tool of bronze. Congrats. And as for now,\nthis shall now conclude this book on bronzesmithing . You now know what to do, and you know the basics. Now comes the long haul, start experimenting with bronze, try out new techniques, and finds what best suits you. Whether you want this as a lifetime\nvocation, or as a part time hobby, the working of bronze is a complex art, and theres a lifetimes worth of things to find out about this alloy, and the wondrous things one can make from it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Bub & Nub", "Falum'Lur", "In my travels to Krugmar, I saw two orcs fight, with gleaming iron swords, to the death over a petty dispute. I stopped to speak to a shaman-in-training, who explained to me that one orc had not shown him the respect he deserved and had thus\nchallenged him to a duel to the death. This baffled me; but the shaman-in-training went on to recount the legend of Nub'osh. He tells of the orc, who lived many generations past; but his name had been immortalized. Nub'osh was the chieftain of a\nvery prosperous orc tribe. He was loved by the members of his tribe, and envied by those of other tribes. Legends of his deeds had spread among the tribes of Krugmar; he was depicted a god walking amongst mere mortals. He considered himself to\nbe both the wealthiest and most fortunate of orcs, favoured by the gods; that his legend would live forever. One day, the elder shaman of a poor tribe visited Nub'osh - this was not uncommon - to see the tribe of the god-like chieftain. Nub'osh\nshowed the shaman his tribe, his beautiful farms and livestock, and finally,his collection of gold artifacts. Nub'osh interrupted the tour to ask the shaman who he believed to be the most fortunate orc. Expecting his name, he was astonished at\nhow quickly the shaman responded, \"Kragor\".\"Why do you say that?\", Nub'osh asked, or rather, \"Why lat be blahin' dat?\"; trying to hide his anger, in respect.\"Kragor was a peasant who worked in the mill, over his life, for fifty thousand days;\ntook up arms to defend our tribe; and died doing so.\", he replied.\"A peasant, you say?\", Nub'osh could no longer hold his anger,\"Then, tell me, shaman; how fortunate do you take me to be, if not as fortunate as a peasant?\"\"While the wealthy orc\nis better able to content his desires, and to bear up against a sudden buffet of calamity, I cannot say. Call him however, until he die, not fortunate but happy.\"With this, the shaman was expelled from the village, and given a single loaf of\nbread and cut of meat to make his trip back. And as the tribes that were at present powerful, were weak in the olden time, and as the formerly great tribes fell to his own, becoming insignificant; such a fate befell his own tribe. Nub'osh,\nhowever, did not take up arms like his brethren, convinced his army was unbeatable. And while no army is, or ever was, invincible, he had foolishly believed otherwise. Nub'osh was dragged by his hair from his chambers to the town centre, where\ndozens of orcs took their turn spitting and urinating on him. He was burned alive, as he cried the shaman's name,to be eaten. His brother, Bub'osh, however, had fought most valiantly, even when all hope was lost; and was rewarded as such. His\nbody honoured by the invaders, his soul blessed by Krug, and his name immortalized.\n\n-Aveli, from the Travels of Aveli", ""),
