hen the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty.\nThis is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will\nproduce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? The answer to this is rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What\nis meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list of required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows,\nfingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep and Calm Dispostion\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about. Even having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins,\ningredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n*Strange numbers*\n((13/6/13))", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy 2", "Kardel Irongut", "___________________ALCHEMICAL FUNDAMENTALS: A theoretical overview for the aspirant alchemist.\n___________________Penned by: Professor Doctor Kardel Heronsson Irongut, Alch.D, Arcn.D,M.D\n___________________PART 2: ALCHEMICAL LOGIC\n___________________CHAPTER 1:  THEORY OF POTIONCRAFT\n___________________Overview: Reverse examination of potioncraft, hypothetical potioncraft\n___________________\nEquipped with the base fundamentals (as mentioned in the tome preceding this one), the next logiecal question an aspirant alchemist should as is: “How do I use this information to brew potions and oils?”\nBefore we can begin looking into the practice of brewing, we must first understand the logic behind brewing potions and oils. This logic is paramount to beginning the practice of alchemy, as it underlies all processes of the discipline.\nFirst, let us recall the four elements, which are central to alchemical logic. Alchemical processes mix symbols of elemental Fire, Water, Earth, and Air to create brews with effects not initially encapsulated by the symbology of said elements.\nThe best way to illustrate the manner by which these elements are combined is to take into consideration a hypothetical scenario in which a potion consisting of a base of water is constructed from the following reagents:\nTwo weak water reagents, one weak earth reagent, and one weak air reagent. There is an underlying assumption that these reagents hold no adverse effects to counter each other's properties.\nWhat could such a mixture yield? First, let it be noted that this is a lesser potion, as evidenced by the usage of weak reagents and water as a base. This potion could not be any stronger due to this fundamental fact.\nSecond, let us examine the implications of including those aforementioned reagents in tandem. Water represents life, mending, rebirth, and so on. Thus, it could be deduced that the two water reagents function for healing purposes.\nLet us also examine the other reagents. A weak earth reagent represents fortification, spirit, and body. Perhaps its usage is to fortify the healing qualities of the previous water reagents.\nThe air reagent might seem out of place, but its function can be discerned. Air represents speed, efficiency, acuity, and quickness. Thus, such a reagent could have been used to quicken the effects of the potion.\nWhen all of these are considered in tandem, the potion’s function becomes less ambiguous: this hypothetical brew is meant to be a lesser potion of mending. It’s ultimate purpose is discernible from the materials used in crafting it.\nBy examining the alchemical process backwards, we can understand the procedure thoroughly. Brewing first consists of deciding a purpose. Once this purpose is decided, the strength of the brew is also decided.\nOnce those two variables have been fixed, the alchemist then works to realize his purpose. Reagents are used for the things which they represent, each reagent functioning as a component for the purpose of the alchemist.\nThus, when the reagents are aggregated, they come together as pieces of a whole. The life and mending qualities of water, the fortification of earth, and the speed of air combine to produce a brew which\nmends wounds (water and earth) more quickly (air). This logic is universal, and applies to all alchemical products from the weakest of potions to the strongest of oils.\nIt should be noted that, in reality, one cannot simply combine any water, earth, and air reagents in this fashion to create a potion of mending. Reagents, most often herbs, have their own physical properties.\nFor example, the reagent Athin, which is a strong water reagent, might theoretically also represent life. However, it’s natural properties make it toxic to consumption, and counteract that elemental aspect.\nSpecific reagents and their elemental and natural properties shall be covered in the next volume of this book. For now, a theoretical understanding of reagents suffices.\n___________________CHAPTER 2: APPLIED LOGIC- HYPOTHETICAL POTIONS\n___________________Overview: Potion of strength, potion of celerity, flaws in logic, consequences of potion usage\n___________________\nIn order to best consolidate the lesson in logic thoroughly provided in the previous chapter, more hypothetical scenarios should be examined. Let us take, firstly, a classic recipe for a potion of strength,\nThe recipe recommended by the legendary Ithric, in his Tales of a Travelling Alchemist, for such a potion is one based on Aqua Vitae. It utilizes three symbols of earth and one symbol of fire.\nSuch a combination would yield a greater potion of strength. It utilizes three symbols of earth, representing fortitude, strength and resilience. It also utilizes one of fire, representing added power.\nYet another hypothetical from the same work would be a potion of celerity. The recipe dictates the use of a base of distilled water, and two symbols of air. It is quite straightforward.\nSuch a potion utilizes air for speed, and utilizes it twice for added effect. Such a potion would make the user faster and more agile. Personally, I would also vie for the usage of an additional water reagent, for agility.\nNow, back to the world of hypotheticals. Suppose we wished to create a potion to resist the cold. A novice alchemist might suggest a base of distilled water, a water reagent for coldness, and an earth to represent resistance.\nThe brewer would be in for a surprise when such a potion would produce in him a resistant hypothermia. Indeed, the symbology of the potion represents such: resistant cold!\nTo create a potion of resistance to the cold, one would need a fire reagent and an earth reagent. Alchemical logic contains such ‘loops,’ and they must be mastered if the novice wishes to progress to mastery.\nAn additional note should be made on the nature of potions and alchemical brews in general. Alchemy is governed by equivalent exchange: thus, every benefit reaped from a potion would be accompanied by an equal consequence.\nTake, for example, the potion of strength described above. It would make the user stronger for a while, before debilitating their muscles after it wears off. Such is the nature of equivalent exchange.\nAs always, a mentor is recommended to guide an aspirant alchemist to mastery. Attempting to realize these brews without supervision results in comical and often deadly failures.\nA NOTE ON THE AUTHOR:\n\tProfessor-Doctor Kardel Heronsson Irongut, an alchemist of prestige and great renown, is famed for his contributions to the Alchemy community.\nMost famed for standardizing the recipe for Alchemist’s Fire and creating the recipe for the Potion of Regeneration, Doctor Irongut holds several diplomas and certifications in all types of alchemy and currently teaches at the Academy of Holm.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy 4", "Kardel Irongut", "___________________ALCHEMICAL FUNDAMENTALS: An overview for the aspirant alchemist.\n___________________Penned By: Professor Doctor Kardel Heronsson Irongut, Alch.D, Arcn.D, M.D\n___________________PART 4: METHODOLOGY AND PRACTICE\n_____\n     `.___,'\n      (___)\n      <   >\n       ) (\n      /`-.\\\n     /     \\\n    / _    _\\\n   :,' `-.' `:\n   |         |\n   :         ;\n    \\       /\n     `.___.'  FOREWORD:\nFirstly, a congratulations is in order! If you are reading this tome, it is assumed that you have completed the long list of readings preceding it. Secondly, caution! These techniques are not for the clueless!\nAlchemy is a versatile art with profound theoretical foundations, but with even more complex practical methodology. In order to manipulate the very fabric of the material plane, certain skills are necessary.\nThe following book is a short dissertation on the basics of carrying out the act of potioncraft itself, crafted as a supplement to a physical mentorship. Do not attempt these techniques without supervision!\nParticularly, the author of this work warns those delving into the art of the Material Plane to chase after those myths and legends that they will not create the Philosopher’s Stone by these techniques.\nThose myths are impossible to achieve, and caution is advised to those audacious enough to even attempt such feats.\n___________________CHAPTER 1: TOOLS AND FIXTURES\n___________________Overview: Basic alchemical equipment, Cauldrons, and Alembics.\n___________________\nThe Alchemist’s arsenal needs be built from the ground up. The first components of this arsenal are several flasks, of the standard Alchemical type. Such flasks may be commissioned from a glassblower.\nAlongside his flasks, and alchemist need equip himself with all types of herbological tools. A mortar and pestle are rudimentary. Jars for preservation of reagents are also necessary.\nNext to these fundamental items, the alchemist must also have an iron ladle (not wood: it is easily corroded) and a silver or gold coated knife (these two metals are proven to be sterile).\nA source of fire, either from a furnace or, preferrably, from an Alchemical Burner, is necessary as heat is fundamental to alchemical reactions. Potions cannot be brewed without it.\nNext, and Alchemist must secure for himself two very important objects: a cauldron and an alembic. These are the objects in which potions are brewed, and serve similar but divergent purposes.\nA good cast-iron cauldron with a tin lining is used to create new recipes. The cauldron allows more space for error and easier control of the brewing process, making it ideal for experimentation.\nAn alembic, procured from any herbology shoppe, is fundamental in replicating extant recipes. It allows for less room for innovation and error, but is more efficient in replicating results.\nA machine which expedites air flow in the room, or some sort of ventilator is also recommended. These fixtures must be improvised, as they are not offered commercially by any craftsmen.\n___________________CHAPTER 2: BREWING\n___________________Overview: Brewing with Cauldrons, Brewing with Alembics\n___________________\nThe process of brewing differs significantly from the Alembic to the Cauldron. An alchemist is always recommended to begin brewing with a cauldron, as the cauldron allows for more flexibility and error.\nWhen brewing with a cauldron, a constant source of heat must be established to heat the cauldron evenly throughout the process. Heat the cauldron until drops of water bead when touching its surface.\nWhen finished heating, fill the cauldron with the base of choice and let it simmer until a continuous stream of small bubbles is visible rising from the bottom of the cauldron to the top of the base.\nThen, add your reagents of choice, starting with the weakest and finishing with the strongest. It should be noted that, the more reagents which are added, the more volatile the potion will become.\nIf the potion becomes volatile, carefully but vigorously stir the contents of the cauldron with an iron ladle (avoid wood due to its easy corrosiveness). Stir until the brew settles back to a normal simmer.\nIt should be noted that Greater Potions are only able to be brewed in a cauldron. An Alembic does not have the sufficient capacity to brew any potion stronger than a moderate one.\n((OOC: When adding a reagent, standard brewing roleplay etiquette dictates that you roll 20. Reagent integration suceeds with the following rolls: weak, 7+; moderate, 10+; strong, 15+.))\n((If you do not roll the sufficient number the first time, emote the cauldron slowly boiling out of your control. You have three rolls per reagent to get the desired number. This prevents powergaming/mass production.))\n((Failure to roll the sufficient number three of three times will result in the potion being ruined, or having an effect other than its intended one. Adjust this process to your skill level and RP style accordingly.))\nAn Alembic is relatively straightforward to use, but the exact methodology depends on the type of alembic an alchemist has. Generally, an Alembic has two chambers: an upper chamber over a flame and a lower one.\nBefore using the alembic, extracts of the reagents to be used are prepared in a base of distilled water. It should be noted that an Alembic cannot brew greater potions, or potions of a high complexity.\nThese extracts are then poured into the upper chamber, and there they are left to simmer and distill. As the chamber maintains a constant pressure, the chance of failure is negligible in an Alembic.\nThe extracts input into the upper Fusion chamber will gradually travel through the downshoot of the Alembic and settle in the lower Condensation chamber. An alembic produces one dose of potion at a time.\nIt should be noted that experimentation can be carried out in an Alembic, but it is difficult due to the concealed nature of the reactions. A cauldron allows the alchemist more maneuverability in this regard.\n((OOC: Alembics are usually only used for mechanical brewing, and RP styles with them depend on the user. As always, it is good etiquette to include a chance of failure in your alchemy by rolling 20)).\n#######)#################(###)#############)###(###############)##(#############_______###########(_______)##########/          \\######  ##/             \\#####\n##|               |#####  ##\\             /##### \n###---------######\n###\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\######\nA NOTE ON THE AUTHOR:\n\tProfessor-Doctor Kardel Heronsson Irongut, an alchemist of prestige and great renown, is famed for his contributions to the Alchemy community.\nMost famed for standardizing the recipe for Alchemist’s Fire and creating the recipe for the Potion of Regeneration, Doctor Irongut holds several diplomas and certifications in all types of alchemy and currently teaches at the Academy of Holm.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy I", "Kardel Irongut", "___________________ALCHEMICAL FUNDAMENTALS:\nA theoretical overview for the aspirant alchemist.\n___________________Penned by: Professor Doctor Kardel Heronsson Irongut, Alch.D, Arcn.D, M.D\n__________________\nCHAPTER 1: EQUIVALENT EXCHANGE AND THE ELEMENTS\n___________________Overview: Equivalent Exchange, The Elemental Tetrarchy, and Basics of Symbology\n___________________\nWhen asked to define alchemy, most students tend to respond that alchemy is the making of potions and salves. Although not incorrect, Alchemy has more dimensionality then this. At its core alchemy is based upon the natural law of equivalent exchange.\nThe law of equivalent exchange states that one must give in equivalence of what one wishes to get. The trope of the alchemist, turning lead into gold, is rendered false by this law. Gold is not lead, and therefore lead cannot equate to gold.\nA gifted alchemist can attribute to lead those qualities which would make it seem like gold, but it is impossible to literally turn lead into gold. Alchemy is thus fundamentally about editing properties, exchanging reagents for said properties.\nAt the core of equivalent exchange are the elements. The elements are a tetrarchy: fire, water, earth, and air. Alchemy utilizes the symbolic meanings of these elements, morphing them to create a variety of effects within alchemy’s constraints.\nThe element of fire represents destruction, damage, power, and chaos. However, it also represents emotion, gusto, love, and passion. This twofold symbology is common to alchemical elements, and can be utilized for a variety of effects.\nWater represents life, birth and rebirth, agility, flow, and healing. It also represents sterility and coldness, both metaphorical and literal. Water is often used to produce mending effects, but can also be utilized for destructive purposes.\nEarth is the most straightforward of the bunch. It represents flora, fauna, spirit, fortitude, stalwartness, strength, and reinforcement. It is often used to fortify potions, and on its own is the base for such brews as the potion of strength.\nThe element of air is abstract, and represents knowledge, wisdom, acuity, clarity, perception, speed, and alacrity. It is often used for potions such as that of speed, or to quicken the effects of a brew.\nCHAPTER 2: REAGENTS AND BASES\n___________________Overview: Theoretical reagentry, overview of representative symbology, overview of symbological strength, and study of solvent-bases\n___________________\nThe elements are made useful to the alchemist through their appearance in the material plane as reagents. Reagents, also referred to as symbols, are naturally occurring items which represent aspects of the aforementioned elements.\nReagents are classified in three categories: weak, moderate, and strong. These categories do not indicate the physical toughness of reagents. Rather, they indicate their rarity, and therefore their value, in accordance with equivalent exchange.\nWeak reagents are common symbols which represent their given element slightly. Coal dust would be a weak reagent of fire: it is common and does not immediately represent any of fire’s aspects.\nModerate reagents are uncommon symbols which fairly represent their given element. Serpent’s stalk is one such reagent, which grows by forested lakes and contains a gelatinous core with burn-relieving properties, representing the healing of water.\nStrong reagents are very rare reagents which almost perfectly represent their given element. Crimson Vase is such a reagent, found scarcely in the depth of sprawling woods and containing a corrosive acid representative of fire’s destruction.\nReagents are mixed within solvents known as bases. Bases come in three varieties: bases of water, bases of distilled water, and bases of Aqua vitae, or distilled alcohol. Lard is a fourth base, used in making alchemical oils.\nCHAPTER 3: ELEMENTARY POTIONCRAFT\n___________________Overview: Alchemical brews, strength of brews, typology of alchemical products \n___________________\nPotions come in three varieties: lesser, moderate, and greater. The more potent the potion, the more difficult it is to brew. Lesser potions are the least potent, and greater potions are the most potent.\nLesser potions have a base of water, and utilize weak reagents. This results in weak and often inadequate brews. These brews are brewed by students to hone their practical alchemical skills.\nModerate potions have a base of water and utilize moderate reagents, with weak reagents sometimes used to minorly alter effect. They are difficult to brew, and consist of the majority of potions. Their potency is standard.\nGreater potions use a base of Aqua Vitae and strong reagents, also utilizing the other two classes. They are notoriously complex to brew even for masters, and are infamous for their dangerous volatility.\nOils are another subset of alchemical brew which utilizes lard as their base. Oils are applied topically, and their effects range from healing to destructive. Oils are extremely difficult to brew due to the consistency of their base.\nIt should be noted that brewing any alchemical substance should not be done without supervision by any novice alchemist. These substances are volatile and can prove deadly. Thus, mentorship is recommended for any aspirant alchemist.\nA NOTE ON THE AUTHOR:\nProfessor-Doctor Kardel Heronsson Irongut, an alchemist of prestige and great reknown, is famed for his contributions to the Alchemy community.\nMost famed for standardizing the recipe for Alchemist’s Fire and creating the recipe for the Potion of Regeneration, Doctor Irongut holds several diplomas and certifications in all types of alchemy and currently teaches at the Academy of Holm.\nPRINTED ON THE 9TH OF THE SUN'S SMILE, 1658. JOURNAL OF THE ACADEMY OF HOLM.\n\nTHIS WORK MAY NOT BE COPIED, REPRINTED, OR DISTRIBUTED WITHOUT PERMISSION OF THE AUTHOR.\n\nHolm City, Consulate of Holm.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Mind C1", "Jaya Jayamen", "A Game of Minds\n\n         ~Originally by\n      Sofetios Jayamen\n\n      ~Transcribed by\n        Jaya Jayamen\nChapter One\n       Awakening\n\nPulsing. That is all I felt as I started to see again. Everything was a hazy black, even though my eyes were open. Another pulse happened inside my head. Things were beginning to become clearer.\nI could make out that I was in a small stone room… and there was something else in here with me. \n\n“Finally awake are we?” said a gruff voice.\nI could now see moderately well and saw a person sitting across from me looking straight at me. I coughed and said slowly, \n\n“Where am I?” My head still throbbing though getting better. The person then said,\n“You’re in the holding chamber, along with me.”\n\n“Holding chamber?” I said. “Holding chamber for what?” \n\nAt that moment there was a slight creak to the right, as a part of the wall opened up like a door.\nThe person got up and came to me, holding out a hand. He said,\n\n“We had better get going. They don’t like it when we stall.”\n\n I grasped his hand and he hoisted me to my feet. I nearly fell over but he helped me catch myself.\nI looked upwards and far above saw there was an opening to the sky.   I said,\n\n“Who are they?” The Person helped walk me toward the door.\n\n“They call themselves…The Followers of Truth.” He said.\nWe got to the door and opened it. A burst of cold air came in.  In the room was only a table that had frost on it, A fireplace to the right that had a metal pan on the fire with what appeared to be a steak on it,\na small vase with a small top for an opening was on the floor with a lid next to it, a poufy cushioned chair was next to the fireplace, and there was also another  fireplace at the far back of the room.\n“Great…”  Said the person. “ A Puzzle.” He walked with me into the room. As we got in the door slammed behind us. “Can you stand now?” \n\nI nodded slightly as I took my own weight back.\n “Good. My name is Jed. And you are?”\n“James.” I said.  Suddenly  I felt an overwhelming hunger as I smelled the smell of cooked steak. \n\nI looked to the steak and started to walk over, but as I did Jed caught my arm. “What?” I said.\n“The thing you should know about this place, is that it’s a death trap.” Said Jed with a serious face. \n\nHe lifted his sleeve and there was a severe gash about a foot long. “That.” He said pointing to it, “Is what happens when you aren’t careful.”\nI nodded, swallowing. “We had best get to solving this. Before the cold kills us.” \n\n I hadn’t noticed but my toes were starting to get numb. \n\n“What should we be looking for?” I said.\n“A key to get back into the room we came in.” Said Jed.\n\n“What!?” I said, “We were just in there?” \n\n“Trust me James.” Said the man. “We are better off in here for the moment.\nWhat you need to understand is that The Followers of Truth are ruthless. If you don’t do what they want you to, they will make you do it, one way or another. Put your ear to the door.”\nI went to the door and put my ear on it like he said to, There were clicking sounds in the room. Like a giant insect walking in there.\n\n“Hear that?” Said Jed. “That’s the Grimmer.”  I got my head off the door and said. “The Grimmer?”\n“Yes.” Said Jed. “A fearsome thing, they release it through the ceiling in the room to make sure we are out.” Then a thought occurred to me. I said,\n\n“How do they know we are still alive?” Jed Shrugged the said,\n“Well If the Grimmer doesn’t come back early I guess that means we are still alive. Either way we better find the key to the door. Before they push us to find it. Now where is that key…” \n\n“Maybe it’s in the chair?” I said.\n“Seems poufy enough to hide it.”\n\n“Or to hide something else.” Said Jed. “Always test things.”  Jed took a glove off and went to the couch. I followed but at a distance. Jed, a few feet from the couch tossed it on the Seat.\nSuddenly there was a click. The cushion rose at an incredible speed to the ceiling, crushing the glove, then lowered back to position. The cushion didn’t even flatten, like it was rock hard. The glove fell on the floor. Jed looked to me, nodded, and said.\n“See?”  As he picked up and put his glove back on. “Never touch things directly.”\n\nI took off my own glove went to the jar on the floor.  I kind of whacked it with my glove. It just knocked over… and something clanked inside it.\nJed looked to me and said “Be careful.” I put my glove back on, picked up the jar and looked in the opening. The key was inside it. \n\n“The key is in here!” I said. I turned the jar upside-down and the key came out… a little. It was stuck.\nJed let out a small grunt. “They never make it that easy.”\n\n Suddenly there was a trickling and hissing sound coming from the right fireplace.  There was a small amount of water coming above the fireplace.  Jed cursed under his breath.\nI then panicked. I tried to smash the vase against the floor. \n\n“Stop!” Said Jed. The vase landed on the ground in my hand with a large thump, but didn’t break.\n“Careful you idiot! That could have been filled with glass as well as the key.”\n“Well what are we supposed to do?” I said in a panicked voice. Jed then said, “Not panic. That’s what they want us to\nI rushed over and tossed it on the cushion. The Cushion rose again with great speed and there was a loud clunk.\n\n The cushion went back down and the vase, still whole, rolled off it. Jed cursed again. “So it’s unbreakable.”\nJed then angrily threw his glove at the frosted table. As it hit the side, it stuck and turned frosted too.  I came to a sudden realization. \n\n“The water!” I said.\n“Yes I know.” Said Jed looking down at the ground, which had water rising a half inch above it.\n\n“No I mean-“ I didn’t explain. I ran to the jar, picked it up and the lid.\nI ran to the fireplace that was now extinguished, and filled it with water, then screwed on the lid. I turned to Jed who looked at me and looked like he just realized what I was doing and nodded. I Hurried to the table and tossed the jar at the side.\nAs it made impact it stuck, frosted, then in a few short seconds the jar exploded. Shards went everywhere. Startled, I yelled and was knocked back on my backside.  A few cut me on my arms and legs from the jar shards.   “Jed!” are you alright?”\nI heard a few curses from the back of the room.\n\n“I’m fine.” Said Jed. ”I should have seen that coming.”  I saw that there was a solid bit of ice in the shape of a vase on the floor near the table, and inside was the key.\nI picked it up and headed to the extinguished fireplace, picking up the pan.  I didn’t head to the chair to smash the ice because I didn’t want to risk breaking the key.\nBy now the water was an inch and a half deep and I couldn’t feel from my ankle down. I ran carefully to the other fireplace, which was luckily a bit raised off the ground, but not much.\nI put the pan over the fire and the ice on top of it. The pan was about 2 inches deep. Jed came behind me. After about a minute, the pan was full of water, but the ice wasn’t melted all the way.\nI took the ice in one hand and tossed the water in the pan on the ground with the other, then put the ice back on the pan and started melting it again.\nAfter another minute the key was free. I took it and went to the door where Jed was now with his ear on the door. He took his head off the door and said,\n“The Grimmer isn’t there anymore.”\n\n  I unlocked it and opened it. The Grimmer wasn’t in there. I went into the room and Jed followed me, but suddenly the door closed, there was a hissing sound, like air through a tight space.\nI started coughing, as if the air was bad. So did Jed. \n\nThen everything went black.\n\nEnd of Chapter\nThank you for reading the Jayamen family’s books! If you have any feedback or enjoyed reading, please let me, Jaya Jayamen (( Sofetios )), know. Peoples responses help me write more books! Thanks again! ~Jaya Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Mind C2", "Jaya Jayamen", "A Game of Minds\n\n       ~Originally by\n    Sofetios Jayamen\n\n     ~Transcribed by\n          Jaya Jayamen\nChapter Two\n      The Escape\n\nI heard a voice far away “Get up…” I struggled to get myself into consciousness. The voice came again, only a bit louder. “Get up…” Then suddenly I felt something slap me in the face. Hard.\n“GET UP MAN!” I burst into consciousness. Jed was kneeling down in front of me with a serious worried expression. \n\n“Wha- What’s going on?” I said hurriedly. Jed just said,\n\n“I don’t know. That’s the problem! Now get up!”\nI stood up hurriedly as Jed did too.  Jed looked to the stone wall and said, “The door, it hasn’t opened yet.” \n\nCautious \n\n“What does that mean?” I said.\n“It means that if we don’t get in there soon, we’re dead.” Jed said as he pointed up. \n\nI looked up to the opening in the top. I saw the sun blaring down at me.\n\n“What do you mean?” I said now a bit panicked. Jed cursed and said,\n“The Grimmer! That’s what I mean! The door always opens by noon!”\n\n A loud high pitched squeal then came from somewhere above us. Jed cursed again as he ran to the wall where the door was and started banging on it.\n“Let us in you cultists!”\n\n I was about to run forward to him but when I took the first step There was something hard beneath my shoe. I looked down. It was the key. Hurriedly I picked it up and ran to the door. Jed looked at me and then to the key.\nThere was another squeal from above. I looked up and there it was. It looked like a 6 legged thing from a nightmare. This time I cursed. I jammed the key into the small hole and turned it.\nIt opened and me and Jed went in quickly and then slammed the door shut. When we turned around, we didn’t see the furniture and things from before. We saw a person and two others beside him in black robes and hoods, which concealed their faces.\nThe middle one was clapping and smiling, he said,\n\n“Well done, well done. I didn’t think you would make it through that last one, James.”\n\n“Who are you?” I said. Then Jed said slowly,\n“They’re The Followers of Truth.” \n\n Suddenly the man in the middle’s smile disappeared and got angry as he looked at Jed and said,\n\n“Quite, fool. You have no idea what that name even means.”\nThen the man’s head turned to me as he smiled again,\n\n “Now James, don’t you recognize me? Or maybe you wouldn’t seeing as we’ve never seen each other directly. I am your father.”\nI was taken aback heavily at this remark as Jed looked at me. I said, \n\n“What are you talking about? My father is a merchant, not you!”  The man just laughed and said,\n“Your ‘father’ was just a tool. Did he never tell you how he got you? Oh the things people tell their kids. Such a shame.” \n\nThe man smiled a wicked smile then said, “Bring them in.”\nSuddenly the floor behind the man and the two black robed ones opened like a trapdoor. \n\nThree people with black robes emerged and were dragging two people with them, one was a girl that looked in her late teens, and the other was…\n“Father!” I yelled. \n\n“Lily!” yelled Jed next to me. \n\nWe both ran towards them but the two black robed ones with the man came forward with great speed and caught both of us with an iron grip.\n“Yes, Father indeed.” Said the man that was in the middle with a chuckle. \n\n“and a father no more. Take them to the other holding chambers.”\nTwo of the black robed people that had the girl and my father went down the trapdoor again, but the other one stayed.\n\n The wicked man turned to see the other black robed one there and hissed, “Didn’t you hear me? Go with them you idiot!”\nThe black robed one that he was talking to then said,\n\n“Not today.”\n\n The black robed one extended his arms quickly toward the two ones holding Jed and I.\nTwo large metallic needles flew out of both sleeves hitting both of the ones holding us through the neck.\nBefore Jed and I could make a move, the wicked man, startled, flew past me and Jed with amazing speed and went through the door as the man who had the needles shot one at him.\nThe door closed right before the needle hit, but the needle went right inside the keyhole sticking in it.  There was a commotion from underneath the floor where the trapdoor was.\nThe person who was shooting the needles took off the black robes in a quick motion. The person had a rope around his shoulder to his waist with a grappling hook. I slightly recognized him, but I couldn’t place his face. He said,\n“Come on!” as he ran toward the door the other man went through. Jed seemed to get his wits together and said, “Come on James!”  I hesitated and said,\n\n“But my father!” The man who was in the black robes then said,\n“We’ll get him later, trust me, but if you want to live to see it we need to go now!” I hesitated a second then said,\n\n“Fine. How do we get out?”\n\n“Through the door.” Said the man opening it.\n“But the Grimmer?” I said. Then I saw inside and nothing was there.\n\n“He used it to escape.” Said the man, “It’s his pet. Now come on!”\n\nJed and I hurried through the door after him.\nThe man slammed the door shut and removed the needle keeping it unlocked. I looked to the man as he unraveled the grappling hook from around him, swung it around, and threw it up out of the ceiling.\nHe tugged on the rope hard to make sure it was good, then started climbing up it, Jed followed, and then me. I hadn’t climbed a rope before, so I was much slower than the others. There was a banging on the door below.\nThe man had gotten to the top and Jed was nearly there as well. I was only 10 feet above the floor and it looked like I had 20 to go.  Jed got out the top and I heard the man say, “Help me pull the rope.”\nI was slowly being pulled up to the top as I still attempted to climb. I was just at the top when the door below burst open and people in black robes came in. I looked down as I grasped the side of the top. One of them had a bow and arrows.\nI was just about to get out about to my waist when I heard the sound of a bow being released. I yelled as I felt an arrow pierce my leg.\nI lost my grasp and was about to fall back into the hole, when Jed let go of the rope, dove, and grasped my arm at the last second.\nThe man ran over too and grasped my other arm, together hoisting me over the side of the hole just as another arrow flew through it. I put my arms around both their shoulders as we stood up.\nI looked around for a second, We were in what looked like an abandoned town with many large holes in the ground that looked like the one we came out of.\n“I have two horses at the entrance, let’s move!” Said the man as he coiled his rope and grappling hook rapidly. We made our way to the entrance as fast as I could go with the arrow still in my leg.\nAs I looked back I saw another grappling hook come out of the hole. After half a minute of us moving and pain in my leg, we made it to the entrance. Like the man said there were two hardy looking horses tied to a post.\nI looked back again and saw a black robed arm come out of the hole.  The man got me to one of the horses and helped me on it with surprising strength. “Get on the other one.” The man said to Jed.\nJed did so as the man cut the ropes attaching the horses to the post with a large dagger that was in his belt.  He then climbed swiftly in front of me on the horse and got it moving as Jed got his moving too.\nThere was nothing coming from behind us as we got to a full gallop. I looked behind us one more time to see the people in black robes just standing at the entrance we were at. One pointed at me and made a motion of slitting his throat.\nI looked ahead of us, holding on as we rode onward.\n\nEnd of Chapter\nThank you for reading the Jayamen family’s books! If you have any feedback or enjoyed reading, please let me, Jaya Jayamen (( Sofetios )), know. Peoples responses help me write more books! Thanks again! ~Jaya Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Mind C3", "Jaya Jayamen", "A Game of Minds\n\n         ~Originally by\n     Sofetios Jayamen\n\n      ~Transcribed by\n           Jaya Jayamen\nChapter Three\n        The Arrow\n\nAfter about five minutes of riding, I began to feel cold and weak. I looked down to the arrow in my leg, for some reason it was already clotted over with the blade of it still in my leg.\nThe man in front of me noticed my shift in weight and looked at the arrow to for a second. Then he cursed, looked over to Jed who was riding beside us and said, “Stop the horse!” Both Jed’s horse and the horse I was on came to a stop.\nThe man got off and motioned me to get off. I got off as he helped me down and Jed got off his as well.\n\n“What are we stopping for?” Said Jed impatiently.\n\n“The arrow.” Said the man.\n“The arrow can wait!” Said Jed. “It’s already clotted over anyways.”\n\n“Exactly why we need to stop.” Said the man. He took the horses rein with one hand and helped me walk with the other.\nJed followed behind us with his horse as we went into the thick woods on the side of the path we were on. \n\nWe were walking in the woods for a bit when the man said, “Do you feel cold? Weak as well?”\n“Actually yes.” I said a bit worried. “Isn’t that normal for an arrow wound?” The man cursed under his breath then said,\n\n“No, not this fast at least.”  I looked at him and said, \n\n“What do you mean?” The man sighed and said,\n“The blood already clotting on your leg isn’t a good thing, It also isn’t just a coincidence. The clotting allows the blood to circulate unhindered.” I put the pieces of information together, then said,\n\n“Are you saying I’ve been…”\n“Poisoned, yes.” Said the man seriously. I panicked and reached down to the arrow to grasp it and pull it out, but the man grabbed my hand before I could do it. “It’s best to leave it in for now, trust me.”\n“What are we going to do!?” I half said half yelled. The man didn’t say anything for a few seconds then said,\n\n“We are going to see an old friend of mine.” I nodded slightly, regaining my wits as we walked on into the woods.\nWe were still walking in the woods until I spotted a wooden hut with a straw roof. By now I was freezing and stumbling even though it was sunny out and I had the mans support. The man tied both horses to a tree then we went toward the hut.\nWe came to its entrance and the man peeked inside. There was no one there.  “Era?” The man said. “You in here?” We walked a bit into the hut on the wooden floor until there was a crunch of paper under the man’s foot.\nThe man frowned and picked up the paper, but as he did, there was a quiet ‘snap’ and a ‘rip’. A piece of the paper was torn off by something. Then I saw it.  It was being dragged fast by a small piece of string.\n“MOVE!” The man yelled. He basically ran and dragged me out at the same time. Jed was right behind us. We were about 30 feet from the hut when I felt heat from behind me. We slowed down, stopped, and turned around. The hut was in flames.\nI turned to the man and saw he had fear and anger in his eyes. He looked quickly at the paper. Then he jammed it into a ball, yelled, and threw it at the fire. The man was breathing heavily in anger, Then as he started to calm down,\nhe said in a lower voice, “They have her…”  Neither Jed or I said anything, Until the man turned his head a bit to me and said, “I’m sorry, but you are most likely going to pass out with the poison.”\n\n“You mean I’m not going to die?” I said.\n“No, you’re not.” Said the man, “He, wants you alive.” For some reason I felt that passing out would be worse than dying. \n\n“There’s got to be another way to get something to cure the poison.” The man shook his head and said,\n“The only other place that would have the poison’s cure is Era’s Stash. Even I’ve never found it.” The hut was nearly burnt to the ground now.\n“Then lets go!” Said Jed, “We can put James on the horse, get out of here, and find someone else to give the cure!”\n\n“Don’t you understand.” Said the man sadly.\n“We’re most likely surrounded, or soon to be. That trap wasn’t meant to kill us, it was meant as a signal.” These words made me feel more chilled then the poison did. I turned to the hut once again. Then, I saw it.\nThe hut had burnt to the ground, but also the wooden floor that was there, what was left was the outline of a square on stone. \n\n“Look!” I said pointing to the remains. The man looked and his eyes got wider.\n“Could it…” He started saying but never finished. “Let’s move!” We went as fast as we could toward the remains but I could barely walk. When we got there, the man pulled out his long dagger and fit it between the crack where the outline was.\nThen used it like a lever lifting the square indent. As it rose I got a strong smell of herbs coming from underneath. The man held the stone square up and told Jed to help me down. There was a steep stepping ladder going down.\nAs I descended with Jed’s help I noticed bottles and beakers in the faint lighting as well as their shadows against the wooden plank walls. There were also cabinets against the wall as well as a large wooden bookcase, except there were beakers on it.\nWhen we got to the floor, the man came after us. Jed sat me down on an old wooden chair that was there as the man went though cabinets and bottle looking for the right one.\nThe man ran to me carrying a small bottle, a piece of cloth, a leaf, and a piece of leather. He opened the bottle and whispered, “Brace yourself, this will hurt. It is very important you don’t make noise.” He gave me the piece of leather and said,\n“Bite on this.” I did so. He grasped the arrow and carefully but quickly took it out, along with a bit of the clotted blood. I grunted in pain and bit down hard on the leather as it was removed and the blood started rushing out.\nHe then quickly poured some of the liquid in the bottle on the wound. It felt like lava as it fizzed and bubbled. I felt like I was getting stronger and warmer at the same time but it was still painful.\nThen the man after pouring more on quickly put the leaf on the wound and wrapped the cloth around it, tying it in place. As soon as the leaf made contact with the wound I felt a rushing feeling of relief, as if the leaf was absorbing the pain.\nAfter the man was done, he whispered,  “That should do it. Now we better get out of here and fast. Can you stand?” I tried standing up, I was a bit wobbly but much better than before. “Good.” whispered the man to Jed and I.\n“They are probably waiting above us as we speak. Now our only chance is to find an alternative exit. Era would most likely have made a side tunnel down here. Let’s get to looking.”  We then dispersed around the room quietly.\nJed was checking large cabinets, as the man was checking the floor by knocking on different parts of it. I decided to do the same thing with the walls. I went all around the room and all the wood planks had the same sound when I knocked on them.\nI looked to the other two, the man was shaking his head as he kept knocking on the floor, and Jed was making his way to the bookcase, until he tripped. Jed reached out attempting to grab something to catch his fall. He ended up grabbing an empty beaker.\nInstead of him crashing to the floor and breaking the beaker, The beaker stayed on the shelve, as if it was attached somehow. Jed gathered his balance and stood up looking at the beaker. Then man came over and I did as well. Jed then whispered,\n“Who makes a beaker that won’t come off?”\n\n“Someone who doesn’t want it to fall off.” Whispered the man as he went to the side of the shelve. He motioned Jed to get to the other side. “We’re moving this to the side, on three. One, Two, Three.”\nJed and the man carefully lifted the bookcase and carried it to the side dropping it slowly. Behind the bookshelf was… more planks. The man went to them and knocked on one.\nIt sounded the same as the others. He cursed and looked like he was thinking hard. Then I noticed something, I looked up and there was a tiny beam of light coming from the top of one of the planks. I whispered,\n“Look.” To the man. He looked up where I was looking and whispered slowly,\n\n“False planks? But how? It would have sounded hollow…” The man went over with his dagger and put it in-between the wood.\nHe wiggled the blade hard and the planks shifted slightly to the side. Both the man and I looked into the crack it created. The planks were about a foot thick, and on the other side there was what looked like a tunnel. “No wonder…” he whispered.\n“Get me the chair you were sitting in James.” I went over to the chair and brought it over to him. He took it and placed it against the planks to the left of the one that had the light, out of its way.\nHe took out his dagger and pulled the plank toward where it was cracked to make a small bit of space on the bottom of the plank. He put his dagger in the middle of the bottom space so the plank was balanced on it.\nThen he put his hands under the plank and moved it the plank out a bit, but then it got stuck. He whispered a curse then he whispered to me,\n“Now I know your still weak James, but I need you to get on this chair and pull the top of the plank, Jed and I will catch it so we don’t make too much noise if it falls. And here use this to get enough of the plank exposed to pull.”\nHe pulled out a second dagger from his belt and held it out. I nodded and took the dagger. Then the man nodded and I got up on the chair. I put the dagger in-between where the plank met the ceiling.\nI wiggled it up and down until there was about an inch of the plank exposed. The man looked up and whispered “Now pull.” I pulled with all of my strength and so did the man.\nThere was a slight scraping sound of the dagger being dragged with the plank across the ground. Jed grasped the plank and helped too when it was out enough.\nAs soon as it got free from the wall, the man quickly took out the dagger on the bottom so the plank didn’t lean one way and topple over. “Alright.” He whispered. “Now to lean it down, slowly.”\nThe man, Jed, and I slowly leaned the heavy plank down on the floor so it didn’t make much of a sound. When we finished, we looked through where the plank was. There was a stone passageway and on the side of the left wall was a lit torch.\nThe man went through the opening and Jed and I followed. He went up to the torch and whispered,\n“This torch was lit about an hour ago… So, maybe she isn’t captured.” He smiled. “Let’s go, I hope this leads somewhere good.”\n\nEnd of Chapter\nThank you for reading the Jayamen family’s books! If you have any feedback or enjoyed reading, please let me, Jaya Jayamen (( Sofetios )), know. Peoples responses help me write more books! Thanks again! ~Jaya Jayamen", ""),
