        StaticBook("Pendle to Now", "Madeline Applefoot", "Recent Halfling History by Madeline Applefoot\n\nWhile Petyr and Faldo have written great works concerning halfling histories of old, I beleive that a book on newer halfling events should be made, and thus I have made this book here.\n-Madeline\nThe Sundering of the Halflings:\n\nAfter the villages of Mellsburry and Reedsborough died off with time, us halflings were left without any place to call home except for the hamlet of Rivershire, which was too small to fit us all, and didn't cary any of the\ncomforts of the previous villages. The rest of the halflings went to live with the Druids or inside of Metz and Lorraine, the halfling population scattered, forgotten. Little happened during this time, as I recall. Most of this time was spent looking for\nthe other halflings, and going about doing whatever it is we do when we are left with no village to call home.\nThe Early Days of Pendlemere:\n\nEventually, us wee folks got fed up with having no good village to call home, and halflings such as Gerald, Merridolph (my brother), Larry, and numerous others sent out to build such a village. After a quick talk, it was\ndecided that the new village would be in Lorraine, for the countryside there was very green and fertile. I, unfortunately, was not one of these halflings. I was busy wandering southern Ceru, wondering where all the halflings of Reedsborough went. However,\nthose that were there tell of a glorious time for the halflings, a time of new things and the old ways coming back. They found a spot along Cotton Lake, and began building. Many fun times were had, including a grand and dangerous adventure into the Sunles\nSanctum itself! Ah.... I wish I was there. \n\nEventually, the early glorious of Pendlemere passed by, with the death of Gerald and the Murder 'n The Morn, where Orcs attacked Pendlemere and took many lives. The halflings who remained alive tried to rebuild\nwhat had been lost, but it could just not be done with the sudden drop in existing halflings.\nThe Middle Days of Pendlemere:\n\nDuring this time, I managed to find my way to the village of Pendlemere and begin living there. I still remember the green hills of that place, and I still remember Gerald's old burrow, which I took as my own. Less things\noccuring during this time then during the time before it, and the only real thing of importance that happened was the Festival of the Harvest. The Festival lasted through the entire day and into the night, and consisted of feasting, pie eating, singing,\ndancing, drinking, shogging, and all sorts of merry things us halflings do. The Festival was on my brother's Eleventy First birthday as well! How fun!\n\nMy brother was given a magical chalice as a gift, a chalice that never ran out of tasty booze inside.\nHe drank and he drank and he drank and he drank and he drank some more, untill he keeled over and died of alcohol poisoning. Without the sense of progression that going through each mug of booze gives you, he had no way to tell how much he was drinking.\nThe chalice was declared a dangerous artifact, and it was buried under the soil by Cotton Lake so that none could use it's deadly power. Plans were made to eventually dispose of it, and the Pendlemere continued on.\nThe Last Days of Pendlemere:\n\nNew halflings still came into the village every so often, yet the village was not what it once was. What was once the grand home of the halflings turned into quiet land of little interest to anyone who didn't live there\nI married Harold Withfoot, and with him I had two children, Caroline Applefoot, and Merry Applefoot. The wedding was supposed to be a grand festival like the Harvest Festival of Pendlemere that had come before, yet only a handful of halflings showed up.\nMore plans were made to destroy the Evil Chalice that had killed my brother, yet none of these plans were acted on. The chalice remained buried in the soil untill the end of Axios itself. Other than my wedding, not much else happened. A few dwarves came\nby and enslaved Olive Burrows, some random biggun came along and robbed the Storage Burrow clean of everything, but for the most part, the tale of Pendlemere had come and gone.\nThe Village of Mapleshire:\n\nMapleshire is a large topic, much like Pendlemere, yet I will not even try to describe it all here, or even chop it up into segments. Too much happened, and it's all a jumble in my head now.\nMapleshire was formed in order to bring new life to us wee folks, and it did that very well. Life was generally good, and if anything, the place was too noisy instead of too quiet. \n\nMapleshire was constructed by the Sutican Gulf, and had connections to\nthe sea and to Sutica, just like Reedsborough of old. Hektor Blackwood, a local wizard, visited us often, as he had during Pendlemere, and we shared many good times with him. The tavern was always busy, although burrows were used for drinking as well.\nHektor, as a Canonist monk, tried to convert us to Canonism, claiming that the evils bigguns did in the name of Canonism was just because they were twisting it's word for their own goals. He built a Canonist Chapel, and tried to get a priest to hold mass\nin the village. We met up with a High Priest of Canonism, and me and Hektor discussed the recent Canonism with him. Many words were said, and eventually homosexuality was brought up. Hektor's apprentice asked the Priest if homosexuality was accepted by\nthe church, and the High Priest said that it wasn't. Later that day, Hektor burst into tears, abandoning his quest to convert us, and likely falling out with Canonism alltogether. It was no longer a good religion taught wrong, it was a bad religion,\na religion halflings should have no part in.\n\nWhen Ceru started to fall apart during Axios' demise, Hektor, his apprentice, Harold, and I gathered together to summon Lord Knox himself for guidance. A ritual was planned, yet Lord Knox showed himself\nwithout the ritual, right there in the storage burrow with us all. Lord Knox was a scary fellow, as I recall. Yet he gave us good advice. He told us to flee, and to save ourselfs. To put a long story short, we followed his word, and ended up in Atlas with\nthe others...\nFernswood, the Walled Village:\n\nIn the beginnings of Atlas, we lived in Fernswood, a village inside of Sutica. And when I mean inside, I mean inside of the biggun city itself, as there were no other suitable spots in the rocky terrain of the isles.\nThe halflings all moved into this Fernswood place after it was built, and we lived there for a short while, with nothing of note happening other than the establishment of the Fernswood Sheriff's department by Wimpy Lee. The village went against almost all\nof our halfling values, it was inside of a walled city, it was too close to bigguns, and it wasn't made anywhere near the countryside us halflings adore. It was too much biggun, and not enough halfling. To put things short, it didn't last long.\nThe First Dunshire, a New Village: \n\nUnlike all the other villages written about so far in my book, this First Dunshire is a village you can still visit! Just spend some time looking around the Whispering Crossroads, and you'll find it!\nIn order to escape the horrors of Fernswood, a few halflings (most notably Hobbs Burrows) set out to make a better village in the wild lands of the Whispering Crossroads. The flowers there are crisp and fresh even in winter, and the village is surrounded\nwith an aura of halflingness that even Pendlemere didn't capture. It consisted of a handful of burrows around a lake, an ale tent, and many flowers and pumpkin lights to make the place feel like home. Abandoned, yet still a place full of wonder. Beautiful\nThe Second Dunshire to Present Times:\n\nAfter Dunshire and Fernswood surviving as seperate villages for some time, the halflings grew weary of the seperation. Eventually, the monks reached out to us. They let us live in a small area of the Cloud\nTemple lands, and we gladly accepted their offer. In this new Dunshire, our peoples are safe from harm, and we may be peaceful as we have always been. \n\n\nIt is with this, I conclude my writings. Not much else needs to be said, other than\nour continued survival as a race, and our good blessings to all the good folks and bigguns out there! Drinks for all! And may all have a merry good day!", ""),
        StaticBook("People's Rule", "Daisy Applefoot", "People's Rule\n\nby Daisy Applefoot\n\n\nFor far too long have the nations of Atlas gone without the instituions needed to guarentee the happiness and prosperity of their people. These institutions are none\nother than Democracy.\n\nBut what is Democracy? Is Democracy an election, where the people are allowed to choose which of the ruling class they'd rather have lord over them? Is Democracy a tavern where people freely discuss ideas? Is Democracy all of those\nyet none of those all at the same time?\n\nOf course, Democracy is not any one thing. Democracy is a culture, an ideology. It is a way of governance and a way of living life. It is putting the needs of the many above the needs of the few. It is hearing the\ncries of the downtrodden, the oppressed, the huddled masses yearing to be free.\n\nThen how are we to do this \"Democracy\" stuff anyways? How are we to organize ourselves to be Democratic? The answer is simple. Instead of lords, merchants, politicians,\nkings and queens, warmongers, and other non-democratic members of society's ruling class, we ought to organize ourselves to be tailors, farmers, miners, blacksmiths, shipbuilders, bakers, and workmen, for from these people true power is derived, and\nwithout these people society falls apart. We do not need any lords ruling over us, any merchants controlling the prices of bread, or any kings bossing us around. Nor shall we even compromise and allow politicians to rule over us, for they are subject to\ntheir own whims and desires instead of solely the interests of the people. The people ought to gather themselves around the tavern and talk amongst each other as equals, for this talk is the true voice of the people, and the true call of justice. Let the\npeople set the prices of the markets, and decide what laws are to be passed and how. Let the people control this all, for none know the interests of the people better than the people themselves.\nOn Mercantilism\n\nMercantilism is not Democratic, despite what others may have you think. It relies on the strong pushing out and feasting upon the weak instead of the two bonding together for mutual benefit. It controls the markets and sets the prices\nbased on profit instead of on societal benefit. The few cases where the interests of Society and the Merchants align are rare and few indeed. The merchant wants to make as much as he can from his goods, and the people want to pay as little as they can for\nit. This is an inherent conflict between people and those who sell them bread that cannot be ignored. Maybe in the split few moments between buying a loaf and eating it, the two might consider the trade to be mutually beneficial, but such beliefs soon\nfall apart when consumer checks his pockets for the spare change to buy another loaf, or when the merchant draws in his daily profit and frowns upon seeing its miniscule size. \n\nKnowing that Mercanilism is undemocratic, how are we to organize the\nmarkets in a way that is Democratic? The answer is simple yet again. Instead of the merchant running his own stall, let the men and women who produce the goods run the stall. Let them collaborate with the people to set prices that are fair for all.\nOn Feudalism\n\nFeudalism, even more so than Mercantilism, is undemocratic, for what could be more undemocratic than a few people born into their position lording over others without their consent? The Nobles are not the friends of their Serfs, for just\nlike the Merchants their interests lie against that of society. The Nobles exist to expand and gain monentary and political power, whereas the Serfs seek to survive with the Nobles stealing as little of their produce as possible. In a Democratic Society,\nthere are no Nobles just as there are no Merchants or Politicians. Every one of them is subject to their own interests instead of that of Society, and thus fall into conflict with the masses. If the people were to organize themselves in the most\nDemocratic ways possible, these groups of people would no longer be allowed to do what they do. It would be a land of people ruled by the people, for the people, and of the people.\nPeople's Rule\n\nSo, what does it mean for a society to have the culture of Democracy in it? Does that mean everything from the baker's baking to the seamstress' sewing are to be held to society's whims? Of course not, for that is absurd. Democracy\nis not when the common man has to obey what everyone else says just as harshly as he had obeyed the old authorities. Democracy is when all of these people, realizing their common goals and dreams, band together to decide all that will happen with their\ncombined efforts. Thus, I'd say the greatest example of a truly Democratic Society would be that of the halfling village, for there the people produce and care for each other as they would family, and their governance does not come from any politicians,\nbut from fellow inhabitants and workers who can hardly be told apart from the common halfling at a first glance.\nPeople's Government\n\nIn a society where all these are true, where every man and woman cares for the other like family, and where people gather together to collaborate and decide what is to be done, the concept of Government would be quite foreign, yet\nfamiliar all the same. For those who follow Democracy, Government is not something that is seperate from the people, but rather it is made of the people and serves in their direct interest. If ever the Government stops following the wills of the people,\nit's rules and laws are swiftly done away with and replaced by something that DOES represent the people. Governance does not have to be a foreign thing that lords over from above. It can be different. We all can be different. The economy can be different.\nIt is time for the Democratic Revolution.", ""),
        StaticBook("Perfect Society", "Madeline Applefoot", "~The perfect society is still possible!\n\nYou there, reading this book. Are you tired? Cold? Hungry? Without the education or skills you need to survive? Part of the reason you do not have what you need is because others are busy exploiting on your work!\nThey do not work themselves, usually. Yet by disguising their robberies of people's labor by calling it \"Mercantilism\" and \"Profit\", they are able to take the fruits of society's labor and give it all to themselves! By removing these people from their\nposition, we can help society reach a better, more communal, and more humane existance. Everything should be shared in a community storage and distribution centers, with the goods and services people provide being sent out back to the people, without some\nevil middle man who makes people fork over unnatural and immoral coinage! By being collective, people who need food will always get food, and all will be able to live peacefully and happily. This society would not be led by a monarch who's just as greedy\nas the merchants, but rather led by talented individuals who are elected by the people based on merit instead of inheritance. By doing all these things, you may be living in a perfect society sooner than you think!", ""),
        StaticBook("Permissions", "Valencia Mournstone", "I, BronCloch, give permission for SixShooterHyena, Keening, and Xalid to have rights to anything in Nordengrad. This includes chests and doors. If they are not added, this is my permission for them to be added. Please admin them on all doors and chests if\nthey are not already done. Thank you.\n\n- BronCloch", ""),
        StaticBook("Personal Records", "Artorius Thannius", "The Governance of Rivel\nThe city-state of Rivel has been described by foreigners as a “mageocracy.” Such a term is clearly tautological: it is an obvious fact to all citizens of Rivel that skill in the arcane is a requisite for the\nforesight required for governance: however could one control and lead a populace without the wisdom of the arcane and the insight into the thoughts and actions of the populace offered by mental magics and scrying? Therefore Rivel is organised\ninto a two-tier society: the enlightened citizens such as ourselves skilled in the arcane are led by the Circle of Governance and its leader, the Spellcrafter Archon. The Enlightened Caste of Spellcrafters can be identified by our\npointed ears. The round eared, magically blind and short-lived Lesser Caste of Labourers act as workers and servants. They own property and instead live in worker quarters in the homes of their Enlightened betters. Obviously we do\nnot allow them to take positions of authority and responsibility as their magical blindness means they would do a poor job indeed: better to not allow them to try. Those who display some magical skill are given directorial positions over the rest of\nthe Lesser Caste, but they do not live long enough to achieve the true arcane mastery required to join the ranks of the Enlightened.\nThe city also makes use of semi-intelligent pack animals known as orcs, which once broken can be\ntaught basic speech and simple tasks. They are incredibly useful as beasts of burden but despite the word of what are clearly rumours and the work of agitators are not capable of higher thought any more than cattle are.\n- Artorius Thannius, Exalted Spellcrafter Archon of Rivel", ""),
        StaticBook("Pertaining: Kaz", "Axel Swale", "Dear Erin,\n\n I know the ending of our engagement must be hard for you. I do not mean to hurt you more by leaving a letter. Instead, this letter is about your neighbor, Kaz. \n I travelled to Halsworthy to pick up the ring and cancel the order. But when I\nwent to Kaz's shop the door was unlocked and the place was trashed.  The place seemed intentionally ruined and There was no sign of Kaz. I fear for his life now. I believe he has been taken by some group and I request your help. I need all the information\nyou have on Kaz, for instance, his full name.  I hope that you can help me work towards finding and saving Kaz.  I'll inform the guard and work from there, alongside your information.                                  Signed,                Axel Swale", ""),
        StaticBook("Petrusian Men", "Cederick de Bolbec", "Orenian Support\n\n[!] The Wax seal of House de Bolbec is Stamped at the top of the document.\n\n\"It is on this day, that I shall speak to my allies, as follows in Petrusia.\"\nList of Formal Allies.\n\nProfound Clergyman.\nFarmer A.\nAymas A.\nAymas B.\nAymas C.\nAymas D.\nAymas E.\nAymas F.\nAymas G.\nGuard A.\nGuard B.\nGuard C.\nGuard D.\nOffical A.\nOffical B.\nOffical C.\nMerchant of Status A.\nTavarn A.\n\nFamily FAO\nFamily Gre\nFamily Vis\n-His Grace,\n\nCederick Augustus William Hanover Gregory de Bolbec Winchester", ""),
        StaticBook("Philemon", "Arabella Longford", "The book of Philemon,\nNIV\nPaul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy our brother,\nTo Philemon our dear friend and fellow worker,\nalso to Apphia our sister and Archippus our fellow soldier—and to the church that meets in your home:\nGrace and peace to you from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nThanksgiving and Prayer\n\nI always thank my God as I remember you in my prayers,\nbecause I hear about your love for all his holy people and your\nfaith in the Lord Jesus. I pray that your partnership with us in the faith may be effective in deepening your understanding of every good thing we share for the sake of Christ.\nYour love has given me great joy and encouragement, because you, brother,\nhave refreshed the hearts of the Lord’s people.\n\nPaul's Plea for Onesimus\n\nTherefore, although in Christ I could be bold and order you to do what you ought to do, yet I prefer to appeal to you on the basis of love. It is as none\nother than Paul,\nan old man and now also a prisoner of Christ Jesus,\nthat I appeal to you for my son Onesimus,\nwho became my son while I was in chains.\nFormerly he was useless to you, but now he has become useful both to you and to me.\nI am sending him, who is my very heart, back to you. I would have liked to keep him with me so that he could take your place in helping me while I am in chains for the gospel.\nBut I did not want to do anything without your consent, \nso that any favor you\ndo would not seem forced but would be voluntary.\nPerhaps the reason he was separated from you for a little while was that you might have him back forever,\nno longer as a slave, but better than a slave, as a dear brother.\nHe is very dear to me\nbut even dearer to you,\nboth as fellow man and as a brother in the Lord.\nSo if you consider me a partner, welcome him as you would welcome me. If he has done you any wrong or owes you anything, charge it to me. I, Paul, am writing this with my own hand.\nI will pay it back, \nnot to mention that you owe me your very self. I do wish, brother, that I may have some benefit from you in the Lord; refresh my heart in Christ. Confident of your obedience, I write to you, knowing that you will do even more than-\nI ask.\n\nAnd one thing more: Prepare a guest room for me, because I hope to be restored to you in answer to your prayers.\n\nEpaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus, sends you greetings. And so do Mark, Aristachus,\nDemas and Luke, my fellow workers.\n\nThe grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pico Rules", "Jaya Jayamen", "Pico Rules\nThis is a 2 player game. The game is composed of 11 cards. These are numbered 4 through 13 plus 16 and each also bears a number of stars. (From one on the 4 card to four on the 16 card.) A round is played as follows:\neach player is dealt 5 cards with the eleventh card shown to both of you. (Note that this means that you know exactly what cards your opponent holds.) (( I recommend using /roll (number) to randomly pick the cards being dealt.\nAs to which cards are which number when rolling, Just have the cards next to each other in your inventory and go in the order top to bottom, left to right, the top left card being 1 and the next one in the order being 2 and so forth.\nStart with /roll 11 to pick the first card from your inventory to be dealt, then /roll 10 to choose the second and so on.)) Each turn both you and your opponent secretly select one of their respective cards and reveal them simultaneously.\n((perhaps use PvP timer as a countdown for this.)) The higher card wins and that player sets that card aside scoring the number of stars on it. The loser retains his/her played card.\nHowever, if the higher card is more than twice the lower card then it's the lower card that is placed aside and scored. So the 13 will beat the 10 but lose to the 6. Play continues until one player is left with but a single card.\nPoints are then scored and players swap hands and do it again. This ensures that each game is exactly fair as both players will have equal opportunity to play a strong/weak hand. After these two rounds the points are totaled and a winner declared.\nThere are, at most, fourteen card plays in a game and so the game is usually over in two or three minutes. (( just in case it comes up, the two red stars by “Player Approved” do not count when scoring cards. ))", ""),
        StaticBook("Pirate Insults", "Nemir", "How to\n          Insult\n           Like a\n             Pirate\n-------------------          Written by:\n   Nemir Valnelis Ward\nSo you want to talk like a pirate?  Well you've found the right book!  After reading this, you'll surely knock your enemies down wish just your tongue.\nStarter words:\n- Idiotic\n- Parrot-loving\n- Mumbling\n - Blathering\n- Bleating\n- Sheep-brained\n- Pin-headed\n- Pig-breathed\n- Cricket-sized\nMiddle words:\n- Seaweed-slurping\n- Duck-billed\n- Pus-faced\n- Toothless\n- Knuckle-dragging\n- Cross-eyed\n- Scurvy-ridden\n- Clam-tongued\n- Yellow-bellied\nLast words:\n- Whale-fart\n- Cow-pie\n- Sack o’maggots\n- Cabin boy\n- Swabber\n- Waste of skin\n- Bag of vomit\n- Anchor-head\n- Piece of filth", ""),
        StaticBook("Plague's Bane", "Glacio Whitewolf", "Glacio's Plague Research:\n\nHow to Make:\nMasks:\n7 leather as pants\nTippens Root @ Base\nHow to Make:\nCure:\nBonemeal\nCarrots\nFlesh\nTippens Root\nGold Carrots\nFeathers\nMilk\nEggy\nIdeas for cure:\nInfection against itself\nOrdinary medicines with\nnew\nPlague Book End", ""),
        StaticBook("Play Orc", "BuubHozh Hefner", "PlayOrc\n\n\n*The cover page features a caricature of a bunny with rows and rows of teeth*\nZlutz Uv De Munth\n\n*The following ten pages feature women with disfiguring scars in extremely high quality armor.*\nPruper Tuzk Care\n\n*Listed here is a long and suprisingly informed article about polishing tusks to shine. According to the article, \"Bitchez Luv Shinee Tuzkz\" *\nBuubHozh Hefner\n\n*This article, accompanied by a drawing of an old uruk wrestling two female uruk and a Lur Wolf at once, depicts the life of a fictitious Uruk mating tychoon named BuubHozh Hefner.*\nMojo ztrength, Real ur Fayke?\n\n*Depected in a picture for the article is a battle-scarred, armored elven woman lifting an entire boulder above her head.*\n*The rest of the article is about telekinetics.*\nDe Bear Flattin\n     Ztandurd\n*Below is a drawing of a scarred, scantily clad orc woman snapping a bear's neck with her bare hands. It folds out into 4 pages.*\n\"If Zhe can nub flat a bear wid hur bare handz, zhe iz nub Hozh Mayte!\"\nFlat - Ztickz\n      Agh Lat!\n*The featured weapon this issue seems to be the famous White Rose Arbelest, accompanied by a picture of a scarred human woman firing it.*\n*To one side of it is a column comparing serrated and normal battleaxes.*\nLettur Frum De           Zlutz\n *There's a long (and likely fictional) letter below from a (presumably) female author about how she cant stand an uruk who doesn't smell like the blood of their enemies.*", ""),
