        StaticBook("The Arcane Arts", "Shariana", "-------------------The Arcane Arts\n    By Shariana TWW\n-------------------\nIntroduction.\n\nIn this book, you will get an understanding of the different areas around arcane void magic. This can be useful, if you practice these arts, or fight against an Evocationist, Thaumaturge or Conjurationist.\nChapter 1.\n\n     - Evocation -\n     The Sorcerer\n\nPerhaps the most well known of the four arch-types of magic.  Evocation involves evoking the elements of air, fire, earth, water, and lightning from the void.\n-Sorcerers cannot manipulate pre-existing elements, only summon them.\n\n-Each element is considered one arch-type of magic on its own.\n\n-The elements must follow the same laws as they do in nature.\n-Sorcerers must be ‘attuned’ to their element, spend time around their element, understand its scientific properties, how it feels etc…\n\n-Sorcerer’s must see the area they wish to summon their element in.\n-Sorcerers can only summon their element in its rawest form.\n\n-Hydromancers can also be Cryomancer (Ice Mage).\n\n-Sorcerers must imagine the element in their mind and bend it in their mind before evoking it.\nChapter 2.\n\n      - Alteration -\n    The Thaumaturge\n\nA true science of magic, Alteration involves altering the physical world around the mage.\n* Enchanting *\n-------------------The art of imbuing magical effects upon an object.\n-------------------\n\n-Changes what the item does rather than what it is.\n-Thaumaturge must be touching the item to enchant it.\n\n-The enchantment cannot alter the object physically.\n\n-Enchantments only last as long as they have a mana source.\n-Thaumaturges must imagine the object they see, in great detail, with the enchantment on it in their mind.\n* Transfiguration *\n-------------------The art of changing an object’s appearance.\n-------------------\n\n-Objects must keep their original mass.\n-Objects can only change their state, not their chemical makeup. (Ie. Ice to water, not water to wine.)\n\n-Thaumaturge must be touching and see  the object to transfigure it.\n-Thaumaturges must imagine the object altering,exactly how they want it, in their mind and the steps it would need to go through for the transfiguration to be possible.\n* Abjuration *\n-------------------The art of protection.\n-------------------\n\n-Wards are barriers of mana which cancel out other, aggressive mana.\n-Wards only block spells which have less mana than the ward was made with.\n\n-Wards only last so long as they have a mana source.\n-The Thaumaturge must imagine their mana being released from their body and forming a barrier around them, in the shape they desire.\nChapter 3.\n\n   - Conjuration -\n    The Conjurer\n\nA highly complicated form of magic, Conjuration involves summoning organic beings from the Void.\n-Beings can only remain in existence so long as they have a mana source which would keep them alive in reality.\n-Conjurer’s need to understand the insides and outs, literally, of the creature they wish to summon lest they get something terribly wrong.\n-Primordial Conjurers can summon elemental beings, though they must have an understand of at least one evocation element.\n-Primordial Conjurers do not need to understand the biological structure of their being, only use their mana,  and an understanding of basic motor skills in creatures.\n-Conjurers must see where they wish to summon their creature.\nChapter 4.\n\n       - Illusion -\n      The Magician\n\nA dark form of magic which involves using mana to alter one’s senses.\n-Magicians can only alter the senses\n\n-Anything a magician does is not actually there, no matter how real it feels, looks, smells etc…\n\n-Magicians cannot read minds….or directly change one’s emotion.\n-This is often known as glamour magic.\n\n-A Magician must have experience with their illusion in real life. (ie. To make someone feel as if they are giving birth, the magician would have to understand what giving birth feels like)\n-Illusions only remain in existence so long as they have a source of mana.\n\n-Magician must be able to see where they wish to produce their illusion.\n-The Magician must (smell, taste, hear, touch etc…) the object before they can produce it, with accuracy.  This is why the Magician must have experienced the sense they wish to alter in reality.\nThe End\n         ~ ~ ~ ~", ""),
        StaticBook("The Arcane Types", "Sarrion Zytiaear", "The Arcane Schools\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nA document from the desk of the First Enchanter, made for those who wish to delve into the Arcane Gift.\n\nBy Sarrion Zytiaear\nThe Arcane Gift is broken down into three different types of schools, each one specialized in it’s own feats of magic.\n\nThe School of Evocation\n\nThe School of Alteration\n\nThe School of Illusion\nThe School of Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nEvocation is one of the easier schools of magic to learn and master due to it’s nature of learning off elementals and learning how to conjour such elements in the void.\nThe school breaks down as the following......\nConjuration\n\nArcane Evocation\n\nAir Evocation\n\nWater Evocation\n\nEarth Evocation\n\nFire Evocation\n\nLightning Evocation\nEach one with it’s own respective element and standards to it, the elemental evocations are fit for those new to the arcane gift, and a great field to expand one's potential.\nLightning Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of summoning the volatile elemental of lightning to one's command, slow to cast and hard hitting, this is a dangerous feat of voidal magic and is to be pursued with care.\nFire Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of summoning the powerful flame, a pyromancer allowing the kindling of fire to spring forth in the void and wield the element to his or her command, this is a volatile magic, and is to be pursued with care.\nEarth Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of summoning the strong earth, a Geomancer studying the minerals around him to create such in the void and draw it forth into useful arts such as defensive walls or projectile rocks.\nWater Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of summoning the calming waters, such a mage studies the elegance of water and summons such from the void to do his or hers bidding, later in the studies one may turn such into dangerous ice.\nAir Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of summoning the illusive wind, the Auromancer spends years studying wind itself, being a harder one to master due to no visual cues for the element, one must learn the element from feeling, meditating in windy areas.\nArcane Evocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe first ‘evocation’ of it’s kind, one of the oldest magics alive the Arcanist studies the element of pure voidal energies, allowing him or her to meld such energies to their command.\nConjuration\n-=-=-=-=-=-\nsometimes called ‘Life Evocation’, as the Conjurist learns how to summon creatures and life from the void itself to do his or her bidding. Being one of the slower paced magics to learn due to it’s intense studies of life.\nThe School of Alteration\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-The School of alteration is a long lived study of altering the world to the mages command, from lifting objects with the mind to changing the very world around us.\nThe school breaks down as the following:\n\n \nTransfiguration\n\nTelekinesis\n\nVoidal Translocation\n\nVoidal Shifting\nVoidal Shifting\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of allowing the user to use the void as a door, blinking from their current position to the next desired position. Such an art takes many years to finite.\nVoidal Translocation\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-the art of the mage opening a rift into the void, allowing them to store and retrieve items of their wish, though it may seem simple in definition this art has proven to be extremely complex.\nTelekinesis\n-=-=-=-=-\none of the more simpler alterations, being the art of lifting objects of the world around the mage with voidal magic. Training the art proves fruitful, allowing the caster to lift extremely heavy objects or make objects projectiles.\nTransfiguration\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nThe art breaks down into three sub schools, Transmutation, Enchanting, and Warding. Transfiguration is, in simple terms, the ability to alter the world around us from the mundane to magic itself.\nThe School of Illusion\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-The school of Illusion is commonly referred as an underhanded school, being use for trickery and foolery. Such a school is a study of the mind, emotions, senses of others and how to alter reality to such.\nThis school breaks down as the following:\n\n \nMental Magic\n\nSensory Illusion\nSensory Illusion\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of altering the sensors of the mind of others, allowing one to cast Illusionary feats unknown to those who are under the spell. Altering the look of an object or making somthing that really isn't there etc.\nMental Magic\n-=-=-=-=-=-\nthe art of understanding the mind, and delving into such. Commonly considered an underhanded magic as it infringes the privacy of others, this art can allow its user to delve for memories and alter them. etc.\nFirst Enchanter of the Enchantry,\nSarrion Zytiaear\n3rd of the Deep Cold, 1584\nDisclaimer: These are not literal schools, they're just officiated branches of magic. If you think it's a literal school/schools then you need to check your self. Magic colleges are silly.\n-Sarrion", ""),
        StaticBook("The Arcane, V1", "Meldur the Blue", "______\n\n     Understanding\n       the Arcane\n          ______\n\n     Published 1472\n         Volume I\n\n\n\n\n  By Meldur the Blue\nChapter 1\n\n    Like in all fields of study, for one to adequately learn and retain knowledge of the Arcane arts, he or she must first understand the origin of what is widely known as magic. If a student were to attempt to do so without proper\nfoundation, there is a high chance that he or she would be unsuccessful, and, even if he or she was successful, there is a great risk that the knowledge gained would be used to inappropriate ends. Just as a politician would study history so as not to make\nthe same mistake twice, so must an Arcanist. \n    And so this is what the earliest parts of this book will aim to cover, namely where different forms of magic come from, how it has evolved over the many years, and important figures of study whos works and\ndiscoveries will prove beneficial to the process of understanding the Arcane.\nChapter 2\n\n    All forms of \"magic\" can be divided into two main categories, the first being Void Magic, and the second being Diety Magic, each named after their respective sources. The first of these two categories is the one which we will focus\non at the moment.\n    As mentioned previously, all Void Magic comes from the Void. The Void is the term for an endless expanse of nothingness, not physically of this world, that contains everything that has ever existed or been sensed in the material\nworld. In other words, nothing exists in the Void except for endless potential. At times, a being may attempt to make contact with the Void. If the attempt is successful, he or she may draw potential from the Void and use it in the material world. This\npotential can come in nearly any form. However, the being who attempts to draw it must first be very familiar with the form they wish to it to take. This difference in forms of potential gives us the different fields and sub-sections of Void Magic.\nNow because technically, any being has the ability to draw potential from the Void, any being also has the ability to practice Void Magic. This makes Void Magic the easier of the two main categories to learn. However, it can be difficult to establish\n╔══════════╗\n║\n║\n║   **************      ║  A diagram of the\n║    five elements\n║ is depicted on this\n║          page\n║   **************\n║\n║\n║\n╚══════════╝\n╔══════════╗\n║\n║\n║   **************\n║   A table of tips\n║ for connecting to\n║     the Void is\n║ shown on the page\n║   **************\n║\n║\n║\n╚══════════╝\na connection the the Void, and even more difficult to maintain that connection while drawing potential from it. First, the user must abandon his or her mental perception of reality, and instead open it up to the endless possibility of a limitless nothing.\nAs our minds are finite, it is impossible to grasp something infinite. Because of this, it is only possible to access certain parts of the Void, and not its entirety.\n    Connecting to the void is an exhausting and daunting task, and can take many\nattempts, especially on one's first experience. In order to establish and maintain contact with the Void, one must use the mystical energy which fills our bodies and world known as Mana. Just as performing physical tasks costs physical energy, so does\nperforming magical tasks cost magical energy. When Mana is depleted, or when the user is distracted, the connection to the Void is broken and any potential taken from the Void returns to it.\n    This brings us to the two governing laws of all Void Magic.\nThe first is called the Law of Conservation of Reality. It states that anything performed by magical means takes up the same amount of energy as if it were performed by non-magical means. In other words, there is no endless supply of energy for magic. The\nsecond law is called the Law of Creation, and states that something cannot be created from nothing. This is to say that all magical feats require an equal sacrifice to perform, usually in the form of Mana. It also states that all Void Magic comes from the\nVoid and must return to it. Now this second law is governed by what is called the Evaglno Effect, which says that just as seemingly super-physical feats can be performed at times through the help of adrenaline, so can super-magical feats be performed, but\nat an equally taxing and detimental cost.\n    Now there are four main Archetypes of Void Magic, used to more easily classify the fields themselves. They are Alteration, Mental Manipulation, Evocation, and the Dark Arts. This book will not go in depth on\neach individual field of Void Magic, but it will provide a brief description of each Archetype.\n    Alteration is the most difficult of the four Archetypes to learn. It involves using the Void to shape and alter objects that already exist in the physical\nworld. Subtypes include Transfiguration, Telekenesis, Translocation, Household Magic, and the Whimsical Magics.\n    Mental Manipulation involves using thoughts, experiences, feelings, and images from the Void in order to affect the user's\nmind or the mind of another. It is a very dangerous form of magic, although it cannot physically injure someone directly. The subtypes are Illusion, Mental Magic, and Cognitism.\n    Evocation is the broadest of all Void Magics, as well as the easiest to\nlearn. Through Evocation, a magic user can \"evoke\" potential from the Void and manifest it in a physical way as elements, objects, or living things. Subtypes of Evocation are the Five Elements, Arcane Evocation, Arcane Shielding, and Conjuration.\nThe last Archetype of Void Magic is without a doubt the most evil and dangerous. The Dark Arts are manifestations of pure evil, and serve no purpose other than the corruption of the world and the harm of others. They are outlawed by most Nations, and\ntheir use will yield dire consequences. Subtypes of the Dark Arts include Contract Magic and Okar, Necromancy, Shade Magic, Soul Puppetry, and Fi'hiiran'tanya, also known as Magic Blocking.\nChapter 3\n\n    As even the simplest of creatures is aware, our world is governed by beings of higher power known as Aenguls and Daemons. They are still ruled by the mightiest of all beings, the Creator, who brought everything into\nfruition out of nothing, including magic and the Void.\n    These Aenguls and Daemons are constantly seeking to interact with the physical world in some way, and because of this, many of them offer supernatural powers to otherwise mortal beings. We call\nthese powers Diety Magic. Diety Magic is more difficult to obtain than Void Magic, because one must be granted it by the Spiritual Entity itself, though control can be taught by other mortals with the same blessing. \n    All dieties expect something of\ntheir followers, and so not fulfilling these obligations results in what is known as an unattunement from the diety. This is known as the Law of Servitude, and states that a diety may at any time grant or take away magical abilities at a whim. This is not\nto say that it happens often though, and usually requires some sort of grave act of disobedience.\n    Now because of the first two laws of magic, we must assume that even Diety Magic requires some form of energy or sacrifice to perform. However, in this\nspecific instance, it does not only tax the user but the spirit as well. The magic used feeds off of the connection that the user has made with his or her mystical patron, and so when that connection is used up, no more magic can be performed for a period\nof time.\n    Due to their shared belief in the same or similar Dieties, many users of Diety Magic band together to form Guilds, Orders, and even Cults dedicated to the study and responsible use of their respective field of magic. Popular examples of such\ngroups include the Druidic Order, followers of the Aspects Ceridwen and Cernunos, the Ascended, servants of the Aengul Ariel, and those who practice Muun'Trivazja, Kharajyr Priests of the Daemon Metztli.\n    Now those Dieties which grant magical\nabilities often wish to establish a strong sense of contingency when it comes to their followers. As a result, Aenguls and Daemons alike have been known to strip magical abilities from their followers who attempt to practice other forms of magic. A common\nexample of this would be the Aspects \"unattuning\" a Druid if he or she were to establish a connection with the void. It is because of this nurtured sense of contingency that many practicioners of Diety Magic have harbored a mistrust and dislike of\npractitioners of Void Magic, and vice versa.      Now because there are a great deal of Aenguls and Daemons willing to bestow power upon mortal servants, the number of Archtypes used to categorize the many different fields of Diety Magic is far higher\nthan that of Void Magic. They include Druidism, Shamanism, Rune Smithing, Clerical magic, Monk magic, Ascended magic, Undead magic, Scourge magic, and Muun'Trivazja. Although there are more Archtypes, the Archtypes themselves are less internally diverse,\nin other words, they each hold far fewer fields.\nChapter 4\n\n    Now as we learned earlier, the use of magic still requires the expenditure of energy, whether it be through Mana or the connection with a Diety. However, most mortal bodies are not at all used to using these particular forms of\nenergy, and as such it can take a more costly toll on the user's physical body. This phenomenon is known as Corruption. The fourth law of magic, the Law of Corruption, therefore states that the more energy one puts into developing Arcane ability, the less\nis available for non-magical tasks. Therefore, in theory, an entirely magical being such as a spirit could not take on a real, physical form, except for through magical means.\nChapter 5\n\n    Just as with all things, magic was brought into existence by the Creator at the beginning of time. At first, magic was a very, very rare phenomenon. Not many people had access to it, and even fewer understood it. Only\na fraction of the currently known fields of magic were know during the first hundred or so years of recorded history, but great efforts were made to discover more.\n    Now as time progressed, mortals began to further explore this expanse of nothingness\nknown as the Void. Great wizards arose, and new fields of magic were found. The Arcane Arts were born as an object of study, and more and more people began to understand, study, and practice.\n    It was not at any specific time that the Aenguls and\nDaemons began to meddle with the affairs of Men, they did. As their involvement grew, so did the power and number of their followers, thus leading to the earliest Diety Magic Guilds being formed. These Guilds and their Void Magic counterparts yielded some\nof the mightiest, wisest, and maddest wizards the world has ever come to know.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Art of Ale", "Petyr Brandybuck", "Not many can boast to brew as fine as I, Petyr of AlKhazar. I have decided to write a book revealing some of my craft. I did not start out a fabulous brewer, no. The art of ale takes in-field experience to master. First off, I will describe our\ningredients. We do use the typical ale ingredients, for a start. However, the Cat and the Fiddle Ale has some special parts. For instance, we add yellow wildflowers to our brew. Too many of these causes hallucinations, which is illegal. We use a legal\nlimit that still causes some airy-headed pleasure. We have tried mushrooms in the past, but the taste was never satisfactory. Sometimes we add some cocoa to our special drafts for flavor. Second, I will describe the process our ale undertakes.\nThe fermenting container is of extreme importance. I have tried all sorts of materials to use. Stone containers give a good flavor, but it is not rich enough. Birch has an awkward aftertaste, most say. We've even tried iron and gold containers!\nIt tasted nasty.\n\nThe secret, I've found, is Darkwood. This rare component provides a rich flavor, and feels very full and hearty. It accents the flavors we add. How long we brew for, I cannot say. I can't reveal everything!\nThe third step is filteration. We use fine wool filters to do this process. The maturation is what helps provide potency and feeling. The time of day you brew and the location are two key steps. After all of these long steps,\nthe great results are obvious! Our beer is acclaimed across the land, even by dwarven taverns! I know you will enjoy it if you try. Present this book to me, and I will give you a 10m discount on your first beer!\nThank you for reading, I hope to see you soon! - Brewer Petyr, of the Cat and the Fiddle.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Art of War I", "Evie Avern", "         I. Plans\n\n1. The art of war is of vital importance to the state.\n\n2. It is a matter of life or death, a road to either safety or to ruin. Hence it is a subjectof inquiry which can on no account be neglected.\n3. The art of war, then, is governed by five constant factors, to be taken into account in one's deliberations, when seeking to determine the conditions obtaining in the field. \n\n4. These are: (1) The Moral Law; (2) Heaven; (3) Earth; (4) The Commander;\n(5) Method and discipline. \n\n5,6. The Moral Law causes the people to be in complete accord with their ruler, so that they will follow him regardless of their lives, undismayed by any danger.\n\n7. Heavan signifies night and day, cold and heat, times and seasons.\n\n8. Earth comprises distances, great and small; danger and security; open ground and narrow passes; the chances of life and death.\n9. The Commander stands for the virtues of wisdom, sincerely, benevolence, courage and strickness.\n\n10. By method and discipline are to be understood the marshaling of the army in its proper subdivisions, the graduations of rank among officers, the\nmaintenance of roads by which supplies may reach the army, and the control of military expenditure.\n\n11. Therefore, in your deliberations, when seeking to determine the military conditions, let them be made w=tge basis of a comparison in this wise:--\n13. (1) Which of the two sovereigns is imbued with the Moral Law? (2) Which of the two generals has the most ability? (3) With whom lie the advantages derived from Heaven and Earth? (4) On which side is discipline most rigorously enforced?\n(5) Which army is stronger? (6) On which side are officers and men more highly trained? (7) In which army is there the greater constancy both in reward and punishment?\n\n14. By means of tose seven considerations I can forecast victory or defeat.\n15. The general that hearkens to my counsel and acts upon it, will conquer; let such a one be retained in command! The generals that hearkens not to my counsel nor acts upon it, will suffer defeat:-- let such a one be dismissed!\n16. While heading the profit of my counsel, avail yourself also of any helpful circumstances over and beyond the ordinary rules.\n\n17. According as circumstances are favorable, one should modify one's plans.\n18. All warfare is based on deception. \n\n19. Hence, when able to attack, we must seem unablel; When using our forces, we must seem inactive; when we are near, we must make the enemy believe we are far away; when far away we must make him believe we\nare near.\n\n20. Hold out baits to entice the enemy. Feign disorder, and crush him.\n\n21. If he is secure at all points, be prepared for him. If he is in superior strength, evade him.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Aspects", "The Owl Druid", "~*~Aspectism~*~\n\nScribed by the Owl Druid.\n\nCreated by the Hawk Druid.\n\n\n~*~The Mother~*~\n         and\n~*~The Father~*~\n~*~The Mother~*~\n\nAlso known as the Green Lady, or Cerridwen-The Mother is the matriarchal side of the balance, depicted as a woman wreathed in leaves and vines, shimmering surreally in many different shapes of nature. Cerridwen is the Aspect of -\ngrowth, fertility, life, and nurturement. \n\nWood elves believe that all trees, grass, and other verdant flora and fauna are born and grow under Cerridwen's nurturing touch, living until Cernunnos claims them in death. The green lady is prayed to for good-\nharvest and when growing the massive elderwood trees in which the elves make their homes.\n~*~The Father~*~\n\nAlso known as the Horned Man, or Cernunnos-The Father. The patriarchal side of the natural balance. He is often depicted as a strongely built man with the horns of a stag and a longbow in hand. Cernunnos is the Aspect of the Hunt-\n,of predators, of chaos, and of death.\n\nWood elves believe that all animals have a deep connection to Cernunnos, and often prey to the horned man for luck when hunting, be it animals or sentient prey. While Cernunnos depicts of death, this is not seen as-\nsinister. For all things must die, and it is simply part of the cycle of nature.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Azdrazi", "Davin Lodmund", "The Azdrazi\nHaelun'or,\nAxios\nPublished 1592\nby Icarus Adil\nThe Azdrazi\n================The Azdrazi are an odd creature, draconic in nature, which have recently returned to some prominence in the land of Axios. Upon their head are long, brown horns, and their skin is unmistakably scaly. Their eyes are ora\nand serpentine, their pupils slitted, long and thin. They can retract these fearsome features to a certain extent, though not all choose to. At a glance, the Azdrazi are monsters, and will likely strike fear in the hearts of those who see them.\nFortunately, this is false.\nIn this work, it is my hope to spread understanding regarding the Azdrazi, for these misgivings not only pose undue risks to their well-being, yet also hamper them in pursuit of their noble task.\nThe Azdrazi should not be regarded as monstrosities of evil, or as ne'er do wells of any sort. Instead, they should be treated as neutral creatures pursuant of a righteous task, as I shall explain below.\nTo begin, one must look to the history of the Azdrazi, for their existence predates most of our own. The creatures first emerged during the Great War, borne with the sole intent of stopping the darkness of the Betrayer Iblees.\nMuch of their history has been lost to time or otherwise destroyed,\nbut it is known that the current iteration of Azdrazi stem from the line formed by Azdromoth, prior to his present affliction.\nThe current brood of Azdrazi began with the stirring of one known as Alistair, the child of Azdromoth, and father of the remaining Azdrazi, at least as far\nas is currently known. He returned and began to create other Azdrazi, though the method of doing so is still unknown. Unfortunately, it was not until after this began that they learnt of Azdromoth’s descent\nto existence as a Drakaar, a fate most Dragaar\nescape by confining themselves to stone.\nWith this knowledge, the Azdrazi have severed ties with their progenitor, instead pursuing the goals their very existence is predicated upon.\nThey exist to protect man against the forces of darkness, and\nhope to one day find a cure for the blight which affects their father and grandfather, Azdromoth the Arch-Drakaar.\nThough these creatures have found conflict against the Paladins of Xan, as is natural giving their order's aversion to the dragonkin,\nwould seem a truce may be found. The disciples in the Cult of Azdromoth also combat the Azdrazi, as is natural for those who worship the Arch-Drakaar in its present form. Yet beyond this, the Azdrazi hold no conflict with man, and should indeed be\ntreated. in accordance to their intentions: to protect man against the dark. In conclusion, I encourage all who find themselves in the presence of Azdrazi not only to stay any hostility based on assumptions of their aesthetic, yet also to thank them for\ntheir service to the realm of mortality. It seems that we all owe them our appreciation, and they are yet to find it.\nNotes\n===================\nThe information for this piece was gathered through the author's interview of Mister Suldaeroth and Mister Favnir, two Azdrazi who freely gave information regarding\ntheir existence. Their support of\nintellectual pursuit will not go unforgotten, so long as the information they have offered does not prove false. If you see them, thank them for their service both through this tome, yet also against\nthose who promote\ndarkness in our realm.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Azdrazi", "Icarus Adil", "The Azdrazi\n\n        Haelun'or,\n           Axios\n\n     Published 1592\n\n\n\n     by Icarus Adil\nThe Azdrazi\n===================   The Azdrazi are an odd creature, draconic in nature, which have recently returned to some prominence in the land of Axios. Upon their head are long, brown horns, and their skin is unmistakably scaly. Their eyes are orange\nand serpentine, their pupils slitted, long and thin. They can retract these fearsome features to a certain extent, though not all choose to. At a glance, the Azdrazi are monsters, and will likely strike fear in the hearts of those who see them.\nFortunately, this is false.\n   In this work, it is my hope to spread understanding regarding the Azdrazi, for these misgivings not only pose undue risks to their well-being, yet also hamper them in pursuit of their noble task. The Azdrazi should not be\nregarded as monstrosities of evil, or as ne'er do wells of any sort. Instead, they should be treated as neutral creatures pursuant of a righteous task, as I shall explain below.\n   To begin, one must look to the history of the Azdrazi, for their existence\npredates most of our own. The creatures first emerged during the Great War, borne with the sole intent of stopping the darkness of the Betrayer Iblees. Much of their history has been lost to time or otherwise destroyed, but it is known that the current\niteration of Azdrazi stem from the line formed by Azdromoth, prior to his present affliction.\n   The current brood of Azdrazi began with the stirring of one known as Alistair, the child of Azdromoth, and father of the remaining Azdrazi, at least as far\nas is currently known. He returned and began to create other Azdrazi, though the method of doing so is still unknown. Unfortunately, it was not until after this began that they learnt of Azdromoth’s descent to existence as a Drakaar, a fate most Dragaar\nescape by confining themselves to stone.\n   With this knowledge, the Azdrazi have severed ties with their progenitor, instead pursuing the goals their very existence is predicated upon. They exist to protect man against the forces of darkness, and\nhope to one day find a cure for the blight which affects their father and grandfather, Azdromoth the Arch-Drakaar.\n   Though these creatures have found conflict against the Paladins of Xan, as is natural giving their order's aversion to the dragonkin, it\nwould seem a truce may be found. The disciples in the Cult of Azdromoth also combat the Azdrazi, as is natural for those who worship the Arch-Drakaar in its present form. Yet beyond this, the Azdrazi hold no conflict with man, and should indeed be treated\nin accordance to their intentions: to protect man against the dark.\n   In conclusion, I encourage all who find themselves in the presence of Azdrazi not only to stay any hostility based on assumptions of their aesthetic, yet also to thank them for their\nservice to the realm of mortality. It seems that we all owe them our appreciation, and they are yet to find it.\nNotes\n===================\n\nThe information for this piece was gathered through the author's interview of Mister Suldaeroth and Mister Favnir, two Azdrazi who freely gave information regarding their existence. Their support of\nintellectual pursuit will not go unforgotten, so long as the information they have offered does not prove false. If you see them, thank them for their service both through this tome, yet also against those who promote darkness in our realm.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Azdrazi", "Mikael", "The Azdrazi\n                -=-\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n           Written By\n        Mikael Lennox\nTable of\n          Contents\n              -x-\n\nPg 3-5. Introduction\nPg 6-9. Orgins\nPg 10-14. Abilities\nPg 15-16. Immaculate Blade\n\nPg 17-18. Dedication & Thanks\nThe Azdrazi are a group of individuals gifted with the soul and blood of dragons. These creatures serve as protectors and guardians to the descendant races. They protect us from the darkness that constantly threatens our world. Their cause is a noble one.\nIn order to become an Azdrazi one must go through perilous trials. There a multiple trials, each with their own unique dangers and puzzles. If the acolyte makes it through these grueling trails they will be allowed to go through something known as\n'The Transference\".  Not much is known about the process of the Transference, however it is known that its extremely painful, and possibly even deadly.\nAzdrazi are the children of Dragaars and Drakaars. Most known Azdrazi are children of the Dragaar Azdromoth. Although he is currently corrupted, making him a Drakaar, he is still their father, and they search tirelessly for a way to heal him.\nThe original Azdrazi were mortals that were rewarded for their faith Dragaar.  However it wasn't long before they suffered greatly. The Dragaar Azdromoth was turned against them, and violence ended many other Azdrazi. Very few remained after this time\nand with their father sealed away, hope was lost. Azdrazi faded away, the age of fire had ended. Until something shook the pillars of creation once more, then came the resurgence of dragonkin following the deeds of Mordring and the freeing of the black\ntitan from his ethereal chains.\nAzdrazi have many useful abilites. However unlike most mages and magic users Azdrazi do not possess a mana pool. When they are created their mana pool is changed, into something known as the 'Inner flame' this acts as the Azdrazi's mana pool. They draw\nfrom it in order to use the abilites their gifted with.  \n\nThe first ability these creatures possess is the ability to hide their draconic appearence. Normally Azdrazi have lizard-esqe eyes, that glow like fire, horns and black scaly skin. Though by will\nthey can revert to a human-esqe appearence. However their eyes will still hold the glow and appearence, its not a perfect transformation. \n\nAzdrazi have an immunity to fire and heat. They can bath within lava, or stand within natural flames.\nThey can summon flames from within themselves, drawing it from their Inner flame. They can light their weaponry and bodies aflame. They can cauterize wounds. They can even breath fire. \nTheir flames are able to deal concussive damage to beings of spectral\nor undead orgins. Though to others it acts like normal flames.\nThe Immaculate Blade\n\nThe Immaculate blade is a title given to an Azdrazi blessed by Azdromoth. The Immaculate blade is the only one capable of creating new Azdrazi, and ending them. The Immaculate blade is often considered the leader of Azdrazi, though\nin recent times, the Immaculate blade isnt always the leader, sometimes they are elders.\nI'd like to thank Suldaeroth for awnsering the questions of a curious scholar. This book wouldn't have been possible without his assistance and guidence. \n\nI'd like to dedicate this book to the Dragon-kin. Thier cause is noble, and the descendants\nare lucky to have them. This book was written in a time when not many saw the truth of Azdrazi, many feared them because they were different. Please keep in mind that they protect us, we've nothing to fear.\n\n-M.L", ""),
        StaticBook("The Bandits Cry", "Ser Adande Williams", "[!] The Book Would be Written in Cursive and Signed at the top right corner of the first page and the rest would be nothing yet articles of text resting on each page written wish passion and steady hand writing.\nThe Slaughter Of The Weak. Written by Ser Adande Williams.\n\nTo Those Whom May Be Reading this, you will read of the memorial day which we showed the strength of whitepeak against the infamous group that goes by \"The Reivers\"\nOn The 14th Of The Grand Harvest The Men of Whitepeak along with some others were heading home as for i had unattended business to handle. On My way home, I Ran into them doing a stand down with the reivers as they were trying to defend an innocent man.\nThe fight was then taking into a wrong toll and the three men of whitepeak along with one man who is an ally and another who was getting robbed then decided to take the primitive action and attack the Flays. They took off using typical flay tactics by\nheading in and out of there two seperate towers that they have. Which is located right next to curon. Eimar Var Burgundar Baited the Weak one of them and Took him on and finished him on his front lawn. It was a pitiful sight to see them crumble as such\nonce the dark elf died we then left him on the floor and went searching across the lands for the rest. With a lack of communication I stepped far out and got taken by one of the dark elves almost not being able to push through then barely getting out and\ntaking a break while regrouping with the group and bandaging up. Once Bandaged up and ready We then Chased around one of there weaker ones, probably easiest of them all was then slain easily by Ser Adande Williams, Myself with the help of an ally at his\nside. I later Then almost killed one at the front gates of there Tower. I Had then stepped back and once he ran trying to use flay tactics, I Fought him on the bridge of Curon And Easily Slain him where he stood leaving his blood splattered on the front\nbridge of Curon\n\nWritten by: Ser Adande Williams", ""),
        StaticBook("The Bardic Rite", "Oliver", "The tasks we set out before our charges are not tests of their character or dedication, they are lessons, first and foremost. The test doesn't come until they have breathed their first breath as a Druid, --\n-- and felt the touch of the world as they realize their new role. Druidism is the test, not the reward, and Dedicancy is the lesson, not the trial.\n\nAnd the first lesson is to open their eyes.\nFor if they are blind, they will trudge along through all the lessons you could give without being able to see what they truly mean. \n\nFor this reason, the bardic rite is no simple talent show. It is not a test. It is no pointless tradition.\nIt may seem like a fun excersize to draw your Dedicant in, but it is an important lesson nonetheless. It is a way to open the Dedicant's mind to the path that lays out before them.\nA way of initiating them into the mindset that will be natural for them when they finally bloom.\n\nPoetry has come to be the staple, yes, but the rite makes no such requiremt. At least, not by my interpretation.\nA poem is simply what most people assume they need to do, and the presumption has infiltrated even the Guides. Creativity is a Druid's greatest asset, and this line of thought inhibits it, both for those who teach it and those they teach.\nA bard is no simple poet. A bard is an artist of many muses. A spoken word, a poem, a melodious tune. Harmony by any means is not the sole medium by which they express themselves. Songs without words, stories without structure.\nBrush, chisel, knife or even fire. Bards are storytellers, keepers of one's culture, and weavers of feeling, not through poetry, or even just tales. At the heart of every bard is art, not simply song and dance, or even a pen.\nPoetry is just one avenue they pursue. Claiming to know unwaveringly that the only true path is down that avenue.\nClaiming that the 'Bardic Rite' itself requires it, limits the Dedicant's ability to truly show their colors, to truly inscribe their personal dedication to nature into their work.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Best Vote", "Ser Philip 'The Valiant'", "I, Ser Philip de Honneur, First of his name, Father of Charles and Juliet de Honneur, cousin of Thomas de Hartcold, defender of Arbor, future warden of the West, The Most Valiant of them all, hereby vote for Ellie Underwood as Voice of the Senate", ""),
        StaticBook("the bible", "David", "i like hairy arab porn", ""),
        StaticBook("THE BIG SADD", "Kairn Irongrinder", "Hello darkness, my old friend\nI've come to talk with you again\nBecause a vision softly creeping\nLeft its seeds while I was sleeping\nAnd the vision that was planted in my brain\nStill remains\nWithin the sound of silence\nIn restless dreams I walked alone\nNarrow streets of cobblestone\n'Neath the halo of a street lamp\nI turned my collar to the cold and damp\nWhen my eyes were stabbed by the flash of a neon light\nThat split the night\nAnd touched the sound of silence\nAnd in the naked light I saw\nTen thousand people, maybe more\nPeople talking without speaking\nPeople hearing without listening\nPeople writing songs that voices never share\nAnd no one dared\nDisturb the sound of silence\n\"Fools\", said I, \"You do not know\nSilence like a cancer grows\nHear my words that I might teach you\nTake my arms that I might reach you\"\nBut my words, like silent raindrops fell\nAnd echoed\nIn the wells of silence\nAnd the people bowed and prayed\nTo the neon god they made\nAnd the sign flashed out its warning\nIn the words that it was forming\nAnd the sign said, \"The words of the prophets are written on the subway walls\nAnd tenement halls\"\nAnd whispered in the sounds of silence\n\n\n\n\n\n-Inspired by Ulhart Grandaxe, who owes me a new horse named Lebron. He killed my last horse named Lebron.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Blood Age", "Kardel Irongut", "___________________THE BLOOD AGE\n___________________WRITTEN BY VALEN GRANDAXE, THE SCRIBE, PARAGON OF OGRADHAD\n___________________Reproduced in Holm by the Publishing House of the Holmic Academy of Arts and Sciences\n___________________\nThe following story is taken from a tome of the Runelords which offers detail to the full extent of 'The Great Collapse' and the true nature of dwarven government before the fourteenth century. By the year 1254,\nin times remembered only by the oldest of dwarves living today, there occurred a pivotal event in dwarven history known as ‘The Great Collapse.’ Emperor Thorgarn Ironborn, the ruler and heir of\nhe Ironborn Dynasty ruled over the Empire of Khorvad with an iron fist, leading a race of dwarves who had slowly become divided by cultural differences. Early in its reign, the Grandaxes and Frostbeards\nmighty dwarves of the icy northern mountain ranges, refused the rule of the Emperor and instead made their own lives as exiles, living in great holds carved from wood and stone.\nEven the forest dwarves refused to live under the rule of cave dwarves and were persecuted and hunted down by their own kin until they faced near annihilation. The Dynasty kept a tight control\nover its inhabitants and had their Empire stretch across the lands from the city of Kal’Urguan to the far reaches of eastern Aegis. Beneath them stood clans who out of either fear or greed would aid the Empire in times of war.\nSociety was cruel and unforgiving with public executions and show trials held regularly for any who posed a possible threat to the Emperor. Any public opposition was stamped out quickly, the only knowledge of times\nbefore the Dynasty being held within ancient scrolls of the Clergy. During the ‘Great Collapse,’ scholars and elders of the dwarves were rounded up and publicly executed\nfor what were claimed to have been heretical writings against the Empire. These scrolls were burnt and all knowledge of past ages lost as an attempt to purge any unwanted information from the Empire.\nIn years prior to the Empire, Runesmithing had been used by the dwarves as a force of good, passed down from generation to generation descending from Urguan himself. Always throughout history, a Triumvirate of Runelords\nhad managed its use, ensuring that it would never be abused for personal gain. Knowing the nature of the Empire, the Triumvirate had gone into hiding, fearing what would happen if the full extent\nof this knowledge was spread. However, the Runelords had already made sure to pass down much of the knowledge to their closest of kin. Dwarves were forced to give up what knowledge they had while those\nwho refused were swiftly executed. Alas, the damage was already done and the Ironborn utilised what little knowledge they had possessed, with devastating effect upon the dwarven populace.\nKin slaying became a common practise, with the experimentation of grotesque and blasphemous flesh runes. The weak were enslaved, under fear of torture if they were to ever attempt escape\nand Thorgarn quickly became known as the cruelest of his line. After the ‘Great Collapse,’ the largest and most destructive civil war in dwarven history ensued. Long had opposition been forming against Thorgarn’s rule\nand so too that of his father and older brother. Rumours had often spread that Thorgarn himself had murdered his own brother in order to leave himself the only true heir to the Dynasty.\nAfter the massacres and dishonourable acts made against their own kin, it wasn’t long before opposition would take action. Far to the north, clans of all creeds and cultures gathered at the summit of Mount Arvas,\nwhere hundreds of years prior, ancient Mountain Dwarves had defeated the immortal, Ondnarch. Here, many elder clans including the Grandaxes, Frostbeads and Ironguts united to form the Remnant of Urguan,\naiming to restore the old ways set out at the start of time by the father of all dwarves, Urguan. At its head was Simmpa, a cave dwarf in exile who had witnessed first hand the crimes of the Ironborn.\nNo longer would dwarves stand idly by as their home was ruled by kin slayers. In time, the horns of war would sound and from the north came a vast alliance of clans, each under a banner of freedom and justice.\nYet they would show no mercy to those who remained loyal to the Empire and hundreds upon thousands of dwarves lost their lives throughout the course of the war, lasting just over thirty years.\nBy that time, the Ironborn were surrounded, barricaded away in the grand halls of Kal’Urguan, knowing only too well what would happen if they were caught trying to escape.\nThey would fight until their last dying breath and so when the city was sieged, it was Simmpa himself who met the Emperor in battle. Before the throne of Urguan, they would duel to the death.\nOf course, it was Simmpa who gained the upper hand and in the end, he who beheaded the Emperor and claimed Kal’Urguan as his own.\nHenceforth, a new Kingdom was forged from the ashes of the old Empire and the name Khorvad shunned from history forevermore.\nDuring the course of the war, many dwarves were familiar with the art of Runesmithing and its abuse was largely to blame for the mass murder of many elders and scholars who had written texts presenting an unfavourable view of the Dynasty.\nIn order to combat this, a select cult of Runesmiths forged a secret society, in order to protect the last remnants of the ancient art. Continuing the old traditions of the Triumvirate, they would pass it down from ancestor to ancestor,\nbound by a blood oath never to use it as a means of furthering personal or clan related goals. Never again would it be the cause of such great destruction as had come during the Blood Age.\n(D) ALL CREDIT FOR THIS WORK GOES TO VALEN GRANDAXE, WHO RECOVERED THESE ANCIENT TEXTS.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Castle...", "Nemir", "It was a normal, quiet day for me.  I was at the Library of Dragur, sitting alone behind the front desk.\n\nMy friend, Tara, rode in on her horse, I believe she named it Pinecone?  Anyways, we greeted each other and I told her\nof the stables we've got for travellers near the entrance to the garden.  She tied up her steed there and returned, asking if we had any books on medicines.\n\nI was in the middle of telling her our variety of books fitting that general section\nwhen an elf walked in.  It was another friend of mine, but I suppose friend is a rather strong word to use since it had been years since I've seen him.  It was Ibar, a rather goofy and clumsy paladin.\n\nI was quite surprised to see him, especially\nat the library.  I had tried asking him where he had been and whatnot before some young man charged into the building, shouting that his friend needed help.  He looked as if he was a resident to Haria due to his robes and the fact that he\nkept thanking \"Allah\" about us being there. He said his friend Chet needed help and that he had gone into some large keep.  The young man seemed to've been in a state of panic and was out of breath so I sorta understood about half of what he said.\nThe three of us decided to help the poor man and Chet so he gave us a key saying that it'd be needed in order to enter the castle.  So the three of us went on, the young man staying behind.\n\nTook us about a half\na day walk or so until we reached the large castle, good thing Tara knew where it was exactly otherwise I got a feeling we would've been wandering around longer.\n\nAs we approached the gigantic keep I felt uneasy.  Something\nabout it seemed off to me, something dark.\n\nIt didn't take too long for us to find a small ferrum door on the side, the key unlocked it and we entered a dark room connected to a hallway lit by a dim candle.  I took the candle so I could at least get\nan idea of where I was going.\n\nThe hallway led into what looked like a cave?  There was another candle which was taken by Ibar.  The small cavern-like tunnel lead upwards, into an overgrown garden.  A harsh wind put out our candles, but we had the\nsun now so it didn't matter too much.  The garen seemed to've been singing with little critters.  It was the only thing which I found calming within the place.  Tara ended up finding another door hidden away, Ibar seemed eager to see what was behind it\nbut I wanted to continue searching around the garden before going through more doors.  Tara agreed after unlocking it, but Ibar was a bit of a piece of shit and bolted through it.  So we just decided to follow, not wanting for us to get split up.\nThe room we entered a few peculiar doors.  Were they doors?  No idea, but that's what I'm calling them.  The metal they were made of had a red tone to it, there was an odd seal on the door as well.  We couldn't get past it so we moved on to this hallway.\nIt lead into a room with a hole in the roof and a pit in the center of the floor.  Something clearly happened here.  Tara and Ibar quickly climbed down into the pit, if one were to look closer there was something at the bottom, laying on the ground.\nThe creature twitched here and there, as if it were alive.  And it was, but barely.\n[!] The notes don't appear to be finished.  The rest of the pages are either empty, filled with frustrated scribbles or hastilly written words, singularly placed such as \"cursed\"; and some sketches may be viewed.  Some are of a grotesque gargoyle\nappearing to have come to life from its stone extistence.  The others appear to be of a tall and aged man with scribbled out eyes and the words, \"Not right - Inhuman - Unsettled\" are written near the head.\nOn the back cover of the book is the name, \"Nemir V. W.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("The Clerics", "Karyssmov Faroe", "The concept of holiness. The grasp of Purity.\nThe Light.\nThe Flame.\nThe Fire.\nThe Stag.\nThe Vehement Lord.\n\nWhat does it mean?\nWhat are these things? How does one better themselves via these?\nThere are many paths of purity, seeker.\nThere are methods and concepts beyond our own grasp, set in forth by the Aengul, Tahariae.\n\nBut who is Tahariae?\nTahariae is the Aengul of Purity. He represents the Light that is both a concept, and a literal thing. Perhaps you have been healed by a Cleric before. Have you felt the warmth and calmness flow through you? That is the Light, made manifest.\nThe Light has many properties to it, but it always comes from the Lord of Light. The Light can heal, purge taint, remove poisons, even remove curses...\n\nBut at the same time, that is not always enough.\nThe comes the Clerics of War, myself being one. War Light is slightly different from the Light used to heal. It is manifested from emotion, thus the more passionate a Cleric becomes, the stronger their spells are as well.\nHatred. Zeal. Vengeance. This can fuel the Light of a War Cleric. But at the same time, some choose not to call upon the more primal of emotions. Instead, they focus upon anger. Sorrow. The thoughts of what would happen, should they fail.\nRegardless of it all, the War Light is still Light in its stature. One may notice it is 'hard' compared to the 'liquid' state of regular Light. This is due to the Cleric, who wills it into this shape to better combat the forces of darkness.\nAll Clerics may be both a 'Priest' Cleric and a 'War' Cleric. There is no need to choose merely one. I am trained in both, using them to their extent to render the Lord's will upon this plane.\n\"But,\" I hear you say.\n\"What is the Lord's will?\"\nWell, my friend, it is simple. It is to eradicate 'impurity'. What is impurity? It is taint. Look towards a ghoul or lich. They are different from you and I. They are not \"Descendants' anymore.\nThey have given their soul for the ruinous powers. How unjust, how folly their decision. They are wrong, and as such, the Lord of Light sends us to right these wrongs. We are the instruments of the Lord's will. And by His will, you are protected.\nClerics have a duty to purge undead and impurity, wherever they should find it. They do it to keep you safe. Not out of desire for violence, not out of desire to subjugate, but simply to keep us safe, and because the Lord demands.\nIt is why Clerics will always heal you. They will preserve you, return you to your normal form. For injury is Impurity, for is it not who you were. It is wrong, and thus the Light grants us the strength necessary to save both your life...and soul.\nWe are bound by what are known as 'Tenants'. These five rules dictate the lives of a Cleric, and they must follow them closely, lest their soul be torn apart and expelled. Yes, big stakes.\n\nWhat are the Tenants?\nLet's find out.\nThe First Tenant\n\n'Shelter and Protect thy Brethren'\n\nTranslated, meaning...\nTo protect fellow Clerics in their time of need.\nThe Second Tenant\n\n'Cannot Stray from thy Faith'\n\nTranslated, meaning...\nDo not strayin dedication to the Lord. Be diligent in prayers and thanks to Him for His Gift to us.\nThe Third Tenant\n\n'Shall Guide the Lost'\n\nTranslated, meaning...\nTo guide the Descendants of the Four, and protect them from the forces of Impurity. (IE: Undeath)\nThe Fourth Tenant\n\n'Shall not Kill, Harm, Nor Spill the Blood of an Innocent'\n\nTranslated, meaning...\nClerics cannot harm a Descendant for no reason.\nThe Fifth and Final Tenant\n\n'Abuse of Thine Gift and Punishment Shall Fall to You'\n\nTranslated, meaning...\nWe cannot avoid healing a Descendant. We must, for the Lord commands.\nThat's a lot of rules!\nIt's true, but they guide the path of the Clerics as they go forth fo fulfill His Holy Mission.\n\n'But, what is that?!'\nWell...let me tell you.\nTahariae's mission upon these realms is to preserve Purity, and the Descendants of the Four. The thing standing in his way of that, is Impurity. The Undead, for instance, are one such Impurity. They are to be expunged, unless they serve the Light.\nAn interesting path of being a Cleric is their Gift. Now, you may not know much about followers of Aenguls, but oftentimes those who follow one do not experience voidal poisoning, therefore we keep our muscle mass. This is because our aengul blesses us.\nI thank you for spending some time reading through this instructional book. Should you have any questions, feel free to ask any nearby Cleric. They'd appreciate any thanks you could give, for our job is  oftentimes a thankless one.\n-Karyssmov Faroe\n8th of Snow's Maiden\n1637", ""),
        StaticBook("The Commandments", "Schreck'Lak", "The\nCommandments ᛋᛋ\n\n\nA comprehensive list of how to avoid degeneracy and please the spirits\n\n\nWritten by Schreck'Lak\n*Written in Blah*\n1. It is the avowed duty and responsibility of all orks to secure and assure the existence of the orkish race upon the mortal plane. To do elsewise is a direct insult to Krug and his blood which you bear.\n2. Be fruitful and multiply. Do your part in increasing the proportion of Krug's sons among the mortal plane.\n3. The guiding principle of all your actions shall be: \"What is best for the orkish race? What is best for Laklul? For Krug?\"\n4. Remember that the inferior inferior races are our enemies, and that the most dangerous of all is the human race. It is our immediate objective to relentlessly expand the orkish race, and keep shrinking our enemies.\n5. Show preferential treatment in business dealings with members of our race, the orkish race. Phase out all dealings with the kin of Malin, Horen, Urguan and those cat things.\n6. The offspring of interracial mates are infertile, pathetic creatures. While recieving all the curses of their lineage they recieve nothing. Do not mix your blood.\n7. Might makes right. Orks are to strive consistently towards physical, mental and martial excellence. Weakness is a disgrace to your racial comrades and ancestors: avoid it.\n8. Remain loyal to the spirits, and the spirits shall remain loyal to you.\n\n\n\nPraise Krug", ""),
        StaticBook("The Coronation", "Imperial Historian", "The Coronation of Tuvya Carrion\n\nWhilst the papal coronation had been a relatively affair, with what little members of clergy making their appearance, the splendor of the Imperial coronation had nearly overwhelmed\nthe care-takers . The laws which forbid the foreign races were suspended in order for all to witness the glory of Tuvya's ascension, and the Cathedral was brimming with local denizens to farfolks coming to test the new Imperial government.\nIt was well-known of Tuvya's regard for the impoverished and commons, which many would assume to mean a raucous, chaotic ceremony, plagued with all the ills of a congregation of the lords and low-lifes.\nIt was the newly appointed Daniel I who presided as High Pontiff over the ceremony. As the cathedral doors shut, the Orenian anthem began to echo throughout the Cathedral, signifying that the Church was ready to perform the Imperial Coronation.\nAs Daniel cited the duties of the Emperor, a warden of both faith and state, and ruler of all mankind, the demagogue of foreigners and Oreni citizens came to a hush as all listened to the Pontiff's words. Then, Emperor-Elect Tuvya was brought forward.\nKneeling before the Lorraine and the High Pontiff, he recited his oaths to church and state, acknowledged the responsibility to his countrymen, and accepted the Crown of the Exalted before him; an intricate artifact bearing the saints and sigils of\ncountless Orenian legend.\n\nAs the ornate crown rested upon Tuvya's head, Daniel retrieved an ancient church relic; the gilded blade of St. Adrian, a Sarkozic treasure long lost by the peoples,\nbut carefully preserved by Franz-Josef Carrion, and later Daniel. Delivering it upon Tuvya, Daniel I commanded the newly crowned Emperor to defend all whom sought to harm Oren's people, property, and faith. Daniel then bestowed\nupon him three names; to the Raevirs of his blood, he was Tsar Tuvya Sigismundovic Koravic, to the church, he was Imperator Augustus Tobias Sigismundos Karovium, and in the Common Tongue, he was Emperor Tobias Carrion. His styling\nfor his peers was up to their preference, whether as His Imperial Majesty Tobias or Tuvya. \n\nAs the procession came to an end, the Hymn of St. Godfrey filled the halls of the Cathedral, as Tuvya led the native Orenians out\nof the Cathedral to parade in the streets in celebration of an Emperor crowned. Notices of the occasion were  spread throughout the realm, letting all in Althera know that an Emperor ruled in Oren.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Crooked Man", "Aurea Terin-Acosta", "I saw a man being robbed,\nThought I would trot along, \nThe crooked man stopped me, \nAlthough I was soggy,\nAs I fell in a bog.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Defection", "[Okarir'maehr] Odessa Visaj", "The Visaj Defection\nJournal\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWritten by:\nOdessa Visaj\nDay 1:\nThe Defection\n\nElcihi still slept, as I packed the last of my most valuable things in a small bag. The bag was the only bag I had, which I could travel with properly. It forced me to leave more things than I truly wanted to.\nI had to prioritise greatly. My harp and a certain collection of books written by great Visajs were musts. But all luxuries above that, had to be left. The escape and defection was a success, even if carrying all my things were extremely uncomfortable.\nThe first night, I took refuge at the docks by the Spawn. I did not know exactly where I was heading, and risking getting ambushed in a forest somewhere wasn't on my agenda. It was cold, but I slept better than most nights in my bed in the corrupted city\nof Haelun'or. \n\nAthedil, a great llir of mine, had recommended I seek out the Warhawkes. They were to be a peaceful and welcoming people. That was my objective and goal for the next day.\nDay 2:\nThe Warhawkes\n\nI arrived to Leyu'celia while the sun was at it's highest on the sky. At first glance, the city looked more like a camp. Dirty and muddy paths, low and poorly designed houses. Not even a Haelun'or slum would look like this.\nI met Erolas Ba'ikana. The Prince of Mali'ker and Chieftain of the Warhawkes. A collected Mali'ker, who showed great hospitality as he offered me refuge. Even though I was relegated to the basement of the Tavern, it was appreciated.\nMy room was in connection to some sort of facility which seems to be used for some sort of self-medicating purposes. The drugs provided sound nothing like the ones I know from Haelun'or. I will do more research into the phenomena later.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Diamond[s]", "N.Y.", "vvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvThe\nDiamond[s] - N.Y.\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\n\n(A collection of poems written by a soldier. Found in the rough.)\n\n(Includes 5 Poems)\nThe\nPrayer - N.Y.\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\n\nI worry if I go to war,\nMutts will come knockin’ on my door.\nPlease protect my loved ones, Lord.\nMake me hard as diamond \nso I can crush these hordes.\nAnswer this, I’ll never ask for more.\nI wrap this prayer up and trace the cross\nOn the tabard that my grandfather wore.\n\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\nThe\nLifestyle - N.Y.\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\n\nI’m afraid of my lifestyle.\nI’m figurin’ out my life now.\nBut f**kas comin’ at my life now.\nIgnited powder to blow my house down.\nThey don’t wanna see me breathin’ this evenin’.\nNo matter how much I try to reach em’ and lead em’.\nI lost my eye because of one fatal accident.\nI guess my swordplay needed more practicin’.\nThat’s when I learned that war wasn’t for every-body.\nNearly lost mine so I killed the f**ka’ before they got me.\n\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\nThe Old\nTendencies - N.Y.\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\n\nOld Khalestine, city filled with Hash fiends.\nCrazy Hassan used to ride camels with a mad lean.\nThat’s how you remember it, that’s how I remember it.\nI done caught a couple arrows, been stabbed,\nHence why I keep my sword strapped.\nAnd truthfully, I think over half yall’ b***h made.\nAnd if you don’t trust my ‘A’ Grade,\nGo on, my Qali, come and test my blade.\n\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\nThe Woman sent\nfrom Allah - N.Y.\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\n\nYou shine like diamonds,\nYou’re bright as gold,\nYou soothe my achin’ soul.\nTruth within your eyes,\nNever told me a lie.\nI hold you up to the sky,\nI tell you that I might die\nbefore I hit twenty five.\nbut you give me peace of mind\nand remind me that I’m alive.\nOur reunions are a surprise,\nSo bright that with you I’m never lost.\nWhen I first stumbled into you,\nI knew that you were sent from God.\n\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\nThe Land\nOf Eden - N.Y.\nvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv\n\nOpen up your eyes, tell me what you see in-\nside of me, tell me exactly what you thinkin’.\nCould this be love, that be how it’s seemin’.\nAnd you should know that I been meanin’\nTo take you back to our slice of Eden\nWhere the flowers and the trees are meetin’.\nJust hold my hand, I can take you to that land\nGoodbye to sand, where our people damned.\n\n vvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv", ""),
        StaticBook("The Druid Guide", "Anorak", "The Order\nThe Druids are a group dedicated to preserving the balance of nature. All Druids undergo years of learning and studying in order to establish a strong link with nature.\nAlthough some are known to walk the land, spreading the teachings of Druidism, the Order officially resides in their Grove. Despite the misconception that only Elves can be Druid, the Order is open to all races, as it welcomes all who\nwish to follow the way of the Aspects.The Druids do not recognize those who proclaim to walk the way of the Druids yet do not take the time to go through the studying. That is to say, only those who have been promoted to the\nstatus of Druid by one of the Guide rank or higher will be acknowledged for their wisdom.\nTotems\nAll of those who are full-fledged Druids possess a title, sometimes referred to as a totem. This totem represents their history, their personality, their approach to life.\nIt is up to the Druid to identify themselves with an appropriate title. The time at which a Dedicant becomes a Druid is up to both those higher in the Order and the Dedicant themselves.\nPowers\nIt is well known that Druids are some of the best healers in the land, yet the true extent of these powers is unknown. Druids have been known to bring people back from the brink of death and restore sight to the blind.\nThe Druids are deeply connected to nature, yet do not control nature, but serve it. To try to have power over something without understanding is against their ways. It is more similar to asking nature for favours, and then doing nature\nThough nature will sometimes do very large favours, if the situation calls for it.\nResponsibilities\nThe Druids pride themselves with being the keepers and protectors of Nature. They will allow the chopping down of a tree, possibly even the removal of a forest, but in return they expect each tree to be replanted elsewhere.\nDruids are non-violent, and due to their long periods of studying in their libraries, are known to be fantastic sources for information. However, despite their non-violent tendencies, Druids will do whatever it takes to preserve\nit is threatened, and will resort to fighting if the situation demands it.The responsibilities of the Druids are vast. They teach their ways to all those willing, and sometimes unwilling, to listen, serve Nature by adhering to the Natural Cycle,\nand provide their healing hand to all land and creature which has been unnaturally harmed in some way. In oversight, the responsibilities of the Druids are innumerable and great.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Edict", "Karim Vayle", "The Crimson Edict\nAnd reasons you should join:\n\n1. We believe in equality. It doesn't matter what your race is, you're a potential member. That also means we don't tolerate slavery, so you've gotta help deal with that if we find any.\n2. We know you don't like necromancers, and we don't either. That also applies for any dark magic that screws with or hurts other people.\n3. We're more than just mercenaries. We're a thriving community made up of all kinds of people, including those who don't do much fighting. A good example of this would be the handsome and charming Head Bard, Karim Vayle.\n4. Almost-free lodgings! You need to be active and a contributing member, but other than that you don't have to pay a mina to stay!\n5. Loyalty. We're not gonna sell you out, mostly because of a life oath. That means you don't fuck us over either, or we're gonna hunt you down.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Elvenesse 1", "Kardryn Oranor", "The Elvenesse Papers\n             [1]\n\n\n\n\n On our government's\n       economical\n     transparency.\nThe First Seed, 1664, the High Prince issued his new Common Defense law. The law entails that citizen's of our Dominion home must take up arms to help defend our nation in times of need, such as during a raid.\nIf us citizens refuse to take up arms in occasions like these, we must pay taxes to the crown, with the exception of pregnant women, children and the severely disabled. Where do these taxes that we forces us to pay go though?\nIn fact, not only these taxes, what are all the payments to the state used for? The minas we pay to the Treasury when buying a home or shop, the fines we must pay for our crimes? Where does the money go? What does our crown use it for?\nWe need our government to be more transparent, how else can we know that the money of our taxes, treasury payments and fines is being used wisely and for governing purposes, rather than being used for the High Prince do go on a spending spree?\nThere's a very simple solution to this problem that is the fog around our nation's economy, created by the government. The treasury needs to keep record of all the state transactions, all the income and expenses. These records need to be available for the\npublic to view! We have a right to see where our money goes to and be asured that it's not used improperly.\nAnd for those who find this all far fetched;\nlook at the Silma seed themselves! They're living the fancy life in their big manor, their family\nbeing one of the biggest merchant families of Atlas, but does that wealth all come from trade? Or also from our tax money? Now I am in no way hostile to the Silma's, nor do I expect them guilty of such activities, but there is no way to be sure of it\nunless our High Prince decides to create more transparency in his government.\nA lack of transparency eventually results in distrust and a deep sense of insecurity.\nTransparency will lead to reduced corruption within the government of our nation.\nI write this short essay to ask for attention on the matter and to use this attention to set our government to work on changing it's policies for the better. We need to trust our High Prince, but trust rests on a strong foundation. One he needs to build.\nWith great consideration,\n\nYour obedient servant,\n\nK. Oranor", ""),
        StaticBook("The Elvenesse 2", "Kardryn Oranor", "The Elvenesse Papers\n            [2]\n\n\n\n\n On the connection of\n          powers.\nThe Domestic Charter of Elvenesse, written by the Communal Lords on the 16th of the Sun's smile, 1659. The first part of it, Article I, Section I reads: \n\"The High Prince’s duties range to all of the following:\nExarch Supreme to Over Judicial Proceedings, Leader of the Elvenesse, Commander of the mali folk, the Foreign Diplomat of the Communal Government, and the Monarch of the People...\" This is the first section of our constitution, the constitution of our\nyoung nation of all elves. It is in this section that our High Prince's powers are defined. It is here that our constitution states how our High Prince holds the power to create laws, judge over these laws and take action against the trespassers of it.\nAnd thus, it is this section that makes our nation's leader a possible dictator.\n\nLoriens Silma is a kind and good-natured man, a proper man to lead our nation as our High Prince. But what if he was insane? What if he was a maniac? A madman?\nHe'd have the power to do whatever he wishes to do. So what would we do against this? We can't do something against this. We can't do something for the High Prince holds the power to make his rules, control them and enforce them, leaving the citizens\npowerless. So what went wrong here?\n\nThe High Prince or in fact any ruler should not be an absolute ruler, an absolute ruler being someone who holds all keys to power. These keys to power are the positions to make decisions.\nThe keys of making the laws, judging on the laws in court and executing actions against offenders of the law are three keys that should not be in the hand of one person, for these three keys can together lock the door to freedom when in the wrong hands.\nThus I hereby plead to divide these powers. It benefits our nation to have these powers separated, to have one authority that makes the laws, controlled by the public, to have one authority that judges those who offend the law and to have one authority\nthat takes action against offenders of the law. For when we split up these powers and keep those who hold them in cheque, we can significantly reduce corrupt governance and give a madman leader no chance. Now I ask our High Prince one thing:\nHow long will it take him to put this divide of powers into place? How much longer will the mali folk need to live in a possible dictatorship?\nWith great consideration,\n\nYour obedient servant,\n\nK. Oranor", ""),
        StaticBook("The Elvenesse 4", "Kardryn Oranor", "The Elvenesse Papers\n             [4]\n\n\n\n\nOn the contradictions\n of our constitution.\nOur constitution, or at least the writ that ought to be labeled as such, and has been labeled as such in my earlier papers, has been written by Azoth Hawksong. Some may consider this constitution to be a work of art, and mister Hawksong to be a genius.\nMost individuals however don't think as highly of the man who wrote our 'constitution', for the constitution, as it's writer, is odd, to say the least. Not only does this set of laws written by Hawksong, who nonetheless is a professor of Legal Studies at\nthe academy of Holm, include all the issues that have previously been adressed in The Elvenesse Papers, it also holds with it a great set of contradictions, some of the clearest of which can be seen within Article 5, also known as The Communal Government.\nIt is this Article where the limits of our government are specified. Not at all an unimportant piece of the puzzle. Thought it is also this Article that throws overboard any logic that captain Hawksong carried on his sinking ship known as our constitution\nFor instance, the second 'rule' for our government is, to quote; \"The Government shall not enforce Law upon the entire land without the MASTER VOTE.\" \nNow maybe prof. Hawksong isn't aware, but a government's purpose is to enforce law.\nOf course, those who put any thought into their reading of said rule realize that it means that the government is not allowed to make laws without the MASTER VOTE approval, though this is NOT what is stated, and thus this can easily be misinterpreted.\nFurthermore, to quote rule seven; \"The Government shall allow equal say to the People.\" \nNow those who have read the previous issue of The Elvenesse Papers already know this, but for those who haven't: our constitution does NOT grant equal say\nto the people. Not in the slightest. The people only get a say in one matter, the MASTER VOTE, and even there the voice of the public is outscreamed by the many more voices that the government itself poses. Thus this is another fine detail of\nProf. Hawksong's artwork, another contradiction to add to the bunch that exists in a work that should hold none. It is possible for us to continue on about many more of these obvious mistakes made by Hawksong, though to keep this paper short we will end\nwith the best, if comedic value is concerned, one of them all. This is, to quote, rule four: \"The Government shall not seek to sow deception in the People.\" \nThus it pains me to see, that with this work of his own, Hawksong breaks this rule.\nWith great consideration,\n\nYour obedient servant,\n\nK. Oranor", ""),
        StaticBook("The Emerald Way", "Guyden Aureon", "~*~ The Emerald ~*~\n           Way\n\nScribed by the Willow Druid.\n\nTeachings by Archdruid Hurricane.\n\nDate: 13th of the First Seed, 1660\n\"Blessed are those who follow the Emerald Way.\"\n-----\nOn yesterday's eve, I witnessed a sight most common, though for some unknown reason, it struck me strange. An elk was torn into by a beast, and ripped into, to eat.\nA common occurance, yes, this is known. 'Tis the way of nature, the beasts eat the common gazelle, and so on the predator eats the prey, and the prey feeds on what they can. There is no evil in it, the predator must eat as much as the\nprey, and the prey must eat what it can, and there is only survival in it. The predator feels naught for devouring its prey, nor does the prey when it grazes upon the grasses, despite life from the world being taken out.\nSuch is the unseen way of all natural life, life passes on and on and through each moving piece of it. There is naught evil or good, only what is and what survives.\n\nSuch also is the way of druidism,\nour gifts are given without thought of good and evil, but with only this delicate balance of the cycle of life in mind. Druids are expected to be as neutral as nature in this sense, are we not? It is the duty of druids to correct\nthe balance, wheresoever it may be corrupted, without heed for borders. It is our duty to correct it, we must. We have been charged by the aspects above to do our sacred duties, and the order has decided we must do it without\nheed for borders.\n\nBut this charge ignores a most fundamental principle of our life as descendants, of how we have grown and developed too live with moraility, to have a sense of good and evil, and all in between.\nIt is what makes us what we are, it is what gives us our humanity, and this makes us truly and beautifully unique. We are not a part of this balance the aspects have created for us, but we surely live with it. To attempt to live\nwithout morality, to attempt to live neutrally and strive to think as the aspects do, without heed of what is good and evil, this is unnatural of our own being. It makes us only as simple as the beasts and prey that are in\nthe balance we seek so desperately to protect. We cannot, and we should not deny what we are, how we think, but we must find a balance of our own, and look over the balance with a sense of right and wrong.\n\nThis is what I feel\nthe Aspectists have found quite rightly in the founding of the Naelurir. To live along with both the balance and the descendants, separately, yet together. In this, we are allowed to exercise our sense of right and wrong and\ndo our duty to protect the balance with great strength. It is not the nature of men to keep out of everything, we, as Malin's kin, must be involved in the survival of our people, and our culture, for surely the theatre of the\nworld seeks to stamp us out yet. One would say it distracts us from our duty, to be a part of a nation. This is not the case, nay. I might even say it better allows us to perform our duties. While we cannot live as neutral,\nwe are able to do our best to spread what is good, and what is just. It is of the utmost importance that druids maintain strong ties to the world they live in and protect, they cannot fly above it all. Neutrality is a disease\nthat leaves men weak, and staggeringly alone, as many beasts are. Fellow priests of the balance, I say to you. Do not catch the disease of neutrality. Hone your sense of right and wrong, fight always for the path of good\nfor you and your people. Do not become unnatural men, stripped of all sense of what is good in the world. In morality, we find our strength together.\n\nI do wish to begin a great awakening in our faith, an awakening of\nour morality and duty. Altars lie around our sacred city, placed there by what seems to be a Nightingale and a Falcon, and upon them is inscribed the phrase \"Blessed are they who follow The Emerald Way.\" I take it as a sign,\nas a great stroke of faith that we must revitalize ourselves, and make something of that phrase. During my pilgrimage, I have meditated a great deal, and I shed a brilliant ray of light on myself and our faith. I have pledged\nmyself in blood to tenets I now establish, tenets of the Emerald Way, and I do thusly take them upon my soul along with my oath as a Naelurir. I encourage others to take the same vows with me, so we can move toward a better\nfuture for us all. May the aspects strike us down where we do stand should we break them, and may their wrath be furious and swift.", ""),
