        StaticBook("Treating Wounds", "Kristoff Herrick", "Wound healing\n\nMy time in the Order of Saint Amyas did teach me a few things, healing wounds is one of them. \nLet's start by steps\nStep 1- Cleaning the wound. \nAlthough that might sound simple, water will not do, for a gash or\ndeeper wounds you'll need something with alcohol in it, like beer or something of the sorts. (ofcourse you need to take the dirt off first if it has dirt in it). \nStep 2- Suttering the wound. \nFor that you'll need a needle and some thread.Sounds easy\nright? Well not really, you need a steady hand and you also need to do it by these...Let's called them sub steps.\nSub Step I Heat up the needle. You need to do that incase of the needle carring any illness.\nSub Step II Clean the thread.You need to do\nit beacause you don't want dirt on your wound.\n \nSun Step III-Suttering You start at well the start of the gash or deep wound (Warning this does NOT apply for small wounds.) \nYou'll then sutter like your sewing a rag, a messy and bloody rag\nthat screams like a little girl, non the less a rag. \n\nStep 3-Bandaging\nA bandage can be almost anything, a old rag (As long as it's clean) A bed sheet (As long as it's clean) etc. Then you just wrap it around the wound. Done\nNote: If there's any sign of infection after or  before you done this process you'll be better off cuting off the leg.", ""),
        StaticBook("Treating Wounds", "Dela Vaik", "My time in the Oder of Saint Amyas did teach me a few things, healing wounds is one of them. Lets start by steps.\n\nstep 1- Cleaning the wound\nAlthough that might sound simple, water will not do, for a gash or deeper wounds you'll need something\nwith alcohol in it, like beer or something of the sort. (Of course you need to take the dirt out if there is any in the wound first.) \n\nStep 2- Suttering the wound.\nFor that you will need a needle and some thread. sounds easy right? well not\nreally, you need a steady hand and you also so need to do it by theese... Lets call them sub types.\n Sub type 1\nheat up the needle incase it holds any illnesses.\nSub type 2 clean the thread\nYou need to do it because you do not want dirt in\nthe wound.\nSub type 3 shuttering\nYou startat well the start of the wound or gash.(warning this does not include small wounds.) you'll then shutter it like you are sewing a rag, a messy, bloody rag that screams like a little girl, a rag non of the\nless.\nStep 3- bandaging\nA bandage can be almost any thing, a old rag (as long as its clean.) A bed sheet (as long as its clean.) etc. then you just wrap it up. Done.\n\nBy: Kristoff Herrick", ""),
        StaticBook("Tree of Light", "Aluuvia", "=======================         The Tree of\n              Light\n    A fictional story\n=======================\nImagine sleeping through the end of the world.\n\nIn the madness and confusion of it all, an end not prepared for, you fall between the cracks, forgotten and lost.\nWhen you regain your sense of self, what little you can, there is no sense of time nor space to accompany it.\n\nThis is the tale of those dreamers, who once slept through the end of the world, and never returned.\nThere are some who say that when the Undead finally gained victory upon us, conquering Aegis in the name of Iblees, the land became rot, foul and fire, disease and damnation. In the name of their Fallen Lord, that Daemon who once\nbetrayed all the Creator had made, they remade the land into an eternal pit of suffering. Those stragglers who did not flee in time were made his playthings in slavery for eternity.\n\nBut others say that when Iblees took the lands of our fore fathers,\nit was a sight the Creator could not bear.\n\nHe woke from his slumber and in pride and in spite, slipped Aegis from reality, and so it fell out from beneath the feet of those who failed to flee in time, and they too fell.\nAnd they fell.\n\n\nAnd they fell.\n\nDown, down they went, so far down they started going up again.\n\nAnd up, and up, and up.\nUntil finally they stopped, on solid ground, though sleeping still. And how long they slept they never knew, for they had lost all sense of that, of time and of sight and of place.\n\n   They heard through sleeping ears, the breeze\nin the air and the sound of water babbling through a brook, of gentle rustling through the branches of nearby trees.\n\n  Though having slept for time unknown, still they could smell as well as you or I, the pine on the air and\nthe scent of rain on the front of a storm.\n\n   When they stood, they felt grass beneath their feet, smooth and scratchy, which made them wiggle their toes though that only made it worse. As they walked, they stumbled, over rocks and roots and\nthrough slippery streams, the babbling brooks they could hear and taste when they fell, but never see. And they could not see, not the scratchy grass or the rocks or the brooks, not for want of eyes or sight, though they did not know this, but due purely\nto the deep dark they now dwelled in.\n\n  In time those lost learned to live in this place they had fallen up to. They ate the grass and the plants, raw and rough, for to their dismay they could make no fire. The branches of the trees bent but\nand bent but would not break, and there was no dead wood upon the dirty ground to be found. \n\n   Those wise stayed together in a group, finding each other by the sound of their voices. Those arrogant left, believing\nthemselves wiser to go in search of a place with light. Those too frightened to live would simply lie down and vanish in the dark. They had lost sense of time, no minutes or hours to count but only the gentle rhythm of the breeze and rustle of the leaves.\nBut time did pass no matter their regard for it. To you or I it may have seemed years, decades, centuries. To them it was an eternity. And in time they adapted to the dark and grew accustomed, and ceased searching for the light they were certain was\nnot there.\n\n   They travelled further and further into the dark lands, exploring with their hands and feet, ears and tongue. They lived happily and well, twisted from what they were as they had become.\nIn time they came upon the base of a large tree, it's girth such that though they could walk around it, it was such a feat they felt exhausted by the end of it's circle. They could feel it's branches overhanging, the willowy strands of leaf brushing\ngently against their faces, reachhing down and caressing their arms. So exhausted from their travel 'round it's base were they, that they lowered themselves to the grassy ground it reached up from, closing their eyes to rest.\nThe branches reached down, draping their leaves across the sleeping creatures.\n\n   When those lost creatures awoke, opening their eyes despite having no use for them, they opened them wide in astonishment, for\nthey saw the land about them, a set of bright and vivid hues, of greens and oranges and yellows and blues and all sorts of contrast and color.\n\n   They looked to the tree that had been their haven for the night, for there it was brightest, and they\nsaw the source of the light. Small things, fruits of luminescence, grew upon the tree. They bulged from the knots in the fine, dark wood. They hung from the branches, fully-formed and hardly so, peeking out of the centers of orange flowers.\nThe creatures stood slowly, regarding the tree of light and all the land it illuminated, the whole of the world they had fallen into from wherever they had come from before.\n\nBut they did not rejoice.\nInstead they looked upon each other and all they could see was rage and hate. They had lived happily and peacefully despite the blackened darkness they found themselves in. Now they had sight once more and they looked upon their twisted visages with\ndisdain. A flood of knowledge from their regained sight washed away their ignorance, and with it, their bliss. Now they looked upon one another with empty, blackened hearts, and theyr rage took them and others, at the base of the tree of light.\nWith this knowledge of their disfigured selves, and the light gone from their hearts, they lashed out at one another, ripping into flesh with their overgrown nails turned claws, and spilling blood upon the roots of the tree that had wronged them.\nFor nothing can make something out of nothing and the tree was no exception. It had not created the light on it's own accord, but taken it from the hearts and souls of the Lost Ones.\nThat is the fate of those left behind, lost in the shift between worlds.\nThe tree, they say, is immortal and infinite; It reflects itself across all lands, and though once it was simply a tree, not it carries with it a light, born in it's fruit and it's seeds, born of the light of those Lost.\nAnd there are those who have found the tree and seen it's blossoms illuminate the shadows. \n\nBut for a blink of the eyes or a turn of the head, the tree is gone again, darkness where light once stood.", ""),
        StaticBook("Trial of Words", "Murlocs", "The Trial of Words\n----------------\nBy The Unnamed\n__________________\n|                          |\n|          [][][]          |\n|          []| |[]          |\n|          [][][]          |\n|_________________\nTo be nothing, I am stone.\nTo be fragile, I am glass.\nTo be cruel, I am cold.\n\nIf not a metaphor, what am I?\n\nA heart.\nWith a head, no arms.\nWith a tail, no legs.\nDevoid of a body.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nA coin.\nAt the hour of darkness, they come as a guide.\nAt the hour of light, they are lost themselves.\n\nWho are they?\n\nStars.\nI do not breathe, but I drink.\nI do not run, but I eat.\nI do not drown, but I fear.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nA fish.\nI give life, but I am the villain.\nI have no name, but I am the most known.\nYou ask for me, but I never come.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nTime.\nYou give it,\nBut you keep it.\nIf you turn back,\nIt is meaningless.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nYour word.\nIt runs with no legs.\nIt has a mouth with no words.\nIt has a bed but is sleepless.\nIt does not live, but flows with life.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nA river.\nI devour, but I do not eat.\nYou can fight me, but I am not solid.\nI am feared, but wind is my enemy.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nFire.\nYou can see me, but I cannot see you.\nWhen you look in to me, I will look you in the eye.\nAnd you will see, that I do not lie.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nA mirror.\nIt can carry, but can devour.\nIt is strong, but kisses the clouds.\nIt can be lethal, but gives life.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nWater.\nWhen I weep, I am dark.\nWhen I am empty, I am light.\nWhen I'm angry, I yell.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nA cloud.\nWith a single tooth, he howls.\nThe bane of the forests;\nThough he can be swung as a killer.\n\nWho is he?\n\nAn axe.\nThe men with most want it.\nThe men with little despise it.\nIt is the feeling of death,\nBut is felt by the emotionless.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nNothing.\nThey dig and feed, but never need any air.\nThey can be small, or huge, but never nothing.\n\nWhat are they?\n\nRoots.\nThe young wish for it.\nThe old loathe it.\nIt is the killer of man,\nBut the strength behind wisdom.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nAge.\nIt is, but it is not.\nIt is good, but it is bad.\nTo the left, it is to the right.\nDuring the day, it is night.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nA paradox.\nA lot of bark, but it is never heard.\nIt stands straight, but can be struck down.\nIt is life, but it can die.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nA tree.\nI birth as green; I die as brown.\nI dance upon the littlest gusts of air.\nI drift far from my home as I wither.\n\nWhat am I?\n\nA leaf.\nHe who held the light for those dim nights.\nThe one who stands, but not always.\nGrowing shorter and shorter.\n\nWho is he?\n\nA candle.\nShe sings, but never talks.\nHer shadow passes the ocean, but always dry.\nHer home aloft, but never settled.\n\nWho is she?\n\nA bird.\nIt flies swiftly, but doesn't move.\nFor the young, it is forever.\nFor the old, it is gone.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nYouth.\nTo guard his gold, he bears the sword that is his life.\nA warrior amongst many, to protect and serve.\n\nWho is he?\n\nA bee.\nHe sits on ice, but is not cold.\nHe sits on tables, but is not ashamed.\nHe watches, but does not speak.\n\nWho is he?\n\nThe sun.\nWith wings that do not flap.\nThe dead that carries the living.\nIt cannot see, but it moves.\n\nWhat is it?\n\nA boat.\n*Some sort of gibberish litters this page. It makes you ponder.*\n\n¡ʎoɾuƎ ˙sɔolɹnW ʎq noʎ oʇ ʇɥƃnoɹq 'sǝlppᴉɹ ɯoʇsnɔ ll∀\n\n---\nThe Riddler.", ""),
        StaticBook("Truth", "Joil Diagak", "He moves with the shadows, and stalks with the wolves.\nHe flys with the bats, and bites with the knats.\nHe jumps with the frogs, and walks with the dogs.\nAt the end of the day, he's just gonna stay.\nSo why fight back,\nwhen he's all that you lack?", ""),
        StaticBook("TTOTWA: Book I", "SapphireShard", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist \n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n \nVolume I of V\nForeword\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest in the art of alchemy when there are spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion.\na job than an apothecary’s potion.\nMy response to this statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking,\none mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound,\nshroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak with the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell,\nbut it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows those without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy \n---------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought\nand he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking, question.\nThis question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff\nNow as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains the same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing?\nThe answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent Exchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing.\nAs the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule applies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy.\nAlthough I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of alchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something\nMuch like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing.\nKnow that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of what they wish to\nother representations of what they wish to make.\nFor example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an -inked out-, a mixture of -inked out-, -inked out-, and a dash of -inked out-. Why would one need these items?\nThe -inked out- provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the -inked out- the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter.\n-inked out-, a -inked out- that grows most often in deserts, is often -inked out- and often associated with -inked out-. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -inked out-. -inked out-, another powerful symbol of -inked out-,\nmixes well with the crushed -inked out- to produce something truly strong. The-inked out-, an optional portion, is another symbol of -inked out-, albeit both weaker than -inked out- or\n-inked out- for both a -inked out- and -inked out-’s fire burns far greater than -inked out-.\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always.\nOne must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too much will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask?\nWhen balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchange, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well.\nBut when you disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required,\nfor one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty. This is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos.\nThis can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will produce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results,\nand in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? .\nThe answer to this is rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin.\nThis is best provided in a short list of required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep a Calm Dispostion\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about.\nEven having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins, ingredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\n((OOC Footnote: Due to the constraints of MC books, the Elememts of Alchemy must be made into a seperate book. It is titled \"The Tale of the Travelling Alchemist: Elements of Alchemy\"))", ""),
        StaticBook("Turning [10/16]", "Rokag'Gorkil", "The first were broken by the tides, the oceans swell to drown,\nCities devoured and peoples scoured, all by the prince to crown.", ""),
        StaticBook("Turning [12/16]", "Noria Onyxheart", "His blazes drew the second's praises, hearing death for the first. Undone by their ignorance, for with their savior they are cursed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Turning [6/16]", "Ti'Khoraha", "Oh he feels them, each bush and blade, dying now in every glade,\n\nA name he gives the screams he hears, he will be their kindly shade.", ""),
        StaticBook("Turning [9/16]", "Noria Onyxheart", "For gracious silence, he needed violence, purging all their kind. This land was but just another, and then the fates came aligned", ""),
        StaticBook("Two Doors", "Dolan Velethen", "Two doors, both opaque in feature, stood in front of two staggly men. On the left stoff Veren, a human whose life was reached half. To his right stood Hijol, a wood elf younger than a fox out of season.\nIn between them was a sign that read \"Choose carfully\". Hijol inspected the sign and claimed that the sign was closer to the right door than the left one.\nAfter some long discussion the two decided that they must both choose the same door. They felt tired and rested.\nThe next marrow Hijol woke to fine two sleeping children. Both girls of unrecognizable race. He awoke one and quickly told her his tale. \"The right door is correct\" He notes that the sign has dissappeared. \"There was a sign that read 'There must be only\none right door'\".\n\nMeanwhile Veren awoke to find the other girl. Afyer he told her she must not trust Hijol, the two groups conjoined.\n\nFighting words came out. In one corner stood anger and spite.\nThe other stoff curriosity and fear. The winner, only the one brave enough to turn a knob,\n\nThey grew weary and slept another night. The next marrow Hijol's girl and Veren's girl awoke to find themselves alone and, adults.\nIn Hojol's girl's mind she had two facts, she must choose a door with Veren's girl, and it has to be  the right one. Veren's girl was to scared to speak to Hijol's girl.\n\nHijol's girl only had one option, in her mind, she must take out veren's girl to\ncontinue...\n\nHer fists hit her face. Her blood tained her tunic. Fist meet fist, and death was quick.\n\nHijol's girl turned the right knob slowly. On the other side was horrer. Both doors went to the same place. A small\nbedrock room with a sign that read \"Life isn't going to bend it'self to your perceptions.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("UA Vol. I", "Kelthran Parmandil", "Unearthed Arcana, Vol. I\n\nThe Void\n\nAn arcane tome by Kelthran Parmandil§\n\"The Void is nothing, yet everything. Like our imagination it may be dull and empty; or it can be full and colorful.\"\n\n-An Ancient Arcane Proverb\nChapter One: Existence\n\nThe Void is a plane of existence where things may be created with the use of one's imagination and willpower.\n\nIt is a plane where nothing exists until a being of great will, often a mage, wills it into existence.\nFor one to connect to The Void, a basic line of communication must be established. This line of communication is generally established through meditation and rigorous mental practice.\n\nThese lines of communication, when first established, are\ngenerally quite fragile. Due to the difficult nature of sustaining such fragile lines, novice mages can have their connection to The Void severed even by minor distractions.\n\nBright flashes of light, loud noises, pungent smells or tastes, pain and\neven sudden changes of temperature are all examples of things that could sever a mage's connection, and stop his or her spell.\n\nThey may otherwise sustain their connection until they run out of mana, which will be covered in Volume II. This can\ncause the mage to suffer great mental duress, fall unconscious and, in some cases, even die.\n\nMasterful Mages, however, have had time to strengthen their connection to The Void through many years of practice. Their line is not as easily severed, and\nsuch practiced mages usually only fail in their concentration when struck or shot.\nChapter Two: The Effects\n\nConnecting to The Void, as mentioned in chapter one, is a long and arduous process. Chaneling its energy is well known to have adverse effects on the user. These effects include, but are not limited to the following.\nWeakness: The most iconic effect is that of physical weakness. This taint manifests in both the musculature and immune system of the mage; eating away at their tissues and ligaments, and also causing the mage to be more prone to illness and infection.\nIt is also for this reason that mages do not often wear armor. A suit of heavy plate or leather can cause unneeded physical exertion on the wearer. Compounded with the mental exertion of the spell, casting while armored becomes a very difficult process.\nThis is also why mages do not often wield blade or bow. Due to the degradation of their strength, compounded with the required physical prowess required to swing a sword or draw a bow perfectly, mages cannot attain a master level in these arts.\nA popular hypothesis is that The Void is simply not meant to be interacted with, and that prolonged connection slowly weakens the user over time.\n\nThis implies that energies of The Void are poisonous or draining, and that they taint its users.\nAnother, less popular hypothesis is that some energy is lost in transmission between the mage and The Void. \nThis implies that every time a mage casts a spell, some energy is premenantly drained from the material realm. A truly frightening idea.\nFrailty: An extremely common fault among mages is their frailty. A multitude of mages report that, after having practiced magic for an extended period of time, their fortitude is dramatically reduced.\n\nBones are more easily broken among mages, and\ntheir blood does not easily clot, causing wounds that may be considered moderate by a seasoned warrior to have potentially severe consequences for a long term mage.\n\nIt is for this reason that most mages do not often participate in combat or\nmartial training. A vicious cycle, considering that this degrades their muscles further than the prolonged use of magic would normally cause.\n\nThe theories as to the reasons for this frailty remain the same as its counterpart; weakness.\nChapter Three: Connection\n\nForging a connection to The Void is no easy task. As mentioned in chapter one, it requires complete control of one's own mind and exceptional power of will.\n\nTo paraphrase the quote at the\nbeginning of this book, \"The Void is nothing, yet everything...\".\n\nThough it is impossible to focus on everything at once, it is not impossible to clear one's mind completely. This is how mages have attained a mindset close enough to The Void to\nproperly forge a connection.\n\nThe steps to clearing one's mind are different from individual to individual, but the one constant that does not vary is that of meditation.\n\nThe first step to a student's first connection is to\nfind a quiet and comfortable place. If individuals speak or shout nearby, it will likely interrupt the ritual, and the student will need to start over.\n\nThe second is to find a comfortable place to sit. The first attempt is often lengthly, and\nif the student's legs tire, it will surely break their concentration.\n\nThe third and final step to the first connection, and any connections thereafter, is to clear one's mind completely.\nThis final step, and the most important of all, is acclaimed by many to be the most difficult.\n\nThe process of clearing one's mind cannot be taught or guided. As they say; the mind is a muscle, and it must be excersized like any other.\nBut be wary, ye who would harness the great power of The Void. For it is a dangerous, winding path - upon which many die.", ""),
        StaticBook("UA Vol. II", "Kelthran Parmandil", "Unearthed Arcana\nVol. II\n\nMana\n\nAn arcane tome by Kelthran Parmandil\n\"A mystical energy that surges through every living thing just as blood runs through the four races' veins. At the hands of The Creator our world is filled with mana that ties everything to The Void and The Heavens from which he wove the beginnings of our\nuniverse. It exists in a constant ebb and flow, and is perhaps the force that makes every living thing so beautiful. The blossoming of a flower amidst the bitter winds of winter is a testament to the magical qualities of mana, that even beyond the sun's\nrays and the nutrients of fertile ground there is energy within that frail creature to perform something of wonder.\"\n\n-Anonymous Mage\nChapter One: Mana\n\nWhen mages are connected to The Void, they may create whatever they like within it. To draw a spell into the world, however, and apply it to someone or something; a mage must use mana.\nMana is as invisible as the air around us and flows through it like winding streams, connecting every living being.\n\nLong ago, mages learned to use these streams to draw from The Void; working great thaumaturgical wonders in the\nmaterial plane to weave both creation and destruction across the realm.\n\nThough mana in this realm is bountiful; mages cannot use the energies of other creatures, or the latent streams that flow through the air - severely limiting their\npotential power. A mage instead learns to work his or her own mana like a tool, using it to draw precious power from The Void and into our realm.\n\nThe mana is lost by the mage, however, as he or she expels it into the air as a spell, and takes time to\nreplenish - much like one's stamina after hard work.\n\nThough the similarities between the two are evident, a mage who foolishly depletes his or her mana runs risk of much greater peril than exhaustion. The excessive depletion of a creature's mana\nis known to cause great agony; eventually turning the creature comatose until recovery, or killing it outright.\n\nEvery living being in the material plane naturally creates mana while growing or resting. Healthy creatures naturally grow and rest far\nmore quickly than their unhealthy counterparts, and it is for this reason that mages who eat healthy, excersize often and remain in a calm state of mind regenerate their personal store of mana (their mana pool) with greater speed and efficiency.\nChapter Two: Mana Pools\n\nA \"mana pool\" is the amount of mana held in the aura of a specific creature. The richer in mana the aura, the larger the pool and vice versa.\n\nA mage with a larger mana pool can cast grander spells\nand sustain them longer than their less talented counterparts. \nThough the size of a mana pool grants definitive advantage, it is only the baseline in measuring a mage's capability. The second and far more important part of a mage's spell casting\nability is, of course, skill.\n\nSkill is fairly self explanatory; the more time a mage spends perfecting his or her spell casting ability, the more effectively he or she will be able to use the mana that surrounds them.\nDifferent spells, of course, require different amounts of mana to cast and sustain. Though there are exceptions, it is almost always the case that mages who practice the more \"expensive\" schools will learn to use their mana pools more effectively than\ntheir colleagues. A list of magics pertaining to The Void is as follows, sorted from least intensive to greatest.\n\n-Illusion\n-Elemental Evocation\n-Alteration\n-Arcane Evocation\n-Conjuration\nInterestingly enough, learning the more intensive types of magic has been recorded to give an unnatural boost to their practicioners' mana pools - allowing them to use their less intensive abilities with more inherent ease.\n\nFor example; a mage who\nbegan as an illusionist and later took up conjuration may find themselves able to sustain their illusions for more lengthly periods of time, even if they had already reached a level of exceptional skill in the field.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ulrah by Taveric", "Aeluin Tundrak", "Kaz'Ulrah\n\nWest path,\n-------------------Path leads into farm land, to the south there is a huge fort on the side of a mountain, I was able to slip in. seems to be a training camp and mine, heads of elfs and humans scatterd on the inside.\nKaz'Ulrah\n\nWest path,\n-------------------\nFurther in the west path that leads to the moutain entrace. 3 guard towers, one to the left and right on the entrance and one more above the entrace, surounding areas are stalls or farm land, rest is empty.\nKaz'Ular\n\nSouth Fort Inner,\n-------------------On further in spection, It seems they are building something, there is lots of tents, mines going deeper into the moutain and one huge main tunnel but it is blocked off. deffence seems to have towers on-\nEach corner main line wall scaling the whole way around  has its own tower and path connecting everyone, only one main lift that goes up and down, and its at the front entrace only being blocked off by one gate, note there is no secondary ways up or -\ndown the lift is there only way.\n\n\nMission log end,\n\nTaveric Sylric\n------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Undead Stronghol", "Simon", "Simon's Report\n\nUndead Fortress\n\nTop of a mountian south of evermoor, green goopy river.\nElf told me some stuff:\ntrapped enterance, large hole, device that pushes you into said hole, arrow traps, secret enterance at bottom and right side, enterances open from inside, tunnels and crypts, morghuuls reside inside.\nVictim spoke to me:\nremembers a man named tristian, led her off the wall into the cropfeild. lost her hair, lost her tongue, and all her fingers but left ring finger. they ate her fingers. they wore dark robes. man named master. took her to him.\nShe was meant to be an example?", ""),
        StaticBook("Undead: Overview", "Friedrich", "A Brief Overview\n- = - = - = - = - = -\n Undead are the most vile creatures within our realm. Creations of dark magic, they consist of numerous different kinds. They have Ghouls, Liches, Darkstalkers, Dread Knights, and Pale Knights. There are\noccasionally others such as Ghosts and Gravens, though arguably they aren't considered 'dark' to the same degree as the others. Their abilities extend from mere increased fighting strength to magical augmentations such as lifedraining.\nGhouls\n- = - = - = - = - = -\n The most basic of undead entities. They are shambling, walking corpses brought forth through dark magic. By absolutely no means are they to be considered an intelligent creature. Instead, they are persistent.\nGhouls feel lessened pain and no fatigue, with no need for food or breathing outside of lifeforce. They can obtain this lifeforce from the flesh of others, presumably. They are caused harm by any weaponry, though gold is more effective.\nLiches/Darkstalkers\n- = - = - = - = - = -\nThese dark beasts are the pinacle of dark entities. They posess life-draining abilities, able to drain someone's very essence in order to feed themselves while harming others at the same time.\nThey are incredibly hard to kill do to lacking any nerves or organs within their body, composed entirely of their skeleton. Contrary to popular belief, touching gold alone does not bring them harm, but instead it can cut through them like iron to living\nflesh. Liches are capable of casting spells without feeling fatigue, making them great spellcasters that are hard to stop. In return, they have almost no physical strength. Darkstalkers are the opposite, with physical augmentations to their being.\nDarkstalkers cannot learn magic normally, though both them and Liches may become Necromancers - as seen historically in attacks on the Dominion.\nDreadknights And\nPaleknights\n- = - = - = - = - = -\nSimilar ideas with only slightly different executions. Both of these creatures are magicless constructs, with Paleknights made of greenish-blue stone and Dreadknights of thick metal. They are both\nphysically massive and powerful. To fight them you would do best with slayersteel, blunt weaponry - perhaps a warpick on Paleknights.\nGhosts/Gravens\n- = - = - = - = - = -\nTo begin with, Ghosts and Gravens are caused by brutal slaughterings upon those either unaccepting of death orwith unfinished business. Gravens posess a physical form, though with a generally spectral appearanc\nabout them. They exist to accomplish a goal they had in life, demanifesting to again permanently die if they fail to accomplish such. Ghosts posess four types: Haunts, Revenants, Specters, and Poltergiests. Haunts are unaware of their death yet, living as\nnormally as possible. Revenants know of their death and are beginning to learn how to use their powers of telekinesis and turn invisible. Specters are those ghosts who come to accept their death and aid in others doing the same. Poltergiests have refused\nto accept such and generally turn evil and dark.", ""),
        StaticBook("Unfinished Book", "Unknown", "[!]On the first page of this book there is a slender tower of stone stretching upwards into an empty sky.  The structure is perched on a steep mountainside freckled with snow, stones, and a few flowers.  A fir forest is seen in the distance.[!]\nMy dreams are just utter darkness now.  I despise them just as the old ones, but what would I prefer?  Being awake and having this numb feeling inside of me with tears constantly rolling down my cheeks, or sitting in nothingness?\n[!] The rest of the book is empty.", ""),
        StaticBook("Unfinished Notes", "Nemir", "[!]In elvish the words read, \"Be warned those who enter here, you cannot kill what you do not know, and we did not know.\"\n-------------------\nothelutal'ame?\n-------------------[!]The phrase above is translated into common.\n\"Do not take this warning as a challenge; Only the spirits can protect you here, and even then, only just barely.   It was cowardice to run from a fight, but not cowardice to avoid what is only death.\"\nLearn what Mali'ii means\n-----------------\nurthir'lari metta'igne = The Phoenix Queen\n\n[!] The rest of the book remains unfinished, and the name, \"Nemir V. W.\" can be read on the back cover.", ""),
        StaticBook("Unsigned Letter", "Anonymous", "**The letter is written in very bad writing; clearly written by someone uneducated and of common birth.**\n\nHis / Her Majesty, the King and Queen of Santegia;\nYou have met this little boy, Arthuer, whom was just born hours ago.\n\nHis father will not accept the boy as his and refuses the burden.\n\nHis mother is very ill and without fortune to afford the child.\nHe has no other relatives that may find a place for him in this world.\n\nWould you be so good to help this innocent soul?\n\n**The leather remains unsigned.**", ""),
        StaticBook("Unsorted notes 1", "Alma Kersley", "*This page contains a rough sketch of the map of Atlas, done with ink. A small black squares are scattered around the map, each has letters written next to it.*\n9th of the Deep Cold\n-------------------A merchat at Malin's domain possesses a steamship. He claims to have had it crafted with the help of an elven tinkerer, named Tajo the Bald. He says the man resides at Krugmar and takes lots of coin for his work.\nPS: Avoid Rex, quite possibly a slaver.\nPSS: Rex could be a leader of the Krugmar, he goes by the name 'Rex Skahing'\n*This page contains a rough image of a small steamship. The vessel has two masts and a structure made of stone (part of the engine?) planted on the deck between masts. On each side vessel has a wooden wheel, alike to those used at watermills.\n6th of Snow Maiden, 1661.\n-------------------Met some of the so called 'sprites', namely Ula and Muu (one purple and another one grey). Most peculiar folks they are. About 6 inches tall and have wings, which inclines certain uses in matters that may come.\n8th of Snow Maiden's 1661\n-------------------Presa de Madera's docks hold some great vessels. Finding their captains shouldn't be much of a trouble, would it?\nFind Tamnaeuth Terin\nHe healed Stephanos of Mystra.\nThere are clerics known as Gabriel and Duncan, Tamnaeuth is supposed to be in the same organization.\n-------------------A book called 'Into Nothing' may describe Iblees? Or some daemon?\n10th of Malin's Welcome 1661\n-------------------I've got assaulted by a deathknight named Blackwood in the city of Holm. A man named Samuel Lythian came to my rescue and started a fight with the undead, allowing me to get to safrty. More men came to\nhelp, among them were Garolletios Earl, Norton Brown, Athonir Silva and Holm's general (?). Undead killed Samuel and then fled deeper into the city with all the rest chasing him. Me and Garoll went to bury Samuel at his village. When we finished, he\nreturned to us from the cloud temple, not remembering what happened to him. Then me and Samuel returned back to the Holm, where Norton told us that undead was... dead (haha).\n-Lost 50+ minas to Norton, damn it\n-Find a tailor/armorsmith to renovate your outfit\n-Dimlin offered me to join the brigade. Note that as a possibility\n-Ryszard Dunin looks for his bride. Lucienne be Bar of Savoys. Blk hair, brown eyes, red dr\n9th of the first seed\n-------------------There's an elven corpse hanging on the cross near Gotha. Word 'murak' is carved on her. What does it mean?\n-------------------What is the confederation of Hammers?\nCrowned archer says a one\nI regret what I have done\nWhat I brought and what had spread\nNone could ever outrun\nWay-a-wither way-a-rot\nAll the past now matters not\nBrawny savage hollers two\nI care not if you are true\nIf you raise your hand with metal\nYou will get a metal too\nWay-a-totter way-a-fall\nWill you join me in a brawl?\nScrawny noble wheezes three\nI expected you to knee\nPart with that you have behidden\nOr you'll dangle from a tree\nWay-a-\nShrouded fella whispers four\nI have key to many door\nFolken walk through them a plenty\nAnd will walk a plenty more\nWay-a-fiddle way-a-foo\nThere is one awaiting you\n7-8 of Malin's Welcome 1663\n-------------------Me and Athonir have found a burning camp on our way to Dominion. There was dried blood, torn tents and footprints. The place reeked of corpses. We have found a bottle of blueberrie wine there.\nAurelius Horen orders some Roger to stand up. He asks about passing of the Adrian Rothesay, who was alive during reign of Sigismund. Adrian wrote a book called 'Necrology'. The book desctibes the nature of Liches and how to defeat them. Roger Whitt has\nbeen tasked with a quest to find it, or at least its copy. \n\n9th Grand Harvest 1663.\n------------------\nApparently King has been assigning knigtly quests. Aragon Silversteed steps forth.  'Your majesty, if something needs done, I am at your\nservice' says he. King orders Aragon to stand before him. 'I wish to have a stone' King says. He meant a black onyx, which comes from meteorite. Infused into the blade, it would sharpen the sword greatly.\n-Tippen's root will stop bleeding\n-Blissfoil. Cold herb with blue flowers, grows on a slippery stalk. Rub in small amounts to ease one's pain.\n+Tippens root grows on dead trees and animals. Has bad scent and purple flowers. Rub on the wound but\nit's smell may cause vomiting.\n-Vitality tincture. A weak potion that could help against simple illness and pain.\n+ Adelmar Oliveira.\n- Kilgrim, a famous dwarven alchemist had discovered a potion of longevity. Dorin Irongut knows its recipy, but he is not willing to share it (not yet at least)\n-I have met Daniel, some kind of undead who got confronted by another one, a dwarf in his past life. They spoke of some Toad.\n-Lord Silversteed spoke of the undead hordes beyond the wall in the frozen south. They have taken hold in some 'Black Fortress' and will seep through a breach in the wall.\n- Norland was attacked dut to its agression towards Arbor\n10th of Sun's Smile, 1664\n===================Tourney and feast in Belvitz ended up with a massacre. Prince of Renatia, Antonius Horen, attacked Eimar var Burgundar and his men, thus going rogue. Rogue prince was captured and taken to White Peak. King\nand his posse moved out immediately, Aragon and his men with him. There he talked to his son in private and ordered to exile him to Aeldin via his ship, docked near Cloud Temple. (What is Aeldin?)\nDruidic aspects. The mother and the father. Elves follow them. Mother - life and growth. Father - hunting and eating. Together they bless the land with plants and animals. Malin was originally a druid. Druids follow apectism. Druids are sort of servants\nof these aspects, employees of gods.\n\nTREELORDS\nYou let the tree take your soul and it grows the body which behaves like your old one. You must be murdered for the tree to take your soul.\nI was introduced to the concept of druidism and aspectism by Midnus Anarion of Laurelin, a rather tall elf. He suggested finding druids of Naenulor? and posing myself as adunian to begin studying nature magic. Nature magic is incompatible with void magic.", ""),
        StaticBook("Update", "Armin Ambertoe", "Update:\n\nThanhium dust has spread from the lab to the vault and so the vault has been sealed. DO NOT attempt to break said seal or enter the vault due to a risk of thanhium spreading into the library. Also, DO NOT swim in the water downstairs, it is also\ncontaminated with thanhium.\n\n-Armin", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Letter", "Unknown", "--------------------------------------\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-------------------------------------\n===================The location of this said beast and phenomenon is unknown. This creature, is something of nightmares... It's limbs are quite long and it has claws which can turn the thickest hide into butter. Over the recent weeks, I have spotted this\n===================-creature a number of times. It is accompanied by some sort of phrase which lingers in the back of your mind.\nI do not know the origins, or even the behaviours of this creature but it does terrify myself. I have seen many a party go\n===================after it. Their bodies are normally found as unidentifiable mounds of flesh when I check the nest once again...\n\nI worry for the Vailorians...\n\nThough there have been rumors and small gestures that the creature\n=================== -is able to break swords with the strength of its hide. It's vacant eyes able to pierce the visage of the bravest men. Even those whom believe in gods and aspects think this creature was a mistake. A myth which has come to life, to\n===================-haunt all those whom try to find it.\n\nThe Cannibal roams, it hunts and it will kill.\n\nThe Vailorian Bounty Company will have to be contacted to try and defeat this beast, as my own company has fell prey to it for far too long.\n===================May the bounty company have mercy upon my troubles, knowledge will be found in the coming weeks.\n\n\n-Terryn Cairse\nVailorian Explorer.\n===================[!] The last page is covered in maddened illustrations of humanoid figures, with small suns lining the corners. Lines gesturing from the sun towards the darkened humanoid.\n[!]", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Matters", "Tarien Arvius", "Missive to the Spellcrafter Archon I - The Archive of Urguan\nTo the Spellcrafter Archon,\nThe Radiant Man led us to a cave beneath the Thahn Mountain. There he performed some magic unknown to us and the door opened. Beyond it\nwas an archive, with a great rotating crystal set into the middle. One of our number was fool enough to touch it and fell into a coma from which he could not be roused. Unable to wake and thus unable to eat or drink he died within a few days.\nThe archive itself was a trove of Forgotten Dwarven texts, some sort of archive. They were so ancient that in our enthusiasm we unfortunately caused damage to a few. We took all texts that appeared to be of significance and are bringing them back to\nRivel for study. The Radiant Man believes the Forgotten Dwarves hold the key to the defeat of the Ravager. If they held the power to strike down an archdaemon, why are they extinct?\n- Spellcrafter Tarien Arvius", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Missive", "Tarien Arvius", "Missive to the Spellcrafter Archon II - The Seal\nTo the Spellcrafter Archon,\nWe have completed our studies of the Forgotten Dwarven texts, and the revelations are astounding. The Forgotten Dwarves indeed date back\nto ancient times and the first war against the Great Ravager. It seems that the architects of the city devised a plan to deal with the Ravager: a machine capable of sealing him away in a plane of existence forged by the dwarves themselves. The\ncaveat was the power source of the machine: it had to run on souls. The Forgotten Dwarves suffered a schism over this: while most of the ruling caste were in agreement, one dwarf refused and led his followers to the surface to join the other races in\na coalition against the Ravager. The remainder, lacking confidence in the other races, prideful in their abilities and unwilling to depart the Hall of the Ancients and abandon the abundant veins of valuable materials it was built upon,\npressed onwards with the grand machine they called the Seal.\nWhile we understand not the intricacies of the device it seems to consist of three parts: four towers called soul capacitors hold the trapped souls of unfortunate\nmortals that power the machine. Two colossal engines known as Plane Forges create and sustain the “pocket plane” as we call it, and a huge disc known as the Ethereal Magnet somehow captures higher beings and draws them into the huge\naperture in the floor that acts as gateway to the pocket realm.\nIf such a machine exists, and the Forgotten Dwarves completed it, then this could be the path to victory. Obviously fuelling the machine would be problematic: it would be wrong to feed\nmembers of the Enlightened Caste to it and the Lesser Caste is vital to sustaining our way of life. However, if the Forgotten Dwarves did successfully fuel it then we need not.\nThe books reveal the location of the hall, and my fellow\nfollowers of the Radiant Man are keen to set off. I am actually beginning to worry about his influence over them: the powers the Radiant Man grants are easily the equal of our arcane abilities, but those who draw on them most heavily seem\nto be the most loyal to the Radiant Man by far. Either they see him as a more worthy governor than the Circles, or, as I fear is the case, the Radiant Man’s power comes at the price of one’s psychological independence from them. I advise arranging for\na few of my men to be detained in the city and studied. In the name of security, of course.\nWe will set out for the Hall immediately. I have but one cause for trepidation.\nThe Forgotten Dwarves commenced construction on this machine,\nostensibly completed it…\n...and then they went extinct.\n- Spellcrafter Tarien Arvius", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Report", "Conscript Farald", "SCOUT REPORT [The contents seem very hastily scribbled, to the point where it's difficult to read]\n\nYOUR MAJESTY,\n\nI can waste no time with formalities. The Wraiths from the south have come down in force and made a camp on the\nedge of the Yatl Wasteland and the Sleetfells south of Markev. They number in the hundreds, and seem well-armed and equipped. They are like a full military force, sire. I implore you to rally all our forces and meet them in battle before they march closer\nto Markev.\n\n-Conscript Farald", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Request", "Odessa Visaj", "\nMost Blessed Tailor, I have a request of the uttermost importance. I was assaulted by a filthy Mali'ata in the square and the vile excuse for a Mali made my beloved dress tear at the knees. I therefore request, and it be urgent, a replacement.\n\n\nPlease speak with me for details.\n\nOdessa Visaj.", ""),
        StaticBook("Urgent Warning!", "Axel Swale", "Warning to the guard of Holm to be on VERY high alert in the coming times. 21st of Sun's Smile 1674 is the date and a group of Four undead came into The Dancing Igor and killed multiple men! They said they'd return to destroy all of Holm with an Army\nbecause we refused to sacerfice one of our own to them. Earlier in Saint's day a horde of undead seiged Holm and was repelled thankful. But for these reason Holm should keep its gates closed and protect it's people", ""),
        StaticBook("UrUKz GuID", "Bogrum'Lak", "Diz izh WeY tuH pEk uhp Da UruK Wumun.\n\nBai BOgRum.\ndIz IZH StUP AsH tuH Da WUMun GuDe.\n\nSTup Ash.\n\nfUInd dA wUmUN laT wUnd, UzE uR Lukz tUh pEEp ef Shi WuHNd LaT.\n\nWuRKz Un TwiGGEhZ.\nStUp DuB\n\nKlUmP dEm, Ef dEy sTel alIv, dEn KeP dem.\n\nEf Nub.\n\nFlaT dEm.\nStUp GaKH.\n\nMeK heeR uR zNaGA.\nNuW uH HAf a gRiL uF laTz Uwn.\n\n\nBud LaTZ Weel Neba bI leIk BoGRUm'LaK.\n\nUnLEZ LaTZ WUnD tuH BI BoGrUm'LaK ZnAGA.\n\nUKE GUG'YE.\n-BOgRuM'LAk", ""),
        StaticBook("V Veritatis 6", "Jacob Chapel", "Verbo Veritatis\nSixth Edition\n23-3-1480\n\nIn this edition:\n*Knights Rebellion\n*City of Dwarves reclaimed\n*The Karovian Column\n*Words from the Emperor\nAnd much much more!\nReporters needed for the newspaper\nDear readers, as always we are in great need of reporters for our esteemed newspaper, especially now Ralph Riley has stepped down from his position because of his prolonged battle with illness. Since patronage has\nbeen secured, we are able to pay much better rates for articles. As a reporter you can expect up to 500 minas per article!\n\nThe work is freelance, and you are paid per article delivered. Please contact Jacob Chapel if you are interested.\n((Contact me (Pepernoot) ingame or on the forums!))\nThe Knights Rebellion\nIn one of the greatest moves of treachery in our time, Maric Varodyr, once Grand Knight of the realm, and part of the Order of the Red Dragon has taken up arms against the Empire. After word had reached the Emperor that Maric was\nconspiring against the integrity of the Empire, intending to break it up into pieces. Immediately the Emperor dismissed him from his position, but Maric fled to the Keep of Angren and barricaded himself in there, along with a few rebels.\nOur grand Emperor demanded Maric to leave the keep, but the traitor defied these orders and claimed the keep for his own. Hiding inside the keep he proclaimed himself King of Renatus, a claim so audacious and without any real claim to the title,\nhe must surely be absolutely mad drunk on power.\n\nAs of now the traitors are still hiding behind the walls of Angren, awaiting their certain and inevitable death. Quoting our Emperor: \"Do not recognise this traitor who has so aptly deemed fit to call\nhimself King without any form of legitimization from the Church or the faith. Fight back against the foreign tyrants who would dare infringe upon our sovereignty, despite their signing of various treaties intended to ensure such would not be so.\nYet do not fight because of who you are, Highlander, Heartlander or Farfolk. Fight because by the creator above, you will never allow such a spineless snake-tongued worm as Maric Varodyr to sit upon the throne of humanity”\n\nCarry on Carrion!\nThe City of the Dwarves reclaimed\nSince the reconquest of the dwarven capital, the dwarves have been very busy to rebuild and reclaim their old capital. Nearly all signs of the Undead occupation have been removed and a lot of work is being done to\nrebuild the homes of the dwarves, under the guidance of Lord Balek Ironguy and Thurland Grandaxe, the architects of the arts guild. The new city will be completely situated in the mountains, with farmland in the valley in front of the entrance to\nthe mountain home.\n\nThe new city has been named Kal’Karaad, and it should be ready for habitation in 2 saints days ((11 November)). We of the Verbo Veritatis wish the dwarves happiness and prosperity in their new capital, and hope that it may last\na thousand years. Sadly the dwarves have not returned our goodwill to help in the current rebellion of the knights, and have retracted their offer to come to our assistance.\nFormation of the House of Magnates\nThe Emperor has formed Oren's first legislative body! Known as the House of Magnates, it consists of representatives, or magnates, of all the nobles of the realm, along with the burghers of Petrus. The higher one's\ntitle is, the more representatives and votes, one gets. The body votes on appointing various ministers, akin to privy councillors, who make up the government. It is led by the Lord Chancellor who acts as virtually any under-king to the Emperor,\nworking on the day to day affairs of the realm. Currently the Chancellor is Joachim var Sarkoz, a learned barrister from the court of King Francis who has been appointed by the Emperor to help facilitate the establishment of the Magnates while the nobles\nand emperor focus on the military expenditures at hand. He shall rule for three years, before he is either voted in again by the various Magnates or a new Chancellor, or known more colloquially as the Prime Minister, is appointed by the Magnates.\nLet us hope Prime Minister Joachim can guide our country to glory!\nHoly ward over Petrus\nOn the 10th of The First Seed, mages have cast an undead weakening ward over our grand city of Petrus. The main effects of this is to dispel the effects of their magics. The mages creating the magic, one of them being the ancient\nwizard Blundermore, forged a magic crystal and lifted it up into the air, defying gravity. From the crystal lights and other magical effects originate, bringing a feeling of safety and calm over the city.\nThe populace can rejoice over this noble effort of the mages, and it is hoped that the ward will keep the undead away for a long time.\nThe Karovian Column\nA Tragic End\nOver to the news from Karovia this week, and it appears that her husband’s reluctance to give her a child has finally pushed poor Lynn Rovin over the edge. The other day, her body was found lying at the bottom of\na cliff face, her stomach punctured with knife wounds. Though it has not yet been confirmed, early evidence appears to suggest that the blows were self inflicted. What an unpleasant end to what could have surely been a most enduring marriage...\nThis newspaper would like to extend its deepest condolences to her widowed husband, the Imperial Prince, Aleksandr Carrion for his tragic loss.\n\nIn other news, yet more spook-filled sightings of the Imperial Prince’s late twin brother, Yakov Carrion!\nAs reported in Edition Five, little is known of Yakov’s decision to betray Oren to the Undead scourge, though recent events suggest he has returned to to seek vengeance against close members of his family. We can only hope that the guard remain viligant\nin these dark and dreary times. In brighter news however, Aleksandr is said to have found a new wife to replace his dearest beloved. Rumour has it that this is said to be Lynn’s very own sister! We suspect it shall not be long before we have another\nroyal wedding on our hands!\n\nSo dear readers, with his wife’s demise and his brother’s fall to the Undead, where will Aleksandr turn now? Find out next time in the Karovian Column of Verbo Veritatis, Edition Seven!\n-Our anonymous source in Karovia\nWords from the Emperor\nTo the loyal fellows of Orenia,\n\nThough some minor rebellion plagues our lands, a simple matter it will be to drive out the meager forces assembled under the traitor Maric Varodyr.\nSuch a petty uprising is of little consequence in the grand scheme of our Holy Empire, such writing on this day regards the welfare of our capital, Petrus.\n\nWhen I had first been elected by the Electorate to serve as Emperor,\nPetrus had been little more than a ghost town. Now I am happy to say Petrus is the single most populated city in not only our realm, but of the world itself. This cannot be attributed to I, but to the true heartlanders of Petrus who have graced this city\nwith life. Petrus stands as the jewel of our modern world, in not just the strong economy, but in terms of cultural regards, social mobility, and military might. \n\nNowhere else in Athera can one find a public library with such a vast array of knowledge\navailable to the public, nowhere else in Athera maintains our efficient system of free food, and nowhere else in Athera upholds a city guard as do our Wardens of the Imperial Watch, a Watch at current roster twenty-four men strong.\nThe average daily income to Petrus coffers is 5,000 minas. The average yearly budget for the Imperial Watch is 20,000 minas. Petrus maintains a budget of 44,000 minas for building improvements and public works. Average yearly tax income is 4,000 minas.\nPetrus holds 53 separate plots, of which only 8 are not filled at the time of this writing; 43 plots had paid taxes last tax cycle. Truly the economic structure of Petrus is unmatched by any city in the world, and this does not take into account the great\nndustrial capability of our Holy City. Within Petrus are produced the finest weapons and armor in the entire world, with the most efficient labor force in the world harvesting vast quantities of wheat, ores, stone, and wood everyday.\nUnlike the treasuries of many other nations, the majority of goods produced are not stashed away but sold as surplus to the citizens of Petrus, weapons and armor worthy of Kings can be found for open sale and in quantity, at a pittance compared to what\none may find at the cloud temple auctioneer.\n\nThough there is much more than just numbers to Petrus, I hope this has given some insight into our holy Capital and I am proud for all the fellows of Petrus who make our city the jewel of the world.\nCreator watch over us all,\nTuvya Sigismundovic Carrion\nReturn of the Order of Saint Amyas\nThe Order of Saint Amyas has been reformed, with Adrian de Bar as its new Grand Master. Shockingly though, Adrian quickly pledged his support to Maric’s traitorous cause and was convicted of treason.\nAfter persuasion by Oren, and a lot of mercy on the side of the Emperor, he was returned to the fold quickly though. Hopefully he is wise enough to not betray Oren again.\nWe hope that the Order will become a beacon of purity and righteousness, especially in these dark times, and that honest God-fearing men fight for its cause.\nPetrus tavern under new management\nAs the old Throdo has not been able to properly take care of the tavern at the central square in Petrus, the tavern has been granted to a new owner. The tavern has been renamed “the Drunken Dragon”,\nand it has been quite busy indeed.\nThe Verbo Veritatis wishes Sam good luck with running the tavern, and may many drinks be drunk!\n\nWe hope you enjoyed the newspaper, and remember: Athera’s greatest bookstore, “Books of Oren”, is the publisher of this great paper. There are shelves upon shelves of amazing books for incredibly low prices. Visit us at Abbey Road 13, Petrus!", ""),
        StaticBook("V.L. EVALUATION", "Doctor Everage", "14th of The Deep Cold, 1678\n\nValerica Leyu'ilueth\nAGE: N/A\nDOB: N/A\nHEIGHT: N/A\nWEIGHT: N/A\nPSYCHOLOGICAL EVALUATION\n\n-Not performed\nPHYSICAL EVALUATION\n\n-Not performed\nSOUL EVALUATION\n\n-Not performed\nCONCLUSION\n\nValerica, at this time, is four months pregnant. She has never been pregnant before. Her lover, Myriil, is supportive and involved in her journey.\nTREATMENT\n\nI advise that, during her pregnancy, she consumes blood lotus soup. Once an elven day she is to visit the clinic to receive the supplement. \nDuring these visits, she is to receive medical guidance and assurance. She hopes to deliver\nin the confines of the clinic, and that is the goal. \nShe is to be monitored for signs of early delivery and miscarriage.\nAPPROVED\n\nDoctor Harlan Everage of the Caras Eldar Clinic\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Vailor And Orgon", "Turkûrz'Raguk", "Orgon And Vailor\n===================       Chapter 1\n===================Ohrgohn... Zpeerihd ub Dizeaze agh Failuhre.\nDah Rehx, Phaedrus'Yar, Ehlekted dihz Zhryne agh eeht ztuuhd fuhr uvah 40 Yeehrz. Eeht Behgan Kurrahpteehng Vayluhr.\nChapter 2\n     The Begginning\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-The crowds of Uruk sat around the fire, looking deeply into the Glistening heat of the past. \"Mih Rehmembah.\" Said Turkurz'Raguk\n\"Mih Remembah day Dey Dah whule Uzg Zhuuk.\" He closed his Eyes, -\n- The day of Reckoning flashed in his mind: { The uruks were enjoying Sparring practise in the Arena of San'Thraka, The heat of the Desert Boiling their veins. Whilst Jukha and Yuglix'Lur were fighting, A Large Explosion could be heard, and the waves-\n-  Of wind would near, Shaking the whole of Vailor. The Rex, Kharak'Raguk Ran outside of the Palace, looking up into the Sky as all grew silent, before the Uruks started asking him Questions in fear.\n\"Zhud Uhff, Fulluwh Mih Bruddahz agh Ziztahs! Fuhr Wih-\n- Trahvel thru dah heehtz uhb Ouhr huhm!\" He took off, leading the might Band of warriors through the Desert, beads of sand Dripping from their Flesh. In the Distance, a hole the size of 20 Ten feet ologs could be seen, the Bustling of Life inside of it -\n- The birds Chirping and the Vast amount of Creatures Stomping in the Thick Bushes. The Rex held up his fist, and the army stopped, He leaned over the side, viewing the Desert. The Rex's Head looked to the side, a Ladder went down into the hole, and so -\n- They climbed down Exploring the Cave.\n\"Dihz ihz Bub'hozh Bruddahz, Wih kahn huhnt heyrr!\" A lur said, shile a Yar inspected the Giant Tortoises, Scorpions occupying the Cave.\n\nSuddenly, the beats of wings could be felt and heard from behind-\n- A large scaly Crature, The head of a Lizard, the Tail of a snake and the wings of a Demon, Emerged.\n\"DRAKOOON!\" Jukha Screeched in horror, Viewing the massive beast. The beast Flew swiftly out of the Hole in the roof of the Cavern, The Uruks watching -\n- In awe. Kharak'Raguk scowled at the Dragon, Him turning to explore further.\n\nThe Group stumbled Upon a Cave, hearing incomphrehensible talking inside. A Rugged Face looked outside, it's head twice the size of a Uruk's, and its body A little bit bigger-\n- Than a clumbering olog's. In the Centre of It's head, Was a Singular Bolging eye, Glaring at The uruks, It stepped out and another appeared, The one wielding a mace and the Other A large tree -\n\"Azh-Peehperz!\" The Rex alerted the Group, The sound of Swords unsheathing and Axes Unstrapping would be heard. And so the bloodshed began.\n\nThey battle the beasts for hours upon hours, Before Both fell. The Uruks roaring in Triumph, Altough -\n- In the back, The rex would stand with a worried expression on his face. He turned and Saw a shrine, The Spirit Zagbal, Of the Jungles stood there. \n\nIn fear that they Offended Zagbal, They started Sacraficing to It. Jukha and Turkurz taking the Cyclops'\nRugged old mace. That soon becoming the main design of the Orcish Flail.} The Burly Red orc Sighed, Staring deeper into the Fire as the other's thoughts began to Wander into the Past, Into Vailor...\nChapter 3\nOrgon's Shrine\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nThere is a lesson to be learnt by all Shamans and orcs, as even they can become greedy with Power...\nKharak'Raguk Gave Orgon too much Power, Which resulted in Vailor becoming destroyed, -\n- Though it is Unknown if Orgon survived the Attack, The threat still lives on, as the Taint lives on...\nChapter 4\n      Vailor's End\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-It was a abnormal day at the Cloud Temple, as a Group of adventurers Attacked Orgon and Defeated him, His death unkown.\nThe taint spread, Destroying The docks as all sorts of races rushed towards -\n- Their boats. The Rex Stood at the Pinnace, as the Magical Rift opened to grant Gateway to a other Realm. All sorts of Orcs Gathered on the Uzg's Boat, as the Clumbering Ologs and Uruks Managed the Oars, Rowing towards the Rift.\nAs they went through The might Rift, They See the Realm of one taht no one has ever dared to Venture to.\nThe Spirit Realm, Where all the Immortal Spirits Reside, Lesser or not.\n\nA flying man Came forth, his head covered Entirely in eyes. -\nHe was Letrothak, Spirit of Warnings.\n\"Descendants.. You have come here to Venture out of Vailor, But I fear my Lord Apohet Forbids it.\"\n\nA Glistening Light Appeared and Blinded the Descendants for a few moments, and Letrothak was gone.\nThe descendants Rowed Forth, Ignoring the warnings, For they had nowhere to Go but Forward, In doing so, They paid the Price, as Apohet's minions Attacked, Made from Water, Fire and Death itself.\n\nBut the Fought Valiantly, Crashing through the Rift. -\n- When They entered, They saw something Extraordinary. Islands, Fresh from Civilization. Signs of Life were everywhere, as The Orcs Settled, and began their life in the Jungles of The Uzg. It was the Year 1571, And the Age of Axios has begun...\nFinale\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=This book stands as a Warning to those that Dare to give the Spirits too much Power, That dare to Make the same Mistakes as We did. Do not Let Axios Fall. For The Rex, For the Uzg, For KRUG!", ""),
        StaticBook("Vaznakoh Vorznak", "Aurna", "*This thick tome of paled ivory paper is bound in green leather. Leaf, flower, and stem patterns embroidery line its binding and covers in chartreuse yarn, decorating its flexible albeit unyieldy form. Written within is an extensive description on\nanatomical notes interrupted by elaborate diagrams and charcoal illustrations of various humanoid and demonic forms. Forms of flesh, constructs of bone, and numerous animalistic draws are depicted within alongside scribbles noting names, functions, and\nother applications of the body regarding physical function. After the anatomical diagrams of beast and men comes more hellish pictures, charcoal and ink depictions of demon anatomy. The writing in this section shifts into an obscure language, one borne of\nsharp, runic characters familiar to few as the Black Lanuage of Al'tahrn-Durngo yet the letters do not read of any word and must be of some foreign dialect. Those unfamiliar would identify the writing as something infernal but nothing more. The\ndiagrams read of otherworldly imps at first, small and squatty creatures with a wide array of features yet they all boast mantles of horns, malformed bodies, and crude tools of murder such as great talons or a wicked maw of fangs. No natural world\ncould support such life without going extinct from vicious overpredation. Deeper you read of more hellions and infernal creatures yet they begin to grow in size and ferocity, practically screaming from the page. Beastial demons cover the pages, scaling up\nin size and flaming features to the point where the demons become humanoid and limbed, there being a mere three of these more personable creatures versus the scores of previous imps and beasts.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Veluluai", "Aluatis Lorenthus", "=========================Veluluai\nBy: Peurith\n=========================Transcribed by Peurith - Scribe of Malin.\nVeluluai - wife of Uradras - advisor of Malin, leader by mandate of the Scholarly Order of Maehr.\nAppointed to the study of deific powers following the defeat of the Archdaemon, Veluluai spends many years at sea, returning only to speak in private conference with our king.\nAs of now, she has been gone from the capital for a decade. Perhaps in time we will discover the secrets she uncovers for our people.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vitalius' vote", "Vitalius Maarsen", "Ellie Undertree", ""),
        StaticBook("Vladan's Vote", "Vladan Válek", "I, Vladan Valek, Marshal of Adria, Captain of Belvitz Guard, Royal Huntsman of the Kingdom of Renatus, First of His name, \n\nDo vote for Broos Wellis, First of his Name, Journeyman of the Belvitz Guard,\n\nFor the Position of Maer of Belvitz.", ""),
        StaticBook("Void and Origin", "Maleos Ilindith", "Origin Based upon the Sciences - Additions to Void-Oriented Theory\n\nBy Maleos Ilindith\n\nThe Chapters line up with those in \"Theory of Void-Based Origination\", excluding the first\n\nChapter I: Baseline and Origination\nTo go along with the author's initial thinking of a void based world would be on the right end goal, but not with the corect facts to support it, for the thought of 'the creator' as the universal baseline of origination stories around a character cannot\nbe expected to possess qualities of creatures such as their own. Yet, the conditions of such a creation of the world can be seen in life as we know it; and thus, it can be mimicked. Contrary to the belief of religious tomes, a being does not need to start\nthis process; for we would then have to wonder if said living being has existed for eternity doing absolutely nothing. It is far more logical to go on the assumption of non-living objects, having the potential for life, as observed in magic, but not being\ninnately \"storing of life\". We also can assume the reverse, in that live beings can become non-living, and their material as a potential for living material quickly decays exponentially with time by assuming the death of the subject requiring such.\nWith this baseline of knowledge, we can presume that it is far more likely for a simple base of something to have spontaneously changed to morph into more complex series of things, such as is observed in alchemy. This doesn't require for that to be the\nbeginning of time as we know it, for that could have began after another reaction from the base ingredients that reacted and continued to react for some time. It is equally difficult to imagine the world having a starting point as it is to think of it as\ninfinitely old. For the time being, neither can be proved, as we cannot see creation past creation when the most recent creation may have simply started with those ingredients when the world began as opposed to having a deal of time to react.\nChapter II: Alchemy and Change\n\nStarting with theory on how the void can permentantly change the world, as shown in the work that is reviewed, we can add logic of alchemy, as was requested by the author in further proving the thesis.\nIn knowing that the void can make change onto materials and make things where none previously existed. But, the void and magic behind it /is/ something, and in the evidence shown in the reviewed work, we can conclude the connection between magic and\nmaterial. Magic being bound to laws of nature, that is, not the magic that would be used to fill in the cracks of ignorance. With this, we may further settle that the point of \"nothingness\" was simply just the base state, which is now illuminated to be\nthe void. As alluded to earlier in this chapter, we can combine this relationship of void changing to material to prove that it is possible to change it back, as alchemy would show in change of material to material, and the ability to convert to base.\nSo, materaial and magic must fall under the same princaples, just being different states. We prove that materail can be transfered to that of voidal fabric. No mage has done this for the obvious reason if it being directly unuseful, for the mage /needs/\nthe void to then compose voidal fabric. But, as difficult as it seems to prove, the Translocationists have already bound this thesis to theory; for they transfer matter to void and back, creating void where there is not as a form of a gate, and then\nbind the connection. This gives way to thesis on how the void perhaps is not another plane altogether, just a state of materail that trained subjects can bend at their will. Of course, the voidal pathway fizzles out after some time after the conversion of\nmaterial by more common familiarity, that is, non-magic material, fades to no longer be transmuted to voidal fabric.\nChapter III: A Designer's Lens\n\nSimilar to artists, it is typically vastly more difficult to /create/ than it is to mimick. Magic being difficult enough, seldom do subjects have enough mana AND manage to create something that doesn't collapse instantly\nfrom being a draft from the mage's mind. We can see when the draft more often works, for individuals such as translocators, who have made what they will forge in the void in the world more familiar to them, most often in sketch or diagram. This is likely\ndue to the disconnection between the area of magic in one's mind and the area of abstract layout, existing on seperate hemispheres for most beings. We can also infer from mana and our revelations from the previous chapter that mana exits alongside will,\nwhich is trained direct with control and abilities gained in training in magic and meditation. Meditation for mages is not simply clearing one's mind to adapt the void or present a state as it, for the mind, if blank, would cease to function. The mind\nalso presents a canvas similar to that of the world in that it never truly \"stops\" or \"clears\" itself. The formation of the mind too is of building upon from the natural world. All of this chapter only further proves the thesis presented:\nThe natural world and the void can be interchanged and exist in the same set of laws of nature, varying accordingly from state of material. Unlike the reviewed work, we conclude that the void is harnessed out of the power of individuals which too come\nfrom the void, most often indirectly. The reviewed work assumes that the void is harnessed by itself. This would be incorrect in that the void is rather static without the interaction of magi if it is to exist in it's own plane; so we ought to conclude\nthat that is physically impossible, for the void would need to exist alongside the natural world as we have proved, with the obvious extremely rare cases of voidal material reacting with itself in accident, being the likely begin to the world as we know.\nIt also differs in this theory shows that everything is impermenant, as everything can be readily changed in the natural world and, as distinguished befor the theory, in the 'voidal plane'.", ""),
        StaticBook("Void Transloca.", "Evelangéline", "About Void Translocation:\n\n- Void Translocation, the voidal art of storing objects in the void, is one of the provailing magical arts in the means of utility.\n\n- A Void Translocationist can place and retrive objects-\nthat they have placed in the void anytime and anywhere they desire. \n\n- Although it may seem fairly easy for the eye, it requires intensive studying and observing to be actually translocating even the smallest of objects.\nBasic Understanding:\n\n- First of all, although it may seem that the object is being transferred inside the void in one piece, it isn't. The object breaks down into tiny pieces, moving freely inside the void. To call for the object, a void transloca-\n-tionist has to remember the physical properties of the object.\n\n- The magic happens in a rather interesting way. First, a lavander speck of light appears at the desired location of the void translocationist, growing larger-\nto fit the size of the object. Then, the void translocationit simply pushes the object inside the lavander circle of light, the light swelling in side before dissapearing again. To recall an object, the translocationist does the same with the lavander-\ncircle of lightly. Only this time, the object starts to form at the enterence of the light, before simply dropping down. One can say this could be used to drop heavy objects on others' head. One would be correct. However, it'd be a lot slower and mana-\nwasting way of attacking if not mastered than the other evocation arts, which is the main reason this magic is used for utility, not offense.\nConditions for Translocation:\n\n- The translocationist must be knowledged of the object, having it in clear vision. One cannot translocate an object behind walls. To further explain, a translocationist needs to know the physical properties-\nof the object before even attempting to send the object to the void. During my studies, I have carried a notebook with me to note the properties of the objects I wanted to translocate. Like my doll, or the anvil I keep with me for any emergency smithing!\nFirst, make a list of the physical properties of the object.\n\nWhat does it look like?\nIs it soft/rough?\nWhat colour is it?\nIs it cold/hot to touch?\nIs its surface smooth or rough?\nHow does it react to-\nfire and pressure?\n\n- These are only examples, there may be more if the translocationist feels there is more properties to cover. For example, I'll be covering a simple silver knife's properties on the next page;\nSilver Knife,\n\n- Made of silver, is durable\n-Cold to touch\n-Reflects light\n-A small 'Eva' is carved into the handle part,\n- Shaped like a long rectangle but with a sharp edge,\n-Used for cooking-\npurposes,\n-Durable, doesn't bend with pressure;\n-When reacted with fire, keeps shape when reacted with the average heat of the fire.\nMore Things to Know:\n\n- As in all magical arts, one must not use more than their existing energy. Failure to do so will result in fatigue and maybe even death.\n\n- As the objects gets more complex, the mana required-\nto translocate it increases as well. Because of their extreme complex design, living beings cannot be translocated in any instance.\n\n- For being fairly complex and lacking solidity, liquids also cannot be trans-\n-located.\n\n- As the mass of the object increases, so does the mana required to translocate it.\n\n- For any questions, do not hesitate to contact me, Evelangéline for further information.", ""),
        StaticBook("Voidal Basics", "Koralon Onyxheart", "Voidal Basics\n\n- - - - - - - - - - - \n\nBy\nKoralon Onyxheart\n\n\nTeaching Coursebook\nfor learning Arcane Theory.\n\n------------------\n§i1. Soul & Mana\n\n§§Every object, every living being is made of mana. Every soul expels excess mana.\n\nThis excess mana can be used to manifest spells.\n§i2. The Void\n\nThe void is an emptiness. A realm that can be altered with mana, and a realm from where mana can draw on effects to affect the mundane.\n3. Void Connection\n\n§§Any mage will need to develop a void connection. A connection has to be widened through practice. Connection drains one's strength, both actively and passively. One has to connect to the void to cast spells.\n4. Mana Anchors\n\nAll spells are sustained by a mana anchor. These can be targeted by abjuration, sometimes by physical attacks even. These may be visible.\n5. Line of Sight\n\nImagine mana anchors bound to the mage by a string. This string cannot be broken. The mage needs to maintain line of sight to their anchor, and thus, to their spell.\n6. Aura Theory\n\nEvery mage has an aura, with a specific colour. This manifests in most of their spells, and is visible when they connect to the void.\n7. Voidal Corruption\n\nThe void is not without dangers. A mage too power-hungry, or a mage non-careful may easily rip a tear into the void. Similarly, sites of rituals or great spells may leave so-called \"tears\".\nThese can be temporary or permanent. A tear is a site where voidal energies freely flow into the mundane world and influence the surroundings in a chaotikal (pun intended) way.\n8. Mage Circles\n\nMultiple mages may cast a spell together using circles. Circles, however, have a fair chance of ripping a tear into the mundane.\n9. Mana Gems & Obelisks\n\nMana may be stored in mana gems. These cannot be used to cast, per se, but to fuel enchantments.\n\nMana gems have to be regularly re-charged by mages.\nA mana obelisk is a giant mana gem with a large amount of mana. Mana obelisks are used to mass-recharge mana gems without exposing mages to fatigue.", ""),
        StaticBook("Voidal Theories", "Ash", "By\nKoralon Onyxheart\nTeaching Coursebook\nFor Learning Arcane Theory\n1. Soul & Mana\nEvery object, every living being is made of Mana. Every soul expels eccess mana.\nThis ecces man can be used to manifest spells\n2. The Void\nThe Void is an emptiness. A realm that can be altered with mana, and a realm from where mana can draw on effects on the mundane.\n3. Void Connection\nAny mage will need to develop a Void Connection. A connection has to be widened through Practice. Connection drains one's strength, both actively and passively. One has to connect to the void to cast spells\n4. Mana Anchors\nAll spells are sustained by a Mana Anchor. These can be targeted by Abjuration, sometimes by physical attacks even. These may be visible.\n5. Line of Sight\nImagine Mana Anchors bound to the mage by a string. This string cannot be broken. The mage needs to maintain line of sight to their Anchor, and thus their spell\n6. Aura Theory\nEvery Mage has an Aura with a specific colour. This manifests in most of their spells, and is visible when they connect to the void.\n7. Voidal Connection\nThe Void is not without dangers. A mage too power-hungry, or a mage not careful may easily rip a tear into the void. Similarly, sited or rituals or great spells may leave so-called tears in the void. These can be temporary or\npermanent. A tear is a site where voidal energies freely flow into the Mundane world and influence surroundings in a Chaotikal way.\n8. Mage circles\nMultiple Mages may cast a spell together using circles. Circles however have a chance of ripping a tear into the Mundane.\n9. Mana Gems & Obelisks\nMana may be stored in Mana Gems. Can be used to cast, per say, but to fuel enchantments. Mana Gems have to be regularly re-charged by Mages.\nA Mana Obelisk is a giant Mana Gem with A large amount of Mana. Mana Obelisks are used to mass re-charge Mana Gems without exposing Mages to fatigue.\n-Copied by Ash", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 10", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 10\n\n\"The Yatl Wasteland\"\nIt is now that I trek across the frozen reaches deep in Atlas's interior, just north of the great wall. I have journeyed out from the protection of the Frozen Pines, and now brave the harsh cold. \n\nStorms are frequent here, and I've had to stop multiple\ntimes to avoid becoming hopelessly loss. I’ve steadily travelled east, hoping to find an opening in the wall I might be able to plunge through -- if only for a few days -- before returning, but I’m not sure I’ll get the luck.\n\nUnfortunately, there's very\nlittle in the way of wild game this far south, so I have had to scavenge what I can from exposed brush, and make a heavy dent in my rations. I have added some salt and grinds to my water, in the hope that it will help it to avoid freezing, and it seems\nto be working so far. \n\nI’ve encountered what seems to be a large structure, built directly into the wall itself. What I can only describe as spectres appear to guard it, ghastly men and women with the appearance of soldiers. I tried speaking with a pair\nof them, but they rebuked me. All my attempts to enter the place were denied, the final time with force. Retreat seems the only option for now.\n\nI have at last reached the edge of the eastern seas. It seems the great wall has no openings for me to probe,\nunfortunately. I will follow the coast north, and hopefully collect some fish along the way. Then, I will return to my explorations.\n\nIn the ancient texts I’ve come across, the frozen south was called 'Yatl', but it seems to be a barren place now. I will\ntitle it 'The Yatl Wastelands'.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 11", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 11\n\n\"The East Peak\"\nHere, at the coast, the air is warmer than it was in the interior of the Yatl Wastelands. I suppose the sea must buffet some of the intense cold. I have found a thin coating of ash on the upper branches of some of the trees, and I take it to be ash from\nthe firelands to the west.\n\nHere, this peninsula must be the eastmost part of Atlas. I have scoured the ancient maps quite thoroughly. Though they are somewhat unreliable, and differ from each other substantially, it seems almost certain this is the case.\nI have seen what can only be described as shadow-men among the trees, here. They are not hostile, clothed in robes to hide their burnt forms, but I have spied their flesh in the sun a few times since I first saw them. They are frightening things among the\nashes.\n\nI have tried making contact with them, but they fled from me when I attempted to.\n\nThe relics of the land here reveal it to be a zone obsessed with astronomy and astrology, littered with random markings and ruins within the\nground. I saw a lighthouse, its lamp still flickering, but I dare not explore it.\n\nThis is a strange and haunted place.\n\nI will head west, away from this coast, and deeper into the mainland. Here, the cold breaks, and I can return to warmer\nlands. I will call the peninsula \"The East Peak\", I think. Hopefully, I'll not find a region like it again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 12", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 12\n \n\"Burztorgo Fire Lands\"\nIf there are two words I would use to describe this place, it is ‘horribly hot’. The ground is covered with a thick layer of ash, and the air is choking with volcanic fumes. It almost makes me long for the haunted forests of the East Peak. \n\nThankfully, I\nwas one of the few monks who learned of spirits from our Uruk brothers, so I was not too frightened when I saw their shapes wandering across the lavapits. It seems this area is very close to the orc’s spirit world, for I can think of no other reason why\nspirits would want to wander our world, here.\n\nI have found among the rocks and ash what appears to be a large, abandoned temple. It is badly damaged, but appears to be quite a treasure-trove of history. \nI’ve managed to translate some of the carvings inside, written in a kind of ancient aeldinic tongue. They describe a great empire of humans, called ‘Shirvani’, which ruled over southeastern Atlas, and their brother-people, called the ‘Dakhlani’, who\nexisted as nomads throughout the Atlas interior. It seems both of these peoples originate from ancient settlers, come from Aeldin.\n\nThese carvings describe a history rife with conflict, including that with orcs and elves! This, I will be aware\nof as I investigate other ruins. This temple appears to have been one of the Shirvani’s principles houses of worship, before a period of decline. \n\nThe carvings grow sketchier here, but it seems that all human presence was in Atlas was eliminated by\norcish invasion, save for this temple, and a few other outposts of mankind near the great wall.\n\nThe priesthood eventually faded to nothingness, it seems, without any populace to sustain them.\n\nPer the carvings, this place was called\n\"Burztorgo\". So, I think I will call this place the \"Burztorgo Fire Lands\". And with that, I will depart this place, and head further northwest, closer to the rivers and plains.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 13", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 13\n\n\"Sleetfells\"\nAt last, I am away from the frozen wastes and the fiery hotlands. I’ve entered into a snow-dusted evergreen forest. It is calm here, and the sky is bare of any clouds. The light dazzles off the trees quite majestically.\nThere are some ancient signs of activity, here. A few of the older trees bare ancient markings, but I cannot decipher them myself. Many of them have irrevocably eroded with the passage of time.\n\nOtherwise, the only other archaelogical\nfind of note is what seems to be the burial site of a horse, at the edge of the forest bordering the plains. It is of an impressive size, and seems to have been well-cared for. The creature was buried in full leather hide.\n\nI've managed to acquire a few\nfoxes, which will do well as rations. After partially blocking up the entrance to their den, I began a fire on its top, asphyxiating them. I will be drying their meat. The fur I will use to patch my travelling garb, which has grown quite tattered from all\nthe journeying, particularly my poor boots.\n\nI think I will name this place “Sleetfells”, for the tiny specks of snow that tumble off the trees. For now, I will depart this forest, and ford the northern river to my next destination. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 14", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 14\n\n\"Timberwoods\"\nI’ve dried out most of my clothes on the campfire. I have an abundance of rations, and near this river, I will be able to fish and gather freshwater easily. In the nearby woods I’ve spotted what appears to be a few ruins, so I will spend some time\nexploring before moving on.\n\nDiscovery! I have found a few texts and scrolls, tucked away in a lockbox within an overgrown home. They are decayed, but I will recover as much as I can.\n\nIt seems this place was a town called\nWhiteforest, under the control of a House Forescout. These texts describe 'an evil lurking behind the trees. Wispy and translucent in daylight, they would materialize as twilight died, revealing their silvery silhouettes with glowing emerald\neyes'. I have yet to see any such activity myself, but I will take the precaution of preparing my departure soon.\n\n'A snap of a branch, and all emerald eyes would be drawn to you in an instant. Noise interests them, as they start to drift towards the\nunsuspecting victim. Run, and they start to chase' and furthermore 'Any part of the body that comes into contact with these manias would feel that part of their body shrivel up and wither, scarring the victim with a powerful curse. Only holy arts and gold\npowder are safe against them, though to fight is to draw more attention and eventually lose in attrition.' \n\nThey come to call these mysterious creatures the 'mania'. Those spawning from murders that take place in the wood are apparently drawn to\nblood. The citizenry of Whiteforest attempted to combat the spectres of the wood, but in the end they seem to have failed, and abandoned their home.\n\nI would love to learn more, but we monks are rather risk-adverse. I will be heading out towards\nthe northeast. The name I will give this place is the 'Timberwoods', for the proud timbers that stand tall all throughout the forest. Hopefully, it is not as haunted as these texts say.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 15", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 15\n\n\"Forkwoods\"\nAfter a brief trollop through an open field, I’ve ventured into another group of woods. These are less dense than I found in the Timberlands, and of a different sort of wood. I'll bring back a sample and provide it to the druids -- they will surely be\nable to identify the species.\n\nThe air is tinged, very faintly, with the salt of the eastern sea. I must be drawing closer to the ocean.\n\nSkunks, groundhogs, and some small marsupial species seem common here. Fortunately, I hang\nmy supplies before sleeping, so there is no concern that they will get ahold of any of my rations or water.\n\nLittle evidence of activity is here, though I did find a few weapons embedded in some of the trees. I’ve also encountered what seems to be a\ntablet describing a creature, but it is preserved immaculately, and written in modern tongue. I can only suspect it was carried here by one of our kin, who somehow reached  the land before the monk scouting parties. It reads:\n\"Our god is a wooden thing, born with the forest's blood and heaven's womb. In the shade of his branches we will find our salvation. He will take a seed of oak, spruce, birch, kapok, acacia, black oak, and ash, and plant them together, and grow a new\nworld. Feel the cobbles crumble between your molars.\" They go on to discuss a large wooden creature destroying townships, but I suppose it must be metaphorical for something.\n\nDruids are good for identifying plants, but, some are quite\nterribly mad.\n\nRegardless, I will soon be departing this forest, and heading north into the mountains. I will name this region the 'Forkwoods', as the forest is forked near its center into several smaller regions of wood.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 16", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 16\n\n\"Twin Highlands\"\nThese are not quite as harsh as those in the Huckery Highlands, with a large rising plateau demarcating the space between the two largest peaks. I will hike to its top, and do some mountaineering. I don’t think the Forkwoods will disappear from my sight\nuntil I crest the mountain itself.\n\nThe air is brisk here, cool and devoid of moisture. Updrafts are occasional, but not too strong. Mountain goats are not uncommon, hopping along the rockfaces with amazing precision.\nI’ve come across what seems to be a guidepost, carved into the solid rock of the mountain. There are a few stone tablets which appear to be carved with banking information. This is distinctly dwarven architecture! This marks the first evidence I've seen\nof them in Atlas’s past.\n\nThese seem to be records of a clan of dwarves that split off from the main group in the Chalk Alps, and decided to mine within the mountainous region of the 'Twin Highlands'. The records indicate that resources aplenty were\ndiscovered in these mountains and that the expedition was extended, to terrible consequences. Apparently, this region is home to several creatures of pure crystal, that aggressively defend their home. The records do not indicate what became of the dwarves\nIt only comes to me now, but when I was sleeping on my way up, at night, I did sometimes hear rumbling underneath me... I always thought it must've been an expansive underground river, but perhaps not. \n\nI best depart this place soon. I will use\nthe term the dwarves used for here, and call it the 'Twin Highlands'. I’ll next travel east, into the saltwater swamps, and see what else there is to discover.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 17", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 17\n \n\"East Bight\"\nComing out of the Twin Highlands, I find myself in a wide open plain. I’ve come out of the peaks a bit more north than I was expecting, so I’ll have to head southeast in order to reach the saltwater swamps. For now, I will spend some time picking through\nthese fields. \n\nThere are a few small forests in this region, but it is primarily dominated by expanses of grassland. I’ve given wide berth to herds of horses, which are not too uncommon here, lest I be trampled. There's some ancient evidence of\ncivilization I’ve noticed, mainly in the form of cobbled roads, but it seems like all the buildings have decayed over time. Perhaps these horses are descended from ones brought over by the first settlers.\n\nI've encountered a few different kinds of tubers\nhere. They are universally plump and stout, though vary in color from a bright orange-pink to a deep brown. They have no distinctive smell, but taste bitter, and fare little better when baked. Smelling a bit like rosemary and thyme, I think they might be\nbetter turned into alcohol or potato-bread, but sadly, I lack the supplies to attempt it.\n\nThe forests are mostly low-standing trees. Though boding ill for timber industry, I've managed to snatch a few eggs from nests, which will make a tidy supper.\nAt night, there is little in the way of beasts here, but the night is kept alive by other creatures. The sound of grasshoppers, thrush spiders, and buddles fill the darkness. It actually makes sleeping quite a challenge. \n\nI will name this place 'East\nBight'. According to my maps and the stars, the river to the far west of here should mark the beginning of Mid Bight, so this place being east, it seems only natural.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 18", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 18\n\n\"Swaymoors\"\nFinally, after much travel, I have reached the bogs. The air has become damp and heavy, and I can smell the rich stench of sulfur and rotting plant life oozing up from the swampwater. The clouds hang low in the sky, grey and foreboding. \n\nI would say a storm is brewing, but it is most likely always overcast in this region. The leaves of the mangroves show little green to them.\n\nI’ll do my best to stay out of the water, and travel over land as much as possible as I head towards the coast.\nI’ve chartered (well, asked politely) one of my brother monks to sail me farther out, toward the eastern isles, once I’ve gone through this place.\n\nI’ve found a few collections of stacked rocks, that bare some resemblance to idols of Laklul, the orcish\nspirit of swamps. Perhaps this was one of their sites of worship.\n\nThe bugs are ravenous here. I’ve cloaked up most of my body, despite the humidity, yet they still find ways to get inside and bite at me. If I don’t pass out from the heat,\nI’m certainly going to pass out from bloodloss! The waters seem relatively calm, however, and I’ve only spied a few turtles and small crocodiles. I am lucky enough that I haven’t fallen into t\n\nI spoke too soon. I should not write these journals while\ntravelling. I’m going to have to dry my leggings out on the fire.\n\nFortunately, I am just about there to the coast. As was carved in the Laklul rock-stack, I’ll call this place 'Swaymoors'. Now, to find my brother monk and his boat.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 19", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 19\n\n\"East Downs\"\nI depart off the shores off the Swaymoors, and head out into the ocean, with my new, though to be short-lived, companion: brother Hedaket. A stout and proud fellow, he says he is human, but I suspect there is some dwarven blood hidden in those veins.\n\n\nSkipping a few pages so he doesn’t see that, hah. We’ll be catching the currents south, and wrapping around to reach the isles to the east. The area called the ‘East Downs’, per ancient record, is along the coast here. Unfortunately, I'll not be able to\nexplore it personally, though the stories say that there was once a great city here, which was destroyed.\n\nIndeed, at the bottom of these waters are ruins. In some of the shallower regions, I can see the faint shimmer of stone deep below the surface.\nThe fish here are plump, fat creatures. It’s remarkable they’re able to stay so large! I’ve spent much time fishing off the edge of the boat. I’ve found salmon and carp both. Hedaket has been appreciative of it. In truth, I'll be saving some of these fish\nfor rations in my travels.\n\nFortunately, no storms have slowed our travels. We’ll be at the nearby isles soon. I hear the people called Norlanders are looking to colonize this region.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 20", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 20\n \n\"Coral Peak\"\nI’ve been dropped off on these isles by my temporary companion. He’ll loop around the island by sea, as I trek over it by land. We have a while left to spend together, before he’s back off to Cloud Temple, to continue our effort creating a base camp for\nour colonization.\n\nAs I trudge across the shore, it surprises me we were able to get this far in. The reefs look powerfully sharp, and I can spy the shapes of great fish snaking between the rock. I’m glad we were not prey to the predators swimming below.\nThe trees here are stout, and not very dense. Their sparse canopy snags some of the heaven’s light, but the rest is caught by large ferns and bushes closer to the ground. Streams here are not far enough from the ocean to be freshwater -- to my unfortunate\ndiscovery. I would dig down and test the soil, but I lack the time or tools for such exploration.\n\nI’ve found a small shrine at the isle’s peak, bearing a time-worn statue resembling an amalgamation of woman and squid. It is elven above the waist and\ncreature below. It may be some kind of tribal fetish, but I have found no signs of activity. There is a cave system here, as well, but I will neglect exploring it for now.\n\nI will name this place the 'Coral Peak', for the vast coral walls that rise up\naround the isles here. This was a quick sojourn, as the major isle here was not terribly large. Soon, I will be off to the oceans once more, headed further northeast.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 21", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 21\n\n\"Lobster Isles\"\nThe trip over from Coral Peak was uneventful. Brother Hedaket tells me that, from the shape of the clouds, a storm will be striking this region shortly. He will make camp inland, and safely store our boat. The rains should begin during the night, so I\nwill use most of this day to explore the surrounding isles. The sandbars are shallow enough to connect them, I need only be careful I am not trapped by the tides.\n\nThe wildlife here is much like that of the Coral Peak, though I have found some scatterings\nof ruins. Some show evidence of fire damage and weapon strikes. Others are markedly more overgrown, and seem to have actually been crushed by roots.\n\nI've recovered some carvings in the ruins of one of these homes. It is written in an old variant of\norcish. Though I am unfamiliar with the tongue, Hedaket is able to translate it for me.\n\nApparently, this is the ruins of San'Rahz, a small city once colonized by the orcs. The region was once a bustling area known for the seafood trade, where travellers\nfrom all over the land would dock ships along the island's borders and come to buy fresh shellfish from its occupants. The settlers lived under the influence of a sea spirit named Chinook, who demanded tribute from all who visited the settlement in return\nfor the gift of plentiful food sources. Many tribes made a home on this fairly peaceful land, until another group of orcs (either on the mainland or another nearby isle, the text is unclear) declared war on this region.\n\nMost homes were burned down and\ndestroyed in the midst of the war, some transformed into outposts. The sea spirit was disgruntled by the hostility between the orcs. It was upset over the distraction from its own worship. Chinook released a giant crab which attacked the occupants of the\nisle. As the story goes, the orcs were driven out of the area back to the mainland, except for a group who 'realized the error in their ways and re-established their fervent worship of the spirit'.\n\nThere is no word of what drove off these last orcs, as\nthese isles seem quite abandoned. This ancient war does not explain the overgrowth I've seen on some of the ruins, perhaps it is related to the vanishing of these last pockets of orcs.\n\nThe storm has begun. I will shelter with Hedaket for now.\nI will name this place the 'Lobster Isles', for its crustacean lore.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 22", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 22\n\n\"Jade Peaks\"\nWe've reached another island chain, not too far from the Lobster Isles. There are a series of stone pillars here which stretch high into the sky. It's quite clear someone has gotten here before us, however -- some of these pillars have small monuments and\nstructures constructed on them.\n\nBoth I and Hedaket have tried approaching the largest of these structures, but some sort of powerful energy-field prevented us. We're not sure if it's magic, or something else.\nThe architecture is familiar. We saw much of it back in Axios, in the form of the Hou-Zi ruins. It seems a small group of them came to this land, long ago. We will have to make due with what history has been preserved in the smaller monuments on the\nnearby isles.\n\nAh, here we are!\n\n\"For the next 200 years, the Hou-Zi lived in peace, zealously following the eightfold path. Their diet was vegetarian, they pursued simple professions like baking, weaving, calligraphy, and\npottery. Meditation was encouraged to achieve right view, right thought, right effort, and right mindfulness. It was practiced three times a day, at dusk, at noon, and at dawn, as well as privately in the Hou-Zi's own homes and shrines.\n\nMany of the\nHou-zi who had come over from the old Empire had been soldiers, and their fighter's ways had not left them. They held in them the ancient Hou-Zi art of Chi Manipulation. The healing touch of the way of the moon became immediately popular, as well as the\ndiscipline and fortitude of the turtle and the agility of the crane. These arts spread quickly among the Hou-Zi of the temple, and mastering them became key parts of conquering the eightfold path. Right action, right effort, right livelihood. The way of\nthe crane took slower to adopt, seen as a killing art. Hualian himself issued an edict that its tutelage was permitted, if its masters developed a new style that avoided death and acted in self defense only.\"\n\nThe text, called 'A Chronicle of Hualian' is\nactually written by a human, one 'Darfur Melan'. I suppose he must have been a visitor to this Hou-Zi monastery, and was granted permission to record their history. Still there is nothing in here about the energy-field that is currently stopping us from\nexploring the monastery proper.\n\nWe will have to depart for now, to head toward the mainland. The mysteries of this place must wait until I can call upon the other monks to send an investigation party. Once we reach the shores, I will task Hedaket with\nheading south, back to Cloud Temple, to inform the others of these ruins.\n\nOh, yes, I still need to name this region... I think I will call it the \"Jade Peaks\", for the lovely architecture of the Hou-Zi, constructed on the mighty stone pillars of\nthese isles.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 23", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 23\n\n\"Rep Isles\"\nWe’ve been forced to return to the isles by a storm squall, likely following the storm we had encountered in the Lobster Isles. Hedaket and I have pulled the boat onto land, and taken shelter in a small cave system. I can still just about make out the\nJade Peaks from where we are now, but I suppose I will still explore this place. Perhaps I can give it a name.\n\nThe rain pours steady. I can hear its gentle patter on the rocks outside the cave entrance, dripping down in rivulets. They are a hundred tiny\nrivers, coursing over boulders and down embankments, forming miniature waterfalls and pooling into minuscule ponds. A low mist rises up from its surface.\n\nAh, I wax poetic. Truly, the world is full of phenomenal beauty. Hedaket managed to stoke a fire\nusing some of the of the dry underbrush of the jungle, before it had been fully soaked through by the rain. Quiet fortunate, in fact, as it wards off the snakes and spiders that seem omnipresent in the tall grasses and at the cave mouth.\nThere are a few faded carvings in the walls at the far end of the cavern, but the cave itself only runs maybe ten or so meters deep. There is little other trace of activity.\n\nThe carvings depict what seems to be animals swimming toward the mainland,\nwhere a figure, with open arms, is standing. The figure is humanoid, but seems unnaturally large.\n\nThe rain has started to end. I think I will name this place the ‘Rep Isles’, after the repeating pattern of the falling rain. We will prepare ourselves, and\nthen head back for the mainland, according to the plan I listed in my prior volume.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 24", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torland\n\nVol. 24\n\n\"The Mixed Knolls\"\nHedaket has dropped me off at the shore with my belongings and supplies of water that will hopefully allow me to traverse this desert expanse. There is some life here, at the shore, consisting primarily of trees and low-lying shrubs, but farther inland I\ncan already see the endless sands. Hedaket has offered to come find my dessicated body should I perish here, but I think he means it in jest.\n\nI now trek underneath the harsh sun, toward the mountains in the south. My mouth is parched, but I must\nconserve what little water I have in order to reach the rock. The sand is soft, and difficult to keep footing on when heading downhill.\n\nThere is little sign of old civilization here, but I have seen a few orcish totems. Part of the desert's history\nwas described on them, in pictures and broken Blah.\n\n'The Battle of Enrohk's Valley continued to wage on, brother against brother. The valley quickly grew thick with the stench of death and decay, the sands drowned with a deep red. Among the living\nthat battled on top of the dead it was fairly common for one to rip a bone out of a corpse and use it as a weapon. Once the battle was fought out within Enrohk's Valley, the amount of dead had very much crippled the tribes of Dranor and Cazzo.'\n\nI suppose\nthe villages of these orcs are now submerged under dozens of meters of sand. Maybe an excavation will halp in the future.\n\nI think I will call this place the \"Mixed Knolls\", for the hills of both sand and rock that mark this desert. For now, I head up\ninto the mountains.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 25", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 25\n\n\"Gules Mountains\"\nI’ve finally traversed the desert of the Mixed Knolls and reached the mountains. My watersack is grateful for it, as I’ve found a few small brooks I’ve been able to fill back up on. I’ll head up these mountains and to the south, before wrapping around\ntoward the mesas to the west, and the final quarter of my journey.\n\nThe valleys here are harsh, rockey things, buffeted by years of wind whipped up across the desert. Every once in a while, I will catch in my mouth bits of sand. The air is dry and smells\nof nothing but stone and the ages. Indeed, I can barely hear any birds, and spy only the occasional vulture prowling the sky.\n\nThere are a few faint trails leading through this area, marked by small totems of orcish design. I imagine this is a\ncontinuation of their civilization from the desert. Perhaps they also had settlements on the nearby isles? Judging from what I have already read, it seems this area was colonized by multiple orcish clans.\n\nI have found a few small collapsed buildings in\nthese mountains, possibly a trade outpost or something similar. The deterioration makes it hard to tell.\n\nCircling around the tallest mountain here, I sense something… strange. It is unnerving. Not like the undead I heard stories of, but\nsomething else. I occasionaly found the ancient corpses of orcs, trapped in the rocks, smashed apart.\n\nThere is a spiritual energy, and not the kind that the orcs commonly display. It is difficult to describe, like the death throes of a\ncreature.\n\nI will name this place the 'Gules Mountains', and head further south, toward the plains below. There, I might catch some wild game.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 26", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 26\n\n\"South Downs\"\nIt has been a quick journey down the Gules Mountains, and I am once again at the coast, walking through grassy plains and sections of woodland. This area may be unworked by the hands of the descendants, but it is also calm.\nThe soil here is either grey-brown mud or white. There is much clay in it, and it seems that it would be fairly poor for any settlement. The only tenable areas would be in the forests, and only after cutting and burning the wood to create farmland.\n\nCloser to the coast, I’ve noticed the remains of very old docks, partially submerged. Most of them have been battered to nearly nothing, but some of the more sheltered constructs are partially intact. \n\nI can't tell from what civilization they\noriginate. They are possibly orcish, considering their closeness to the other totems I have found. Oh well. I suppose it will remain a mystery, until someone plumbs the sea’s depths. \n\nFor now, I will head farther west. I will title this place 'South\nDowns', simply for being the downs south of the Gules Mountains. Ah… but I could see it being confusing, given its geographical position. I will need to put thought on this.\n\nOh, and the birds here taste delicious. Very good, roasted with swampgrass.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 27", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 27\n\n\"Golf Isles\"\nI’ve waded offshore, to some of the isles near the South Downs, to briefly inspect them. They are rather small, perhaps the smallest feature of Atlas I have so far explored.\n\nThere is fish aplenty around this area. I have caught three salmon in two\nhours of fishing, as well as a number of other fish -- including an entire squid! The salmon were tremendously large,  much more than what I normally see in this breed. I suppose there must be something about these waters which grants them the chance to\ngrow.\n\nI think I was right about my suspicions of the South Downs having received orcish settlers, as these isles have a few totems constructed on them. They are primarily to Laklul, and assorted spirits of the sea and storms.\nThe totem to storms seems to be damaged, however, in a strange, intentional way. It also seems spiteful, or maybe… impulsive? I’m not sure why an orc would do this. \n\nRegardless, I’ll return to the mainland shortly. I will name these isles the\n'Golf Isles', for the golf in which they can be found.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 28", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 28\n\n\"The Lockmoors\"\nI have left the Golf Isles and returned to the mainland, heading west until I have found a tremendous bog. I'll just skirt its edges -- even now, I have seen serpents slithering through its waters, and the tell-tale bulge of the backs of crocodiles.\n\nI am\nintrepid, but not at all suicidal!\n\nHum, actually, it seems that my discretion has been rewarded. I've found a small tome here, describing what appears to be a brief history of this place. A choice find. I will transfer some of what I learn here.\nIt appears the elves settled here. The swamp is mostly an endless maze of twisted roots, fallen logs, massive reeds, and waterways that can keep one lost forever. However, deep within the murky swamp, the small group of elves learned to adapt and coexist\nwith the creatures there.\n\nOne such creature was a Mani Spirit known as Siss'siri, Prince of Snakes. Siss'siri's presence was felt strongly when he drew near, and one would feel compelled to take heed when he approached. The mani spirit protects his\ndomain fiercely and, more often than not, various travellers would simply vanish with only their empty boats floating along the waterways. The mani spirit was cunning, using the murky, endless waterways as its hunting grounds and disguise from being seen\nuntil it wished to be.\n\nThe text is not clear, but it seems that at some point, this Mani creature was somehow corrupted, or changed. This swamp is not hospitable any longer.\n\nI will call this place the 'Lockmoors', for that is what it\nhappens to be. I now travel north, to the jungles above this bog.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 29", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 29\n\n\"Wonkawoods\"\nAfter leaving the Lockmoors, I’ve head north. This area is dominated with a large luscious rain-rich bowl, nestled on most sides by mountain ranges. I surmise that the clouds become trapped here after arriving on the western winds, and turn to rain,\ncreating a pocket of high wetness. It would explain why this area can be so green next to swathes of desert and mesa.\n\nI’ll spend a short while exploring it, before looping back around to its sole exit. My legs will thank me for not climbing mounts.\nThe area is very dense. Low-lying shrubbery, with almost endlessly variety in leaf pattern, covers the forest floor. The trees tower, creating a canopy that shades the ground from much of the sunlight.\n\nThere is a dominating smell of earthy richness and\nplantlife, with the flowers very pervasive. I suppose they must use this as a way of attracting insects.\n\nThere is little sign of activity here, unfortunately. I have found a few small wood-and-stone fetishes resembling frogs, though not of\norcish design. They are rather old, and seem to depict frogs in various poses. At the bottom of the central lake of this wood, I saw a similar totem glistening deep underwater.\n\nAh, I could’ve sworn I just heard croaking, actually, coming from deep\nunderground, no less. Most curious! I must have frogs on the brain. Oh well. I am sure its nothing.\n\nFor now, I will call this place the 'Wonkawood',  for the croaking creatures I have become fixated on while here, and also for its great trees.\nNow, I circle back around toward the wood’s entrance cum exit, and head north.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 30", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 30\n \n\"Bole Mounds\"\nAfter mapping the Wonkawoods, and gathering the supplies needed for my journey, I’ve headed further north, into the rolling hills and mesa at the furthest tip of Atlas from the great Wall.\n\nThe air is dry here, but it is not quite as barren as it was in\nthe deserts to the east. During dusk, and at the break of dawn, I've seen the wandering forms of dingoes and mountain lions, prowling the distant recresses of the stone. I've managed to snag a few snakes, in fact. They cook surprisingly crispy, and lack\nmuch of the gameiness common in wild beasts.\n\nAt the bottom of the mesa, there are some smaller, low-running rivers. Despite their serving as a water source, I’ve seen few animals use them. Some shrubbery pops up near them, however, but most of it is\nlow-lying hardwoods. To be careful, I will filter and boil it before refilling my waterskins. Hopefully, this will prevent any metals or bad waters from contaminating it.\n\nI’ve seen signs of orcish civilization here, as I have seen throughout much of\nnortheastern Atlas. Some totems and shrines to immortal spirits, mostly. Many of them are badly decayed from age.\n\nUnfortunately, I can divine very little about history from them, except that some catastrophe happened, long ago. Perhaps there is something\ndeep under the sand and rock that could answer me.\n\nRegardless, my journey continues, wrapping around the mesa and back toward the southwest. I think I will call this region the ‘Bole Mounds’, for the great rocky hills that form the majority of the area.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 31", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 31\n\n\"Pridelands\"\nAfter finishing my journeys through the Bole Mounds, I have headed south, into a vast area of sparse plains dotted with towering mountains. \n\nThe plains themselves are unremarkable, filled with tall grasses and stout trees. There are occasional\nwatering-holes which are occupied by birds and hoofed beasts. Although they are too fast for me, I have managed to spear two fowl, which I will eat for dinner. The mountains themselves are more interesting, showing evidence of having been created\nunnaturally.\n\nI have actually found amongst the mountains some ruins of elvish and orcish design. It seems that, for a time, orcs and elves lived here in relative harmony. I will write below what I have learned.\nThe elves, under the leadership of the druii Faelen Ilitris, set up a small hamlet with her students, to worship the local Mani of the area. This was Norra, princess of the panthers. Freygoth,\nhaving received his worship from all those who travelled\nin the area, including the elves, was not disturbed by the presence of Norra. In fact, it was said that the two became good companions, with the shamans gleefully retelling the adventures of the White Ape and the Black Panther, investigating disturbances\nwithin their perfect domain. This land was their pride and joy, and was so aptly named the ‘Pridelands’.\n\nAt least this means naming this place will not be a challenge.\n\nStill, there are later stories which suggest the mani and spirits here came to blows,\nafter the mountains were formed. Then, there is only written a brief word about bewaring quaking aspens, but I’ve seen no such trees here. Apparently, they are terrible to build houses near.\n\nRegardless, it is time to move on. I now head further\nsouthwest.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 32", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 32\n\n\"Spring Hills\"\nJourneying out of the Pridelands, I have at last returned to the green fields and pastures of Atlas. This area we actually have some records on, which have survived among the repositories of the merchant guilds of the world. The archmonk wisely purchased\na tome detailing this history of this area, calling it the ‘Spring Hills’.\n\n\"Dotted with pockets of small creeks, steaming with abundant vitality, the Spring Hills have traditionally been known as a land of healing to any who ever soaked in one of its\nrejuvenative pools. The favourite resting spot of the Spirit Akezo, it was rumoured that he personally took a dip in one of its many creeks after a long travel. When Akezo relaxed, he accidentally let slip of his powers, causing the water to heat up from\nabsorbing all that spiritual power, several of the larger creeks bursting from its confines, joining together to form several rivers running through the land. From that day forth, Spring Hills became a place known to heal wounds, and to meditate and\npractice healing.\n\nOne day, a beautiful yet blind maiden ventured into one of Spring Hill’s more secluded creeks, spotting the blonde Avatar of Akezo, known as Lekeni in his travels. Lekeni, feeling sorry for the maiden, promised to restore her\nsight if she was to come bath in this pool every day for a hundred days, as long as she would remain sincere as she is currently without her sight. The maiden agreed instantly, and made the pilgrimage every single day without fail.\nShe would bathe dutifully in its waters, occasionally conversing with Lekeni if he happened to be present. Lekeni grew fond of the maiden, and started being careless about his forms, choosing to appear however he wished as she could not see.\nUpon the maiden’s hundredth day, whilst in his winged serpent form, he dutifully restored her sight. The maiden excitedly opened her large round eyes for the first time since birth, only to be greeted by the strange and somewhat horrifying creature,\nletting loose a scream.\n\nDispleased by her lack of gratitude, he promptly turned her into a rabbit, the only feature left from her mortal form being her now wide eyes.\"\n\nThank goodness I am no beautiful maiden.\nBut that is all I will write here, for now. I have restocked my supplies on wild berries and springwater, and will be heading further on towards the nearby forests.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 33", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 33\n\n\"Serpentwoods\"\nThese woods are only a short trip away from the Spring Hills. I am close to Cloud Temple, and my brothers, once again, but my journey is not yet over. Really, I’ve begun to long for those sandstone halls, the stern gazes of the archmonks, and the dusty\ntomes of our grand library, but I will be able to return there soon.\n\nA serene, almost tranquil beauty can be seen between the oaks and birches of this place. I’ve seen what seems like thousands of moths fluttering through these woods,\nmaking home here with shimmering particles of dust that seem to dance within the air, especially when the moonlight shines down upon the woods. \n\nAs luck would have it, I’ve also found what seems to be a ruined elven temple, judging from\nthe construction and iconography. It’s severely overgrown, and there are a few elvish skeletons about, but there are records here which indicate what happened in the region.\n\nApparently, the elves resting here were once priestesses that\nsettled here to study and even worship the moths and forest with what it had to offer. The moths, which were dubbed Velulaei or ‘moon moths’, have an almost mesmerizing effect when the moonlight would cast down upon them. The dust-like silk they produce\nhas several properties, including a minor sleeping or paralyzing effect. \n\nApparently, things were ideal until a particular intense breeding cycle, which toxified the air and caused the priestesses here to collapse into an irreversible sleep.\nFrom there, it seems only natural that they would’ve starved to death in their sleep. Though, there is something inconsistent with that story: a few of the corpses show evidence of blunt force trauma, like they were smashed with heavy wooden objects.\nI cannot determine if these injuries were inflicted before or after the priestesses were put to sleep.\n\nI will call this place the ‘Serpentwoods’, as it is written. Why it is named that, I will never know.\nMaybe Mothwoods might be better… Regardless, I must now head west, wrapping around the Spring Hills to enter into another section of wood. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 34", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 34\n\n\"Loftywoods\"\nI have wrapped around the bottom of the Spring Hills to reach this heavily wooded region. The canopy rises high above, cascading rays of light falling down all around me, it’s rather magical. \n\nHowever, this place is not just magical in its beauty, for\nsome very real power permeates this place. There is an unusually large number of fae creatures here -- I have seen more than one sprite, and even a cervitaur, prowling the depths of this wood. Fortunately, none have attacked me outright.\n\nI have been careful enough to limit my provisioning to the plants, scrounging for berries and tubers in the soil. This place is rich with life, and this doesn’t put too much a strain on my resources. Some might say I am being a bit too cautious, but I\nwould rather not be torn apart by a beast under the sway of the Aspects.\n\nThis place is truly lush. I have seen the most miraculous displays of nature which I cannot describe in words.\n\nAs I saw in the deep south, I have seen\nhere what seems to be a giant footprint, pressed deep into the rock and soil. There may in fact be traces of these throughout all of Atlas, worn away by time… perhaps they are the sign of some cult, or religious group, which once travelled this land.\nThe name I shall give this place will be 'Loftywoods', for the way the canopy shudders in the breeze above me. Fortunately, some of my brother monks already travelled here, and have prepared some rough canoes for travelling to the nearby isles.\nI will take one, and then isle-hop back down toward the mainland.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 35", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 35\n\n\"Hiisht Isles\"\nThe canoe was rough. My brothers are not very talented in the art of shipbuilding, it seems. At least, my bottom is chafing from the trip over.\n\nPerhaps I am being rude, and the seas were merely rough. Oh well. I have left the Loftywoods behind, and now\ntravel to the most northwesternly isles in all of Atlas.\n\nThe volcanic soil here is rich, and thick grasses rise up all around this island. There is little in the way of tall trees, so I suppose a recent eruption may have wiped them out.\nThere is some evidence of colonization here, by dark elves no less. I've identified small ruins which are indicative of their civilization, but little has come in the way of text. It seems this area may have already been razed in the past, by war.\nIt seems that the dark elven spirit may still haunt this place. I am growing increasingly wary. I will speed up my departure.\n\nI will name this place the Hiisht isles, for the hissing lava at the height of the largest isle's volcano. Thankfully, it is not\nactively erupting, so I am quite safe for the moment. I've gathered up some of the water from small pools forming farther in on the island, and boiled it. This will supply my trip as I journey farther south, along this island chain.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 36", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 36\n\n\"Tide Isles\"\nFarther south of the Hiisht isles, I have come to find an archipelago of tropical islands  covered in palm trees and exotic plants, lemurs and mud crabs.  \nHere is truly a beautiful tropical collection of islands, each hosting their own exotic creatures.\nI’ve seen multiple species of birds and crustacean, as well as other rarer creatures.\n\nThe crabs here taste splendid, with a meaty, almost gamey flavor more typical of wild boar or muskrat compared to seafood. They seem to venture in through the tides\nsettle along the beaches, creating small displays of colorful rocks and kelp. Perhaps it is some kind of mating ritual.\n\nThis place, like the land-locked Loftywoods, seems to be connected very strongly to the Aspects. I\nhave seen the typical markings of satyroi and cervitoi, and the curious nests and constructions of their kind. It seems that, perhaps regularly, they must journey to these islands. However, I am at a loss to where they come from or go. I've seen no signs\nof them being here, currently, but the markings are not so faded as to be from ancient times.\n\nPerhaps there is some regular convergence event here. Interesting.\n\nI will name this place the Tide Isles, for the waters rise up onto\nshores, how beautiful it looks under the moonlight. Now, I return to the mainland, for the last place I will be exploring anew.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 37", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 37\n\n\"West Peak\"\nAfter leaving the Tide Isles and venturing across the ocean, I have left my makeshift canoe by the shore of the mainland.\n\nThis land is dotted with chalk outcroppings and is covered with rocky shores, a sharp contrast to the more tropical isles near\nhere. However, I’ve found a few ruins of settlements, dwarven in nature, just on the coast. It seems this area served as a small trade outpost of sorts, before being abandoned.\n\nOn a small isle, which I have not yet ventured to, is a lighthouse ruin\nsitting ominous out on the ocean. It has sturdy and stout construction, but seems to be in a state of powerful disrepair. I will not travel out to it myself, but perhaps some adventurous soul might.\n\nThis place was settled by those who sought to expand\n\"Arakh'Vath\", a dwarven settlement.\n\nI will call this place the 'West Peak', for being the westernmost part of all of Atlas. Now, I will make the trek back to Cloud Temple, and deliver a summary of my reports to the\nArchmonk.\n\nThen, I think I will finally travel south, with a detachment of my brother monks. We will travel beyond the Great Wall, and discover what lies within the distant south.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 38", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 38\n\n\"The Great Wall\"\nOh, here we are again. The ice rises before me, mysterious and glistening.\n\nI've felt this thing calling me ever since I first laid eyes upon it, back when I was travelling south to scout out this land.\n\nThe... ghosts, spectres?\nWhatever those things are, we’ve not approached them yet. We’ve brought gear to try scaling the wall, if negotiations to get passed it break down.\n\nI hope it doesn’t come to that, I’m a poor climber.\n\nBut we’ll see. The world beyond that wall,\nI’ll see it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 4", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 4\n\n\"Wilderlands\"\nThe Chalk Alps were taxing to my supplies. I had already suspected I would have to, but now I know I will have to spend some time foraging. The plains I have entered do not seem like a bounty, however.\n\nThe trees are sparse, and do not bare\nmuch in the way of fruit. Instead, there is an expanse of rock with little vegetation. The winds blow brisk from the west, tinged with the distant smell of the sea. I must be getting close to the coast. This area of Atlas might seem unremarkable at first\nglance, but I have found a few interesting trinkets scattered on the bluffs. Bits of wooden trays and shattered pottery, rusted hoes and scythes, small bags of coinage, and even some scattered skeletal remains. \n\nI have decided to name this place the\n\"Wilderlands\", as this place was clearly once home to a people, but they have been now replaced by only beasts. Ironically, this was actually one of the few places where the wildlife was passive. Perhaps this can be owed to the short time I spent here.\nI have headed toward the southwest, where a few isles rest just offshore. A few sandbars extend out toward them. I will make camp near the shore, and wait for low tide tomorrow. Hopefully, a path will present itself, and I will not be obliged to craft a\nboat.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 5", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 5\n\n\"The Shrieking Drake Island\"\nThe first isle in this chain I could reach just by navigating the inlets at low-tide, but I’ve had to assemble a crude raft to reach this most distant island. It has buffeted most of the waves, so I have not had any trouble with the sea. \nI’ve kept largely to the outer reaches of this island. I’ve noticed quite a few large beasts prowling the inner reaches, perhaps even a titan, and I’d rather not stirr their ire. I’ve encountered a few sites of stone carvings at bluffs near the shore.\nThey seem to indicate there should be a large altar somewhere on this island -- they show depictions of the island with the altar near its center -- but I have yet to see any trace of the altar itself. I would have to return, more prepared, in order to\nnavigate the beasts and investigate the possibility of this altar being somewhere near the island’s core. For now, I can safely return to the mainland.\n\nThe hills and plateaus of this island are harsh and unforgiving, and so I have chosen to name this\nplace… was that the cry of a wyvern I just heard? Hmm. I must paddle a little faster. I think I will call this place “The Shrieking Drake Island!”\n\nI will be next heading toward the southeast. When I was in the reaches of the Wilderlands, I saw a\nheavy forest toward the south I would like to explore.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 6", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 6\n\n\"The Evergreen\"\nThe raft I assembled to take me to-and-from Shrieking Drake Island, has, much to my robe’s chagrin, been broken apart on the rocks of the shallows. My shins are worse for wear, but I am otherwise fine.\n\nI’ve headed further inland, to south\nof the Wilderlands. I’ll make camp in these woods for the night, as I’ve seen very little in the way of monsters among the pines. More importantly, I have found the ruins of a stone altar, with a ring of mushrooms around it. Resting on the altar was a\ntablet, which I have made some progress deciphering. It appears to be druidic in nature, but the sigils on it are not written in any language I am familiar with. It has several depictions of an ent, towering over forests and mountains,\nwith a rather unpleasant look on its face.\n\n\"The Evergreen\" will be a suitable name for these woods. Its ancient trees tower into the sky, full of vibrant green needles that glisten, even when the whip of cold winds from the south feels like it might\nfreeze everything.\n\nI will be next heading toward the east, taking a brief detour from my route leading into the frigid wastes. There are some rolling hills and stout mounts I’d like to explore, before I find myself freezing to death.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 7", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 7\n\n\"Huckery Highlands\"\nI can feel the stringy muscles in my legs tingle from effort, but over the last few days of travel I have gotten used to it. Breathing more deeply, my back straighter -- perhaps it is right what they say about outdoor activity having a positive effect on\none's health.\n\nThese hills are awkward for travel, and not pleasant to the eyes as the great mountains are. Most of the rock is bare, with little plantlife, and I've taken to wrapping my hands to aid in the climb. I will make for the highest peak and map\nthe immediate area, and then head back down.\n\nI've collected a few trinkets from among the rocks. They seem to have some latent magic in them. Indeed, there are what appears to be some ancient collapsed foundations built directly into the hills, but\nthey've all been too badly decayed for me to piece together what might've happened. Scorch marks, so maybe a great fire from the nearby forests spread here, or some sort of magical accident. I have found a few damaged texts which seem to refer to a\n'seeking flame', so it may even be a combination of these things.\n\nOn account of the stout hills in this part of Atlas, I have decided to name this place the \"Huckery Highlands\". The view is quaint and all, but there are more impressive places\nelsewhere in this land.\n\nI will next be heading toward the south, after taking a brief stop at the southern rivers to fish and refill my water supplies. When I was on the hilltops I could see the great wall, looming in the distance, almost calling to me.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 8", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n\nVol. 8\n\n\"The Barrow Downs\"\nThe Huckery Highlands fall behind me, their rolling hills and rocky rises giving way to dense pine forests.\nI have already made my way west, and I am following a river. I had quite the luck, catching a few trout that will serve to supplement my rations.\nOnce the river curves north, I’ll search for a suitably narrow region to begin my crossing.\n\nCuriously, I spied for the first time a moose since I began exploring these forests. I had begun to think they had no presence in Atlas, but it must merely be\nthat they are isolated to more southern regions. It seemed to be a bull, as I had to give it a wide berth to avoid it snorting at me. \nOnly now I am scribing this down, but I have found what seems to be a crypt, built into a hollow beneath a tree. Much of\nit has been badly overgrown and irreparably eroded by the passage of time, but I have found carved into the stone a few glyphs and what appear to be mythological illustrations. On one, for example, a large humanoid creature, with trees growing\nfrom its body, is shown scaling the mighty wall. I will be recording the gylphics so that I can attempt to decipher them back at Cloud Temple.\n\nI think I will call this place “The Barrow Downs”, on account of the tombs I have found here. Truly, this\nan ancient and haunted place, where even the trees are unwelcoming of newcomers. I pray Rellenia finds time to comfort this place.\n\nFrozen forest is next on my list of things to explore, but I do not look forward to it. The south is a frigid waste.\nHowever, I will brave it all the same, as that wall still calls for me.", ""),
        StaticBook("Volume 9", "Monk Torlan", "The Explorations of Monk Torlan\n \nVol. 9\n\n\"The Frozen Pines\"\nNaming this place will be easy. These old frosty woods leave little room to interpret them as anything else! Indeed, I’ll call this place \"The Frozen Pines\". \n\nI’ve left the Barrow Downs in the north, and now trudge through deep snows and across frozen\nlakes. The air is clear and clean here, all the moisture turned to ice. It feels as if I can see for as far as I was able to on the mountaintops.\n\nBy my luck, I have managed to catch a caribou. I was able to frighten it off a high sloping embarkement, and\nit was gravely wounded in the fall. After putting it out of its misery, I’ve begun butchering the corpse. I’ll make camp and produce jerky from its meat, drying it out by the heat of the fire. Unfortunately, I haven’t found many herbs, so I'll be\npreparing it only with sea salt and smoke. I’ll save the leather for Cloud Temple.\n\nI have found what seems to be some large footprints in the rocks and soil, headed south, a few meters in diameter. It appears that they were man-made, with either wooden\nor stone tools, as their edges look too sharp and unusual to be of any living creature.\n\nLooking out from the forest’s edge, I see only wastes before me. I have read in legends that this place was once called Yatl. Now it seems to be nothing but snow.", ""),
        StaticBook("vote", "Klauss Rotgreiff", "Horrace Thassarion\n**the name was scribbled in**", ""),
        StaticBook("Vote", "Bertholt Kühn", "Gorrace Thassarion", ""),
        StaticBook("Vote", "Ser Magnus Silversteed", "Ellie Undertree", ""),
        StaticBook("Vote", "Khazara Tyranor", "Ellie Undertree", ""),
        StaticBook("Vote FALK!", "Falk Rottencock of Skelmersdale", "T H E   G O L D\n\n    Y A H U D I S T\n\n    M A N I F E S T O\n\n-------------------\n\n    Falk Geldmeister\n\n         1 6 4 4\nPREFACE\n\nThroughout civilized antiquity, men- when faced with the pressures of an election -falsely promise what the masses wish to hear in hopes of swaying them to cast their vote in the hypothetical man’s favour.\nTo these men who offer these false promises, I ask “at what cost?”\nFor as per the principles of alchemical equilibrium, both halves must be balanced- you cannot have one force without the counter-acting,\nor the equation will fall flat. A city is the same in this respect: lumber does not fell itself, stone does not find itself quarried without any infringing labour, and workers do not work unless payed!\n\nAt the behest of the principles of\nmercantilism and my associates alike, a climate can be forged that finds balance between you the people and businesses who wish to settle amongst the Haenseti, whether domestic or not. \nTHE GUARD\n\nThe issue of the guard is non-existant, though I commend Hademar for his thoughtfulness. Simply put, the instance of Jan Kovachev was one lone amongst the many: a tragedy no less, but a singular tragedy. Rarely do high ranking\nmen fall with their retinue present, lest this retinue was incompetent in the actions of administering orders, coordination, martial art or otherwise.\n\nExpand the current guard to the figures (two-fold) presented by the Right Honorabe Hademar and far too\nmany men are left toting power that should simply not be toted by some men. Moreover, a state with these men presents calls with opens arms for a corrupted police state to be bred- thereby inhibting growth through advancement. And this has been seen\nthroughout our history; through the likes of the White Rose and the Felsen Guard.\n\nInstead we should focus on the training of those who currently bolster the ranks of the guard; breed within their hearts zeal, within their conscience the ability to\nuncannily wield a weapon, and within their heart, love for their nation. On their backs we shall place equipment of a high standard - not the dregs of the old Haensetian military - from the domestic and monetarily uninhibted Haensetian smiths.\nAdmittedly, though, in recent times the roads have become littered with lowlife scum. To combat such, and to allow free passage to traders, a specialized patrol of hardy veterans would, under my administration, care for these roads and you: the denizens\nwho traverse them.\nTRADE AND TAXATION\n\nMen wishing to trade will not be impeded by crushing corporate tax figures, such as those present in Adelburg, but instead will pay a reasonable one off price of between 50 and 100 Minas. These numbers will not be aggregate\nthough it should be noted that native Haensetians will be permitted the smaller value, and perhaps outsiders a larger figure for the liberty to trade within the prosperous Haenseti. \n\nThrough mercantilism the Haenseti people will collectively assume\nwealth from the minerals that are collectively YOURS! As such with the lumber that sprawls vast. But, it shall not be felled to oblivion like corporate maniacs of yore; we shall follow the principles of my great-great grand father, who said wisely that\nyou should take only what you need, enough to support ourselves and no more.\n\nHOUSING\n\nHousing shall follow a similar principle under my administration: each home will be valued and sold at a set cheap prices depending on the\nrespective size of the home.\n\nThis is how it is currently is in Haense- you needn't be afeared for the status of such so long as you vote for my administration.\nCONCLUSION\n\nA wise man once said that chess was a kingly game- but why play when you are no king? Whilst the kings upon their high thrones dabble amongst themselves, you, under my administration, shan't worry for potential folly,\nthat come as a result of their board games. No, for within your purse will be coin; and you will go to sleep with a full belly, against your wall will be a weapon to defend your ilk, and above your head will be a roof; thatched together\nnot with unfullfillable and unjusitfiable promises, but with stability. Your house will be stable, just as you expect it to be, and so will my oversight.\n-------------------\n    VOTE FALK FOR\n           MAER\n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Voting", "Hans Schulc", "Horrace Thassarion", ""),
        StaticBook("Vouch", "Nerio Grandaxe", "Ah believe t'at Thorlond Frostbeard deserves tah learn teh art o' runesmithin'.\nAh t'ink 'e is trustwortheh 'n' ah will vouch for 'im. T'ank ye!\n-Nerio", ""),
        StaticBook("Waldenian Schism", "Commodus de Jedih", "dddddddddddddddddddThe Formal Declaration of Schism of the so-called “Church of the Canon” and its so-called High Pontiff Daniel\nkssssssssssssssssss\n\nIssued by Konig Arn von Aesterwald\n\nTranscribed by Comm.\n1664\nNOTE: The following is a controversial piece and is transcribed for historical purposes to document the first well known schism from the Church of Canon.\nkkddddddddddddddddd\nO, Spirit of the Creator, teach us how to pray. Lord of all and Creator of all lands of the world, under the world, and above the world, we give You thanks for Your glory, blessings, and justice. We further petition Your Majesty,-\nSovereign King of all Creation, to vest in us the mantle of truth, to pardon our transgressions, and guide us in the way of Your blinding light. We ask all of this in Your holy name. Amen.\nGreetings and Apostolic Benediction to all who read these words and worship to the Most High! It is with great sorrow and regret that We, a humble servant of the Lord Gott, feel compelled and inspired to separate-\nand cease all ecclesiastical fealty to the so-called High Pontiff. This separation weighs heavy on Our heart as we take witness to the grave error on the part of the Canonist faithful and hierarchy.\nFurthermore, We utterly denounce and refuse any recognition of Daniel, the pretender to the Pontifical throne. We feel this is certainly warranted and is needed for Our faith to survive as true and as the Creator intended.\nIn this event, it is what the Faithful does not see, rather than what they do. They do not see the ecclesiastical visibility that was so beloved under the reigns of Pius II or Paul III. It is then that we declare the latter of those-\ntwo the last true High Pontiff. Any claimant after the deposition of His Holiness is not recognized. We therefore clarify for the Faithful the potential error that could exist in each and any ecclesiastical decision or decree after the time-\nof the dissolution of the Papacy.\n\nWe see with disgust and fury the ruthless actions being made against the Waldenian people by clergy of the Canonist faith. These heathens, though in error of faith,-\nwere purged by holy fire by the Bishop of the See of Vanderfell and was allowed to continue unchecked. It is then with equal sorrow as to before that the Cathedral Church of St. Godfrey and the Diocese therein is -\nto be placed solely under the jurisdiction of the Crown and shall be henceforth the “Royal Cathedral”.\n\nWe witness with curiosity the purported High Pontiff’s support for banditry and his silence on the molesting of travelers in and -\naround Vanderfell. The state of war that exists between the Crowns of humanity has been responded with silence by the heretic, Daniel. This silence is damaging and scandalous for any man claiming holiness.\nWe recognize and affirm the title held by Our father; that title being “Apostolic”. We recall to mind the reign of Pius II: The only Lucienist to ascend the highest throne on the World. It is crucial to understand that the True Faith in Aesterwald-\nwill be founded on the principles taught by High Pontiff Pius II. Notably, propagating the faith in an ecumenical though firm manner, Harmonious existence with the crown, cultural advancement, and most importantly: the salvation of souls.\nIt can be clearly seen that some subjects in the Konigreich are not entirely in the fold of the light of the Creator. However, as the salvation of souls are of highest importance, We steadfastly believe that the soul must be nurtured and fostered-\nin growth, as children must be given time to learn, and a heretic must be taught, not purged by holy fire. It can be correctly noted that when an arm is infected it must be severed from the body lest the body dies,-\nthis amputation is the last stage of curing the disease. Remember, brothers, that when a heresy infects an arm, the heretic must be severed from the body of the faithful only after attempts have been made to correct this disease.\nTherefore, We feel it Our royal duty and commission to provide the Faithful with zealous and likewise faithful clergy. The sheep will be led astray with the current shepherds. We will appoint, to Our Court, holy men.\nWe implore those who believe in True Faith and the rupture that is apparent in the Canonist heresy to join with the Crown of Aesterwald in worshipping the Creator.\nIn sum, we declare the Church of the Canon, its hierarchy, and its leader anathema. We seize all church properties in the Konigreich for the Crown. We declare the High Pontificate vacant since Paul III.\nMay the Creator grant us strength in these coming days and growth in the years ahead.\n\nKonig Arn von Aesterwald\n\nHermann Lutzow, Consecrated Brother", ""),
        StaticBook("War Nation Clans", "Nazark'Gorkil", "The Clans of The History of The War Nation.\n===================This book will give a brief description of the many clans of Orcish history.\n\n\n\n===================Dedicated to Theruz.\nGorkil-\nOne of the oldest clans in existence. This clan is notable for their self inflicted scars and their vulnerabilty to the curse of Krug. Bloodlust. Their symbol and mount are boars while their colors are black and crimson.\nLur-\nOne of the most Ancient clans to exist sprouting from the common Ancestor of the Clan, Lur. This clan is most notable in their features of Taming and Hunting. Their symbol is of a Lur Wolf. The colors consist of Blue and White from Lur Legends.\nBraduk- A clan of giants diescended from Braduk and immortalized through the actions of heroes such as Kharneth. The Braduks are a race of giants descended from the blood of ologs. They ride rhinos bred in the harsh deserts and record history.\nDom- Descendants of the fourth born child of Krug, this clan bears a notorious history of dark shamanism, combativeness, and deceit. Notable for their ashen skin tones and ritualistic practices, the Orcs of Dom are equally admired and feared.\nLak- A barbarous band of blue-hued zealots in pursuit of usurping the spirit of nature, Freygoth, with their patron: Laklul, spirit of wetlands. Often they reside in marshes and swamps, their mount/symbol is the toalak, a toad. Formed from Doms in Athera.\nRaguk- The ubroken of Clan RAGUK, a notoriously merciless band of behemoth, hardy uruks. Their hide is stained with a deep Red hue,much akin to the blood that smears their palms.Their bloodlust and militaristic prowess grants them a bloody reputation.\nRax- Named after Krug's first son, Rax, these red to pink skinned orcs are of a long deceased clan. The clan fell from it's height when Mogrocka'Gorkill slayed their leader. Their cubs were taught to fight by shamans from a young age.\nKaxil- A long since \nwhitewashed clan that\nwas once canabilistic is now dead. They were dishonorable and helped to antaginize many clan wars. In time the Kaxil clan became members of an undead legion and enemies of the War Nation.\nYar- This clan was founded in Anthos by Malog'Yar. Valuing the pursuit of wisdom and doing the will of the spirits is what the clan is primarily known for, though this doesn't stop them from follwoing their brothers into war. Sigil is a desert tortoise.\nRam- Large orcs who are dark of skin and have horns protruding from their head. They decided to embrace and cultivate their bloodlust but were soon corrupted by dark shamans. The clan was whitewashed and most of them were hunted down and killed.\nAzog- A vicious clan of orcs that are notorious for their ability to appear in the War Nation out of no where and quickly start a clan war. They are favored allies and feared enemies who bare the symbol of a hammer on their banners.\nUgluk- A clan descending from Gorkil and most well known for being lead by Pok'Ugluk is long since abandoned. They have a yellow bull for their sigil and ride bulls as their mount. Similar to their father clan they participate in scarrification.\nFud- A long since abandoned clan of Cave Ologs who are devoted to Glutroz. They are led by a Grubgoth who, throughout the majority of the clans existence, was a red olog named Wud.\nUblu- A goblin clan birthed in Athera. Their leader, Deka'Ublu, attempted to unite all goblins under one bloc. The movement had little traction and the clan slowly faded from the face of Krugmar.\nBork- \nA clan of highly loyal Orcs who were welcoming to all outsiders and gave no mind to who was in their ranks. The clan is dead.\nBaxle- A clan of goblins formed by the shaman Fizldank. It was humble and had to lease land from clan Lak. With the disappearing of Fizldank, the new leadership insulted and attacked clan Lak, resulting in their systematic extermination in a clan war.\nTuk- An ancient goblin clan which saw revival in Vailor. They worshipped Tukki, an ancestral spirit who willed for goblin unification. They were exterminated in a clan war with Lak after they had an openly queer pacifist became their warchief.", ""),
        StaticBook("Warding", "The Unknown Sorcerer", "Transfiguration; The Utility magic\n\nBook Three - Warding\n\nWritten by an unknown mage and translated by Sarrion Zytiaear.\nWarding & Abjuration:\n\nAnother ability of the alterationist: Being able to confuse the mana of a spell of an arcane magic the alterationist understands, sometimes into the void and other times simply resisting the spell.\nThese spells can weaken, or simply dispel. Visible in the title, this ‘mana confusion’ is broken into two types: Warding and Abjuration.\nWarding: A stationary ‘shield’. While it cannot protect against physical harm such as swords and crossbow bolts, magic falls prey to these wards. Warding takes some time to create, making it less of a combative spell than its brother Abjuration.\nThese wards are unable to fully cancel out magics, rather only able to weaken and resist their influence.\nAbjuration: A counterspell; however only towards one magic, rather than multiple. This is a quick shot, taking two to three actions to cast. It can cancel out arcane spells scaling by each tier,\nbut anything other than that will fall to the same system as Warding. It can be shot as a transparent bolt or be used as a close range pulsation on an area.\nHow they function:\n\n \n\nThese spell(s) create a clump, or net of mana that will hit a spell or magic head on. Once so, the programmed ward or abjuration, having its mana given the knowledge of the spell/magic\nin question, will try to entwine with the spell and become one with it with as much mana as required, and by doing so -- the spell gets confused, knowing naught what to do -- the spell gets sent back to the void,\nweakened, or manipulated. This is generally done by having a greater amount of clumped mana than the other spell, meaning that some spells might be enough\nto tear through wards and abjuration like a hit knife through butter. As stated previously, to ward or abjure something, the caster will require fairly good knowledge, if not very in-depth knowledge of the magic.\nThis only works on Voidal Magic though, lest new situations and abilities unfold themselves.\n\n \n\nAs the alterationist becomes more knowledgeable and progress in Warding/Abjuration,\nthe user will be able to weaken and cancel out magics of a higher spell capacity. This tool is useful, especially with dampening a perhaps fatal spell. Yet, there is always more to learn.\nReflective Wards/Abjurations\n\n \n\nEnter the reflective side of things. Similar to how standard wards and abjurations entwine with the spell, this effectively\ndoes the same - but instead of sending it back to nothingness or weakening the spell, it will seek to override the spell's mana and send it elsewhere. Doing this will have the caster temporarily\ntake control of the spell's active mana, having the victim spell's cost eat at their mana and leaving only enough time to divert the spell, actually hitting spells back will be highly unlikely -\nas one needs to account for the amount of mana it takes to override and hijack.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Warhawke Maids", "Masked Man", "Busty\n        Warhawke\n          Maids\n\n      First Edition\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\n\n            [!]\nA scantily clad dark elf woman would be drawn on the cover.\n[!]\nThe pages would be filled with many drawings of female Dark Elves dressed in various maid outfits.\nThe back of the book being taken up by a short story.\n/('-')\\\n           O[]O\n           /_\\\n            |  |\n\"Oh! master you've returned!\"\n\"My King, I have come to clean your chamber\" I said to the closed door.\n\n\"Yes, I have been waiting\" I heard him say through the door. \n\nI let out a sigh of relief, I wasnt late after all. I entered the room looking around.\nPapers were all over the floor near his desk. 'perhaps his duties were stressing him out' I thought to myself.\n\nHe smiled to me as I entered \"Ah, its you Aiela\" he spoke softly as he sat at the edge of his bed.\nI gave a small bow to him, turning to tend to my work.\n\nI had just arranged his desk when I noticed he was still looking at me.\n\n\"Is something wrong my king?\" I said hesitantly.\nHe let out the light heartfelt chuckle he always did when something amused him.\n\n\"Nay a thing Aiela, just appriciate all the work you do for me.\"\n\nI felt my cheeks warm slightly, turning to focus back on my work.\n\"I assure you its nothing at all, simply my job.\" I said as I turned to tidy up his dresser.\n\nI heard a slight movement from behind me, turning to peer over my shoulder.\n\nI watched as he rose from his bed.\n\"Why do you do this job?\" he questioned as he came closer to me.\n\n\"It is my duty to serve the royal family\" I softly as I turned back to my work.\n\n\"To serve?\" he repeated.\n\"Yes sir, my life belongs to this family and I will spend it in service to it.\" I said plainly, having repeated the words many times.\n\nI felt him draw closer, my attention to my duties slowing.\n\n\"Sir?\" I said, turning.\nHe was standing before me, the dim candlelight illuminating his face. His deep blue eyes as deep as the ocean beneath his black hair, darker then the nights sky.\n\n\"You've been a great help to me all these years, Aiela\" he spoke softly.\nI looked up to him, backing into the dresser.\n\n\"Th-thank you for your praise my King\" I spoke softly, my cheeks beginning to burn passionately.\n\nHe smiled \"You've always stuck closer to me then the other maids.\"\n\"I just want to help you, my King\" I spoke, almost in a whisper.\n\nI was unable to look him in the eye, casting my focus to the floor. I felt his hand cup my chin, bringing my gaze to his eyes.\n\n\"I thank you for that, Aiela\" he said, as he came closer.\nI felt my heart flutter as he stood pressed against me.\n\n\"m-my king\" I said one more time, his fingers guiding my face to his.\n\nEnd of Story 5\nAct 1\n\n-Anonymous Fan", ""),
        StaticBook("Warrior's Morals", "Rex Shakul'Gorkil", "-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nA WARRIOR'S MORALS\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n\n\n\n\nBy Rex Shakul'Gorkil\n\nScribed by Skribbl'Gorkil\nMORAL 1: WEAKNESS.\n-------------------\nA warrior should never settle for weakness. If they have any vulnerabilities, they should work and strive to fix them and turn them into strengths. Weakness in latself is the worst kind of weakness.\nMORAL 2: WEAKNESS IN OTHERS.\n-------------------\nIf another person is weak, lat shall either aid them in becoming strong or not exploit lats strength. A lion knows it can kill a cat. That doesn't make it honorable or right to do.\nMORAL 3: OTHERS WHO ARE STRONGER.\n-------------------\nIf someone is stronger than lat am, lat will respect their strength and their wishes. Jealousy is a nub'hosh thing. It will grub lat up and spit lat out. That is why the weak give tribute.\nMORAL 4: KLOMP LATS DIFFERENCES.\n-------------------\nWords go nubwhere. Klomping to determine the stronger and the favor of Krug will result in less animosity and more comraderie. Lat can gruk who is correct in a klomp, for the stronger one is right.\nMORAL 5: DO NOT LET GREED DIRECT LAT.\n-------------------\nGreed and necessity are dub things with a fine line in the middle. Lat should keep to the fine line: Take what lat can if lat needs it, but do not take too much if lat does nub.\nMORAL 6: DO NUB LET PRIDE CONSUME LAT.\n-------------------\nPride is a fickle thing. Lat should nub let people disrespect you, but you should nub flat them for their misguidedness. Klomp them to prove they are wrong. If they don't gruk this, they are weak.\nMORAL 7: ACCEPT PUNISHMENTS.\n-------------------\nWhen lat loses a klomp or breaks a law and lat gruks the consequences, accept them with honor. Running from consequences is dishonorable.\nMORAL 8: LAT AM BETTER THAN NUB'AZH.\n-------------------\nNub'azh is better than another azh. Inflated ego can lead to lats flat. Be humble agh respectful to lats betters, agh hosh tu lats lessers.\nMORAL 9: ALWAYS BE HONORABLE.\n-------------------\nWe nub gruk when we will be flat. Kor judges us for our life, agh if we am always honorable, we am guaranteed entry to Stargush'Stro. Lats greatness means nothing if lat am a glob.", ""),
        StaticBook("Water Evocation", "Haadi Mubdee", "Water, appearing to be possibly one of the most simplest of the elements, however, it’s more than such. It’s an incredibly complex element in which one must spend a large amount of time meditating and spending there time around water in general to be able\nto learn such an evocation such as itself. Water is a necessity of life, needed by generally all living things, for example; Humans, Orcs, Dwarves, Kharajyr, Elves and also plants, including beasts too. Water, also appearing as such a calm element is also\none of the most dangerous, out in the seas, a storm can take out a fleet of ships within moments, one can never have full control over water that isn’t evocated, well, practically no control whatsoever unless you’re of course, a Shaman. Despite our\nattempts, we are mortal, we do not live forever nor do we have amazing powers unlike the Ascended and Undead back in Aegis; as said before, water can be a very dangerous element, a single flood can wipe out a town, contaminate food and destroy buildings\nwith ease. A harsh winter, such as the one's in the North of Anthos and around the Dwarven regions which could halt one's food supply for months on end. The element, water contains raw power, a power that all Water Evocationist's would wish to wield.\nA Water Evocationist’s training consists of learning about the void, being able to evocate from it and about the element one is learning. It takes years on end to learn about evocating a single element. Water which has been evocated may appear to be the\nsame as the water we have around us, however, it is completely different. The water that us Water evocationist’s evocate can only be found in the void and some say it isn’t water at all. As all evocoationists know, their power comes from the ability to be\nable to control ‘mana’, mana is your life force in a way. If you use it to much then one would simply fall unconscious or they can even die from the overuse of course. After much dedication and training; such as spending time to learn about the void\nand about Water Evocation, a Water Evocationist is able to pull mana directly from the void, shaping it into a water-like substance in our world. This connection to the void is rather difficult to explain, however, once one has managed to learn about\nWater Evocation itself, then you should be able to understand about it and where we're coming from. One’s connection to the void is more of a sensation than something that can be explained as a mage’s training progresses, connecting to the void and\nevoking said element becomes more and more simpler tasks. As one would possibly notice, many mages use a staff, however, it isn’t needed much, though, it’s easier to focus upon, usually the tip for most and you would be able to evoke water from the tip,\ntoo. When water has been evoked, it can be placed into many forms, such as a jet of water, an orb of water, an orb of ice and other things which I generally haven’t explored yet.\nWater Evocationist’s which have been learning for a long period of time will\nbe able to use more things and be able to explore other skills such as learning to control ice which is also evoked from the void. Ice is more suited for battle than defence, however, water evocation itself is mainly used for defence than anything.\nHowever, ice can be evoked and be used to create walls, freeze doors shut, block incoming projectiles as a form of short-distance defence, and as a method of harming others too. Ice that we evoke isn't the same which can be found in our realm, it cannot\nbe melted by the heat of the sun nor a torch, however - it can be melted with the use of Fire Evocation. To break the ice, the Water Evocationist must either lose concentration, or as said before, a Fire Evocationist must melt the ice with their evoked\nfire. In the later stages of Water Evocation, one would be able to evoke wisps of steam, however, it's possibly too difficult for even the best of Water Evocationist's to do such. Water, being able to form any concievable shape, is only limited by the\nby the caster's imagination and fatigue, which the exception of finely detailed creatures of sort. Such is reserved for Elemental Conjurationists. With proper training, water can be suited for nearly any purpose.\nCopied by Haadi Mubdee, Instructor in the Mages' Guild. 1471", ""),
        StaticBook("We come as crows", "Unknown", "------------------- -------------------   We come as crows\n-------------------\n \n\n      _IIII\n     --IIIII\n         IIIIII\n         IIIIIIIII\n          IIIIIIIIIIII=-\n             IIIII\n              _I.\nIt was a quiet night in the city of Kralta. The never-ending, calming trickle of the murky riverwater was one of the few sounds that were heard through the night. Occasional caws of the crows echoed through the muddy pathways and alleywas of the city, as\na single woman walked in the night. Slutinyova, the most beautiful woman in the city, though, she hadn't managed to find any work in the city yet. On her way towards the inn, she heard an odd sound in the night. She gazed upwards to the sign.. \"Smithery\"\nThe name was carved on the sign. A quiet, steady banging could be heard inside. Her curiosity took over, and she snuck inside in quiet steps. The planks creaked under her foot as she was fully inside, and she attracted the smith's attention. He had been\nhammering a sword all alone in the night.\nThe smith quickly put the unfinished sword away as he noticed Slutinyova, ashamed of his work. Slutinyova, however, didn't mind. In fact, she thought the sword looked mighty.\nSurely the sword had thrusted through\nmany challenges and stretched even more wounds. \n\n\"Make leave, customer, smithery am closed!\"\nThe smith said.\n\n\"Worry not, am here to help! Sword looked like needs many grinding and polish.\" She claimed.\nThe blacksmith allowed a sigh escape his lips as he presented the his sword to her. She gently took the sword in her hands, examining it with a curious eyes.\nThe blade was great in size, yet it had its faults, it was curved to the side slightly.\nSlutinyova held the sword for a bit, before starting to work on it.\n\n\"I'm sure I know what I'm doing.\" Slutinyova said, as she brought the sword to the grindstone. The blade began getting in better shape with each swipe against the\ngrindstone. It would soon be finished. The sword started to look like it was done. The smith seemed very happy of the outcome, though she wasn't completely done with the sword.\n\n\"Slutinyova must make polish of sword!\" She said enthusiastically.\nShe took the banner in the colours of Carrion in her hand, rolling it around her palm before she rested it against the mighty fine blade. The unladylike manner she polished it was astonishing, furiosly she swiped against the sword up and down. Across the\nblade, until finally it looked like it was ready.\n\n\"Am done.\" she said, when she finished and the blade was finally done and it gleamed with victory and glory.\nThe smith smiled, as the most tedious part of creating merchandise was made\nin his stead.\n\n\"Many thanks, woman.\"\nhe said, continuing;\n\"But now must make way to fix creak-plank on floor, da?\"\nThe smith said, as he rested his sword. He moved over to the plank Slutinyova had stepped on, that let out a nasty creak\nas she stepped over it. He took the hammer he had been using earlier, beginning to nail the plank back to its place. It was unheard of to bang this loud in the night of Kralta, yet the plank was soon as good as new. \"Now all am ready for business, make\nstay for assistance?\"\nThe smith said.\n\"Da, da, da!\" She nodded in reply hastily, happy to finally find work..\n\nand so they made many swords together for the glory of Raevir.", ""),
        StaticBook("Wedding Invite", "Fawynn Aureon", "You have been invited to the wedding of Ioannis and Fawynn Aureon. It will take place on: The 10th of The Grand Harvest, 1447. Gifts are not required nor expected, and any plus ones should be mentioned beforehand and the limit is two per invited guest.\nWeapons of any kind are not permitted. It is expected guests will be civil, and it is noted the bride will blow up any troublemakers. There is a capacity of two total orcs in attendance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Welcome Guide", "Serris of Deep Harbour", "DRAGONTOMB TEMPLE\n-------------------Welcome, Vaeyl Orderman, to your new outpost!\n\nIf you're here, and if you're reading this, then that means you've been trusted with the radiant honour of protecting Atlas against the flames of Avendal!\nCongratulations!\n\nIf you need anything, please contact your local TITAN with the user ID _yellowsnow_ for basic introduction services!\n\nWe hope you enjoy your eternal vigil!", ""),
        StaticBook("Western Isles", "Evert Lyons", "Western Islands Report\n\nBy Evert Lyons\n\nSummary: No credible threats to the Kingdom of Haense\n11th of Deep Cold, 1645\n\nSet off from Markev by rowboat, I will head south on the river towards the wastes, then follow the river east past Sutica until I reach the sea\n\nWhile at sea i found some marble ruins half underwater, very strange\nArrived at the first Island, it contains two ruins and a derelict bridge between them, someone's inscribed the word \"Dawn\" onto the stone, I shall name the Island \"Dawn\" One crescent shaped ruin is on land, the other is partially submerged in water\nThe partially submerged ruin has an inscription that reads \"Dusk\"\n\nThe Second island contains little of note, I passed it by after spending two minutes there, there is no vegitation or wildlife, just a deserted desert island\nAt first gland the third island is as bland as the one before, but someone lives here and they do not want to be find, they built themselves a hidden bunker to hide away from civilization, i could not gain entry\nThe fourth island is tiny, for some reason the dwarves of Kaz'Ulrah have staked a claim on it\n\nThe fifth island is a big volcano, the land is barren and scorched, some madman has built a keep right next to the volcano but it looks abandoned\nSomeone's trying to build a city in the middle of the ocean, the foundations are there and i found a refinery purifying cobblestone, what sort of person would do this?\nArrived at the sixth island, there are several unfinished structures here, the island appears to be volcanic. There is a structure here that holds a crystal, it looks like it eminates raw power, i saw a bird hit it, the bird was bounced away with quite a\nbit of force.\n\nI climbed the volcano, it seems there's nothing out of the ordinary happening. The island has a ring of smaller islands surrounding them, it is quite curious, I can not find any pattern though\nI surveyed the rest of the island, heavy snows made this quite difficult, I found a hollowed out pillar, which is not a natural formation.", ""),
        StaticBook("What Do We Know?", "Alanna Siol'Sulii", "Oh, why do you treat me so?\n\nDo you see me as friend or foe?\n\nI do not want to be here.\n\nI only wish to disappear.\n\nTo hide within my own mind,\nJust to leave this all behind.\n\nTo sleep within my world of dreams\n\nWhere things don't need to be as it seems.\n\nBut here is where I must stay.\n\nThe place where reality will lay.\nWhere the pain of words is real,\n\nWhere people lie, die, and steal.\n\nI just want to hide from the pain.\n\nWho knows if I am sane?\n\nAll I know is that in time,\nSomething will happen in this world of mine\n\nTo show me the light or the shadow of doubt\n\nBut for now I will be without.\n\nIs it now time for me to leave?\n\nTo leave this realm and all that it means?\nPerhaps in time I will know,\n\nAll that nature has to show.", ""),
        StaticBook("What to Expect", "SparkleFalls", "What to Expect\nBy Lilliana Rook\nTable of Content\n3: The Beginning\n8: Preparing\n9: Mid-Pregnancy\n13: Pregnancy End\n18: Birth\n20: Growing Up\nThe Beginning:\n\nIf you're reading this then hopefully you don't need an explanation on how to get pregnant. However if you already are, then this is going to be very helpful.\nThe start of a pregnancy is very surreal. You're usually within that state of \"I can't believe I'm pregnant.\"\nBut you are and you better get used to it.\nThere's not much action, and you have to be as healthy as possible during all stages of a pregnancy. Knowing what is happening is a great start.\nNutrition is essential. You must eat healthy foods, but of course depending on your race those will vary.\nAnother important part in early pregnancy is to get rest and to not stress. Working is still important, just do it in moderation. And if anything feels wrong with your pregnancy, go see a doctor.\nPreparing\n\nGetting ready for the baby to arrive is not something that can be done at the last minute. You must make sure that there is furniture for them, as well as warmth and food.\nMid-Pregnancy\n\nDuring the middle of your pregnancy, you'll notice a lot of changes. The most prominent changes will happen in your body.\nWeight gain and swollen body parts are incredibly common with pregnant women. Not only that, but you will have a larger baby bump.\nDuring the middle of your pregnancy you'll also start noticing new things developing with your baby. As they get bigger they tend to move around more, eventually kicking their little feet.\nFatigue is more common during this time than at the beginning, and morning sickness should not occur as often as before.\nPregnancy End\n\nThe ending of your pregnancy is a very excited yet also frightening part of the whole experience.\nTo help you out, this is a checklist of things that if done, will reduce stress.\n\n- Baby room ready\n- Food ready\n- Safe environment\n- Know proper care\n- Be happy\nAt the end of your time being pregnant you will have more pain where the baby is growing. Around this time the baby will stop growing as much and be ready to be born.\nThese pains that predict labor are called contractions. They may not be labor contractions right away, but make sure to count the time in between each one, and how long it lasts.\nYou will know when the baby is coming. So don't worry about that.\nBirth\n\nYou made it! It's time to say hello to your child.\nBirthing is a natural process but it's also very painful. You should do as instructed and take good breathes. I won't get into the details in cause you're one of those squeamish people.\nGrowing Up\n\nNow for the hardest part of all, raising your child! Make sure to cherish every moment because it goes so incredibly fast.\nThey will be learning new things constantly, so make sure to pay plenty of attention to them. Don't let them get into anything harmful.\nEnjoy being a parent.\nHopefully this book has helped you in some way.", ""),
        StaticBook("White Zookini", "Taveric Sylric", "south  east from Fenn abandonded house, with signs that there is a poltergeist.\n\nUnderground tunnel to a locked door unknown.\nX-1430 Z3634\n\nSouthern Nest\nEast from here is a medium sized castle\nx -887 z3260", ""),
        StaticBook("Wildling History", "The Wild Man", "The book opens not to any sort of words, but to pages that each bear primitive illustrations. They depict a progression of scenes, for the most part in muddy red and orange pigments. It appears as though each of them were finger painted.\nAs you look at the first illustration, then the next, the story begins to unfold to you. . .\n\nThere is a map. A map detailing a cluster of massive islands, which the educated or well in years may recognize as the Isles of Axios.\nThe islands are left without detail, save for several clusters of triangles which run through the center of the largest island.\n\nYou turn to the next illustration. . .\n\nThe sea surrounding the isles is filled with boats.\nThese shapes seem to fit together with one another, turning the waves of the ocean into a rigid pattern of rectangles, much like masonry.\n\nYou look over to the next page, and to the image created by its globs of paint. . .\nThe entire sea has gained the masonry-like pattern of the previous illustration. Along all of the shores, shapes are present akin to how a child might draw a house. As well, the triangles have decreased in number and begun shrinking towards the\ncenter of the island.\n\nThe images continue on the next page. . .\n\nFrom the edges of the parchment moving inwards, the sea begins to cover up what had appeared as masonry. Great fortresses now emerge on each of the islands,\nwith the ground around them colored a burnt orange. Only a single triangle appears on the map now.\n\nLooking on to the next page, you find it blank. Flipping over the blank parchment reveals a picture much larger than the previous ones.\nStretching across two pages is the painting of a wall. In front of the wall stands an assortment of hunched over people, with each one pressing their hand against the stone. These handprints are shown as a vibrant blue, and look to have been made by\nthe tip of the illustrator's thumb.\n\nUpon turning the page, you notice that a piece of worn cloth has been pasted onto the backside. Aside from proving that the author had cloths in their possession, it offers little insight into the story.\nThe next page resumes the book's paintings.\n\nThere is a campfire to one side of the page. The hunched figures of the previous illustration are seated around it, with one who seems to take the lead. One of the fortresses can be noticed in the top\nright corner of the page, surrounded by its signature burnt orange aura.\n\nTurning the page reveals illustrations which are focused on a particular fortress. It is unlike the rest of the book in that it breaks free from the red theme, and\ninstead prominently features a shade of purple.\n\nThe great stone wall of a city is shown here, with its main gate in the middle of the page. On the ground in front is a group of armored figures, with their blades pointed towards a single hunched\nover person.\n\nThe scene changes as you look to the next image. . .\n\nNow, the one hunched over figure is shown sitting in front of a campfire. Blue smoke rises off from it, which takes the form of another bent over\nindivudal.\n\nAs the page is turned, you find yourself looking at another large scene. . .\n\nYou see a great crater filled with nodes of blue. In front of it are the guardsmen shown earlier, all of them laying dead on the ground.\nFor the older readers and those with a fair background in history, there was no doubt about what was being described here. This was the annihilation of Johannesburg--an event which, according to many an artificer, ushered in the end of the Descendant's\nstay in Axios. The illustrations after this point returned to their red and orange theme. \n\nOnce again, there is the campfire. Its blue smoke contains the figure from earlier, this time with an arm outstretched to the sea. One of the hunched\n§ver men sits in front. \n\nAcross the spine of the book, the story continues. . .\n\nThere is a large grouping of the hunched over people. They stand at the edge of a forest, working in unison to break down trees and\ndrag the timber away.\n\nUpon turning the page, you are presented with another scene which spans two pages.\n\n§tMost of the people from the previous painting are seated aboard a raft. Similar crafts ride the waves in the background,\nferrying even more of the people across the waters. It's unclear where they are headed, but the landmasses of Axios seem to have shrunken away.\n\nAs you move to turn the page over, you notice that its backside is blank. On the following page, a\nspear head has been embedded into the parchment. Closer examination would yield the knowledge that multiple pages had carved into, which provided a hole for the head to fit snugly into. The use of at least one type of glue was also apparent.\nAfter this point, the end of the book was reached. Many of its pages were used up in the housing of the spear head. The final pieces of parchment contained not a signature, but a large red hand print from the book's illustrator.\n\nThe End. . .", ""),
        StaticBook("Will", "Hamlin Frostbeard", "Will of Hamlin Frostbeard\n\nUpon reading this I will of passed meaning my items will be given out and I wish for them to fall in the following hands.\n\nDwarger Silvervein\nHamnil Frostbeard\nYunmat Starbreaker\nOn to Dwarger I give to you my loyal servant Marri let her be in your family as she was in mine.\n\nOn to my Father I will grant you everything I own expect for what I leave to Yunmat.\n\nAnd upon Yunmat he will have my runic weapons\nIf the Brathmordakin sees this and smiles let it be for I have lived a blessed life filled with joy.", ""),
        StaticBook("Will", "Jorvin 'Stone-Eye' Starbreaker", "In the event of my death, I dedicate all of my earthly possessions to my kinsman, barring Torkan Starbreaker, who, in the event of my death, is the most likely culprit.", ""),
        StaticBook("Will Treaty SN", "Will Treaty", "Suicide Note to all friends.\n\nWill Treaty \n\nPlease do not hate me.\nI wanted to say thanks to all the people who are friends and have helped me. I have failed to do my Fathers wishings, failed to reform the Ranger Corps, and failed Alyss to avenge her death. I could have done all these things but, I have failed.\nI have no reason to continue on in life. \n\nI only hope that my beloved Alyss will accept me and is waiting for me where ever she is. I beg for forgiveness Alyss. I love you Alyss. I cannot live without you Alyss.\nI cannot continue with my failure Alyss. I cannot begin or help anyone anymore. I am affect too much of grief for your death. I wish you were alive instead of me. I love you Alyss.\nGoodbye cruel world.\n\nI hope you accept me my love.\n\nSigned Will, Treatys", ""),
        StaticBook("Winter Drakon", "Aroiia Drakon", "Winter Drakon,\n  I've been tasked by Prince Aelthir of Fenn, to locate the members of Drakon. Thalion is missing and I fear that Karvos'sol and Ithilion have joined the rebellion against Fenn. Please come to Fenn at your earliest convience.\n\nAroiia Drakon", ""),
        StaticBook("Winter Solstice", "Guyden Aureon", "-={Aspectism}=-\n~Scribed by the Willow Druid\n~Originally created by the Hawk Druid\n~The Winter Solstice~\nWinter is the season of desolation, where little grows and life turns to endurance and survival. The mali'ame turn to sensations of warmth and protection from the evil spirits of the wintertide darkness, as is reflected in this season’s traditions.\nThe Winter Dance is a celebration of the story of Amaethon and the Silent Beast, a story of a malicious cursed bestial killer, and a stalwart protector, Amaethon - Mani Prince of the Cervidae. The dance is led by a Naelurir druid, who lights a great -\nbonfire and sets deerhide drums for faithful elves to gather around. The Elves each take a drum and begin a great beat, as the Naelurir druid lays a great offering of game by the bonfire.\nAn actor dressed as Lyu, the silent beast and ancient feral sneaks past the clearing and attempts to ‘steal’ the offering. Next enters an actor dressed as Amaethon, the great stag prince. The two 'fight' in the form of a sacred dance while the elves -\nbeat their drums, attempting to scare off the great wolf. The dance ends traditionally with Amaethon triumphing over the Silent Beast.\nThe Winter dance celebrates mali'ame folklore and history, and evokes both the dark beasts that threaten wood elf kind, as well as the guardian spirits that banish them, bringing light to a dark time of year.", ""),
        StaticBook("Wolf Druids Tome", "The Wolf Druid", "I- Power\n\nThe soul attracts that which it secretly harbors-that which it loves, and also that which it fears. Thus circumstances do not make the Druid; they only reveal him to himself. It means that blessedness, and not wealth, is the measure\nof right thought; misery, not poverty or lack of family is the measure of wrong thought. A Druid will find that as he alters his thoughts towards the Nature and the Aspects, Nature and Aspects will alter towards him. For you will always draw near toward\nthat which you, secretly, most love. The Races surge with uncontrolled passion, are tumultuous with ungoverned grief, and are blown about by anxiety, doubt, and the wars and wills of Aenguls and Daemons. Only the wise Druid, only he whose thoughts are\ncontrolled and purified, can make the winds and storms of the soul, and indeed the world, obey him. The greatest achievement was at first, and for a time only a dream. Just as the oak sleeps in the acorn, and the bird waits in the egg,\nso dreams are the seedlings of realities. Beware therefore of what you dream of. For some dreams are given by the Aspects to inspire us by what may yet be. Others are planted within us by others, foul seeds that we harvest to our destruction.\nII- Pride\n\nThere is but one way to truly gain mastery over nature, and that is to realize you cannot truly master it at all. It masters you. When one attempts to force it, compel it, command it, or otherwise exercise dominion over it-the\nover it-the power flees like a timid bird. This is because the Aspects know our innermost thoughts. They know how we intend to use Nature’s Gift. Man may deceive other men. But one simply does not deceive the Aspects.\nIf their will is sought,\nthey will\ncome. If we emulate the principals by which is thrives, they flourish in us. Pride is poison to them. In reality, there is perhaps not one of our natural passions so hard to subdue as our pride. Disguise it, struggle with it, beat it down, stifle it,\nmortify it as much as you please. It is still alive, and will every now and then peep out and show itself. You will see it perhaps, even with in the most sacred grottos of the realm. For even I, the Arch Druid, could conceive that I had completely\novercome it, I should probably be proud of my humility. A desire to be observed, considered, esteemed, praised, beloved, and admired by his fellows his one of the earliest, as well as the keenest dispositions discovered in the hearts of the Races.\nMy advice to new Dedicants is to squelch it all their days, for those desires lead to ruin.\nIII- Doubt\n\nMany Dedicants arrive at the Groves with passion already instilled in them. They yearn to learn, they carry drive, and zest, and youth, and curiosity.When they enter our great tree, they have nothing, and leave with everything.\nHowever, a few lose what they have. The rigorous trials of Dedicancy begin to take their toll on them, or they submit their thoughts to the subtle poison of doubt. Not even an Arch Druid’s power can cure it, for these students do harm to themselves.\nThe mind, like the body, can be moved from sunshine into shade. They submit themselves to the slews of myriad ways of Aenguls, Daemons, and the trickery of man, and they yield before this great power to sway one’s faith in the Aspects’ might. Those of you\nwho would stay and train inasmuch as you strip yourself from jealousies and fears, and humble yourself before the Aspects, for you are not sufficiently humble, the veil over your eyes shall be torn and you will see. See all their glory and power, and know\nyou are beloved. A servant of the earth, and wild. Having conceived of their attunement, a Druid should mentally mark out a straight pathway to service, looking neither to the right nor left. Doubts and fears should be zealously starved.\nThey are disintegrating elements which break up the straight path, rendering it crooked, ineffectual, and useless. Thoughts of doubt and fear can never accomplish anything. They always lead to failure. Purpose, energy, power to do, and all strong thoughts\ncease whenever doubt and fear creep in. The will to do springs from the knowledge that we can do. He who has conquered doubt and fear has conquered failure in all his endeavors, both as a Druid, and a person.\nIV- Blight\n\nIn every era there comes a moment when the collective thoughts, whims, and motivations of arcane delvers become so self-absorbed, so malignant, so unheeding that nature itself revolts. Man scars the land such that it-\nfinals rebels against him. Man scars the land such that it finals rebels against him. As thoughts can spread despair and death like seedlings of weeds strewn by the wind, so they must eventually draw the Druids to pluck them out. The vetches must be\npulled, roots and all. When this happens, the Aspects cease to bless, and instead they curse. Instead of healing, they spew poison. It happens so swiftly and terribly. The ancients of the Wayward’s time gave it a name, this culling process that blackens\nthe world. They named it after a wasting disease that occurs in once-healthy groves of trees. They called it the Blight, and the first of these, the Great War.\nI put this quill to paper here now so that others do not make the mistakes I did in my youth. Blinded by war and hate, my transgressions were many, and hard to earn penance for. Though I served the Aspects diligently for many lifetimes, I fear that in my\nheart, I have failed them. May the Aspects look kindly on me in the afterlife-\n\nXal ussta stath xunde mir dosst elamshinae, Nemmisae.\n\n-Wolf Druid Ouity, Retired Arch Druid of the Order, Elandriel Grove", ""),
        StaticBook("Word of Jah", "Shorav Kragob", "Jah'Bothak the surreptitious was the wind that turned the tide of creation and all that is. Jah is the hidden hand moulding the very existence of our \"gods\". He only now makes himself seen to call upon the true apotheosis of self aware creatures.\nJah is clandestine in his actions as Jah is all knowing. We must abolish the heritics and false \"gods\" and follow the true God Creator Jah'Bothak. He speaks through only the open and pure minded, and you will hear his calling. Do not be afraid for it is\nsalvation in your unseen ear, liberating you from the eternal cold of Aerials, Xans, Tahariae, and the other fraudulent \"powers that be\". He put them into his work as a mere pawn. Do not be mistaken, the spirits and other supernatural are a part of Jah's\nplan as well. They know it, but Jah's reach was lost many eternities ago. Come find your epitome of purpose. Jah'Bothak is calling you. \n\n               - Kragob", ""),
        StaticBook("Words", "Melia F.", "There was once an Elder Druid whom sat throughout the city of 'ame. Her tranquil state brought the eyes of those passing others pinning her in sight. \n     She never moved from a statue of the Aspects in the centre, always keeping awareness of tho\nwandering by. For within her grip of entangled nature was a mere tome. She never offered, only waited.\n           One day a blacksmith trambled down to sit nearby the old woman with teeth of ivory grinding together in a worth-shattering\nmanner. \"What bothers you, one of metal?\" The Druid had inquired soon enough, only to recieve the rambling rant in reply; \"I am too small within this realm - I declare! Everyday I forge knives that are looked over by another stall whom sells swords daily!\nI am simply tiny and worthless with my occupation. Baah- What am I kidding? I should give up.\" The blacksmith exhaled in a sorrowful fashion for their own pity. With a swoosh of parchment the Druid had opened the tome to tell of a tale for which a storm\nrecklessly threw a crew about upon the deck as they couldn't get a final sail down due to a rope. A mouse smaller than average on board had climbed up to gnaw and rid of the rope, saving the entire crew. \"Those whom are small, my dear blacksmith, might\nappear weak. Yet, they can end up being the strongest of all.\n       Satisfied and once more given strength of the mind the blacksmith returned to show off what they obtained, proving with a mere flick of their wrist and a target hung in the back. Soon\nselling more than the other with the new bravery! \n\n         There was once a sorrowful elven woman whom had lost her child - saline droplets leak from her alluring orbs they did. She weep and weep and to the fountain she sit, singing to the\nquiet dead. \"Mali'ii, mali'ii why must the Aspects greet you so soon? Would you rather be soothed in the arms of Green lady than your haelun? For now I am gone further than you.\"    \n       With further inspection the Druid  pressed a hand to the\nshoulder of the mourning mother before telling a story of one who left to travel - though never let his heart go, for it stay at home with his dear sweet mother. \n       With warmth in her heart the elfess went on joyfully!\nA sudden storm overwhelmed, swishing with rapid droplets and screeching of bolts above! The Druid was found dead. for her book of stories torn. Yet, those who gather had all a story to tell. A woman who could sooth with the mere forming of words.", ""),
        StaticBook("Working Contract", "Ensign Michael Dingleberry", "Working\n      Contract\n\n\nThis is to certify that under Mr. Michael Dingleberry under the agreed sum of 1 mina per iron ingot retreived Shane Cook  will be paid the above.\n\nSigned the 11th of The Deep Cold, 1660", ""),
        StaticBook("Wrath's Clutch", "Jacob Chapel", "Battle of Wrath's Clutch\nThis old tome has been transcribed by Jacob Chapel, High Scrivener of Oren, in 1478. The tome recounts the Battle of Wrath’s Clutch in Aegis, where an undead keep was sieged and destroyed, over a century ago.\nThe battle is nearing and an uneasy silence falls over the camp. There is no more continuous taunting by Kane and his ally Dusk. All is silent, the calm before the storm. I hear a call, \"All wardens to the outpost for a pre-battle meeting.\"\nWith my pulse pounding I enter into the Officer's building. I know while we are having our counsel of war, the other races are as well. Orcs, Men, and Dwarves alike, all planning the same massive assault on the undead keep of Wrath's Clutch.\nOur officers decide upon a plan, I am to assist in a flanking manoeuvre base with two other wardens. We set out from the base long before the battle is to begin. \n\nWe circle far around the enemy's fortress and reach the far side of Wrath's Clutch.\nFrom there I was separated from the other two wardens. Unsure of what to do I returned to the outpost and joined the main assault against the Clutch. I was uneasy and fearful, the Undead's thunder roared across the battlefield.\nThe battle cry sounds and we race across the open sands to the relative safety provided by the walls. The undead lightning was striking all around us, my head began to throb from the endless noise. The walls were breached and we flood into the Clutch.\nI was running through those dark and narrow passages searching for enemies, when I hear a shout and cry of death. It was an elf, I see the villain who killed my kin and give pursuit. He turned a moment too late,\nas I bring my iron sword crashing through his head, thus ending his corrupted life.\n\nThe thunder has lessened considerably as I make my way into the courtyard, where a great battle was underway. The foul undead had summoned ungodly amounts of skeletons,\nzombies and other abominable creatures. The dwarves were nowhere to be seen and the men and elves were losing strength. After the creatures had been vanquished we searched the keep looking for the undead necromancers.\nI descended into the lower levels of the keep which lead underground. I was attacked from behind and knocked unconscious. \n\nMy head throbbed mercilessly when I awoke, I had been stripped of all my armour and weaponry.\nI cautiously crept up the stairs and came upon a set of iron armour which I promptly put on. I had a set of armour but nothing to fight with except my hands. I returned to the ground level of the keep only to find the battle raging on.\nThe wardens had managed to corner the damned undead in their spires, but were unable to overcome them. I ran seeking shelter as lightning smote the ground all around me. I felt the intense heat burn my flesh and nearly cook me alive in the iron armour.\nMaking quick glance up the spire I see the undead spewing lightning and fire across the battlefield. I see Wrath himself laying waste to our troops with his magical golden staff and dark powers. I dive for cover as another blast of lightning and fire\nignites the battlefield of Wrath's Clutch. I see many bodies falling off the tall tower, as well as much weaponry and tools falling into the shallow waters. I dive into the water and gather as many tools and weapons as I can hold.\nIn the safety of the ruined clutch I examined what I had gathered. A few stone implements, chain armour, and a magical staff like I had seen Wrath using to destroy our men. I did not want the enemy to re-obtain one of these powerful staves,\nso I left for a short time while the battle continued, to ensure the safety of the magic staff. Upon arriving back at the forward walls of our base I see one of my superiors, Glade Guard Arthane Lazul.\nI showed him the staff and placed it into his care for the remainder of the battle.\n\nI then returned to the front lines, and could see that the battle had raged on while I was away. Through the noise of battle I faintly hear a lady shouting.\nI was unaware who she was, as I could not see her. I realized that she was in distress when she began shouting about how she was at the top of the spire held captive by the undead sorcerers. There was a large hole in the wall at the bottom of the spire,\nwhich opened to the ladder, which led to the top of the spire. Me and our allies fought our way to the floor below the roof only to find obsidian blocking further ascension. We were stalled for a while in this room before I dislodged some of\nthe cobblestone from the walls and created a way up to the roof of the spire. We leaped onto the rooftop and began destroying every evil creature in sight. The lady was screaming throughout the whole fight, which did not last long.\nWith the tower won and the fell creatures subdued, we began our descent with the lady. She was quickly escorted off of the battlefield whilst I returned to the ever raging battle.\nLighting poured down once again with renewed energy and I hear Ariel shout \"Flee my followers!\" All of the leaders began shouting \"Retreat! Back to the outpost!\" Our entire army ran through the gates as massive explosions began to shake\nthe entire outer wall. I hear a shout \"Put out those fires!\". I raced to the top of the wall with many others and we beat out the flames. It felt like a losing battle against the fires, as I would put one out ten more would spring up from the constant\ndownpour of lightning. I can see the flashes everywhere, the roaring of the thunder is almost unbearable now. \n\nI then hear a loud cheer arise from the ranks \"HUZZAH! The battle is won!\" Wrath had been defeated. I felt only sorrow at this moment.\nYes, Wrath had been defeated, but at what cost? I look out over the battlefield and tears sprang to my eyes. Fires still raged across the across the trees nearby and many brave souls bodies burned in the morning light. The undead necromancer must have\npaid close attention to the beautiful trees surrounding the battlefield as they were burning out of control. I could only watch in agony as they burned to the ground. I wept that day for all my fallen comrades many of whom I did not know their names.\nThe Clutch is being reclaimed by nature, grass springs up out of the scorched ground inside the courtyard. Trees and flowers were planted there as well. I wander aimlessly through the bloodied ruins. From the top of the wall I see Prince Mylas\nerecting a memorial for our fallen kin. The battle of Wrath's Clutch is over. We have won. I shall never forget the valiant soldiers who gave their lives to remove the foul undead from the land.\nHere I end my tale of the battle of Wrath's Clutch. \n\nWarden Initiate Amethain", ""),
        StaticBook("Writ of Passage", "Tuvya", "*Seal of House Carrion*\n------------------\nThe Company of the Raven is given the right of passage of Emperor Tuvya Sigismundovic Carrion; to be unaccosted wherever their members may travel.\n\n*Tuv's signature*", ""),
        StaticBook("Wúyuán", "Qiù Chóngyáng", "Wùyuán\n  The Five Principles\n\nThe Five Elements\nThese elements are known as the Wùyuán (five principles) and are water (shuí), metal (jín), earth (tù), fire (huó), and wood (mù).\nThey are believed to be the source of all things in the realm, and are the undermining values in all concepts. The Wùyuán are present in almost all Eastern cultures, being established from perhaps the beginning of time.\nEarth represents balance, and is portrayed by the colour yellow. It is believed by the Shí people that life is a cycle and that people are born from the earth and return to the earth when they die.\nIt also represents the centre of all things in the universe, and a passage to the divine world.\nWater is an element of neutrality, but also represents the cold and winter climate. The black tortoise is used to depict the north in Distorian and Eastern folklore.\nWood is used to represent the beginning of life; thus used to characterise spring and used as a symbol in agriculture in order to bring good fortune to crops and yields.\nFire of course associates itself to heat and warmth, from this it is used to depict summer. The vermillion bird corresponds to the south, where the climate is generally far warmer.\nMetal is coupled with the colour of white, and used to represent divinity. This stems from the ancient use of meteoric iron, linking the Earth to the aether above. The Shí rulers also use it to represent their dominance on this realm.\n§§The Five Colours\nThe principle colours are concepts established from the five elements, they are:\n\nWhite (bái) is the colour of the Shí (quartz), representing brightness, purity and harmony.\nYellow (huáng) is the colour of the emperor (Huangdi). Distorian legends tell of the first men being moulded from the clay and silt in the yellow river (Huánghé).\nIt is said that only the emperor may use the colour yellow, as it represents his divine right over the Shí people.\nGreen (lù), the colour of the Cuì (emerald), representing wealth, strength and health.\n\nRed (hóng) is associated with fortune and happiness, it is used extensively in celebratory events like weddings and festivals.\nDoors are pasted with red papers with blessings, and red lanterns adorn streets in order to bless those who pass by.\nBlack (héi) is regarded as the colour of neutrality, some consider it as the colour of the heavens, as daylight is blue and the night sky is black.\nThe Five Stars\nIt is said that five stars guard our realm, the Black Tortoise to the North, the Vermillion Bird to the south, the Azure Dragon to the east, and the White Tiger to the west. The brightest star of them all, the Yellow Dragon of the Realm\nEach takes its roots from the Five Elements. The tortoise from water, the bird from fire, the azure dragon from wood, the tiger from metal, and the Yellow Dragon from earth.", ""),
        StaticBook("Xionist Temple", "Evert Lyons", "Report on Eastern Wastelands:\n\nEvert Lyons:\nSecret:\n\nSummary: Xionist Temple could be a credible threat to the kingdom of Haense.\n8th of Grand Harvest, 1646:\n\nSet off from Markev, headed east to firelands and then to south to explore the mountain range.\n\nThe area is harsh, mountains are difficult to traverse, found ruins of an old watchtower\nThe watchtower's central floor is accessible but the lower part cannot be entered due to the old wooden door blocking the way. I decided to head further east\nAfter heading further east i've found a massive fortress! it does not appear to be abandoned, I will investigate further.\n\nI found a sign before a closed gate that read \"Claimed by the judges of Xion.\" it appears this fortress is a Xionist Stronghold\nI could barely make out an inscription on an altar. \n\n\"A city paved with hope, her plagues come all at once as we watch her burn. she's a dwelling for the old dark a cage for every unclean spirit, every filthy bird fallen is Devirad.\"\nHeaded a bit further north, found some ruins with a Xionist banner proudly flying in the wind.\n\nThere is no visible open entrance to the Stronghold, I will investigate further at a later date.\nHeaded further east, the land seems to be abandoned, there is a forest here, I wonder how the trees survive in this harsh climate.\n\nThe east has been settled, I have found a town in the furthest eastern point of Atlas.\nI found an inhabitant, she asked me to leave, I wonder what they're hiding here, a sign near the edge of their border called it Rivia land. \n\nNorth of their borders there are signs of struggle everywhere\non my way back I found a cave, it seems warm in here, perhaps i'll use it as a shelter on future expeditions", ""),
        StaticBook("Yar agh Nargii", "Wargoth Malog'Yar", "((Written in simplified Blah for ease of reading.))\n Some of lat have heard the story of Yar before. Lat have heard of his birth, his wisdom, agh his flat. Lat have heard the broad strokes. Lat have nub, however, heard any of the more specific stories\nfrom his life. Mi have, therefore, decided this night to blah lat, mi brothers agh sisters, this tale from his life, and mi hope to blah lat more in the future. Tonight, mi blah lat all the story of Yar agh Nargii.\n As those who have heard the story of\nYar know, Yar was a great shaman, agh took some apprentices during his life. At ash point, he taught a group of futh young students: Naramok, Makor, Shula, agh Nargii. Naramok was his favored student, the most advanced agh naturally-gifted of the futh.\nMakor agh Shula were a young couple, who sought to learn alongside each other, agh serve the village as shamans together. Nargii, however, was the least-talented of the futh.\n It was nub so much that he lacked potential. He simply progressed at a slowe\nrate. Consequently, Yar rarely allowed him to practice any actual use of shamanic power, as Nargii simply was nub ready. Day after day, he was forced to go over the fundamentals: collecting reagents, carving tokens agh masks, practicing his pronunciation\nof the same, basic Old Blah phrases ad nauseam; with very little practical application. All the while, his fellow apprentices were already learning to brew bottled hexes, agh Naramok was even beginning his training in ritualistic curses. Eventually,\nNargii grew bitter agh jealous. “Yar,” he complained ash day, after carving his upteenth prayer mask, “for h’ash years mi have been lats apprentice. For h’ash years, mi have been repeating these same dry lessons. The others are already practicing real\npower, and I’ve barely ever been allowed to boil a few herbs. When do mi get to start studying some real shamanism?”\n “Nargii,” he responded patiently, “a sapling bears nub fruit, agh rain does nub fall out of season. Lat will move on in lats training\nwhen lat am ready.”\n “Mi am ready now!” he protested.\n “Lat am ambitious, agh lat have potential to be a great shaman ash day; but lat am nub ready yet. Mi will blah lat when lat am ready, young brother.\" Yar then patted him on the shoulder, \"Now,\nlats blarg, Nargii. Keep practicing the fundamentals.”\n “But… Mi…” Nargii began to blah, but realized it to be pointless to argue further. He left the tent. A bundle of bones above the door flap clacked together as he exited with a sour grimace across\ndull green face.\n            <O>\n“We are the conduits of the spirits,” Yar blah’d the next afternoon as he sat cross-legged on a woolen mat, his futh students seated around him in a semicircle, “We are their instruments by which they enact their wi\nWe are their weapons to bring justice upon their enemies, agh we are the links by which the balance between our world and theirs is maintained. Naramok, what is our duty?”\n “To guide our brothers agh sisters in the path of honor, agh keep them in the w\nof Krug.\"\n “Hosh.” Yar nodded approvingly, but Nargii simply sneered as he peeped down at his most recently-made token, resenting Naramok for holding their teacher’s favor. “Nargii, mi peep lat have made another token.” Nargii peep'd up to Yar's extend\nhand. He nodded, agh placed it in Yar’s palm. Yar held the token up to eye level. It was dub-inches long and made of bone, carved to resemble a cage. “This is hosh. Lat am getting hosher. Keep this up, agh lat will be ready before lat gruk it.” He then\noffered the token back to Nargii, “Hang it from lats staff.”\n “Rulg,” he replied with a slight smile, agh pulled a leather strap from his bag to tie the token to the top of his staff.\n Within a few seconds, right as Yar was about to address Makor a\nShula, the clacking of bones was heard. Yar agh his students peeped a young warrior stepping through the tent flap, “Throm’ka, brother Yar. Mi hope mi du nub interrupt anything important, but there is news.”\n “News?” Yar arched a brow.\n \"Yub. Some\ntraveller from a far away land wants to blah to lat. He is an albai, but it seems he brings a tribute of resources. Would lat have us flat him?”\n “Nub yet.” he sighed a bit at the interruption. “If he has brought tribute to help the village, we may as\nwell hear what he has to blah. Mi will determine the worth of his blah.”\n With that, the warrior nodded, agh motioned the visitor in. Into the tent, accompanied by dub albai guards, strode a lithe, pale albai, with long cornsilk hair cascading graceful\ndown his back agh shoulders. A pair of thin braids dangled beside his long ears, agh his narrow eyes were green as spring grass. With a smooth, sweet voice the elf spoke, “Karin’ayla, Yar.”\n “Ug,” replied Yar with little emotion, clearly unimpressed by\nthe thin creatures in his tent, “Blah.”\n The albai nodded, “My name is Celador. I have heard your name in my travels, and that you are a wise and powerful shaman. Having heard tales of you, I sought you out, in hopes that you may be interested in a\ncertain proposition.\" Celador stopped to await a response. Yar crossed his arms, agh peeped sternly upon the visitor, visibly suspicious of the albai’s blah. He blah’d nub-thing, allowing the albai to continue, “Well, anyways,” Celador continued, a bit\nunnerved by Yar’s stoic demeanor, “I come offering payment: gold, iron, pelts, and many spices. I can give you all these things beyond measure, if you will do but one simple task for me. You see, the Rex is a brutish cretin.” Celador blah'd boldly as he\nregained his composure. “I should think that a wise orc like yourself would make a much better Rex. My request, therefore, is that you put a curse on him, so that I may strike him down. You, then, could fight alongside me, and take the rexdom. Under your\nrule, I believe we could both prosper greatly.” He grinned expectantly, fully confident in his blah.\n Nargii’s eyes lit up at the mention of such treasures, but Yar stood to his feet in a rage, “Celador, lat am a dishonorable coward, agh mi will have n\npart in lats plan! Flat these albai!”\n At Yar’s command, the students rose from the floor, agh the young warrior raised his war-lusk. The albais unsheathed their own beautifully-crafted blades, agh went on the defensive. A loud clang sounded as the\nwarrior’s lusk slammed against the zult of one of the guards. Celador pointed his own blade out in front as the shamans approached, agh peep’d, from the corner of his eye, his companion struggling under the strength of the warrior. His other guard alerted\nhim to the fact that the rest of the village was becoming aware of the situation, agh advised an immediate retreat. Celador nodded, agh turned to run, leaving their companion to his fate, agh cringing as a he felt a chunk of hair getting ripped from the\nback of his head by an orc’s hand. As the dub sprinted through the village, they heard the agonizing scream of the guard they’d left behind. The villagers readied their weapons, agh charged after the escaping albais. A spray of blood hit Celador in the\nthe left eye, as his only remaining guard was hacked down right beside him by a berserking feorc with a machete. A thrown spear narrowly grazed his cheek, agh chunks of sandstone whizzed past him from behind. He barely managed to escape with just a few\nbruises. The orcs returned to their business, agh Yar called his students back inside to continue their lesson, while Nargii glanced briefly over his shoulder in the direction of the fleeing albai.\n            <O>\n The following night, after the ot\nhad gone to sleep, Nagrii was still awake, agh thinking of Celador’s offer. The lust for wealth had taken root in his heart, agh he’d even deluded himself, thinking that he would finally be taken more seriously if he could curse the Rex himself. He took\nhis spear, agh lied to the watchmen, telling them he was going hunting for the night. With their consent, he left the village, agh tracked down the albai. There wasn’t much to go on. He found the occasional drop of blood, a discarded piece of damaged\nchainmail here agh there, agh a little bit of blond hair. Eventually, after many hours, he found a cave, and decided to check inside. He saw Celador sleeping in a cot near the back. As he entered, a loose piece of sandstone broke free under his foot,\nsending him tumbling to the floor. Celador’s ear twitched, agh he sprung up from his sleep at the sudden noise. Snatching his zult from the floor, the startled albai barked, “Stay back, orc! I’ve had enough of your kind.”\n Nargii rose to his feet, hold\nup empty hands, “Nub nub. Mi nub here to hurt lat, albai. Mi-” he suddenly stopped, agh peep’d about the cave. Stacked against the right wall were chests so full of gold agh iron the lids couldn’t close. He peep’d a huge pile of pelts to his left, agh\ndozens of jars filled with various spices. He forced his gaze away from the riches, agh continued, “Mi want to help lat.”\n “How?” snapped Celador.\n “Mi am Yar’s apprentice. Mi will curse the Rex for lat.”\n Celador grinned. He could peep the gre\nNargii’s eyes, “Is that so? Very well then. You put a curse on the Rex for me, and all you see here is yours. Make him weak and fragile. Sap his strength for me. Is that possible, shaman?”\n Nargii peep’d to the bone token dangling from his staff, agh\nnodded, “Yub. That can be done.”\n “Excellent!” Celador picked up a hefty bag of gold, agh tossed it to Nargii, “Consider this a down-payment.”\n Nargii caught the bag with an eager grin. “Rulg, albai. Mi will do hosh work. Lat will peep.\" He turned,\nhurried out of the cave and back to his village with one final “Gug’ye.”\n            <O>\n Arriving back at the village near sunrise, a morning watchman stopped him to ask about the bag agh where he’d been all night. Nargii blah’d him the same lie a\nblah’d the night watchmen, “Mi was hunting. The bag is full of sand rats. Mi couldn’t peep much else out there last night.” With a shrug the watchman nodded, agh went back about his business. Nargii hurried back to his blarg, agh set the bribe money aside\nto begin his first attempt at a real curse. He held up his staff so the bone token hung at eye level, agh began chanting to Ogrol, the spirit of despair, entrapment, agh most relevant to Nargii’s situation, sapped strength. His chant, however, was\nawkward, as he had trouble remembering important Old Blah words. Eventually, he came to the climax of his ritual, agh raised his staff in the air. Then, the token on his staff began to vibrate, agh soon shattered, sending a splintered fragment into his\narm. Nargii then dropped his staff, as a horrible pain filled his body. His bones became brittle agh porous within him, agh he was left hunched over on the floor, too weak to even stand. \n Then, as if he had been waiting for him outside, the tent flap\nflung open, agh Yar stepped inside. He glared down at Nargii with intense disapproval, “Blah to mi, Nargii. Is now the time to take bribes? Is now the time to sacrifice our honor for filthy gain? Are the uruks to be as the gazat, led about by greed? Are\nwe to be as the shara, lusting after power? Perhaps, lat think, it is the time to be as the albai, sneaking around in the shadows. Peep now how the curse lat intended for the Rex has fallen upon lats own head, traitor.”\n Nargii peep’d up in astonishmen\n\"How..?\" he asked shakily in his weakened state. Immediately, Yar’s sister, Voltha the Huntress, entered from behind, tossing the corpse of Celador before Nargii. A thick orcish lig protruded from his neck.\n   \"Mi do a lot of night hunting,\" interjecte\nVoltha, “but mi had never peep’d lat hunting at night before. Mi peep’d lat in the cave with the albai, Nargii...”\n Nargii was speechless, agh without defense. “Nargii,” blah’d Yar, “lat have nub integrity or uprightness in spirit, so lat will have nub\nbody. Lat am a whitewash, agh a traitor to this village and the whole Uzg.”\n Nargii was dragged to the center of the village, agh tied up, too weak to resist. He was sentenced to flat by de-boning. His screams echoed throughout the desert, as his fragi\nbones were pulled from his body in front of the whole village until he was flat. Afterwards, his bones were piled up atop the ruins of his destroyed blarg, agh burned with his ill-gotten gold. The rest of his body was chopped up, agh thrown to the pigs.\nSo, to this day, de-boning remains the Yar clan’s punishment for the absolute nub’hoshest of offenders.", ""),
