        StaticBook("The Kaedreni", "§bThe Bloody Baron", "22nd of Malin's Welcome,\n                              1519\n\n\n         THE STATE\n             OF THE\n           KAEDRENI\n\n\n       by Titus de Sola\n\nDust scatters on the horizon.  Hooves on valley upon valley thunder to a halt, beasts glaring across to the other, obeying their rider's whims.  Carrion birds dare defy the scorching heat and incandescence of the regal\nWhite Sun.  A scene is painted fo\n\nyou that will set the stage for generations to come. But no matter the turmoil nor the degeneracy of either side I find my conscience bothered greatly by the fact that the blood of Kaedrin and Rhoswen turns against eachother under both banners of the war.\n\nThe Kaedreni have opted to let their luster become a finely honed weapon, a greatsword, to be used at the whims of Savoie and Sarkozic alike, though disrespected moreso on behalf of the latter.\n\nBut only the Kaedreni allowance of such be at fault.\n\nAnd it is a weapon, forged inside the lungs of hell, wrought by tens of thousands of the strongest men of our Kaedreni race, that we do now let the others, who have forgotten their place as our vassals, use freely and improperly against eachother,\n\nlike children who can scarcely pick up a fork.  That weapon is our martial mastery and sheer superiority in waging conflicts, gained by our diligence in carrying out the will of God and His first, the line of Horen, and then of Chivay.  No other Race\n\namongst Men can maintain aggression nor offensive nor conviction in the way that the Kaedreni man can; it is simply impossible.  They cry as they attempt to emulate our finest military traditions that they have inherited our place as the finest amongst\n\nmankind, but like all others their walls crumble, their people degenerate, they turn to banditry and villainy, and they destroy themselves from the inside.\n\nThey frequently seek our service as their officers, but they will never be heirs to Kaedrin.\n\nThe free men of the Rose have turned on eachother, instead of their proper enemies: those who envy all we Kaedreni have done.\nPeter must weep, if the dead weep, at the state of our people, our Kaedreni race.\n\nI would weep in his place, bu\n\nwould also hope.\n\nI would not see the blood of Kaedrin wither into nothing in the same way that the Horens did.  Even the common Kaedreni man, at this stage, is more bloodworthy than your mainstay Orenian lord, and no doubt far more\n\nthan those savages of color out of the Duchy of Adria whom want to eat us alive. I would not see our people fall to that fate. I would endeavor to stop it.\n\nI, De Sola, would fight to the death if need be for our people, so long as it granted my\n\ncountrymen victory.\n\nAnd though when fighting we are naturally greater, fight, we must not always.\n\nWe need consolidation.  Ties of marriage between our Kaedreni Houses.  Living space, territories, slaves.  We cannot forget what it means to bea\n\nour name.  We were made to militarily administrate the world, and everyone else living in it are either ourselves, our allies, or they are rebels.  There is no other alternative.\n\nWhich of the three are you, friendo?\n\nMy love and my strongest wishes to those whom this letter concerns.  May it find you in good health, and in the mind for a change of the course of our history.  Hear out this message, brothers, hear out this message and bring Creeping Death to them all.\n\n\nBy Owyn's guidance, we will prevail.\n\nAve, true to Kaedrin!\n\n                         T.d.S", ""),
        StaticBook("Ragnar De Autson", "§bJudas Agnarsson", "Saint Ragnar de Autson IV\n\n\"Those who are free of resentful thoughts, surely find peace.\"\n\n1315-1340\n\nRagnar de Autson IV was a famous saint in the realm of Aegis.\n\nHe considered himself virtuous because he never smoked nor drank.\n\nA brave man he was, he lived in settlement north of Oren called Crimson Vale.\n\nOverun by the Undead in 1340 as part of their conquest for the North.\n\nRagnar wore that of a noble. \nDifferent rumours were passed on about Ragnar:\n\n\"I heard he slaughtered fifty undead beings before dying!\"\n\n\"Ragnar?..Aye I know of him, my father told me he fled the Undead attack at Crimson Vale and took all his gold with him!\"\n\n\"Ragnar was a foolish man, he had a varacious eagerness for gold and valuables during the battle of the Undead.\"\n\nHow can one be such a saint if he was known for being so materialistic. \n\nRagnar was none of the above, however the rumours are slightly true.\n\nRagnar was very apprehensive when he heard New Alstion had fallen from the Undead.\n\nDespite his stressing each day towards the people of Crimson Vale about moving further up North; they did not pay attention to Ragnar.\n\nRagnar fled Crimson Vale and built a log cabin not far from his town.\n\nEach saints week a new person would visit Ragnar asking for advice; as they too would like to leave the city and join him.\n\nRagnar encouraged the people who visited him to stay inside Crimson Vale, and fight for their town against the Undead army.\n\nRagnar was a man of peace and prayed the Undead would not conquer his town.\n\nA man: Thomas Broderick sent word to Ragnar pleading for his assistance as the Undead had unexpectedly turned up and raided the town. Killing innocent children/mothers/fathers etc.\n\nRagnar prayed to God, he clasped his hands together and pursed his\n\nlips. \n\n\"If my people call for my name, shall humble themselves and pray and seek my face and turn from their wicked ways; then I will hear from The Seven Skies and I will forgive their sin and I will shed my blood for their land.\"\n\nOnce Ragnar was finished praying, he took a Ceremonial Sword, sheathed in a gilded scabbard and clipped it to his belt.\n\nHe put on his chainmail haulberk, and his iron greaves. He put on his custom forged Morion helmet made from stainless steel.\n\nHe clutched his kite shield and marched out from his cabin, heading towards Crimson Vale.\n\nFrom a distance he could see smoke had emerged from the village. \n\nHowever Ragnar broadened his shoulders, and a daunted look grew on his expression.\n\nRagnar took large strides as he entered Crimson vale, a miasmic smell of stale and decomposing bodies arised in the atmosphere.\n\nRagnar jeered, as he paced forth into the town.\nHe fought against herds of Undead spawns until he was too weak to fig\n\nRagnar collapsed upon the soil as his Ceremonial Rapier fell from his grasp. \nHis helmet rolled from his head, and blood gushed from his face trickling into the roots beneath him.\n\nAn Undead Spawn hoisted it's warhammer into the air, screeching before plummeting it down upon Ragnars chestplate caving it in effortlessly.\n\nRagnar cried in pain, as the spawn grabbed his pony tail and lifted it into the air, making Ragnar clamber t\n\nfeet.\n\nThe spawn withdrawed scythe from it's sheathe, and abruptly decapitated Ragnars head from his body.\n\nThe people of Crimson Vale shifted their gaze towards the horrifying sight.\n\nThey did not make a comeback nor did they attempt to with their greatest knight deceased dishonourably.\n\nRagnar's head was placed upon a stake, and his sword tossed into lava.\n\nIt wasn't until 1426 Ragnar was finally pronounced a Saint for his bravery in Crimson Vale.\n\n\n\nA fiction story \n\nBy Judas Agnarsson.", ""),
        StaticBook("Edict-Banard", "§bTheran", "Edict of Banard, 1543\n¬†\nIssued and Confirmed by His Imperial Majesty the Holy Orenian Emperor John I Frederick, 14th of Snow's Maiden, 1543\n¬†\nDEAREST SUBJECTS OF ALL STATUS\n\nWith the recent vassalization of Ard Ghorrock following the abdication of Frederick Rovin from the now defunct state of Vandoria, the question of what to do with these lands has been raised. To address this question, the borders have\n\nbeen drawn for a new southern vassal state, the Duchy of Banard, to be ruled by the Emperor's son Prince Charles, Duke of Furnestock. The acting seat of said polity is to be the mountain fortress of Ard Ghorrock. May he guide and expand these lands\n\nexcellently, and in doing so, better both Banard and Oren.\n¬†\nIN NOMINE DEI,\n¬†\nHis Imperial Majesty John I Frederick of the House of Horen, by the Grace of GOD, Holy Orenian Emperor and rightful Emperor of Aeldin, forever\n\nAugust, Apostolic King in Oren, of Savoy, Westphalia, Kaedrin, Haense, Renatus and Salvus, Defender of the Faith, Grand Duke of Kaedrin, Duke of the Crownlands, Carimea, Erochland, Kingston and Leone, Margrave of Greater Kreden, Count of Felsen,\n\nLouvain, Beauclair, Wett and Metz, Sovereign of Humanity and Protector of the Elves, etcetera.", ""),
        StaticBook("Teuton & Wizards", "§bIsa", "A man of the magical type enter the hall. He would look up to the Hochmiester and question why he allows Orcs into his ranks. There seems to be a disruption in the room as the Hochmiester's daughter, Camille is upset about a Sariant calling her a spoiled\n\nlittle shit.\nAs the Hochmiester Sergei calms his daughter, the Wizard appears baffled as he is witnessing the Third Teutonic Order before his very eyes. He stated \"It is as if the dead souls of my kin rise from your vessles.\"\n\nIgnoring his daughter's cries, the Hochmiester looks down from his dias towards the Wizard, unsure of what a strange \"entity\" has to do before him.\nThe Wizard would look towards the Hochmiester with a relentless gaze, staff in hand.\n\nLooking apprehensive, the Hochmiester would push himself up from his seat, making his way down the few steps of his throne. He would question what the meaning of his unexpected meeting is.\nThe Wizard would say that time has brought him before the\n\nHochmiester. He noted that the followers behind him were simply wandering souls.\nFinally the Wizard said \"I come here to judge you.\"\nConfused, Hochmiester Sergei asks what right the Wizard had to judge him.\nThe Wizard said \"By the right of\n\ncreation.\"\nThe old man behind the wizard would begin to wisper to himself as the Wizard and Sergei speak.\nThe Wizard told Sergei he believed that the Teutonic has one end goal and that goal was to end creatures such as the undead.\n\nThe Hochmiester answered Yes to the Wizard's question and that the order protects his fellow kin, the Hansetians.\nThe Wizard asks if the people of this court are well looked after and they all agreed.\nHe then asks how far the Hochmiester may go to\n\nto protect thoses from Hanseti.\nSergei replied, \"As far as the oath allows us to go.\"\nThe Wizard asks to hear the Oath.\nSariant Knight Volten read out the oath to the Wizard.\nThe Wizard would speak some stange words, \"Beliae\n\nSergei would go on to explain how the people of this order are the last of the dying breed of the Tuetonic Order. The Wizard would go on to test the Hochmiester's legitimacy for he has seen flase Hochmiesters in the past. In particular, he named one to be\n\nJonathan black and the rest of the post Dreadfort leaders.\nThough he noted that the Hochmiester Sergei resembled the sway of the former Hochmiester M. Denurem. He stated \"You seem a Hochmiester to me.\" Then he requested to meet with the\n\nHochmiester Sergei Vladov again in the near future when he calls upon Humanity.\nHe asks Sergei if he can trust him to come forth to this meeting under the eyes of Celestia and Hanseti.\nThe Wizard notes all the order are welcome to attend.\n\nThe Hochmiester appeared to be confused before replying \"Ja beliae asere eru bruddaz\".\nThe Wizard would then try to depart before Sergei stopped him and questioned him on why the Wizard seems to know more on the Order than the Order knows of these\n\nentities. The Wizard would state that he studied the Hochmiester and the Order, for he spent his life honing the mind, body and soul.\nThe Hochmiester unsure of the Wizard, question how he should believe what the Wizard has told him.\n\nThe Order has been in isolation since their reformation. \nThe Wizard assured that he did not wish to lead Sergei or the order anywhere.\nHe noted that during the late 14 Century the Teutonic Order discovered the knowledge of the\nAncient Hansetia\n\nthat they left behind.\nIn time he will reveal the knowledge to the Hochmiester but today will not be the day.\nThe Wizard he must be off for he is in search of the Mountain's Crown.\nFinally, Sergei asked one last question and that was for the na\n\nof this unknown Wizard.\nThe Wizard replied, \"Know not a name, but my aura. You can see my name. Feel it as you gaze.\" The Old man behind the Wizard spoke out, saying the Wizard should give a more direct answer.\nThe Wizard replied, that in this\n\npoint in his existance, his name should not be remembered. \nThe Hochmiester would ask when he would appear next, for he wished to hear the knowledge of his ancestors.\nThe Wizard replied that he would come after he had climbs this mysterious mountai\n\nand sit upon its crown.\nSergei would then go on to say how the king of the Westerlands spoke of the Wizaed once. The Wizard identified the King to be Ciaus. He said how Caius was a good man and carried the weight of the late Hochmiester G. Marius.\n\nSergei said how Caius noted that \"there will be a time when he'll be need to serve his purpose. He believed supporting our reformation was his purpose.\"\nThe Wizard said that he adviced the king to stay a King and not seek an Empiral title.\n\nJust as he wishes to see Sergei never rely on anyone but his Order.\nThe Hochmiester said that he will wait the wizards reappears, if the wizard if not just leading them in circles.\nThe Wizard said, \"When I reach the Mountain's Crown. I'll be able\n\nto answer with more clarity. For now, I'm unbound.\" And with that being said the entity departed from the keep.\n\n-14th of the Grand Harvest. 1608\n\nAuthor: Isa", ""),
        StaticBook("Weapons of war", "§bWalter Marshall", "Weapons Of Modern War\n\n By Walter Marshall\n\nVolume I, Weapons of the Golden Crow\n\n23rd of The First Seed\n\nKaedreni Longsword\n\nThe Kaedreni longsword is a staple weapon of all Highlander forces. Able to be wielded with a single arm and carry a shield alongside it. This razor sharp blade is a formidable weapon when used by a professional.\n\n\nHaensetic Arming Sword \n\nThe Haensetic Arming Sword is the primary weapon of the Brotherhood, It is given to fresh Man-at-arms upon acceptance, Although many prefer to use the Kaedreni Longsword when they-\n\nachieve a higher rank and gain access to more weapons, no man in the Brotherhood forgets their Haensetic Arming sword.\n\nScylfing Hand Axe\n\nThe Scylfing Hand Axe is deadly close range weapon popularized and produced in bulk by the Colborn family for use by Haensic soldiers when facing heavily armored foes hand to hand.\n\nTheir expert make and craftsmanship led to the victory of many Haensetic battles. A true staple of the Brotherhood and Colborn artisanry.\n\nStrannik Lance\n\nThe Strannik Lance is a formidable halberd that was adapted from the heavy Ruskan cavalry known as the Stranniks. Now used as a pike in order to fend off cavalry charges,\n\n it is a staple of the Brotherhood of the Golden Crow when fighting those who may be on horseback.\n\nAdunian Claymore\n\nThe Adunian Claymore is a giant sword capable of destroying any foe. Used without a shield, the Claymore is wielded with two hands. If it does not cut through armour, the sheer trauma of being hit by it is enough to stagger a man.\n\nWaldenian Warhammer\n\nThe Waldenian Warhammer is a fairly bulky weapon of significant weight reserved for the slaying of undead or heavily armored opponents. With a blunt face on one half of the head and a piercing crow’s beak on the other.\n\nThe hammer is crafted of the finest northern steel providing a good strong weapon with lots of power and weight behind its swing.\n\nKaedreni Arablest\n\nThe Kaedereni Arablest is a common weapon found during sieges and other large skirmishes, its long range and tough oak stock make it not only durable but deadly.\n\nHowever its increased reloading time and overall bulkiness prevents most soldiers from carrying one when not expecting large conflicts.\n\nBarbovic Lever Crossbow\n\nThe Barbovic Lever Crossbow was made famous during its tenure in the Gold Corps when protecting Felsen from Dreadlanic raids. Carried often by the defenders. The Barbovic Lever Crossbow was a medium power quick reload crossbo\n\nThe men who wielded it praised its ability to fire shots in quick succession while being easy to maneuver within the tight streets and alleys. Mostly used by men on patrol or in urban environments.\n\nBarbovic Shield\n\nWhile many of the  new recruits are given whatever shields are present, that is not the case for the more experienced men of the order. There is a delicate balance between the weight of the shield, and the protection it offers.\n\nThese shields have been forged with that balance in mind, crafted in a curved rectangular fashion. The size offers a fair amount of  mobility to the user, while the curved design is effective at deflecting many different kinds of arrows and projectiles.", ""),
        StaticBook("House Barbanov", "§bWalter Marshall", "History of House Barbanov\n\n\n\nBy Walter Marshall\n\nHOUSE OF BARBOVIC, called BARBANOV in the normal speak, claims lineage from Saint Joren Horenson himself, the first of the High Kings of the North. Further down, the house claims descent or kinship from Barbov the Black, its nominal description,\n\nRovyk the Stern, Saint Julia of Paradisius, Saint Lothar of Balian, Saint Adrian of Leuvaarden, Blessed Jan Jeremi, Blessed Francis the Martyr, Blessed Otto of Vanderfell,\n\n and most importantly, coming from the loins of Exalted Sigismund himself, Prophet of Revelations.\n\nThe House stands as a ducal family in the Holy Kingdom of Oren, with Lord Karl III. holding the duchy of Haense, inherited from his Rovin uncle. Once, however, the family stood as Imperial Princes, the Lord and Lady Barbanov’s father,\n\nAlexander I being Emperor of Oren. Even so, from the Lord and Lady Barbanov’s maternal side, they were once Princes of the Hansetians, being of the now destroyed and defunct Kingdom of Aesterwald.\n\nHOUSE BARBANOV OF HAENSE has no long lineage, that be accustomed to its parent houses of Karovic and Rovin, or its cousins of House de Sarkozy, House of Kovac, or even House of Vladovic.\n\nThe House comes from the loins of EMPEROR ALEXANDER I CARRION and his wife, PRINCESS ARABELLA ROVIN, married per a peace contract between the Empire of Oren and the newly vassalized Kingdom of Aesterwald.\n\nThe union, however, was nay a happy one.\n\nAfter the start of the infamous RENATUS REBELLION, sparked by protests of the arrest of the Grand Knight, the Princess stole the two babes born of her and Alexander’s coupling; the twins Karl and Lorin.\n\n It is unsure how the Princess transferred the babes unnoticed, but eventually the twins found their way under the tutelage of PRINCE OTTO ROVIN, with their father deposed and presumed dead and their mother dying of a hunting accident.\n\nUnder the guise that they were his bastards, the two babes eventually grew from sucking on the teat, to toddlers, and eventual kids, roaming the Waldenian Royal keep aimlessly, as children do.\n\nChange appeared, however, when KING VORON I ROVIN died, replaced by his Raevir-hating son, KING ARN III ROVIN. It is under King Arn’s reign that the Raevir minority of Aesterwald was pushed out, killed, or worse.\n\nHis hate went so far, eventually to curse HIGH PONTIFF DANIEL I, a known Carrion bastard, and declare a schism, putting his own kinsman Hubert as anti-Pontiff. Despite propaganda within the Northern kingdom,  it did not bode well to the populace.\n\nDefeat after defeat, and the SCHISM WAR, as it came to called after the Duchy of Savoy came to aid of the Kingdom of Akovia, rolled on. Desertion from the Waldenian ranks became more frequent, with even the King’s own kin abandoning his unjust cause.\n\nOTTO ROVIN, taking along the child Karl and Lorin, left the Kingdom (before, of course, posting the Condemnation of Arn’s Reign). Otto and the children fled to CYRIAUM, where he took work as the Papal Guard of HIGH PONTIFF DANIEL I.\n\n. It is here where Otto finally told Karl and his sister of his true heretige, where the High Pontiff confirmed as true.\n\nFor the next ten years, Karl trained underneath not only his Uncle, but of the numerous heroes of the SCHISM WAR. As his uncle dealt with the political nature of the Waldenian Realm, Karl fought with the likes of BLESSED EMMA VLADOVIC,\n\nthe woman-warrior with a thirst of heretic blood, the unifier of humanity himself, KING ANDREW I VYRDA, and the current king, KING OLIVER I ASHFORD. From the teachings of King Andrew and King Oliver,\n\nKarl grew to understand the affairs of state and nobility, eventually growing out from the child of a drunken uncle to a successful and witty statesman. It is also during the SCHISM WAR that Karl took up his name BARBANOV,\n\n after urging of his cousin and High Pontiff Daniel I, in honor of BARBOV THE BLACK.\n\nAfter KING ARN III ROVIN died from burning down his precious Vanderfell and Eagle’s Perch, Karl joined his guardian Otto to pick up the pieces. Forming the unsuccessful NORTHERN COMMONWEALTH,\n\nKarl help fight against the Dwarves in the FOURTH DWARF-HUMAN WAR, taking a penchant to the killing of midgets and eventually,\n\n the High Elves who killed the fair KING ANDREW I VYDRA. It is then when Karl, with Vladov allies and the small retinue of the Commonwealth, took the HOLD OF HIEBENHALL, eventually to be named SIEGRAD,\n\nthe new capital of the Northern Realm. Karl established himself as the COUNT OF SIEGRAD thereafter, holding the title to this day.\n\nOnce his guardian and uncle, OTTO ROVIN, left to go chase down rumors of Arn living within the secluded land of AELDIN, Karl took up the reigns of the Commonwealth, disbanding its entirety and eventually to name himself the DUKE OF HAENSE.\n\nAs of now, the DUKE KARL III BARBOVIC stewards his capital of SIEGRAD, one of the most populous cities within the Kingdom, as well as working where his uncle left off: rebuilding the Northern Realm.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Knightly Quest", "§bLyeth", "=======================        Notes upon a\n       Knightly Quest.\n=======================\n\n   We entered the cave at high noon, and found within a fire; The last, we were told, we would see for some time. Those Knights-errant called, servants of the Aenguls, saw no need to rest, and so we did not.\n\n   Venturing on we stepped into a blackened portal, dug into the darkest corner of the cave, as if some spider's nest. Within was a far cry from the light and warmth of the sun and fire we had just abandoned behind us.\n\n   It was darkened within, far more than natural, though not more than any I myself had ever seen. We ventured within, to nigh unfathomable depths. Ever downward.\n\n   There, where we had reached the farthest of the depths,\n\nwe delved into a lightless chasm, even further gone from the light than the last...\n\n   There within we entered a hollowed chamber, a structure sat within it's center. From this came a dim light, easing the darkness around us.\n\n   The construct was of stone, seeming to have been built to either hold or cover something. A thick eldritch fog rolled out from it, obscuring our view.\n\n   This, we were told, was the Black Nexus; Point of imprisonment of the wraithsouls.\n\n   Though ensnared, freeing them would not pass these beings on, but rather return them to their previous forms. The Ascendant was called forth to accompany the Knight alone into the fog.\n\n   Therein laid the inner sanctum of cause, of\n\nthat which had driven those present to be so. Within lay the Soul of Old Lords, and the Nexus itself echoed out an endlessly chattering whisper.\n\n   As the Knight and Ascendant ventured forth, the whispers ceased, only to\n\nbe replaced by the shattering of silence in shout; The voices of the Paladins of Xan, as if calling for help, though neither had need of such. \n\n   Quizzical looks were exchanged between the two, knowing the other hadn't spoken.\n\nAnd so they continued their watch, as the Dwarven Sage and the Knight of Red approached the eldritch artifact, the Black Nexus, the heart of the wraiths. \n\n    The knight kneeled before the Nexus, bowing his head\n\nas if in reverance, much the way I am sure one of the white and gold cloaked men would kneel before a statue of their Patron Aengul.\n\n   The Ascendant dwarf called forth from his Patron her fire, which burned white hot in his hands.\n\nHaving formed it in his grasp, he sent it forth then, into the very heart of the Black Nexus. \n\n   The Aengulic flame blanketed the Nexus, as if to suffocate it, Aeriel's will assuredly clawing to grip it's nails into the souls trapped within.\n\n   Grip did they gain, and with that purchase on the souls, the flame ripped them forth from the Nexus; A shockwave was released, akin to that caused by activation or opening of other powerful relics.\n   In the split second before we were sent hurtling\n\nback, it seemed as if two shadows did depart that place of imprisonment; One, towards the ceiling, and the other, towards the Knight-Errant. \n\n   Swiftly the fire was purged from the surface of the Nexus, unharmed but for a single crack\n\nwhich stretched large across it's surface, like a gaping wound leaking pus.\n\n   The Knight-Errant spoke, no longer the same voice as before. His armor leaked a blackened mist, and the Keeper and Ascendant both\n\nrose forth to challenge him.\n\n   Meanwhile: Observations on the chamber, construct, and Black Nexus.\n\n  Within the chamber lay a myriad of corpses in different states.\n\n   Near the Black Nexus itself sat, kneeled in reverance, a number of corpses face in It's direction, as it having passed while in worship. \n   Evidence seems to point to these nameless worshippers having worshipped the Black Nexus past their\n\ndemise; That is, at the time of their arrival here and final stillness, they were members of the unliving.\n\n   All those gathered had found their final rest in reverence to this; the Black Nexus.\n\n   Set in the center of the chamber, the purpose of the stone construct within the cavern became clear. It was created to support the Black Nexus, which hung from the center of the roof.\n\n   The stone construct itself was well made,\n\nlikely created by the very same ones who now were silent in reverence around it. It was made of many archways, interlocked together to give the utmost support to that which hung within.\n\n  The Black Nexus.\n\n   The Black Nexus was crystalline in form, though fraught with aberrations. Had it a smoother surface, it would resemble a great many things. The Kal'Varak, though on much smaller scale. That would make sense, considering the purpose-\n\nor at least, use, of both of them. \n\n   But the smaller size of it also struck me as reminiscent of a Dragaar's Lexicon, having seen Xandraza's long ago in Asulon. This was my first time seeing one in hundreds\n\nof years, but I still saw a resemblance there.\n\n   Similar purposes of holding, but the Kal'Varak is far more similar to it than a Lexicon, at least in utilization.\n\n   The Nexus, however,\n\ndiffered from the both in the resultant wound from the Ascendant's assault; A cracked fissure within it had formed, weeping a misted form of lifeforce.\n\n   The Paladin Keeper had thrown his spear of light and set forth a great shockwave through the chamber.\n\n   The Knight-errant, true colors revealed, lost his senses, had he any left beforehand.\n\n   The fighting resumed, battle joined with all those of holy inclination present; There came a point where the two paladins were made to collide, the one being thrown back against the Nexus.\n\n   Upon impact, the woman gave\n\na great shriek of pain, not unexpectedly. Her body convulsed controllably and she fell to the ground, unconscious...Or perhaps worse.\n\n   Meanwhile the threads which bound the Gravelord Nimdravur\n\nto our realm were cut loose, and soon he was no more; Not an end met entirely at the blade, though many times he was skewered, but rather simply he fell apart, ash and bits falling loose from him.\n\n   And then our heroes strip-searched, knocked unconscious, and robbed a peaceful librarian woman.\n\nThe End.\n\nOrithur, if the Paladin twits are still allowed in the Library, I'm quitting.", ""),
        StaticBook("Into Nothing", "§bUnknown", "INTO NOTHING\n       Chapter 1\n\nThe first thing that strikes you, is the darkness. The never ending darkness stretched out before you, reaching untill the end of time, but all before you very eyes. I saw it all, The creation and\n\ndestruction of existence itself, and all in the blink of an eye. At first, I was scared, I didn't know what had become of me, or what was to become of me now I was this... monster, or so I belived at the time. They called me Sin, divine retribution\n\nwrapped up in one being. A being bent on the destuction of everything it ever loved, all because noone else would. That's right, we knew it was coming, we had so many chances to stop it. We had so many signs, so many chances that we just\n\nignored and carried on with our pathetic mediocre lives. Our repetative routine, and an existance purely to pass on our DNA.. Pittifull. I wasn't a bad person, I didn't deserve what I have become, yet, I feel that it was necessary, I destroy the\n\ncorruption in the world. Cleanse it of it's sin. Ironic, really, I destroy and take lives, to save and bring more life, purity but at a cost. I couldn't say I don't enjoy it, nor could I say I do, I don't even remember when it was like to express emotions\n\n My mind is a blank, I have memories, memories of emotions, yet they do not create the same feeling now.. My mind is blank, A void. But before all this, I wasn't a monster, I wasn't a saint, either. I lived an ordinary life. The easiest way to put it is:\n\n I died. I can't explain the feeling, of dying, perhaps because  There was no feeling.. Yet, there was, a small sense of relief, all the sorrow, and pain had left me, I was pure, and enlightened, no longer burdened by the world. A faint voice echoed throu\n\nmy soul. A calming voice, I trusted it. It convined me to embrace the light, let it take hold of my body.. And I did just that. It took what felt like eternity, but in reality, was few seconds. The pain, it tore through me, tearing my spirit apart..\n\nBut after the pain.. came none, I was immortal, and nothing would ever harm me again.\n\n      Chapter 2\nI took just 16 minutes for the world, ever nation destroyed by my omnipotence. I turned the world from what was a war filled abyss of chaos.. To tranquility, a world so peacefull, even the garden of Eden would look pathetic to it.\n\nBut I guess that's because it was devoiant of life, well, almost. There were a few surviors.. Whom had set up pathetic shelters for any living they found. Petty mortals, trying desperately to continue their petty existance.\n\nAs I destroyed the last remants, my blood thirst grew, It seemed humanity was not enough, the billions of lives I have destroyed in moments and yet I was not satisfied.\nI had wondered what I had become. I thought I was a monster.\n\nThe truth is, Im not a monster, Nor a bringer of death. I'm just the bridge, for those deemed worthy. For it is only in death, in which we finally acheive what we sought in life.\n\nPain and sorrow melted away in an instant.. That who brings this, is not a monster.. More a divine being, A God.", ""),
        StaticBook("Rodrik and Bear", "§bRodrik Vernick", "Rodrik and the bear\n\nWritten by Rodrik\n\n\n One day a boy named Rodrik went out on a adventure. Rodrik went through the woods for days untill he found a cave. Rodrik thought about entering the cave for some time until he thought that there might be some cool treasure. Inside the cave was\n\nalmost empty but in the corner of the cave he could see a bear almost the size a dwarf. \"human\" the bear said \"can you help me with a problem i have?\". \"What is your problem bear\" said Rodrik. \"I need you to go to a small village not to far from here and\n\nbuy me some Honey to put onto my toast\" said the bear. \"Yes I can help you bear I will go and get you some honey\". On his way out the bear gave him a bag of coins to buy the honey with then he made his way to the exit waveing bye to the bear.\n\ntwo days later Rodrik made it to the village and went to the market *half of the books pages are missing*", ""),
        StaticBook("Bread for a ?", "§bDaeris Maehr'onn", "~'~'~' Bread '~'~'~\n~'~'~' And A '~'~'~\n~'~'  Question '~'~\n\nBy:\n\nDaeris Maehr'onn\nDedicant\n\nQ: What do you think\nof the Druidic Order?\n\n1.\n\"The Druidic Order is an order that has done much for the descendants. It is an honorable Order with a rich history and righteous passion for the defense of the world's balance.\"\n\n- Heshakomeu Sohae\n\n2.\n\"I have known many among your Order and none disappoint. You are a noble Order with a purpose and cause. The realms of present and past owe you a debt. And Gi' owes me a cup of tea.\"\n\n- Hosper Blackthorn\n\n3.\n\"I think it's a bunch of fuckers who sit in rooms all day making babies.\"\n\n- Kairn Calithil\n\n4.\n\"A bunch of scum who aggrandize false gods.\"\n\n- Allynial Rosenheart\n\n5.\n\"The Druidic Order has failed in their task of maintaining balance.\"\n\nQ2: \"How come?\"\n\nA: \"Well, they just have. They're too carefree.\"\n\n- Archibald\n\n6.\n\"I think the Druidic Order has the right mindset on what's truly important.\"\n\nQ2: \"And what mindset is that?\"\n\nA: \"Protecting our home.\" She motioned to nearby nature.\n\n- Indra\n\n7. \n\"Well.. I don't really know much about them.\"\n\nQ2: \"What do you know?\"\n\nA: \"The one's I've seen seem kind, I suppose.\"\n\n- Anastasia Steelwall\n\n8.\n\"I think it's Great.\"\n\nQ2: \"How come?\"\n\nA: \"Aspects, I can't decide. I'd have to say the people. The Druids are some of the most friendly people I have met.\"\n\n- Raine Winterleaf", ""),
        StaticBook("Scholar", "§bCalenias", "A light cam from an unknown scource, forcing me to wake. I opened my eyes and saw sandstone, lots of it. And precious materials. Wherever I was, it must've been VERY expensive to build. I walked through the empty halls and came out at a dock. I had some\n\nurge to board this one boat, it had white sails and was made of dark wood, and it was heading to a place called \"Tahn Docks\". I didn't recognise the name, but it sounded familiar. Once the ship docked after the journey, I was greatful for I had become\n\ndeathly ill. Seasickness the crew called it. When I was done wretching and vommiting into the water, as if on instinct I followed a path that seemed right to my body, but confusing for my head. I got onto a blue cart that said \"Library of Dragur\". It\n\nSeemed right to me. On the journey I got sick, again. And decided it was just motion sickness. I no longor like boats or carts. But then the cart stopped at a gargantuan building, of a pearly white hue. It was the most beautiful thing I had ever seen. It\n\nfelt like coming home after a long journey. I walked inside, and it was indeed a library, but it had a beautiful statue, of, SOMETHING, over a  small wall-less room with a desk, with people gathered around it. Then, as if I had been planning for years,\n\nasked to be a scholar. I didn't know what a scholar was until just then, but at that moment my head associated the word scholar with intelligent, and I then knew scholars were staff at this library. So, then one of the scholars talked to me about it, and\n\ngave me a thing describing how to BECOME a scholar. I was ecstatic when one of the criteria was to write a book. So I decided to write, what you are hopefully reading at this very moment.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Wolf and Pig", "§bKatona Therenal", "The pig and the wolf\n-------------------\n\nOnce upon a time, there was a pig, a pig that enjoyed butchering those who would upset him. Opening them up, trying to find what make them 'tick'. The pig once, had a friend. That friend was the wolf.\n\nStrange, for such different creatures to be friends. But that's what they were. The wolf kept his anger contained, taking it out on himself. One day, the pig threatened to butcher the wolve's family. The wolf flared with anger, but as he usually does,\n\ntook it out himself. The wolf started hating the Pig, losing all sort of friendship he ever had towards him. The pig took this as a sign of hatred, and so, he attacked his family. The wolf twitched with anger. As the pig was arrested the wolf went to end\n\nthe pig's miserable life. He was not able to do so, and was pushed away. As the pig made his escape through death, the wolf followed. And once they were face to face, the wolf took his chance. Using his new found magic he immobilized the pig\n\nHe then started to butcher the pig, as he did so with his family. Anger flared through his eyes as he skinned the pig, cutting off his ears, feeding them to the pig, the pig squealed, asking for a swift death. The Wolf wouldn't allow it, after hours of\n\ntorture, the wolf left the pig to hurt, feel the anger and pain the wolf had put up with. \n\nThis is the story of the Wolf and the Pig\n\nThis is the story of Katona and Miran.", ""),
        StaticBook("Youth and Age", "§bMason Sezarius", "Age and Youth\nBoth different things,\nbut at the same time they're as similar as one can get.\nYouth is full of pleasance, Age is full of care. Youth is like the morning sun, Age is like the sun setting.\nOld age should burn and rave at the cl\n\nthough wise men at their end know dark is right, because their words had forked no lightning. Good men, the last wave by, crying how bright their frail deeds might have danced in a green bay, but wild men who caught and sang the sun in flight and learn,\n\ntoo late, they grieved it on its way. Grave men, near death, who see with blinding sight, blind eyes could blaze like torches and be bright.", ""),
        StaticBook("Smurgla's Task", "§bSmurgla", "Smurgla's Task\n\nSmurgla has gone to the Mali'Aheral city and scattered four good amounts of bonemeal about, being accompanied by a guard, of course. Smurgla has allowed for more flowers and grass to appear!\n\nSmurgla has visited Aesterwald now, planting a flower every so often, but not in the snow! Smurgla met a few nice people and taught them about nature's important.\n\nSmurgla planted regrew some dirt back to grass and Smurgla cleaned up some yucky grass near Orcies' cities. Smurgla had no one to sign off on this one.\n\nSmurlga made grass grow in yucky cave near D'orf city. D'rofs try to kill Smurgla, though, so Smurgla had to do Drood task in cave. No one to sign off on this one.\n\nSmurgla used the last few pieces of bonemeal in the Drood's grove, itself. Smurgla thinks it is best to give back to where your roots are from. Smurgla planted a few flowers near the gate.\n\nSignatures:\n\nMali'Aheral:\n-- Talimeracahe\n\nAesterwald:\n-- Haleth MII", ""),
        StaticBook("A Better World", "§bOrithur, The Archivist", "To Further The World\n\n - - - - - - - - - - -\n\nChapter 1 - Time\n\nWithin our world exists an eternal, unquestionable constant, and it is named 'Time'. This variable is the single most destructive thing any reality may ever\n\n hope to observe. Time withers trees, its crumbles rock, and it takes lives. But time brings about discovery and new life, it ushers existence itself. And while time will forever establish events as inevitable, it can never entirely control them. This,\n\nremains up to us. Whether Dwarf, Orc, Human or Elf the path is never fully laid for us, and it is our combined efforts that see the future carved in our image.\n\nIt is no lie to say that the image we carve today, is one of war, distrust and self\n\ndestruction. We are inherently flawed in that way, but with effort we can overcome this.\n\nOur world holds so much dear potential, and every day I see glimpses of it in the smallest of ways. In how a child's curiousity beams as it learns upon the\n\nnature of a beast we as mortalkind have seen driven to near extinction - the Dragon.\n\nIn how we theorise our own existence and purpose through science and religion. In how we seek moral absolutes and put value upon justice, honour and sanctity.\n\nWith our dear friend Time, these morals continue to grow. With each day a small part of our ignorance to the world and its design dies, but this is a slow happening. For over a thousand years the change has been minimal, because we do not as a collective,\n\n seek change. We find bliss in ignorance, we are content with our limited knowledge, and we are blind to what it means to progress.\n\nIf we do not teach our young of the ways of the world, its cultures and its beliefs, then we continue to allow this\n\nignorance to fester and grow throughout the generations. Our minds are warped to false realities constructed by our fathers and mothers. We know only what they believe is worth knowing, and from that we can never grow.\n\nChapter 2 - The Mortal Truth\n\nIt is said that our actions speak louder than words, but this could not be more false in the areas of education and study. What we say to our fellow descendant can sway them from one path to another.\n\nOur words to each other can validate what we percieve as truths, or shun what we know to be fact. The pressure of our peers can push us into ignorance, even if we are aware it is happening we are seldom able to prevent it. Only by dedicating ourselves to\n\nconcepts, to study, and to learning can we hope to break free of this ignorance. And only be spreading it can we hope to change the world.\n\nAll races and descendants are drawn to violence and war as a means of solving problems. While it is true that\n\nthis may apply to some more than others, it is a regular truth regardless. I consider myself both a teacher and a philosopher, and yet I am no exception to this rule. I have seen life taken and taken it myself. But it always means nothing. It can never\n\nnot be a waste.\n\nIf we were instead to show mercy where we would bring about judgement, then we prove ourselves greater than ever before. This is however, a foolish dream, for if we wish to be realistic our advance into progress must be slow.\n\nWe must be prepared for small changes at a time, small ways in which our lives alter to bring about the progression of our kind.\n\nInstead of judging, we should be examining. Instead of war, we should have dispute. Instead of light, and dark, we should\n\nsimply have life. To categorise things as good and evil, is to make a prejudgement upon them, disregarding intention and character for an 'absolute' overpowering archetype based on a system of simplicity.\n\nWe must understand and appreciate that\n\nour world is filled with greys, nothing can ever be black and white, all things are situational and all things require consideration. There exist wars today between dark and light, but the reasoning is so upsettingly simple. Dark fights light, thus light\n\nfights dark and vice versa. One side exists to counter the other in and endless, pointless dispute that has gone on for centuries upon centuries.\n\nThe Mortal Truth, is that unless we actively choose to overcome it, is that we are born blind. If we ever\n\nwish for perfect sight, we must first learn to see. This task alone is a difficult one, but what makes it more difficult is that it is not enough. We must then teach others as we do ourselves, a sense of unity is more than essential.\n\nChapter 3 - Fear\n\nFear is a primary blockade in our paths of enlightenment. Fear is brother to discrimination and prejudice. Fear is an emotional response to intellectual situations. Fear is the ultimate primal instinct, and while we may rely on it as a\n\nmeans of survival, to depend on it entirely is to become primal ourselves. We must learn to let go of out instincts and experiment with trust. Trust earns itself in return, much like how violence can only lead to more violence. Trust will eliminate fear,\n\nand then it will grow. I have mentioned frequently of a unity amongst all things, and how it is necessary if mortalkind is ever to hope to advance intellectually in the days to come. \n\nThis unity is built on trust, but trust will never flourish\n\nunless we let it. Unless we force ourselves into positions of trust, and be the first to extend the hand, it can never bloom as the flower it has the potential to be.\n\nNow this is not to say that fear is a useless nor negative aspect of the mortal mind.\n\nTo act in fear is often safe, and has us cautious in times of danger. But one must learn where to draw the line, we must discover at what point does fear no longer help us, but hinder us. There is no specific answer to this question, it is yet another\n\n thing that we as a species must ask ourselves, and make our own judgement on. It is this ideal of self analysis and philosophy that will drive us toward reason and logic. To believe that a single mind holds all the answers, is a simple act of ignorance.\n\nChapter 4 - Culture and Belief\n\nAll across our known world, a variety of varying cultures and beliefs flourish in independence. And while some would argue that to own a distinctive belief or culture is positive, I would argue that we allow these views\n\nto overwhelm all others. We accept what we have been taught to accept as fact, no matter how flawed it may seem. Metztli worship is almost entirely contained to the Kharajyr race, while Cannonism is almost entirely human. The reason that beliefs are so\n\nisolated to individual races, is that they are part of the culture - they are taught to their children as fact and thus grow knowing nothing else. \n\nI admit that many beliefs hold merit, and some may even accurately describe the state of the world we live\n\nin (though none may ever hope to prove it). But I do not believe that any particular religion in itself can claim to be factual when each religion is so incredibly biased toward its racial origin.\n\nWe will believe in what benefits us directly.\n\nWe let faith in taught ideals bring us to war and on occasion, the very brink of destruction. Many would die proudly in the name of what their fathers taught them, but for what reason? As much as we may admire our elders, they are only as flawed as we\n\nare, they will never find truth where we cannot.\n\nBut regardless of this most obvious fact, we fight on regardless. I consider that it is perhaps because we have no alternative, and that confuses us. If we cannot fight for what we believe in simply\n\nbecause we cannot believe in it, then what is there that is worth fighting for? If there is no Creator and no Seven Skies, when our lives are spent what would it all mean?\n\nThis returns us to fear. Fear of the unknown. Fear of a race or culture we do not\n\nunderstand, and fear of a world that we may one day understand far too well.\n\nChapter 5 - Oblivion\n\nAnd thus we must consider the inevitable, one of the few unquestionable truths of the world - Death.\n\nThe death of an enemy, ensures that he will never speak, nor raise a sword against you again,\n\nbut the death of a friend or a brother, will bring about spite and acts of vengence. \nWe like to believe that death is a conclusion, but it is only ever another move on the great game of life, one man's death means another man's quest. And when a game of\n\ndeath is played, there can never be a winner. \nMany believe that death is simply the transition from one life to the next, whereas others believe that death is a simple nothing, an absolute oblivion - and thus we begin to connect the dots between our\n\nmortal aspects: time, truth, fear and faith. All of them are connected to death, and all of them rely on it.\n\nWe often judge a man by his duration and not by his achievements. We often use death as a means to justify achievement itself. Death is to us,\n\na restart. It is the start of an infinite cycle that we have been playing out since the very first death upon our world.\n\nWe fear it, we desire it upon others, we do not understand it. Conflict stems from death, and leads to death. But I ask you to think\n\nnot on oblivion, but on its opposite. We focus so much on the end and forget that we are alive. Yes, an end will come, it always does, but we cannot allow this end to negate the life.\n\nIf we were to accept death as a mysterious inevitability and draw our\n\nattention to means of bettering our lives without death, then we would find not only a more intelligent world, but a more fruitful one. We could make new discoveries, spend time learning tolerance to each others faiths and cultures, learn of the world we\n\nlive in instead of the one that may exist after death.\n\nYes, one day we may truly understand what it means to die, and what it means to commit another to the grave. But we will never understand death if we do not first understand life in its fullest.\n\nChapter 6 - The Conclusion\n\nLearn to use time as a tool, and not to watch it as a clock. Let age not determine proximity to death or naivety of youth, but instead the total sum of your experiences. Where the old are wise and the young are\n\nuninfluenced and uncorrupted.\n\nLearn to understand the Mortal Truth as a flaw to be overcomed through great effort, and not an unquestionable fact of existence. A truth can change should we allow it, our design does not dictate our destiny, and though\n\nwe may refuse to believe that we can overcome our nature, know that it is possible, and know that we as a species have the power to redefine ourselves, and redefine truth.\n\nLearn to use fear situationally, and to sacrifice it in an act of risk so that we\n\nmay leave the boundries of comfort in ignorance, and experience true friendship and alliances that we would otherwise neglect on instinct.\n\nLearn that Faith and Culture are both situational, and can never fully justify an existence as fact,\n\nnor justify the will of any man or nation. We must discover the many forms that faith takes and learn to appreciate them all, for in each there is a hopeful beauty, but we cannot continue to let it consume us entirely. Once more, faith must be recieved in\n\nmoderation, and not allow it to embody us.\n\nLearn to neglect death as a means to an end, and understand it to be an end in itself. We must accomplish all we can before death comes, and never seek to hasten it. For to hasten death upon ourselves or others\n\nis the ultimate form of self destruction, and it is what has torn our world apart for many centuries.\n\nThere is an inherent beauty in all aspects of our being. We are far from perfect, but perfection exists within us. We seek good, but we must learn to\n\ntell the difference between good for ourselves and good for the world. To achieve this, is no easy task - it will require great communication and great sacrifice, to put the ideals of others before our own, but in return you will recieve the same.\n\nEven on the path of betterment, many will oppose you. If you allow yourself to buckle under those that deny change, then you exist as one of the influenced. To be outspoken against a world you know could be better, this is true good, this is true life.\n\n     Befriend Time\n     Change Truth\n     Conquer Fear\n     Control Faith\n     Despise Death\n\n\n- Orithur, Founder of the Library of Dragur.", ""),
        StaticBook("War Orphans", "§bAlexandra", "We see the world as a changing landscape as every day passes. Upon the time of this work being penned, the city of Adelburg on the Isle of Tahn stands, a new hope for the downtrodden and divided human populations. Adelburg is a reminder of humanity.\n\nHowever, the world’ll change. Soon, people will vy for said city to take on a new banner. By any means, the world has changed. Years ago, the city of Johannesburg was obliterated by jealousy, and cowardice.\n\nThousands of lives lost unreasonably due to the ideals of the few. By these standards, the world was changed. Refugees filled the world, war orphans found no home, and starved. The world truly was a hard place for many.\n\nThe life of being a War Orphan is hard. Everything you ever knew about life was gone, when city life was completely exterminated. No more family, no more friends, just yourself, the woods, and struggling to feed yourself.\n\nI had to live alone for most of my early life. I was twelve years old when I found the city of Metz in the Kingdom of Lorraine. There, I lived a weak, malnourished life. I subsided off of whatever the court of that city would give me, mostly food-scraps.\n\nEvery night, I'm still haunted by them though. The faces of my parents, the city's explosion... I remember it all like the back of my hand. I can't sleep some nights, even though that had to be over twenty years ago. I can't forget. Maybe I shouldn't.\n\nThere was a specific occurence on the rooftop of the keep in Lotharingia. It was windy, and my caretaker, a man named Viktor Clarke, had me go up there. The things I saw that night changed me. I used to be so innocent, but now it's all... gone.\n\nMaybe it's true, maybe you really are desensitized to life after years of time in human nations. Beheading, hanging, burning at the pike. All completely credible ways of serving justice. No second chances at life, no goodbyes to your family.\n\nThat's the issue in it all. Nobody has a regard for the ordinary things in life around there. Life is all about the ordinary things, really. The way the sun shines on a warm day, the feeling when you see a friend after a long, hard day... All of it.\n\nWar is a strange thing. People who don't even know each other, murdering for no reason. I'm a pacifist by heart, but I saw war from a distance, once. Horrifying, how men could be reduced to savages by a single order. Maybe there'll be a better future.\n\nMaybe someday the world'll be different. No more war, or conflict. People work together, enjoying each-other's company. No more post-traumatic stress, or unneeded death. Maybe that day is tomorrow, or maybe it's long after we're gone.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOr maybe it'll never come.\n\n\n\n     War Orphans\n\n\n    By Alexandra.", ""),
        StaticBook("Power of Women", "§bJames H. Kyd", "The Power of Women\n\n\n\n\nWritten by:\n\nJ.H.K.\n\n\n\n \"Often times, men attempt to settle matters through shield and sword, yet often times, men forget the power of breast over brawn.\"\n\n Men are pigs. Not in the physical sense, no, lest man has been turned to pig through magical means. However, that is not what this is about.\n\n If you are a man, take a moment to think to yourself about the many women you have (hopefully) encountered\n\n How many of them have you wished to bed? All? None? Most? Chances are, you will have want to have slept with most women you've met.\n\n Now, if you are a woman, think of all the times that a man has tried to bed you. All the time, yes? If not, you're\n\nlikely just some ugly cow that no man wants to be with.\n\n I have witnessed firsthand the control that woman have over men. The thought of a nude woman can simply drive a man to drink himself to insanity and get himself killed. I would know, I have\n\nwitnessed it. Women can tear apart the brotherhood between man and man with only the sway of her hips. \n\n Women can bring a man to his knees, weeping, or imbue him with stalwart bravery to take upon the most challenging foe.\n\n Men are pigs. They are so driven by lust, desire, that often times they are left vulnerable. It is for this reason that women have a powerful control over a man's emotions.\n\n For these next few pages, I will use information I have collected from\n\nvarious women who obliged my questions.\n\n These are their thoughts and views on men.\n\n Surprisingly, many women do not realize the power they hold. Perhaps they simply hold back the truth in order for us men to not find out? That is always a possibility. Regardless, every woman questioned, Human, Elf, Half-Elf, had all said that they hold\n\nno such power over men. The same goes for their looks. It appears that either these women do not feel confident in themselves, or genuinely think that everyone is equal.\n\n However, I did not get much of an answer as to what kind of a man that\n\na woman would find most ideal. Naturally, you would assume a strong- muscled and independent man, yes? Well, it appears High Elves lean closer toward social status, along with intelligence and brain power.\n\nTypical.\n\n The other women believed I had been attempting to flirt with them. Pity. I could not finish my questioning as this rotten bitch took the original fucking book and tore it to shreds! She couldn't even read! Can you believe it? That fuckin' whore!\n\n Nonetheless, I did also manage to interview some men. Their responses were quite interesting.\n\n For example: One High Elf believes that if he were subordinate to a woman, he'd not be submissive enough for her sex dungeon, but to follow simple\n\nremedial tasks and orders given by the woman. How odd.\n\n When questioned how desperately if at all he wanted to fuck the woman beside him, he refused to answer.\n\n That is all I have written. Please, do look around for a volume two if I ever get around to it. Thank you for reading.\n\n\n\n And always remember the power of breast over brawn.\n\n[!] The book is elegantly and neatly signed.\n\n\n\n\nJ.H.L.\n\n[!] Rough sketches of a male stick figure stabbing a female stick figure to death.\n\n\n\"I had to rewrite the whol God-damned thing! Sodding bitch!\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on Blayism", "§bYhorm Aldyhr", ". . .after a successful combination of his three artifacts, we have managed to successfully construct Illius’ guardian. . . oh won’t she be proud of us? A home for the weary once more, that they might find a light to illuminate their dreary souls? Oswald\n\nmay see us as worms to crush beneath his icey footsteps, yet now we may escape his once inevitable carnage. . . a test, or two, and his strength seems to be expanding with every swing of his fist. Oh how proud you must be, I can already envision\n\nthe smiles across their hollowed faces, can’t you?", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of Riddles!", "§bArchembalt", "Archibald Vallero's\n Book of Dumb Riddles\n===================\n\n      O          O\n          = \n            =\n              =\n            =\n          =\n\n     {_          _)\n       <>><<>><<>\n\n1. How does a man truly reach his way into a woman's heart?\n\nAnswer: With the help of a physician, if needed.\n\n2. I have windows, a door, a roof with shingles, but termites do not find home within me. What am I?\n\nAnswer: A head.\n\n3. A man dropped a spoon onto a metal table, and the clash made a 'clink' sound. What is the spoon made out of?\n\nAnswer: Matter.\n\n4.\n\n (109 + 567 + 79 - 4,514) / ((67/5) - 42 + 9) = ?\n\nAnswer: A number.\n\n5. A dark elf, an orc, and a snow elf walk into a bar. The orc invokes a fight between the trio and they all eventually end up dying one by one, some instantly and some succumbing to their wombs. In the end, however, who won?\n\nAnswer: Not the bartender. Now he has to clean the blood from the floor . . . !\n\n6. What is a druid's favorite day of the year?\n\nAnswer: The 7th of every month.\n\n *The book stops short here. A few ink splotches are scattered throughout the page. Odd.*", ""),
        StaticBook("-=Tale of Two=-", "§bLocke Lamora", "It is a power I let course through my veins, and seep into my thoughts. A hindering presence floating amongst my thoughts, dripping poison all the while.. memories crumbling away at its touch, my life growing distant and numb. This poison had a potency,\n\nnot to the effects of death.. but to erase your very memory what made you.. you. Its touch frosty as the tops of the dwarven mountains, with a chill apparant to that of steels touch. I dare not speak further lest it haunt my soul, and damned I be to see\n\nit walk the shadows of my form. So to we shall stroll hand and hand into the night of blackest hue to succumb to the desires of our hearts allowing our minds to be free in their spirits. A tale of two forgotten amongst the pages let you know now that this\n\nbook is the only living remnant of their once bountiful love.. now to you bear the curse to forever be lonesome in your state of mind befitted with murderous rage, and inciteful ideals. *The rest of the pages would appear to be torn away.. droplets of\n\nblood adorning the pages that still clung to the binding..!", ""),
        StaticBook("The Crooked Man", "Reznc", "There was a crooked man and he walked a crooked mile\nHe found a crooked mina upon a crooked stile\nHe bought a crooked cat, which caught a crooked mouse\nAnd they all lived together in a little crooked house\n\nHe met a crooked woman and he found a crooked preach\nThey had a crooked marriage on a crooked little beach\nThey had some crooked children and they lived a crooked life\nThe crooked man, the cat, the mouse, the children and the wife\n\nThey were happy for a number of crookedly long years\nLived a life of crooked happiness, no worries and no fears\nUntil one day the crooked wife upset the crooked man\nHe yelled a crooked yell and then he hit her and ran\n\nThe crooked man came again at the stroke of twleve\nThat was when their crooked life became a living hell\nThere was a crooked man and he walked a crooked mile\nAnd when he killed his wife and kids he smiled a crooked smile", ""),
        StaticBook("Wes Osullivan", "§bVanbrek zu Holtz", "-=Wes Osullivan=-\n\n       Written By:\n         -V.z.H.-\n\n\n\n       First Edition\n\n        Chapter 1\n\nThe sacred objects of my desire are ones not lusted for by men of sylvan heritage.  They are ones of a man newly enchanted by a city that glows both day and night.  A distinct shift has occurred in the world. Those who once made\n\ntheir petty livings slaving away in the countryside have found new hope and oppertunity in the great cities of Athera, namely Petrus.  Perhaps the only two things I have found to rival this great town are money and women.  Standing atop the bell tower of\n\nthe Petrus cathedral, I can not help but feel pulled by the indescribable energy the people emit below, spraying from the torchlit streets into my hungry eyes.\n\nThe priest known as Vincent Divine, who shares a name with an intellectual from old,\n\nwas distinct from his predecessors, favoring kind words and a warm smile over crucifixions and holy war.  His clothing was that of color, and his speech that of healthy and curious skepticism. He and I became good friends after I had moved to the city,\n\nseeking wealth and freedom from an oppressed life in the rustic fields, far from commerce.  Here, anything was possible.  The problems one faced while about the city were much different from those suffered under the tenure of a landed lord.  Now, the only\n\nlords were that of financial aristocrats, watching over the city with a seperate and innately different brand of lordship.\n\n        Chapter 2\n\n\"Good to see you, friend!\"\n\"Indeed, it is a pleasure to find your\n\ncompany in such a lovely location, Wes.\" spoke Vincent Divine, his face shaded by the Maer's Garden.  It was a beautiful time of morning, when the sun first casts its golden arrows into the sky so that they may streak across the fading stars.\n\n\"You are well read, Vincent, does this city remind you at all of the old Aegean capital?\" I asked, eager to compare the advances of Petrus to the standing accomplishments of fabled Al'Khazar.\n\"In some ways, yes.  I believe it is finding its identity, t\n\ncollective mass of humans searching together for a common grounding.  It is too early to tell.\"\nDivine had always been a bastion of intellect and free-thinking, despite his profession as a clergyman.  Ever since the wild influx of migrants from the out\n\nreaches of Oren, he has found himself wildly successful in his career.  I knew to trust his judgement, but the question still lingered in my mind as a bee intent on pollen might hover about a vibrant golden flower.  \n        Chapter 3\n\nArt Kettleston was his name.  He sat in the corner of Parton's Tavern, wooing some hollow girl with equally as empty words.\n\"I am rather rich.\"\n\"Oh?\"\n\"Yes.  I have become so boringly rich.\"\nI frowned a disgusted frown and smirked a superior\n\nonce.  To drown out his annoying voice I strode to the ale counter.  There stood a slightly thin framed girl, beautiful and shining.  It was easy to tell she hailed from somewhere remote in the countryside, somewhere as vibrant as that golden flower.\n\nI fell in nostalgic love.\n\nI found myself standing near Art after an exciting and whimsical conversation with the barmaid, whose name was later revealed to me as Elena Appleheart.  Her reaction to my rather generous tip, given in the form of a large\n\nsum of mina, had already cast the image of my wealth to those in the restaurant.  Finding my way to Art Kettleston, I could not restrain myself from inquiring-\n\"How much do you have, then?\" I politely interjected into his pitiful courtship attempts.  H\n\nresponse was that of a medium sized fund, nothing worth spouting pretentious triumph over.  I brought myself to setting a hand upon his shoulder and offering a sympathetic smile, not for his quanitity of mina, but for his arrogance.\n\n        Chapter 4\n\nDespite my sedulous collection of wealth, I remained- for the most part- humble.  I was not easily angered or driven to a heated feud.  When Elena revealed to me that Art was a writer, I found myself utterly defeated.  This\n\ncasually conceited author had more success as a wordsmith than I had ever.  This, due completely to never having had written before, angered me because of my aspirations to compose but my lack of motivation in doing so.  Reading his\n\nwell-crafted fiction compelled me to take my own shot at penning.  Elena and I became closer entwined during my early dabling in writing, mixing the integrated city dweller and the longing bucolic dame in a dance of love and confliction.\n\nMy first novel was a success.  It sold as rapidly as it was printed, taken home and read, studied.  Studied, of all things.  Even the established Priest Divine found it remarkable and symbolic.  A boiling rivalry was forged the moment Kettleston received\n\na copy.  A clash as fierce as the population war between the declining meadowland villages and the behemoth Petrus.  Elena, charmed by my substantial wealth and dazzled by my success, stayed with my in my flat hung care-free above and\n\nbeside Market Street.\n\n       Chapter 5\n\nThe city shown as bright at night as it did at day.  People seemed to always be about, sometimes heading to no location in particular.  Petrus had become open, inviting.  Just months\n\nbefore my formal moving-in, I would have been scared stepping a meter within the walls- fearing of some form of persecution.  There was an air of welcoming about the city now, and more importantly, about the people.  While the battle between Art and I\n\ngrew horribly intense, with each of our works topping one another, it seemed to serve as the epitome of fundamental change within Humandom.  New intelectual competition had risen in Petrus, exemplified in our condition.\n\nMy drive became,\n\neven above wealth, to beat Art Kettleston at his own game which I so coveted to monopolize.  Even Elena felt forgotten; my obsession with writing had manifested itself in long hours spent dipping a feather in ink and scratching upon soft parchment,\n\ncovering white in black.\n\"Mr Osullivan, good day!\" yelled a familiar voice, in derisive manner, from outside my apartment.  I opened my door, descending the steps to the cobbled street.\n\"Kettleston, have you come to tell me of your golden and monot\n\nlife?\"\n\n        Chapter 6\n\nRed streaked across my face, dripping in scarlet hues upon the roughly lain road.  His fist was dotted with bits of crimson, flung from the impact.  Elena hurried down the stairs with a bandage,\n\nshouting word I could not comprehend, the shock blinding my senses.  She extended her arms, wrapping them around my body as if to usher me back into the home so that the incident might simply be forgotten.\n\"You bastard!\" was the last he heard before a\n\nreciprocal force impacted his despicable features.  The arc of my fist flew across the busy city scene much as the city itself was, rising to an interim peak.  In that moment, caught up in the intensity of the fight, I later realized I had answered my own\n\nprior question.\n\n        Chapter 7\n\nElena was gone the next day, repulsed by city life.  She came seeking a living for her downtrodden self, but quickly became disenchanted with the lifestyle involved in doing so.  She seemed\n\nto have had favored the waggish methods of the detached.  The nostalgia of first meeting her, recalling the golden fields of wheat in doing so, remained with me even as she departed.  I was fused to the city at this point, unable to return to a pastoral\n\nbirth.  Art was downed after my fury found itself upon him, leaving him in a mental state unfit for writing or challenging me.  I had won, but only in a sense.  There was nothing fulfilling in being the only prominant writer within the town's partition.\n\nThe spirit of neverending tournament, with every soul vying for dominance, is what defined my life in Petrus.  A state of constant improvement, and sacrifice.  Now, with the combination of Elena's disillusionment and my substantial wealth, the city had\n\nbecome dark even when lit by the soft and flickering glow of burning torches.\n\n\n        Epilogue\n\nMy personal and domicile arc had crested.  Although the golden flower welted, the bees persisted in\n\na constant search for the lustrous hue.  Unable and reluctant to escape from my own free will, I found myself down the steps of the cathedral tower and back into my dimly lit home.\n\n     Author's Note\n\nThe story of Wes Osullivan is one of true events, altered and brought together as one coherent tale.  The state of the world is a constantly changing one, a story writing itself.  The dramatic shift from rural living to close\n\nurban dwelling is one that may affect us for the better or worse, but it will most certainly affect us.  I am excited to enter this new era, and Wes Osullivan seeks to explore what truly defines our time.\n\n        -V.z.H.-\n\nSpecial thanks to Dravage and Durheim for the inspiration and friendship they confer unto me.", ""),
        StaticBook("Humans raid elfs", "§bTshilda", "My name is Tshilda. I am a witness of a riad on the wod elven city.\nThe soldiers are wearing iron armor, mostly unenchanted. They were in horses. SOme of them with diamond armor.\n\nSorry if I misspell some things I was in quite a stresful situation. I think the soldier were human. I was walking to write my book in the beauiful nature when I saw soldiers on the path. They mistook me for a civilian and let me go.\n\nI walked up the path and met a dark elf. We attempted to diffuse the situation but failed. It was then when a pillar of magic blasted me in the air, with no injuries i ran back to the docks with the dark elf.\n\nOut of curiosity I went back to record this. I was hidden in the bushes when a human helf a blade to my throat. I headbutted him and ran away. this was all I was able to document as I was too scared to go back.\n\nIm back, I decied I need to see how it ends. The humans seem to be gone. I barely got into the city. The people are mostly calm. Luckily very few people died. But some lives were lost. The soldiers appeared to be human.\n\nBut luckily I managed to get out alive.\n\n-Tshilda", ""),
        StaticBook("A Snelf's Greed", "§bJacob 'sharpblade' Jackstorm", "Once upon a time there was a snow elf prince. He wasn't a bit of a cunt; he was a massive cunt. As thus, he eyed Shreck's Swamp with some pretty cheeky greed, this is where the cunt crossed lines into being a bloody well idjit. You see, Shreck's might\n\nknows no ends, he's fucking tougher then me mum and she'll fucking bite your ear off for talking shit about her aweful onion pie. Anyways, Shreck found the fuck out, ork's connections are bloody everywhere, found one in me soup once. Tasted nice.\n\nSo, upon his majestic steed he set out from his swamp in the orkish desert until he got to his shoddy little wall. With a single leap he was over the walls. Killing the entire camp he moved to fucking RKO him, breaking his face, after a backflip drop\n\nkick. Then Shreck killed the snow elf prince and everyone lived okay. Except the snelves; snelves were fucking dead.\n\nAnd that, kids, is why you stay out of the blue cunt's fucking swamp.", ""),
        StaticBook("Libary Of Dragur", "§bJudas Agnarsson", "Libary \n    of\n       Dragur\n\nMy name is Judas Agnarsson, I was recently appointed as a Scholar in the libary of Dragur.\n\nThe establishment gives a decrepit look, however it is rather exquisite.\n\nI was given a study to sit in solitude whilst I write.\n\nDuring my time in the libary I have been having strange...paranormal things happening to me.\n\nBooks falling off shelves, yet no one is around or no breeze to cause them to fall.\n\nNimble footsteps scampering across the floors upstairs; followed by a giggling sound.\n\nThe following experiences I will list will give you a breif elucidation on this phenomenon.\n\nI set my blank tome upon my desk, and dipped my quill in the ink pot a few inches from my paper.\nI was sitting in tranquillity as I began to write.\n\nI cleared my throat, and heard it echo throughout the libary.\nI decided to stay late that eve\n\nfinish my book.\n\nI heard the last Scholar wrench the lever as the gate slowly descended from upstairs.\n\nThe building was now closed, and there was no entrance inside.\n\nIt gave a sinister feeling, my body got chills almost instantly. \n\nI contemplated upon my tome trying hard to not acknowledge the induced disquieting feeling.\n\nI shifted my gaze up from my tome in distress, and paused for a moment.\n\n[!]\n\nTap tap tap tap tap tap\n\nA faint tapping sound coming from upstairs.\n\nIt didn't sound like it was an object causing the name, as the tapping gradually became more louder and clear.\n\nIt sounded as if someone was tapping, yet scratching their fingernail on the wood.\n\nI clambered from my seat and ambled out of my study, peering down the hall.\n\nI didn't bother yelling hello, as I thought it may have been a gust of wind making a loose sign smack against the wall repeatedly.\n\nI climbed the stairwell, and to my suprise the tapping stopped...\n\nI ceased at the top of the stairs momentarily before sauntering towards the libaries counter.\n\nI peeked over the counter, and checked the signs they all seemed sturdy.\n\nI shifted my gaze towards the counter, and noticed three scratch marks had been dragged downwards a number of times.\n\nThey were easily visible from where I was standing.\n\nI slowly backed away from the counter, too scared to scream.\n\nMy heart thumped against my chest, I felt breathless.\n\nI heard scuttles of a childs feet running between the isles in the East Wing.\n\nI saw a phantom of some sort scurry past the entrance of the East Wing.\n\nMy eyes immediatley drawn towards it.\n\nI unveiled unveiled a flask of pure ale from a tattered brown cloth. I bought it up to my mouth and heartily quaffed the ale down my\n\nthroat.\n\nI began to lurch forward towards the Wing. \nI stopped at the steps of the Wing momentarily, before walking down the steps and entering.\n\nI looked both ways, only to find bleak emptiness.\n\nThat was until I heard a distant murmur of my name.\n\n\"Judas...\"\n\nMy body went fridgid all of a sudden, before suddenly arising back to a normal temperature. Did something just pass through me? I questioned aloud.\n\nThe sudden drop of coldness contorted my face.\n\n\"Who goes there?\" I yelled, I stood in a broad posture trying to stand courageous, and not let the wraith be known I was scared.\n\nA sudden jolt of pain struck me, as if someone had pierced my lower back with a hook, and dragged it down forcefully.\n\nI convulsed; collapsing to the floor, shaking uncontrollably.\nWas this really happening?\n\nWhat is this evil daemon?\n\nI felt a tear fall out my right eye as my head layed on the floor.\nI managed to lift my head, and cast my gaze forwards towards a disfigured apparition sharing an odd resemblance to my deceased son.\nWas this him?\n\nHad he come back for revenge\n\nHad he come back to show me he forgives me?\n\nHad he come back to stop me....?\n\nMy face distorted as I percieved it must have been my son.\n\nThe middle parting, his dismembered ear, his maimed features upon his face.\n\nThe figure leered at me, furrowing a brow as if to say I was pathetic and weak.\n\nI extended my hand \"Forgive me...\" but the figure pivoted on his heel and walked away.\n\nA narrowed scintillating white light appeared infront of him.\n\nIt began to stretch to his size as he drew closer, blinding me instantly.\n\nI pryed my eyes open with my fingers forcing them to stay open as I began to crawl forwards.\n\n\"TAKE ME WITH YOU!\" \nMy voice echoed throughout the libary.\n\nAs the figure passed through the light, vanishing.\n\nNothing was left but a breif coruscating sparkle stayed for a moment before dissipating in the void.", ""),
        StaticBook("Punishment I", "§bArchmage Crumena", "The Punishment of Hubris I\n\nLet me tell you a story of the children of Malin, Horen, Urguan and Krug; A tale from the time when God’s melting pot became a cauldron. And many wondered about his beneficence.\nA tale from when God was first questioned and his mortal children cried\n\nout in anguish.\nOur story takes us back to the beginning, when the Four Brother’s children were coming of age in this world known as Aegis. One stood out among the sons of Malin. Born beneath the shade of the canopy on under the pale light of a\n\nfull moon, his father named him Zanunder. Proud and strong was his stature, Zanunder stood tall among his brothers and sisters, resembling their Father in many ways. His hair was the color of the noon-day sun and his eyes a vibrant shade of\n\nforest green.\nIblees came, then, as Zanunder grew. Corrupted his father and uncles, in the end unleashing upon the world a devastating curse. They said that Iblees had been a Daemon, a servant of God. Zanunder began to question this. If God was\n\nomnipotent, surely he would not allow evil to exist. Nor would he do so were he kind, benevolent or even compassionate. But he was not the only being to begin to question the ways of the world. There was also her…she stood tall, her presence unlike\n\nanything he had laid eyes upon before. Hair black as night, lips green as ivy, but the strangest thing about her visage was surely her eyes. The whites of her eyes were a deep, steel blue, and inside they were a light blue, like the clean waters of a\n\ngently flowing stream. He came to know her as Nemiisae, a kindred spirit in this world he viewed as cruel. In short time they fell in love, relishing in each other’s ideals and presence. They came to the conclusion quickly that they should escape\n\nthese lands, hoping that in doing so they would also be able to break free of Iblees’ curse upon the lands. They retreated deep underground in order to begin their plans, for Zanunder did not wish others to know what they had planned, lest they\n\nbe branded heretics.\nDeep beneath the ground, in a large cavern, Nemiisae created a portal to a world known as the Verge. From there it would be easier for her powers to stretch across the lands, locating a fit place for her,\n\nZanunder, and their soon-to-be abundant children to settle. It would also protect them from the scourge Iblees unleashed across the lands. Inside the Verge the two thrived, and quickly multiplied, first growing to three, then four, then\n\nsix, eight, ten, and continuing on. Nearly a hundred years after they first arrived in that place, Nemiisae had finally found a suitable place to settle. However, during those hundred years, Kings had risen and fallen. The Brothers had all\n\npassed away. Kingdoms and Guilds began to rise to power throughout the lands. The curse seemed to barely affect people’s lives. These events had gone unnoticed by Nemiisae and Zanunder, so wrapped up in raising their young and\n\nfinding a place to flee to from Iblees’ curse. Inside the Verge, Nemiisae created another portal. It reflected the green, verdant lands they planned to settle…but not the dangers lurking within. Before they went through with their children,\n\nZanunder stopped his love. He requested that the portal be closed behind them, as well as the portal out of the Verge that they had entered through. Zanunder did not wish for their children to be followed, believing that Iblees’ curse\n\nbeing, feeling love for all the races of Aegis. She believed they could all escape Iblees’ curse and did not wish to deny them the chance to do so. Little did she know that in the end, all who sought her same refuge would be lead to their dooms by it.\n\nbeing, feeling love for all the races of Aegis. She believed they could all escape Iblees’ curse and did not wish to deny them the chance to do so. Little did she know that in the end, all who sought her same refuge would be lead to their dooms by it.\n\nAs soon as Zanunder, Nemiisae and their children stepped through the portal, they realized it had slammed shut behind them; They could no longer return to the land they came from. At first, this was not a problem to them. They were confident\n\nthis land would be their Paradise, the savior of their children. But their children had spent decades cut off from the light of sun, while living and thriving within the Verge. They set out immediately for the thickest forest they could find, in hopes\n\nof slowly acclimating their children to the light. But this was not to be. This land was rife with dangers, of all shapes and sizes, lurking behind corners, within the trees, in the skies, the oceans, the rivers…There was no escaping it. The very least\n\nof these dangers were the skittering, venomous spiders of the caverns. Their children began dying off, one by one, then two by two, then five by five, then by the droves. Finally, Nemiisae chose the least of the threats of this world and blessed her\n\nchildren with that creature’s aspects. Her children became a mix of her blood, Zanunder’s, and that of the spiders that lurked the caverns. They migrated into these same caverns, living amongst and as the Spiders did, save from the beasts and terrors\n\nlurking on the surface. Vicious, cruel, the children shifted and changed into horrible beasts, preying and feeding upon one another.\nNemiisae knew she had to bless them in this way so that they may survive, yet still she grieved for their corruption.\n\nAs well, no matter how hard they tried, Nemiisae and Zanunder could no longer procreate. Horrified, they began to believe it had been a trap all along. A trick by God to punish them for acting out, perhaps?! Or was it just for His own amusement?!\n\nZanunder refused. They argued at length, until finally he threatened to take the children and go elsewhere. He firmly believed if they traveled far enough they could survive, thrive, beat God and the Curse. Frustrated and exhausted,\n\nNemiisae knew she had to stop him somehow, or their children would all perish. She remembered the blessing she had given her children and as Zanunder turned to leave, she changed her form to that of a giant Spider. She put him to sleep with her\n\nNemiisae knew she had to stop him somehow, or their children would all perish. She remembered the blessing she had given her children and as Zanunder turned to leave, she changed her form to that of a giant Spider. She put him to sleep with her\n\nvenom and wrapped him within a cocoon. Then, she bade the eldest and closest of her Daughters to watch over the others, before retreating into the deep with Zanunder, to try and find a way to force open the portal and escape this horrid place.\n\nRescribed by Archmage Crumena V. Ilwindior on the 2nd of The First Seed, 1473.\nTo be continued in the second volume.\n[Credit to TehLulu for the writing itself.]", ""),
        StaticBook("Punishment III", "§bArchmage Crumena", "The Punishment of Hubris III\n\nIn the course of the first two-hundred and fifty years, as Nemiisae searched for a way to open the portal and leave this place, Zanunder aged within his sleep, eventually dying. Bereaved of her husband she fell into a great remorse for having\n\naccidentally killed him. Her mind became clouded, muddled, perhaps even mad. Then her children came to her with reports of these strangers in their lands. She realized then what had happened…The portal only shut on their side. It must\n\nhave been an act of God, she believed. Then she realized, it was a test! As God had tested the Four Brothers, surely he was testing her and these others to become stronger. But her compassion would not allow her to let others fall victim to this trap\n\nshe had unwittingly played a part in. She could not reopen the portal, but she could close it from her side, sealing off both the portal to the Verge and the portal within it to the new world. Then she retreated back into her Caverns, encouraging her\n\nchildren to live, breath, fight, grow stronger. Punish and perish the weak and live for themselves and for her, for it was the only way to gain God’s favor. Then she left them to their own devices, her wishes for their future made clear.\nIt was in this\n\nway that hubris was punished, that the sins of those who came before was tested and they were proven guilty. Had these people physically gone to these lands, surely the curse would not have killed them all. But by passing through the\n\nportal of Hubris which Nemiisae had created, they doomed themselves and their kin. Other groups found the portals between the time Nemiisae realized what had happened and closed it off, but anyone who came through was doomed to suffer and die.\n\nThere was no hope for them as soon as they had passed through those portals into that world. But it is far enough removed that between the first settling of it and our time, no one has ever discovered it again. Beyond churning, whirling seas,\n\ndeadly islands, leagues and weeks away from the lands settled by the Four Brothers, it would be impossible to reach without the assistance of a very strong force indeed. But perhaps it is for the best, that these ruins remain ruins, and the skeletons\n\ngo undisturbed, lest we awaken old evils...", ""),
        StaticBook("The Trapped Boy", "Viper3X", "Day 1\n\nThe ruins collapsed in behind me. I haven't been able to find a way out. I have no tools or weapons, and the rubble is too heavy. I'm almost out of food. No clean water, only a small stream that travels underground. I can hear creepy sounds ne\n\nDay 4\n\nI'm so tired. So, so tired. I haven't eaten anything in 2 days, and my stomach feels empty. The water... the water had something in it... My stomach... it hurts...\n\nDay 6\n\nMy skin! I don't know what is happening to me. I can't feel the lower half of my body. I can barely crawl to the water. Is this the end?\n\nDay 7\n\n*Stains of dark blood litter the page*\n\nI... I can barely move my fingers to write. I don't... I don't know what is happening to me. I hear sounds everywhere. It's like a spider is crawling around in my head. Creator, save me.\n\n*The next pages are torn out or missing.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Portrait Imagine", "§bNyxxa", "-==Portrait Imagine\n\nCaution: this drawing comes from my own imagination and is not a real person.\n\nOn this page dots of varying density create a sketch.\nYou see a female, She has pale, soft skin over a curvaceous yet thin frame. A small, set of lips frame gleaming, prefectly straight teeth with sharp, slightly hooked cainines and fang-like eye teeth.-\n\nHer sleek, dark hair falls in cascades over her shoulders to her back seemingly shiny in some unknown light. Her large clear, chardonay-green eyes shine with mischievous intent. Her body is mostly fur free but for strips of well groomed short black fur -\n\non her face, running from just above the corner of her jaw to the peak of her high cheekbones. Another patch of fur runs from her sleek tail in a v-shape around both sides of her torso getting progressively thicker to cover just under her bosom. Her tail-\n\nis long and sleek and gives the impression of twitching at it's tip. Her petite arms and fine delicate hands are fur covered up to just below her biceps, and her legs, making up nearly half her height are fur covered up to mide-thigh on the inside and -\n\nto her hips on the outside. The only colored ink on the page is the eyes of this felined female.", ""),
