        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "ur kinda ok sometimes <3", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "An Unseen Path, a journal of my time as a Dedicant of the Naelurir, under the guidance of the Owl Druid, Arinislia.§0\n§0\n§0by: Menarra Reffahc\n\nI never saw this path, not once in my life, as one I would come to walk. Yet, here I am. I have committed crimes and sins beyond description in my past, and in my seeking of attonement and redemption, I have come to a woman I have perhaps most wronged, in\n\nthat dark past, the Owl Druid. I took her, tortured her, made her scream. While it was true I did this at the urging of my Shade, I must take some responsibility for these actions.§0\n§0\n§0If I am to find redemption, it needed to start with her. When we\n\nencountered eachother for the first time in centuries, she immediately looked at me with hatred and loathing, and came at me with a blade. For the first time in a long time, I was afraid. Even I could feel nature around us recoiling and shifting. If I had\n\nnot run, I know I would now be dead.§0\n§0\n§0I made several attempts after that to try to speak to her, I knew my redemption needed to start through her. Finally, we spoke, and while I know she does not trust me, she listened, and heard me out.\n\nFinally, she instructed me to go read the library of the Naelurir, and then return to her when I thought of a way to attone to her.§0\n§0I did so, and in that reading, it became clear to me what I needed to do. As Kyral sought out one who hated her to guide\n\nher to redemption, so too must I. I returned to the Owl Druid, and I stated this, and asked her to guide me to my redemption and take me as her student.§0\n§0She seemed shocked, to say the least, and even fled from me. I approached her a few days later and she\n\nagreed to take me, and I gave my oath to the Aspects and the Naelurir. My offering to the Aspects was the robe my eldest child made for me, that has seen so much death and life in equal measure and was deeply sentimental. I cast it into the flame to show\n\nmy resolve to follow this path to whatever end. From here I will give account of my Tasks as they are given to me. I know a 'task journal' is a druidic tradition, and so this will serve as mine.§0\n§0\n§0------------\n\n1st of Snow's Maiden, 1661.§0\n§0\n§0My first task was to study the Naelurir, then return and explain in my own words what their purpose was, why it was important, and what they are. I explained them as warriors and keepers of the new culture\n\nof the Mali people, and the importance of having faith in something, something unifying to believe in. I believe the Owl Druid was pleased with my answer, even if she shows me only impartial judgement. That is okay though, I expect her to be harsh. I need\n\nher to be harsh, or this redemption will not be.§0\n§0------§0\n§02nd of Snow's Maiden, 1661§0\n§0\n§0My second task is to find two items, one to represent life, one death, and bring them to my Guide. I have chosen a seed for life, and an animal hide\n\nfor death.§0\n§0\n§0She accepted these items and my reasoning behind choosing them. I was presented with my second bead for my necklace.§0\n§0\n§0----------§0\n§05th of Snow's Maiden, 1662§0\n§0\n§0I was given a short\n\nlesson on The Balance, and a druid's place within it. I already knew much of this from conversations with the various druids in my family, and the Owl Druid seemed pleased with my prior knowledge. I was given a Task to go out and find a place of imbalance\n\nand bring balance to it alone, only allowed to seek aid should I find an area of Blight needing healing.§0\n§0\n§0I also showed her my new home, which as it turns out used to be the home of Gigarun, the Pine Druid. I didn't bring it up, but I know she and Gigarun\n\nwere together long ago and had a child, I've met the child, Erin I believe. She would visit Lohrahn and Drejan when they lived on the Darkblade Estate with me back on Athera.§0\n§0\n§0I find no small irony in this revelation, and I will care for the home and the\n\nbirch tree inside as she has asked of me.§0\n§0\n§0I prepare now to take my journey out into the wilderness to seek out imbalance.§0\n§0----------§0\n§01st of the Amber Cold, 1663:§0\n§0I have been traveling a bit and not found anything yet...I think I might be\n\noverthinking this. I'm going to continue my journey...§0\n§0-----------§0\n§012th of the Amber Cold, 1663:§0\n§0\n§0I found a strange area of dead plants and soil and sent for Arinislia to come assist me as I thought it was blight. It turns out it is a rare\n\nshuriken flower, which is not native to this land and drains nutrients and life around it, and is difficult to remove. Not only that, if druidic blight healing is used upon it, it spreads rampantly and becomes harder to deal with. So, I've gathered up a\n\npile of dead wood branches and brush and lit the flower aflame, reducing it to ash. This seemed to be an acceptable course of action to Owl, so I think I did well. I am going to return home and rest, then seek her out tomorrow for my next lesson. §0I...am\n\nstarting to enjoy this, I §0think...perhaps§0 I'm on the right path after all...§0\n§0\n§0--------§0\n§03rd of The Deep Cold, 1663§0\n§0I was given my next task, find three Voidal tomes of any sort and bring them to Owl. This should be fairly simple, my family loves\n\nbooks.§0\n§0--------§0\n§014th of The Deep Cold, 1667§0\n§0\n§0I can't believe this took me four years, but I had to deal with problems with my children, then I was deeply sick and bedridden for a few months. I feel fully recovered now and I\n\nhave the tomes, and will be taking them to Arinislia shortly.§0\n§0---§0\n§0She gave me the lesson I expected, explaining how the Void is unnatural and imbalancing, and she seemed happy with my knowledge on the subject. I was given another bead for my necklace.\n\n15th of the Grand Harvest, 1670§0\n§0----§0\n§0I was given my next Task, to go take a sapling, go out and grow it, and write my observations in a journal while doing so. I was apparently evicted from my previous home that I rather liked, so I snuck in with Owl to\n\nextract a sapling from the birch tree inside that I had become fond of napping in. I am going to grow a new one here at my new house and tend to it, and maybe one day I can nap in this one.", ""),
        StaticBook("9th task", "§bBolon Stormtaker", "With being asked what I think nature's balance is about in my own mind. I see Nature's balance mainly alone just nature being left alone to do it's own thing. Letting it grow and even die. Less it's forces by unatural ailments meaning dark magic.\n\nMeaning in words in my own mind I feel nature's better left alone to it's own life. Only coming into it when it's needed. For the most part nature's balance is keeping everything to it's cycle. Meaning death, reproduction and growth too.\n\nWith nature's balance keeping to it's cycle and not being forced to be messed up or broken in words no unatural ailments messing with it then should be entirely left alone for the most part.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Rakai's Journal§0\n§0-§0\n§022nd of The Amber Cold, 1702§0\n§0\n§0[!]A detailed sketch of a large orca leaping from the water, has been drawn at the bottom of the page. The artist seemingly having put quite a bit of effort into it.\n\n[!]The writting is quite messy, though still readable.§0\n§0\n§0My first task was fun! I liked getting to meet all the druids I could find. I also got to ask my friends quite a bit about the druid lifestyle, which I think is really good! I'm happy with my task.\n\n[!]A note would be stuck into the pages of the notepad.§0\n§0\n§0Cerridwen, is seen as the mother of life, from her we have the world around us. Cernnunos, is seen as the father, the hunter. Through him we have the predators and animals.\n\n17th of The Deep Cold, 1702.§0\n§0\n§0I've been having fun doing my tasks, but I can't help but worry that I'll mess one up.. I suppose mistakes are normal however. On a brighter note, I adopted two baby animals, an owl and a pig.. hoot and oink i've named them.\n\n15th of Snow's Maiden, 1704.§0\n§0\n§0I've finished my second task, I had to create two items that represent the aspects and myself, I ended up making a rams horn arrow, and a metal flower pot covered in tiva! I think they turned out pretty.\n\n13th of The First Seed, 1704§0\n§0\n§0Started my third task today! I have to write about one of the regions of this realm, a tundra to be exact. I'm worried about venturing so close to the blizzard.. but i'll be fine. I'll leave Hoot and Oink here though..", ""),
        StaticBook("The Angry Gay", "§bAnorak", "Our story begins at the library of Dragur. Gregory is a rampant homosexual, whose repulsive urges often lead him into sin. The world cannot accept Gregory for who\n\nhe is, and this makes §0him...Angry§0. Gregory tried to take anger management classes, but his instructor was a gorgeous man who he could not have, and this made him angrier.\n\nThe Angry Gay, AKA Gregory, one day stumbled upon a stranger who appeared to have been chiseled by the Skygods themselves, and his name was Bastien. No longer was Gregory attracted to men, for now his love\n\nrested with a Golem. At last, thought the angry gay, I am cured of my homosexuality. And he celebrated. But it was not to be. For Bastien, was a male golem.\n\nThe End. (Copy)", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "", ""),
        StaticBook("Book of Herbs v2", "§bArveldir Des'Nox", "Shade Leaf\nAppearance: Short/small plant, plain leaves, yellow flowers when reproducing, silver color veins on bottom of plant\nEffect: Calmer/moodier, [weed], stress reliever\nSide-Effect: Bloodshot eyes.\n\nOverdose: Hallucinations.\nLocation: Almost any where near grass and shade, it is a weed after all.\nOther: Grows in large quantites, simmilar to normal weeds, makes poping noises when exposed to rain [veins burst] | Can be adminstered through any medium:\n\nSwamp Blossom\nAppearance: Purple green flower, short thick stem, only colorful thing in swamp\nEffect: Nectar possesses something aspects like honey, flower is nutritious\nSide-Effect: Can gum up a throat if too much is consumed raw.\n\nOverdose: You’re breath is going to stink, also diarrhea\nLocation: Branches of swamp trees\nOther: Nectar behaves like quick silver\nAlchemical Use: Moderate air symbol, Speed potions\n\n Tippen’s root\nAppearance: Purple flowers above, dark yellow speckled root\nEffect: Stops bleeding almost instantly\nSide-Effect: It stinks to 7 hells\nOverdose: Blood clots, blood clots everywhere~\n\nLocation: Common in dense forests near fallen trees | Hillsides = purple flowers near it\nOther: Bitter, STANKSSSS\nAlchemical Use: Moderate Earth Symbol, Strength/healing potion\n\nYarrow\nAppearance: A white to pink flowered plant that grows in grasslands and forests.\nEffects: Can reduce inflammation and blood pressure, as well as intensify the medicinal effects of other herbs.\n\nOverdose: Dizziness and fainting due to low blood pressure.\n\nZawabate\nAppearance: Tall stalk, yellow green petals which curl\nEffect: Out of body experiences, hallucinations, pain numbing\nSide-Effect: Numbing and increased healing capabilities.\n\nOverdose: Can destroy one’s mind when used as a poison\nLocation: Desert oasis, in the water\nOther: Nectar from petals is where the good stuff is.\nAlchemical Use: Moderate Water Symbol, numbing/healing potions", ""),
        StaticBook("The Great War", "Saviordude", "The Myth of the Great War\n-------------------Thousands of years ago a terrible war broke out between two mighty guilds of mages - one good and one evil - that controlled great empires. Mages on both sides vowed to fight until they were utterly triumpha\n\nseeking to purge their rivals from the earth. With fearsome magic and drake armies they battled for centuries, neither side winning a final victory.\nIn the proces of their warfare. the mages wrought vast devastation on the world. Forests caught fire an\n\nblazed till fone, islands sank into the sea, and entire races became extinct. Eventually the Aspects awakened from their slumber to witness the savage confict. Shocked by the destrucion, the Godess sent a vision to a single elf; the woman who would become\n\nthe first Arch Druid, Through the vision, this chosen figure saw that she must found a Druidic Order to preserver the fragile remains of her worlds ecology. With the guidance of the Aspects, the order grew in strength until it finally had the power to\n\nintervene in the mages's war. The force of young Druids pooled their powers and together vanquished the members of both battling guilds, transforming the combatants into innocent wild beasts.\nOnce the former mages - no longer caring about good\n\nversus evil - slither, bounded, loped and crwaled off into their ruined habitats the Order began to heal the world. Since then, the Druidic Order continually works to prevent such destruction from ever occuring again. Druids pledge to make sure the wars\n\nof good and evil no longer mar the precious earth. But the order also has bitter enemies in the ancient remnants of the guilds of warring mages; those good and evil mages who luckily escaped the fate of their fellows. Each guild claims it had been on the\n\nverge of victory and would have won, had the Druids not interfered.\n\n-Transcribed by Ol' Treehugger", ""),
        StaticBook("Aegis History", "§bAyda", "Aegis History\n   \n   The Age of Founding And The Before Times\n\n During this time, Druids were brought together and the Druidic Order was founded by the Wayward Druid Respiren Knox. The records of these times may never be\n\nfully deciphered, however. Although we know of some of the first Druids in Malinor, such as Respiren, Swifthide, and Native, very few details are known. Perhaps someday the past regarding these time will be revealed.\n\nThe First Age of Reconstruction \n\nThe First Age of Reconstruction was ushered in by the initiation of Dragon Druid Apollan in to the order. Apollan was the first to express interest in joining the order after reading the Myth of the Great War and spe\n\nlong hours in the Druid Grotto after he was famously murdered outside the gates of Laurelin by a bandit. Apollan quickly moved up the ranks. Meanwhile, the order grew. After initiating Apollan, Respiren expressed interest in 'rebuilding the order'. Every\n\nday, new citizens of Aegis would show up in the grove wanting to become Druids. Most were immediately initiated. As the Order grew so did a number of things including property and knowledge. During this time the order gained a lodge in Ravenhold, as well\n\nas one in the Laurelin docks. Additional, two stalls were bought at the Laurelin Marketplace and hundreds of books were added to the order's library.\n\nThe Age of Abandonment\n\nUnfortunately, due to extremely casual and non-uniform initiation rites of the Wayward Druid, people were joining he circle, only to wander into the wilderness never to be seen again. With no established hierarchy, the Order\n\nlacked organization and purpose. This was made apparent when an Elf named Rowan arrived. Archdruid Rowan arrived from a far off forest, seeking to reestablish his lost circle the 'Druids of Mother Aegis'. Rowan is most likely the only citizen to have\n\nargued with the orders ways. His only backing for the title of Druid was that he 'had to love nature'. When Rowan became annoyed by the fact that he had to be initiated in the order to be allowed into the inner librar, he stormed off claiming he would\n\nstart his own order somewhere else. Rowan was last seen entering the wilds outside his home in the Ravenlodge, and was never heard from or seen again. Fortunately the dark Age of Abandonment was brought to an end by the light of the Dawn Druid Maiavel.\n\nThe Age of Reformation\n\nThe age of Reformation was brought on by the ideas and drive of one Maiavel. With her determination, she was able to rally the remaining Druids to create official lore, hierarchy, rules, and a sense of order.\n\nThe Second Age of Reconstruction\n\nDuring the second age of Reconstruction many more books were written and a Grand Tree in the Laurelin Grove was planted. New dedicants were brought in but less frequently in the first age, and the initiation process\n\nmore careful and uniform. During this age, the current Hierarchy was established with Apollan as Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Knowledge\n\nThe Age of Knowledge was ushered in by Petyr, the Salmon Druid. His creation of the Dedicant's Vows and Attunement Ceremony greatly helped the order as well as his order ideas.\n\nThe Age of Mushroom Transgressions\n\nThis age was brought about by the conflict caused by the differing ideals in the order. Some Druids were becoming increasingly annoyed at the restriction that came with having a grove in the city\n\n(where trees and mushrooms could or couldnot be planted and the structure of the Druid tree.) Other Druids argued that it was their duty to promote balance, and that the runaway growth of mushrooms flowing from the grove was nowhere near natural. Some\n\nDruids wanted a new grove to be created outside the city and faraway. Others wanted the grove to be close. Still others did not want a new grove at all. But, it is important to note that most of these arguments were caused by misunderstandings,\n\nwhich is why this age is sometimes called the Age of Misunderstanding or the Age of Confusion.\n\nThe Age of Renovation\n\nIn the end most mushrooms were removed from the grove when Maiavel stamped her foot and the mushrooms instantly broke (but were unthoughtfully left on the ground, only to be swept up by Deltaro).\n\nA new, secret grove was set up in the wilderness outside Ravenhold, (notable work from Mahten and Aeaira whom spent days working on the road leading to civilization). The Laurelin Grove continued to be renovated and imporoved on, with notable work\n\ncontributed by Lunar Druid Bircalin and Dedicant Deltaro.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aegis History v2", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Aegis History- Post Dream\n\nThe First Age of Dreaming\n\nFor a long time it seemed that Nature was silent. For a great length of time, non had seen any feats spoken about in the ancient Druid tales. But then, as if a seed was\n\nplanted, Nature began to speak, and each Druid began to dream mysterious dreams. It was the Aspects of Nature, Cerridwen and Cernunnos, calling to their newly found children. Wayward Druid Respiren called a Moot to discuss these dreams, leading to the\n\nnext age.\n\n\nThe Age of Aspects\n\nThe moot decided to embrace these aspects as their primary deities.\n\nThe Age of Little Occurrence\n\nNothing happened for a while, except for a few things. There was a small hierarchy change; the Archdruid position was given to three people and the Grand Druid's dscription was changed. John and Maiavel were\n\npromoted to Archdruids but Maiavel soon retired. Her spot was filled by Petyr. Cleric Druid Mahten was also promoted to the rank of Guide. A simple, new recruiting strategy was initiated.\n\nThe Second Age of Renovations\n\nDuring the age the Laurelin Grove experienced a number of additions. A wall was created around the grove with a retractable gate. This was done to keep oout a number of citizens that were harassing the Druids. In\n\naddition, all the enterances were covered up so that one must always use the front gate. More additions to the grove were a kitchen, a melon and pumpkin farm, the cultivation of the Grand Tree's roots deeper into the ground, a tree farm, an elevator\n\nsystem and Druid dwellings at the top of the Grand Tree.\n\n\nThe Age of Poor Judgement\n\nA lot of inner-Order arguments and bad feelinds culminated in this age. Multiple parties were involved and no side was\n\ncompletely right. The end result was the leaving of Seeker Druid Deltaro from the order. The bad taste left in the mouth of many Druids caused Apollan to create the Druid Code.\n\nThe Second Age of Little Occurrance\n\nDuring this age, as its name would imply, not many things happened. The grove was given another renovation, mostly for aesthetic purposes. In addition, a Druid Order Forum account was created as well as a blog.\n\nUnfortunately during this time, Archdruid John's mental health was also starting to slide.\n\nThe Second Age of Dreaming\n\nDuring this age Salmon Druid Petyr had a vison in which the creation myth was shown to him by the\n\nAspects. It foretold of the time before the four races and a great exodus to come. A second Moot was called and all in attendance agreed to accept the vision as truth.\n\nThe Age of Mourning\n\nUnfortunately during this time, John's metal state grew worse. One day, John snapped. Among a number of thing he attacked the Dusk Druid. He then fled to his soul tree (Which he named Marcus) located in the pond outside the Laure\n\nGrove. There he made his last stand. The Druids present at the time were Dusk, Mahten, Apollan, Respiren, Saviticus and Thork the Betrayer (Thork the Blood Druid). In addition several princes and bystanders were also there. John stood on the roots of his\n\nsoul tree and attacked anyone who came near. There was a standoff for some time until all at once the Druids charged the tree. All some traded blows with John, Apollan worked to purify the water around the tree for he sensed that there was a taint at\n\nwork. Suddenly the Dusk Druid produced an axed and chopped at the soul tree until only one column was left. It was then that; with that ability to see inside the tree, Apollan discovered the taint in the form of a block of netherrack. Before Apollan could\n\nshare his discovery, Thork went into a blood lust. He cut down the last column of the tree rendering John unconscious. Then Thork stabbed John's body killing him. In the days followed Thork was shunned for violating Druid Code.\nJohn's tree was turned\n\ninto the place of his burial where his body was burned and the ashes scattered around Laurelin. His will named Mahten as his replacement as Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Expansion\n\nDuring this age, the Nature's Bounty chain was expanded by one new store on the King's Road. The order was also expanded by one new member.\nZannith, the 10 year old son of the Dusk Druid and an unknown Elf moved into the\n\ngrove. Much to the dismay of one Bircalin. Zannith later left the Druid grove to join the wardens and later committed suicide by jumping from the Grand Tree.\n\nThe Age of Knowlege\n\nDuring this age the Druids took control of the library of the Cloud Temple of Aegis. Petyr also retired from his position as Archdruid and became a Hierophant.\nDusk Druid Aeaira was elected to fill the spot and Saviticus\n\nbecame Head Teacher.\n\n\nThe Second Age of Mourning\n\nDuring this age Archdruid Mahten transformed his entire being into a tree in the grove. As a result of his sacrifice, Druid Dusk was named\n\n(in his will) as successor to the position of Arch Druid. In this time she has began to bring new life to the Druids Tree; ushering forth growth and spread of new branches, and brought a new generation of Druids into the Dedicant rank.\n\nThe Second Age of Expansion\n\nDuring this age the Druid Order and Grand Tree grew exponentially.\n\nThe Third Age of Mourning\n\nDuring this age Archdruid Apollan and Dusk were killed under mysterious circumstances. Apollan's position was given to his close friend Horizon Druid Makia and Dusk's position was given to Fox Druid Valas.\n\nThe Age of Poor Logic\n\nDuring this age, everything the Druids knew was proven to be incorrect. Petyr proved that wood doesn't exist, then proved that it does. He also proved that magic isn't real, and that water is fake. Unable to hold all this nonse\n\nArchdruid Darrian was convinced that he had gone crazy and was only dreaming. After jumping to his death from the top of the Grand Tree, Fey Druid Vanyariel took his position as Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Activism\n\nDuring this age many Druids had visions foretelling a coming destruction of all of Aegis. The Druid took it upon themselves to find and recover all ancient artifacts of Aegis so that they may be saved from the end and brought wit\n\n them to new lands. With the help of High Princess Indelwehn the Druids were lead to the Druid Tree at the docks. There she showed them the Shadow Circle, a compound underneath the tree created by Apollan and Respiren to store the First Staff, Seed, and\n\nSapling. It was later learned that Indelwehn believe the artifacts to be fake and intend to fool the Druids. But they were real and were taken back to the Mother Grove. Next the Druids traveled to Galahar where they valiantly defended the city from an\n\nattack by bandits. They then asked the King for permission to excavate under the city in search of another artifact which they believed to be stored under the city. The King agreed but only if the Druids agreed to teach him magic. They also agreed. After\n\nmany weeks in which the Druids found a number of things, the Druids broke into a grand cavern with a sandstone pyramid in the center. Inside the pyramid contained the Golden Mushroom and the Crystal Mushroom. These too were taken back to the Mother Grove.\n\nNext the Druids travel to the Mage's Guild House in South Laurelin. Inside the Mages kept artifact, a enchanted sword called The Bane of Arthropods. The Sword was rightfully the Druid's but the Mages refused to relinquish it. Negotiations were had with a\n\nmage, Deltaro Black, but they did not come to fruition. Finally using the combined power of eight Druid present the force field surrounding the chest containing the sword and it's locks was forced open. The Druids then took the sword bidding a hasty\n\nretreat and brought the sword back to the Mother Grove.\n\nThe Age of Treaties\n\nDuring this age peace was finally made between the Druid Order and the Mages Guild in the form of a contract written by the Druids. Although\n\npeople tend to forget, the treaty was basically forgotten in the next five minutes.\n\nThe Age of Hasty Movement \n\nDuring this age, the Order moveed around quite a bit. First, they left their Laurelin Grove due to the fact of a rumor that all of Laurelin would explode after Vardak opened a portal at the Gauntlet. The Order took up\n\nresidence in the Rose Grove for sometime under the miasma engulfed it. It took the combined magic of several Druids to push it back to the grove's boundaries however. The Order then had to return to Laurelin after they were given word that Malinor wished\n\nto reclaim their grove. Afterwards, when the Clould Temple fell, the Order (artifacts and all) packed up and moved to the Verge. This are ended when Aegis was overtaken by the Undead and all of the inhabitants of Aegis traveled to the new lands of Asulon.", ""),
        StaticBook("Asulon History", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Asulon History\n\nThe Age of Schisms\n\nUpon arriving in Asulon, the Druid order found themselves in disarray. Although there was much to be explored and to be done, this once great Order was reduced to small numbers in\n\nthe treacherous waves of sea and the separation from each other by leagues of land. When finally congregating back in Elandriel the Druids began casting their prayers to the Aspects, in request of a great new grove. However, even as the ground began\n\nblooming before them, internal conflicts arose between the Druids, resulting in Archdruid Delfyr being removed from his position. Overcome with righteousness and anger towards the motives of some other Druids, Rose Druid Delfyr and his once apprentice the\n\nSoul Druid Arik made the decision to split the Order, and bring about the Age of Schisms. Although this was a difficult decision, the Order as a whole emerged stronger for it, for the split of the Order had been the prophecy of the Head Teather, Saviticus\n\nIrongut, who had urged the Soul Druid to speak with the Rose Druid years previously. In response, preparations had been made that ultimately brought both circles out better.\nBoth circles became nearly empty, as the land of the Elves became less and\n\nless populated. During this time, Ice Druid Jameson was elected Grand Druid, and Wolf Druid Ouity was promoted to the position of Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Reunion\n\nMany Druids gathered one day, Jameson, Arik, Ouity, and a few others journeyed to Solace. Upon mounting the fertile land, they grew adamant that this was where they wished to stay, and in a unanimous vote, reunited the circles\n\nafter hastily calling a moot. After the moot, Twilight Druid Evelyn was shunned from the order for her lack of faith in the Aspects, and was last seen by any Druid fleeing the Alrasian army on a count of murder. The Druids were given a plot of land\n\noutside Solace, and construction on the new grove commenced.\n\nThe Age of Disarray\n\nSoon after the influx of new Druids and Dedicants into the order in the 'Age of Reunion', the order soon started seeing its key members disappearing. The 'Age of Disarray' began after the sudden disappearance of Archdruid Arik\n\nand the fall of Salvus. With the destruction of Salvus, the amount of visitors to the Mother Grove grew few and the Mother Grove became more secluded from the outside world. While its sister grove in Skravia flourished, the Mother Grove grew more and more\n\ndesolate. The appointment of a new Head Teacher seemed to stem the tide of decline in the Mother Grove, with new Dedicants flocking to the grove. But it could only last for so long. The incident of the Scion Druid Alan being given a book from the Witch\n\nKyral, which was soon after lost in an accident, sparked a series of disastrous events for the Druidic Order. Out of rage for the lost book, the Witch declared war on the Druids and attempted to conquer the Mother Grove in Salvus. At first the Druids\n\n attempted to negotiate with the Witch, seeking to attain peace. But after the Scion Druid lost the book, the Druidic Order was left with only two choices; They could surrender or fight. Left with little choice, the Druids decided to fight to protect\n\ntheir Mother Grove. At first it seemed that the Druidic Order would be able to sway the odds in their favour, with a deal struck with the Mori'Quessir. But alas, a preemptive attack from the Witch crushed the resolve by the Druids, forcing them to\n\nsurrender the Mother Grove in Salvus. These events had a catastrophic effect on the Druidic Order, throwing it into complete disarray. With the loss of the Mother Grove, the Druidic Order was scattered across Asulon. The Druids tried to relocate twice in\n\nthe coming years, in an attempt to re-organize themselves. But it came to no avail.\n\nThe Second Age of Reunion\n\nDuring this age, the Druidic Order finally succeeded in reorganizing itself. It moved to the great forest of Elandriel, and with the combined presence and power of the entire Order, the Elandriel Mother Tree grew\n\nto an extraordinary height, making it a suitable home for the Druids. With the planning of a new grove to be grown just outside the Cloud Temple Sanctuary, the future of the Order will surely see a massive intake of Dedicants. During this age the Order\n\nhas taken its first steps tp a second Golden Era.\n\nThe Second Age of Activism\n\nAfter a long period of inactivity amongst the Archdruids of the Order, nothing was left but few Dedicants and a single druid, the Solstice Druid Callax. The Order seemed to have finally died, for years of nothing would\n\nhappen. Eventually, Archdruid Valas returned to appoint the Solstice Druid to a Druid Guide. Alongside the appointing of Callax to Guideship, Spirit Druid Saviticus returned from a very long absence to a Guide position. Dedicant Giovanni and Dedicant Vi\n\nwere attuned to claim their totems of Wilting and Serpent, respectively. In the midst of the sudden burst of activity, Song Druid Arik reclaimed the Salvus Grove, making it a point of activity once more and the Wilting Druid grew a grove in the vast\n\nplains of Norjun. A curious seedling began growing just outside of the Mother Grove only to be revealed the daughter of the Mother Tree, Ethanriel, and Arik, Riel’a, an Ent who protects the forests across the realms. The Order had finally begun to roll to\n\nit's feet once more.\n\n\nThe Age of Arch Druids\n\nIn this age, there was much confusion of who was to lead the Order. A strange Druid by totem of Wild Druid, who was Archdruid of a different Circle, was appointed Archdruid\n\nin the empty third Archdruid position. She quickly left the Circle to leave Fox Druid Valas and Aura Druid Beleran as the only two Archdruids once more. Eventually, a moot was called by Aura Druid Beleran to discuss the inactivity among the two Archdruids\n\nand his departure from the position. At this moot, Scion Druid Alan stepped down from his Head Teacher Position. Song Druid Arik, a Hierophant, was voted to fulfill one Archdruid position, while the Order voted on Solstice Druid Callax to fill the other.\n\nThe Serpent Druid was also appointed as a Druid Guide. After the moot had taken place, Beleran took Valas to the top of the Mother Grove where he gave his final goodbyes. His staff was handed over to Valas and Beleran jumped off the Mother Tree, Ethanriel\n\nThe Third Age of Disarray\n\nThis was a short age in which the realm of Asulon came to a close, and amongst all the confusion in moving to the new lands, many Druids became lost once again. The transition from Asulon to Elysium\n\nwas a long and strenuous one, and with the lack of Druids, the Order took a drastic step from its recent revival.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elysium&Kalos", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Elysium and Kalos             History\n\nThe Age of Desperation\n\nAs the shift to Elysium detrimentally decreased the number of Druids, only Archdruid Callax and Druid Guide Vi were left to rejuvenate the Order. They accepted\n\nan influx of Dedicants, and began teaching the Druidic ways, setting the stage for a prosperous future of the Order. However, when the Serpent Druid had been killed, Callax was left with a heavy load on his shoulders.  Wilting Druid Giovanni had made his\n\nreturn and was made a Druid Guide. Together, Giovanni and Callax continued to teach their many Dedicants throughout their time dwelling on Elysium in hope to revive the Order to its true potential.\n\nhe Second Age of Mushroom Transgressions\n\nShroom Druid Milo, an Elysium attuned Druid, had chosen his totem of the Shroom for plenty reason; that being his obsession over any and all fungi. While the Wilting Druid and Solstice Druid argued over the\n\nethics of potting plants, the Shroom Druid spent his time spreading his spores throughout the grove. Druids had mushrooms growing from their clothing and hair, soon enough! The battle over potted plants was joined with the battle of mushrooms.\n\nThe Third Age of Reunion\n\nDuring this age, the Druidic Order followed the rest of races to the islands of Kalos. With diligent teaching, strict conduct, and purpose, the Druidic Order was, again, revived. Under the tutelage and regulation of Callax\n\nand Giovanni, the Order was seeing some of its best days. Druids travelled abroad, teaching of the Druidic ways; they brought life in lifeless places and kept up with their up-keeping of nature. The relationship between the Order and Malinor was growing\n\nstrong, too, as Prince Khel and the Inner Circle made the efforts and conference with each other. Malinor asked the Druids to 'nature-ify' their forest and Druidic activity was boosted.", ""),
        StaticBook("Anthos History", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Anthos History\n\nThe Third Age of Activism\n\nThe Order was successful in gathering on the new land mass of Anthos, not loosing the amount of Druids it had in similar transitions in the past. These times saw an influx and\n\nvariety of Dedicants. Within the new Mother Grove of En'Leawyn, the Mother Tree, the Druids saw success and activity in all forms. Oak Druid Thudrin stepped down as Archdruid, and was voted a Hierophant; Song Druid Arik, yet again, fulfulled the position.\n\nThere was an institution of a new branch of the Order known as the Guardian Branch. The Guardian Branch was supposed to serve as an alternative route to the Guiding Branch, where Druids would serve nature more than the Order, of the Circle. Song Druid\n\nArik became an Elder Druid , paralleled to Archdruid, of that branch.\n\nAlso during this age, mysterious seals were uncovered across Anthos, believed to have bound magnificent power or possibly serving as some sort of gateways.\n\nThe Druidic Order, Shamans, Mages Guild, and many other groups participated in the friendly competition of discovering these seals. Discussions were held between the many groups until they eventually uncovered a possible link between the seals and the\n\nAenguls and Daemons. The events ended suddenly and almost magically when the interest and search for the mysterious seals came to a halt.\n\nThe Age of Influence\n\nTo closen the bonds between the Order and Malinor, the High Council of Malinor granted two Prince positions to the Druids. The two individuals voted, by the Order, to fulfill these seats were Hierophant Saviticus and Archdruid\n\nCallax. As members of the High Council, they advised meetings and added a Druidic influence to the city. Only half of the Druid Guides retained activity during this age, and Dedicants became somewhat difficult to regulate.\n\nThe Second Age of Arch Druids\n\nArchdruid Callax retired from position and had then been voted a Hierophant. He passed the Archdruid position to Sunshine Druid Verden who, soon, passed it to Shroom Druid Milo. Dour Druid Dover also submitted his\n\nposition, giving it to Shadow Druid Victivus. The Guardian Branch became inactive, with only Elder Druid Arik affiliating with it. The Order had been thrusted about in so many directions; regulation and conduct was getting loose.\n\nThe Age of Poor Conduct\n\nLost Druids became plenty, sparking this age. Druids were found affiliating with Shuns and ignoring their responsibilities to nature. Dedicants weren't progressing and Druid Guides weren't being regulated. After\n\nbutting heads with the Shadow Druid for so long, Shroom Druid Milo retired, asking for Hierophant Callax's return. Callax accepted the Archdruid position once again in hope to clean up the Order; as the Shadow Druid continued to ignore his\n\nresponsibilities, he and Callax began to butt heads.\n\nA number of Druids were keen on reviving the Order's service to nature and escaping the wretchedness withheld by others in the Order. Callax called a Moot to make the announcement of this split;\n\nhowever the Song Druid responded, begging the Order not to make the mistake it had during the Age of Schisms. Stag Druid Gavin had believed from the start that he could do a better job at leading the Order than the Shadow Druid. The Song Druid discussed\n\nwith the Shadow Druid and he was convinced to pass onto Gavin his Archdruid position while then taking seat as an Elder Druid alongside Arik.\n\nThe Second Age of Disarray\n\nWith the Stag Druid Gavin serving as Archdruid, the Order was soon to find itself facing some troubling times. Some Druids had major disagreements with his ways of leading and many kept quiet about their opinions of the\n\ntimes. Callax was called, by nature, to serve away from the Order, and so he had, again, retired to Hierophancy. Some believe, however, that he was ultimately pushed over the edge by Gavin's inferior intellect and superior being. The Stag Druid was left\n\nas the sole Archdruid with only a few Druid Guides beneath him, and only one, in particular, doing most of the teaching. The Mother Grove was attacked by a corruption-harboring army from the North-lands and the Order was forced out to find a new grove.\n\nThey had moved to the back of a giant turtle, a friend of the Druids known as Samuel.\n\nIt was awhile before another moot was called; the Song Druid retired the Guardian Branch of the Order when it was finally realized to have served no purpose.\n\nSeed Druid Kefi, who had served as an Elder Druid, was then grandfathered as an Archdruid alongside Gavin. All Druid Guardians then became Druid Guides, and progression became a bit muddled.\n\nThe Age of the Head Teacher\n\nWith the Guardian Druids all automatically acting as Druid Guides, the Order had a mess of Guides who didn't want to teach. Eventually Apple Druid Leyun, who preiously lead most of the Guiding work, was made Head Teacher,\n\na title that hadn't been used in decades. All of the Druid Guides were purged from position so that the new Head Teacher could get the Guiding Branch rebuilt from scratch. A third Archdruid was needed to make up for the removal of the Guardian Branch,\n\n and Oak Druid Thudrin was chosen.\n\nHierophant Callax explained how the Druids had become a burden to Samuel, the great turtle, and how the Mother Grove could be reclaimed.The Druids finally left Samuel at peace, regaining the Mother Grove from\n\nits former corrupted existence. Thudrin retired as Archdruid and passed the role onto Leyun; the Head Teacher position was again shelved. Later, the Druidic icon Wayward Druid Respiren returned with his daughter and they took a hand in teaching new\n\nDedicants. \n\nThe Second Age of Schisms\n\nFor the first time in decades, maybe even centuries, the Druidic Order had found itself in another split. The elder Druids who had returned felt as if their help was ignored and excluded\n\nby the youngest generations. Different sections of Druids all shared different views. The Wayward and Equidae Druids split off and began their own Circle far off, vowing to bring back the old ways of the Order. Archdruids Kefi and Leyun had made moves to\n\nexpel Archdruid Gavin, who had become very inactive, from his position. In retaliation, he left the Order and too founded a Circle, the Equinox Circle that he deemed as his own, with his own beliefs and views. The Grand Druid title was later reintroduced\n\nand bestowed upon the Sunshine Druid Verden to serve as the link between the three Circles.\n\nThe Third Age of Little Occurrence\n\nThe three Circles of Druids had failed quicker than their creations were thought a good idea. Druids were scattered too thin and no new attunements were being made. The Circles remained mostly stagnant and\n\nsome individuals were too proud to regroup.\n\nThe Fourth Age of Reunion\n\nAnxious to receive an artifact which had been stolen years prior, the Solstice Druid Callax gathered Autumn Druid Gavin and Sunshine Druid Verden to aid\n\n him in recollecting the Cerridwen Aspect stone. The Druids sensed its being in Haelun'or. The three searched throughout the city until it was eventually found. The stone was kept safe by the Solstice Druid who also used it to spark ideas about reforming", ""),
        StaticBook("Anthos HistoryV2", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Anthos History V2\n\nThe Age of Persistence\n\nNot long after the Order had been reformed, a terrible plague fell over the forest of Malinor. The Elves of Malinor abandoned their dying home of Luminaire and founded the new city,\n\n nearby, of New Malinor. While some Druids began to disappear as the taint devoured the Mother Grove, Leyun and Callax decided it was best to move locations to the island off of New Malinor's shore called Equinox Isle. To regain the isle from a corrupting\n\nbloodshard, the Song Druid sacrificed himself and was overwhelmed by the corruption that he, too, became corrupted. The Druids of the Order felt deep sorrow for his loss.\n\nThe Druids did not give up on the forest, however, and searched high and\n\nlow for the source of the corruption. The Ent Irgin appeared to them and lead them to the far South where they discovered a lost, and ancient, Circle of Druids called the Snow Druids. Archdruid Zeremdos of the Snow Druids revealed to them how to find and\n\n rid of the source of corruption, and they set off to do so. When a team of Druids and the Spirit of Nature, known as Ernest, had finally found the source of corruption, the Spirit sacrificed his sanity for the Druids' passage. Together, Druids Leyun,\n\n Dain, Milo, Faolin and Aeras projected their power through Callax and they were successful in destroying the source, however, resulting in Callax's combustion.\n\nAs the taint slowly left the great forest, the Order then focused on rejuvenating it.\n\nLead by Shroom Druid Milo, the forest was underway of becoming a magnificent nature reserve. For so long, the Elves of Malinor had neither shown interest nor care in what happened to their previous homeland, leaving it to rot away. However, soon after\n\nthe Druids had began their new project within the forest, the High Council threatened them with a note that ordered them to cease their work in the forest or they would face consequences.\n\nBeing forced out of the forest and with the threats and\n\nand ingratitude presented by the High Council of Malinor, the Order realized they weren't welcome there any longer. With Solstice Druid Callax's return, he and a few other Druids began search for a new grove location. Soon enough, they founded the Cross\n\nRoads Grove next to the Cloud Temple and redirected the members of the Order there.\n\nThe Age of Raids\n\nDuring this Age, the Druidic Order resided near-to the Cloud Temple at the Great Nation Crossing. The Cross Roads grove was magnificently grown by Dahlia Druid Dalari and Shroom Druid Milo, and served as a great homeage for the Orde\n\na good time. However, Orcish raids were frequent. Many times, the Druids successfully conned the Orcs away with useless trinkets, but as the population became scarce around the Great Nation Crossing, the issue of monster raids rose. However, as the Druids\n\nwere soon to move on, the Cross Roads grove was to be gifted to the Kharajyr, who were in need of a home.\n\nThe Age of Semi-Activism\n\nDue to the growing threats during the Age of Raids, the Order decided to integrate with the Wood-Elven village of Leanniel. Dove Druid Elorna and Pine Druid Gi'Garun had already grown a beautiful grove, and the active\n\nmembers of the Order easily situated. For a long time, the Order had seemed to grow and even saw the Dedicant and Druids boards overflow, however, the majority of the members were inactive or only semi-active. The Deathsbane family, making up a good\n\nportion of the Order, grew quite distant during this age.\n\nThe Third Age of Disarray\n\nDuring this Age, the tainted and vile Harbingers and Scourge from the frozen Northlands yet again began to destroy the happy lives of the Elven people and their woodlands. Many deadly bloodshards\n\nwere placed over Elven forests by the Scourge, and the active Druids of the Order were on their toes trying to revive the tainted forests and fields.\nTwo events that weren't so drab and chaotic were the return of the Oak and Sunshine Druids and the\n\nvoting of Dahlia Druid Dalari to Druid Guide.\n\n\nThe Age of Vengeance\n\nThis short Age was sparked with the revealing of the Golden Lance, a group who brought together the Descendent forces of Anthos to put a huge dent\n\nin the Scourge. Many Druids took up offensive positions in the Battle for Leanniel to re-obtain the corrupted forests to purge them of their blight and return them to the Aspects. An Elder Tree was discovered in the frozen Northlands, and it provided\n\nseeds that could cleanse the terrible bloodshards. This Age also brought the revealing of The Fringe, a landmass separate of Anthos but also connected through a hidden portal, which many of the Descendents moved to, including the Druidic Order, in hope\n\nto find it a safe-haven from the Scourge.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fringe History", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Fringe History\n\nThe Age of Dwarves\n\nAs the name of this Age implies, the Druidic Order obtained affiliations with the Dwarves of Urguan. At the transition to the Fringe, a group of Druids found a prime location to begin growth of a new\n\n Elder Tree. Claiming the territory surrounding this location was the Urguan Nation who, abnormally, accepted the positioning of the Druidic Order. With Malinor gone and the Order's relationships so deteriorated with the new Elven nation, the new\n\naffiliation with Dwarves would mark a great change in the Order's history.\n\n\nThe Age of Taxes\n\nThis was a short age where Shroom Druid Milo, a risky tax evader, was finally caught and cornered for his overdue\n\ntaxes. A tax collector appeared at a moot of the Druids, demanding the seven minas Milo owed. United in their hatred of the government tax service, the Druids relieved Milo of his debt and sent the tax collector away from the grove. During this same moot,\n\nFrost Druid Dain resigned from his position as Archdruid, deeming Pine Druid Gi'Garun his successor.\n\n\nThe Third Age of Renovation\n\nThe Arkon Grove had saw many marvelous additions since its\n\n initial growth. Over the expansion of time, the Druid Event Hollow, or the Druid Thicket, was created as a place for congregation and mirth. Apple Druid Leyun built and tended to the Library and storage rooms while Dove Druid Elorna grew a simple Druid\n\nhousing in a tree and set-up Dedicant housing in a cave. Pine Druid Gi'Garun put together the record rooms and later built a college and the majority of the Archdruid offices.\n\nThe Fourth Age of Activism\n\nThe Order began plans to reinstate Druidic activity. Endeavors were first made to relocate the grove near-to the Elves, but the Druids were forced away with the false accusations, insults, and hypocrisy of Haelun'or. They\n\nwere welcomed warmly, however, to remain in the Arkon Grove near the Dwarves. During this same time, Archdruid Callax met with a Bishop of the True Faith of Oren to discuss the religious views and indifference between the Church and the Druidic Order,\n\nclearing up many false assumptions. Despite seclusion from the Mali, Druidic activity did begin to accumulate with the attunements of Squirrel Druid Veren, Salamander Druid Sorensil, Dandelion Druid Virella, and the accepting of new Dedicants.\n\nThe Age of Tension\n\nTensions between the order and the elves of Haelun'or had been steadily rising, and the influence of Oren had eradicated the grace that was once held by the Mali. Their vision of purity, and their heightist ways, had begun to\n\nagitate the Shroom Druid Milo, yet he let it pass.\n-\nAs a token of his good nature, he gifted the elves with a grove of peace. The blindness of Haelun'or was shown in a bombardment of void fire, setting the grove ablaze. Milo, along with the Pine\n\nDruid Gi'Garun and Salamander Druid Sorensil were chased back to Kal'Arkon with fire biting at their bottoms.\n-Milo was not discouraged however, and returned to try and make amends, offering another grove. Despite his good intentions,\n\nthe wood elves placed him under arrest. Negotiations were made by the Pine Druid, and Milo was set for release, until the S.A.S (Shovel Attack Squad), Ivybend's finest group of warriors bore down upon the walls of Lin'Ame, seeking to break Milo out with\n\nforce.\n-\nEventually, they were calmed, but the damage had been done. New laws were set in place by the Sohaer Kalenz, stating that the order must be either ruled by Haelun'or, or purged. It took a great deal of negotiation, and some\n\nfake shuns, to finally settle the dispute between the Druids and the Elves. Because of Milo's good nature and efforts by the Order, this age is also referred to as The Age of Good Intent.\n\nThe Third Age of Mushroom Transgressions\n\nIt wasn't long after Shroom Druid Milo, who was already notorious for filling groves with mushrooms, had overgrown the entire Fringe Grove in large mushrooms that everyone woke up in a new realm\n\ncalled Thales. Some Druids believed the mushrooms had got to their heads, others believed the mushrooms caused the already imbalanced Fringe to collapse in upon itself, and still others believe the mushrooms had no part to play in this shift.", ""),
        StaticBook("Vailor History", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Vailor History\n\n*as scribed from old texts*\n\nThe Age of Diplomacy\n\nIn an attempt to improve relations with the Descendant nations, the Druids opted to move their Mother Grove is a plateau upon\n\nthe land of the Wood Elves of Cerulin. Construction of the Grove soon commenced under the leadership of Pine Druid Gi'Garun, who was voted honorary Grove Grower in the place of Alan.\n\nDuring this time, a group of Druids, spurred to action by\n\nthe return of several Hierophants, managed to convince the High Pontiff of the Faith to no longer persecute the Druids, and to recognize the Aspects under their religion. This brought about the greatest thaw in relations between the Druids and humans-\n\na rivalry that had lasted since the fall of Aegis itself. Empowered by their new friendship, the Druids constructed a Grove outside of the capitol city, Felsen, and relations thrived.\n\nThe Age of Diplomatic Falterings\n\nOld rivalries between humans and Druids flared almost immediately following the promise of peace, constant human raidings and pilferings prompting the Druids to abandon their grove outside the city of Felson,\n\nreturning instead to their grove near Laureh'lin. However, that too was jeopardized as heated meetings between the leaders of Laureh'lin and the Druids brought reason for the Druids to believe they were not welcome upon what the Wood Elves viewed as their\n\nland. Following many a moot, the Druids migrated to the desert of the Caliphate, settling near one of its many prosperous oasis, the Caliphate grove sprawling marvelously from its waters.\n\n The Age of the Naelurir\n\nFollowing the departure of the Mother Circle from the Wood Elven lands, there was a void of Druidic presence within Laureh’lin, filled as Artemic, the Hawk Druid founded the Naelurir, the Wood Elven circle of Druids housed\n\nwithin Laureh’lin. Viewed as the first consensual formation of a separate circle, the Naelurir operate within the city of Laureh’lin and beyond, worshipping the Aspects as the Mother Circle does, though primarily focusing on recruitment and education for\n\nthe Wood Elves themselves. Diplomatic relations remained tense at first, though relaxed as the Naelurir developed and expanded as a circle, and relations became common and fortuitous.\n\nThe Age of the Grand Coalescence\n\nAfter a dream passed the Elder Druids of the order, the Druids were drawn to a forest grove out in the wilderness.  There they were thrust into an underground grove where the Aspect Stones were separated.\n\nTogether the Druids roamed the grove and found the Sacred Stones.  As the stones came together, they bonded together and became a single artifact.  Their power emitted at once, and the Druids finally regained control over the power to stave off any blight\n\nfrom their lands.  This marked a moment of true triumph for the Druids.\n\nThe Age of the Grand Council\n\nWith two circles actively worshipping and serving the Aspects within the same Order, it soon became clear a single figure could not manage the complicated relations between the two separate group of\n\nDruids. This prompted the circles to form a ‘Grand Council’, a group of elder or leading Druids both within their respective circles, and nomadic Druids that pledge allegiance to neither to discuss issues within the Order. The formation of the Council\n\nresulted in a dramatic drop in the amount of useless bickering exchanges between the two circles, promising to bring about even stronger relations in the future.\n\nThe Age of the Father Circle\n\nWith the return of the Mushroom Druid Milo, came the creation of the Father Circle within the once beautiful Verdant Grove, recreated under his guidance and renamed the Father Grove, with the help of the Grand\n\nDruid, Callax the druid of the Solstice, the Father Circle flourished and the druid of the Bull, Toren Camoryn, found himself under their tutelage and ended up becoming both a druidic guide, and overseer of the Grove.\n\nThe Age of the Grand Merge\n\nWith the Grand Druid, Callax’s advise, the three circle heads began making frequent meetings and go into deep conversations about the possibility of a merge, and only soon after Callax' departure from the Grand Council,\n\ndid the three circles officially become united under the Order once more. Being led by three archdruids; Artimec, the Hawk Druid. Taynuel, the Hare Druid. Toren, the Bull Druid.\n\nAnd with such a union, the need for the Grand Council was no\n\nmore and the Aspects, were happy.\n\nThe Age of Rose Transgressions\n\nIn an attempt to ward off the powers of the spirit Orgon, the Rose Druid, Puma Druid, and Phoenix Druid banded together to fill the grove with Roses in the hopes that it would suffice as a shield against the plague.\n\nUpon discovering the flood of roses, the Shroom Druid grew angry and demanded they be removed. When his demands were not met, he threatened to bring the Order down for what he perceived as an insult.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aegian History", "§bThe Pine Druid", "Aegian History\n\nThe Age of Founding and the Before Times\n\nDuring this time, Druids were brought together and the Druidic Order was founded by Wayward Druid Respiren Knox. The records of these times may never be fully\n\ndeciphered, however. Although we know of some of the first Druids in Malinor, such as Respiren, Swifthide, and Native, very few details are known. Perhaps someday the past regarding these times will be revealed.\n\nThe First Age of Reconstruction\n\nThe First Age of Reconstruction was ushered in by the initiation of Dragon Druid Apollan into the Order. Apollan was the first to express interest in joining the Order after reading\n\nthe \"Myth of the Great War\" and spent long hours in the Druid grotto after he was famously murdered outside the gates of Laurelin by a bandit. Apollan quickly moved up the ranks. Meanwhile, the Order gew. After initiating Apollan, Respiren expressed\n\ninterest in 'rebuilding the Order'. Everyday, new citizens of Aegis would show up in the grove wanting to become Druids. Most were immediately innitiated. As the Order grew, so did a number of things including property and knowledge. During this time the\n\nOrder gained a lodge in Ravenhold, as well as one in the Laurelin docks. Additionally, two stalls were bought at the Laurelin Marketplace and hundreds of books were added to the Order's library.\n\nThe Age of Abandonment\n\nUnfortunately, due to extremely casual and non-uniform rites of the Wayward Druid, people were joining the Circle, only to wander into the wilderness never to be seen again. With no established Hierarchy\n\nthe Order lacked organisation and purpose. This was made apparent when an Elf named Rowan arrived. Archdruid Rowan, arrived from a far off forest, seeking to re-establish his lost Circle, the Druids of Mother Aegis. Rowan is likely the only citizen to\n\nhave argued with the Order's ways. His only backing for title of Druid was that he 'had a love of nature'. When Rowan became annoyed by the fact that he had to be initiated in order to be allowed into the inner library he stormed off claiming he would\n\nstart his own order somewhere else. Rowan was last seen entering the wilds outside his home in the Ravenlodge, and was never heard from or seen again. Fortunately the dark Age of Abandonment was brought to an end by the light of the Dawn Druid Maiavel.\n\nThe Age of Reformation\n\nThe Age of Reformation was brought on by the ideas and drive of one Maiavel. With her determination, she was able to rally the remaining Druids to create an official lore, hierarchy, rules and sense of order.\n\nThe Second Age of Reconstruction\n\nDuring the second age of Reconstruction many more books were written and a Grand Tree in the Laurelin Grove was planted. New Dedicants were brought in but less frequently than in the first age, and the\n\ninitiation process was more careful and uniform. During this age, the current Hierarchy was established with Apollan as Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Knowledge\n\nThe Age of Knowledge was ushered in by Petyr, the Salmon Druid. His creation of the \"Dedicant's Vow\" and the \"Attunement Ceremony\" greatly helped the Order as did his other ideas, including spearheading the wiki.\n\nThe Age of Mushroom Transgressions\n\nThis age was brought about by the conflict caused by the deffering ideals in the Order. Some Druids were becoming increasingly annoyed at the restriction that came with having a grove in the\n\ncity (where trees/mushrooms could or could not be planted and the structure of the Druid tree). Other Druids argued that it was their duty to promote balance, and that the runway growth of mushrooms flowing from the grove was nowhere near\n\nnatural. Some Druids wanted a new grove to be created outside the city and far away. Others wanted the grove to be close. Still others did not want a new grove at all. However, it is important to note that most of these arguments were caused by\n\nmisunderstandings, which is why this age is sometimes called the Age of Misunderstandings or the Age of Confusion.\n\nThe Age of Renovation\n\nIn the end, most mushrooms were removed from the grove when Maiavel stamped her foot and the mushrooms instantly broke (but were unthoughtfully left on the ground, only to be swept up by Deltaro). A new,\n\nsecret grove was set up in the wilderness outside Ravenhold, (notable work from Mahten and Aeaira whom spent days working on the road leading to civilization). The Laurelin Grove continued to be renovated and improved on, with notable work\n\ncontributed by Lunar Druid Bircalin and Dedicant Deltaro.", ""),
        StaticBook("Athera History", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Athera History\n\n*as scribed from old texts*\n\nThe Age of Contentment\n\nThe arrival in Athera was rather successful, with very few Druids getting lost amidst the transition.\n\nThe new land held many natural wonders, perhaps the greatest being the grand Father Tree \"Rui'thni\", who watched over the new grove lands. It wasn't long before this grand Elder Tree gave birth to Rui'thorn, the only known Ent within Athera at the time.\n\nDuring these early stages within the new realm, the Archdruid Gi'Garun travelled to the Oren capital of Petrus, and managed to negotiate a peace treaty with the humans, putting a stop to a feud that had lasted since Anthos.\n\nThe new-found peace did not last for long, as several members of the reformed White Rose sought to sneak into the grove and attack any elven Druids they met.\n \nSeveral incidents involving the Metal Druid Sharr brought the relationship with\n\nthe Kharajyr onto thin ice. Luckily, Sharr conceded his actions and traversed with the Pine Druid and the Snow Druid to apologise. The apologies were accepted, and thus peace was restored.\n\nThe Fourth Age of Renovation\n\nHaving found a beautiful new grove, the Druids set to work on blessing their new home. The Scion Druid Alan grew housing within Rui'Thni's canopy and Cub Druid Gildun carved the Notable Druid statues,\n\nwhile many others set about growing the library and hollowing out the grottos. This time also saw the return of the Sequoia Druid Lillith, and the Frost Druid Dain, both of whom quickly returned to Guideship.\n\nA great influx of Druids and Dedicants also occurred in this time, and it seemed as though things were looking up for the order.\n\nThe Fourth Age of Mourning\n\nDespite all the peace and contentment, the Solstice Druid Callax passed during this age, and the entire order were saddened by the loss.\n\nTo add to the pain of the Order's members, the Betrayer, Iblees,\n\nmade his return. Taint and corruption spread like wildfire, and the Order were on their toes fighting the waves of attacks that came upon the grove. One small shred of good that appeared in this age, was the establishment of two new Nature's\n\nBounty stores, one on the edge of the father grove, and another within Greyhaven.\n\nThis age came to a conclusion with Pine Druid Gi'Garun's retirement from Archdruid. After delivering a speech about staying devoted to the order,\n\n(Many Druids had been spending more time out of the grove than in it), he stepped down from his position, naming the Sequoia Druid Lillith as his successor.\n\nThe Age of Sudden Tree-growth\n\nThis was a short age, in which the Wolf Druid Ouity tried to combat the imbalance in the world by growing several large trees within the human capital of Petrus. However, as the\n\nDruids admired his work, an almighty roar from the Seven Skies appeared, eradicating the trees and banishing the Wolf Druid from the realm. He would remain gone for some time before reappearing under mysterious circumstances.\n\nThe Third Age of Schisms\n\nThe order seemed to go silent for a very long time, and beneath the silence, tensions were rising amidst the Aspects' children. The Druids were beginning to get tired of the Archdruids' lack of activity,\n\nand began to leave the order to go their own way. The Grove was almost always empty, and something needed to be done. During the next moot, Bear Druid Isabella was questioned for her clan’s long stint apart from the Order, and defending the premature\n\nattunement of the Honey Druid. Enraged by the hostility of the Order, Isabella walked out of the moot, later officiating clan Deathsbane’s separation from the Order through the creation of the Spring Circle.\n\nMuch later in time, out of lack of faith of the leadership of both the Mother and Spring Circles, another circle of Druids was on the rise. The Verdant Meadow was initiated by a self claimed Druid, Marcus, along with Cub Druid Gildun,\n\nHare Druid Taynuel, and Dahlia Druid Dalari in effort to preserve and continue the ways and fellowship of Druidism, but soon rejoined the order after Sky God Dagoth threatened to end their existence.\n\nThis age came to a close when after being\n\npersuaded by the Shroom Druid, Pine Druid Gi'Garun retook the position of Archdruid, filling the gap left by the retirement of Elk Druid Danic.\n\nThe Fifth Age of Mourning\n\nMilo and Gi had been put in charge of redesigning the grove, and Druids were beginning to return to it as the renovations were more inviting. However, just as the Order was beginning to recover, things took a turn for the\n\nworst.\n\nOne harsh and windy night, the sky turned red as taint began to form in the center of the father Grove. From within it rose the Betrayer, his minions at his side. The Druids fought bravely, but had to back off,\n\nas even their combined force couldn't hold off Iblees' might. They retreated to a chamber, deep below Rui'thni, within which the Aspect stones were kept. Iblees was not hindered by the elderwood roots that sealed the chamber, and the\n\nDruids cowered in fear. From thin air, he produced the tainted Axe of Krug, and with one swift motion, he sliced into the Aspect stones, a blinding light filling the cavern as a huge blast of taint fell upon the Grove. Miasma rose from the once crystal\n\nclear streams, and the crippled Druids had no choice but to flee their once peaceful home.\n\nWounded and mourning for the loss of the Sequoia Druid, who had died defending the stones, the Druids wandered the forests of Fiandria, searching for a place\n\nto settle and recover. They retreated to the Sequoia Grove whilst they healed their wounds and gathered food. It wasn't long before they discovered an island, just off the coast from the Oakhearth forests, and after praying to the Aspects,\n\nit was here that they decided to settle.\n\nThe Age of Resurrection\n\nAfter settling on the island, which they named Elavriel, a group of Druids were summoned through a strange portal by unknown forces, where they emerged in a strange forest. Here they discovered the\n\nrecently deceased Sequoia Druid Lillith and Solstice Druid Callax, whom both returned with them to the order’s great delight. Soon after, Archdruid Gi’Garun took a group of Druids to the ruins of the Father Grove to investigate a strange surge to of\n\nnatural energies that he felt amidst the taint. They uncovered a secret cavern at the base of the ruined Father Tree, where the last of the tree’s life had been diverted to protect three special seeds; the offspring of Rui’thni. One of these seeds was\n\nplanted on Elavriel, and nurtured by Gi’Garun, Callax, Thudrin, Milo and Verden, until it became a grand Elder Tree named Rui’livri. During this time, Verden and Arinislia ascended to the position of Archdruid, and the trio soon had the order back on it's\n\nfeet good and proper!\n\nThe Age of Relapse\n\nAfter some time had passed, both Archdruids Verden and Gi'Garun had vanished without a trace. With Archdruid Arinislia having to act in their stead, both Snow Druid Beth and Squirrel Druid Veren were voted in the elders' places to\n\nensure stability, under the guidance of the eldest Archdruid. Peace was once more restored, shortly before Arinislia was captured and tortured. This then led to the recovery of the Archdruid, who wished to step down upon her return. Solstice Druid Callax\n\nwas named as the successor. However, without the leadership of the original trio, the Order fell in to one of its darkest ages with corruption. A number of both younger and older druids withdrew from the circles to instead wander.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cerridwen", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Cerridwen\n\nSecond Book of Intro to the Aspects.\n\nby Sunshine Druid Verden\n\n**On the first page is a drawing of a beautiful woman wrapped in green, an otherworldly glow about her.**\n\n      Cerridwen\n\nGoddess of Life, Mother of The Morn, Lady of Light\n\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Nature’s Healing\n\nGoverning Season: Spring\n\nIt is said that whenever the sun rises, so too does the great Springmother. Cerridwen’s divine light breathes life into the cold darkness each morning, replenishing the weary with renewed strength and vigor. During the creation of the world she is said\n\n to have personally sculpted every flower and fruit, every nut and vegetable into an object of replenishment and beauty. Cerridwen’s love for the world is unconditional, and she desires peace above all else.\n\nTo experience Cerridwen is to experience the purest form of bliss. You must run barefoot through a bright meadow in the spring-time, your hair down and blowing in the warm breeze as the sunlight washes over your sealed eyelids. You must kneel at the edge\n\nof a coursing stream and stare down at your reflection, watching the myriad of colorful fish swimming by without much care in the world. You must hold an infant in your arms, helpless, trusting, and know what it is to be a mother.\n\n   Rituals and Beliefs\n\n*When any new life is brought into the world, Cerridwen is believed to watch over them as any mother would. For this reason the phenomenon of ‘Baby Luck’ is often attributed to her.\n\n*Cerridwen’s recognized symbol is the sun, which can commonly be found in druidic libraries as a depiction of ‘Illuminating the darkness of ignorance’.\n\n*Small shrines to Cerridwen can be found in family gardens or farms to ward off pests and encourage a good harvest.\n\n*The officiant of a marriage often blesses the lucky couple with words of Cerridwen to encourage a\n\nlong, and child-filled life.\n\n\n          ~*~*~\n       Next Book\n       Cernunnos", ""),
        StaticBook("Cernunnos", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Cernunnos\n\nThird Book of Intro to the Aspects\n \nBy Sunshine Druid Verden\n\n**On the first page is a drawing of a tall man with larger antlers on his head, a bow in his hand. A hard look to him.**\n\n         Cernunnos\n\nThe Great Huntsman, The Green Man, The Horned God\n\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Control of Nature\n\nGoverning Seasons: Summer and Winter\n\nWoe to thee who might seek to prey upon the flock of Lord Cernunnos. Legends speak of arrows being loosed upon the skies in numbers uncountable, raining upon those that seek to disrupt the careful balance of nature. It was he who gave the porcupine it's\n\nquills, the rattlesnake it's fangs, and he who first taught the Elves of ancient times the art of bowmanship. Cernunnos waits patiently at the side of Cerridwen, guarding her peace with the vigil of a hawk and the calm of an evergreen forest.\n\nTo experience Cernunnos is to experience true challenge. You must hide yourself amidst the canopy of a great green forest, your body obscured by a cloak of leaves you stitched together with pine needles and sap, your stomach howling for the smallest hint\n\nof nourishment, your fingers wrapped tightly around a bowstring as you stalk your prey on the forest floor. You must stand steady and vigilant at world’s end, bloody and broken, and counter the pain with courage and skill.\n\n    Rituals and Beliefs\n\n*To this day, all Elves owe their natural affinity for archery to Cernunnos. It is believed he personally taught the ancient Elven lord, Malin, the art as a means to balance the diplomatic race against more violent beings.\n\n*In times of war, it is quite common to hear calls of courage and steadiness in the name of the Huntsman.\n\n*Many of the greatest druids have taken an affinity for Cernunnos, and some believe him to be the leader of the Aspectual Trinity.\n\n*When Druids have a goal in mind, they often refer to it as a hunt in reference to the Huntsman.\n\n*The condition of Albinism is often attributed as a blessing from Cernunnos. Creatures with a pure, white coat and predisposed as honorable and\n\nmajestic beasts.", ""),
        StaticBook("Gifts of Nature1", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Gifts of Nature\nVolume I - Upon Attunement\n\nWhen a Dedicant has achieved full enlightenment on his path to Druidism, he is attuned to Nature and becomes a Druid. Attunement allows the Druid a direct connection to the Aspects of Nature,\n\nnd he may communicate and react with the world on a whole new level. This new relationship grants the Druid empathy with Nature and the potential to gain influence over it. In order to enhance his influence over Nature, a Druid must further closen his\n\nelationship with it and the Aspects; this is done through years of extensive meditation, prayer, and devout service.\n\nThe Gifts of Nature obtained upon attunement from the Aspects are referred to as Nature’s Communion\n\nand Favors of Nature.\n\n~ Nature’s Communion ~\nSharing or exchanging intimate thoughts and feelings with floral and faunal life is what defines as Nature’s Communion. The Druid forms companionships with beasts and shares the serenity\n\nof life with the abundant and diverse vegetation of the world. Nature’s Communion is the bond between Druid and Nature. And since a Druid cannot make sufficient use of the other Gifts without a sufficient bond with Nature, this Gift serves as the\n\n foundation of all Druidic abilities.\n\n~ Favors of Nature ~\nUshering forth the lush verdure of a meadow, twisting and shaping roots and vines and coaxing the growth of a full sized tree are all abilities of this Gift. Through Favors of Nature,\n\na Druid seemingly manipulates the world around him. Often times this Gift is referred to as Control of Nature, however, a Druid does not control Nature; he asks the Aspects for their helping hand, and depending on the bond a Druid shares with the Aspects,\n\nhe is granted his request. A Druid is the caretaker and the servant of Nature, therefore, he uses this Gift only in his service and to promote balance and peace.\n\n~*~\nThese Gifts of Nature’s Communion and Favors\n\nof Nature are those which all Druids can excel in after attunement. Though, a Druid’s ability and influence over Nature depends heavily on the bond he shares with the Aspects. One who exhibits selflessness and persistence will only propel forward.", ""),
        StaticBook("Gifts of Nature2", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Gifts of Nature\nVolume II- Healing\n\nAttuned Druids gain a wide array of natural abilities. The abilities which, arguably, make the biggest difference and impression on the world are articulated by healing the various forms of life. In Druid\n\npractices, two forms of healing exist: Druidic Healing and Blight Healing. These ancient arts are practiced to see that balance is restored to unnaturally harmed lands, plants, peoples, and beasts.\n\nUnlike Nature’s Communion and Favors of Nature, these Gifts of Nature do not solely rely on a Druid’s relationship with the Aspects, but also require knowledge and guidance. Each method of healing must\n\nbe taught by a Druid who is already experienced with that particular method.\n\n~ Blight Healing ~\nLands affected by blight, plague, disease, death, decay or magical scarring are given new life, and in the place of the infertile soil there is\n\nverdure. A Druid hears the cries and pain of Nature when it is scorn and imbalanced. A Druid practiced in the methods of Blight Healing has the ability to infuse Nature’s energies into healing scorn life and with their ability to\n\n“manipulate” Nature, a Druid can reseed dead and decayed wood and other plants and grow them back into the ground.\n\n~ Healing ~\nThe methods of Druidic healing combines the practice of Druidic herbalism with the natural blessings of\n\nthe Aspects and Gifts of Nature. The effects of potent herbs with healing properties are enhanced by the Druid through meditation and blessings. The herbs can then be prepared into various remedies, salves and poultices along with the combination of other\n\n natural elements. Whether it is a leafy patch up or a life-saving antidote, Druidic healers are able to tend to peoples of all races and beasts of any wild with the creative blessings of this skill.\n\n~*~\nThe Gifts of Nature are by far the most noble of Druidic abilities. It is known that Druids are some of the best healers in the land and are only outshone by Clerics and Monks in regards to healing the peoples of the races.\n\nDruids will only do remarkable things with these Gifts in their service to Nature and will become remarkable individuals by going the extra mile to act as the physicians of Nature: plant and beast.", ""),
        StaticBook("Threat&FinalWord", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Critique of Nemiisae\nOr: The Charade of the \"Aspectual Trinity\"\n\n*Elk Druid Danic*\n\nV4 The Threat and Final Words, Final Volume\n\nNemiisae leads us away from the Balance:\n\nA constant theme to Nemiisae’s guidance is so called ‘Self-Enlightenment’. Brother Sunshine writes that Nemiisae teaches us that “... we are better off finding our own light rather than basking in the light of others”. As nice as this sounds, it implies\n\na much darker reality. Nemiisae’s teachings encourage us to separate from the natural order of things. To follow solely her guidance to attain “enlightenment”. She is described to give “salvation” to creatures after “evolving” them into something new.\n\nIf we include the previous statement of her demanding “servitude”, her entire guidance takes a sinister and conquering tone. All of this reaffirms the conclusion she is nothing more than a meddling lesser aengudaemon attempting to mislead and misguide\n\nothers to acquire more cultish followers to expand her power. Her teachings go against all that the Order stands for, and stands in opposition to the very will of the Aspects. A parasite, if you will, feeding off of the thoughts and prayers of a group of\n\npious worshippers.\n\nFor the sake of arguments, let us entertain the idea of ‘self-enlightenment’. What exactly might self-enlightenment infer? The most likely answer is finding enlightenment through self examination and understanding without\n\nthe use of an outside party. How does one find self-enlightenment? According to the book, apparently through finding our ‘own light’ instead of basking in the ‘light of others’. How do you find your own light? By following Nemiisae, of course! But wait,\n\nif you enlighten yourself, through finding your own light, why would one follow Nemiisae, a being that demands servitude? It becomes rather contradictory upon closer inspection. It becomes even stranger when luminescent creatures become an example\n\nof things that have been guided and “attained their own light”, specifically Giant Glowshrooms. As glorious as fungi is, I think it would be a great disservice to them to assume they care very much about self-enlightenment. They serve a beautiful service\n\nin assisting in decomposition to recycle things back into the cycle, the very cycle Nemiisae advocates against confining oneself in, might I add. It seems rather strange that everything Nemiisae seems to advocate rather contradictory or seems to be a\n\nresult of misattribution.\n\nThis is what I consider is the greatest threat to including Nemiisae among the Aspects, and why the concept ‘Aspectual Trinity’ is nothing but a charade. Throughout this entire essay we have studied and dissected Nemiisae\n\non numerous fronts, and each and every time we have found inconsistencies, contradictions, misattributions, and above all, a philosophy that advocates against the very system she claims to be lady of! Her existence within the Order as an Aspect breeds a\n\ntoxic mindset that rejects the traditional authority of the Order and the original Aspects, instead embracing the tyrannical and violent authority of Nemiisae herself. It is pertinent that we remove her Aspect title and reject her as the deceiving\n\naegudaemon she truly is as quickly as possible, including the reeducation of Druids taught under her philosophy to preserve our integrity as an Order that reveres only the Blessed Aspects she disrespects and preserves the Balance that she neglects.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nemiisae&Druids", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Critique of Nemiisae\nOr: The Charade of the Aspectual “Trinity”\n\n*Elk Druid Danic*\n\nV3 Nemiisae and Druidism\n\nNemiisae is incompatible with Druidism:\n\nLet us return to the previously cited book, “Intro to the Aspects: Atheran Edition”, as our primary source of information on this supposed ‘Aspect’, as very little information is found elsewhere. Upon reading the section dedicated to her, an interesting\n\nthing can be noticed: She has incredibly little influence on the Balance. Not only that, but it seems to show Nemiisae is direct conflict with Cerridwen in particular at times. Brother Sunshine writes “Where Cerridwen breathes life and motherhood,\n\nNemiisae demands death and servitude”. As a Druid of many centuries, I find two things greatly troubling within such a statement. One, while the opposite of life is death, it does not seem to explain how exactly Nemiisae’s demands for death result in the\n\nbenefit for the Balance as a whole. A death is never pointless in the natural world, it always has a result that benefits the cycle. Anything else should be greatly despised and resented. Instead of explaining such a thing, it follows up with the\n\nstatement of ‘servitude’. As important as servitude to the Aspects is as Druids, Nemiisae specifically demanding it does little to influence the Balance.\n\nTwo, it seems to greatly misattribute features found in the other Aspects to her specifically. As stated previously, Brother Sunshine claims she “... demands death…”. This is rather curious, as no Aspect specifically ‘demands’ death, nor should they.\n\nDeath is both a result of the two Aspects (Cerridwen and Cernunnos’) realms interacting to maintain Balance, and the result of an imbalance. For example, an Elk is hunted by a pack of wolves to feed their young. The Elk is killed, but not only are the\n\nwolves capable of continuing life from its meat, but the Elk as a population are capable of sustaining themselves and are prevented from overpopulating. The wolves would be Cernunnos’ realm, and the elk, Cerridwen. Death was the result of their realms\n\n interacting and maintaining a balance. When imbalanced, the Elk would overpopulate, consume all vegetation, and thus starve to death, and visa versa with the wolves. To attribute death purely to Nemiisae would do a great disservice to the both the\n\nAspects influence, and discredit the Balance as a whole. Along with that, it makes even less sense to claim Nemiisae somehow ‘demands’ death due to her seeming little interaction with either of the other Aspects realms.\n\n\nCerridwen is not the only one that Nemiisae comes into conflict with, Cernunnos too is shoved aside at times. One clear example is the Spider, one of the most brilliant hunters within Nature. To claim that Cernunnos does not lord over these brilliant\n\ncreatures and does not influence their outstanding hunt is a direct insult to The Great Huntsman. The book does mention the mysterious Mori’quessir, however, leading me to suspect that these are not spiders of Natural origin. My suspicion is reaffirmed\n\nby the line, “... where those who spoke of light were sacrificed in rituals of blood and web”. I find it strange and somewhat alarming that such a natural and vital thing could lead to such violence and ‘rituals of blood’. The inclusion of blood leads me\n\nto suspect even further that these rituals are anything other than some dark witchcraft serving Nemiisae. \n\nIn Brother Sunshine’s defence of Nemiisae, he states that “Cerridwen provides the life, and Nemiisae evokes the evolution\n\nof that life into something new but sustaining. For many species of plant and animal, Nemiisae has been their salvation”. Hardly does this provide an adequate defence for such a dark being, as it once again insults the Aspects themselves,\n\nclaiming that Cerridwen’s gifts upon this world were somehow unsustainable, and Nemiisae was there to ‘save the day’, so to say. This ‘evolution’ Nemiisae supposedly evokes genuinely becomes alarming and somewhat contradictory once you return to the\n\nprevious line, “She exists as a dark mirror of all that Cerridwen stands for, and perverts her creations of happiness and love into monsters of horror and passion.” In one line, it describes her as perverting the creations of Cerridwen, then in a\n\nsudden turn, claims she ‘evokes the evolution’. Evolving creatures into “monsters of horror and passion” does little to assist the Balance, and seems to be more akin to creating monstrosities for her own pursuits, especially once you reconsider the\n\nprevious “demands… servitude” statement.\n\nThis annotation of one of the only tombs of knowledge on such a being gives numerous examples on her complete incompatibility with current day Druidism. Rarely does she influence the Balance or Natural world,\n\nand when she does it’s described as perversion. Constantly does she come into conflict with the other Aspects in the worst of ways, and consistently does is she attributed things that are unattributable. However, the greatest threat of her inclusion\n\nis none of what I have described thus far. The greatest threat is her guidance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nemiisae'sReveal", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Critique of Nemiisae\nOr: The Charade of the Aspectual “Trinity”\n\nv2 Nemiisae's Reveal\n\n*Elf Druid Danic*\n\nNemissae's reveal does not lend credence:\n\nWhile for centuries, there have been whispers from the shadow’s that there hide secret cults and sects worshipping an unknown third Aspect, Nemiisae did not truly reach the spotlight until the turbulent times of Athera. Under conflict with the renewed\n\nUndead forces, Nemiisae was revealed to the Archdruids through a series of dreams, apparently from the Aspects themselves. Druid’s receiving guidance from the Aspects is not an unheard of event, and thus was immediately accepted\n\ncredible with little oversight. As one of the Archdruids to receive these dreams, I paid little attention to the actual credibility to them and took them at face value; A mistake I wish to rectify in this essay.\n\nThe key reason why little attention was paid to the credibility of these dreams as a simply put statement: Why would anyone question the Aspects? They had not led us astray previously. The fact is they still haven’t, because they were not the one's to\n\nbestow such dreams in the first place. At that time in history, the Undead had resurged once again, and their god, the Arch Daemon Iblees, had been released. This created a unique situation within our world, a situation I believe was taken advantage.\n\nAs we know, there are numerous god-like beings that interact with our world, some more powerful than others. Typically, the weaker ones are unable to create interactions within our world and thus are left rather obscure. However, during a time of chaos\n\nand confusion caused by Iblees reentrance into our realm, it is possible that lesser aengudaemons were capable of influencing the mortal world in ways they previously were unable to.\n\nGiven the nature of Nemiisae’s reveal, and the condition of the world upon her reveal, it gives reason to believe that simply because Nemiisae revealed herself to the Archdruids under the guise of Aspect gifted dreams does not lend true credibility and\n\nproof that she is an actual Aspect. Taking her nature into account as described by Brother Sunshine, there is good reason to believe that Nemiisae is nothing but a lesser aengudaemon who had taken advantage of the turmoil to spread her influence, and\n\nthus increase her power, through deceiving one of the most populated guilds in the world. Currently, there is no true foundation of credible evidence to prove that Nemiisae is a true Aspect, now that her reveal has been found to be rather suspect.\n\nWithout the testimony of the Aspects dreams, worshipping her as an Aspect would be the equivalent of taking any other aengudaemon and placing them as a third Aspect, thus showing the illogical charade that is the supposed ‘Aspectual Trinity’.\n\nUnfortunately, many people will find these critiques rather inconsequential and continue disastrously worshipping her, bringing us to our next topic of discussion; What makes the worship is Nemiisae so disastrous? To answer this, we will next delve into\n\n features that make up Nemiisae.", ""),
        StaticBook("Herbs 1", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Druid's Book of\n         Herbs\n\n*Herbs from Solstice Druid Callax.*\n\n-Scribed with additions by Apple Druid Leyun Kameki-\n\nLeyun's Forward\n\nWhen I learned to use the gifts of nature to heal, Callax gave me his list of herbs. It is time to write this information down in a form that can be widely shared. Hopefully it'll be of use to anyone who heals using herbs.\n\n*Athin\nAn herb with a blue visage. It grows at the bottom of almost frozen lakes.\n\nEffects: Athin works as a cooling balm, making an excellent lotion. It also can cause blue hued skin.\n\nOverdose: Mild Frostbite\n\nAlchemical Properties: Athin is a strong water symbol, good for use in colder potions.\n\nNotes: Generally requires at least two people to harvest due to freezing conditions.\n\n*Alabaster Leaf\nAn herb of Alabaster hue. It grows in drier areas in forests, typically near fallen trees.\n\nEffects: As a mild poison, Alabaster Leaf causes dizziness and nausea.\n\nOverdose: Extreme fatigue.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Alabaster Leaf is a moderate earth symbol.\n\nNotes: Often used as a dye/paint. Harvest by carefully cutting the leaves.\n\n*Aqua Nymph\nA small chain of fragile blue baubles that grow underwater. It is often mistaken for frog or toad spawn.\n\nEffects: Severe hallucinations when ingested as an herb. Powerful burn remedy when boiled. Causes the throat to\n\nclose up when ingested in oil form.\n\nOverdose: Stronger effects\n\nAlchemical Properties: Aqua Nymph is a strong water symbol.\n\nNotes: Aqua Nymph is extremely delicate and must now be touched by hands. A correct\n\nway to harvest the herb is to take the thin layer of sediment that it grows in and the whole plant, scooping it into a jar so that it doesn't touch the edge and it's roots remain intact. If done any other way, it will likely ruin the plant.\n\nThe plant can be made into a powerful burn remedy by pouring the whole plant into a cauldron and small pot. When boiled, the plant's buds will release a liquid that will make at thick oil that can be easily spread over burns.\n\n*Blissfoil\nAn oily blue flower that grows in clumps under birch trees.\n\nEffects: Numbness where the oil is applied. It has been known to cause headaches.\n\nOverdose: Permanent loss of touch in the affected area.\n\nNotes: Oils are found near the petals and stalk.\n\n*Bread Mold\nA blue of green mold that grows on bread, cheese, and other food items.\n\nEffects: This particular mold only affects some individuals negatively. In these individuals, exposure can cause a rash or the throat to close up. The mold\n\nhas disease fighting properties that can be brought out by a skillful Druid.\n\nOverdose: Too much can cause confusion, odd behavior, a severe rash, reduced urination, or seizures. \n\nNotes: A druid is required to bring out the disease\n\nfighting properties, and care must be taken to reduce the effects of other types of mold growing with it.\n\n*Blood Lotus\nA rust coloured herb, found growing near entrances to caves in hot climates\n\nEffects: Extremely nutritious, but extended use can turn the skin orange.\n\nOverdose: Kidney stones.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate fire symbol.\n\nNotes: Can be made into a strong, deep red alcohol.\n\n*Crimson Vase\nA vase shaped plant with deep red petals that is commonly found in places with a mild amount of light. The herb grows on tree parasitically.\n\nEffects: Slight burning sensation if it's liquid is spilt. It'll cause rashes.\n\nOverdose: Will burn even more.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Crimson Vase is a strong fire symbol, typically used in love, rage, and envy potions, as well as Alchemist Fire.\n\nNotes: The petals are warm to the touch.\n\n*Crouching Feather\nAn herb with three white, drooping petals. It only blooms at night under large bushes.\n\nEffects: Calming and clears the mind. Can cause light-headedness. With proper Druidic preparation, it can be used as a sedative.\n\nOverdose: Coma\n\nAlchemical Properties: Strong air symbol. It is typically used in clarity potions.\n\nNotes: Crouching Feather is highly fragile. Wind can damage the plant.\n\n*Diddyfuckle\nA bright, purple flowered herb that tends to grow in jungles and plains.\n\nEffects: The purple stems cause extremely itchy rashes. The center ball's juices can be used as a sedative or to clear the mind. Can cause\n\nunconsciousness or death if not prepared correctly.\n\nOverdose: Death\n\nNotes: Tiny amounts can be a delicacy.\n\n*Draugr Tongue\nAn aloe like, orange flowered plant that grows in grassy plains.\n\nEffects: The liquid in the plant makes for a good disinfectant, but stings.\n\nNotes: The stem is not useful.\n\n*Dwarf's Pumpkin\nA stinky, orange leafed shrub that grows by water, usually in swamps.\nEffects: Burning taste similar to ginger.\n\nOverdose: Burns!\n\nAlchemical Properties: Strong fire symbol, commonly used in Alchemist Fire.\n\nNotes: Easy to pick. Smells bad. It's named for it's use in Dwarven brews. The brightest leaves are the most potent.\n\n*Elf's Hair Vine\nA white blossomed herb with an icy white visage. It grows on high cliffs in frozen lands.\n\nEffects: It has a calming effect and can result in more fluid movements, but may cause someone to move too fast, in which case physical\n\nexhaustion could be an issue.\n\nOverdose: Rapid heart beat, burst blood vessels and the life.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Strong air symbol. It's used in grace and speed potions.\n\nNotes: The vine is extremely fragile, and the petals contain nectar.\n\n*Flame Tongue Root\nA bright rust coloured root with a rose-like flower. It can be found in heavily forested areas, burnt out forests, and areas with a lot of sun and water.\n\nEffects: The root can be used as a coagulant, thought it can burn. Be\n\nwarned that the spores can cause sensitivity to touch.\n\nOverdose: Too much blood clotting, strokes, etc.\n\nAlchemical Properties:\nModerate fire symbol.\n\nNotes: Fairly hot to the touch. Watch out for those spores.\n\n*Frost Vine\nA small, brittle blue vine that grows in frosty areas, usually on trees.\n\nEffects: Soothes pain and numbers/relaxes muscles where applied. Full numbness of limbs can occur on prolonged exposure.\n\nOverdose: Frostbite.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate water symbol.\n\n*Goblin's Ivy\nA short brown or green vine that can be found clinging to buildings and rocks.\n\nEffects: Scentless, mild, nausea inducing poison that can also cause mild dehydration.\n\nOverdose: More nausea, vomiting,\n\nand diarrhea.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Weak to moderate earth symbol commonly used in resilience potions.\n\nNotes: Goblin's Ivy can survive in nearly moistureless areas.\n\n*Grobsnopper\nA pink or yellow bud-like flower that grows on trees, typically found in moist forests.\n\nEffects: Potent healing effects. \"Soaks up\" disease.\n\nOverdose: Stomach aches.\n\n*Habgobsnit\nA tall, pale-shaded grass that grows in forests and plains.\n\nEffects: Juices good for numbing. It can provide a sense of clarity... or just get you high.\n\nOverdose: Paralysis.\n\n*King's Ivy\nAn ivy with golden flowers and a dark green stalk that grows on rocks, tree trunks, and old ruins.\n\nEffects: Can be taken as a stimulant, but can cause agitation.\n\nOverdose: Heart attacks, and in general too much energy.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Shun", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Shun\n\nAn individual who has proven to be a threat to the Druidic Order might very well be worthy of a shun. One such individual isn’t someone who a particular Druid or a select few Druids dislike or disagree with, but is someone who has\n\noffended an entire Circle or the Order in some way.\n\nOnly an Archdruid may make the notion to shun an individual, which is done while holding the floor at a Moot. He or she will state the reasons for presenting the shun; following, the subject\n\nof the shun, if present, will answer to the accusations before the Druids of the Moot. Finally, the Inner Circle shall openly vote to pass the shun or not.\n\nOnce a shun is passed on an individual, it shall last until the Inner Circle deems it right,\n\nwith only the best interests for the Order, to end. No Druid of the Order is permitted to speak to a shunned individual or affiliate with them in any way and should, therefore, answer their words with “Much wind pours from your mouth.”\n\nA shunned individual is never allowed to step foot on the holy grounds of a grove, unless requested by an Archdruid in the case of relieving the shun, and shall be forcibly removed from a grove, by any Druid, if he or she refuses to leave. Furthermore,\n\n if a shunned individual continuously harasses a Druid outside the grove, and the Druid or Dedicant has spoken the single statement he is permitted to speak to a shun, then hostile actions may be taken upon the individual.\n\nA Druid may also be shunned from the Order, and sometimes unattuned as well, which is the most dishonored action able to be taken upon a Druid. A shun is nothing to ever take lightly and any member of the Druidic Order, or the independant Circle who has", ""),
        StaticBook("Aspects Basics", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "BASIC KNOWLEDGE OF THE ASPECTS\nBY BIGGS BRANDYBOTTOM\nCURRENT DEDICANT OF SONG DRUID ARIK\n\n*The journal appears to be enscribed in elegant calligraphy, the ink being a mixture of juices from various berries.*\n\nThe Aspects are spiritual beings which, together, have formed what the physical world has deemed \"nature\". There are only two Aspects which the druids acknowledge- their names being Cerridwen and Cernunnos. Without one another, the physical world as we\n\nknow it would fail to function.\n\nThis can be explained as both Aspects have unique characteristics that equally compliment and counteract each other. To elaborate, Cerridwen is known as the \"Mother of Harvest\" and responsible for such.\n\nCerridwen is the goddess of all that grows and develops within the natural world. As the matron of farmers and such, Cerridwen has blessed them with many gifts- some being the seasons, plentiful harvests, and the physical world itself.\n\nAs stated before, Cerridwen is only one side of the mina. Cernunnos is widely recognized as the patron Aspect of hunters and the likes. As the \"Father of the Hunt\", Cernunnos is responsible for the cycle of life and death within the natural world- this\n\nbeing both a compliment and contradiction to Cerridwen. Likewise, Cernunnos had gifted the physical world with wildlife, natural phenomenons, and much more. \n\nOnly together do the Aspects function.\n\nThis can be seen through many examples in life, especially if viewed in the correct way. For example:\n\nImagine a large, flourishing field of potatoes. Though the potatoes may be ready to be harvested, a group of large\n\nswine seem to settle nearby. Over time, the potato crops begin to dwindle as the swine grow and reproduce- thus allowing the nearby village to hunt these swine for pelts, meat, and the likes. Once hunted for a reasonable amount of time, the swine\n\npopulation decreases, while the potato yield increases.\n\nThis example illustrates the cycles that the Aspects have set for the natural world to undertake. This cycle of sorts allows the world to function correctly though,\n\nand the beings of the world must respect the Aspects and their cycles of life and death. Fittingly, it is also these being's responsibility to aid Natures course, and in turn, are rewarded nicely.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Healing", "§bThurak'Yar", "The Nature Of\nDruidic Healing\n\nBy Lillith Winterleaf\n\nDruidic Healing is a topic many are curious about, but few, even amongst Druids, wholly understand the cost on the one performing it. Most Druids use living reagents to assist in the mending process, this is so that they can draw\n\n on the living essence within the reagent and draw less from themselves, but a skilled Druidic healer can also safely mend wounds without any reagents on hand.\n\nDruidic healing is, quite simply, an exchange of living essence from the healer to the patient. The Druid focuses upon the living tissue around the wound and asks the patient’s body to mend itself, giving of their own living\n\nessence to aid in the healing.\nThis is where the cost and risk of Druidic healing is, and you should heed it well. A Druid is capable of amazing feats of healing at times, but all of our healing derives from ourselves and it is possible to\n\ncause lasting damage to oneself while healing the wounds of another. This is why living reagents are used, to lessen the need to draw from oneself. I have personally healed a variety of wounds, from cuts and scrapes to sword clashes\n\nto broken bones to some far worse. They all draw from you to some extent, even when using reagents. Simple wounds you may not even notice the drain upon yourself, it depends how strong your healing has become, and how\n\nfamiliar you are with the process. Practise truly makes perfect in this regard. I am of the belief an example of a mistake is the best way to pass on a lesson, so I will share how I nearly lost my life while healing the King of Skravia back on Asulon.\n\nHe and some of his soldiers had been lured out to the base of a rogue mage that had harassed their kingdom for a long while. When they gained the upper hand and cornered the mage, his trap was revealed, the walls of his base were lined with\n\n explosives, and he detonated them. Three of the soldiers died in the blast, the others dragged a severely injured but alive King back to Skravia and to my inn, knowing I was a healer. His wounds were great, part of his skull was visible on\n\nthe top of his head, and he had shrapnel wounds all across his body, blood everywhere. There was nothing to do but try, I coated the wounds with a mixture of living slime and bonemeal, then began the healing process; drawing upon the living essences\n\nas well as myself to stabilise and mend the kind King laying before me. It took nearly two hours, but his wounds sealed and he was alive and left in a coma for a few days. However, I had given of myself far more than I should have, my skin was pale,\n\nalmost white, and my eyes had lost most of their colour and I could hardly breathe. I am told I lost consciousness; I awoke five weeks later, my every limb sore, my hair a shade lighter, but my skin almost back to its normal tone. A fellow Druid,\n\nJena, had to come out to restore some living essence to me or I would have perished within the first day or two, I was so weakened. Take my mistake with you if you seek to heal, you five of yourself, there is a limit you will reach and you\n\nshould not go beyond it or you risk your life. Find your balance between personal sacrifice and reagents to ease your burden, you almost always have something available to ease your personal strain, even if it is the grass around you or a planter\n\nof flowers outside the window, any living essence can ease your burden if the situation is a bit beyond your ability to heal safely with nothing on hand.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Conduct", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Druidic Code of\n       Conduct\n\n**As created by Apollan in the wake of the Age of Poor Judgement**\n\n-Scribed by Hare Druid Taynuel Viellsinch-\n\nSection A: Druid Conduct\nGeneral Conduct\nA.) Never insult a fellow brother or sister in any way ((OOC or IC)).\n\nB.) Never kill a brother or sister ((for fun, etc)).\n\nC.) Never steal from the order or from a brother or sister.\n\nD.) Don't destroy property of the order.\n\nE.) If you are below the rank of guide, it is not your place to make decisions that affect the order as a whole.\n\nF.) All Druids must conduct themselves in a mature and upright manner ((OOC & IC)).\n\nG.) Never criticize a brother or sister in an overly negative way. ((OOC or IC)).\n\nH.) Calm the heck down!\n\nI.) Always immediately contact a higher ranking Druid if you have a problem.\n\nJ.) Stay active ((on the forum.))\n\nK.) Never criticize someone ((OOC for actions done IC or vise versa.))\n\nL.) A Druid may use violent force if they or other Druids are in danger, it is self defense, or if it is in defense of nature or the balance.\n\nConduct Around Shunned\n\nA.) When a shunned person enters the grove immediately instruct them to leave. If they do not leave instruct them once that you will forcibly remove them if they do not leave. If they still stay a Druid is free\n\nto use any means possible to remove a shunned from the grove and the surrounding area.\n\nB.) When a shunned person with a KOS designation enters the grove immediately instruct them that they will be kill if in the next ten seconds if they do not\n\nleave. When the ten seconds has expired a Druid is free to slain any non-obeying shunned.\n\nC.) When you see a shunned person (even with a KOS designation) somewhere other than a grove, continue on your way and do not try to make contact.\n\nD.) If a shunned tries to make contact with you somewhere outside a grove simply reply with the phase \"Much wind pours from your mouth\" and move on. If they try to speak with you again repeat the phase. If they try a third time inform that if they try\n\nto speak to you again you will reply with violent force.\n\nE.) If a shunned with a KOS designation tries to contact you somewhere other than a grove, inform them that they ten seconds to move away from the present location before violent force is used\n\nF.) If at any time a shunned becomes aggressive or violent, refer to Section A, Subsection 1, Line L of the Druidic Code.\n\nSection B: Initiation and Ranking Up\n\n1.)Outsider to Dedicant\n\nA.) Complete a extensive interview with a Guide.\n\nB.) Recite the Dedicant's Vow.\n\n2.) Dedicant to Druid\n\nA.) Stay a member for at least two Elven weeks. If seen as unready by the guide or higher, shall stay until the guide deems you ready.\n\nB.) Complete a varying amount of Druidic Trials assigned successfully.\n\nC.) Complete a Grand Task.\n\nD.) Choose carefully what totem you will go under. This can only be changed once after becoming official.\n\nE.) Become attuned in the ceremony performed by an Arch Druid.\n\n3.) Druid to Guide\n\nA.) Stay a member for fourElven weeks (being a Dedicant does not count). If seen as unready by an arch or higher, be patient and gain wisdom.\n\nB.) Once deemed as ready, take training underneath the Head Teacher as an\n\napprentice, and watch how they are to Dedicants, how they assign tasks, how they handle the grove, etc.\n\nC.) Study up on the subject, as well as how to perform an attunement ceremony, what the tasks entail, what the interviews for Dedicants are meant\n\nto have, as well as other things your Head Teacher suggests you learn.\n\nD.) Practice giving a few Dedicants tasks under the supervision of your Head Teacher.\n\nE.) Once you and your Head Teacher both feel that you are ready, the teacher\n\nwill discuss with an Arch Druid and you shall begin your guideship.\n\n1.) The punishment for breaking a rule depends on person, severity, the rule broken and previous track record.\n\n2.) The punishment for breaking a rule includes but is not limited to a warning, probation, a shun, a KOS (kill on sight)\n\ndesignation and banishment from Athera ((server ban)).\n\nSection D: Clauses\n\n1.) All rules are subject to change.\n\n2.) Section B, Subsection 1, Line C is optional if the Dedicant does not plan\n\non becoming a Druid.\n\nNote: Some practices are outdated or no longer used. Mainly Section B.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Inner Circle", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Inner Circle\n\nExisting within the structure of the Druidic Order Circle of Malinor, which is the primary and mother Circle of Druids, is an Inner Circle. The Inner Circle is rather different than a Druidic Circle, however. It is part of\n\nan existing Circle, composed of three Archdruids and no set number of Druid Guides who all take responsibility for the inner working of the Order.\n\nThe Druids of the Inner Circle are those who are permitted to guide and attune Dedicants as well as\n\ntrain Druids in the ways of teaching or in harnessing the Gifts of Nature. During a Moot, the Druids of the Inner Circle cast their votes, with only the best interests of the Order in mind, to make a particular decision for the Order.\n\nThe Inner Circle doesn’t dictate the Order, nor does it dictate or manipulate individual Druids. Rather, the Inner Circle has, for countless centuries, only served to maintain order by making larger decisions for the Order and effectively\n\nupbringing new generations of Druids. The Archdruids and Druid Guides of the Inner Circle are crucial to the function and future of a Druidic Order. This ancient and important aspect of Orderly structure should never be forgotten. Blessed be.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Challenge", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Challenge\n\nAn Archdruid cannot be removed from position by a vote or by force. Only if he retires or dies may his position be claimed as empty. However, when an Archdruid becomes exceedingly inactive or loses sight of what's right for the Or\n\nand refuses to retire, his position may be challenged. The Challenge is and only ever should be issued in the most extreme and rare of cases.\n\nWhen all hope is lost and the Order is suffering because of an Archdruid, the Challenge may be instituted a\n\nmay only be instituted by a Druid Hierophant. The Hierophant who issues the Challenge shall create a difficult, and unbiased, trial that requires the challengers and the challenged to put many skills to the test such as exploration, investigation, wisdom,\n\nand piety. Furthermore, the existing Archdruids must all agree to the terms of the Challenge. This event could take anywhere from a few Seeds to a Year, and whoever returns to the Hierophant, first, with the required information, items,\n\nor knowledge will be deemed the victor and shall take the seat of the challenged Archdruid.\n\nIf anyone is realized to have cheated or wronged the Challenge in some way, they will be disqualified. In the case that only two individuals, a challenger\n\nand the challenged, take part in this vigorous trial and one cheats or wrongs it in some way, the other will be granted the victor even if he is the individual being challenged.\n\nAs aforementioned, the Challenge shall only ever occur as a last resort. The Inner Circle, all except for the Archdruid being challenged, must deem it necessary as must a Hierophant to organize and oversee it. This rite should never and cannot be abused.\n\n Those who try to see to its usage in improper context shall feel not only the wrath of the Aspects, but that of the Order as well. Blessed be he who takes any and all proper precautions with a troubled Archdruid before seeing the institution\n\nof a Challenge.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Moot", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Moot\n\nThe Moot is an assembly held for all Druids of a particular Circle to come together for discussion, debate, and decision making. It is a time to worship the Aspects, to make notice of the events of the world, to commit to projects,\n\nand much more. Many times a Moot might serve the sole purpose of voting for a new Archdruid or Druid Guide or to cast votes on shunning a particular individual who has wronged a Druid, Druids, or the Order. Seldom, a Moot might be held to throw a party,\n\nhost a wedding or perform a funeral. \n\nOnly by the word of an Archdruid may a Moot be called. Druids, Dedicants, Druid Guides, and Hierophants all can, and should, present information of importance to the Archdruids, and anyone\n\ncan request a Moot be called. However, it is up to an Archdruid to decide if and when a Moot shall be held and for what topics it shall be held to discuss. Only shall the Inner Circle, the Archdruids and Druid Guides, make a definite decision, based on a\n\nvote count, for the Order. No other Druids are permitted to make decisions for the Order, but their opinions and advice are taken seriously and with much consideration. If the Inner Circle's vote count is split, a Hierophant may cast a vote to make a\n\ndecision, however.\n\nWhen serving as a meeting, a Moot is held in the Moot Room of the hosting Grove. While a Moot is in session, often times the Archdruid who called it will take the floor to present topics. Out of respect, all who wish to speak\n\nshould wait for a pause or until they are asked to speak. Druids should request or stand to take the floor or present a topic. Dedicants aren’t traditionally permitted to attend Moots, but exceptions are most often made. In case that a Dedicant is\n\npermitted at a Moot, he or she shall not speak unless asked to.\n\nThe Moot is an ancient tradition of Druidic culture. Sometimes, non-Druids are even invited to Moots, such as when relations are being discussed between the Order and a Nation or other\n\ngroup. A Moot is versatile, able to serve the purpose for many different occasions. It is one of the most formal, serious, and important aspects of Druidic culture. When in attendance of a Moot, remember to respect the speaker and follow proper etiquette.", ""),
        StaticBook("Absolutism", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Writings of the Elk Druid - Absolutism \n\n*By Elf Druid Danic*\n\n   Absolutism\n\nDruidism. The term that has many forms. It is the basis of the ancient Order known typically as the Druidic Order.\n\nWhile it is what Druids of all kinds follow, their definitions shift, meld, and reform consistently, with no true embodiment. Throughout my countless centuries of following this path, I have faced an innumerable amount of varying opinions and\n\nconfrontations, to which I have used to mold who I am as a Druid and the road I take. While I do not plan on passing from this world to the next any time soon, in these times I feel such a choice will not be given; Therefor I have decided to write this\n\narticle, in hopes it will guide the future of the Aspects Blessed.\n\nSection I - Death is inevitable\n\nWhile I understand such a title is rather bleak or depressing, I feel it is best to understand it first. Throughout the Gardens the Aspects cultivate, two things are certain; Death. Whether it be an elderly goose\n\n passing from this world, or a young cub of a tiger starving to death due to her mother’s ineptitude to care for her children; Death will happen. The latter of said examples is where I feel Druids falter. The Descendants tend to live under the impression\n\nthat they may be able to alter death or prevent it entirely, this belief is naive and futile. They tend to view the world severely short term, allowing their emotions to cloud better judgements in the natural flow of these realms.\n\nAs Druids, it is our duty to uphold the Balance no matter the cost. Returning to my previous example, if a man stumbles upon a small tiger cub not being cared for by her mother, he would take her and care for her, no matter the long term response\n\nto such a decision, solely because he allowed his emotions to cloud his ideas. Now, in place of a man, say a Druid found this cub; What would they do? Many might say ‘Help the cub’ without hesitation, falling upon the same mistake as the previous\n\nDescendant. The minority would say ‘Leave the cub’ and let Nature take its course. This, again, is falling onto the mistake of a clouded mind of zealotry. The, in my personal belief, correct opinion is completely versatile. By inspecting the surrounding\n\nenvironment will hold the answers on why the cub is starving. Is it because of the chaotic and near moronic nature of the Descendants tipping the scale on the Balance, causing the cub to starve? Or is it simply Nature’s continuous battle to remain\n\nBalanced with its multitude of methods to do so, and the result of which is the cub must die? Depending on what the Druid finds will decide how they will act. Unfortunately, many times the end result will be an emotionally difficult one, but do not\n\ndespair; This is the call of the Druid, this is the burden you must hold for the Aspects, and in the end you will be rewarded.\n\nTo summarize; Death is inevitable, and you must accept it as a result of keeping the Balance, less you let emotions cloud\n\nyour duty. Never take a situation at face value, for the truth is typically just below the surface.\n\nSection II - Life is Unending\n\n\nA much less depressing title, but a continuation of the previous section. Within the Gardens, two things are certain; Life. Death is inevitable, and with it, Life is unending. Such is the Balance.\n\n For every natural death, new life spring forth from it. The death of a starving predator allows the dwindled prey population to rejuvenate, and it’s corpse food for scavengers, and bones for new plants and fertile ground. A Druid, while mourning,\n\nshould see natural death as a celebration, a sign of the Balance at work, the great Circle coming circle. It is all in the Aspects perfect system for Life and Death to coexist in such unity. As a Druid when serving your duties, the time will come where\n\nUnnatural death, however, is an abhorrent sight to all Nature. The very idea should be considered the greatest sin in the eyes of all Druids. To cause an unnatural death is the deliberate attempt of disrupting the Balance. While Nature has mechanisms\n\nto deal with such an event, and in the end most if not all will simply be recycled again, it still should be avoided at all costs. Examples of unnatural deaths are the slaughter of life for no apparent reason, the deliberate torture of wildlife\n\n(i.e. unwarranted starvation) when such actions are not needed and only serve to unbalance, and possibly the worst of all, the murder of another Druid. Killing another Druid is to destroy one of the mightiest tools crafted by the Blessed Aspects,\n\nand upon doing so, the one who committed such act must be dealt with swiftly.\n\nTo summarize; Life is Unending, death must be celebrated as rebirth and new existence, and unnatural death is abhorrent to all that is a Druid.\n\nSection III - Nature is Neutral\n\n\n\n“Nature is nurturing”. “Nature is harsh”. “Nature is loving”. “Nature is violent”; All of these are different ways people may describe Nature, and in my humblest opinion,\n\nall of these are misunderstanding, delusional, and ignorant perceptions of Nature itself. The Wild cares not if a cub dies of natural means; it cares not if a garden blooms, only to be devoured by a cow. Nature is Neutral. So long as all of it is by\n\nnatural means, it is balanced and all is right. The Druid should always follow in suit of the Nature’s example, never straying due to their own moral beliefs or faulty lines of thought. The Druid should not strive for a lush and forested land as far as\n\nthe eye can see, as s/he should not strive for a desolate landscape of sands and dunes. The Druid should never punish the hunters, and nurture the prey, nor should s/he hunt to her heart's content and neglect the meek. Nature is Neutral, and thus we must\n\nbe neutral. So long as it is in harmony and perfect Balance as designed, all is right and the Druid has and is continuing to do their duty to the Aspects.\n\nTo summarize; Nature is not a delusional idea of any emotion or benevolence, it is neutrality,\n\nDruids must follow in example of said neutrality, and must cast aside personal beliefs or desires to assure they continue their duty of the Aspects to maintain the natural Balance.\n\nEpilogue\n\n\nWhile these guidelines may be useful, and even invaluable to some, do understand that these are my own discoveries and realizations. In no way are they infallible, or required for you to follow. You will form your own concept\n\non what Druidism is throughout your journey, I only wish to share with you my own. To perhaps open your eyes to view you didn’t consider, or ones that may conflict with your own that causes you to not only reconsider, but grow as a Druid\n\nwith your belief and faith more clearly defined. Whatever the outcome, I wish you goodluck and that may the Aspects bless your path.", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro to Aspects", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Intro to the Aspects:\n   Atheran Edition\n\n-Sunshine Druid Verden\n\n\nThe Druidic Aspects are an ancient and powerful duo of gods whose names are synonymous with Nature itself. Each of the Aspects\n\nmaintain their respective domain over the natural world and cooperate to establish true balance, a feat which has been undermined by the efforts of mages and the greedy (Further detailed in the book, ‘The Great War’). Because of this, the Aspects have\n\nblessed their divine will upon individuals within the mortal realm, dubbing those chosen as ‘Druids’. The purpose of these individuals was clear: eradicate the unnatural, allow life to prosper, and defend nature against unordained destuction.\n\nWith this creed in mind, the Druids have propelled themselves into the modern era under the careful supervision of our benefactors. But, who are the Aspects?\n\nRead their books to know more.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid Origins", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Druid Origins\n\nBy: Apple Druid Leyun\n\nThe origin of Druids has been long obscured by time and myth. The current iteration of the Druidic Order began with Respiren in Aegis. He was not the first druid, and this was not the first Druid\n\nOrder, but it is where our accounts of druid history begin. \n\nTo peer further back is difficult, but there does exist a couple different accounts of the origin of Druids. These myths offer our only glimpse at what may have been.\n\nThe first account is one that has been taught throughout the existence of the current Order. The Myth of the Great War tells the story of how the first druid came to be, though it is interesting to note that we have two versions of this account with\n\ndiffering details between them. The account labeled \"Version A\" in the Druid Library is a copy we've been able to verify having come from Aegis from a time pre-dream. It's interesting to note that this version speaks of a Goddess of Nature,\n\nfurther supporting the the dating of this version being pre-dream.\n\nThe other version we have, labeled \"Version B\" in the Druid Library, clearly is a much newer copy. This particular copy is known to have been transcribed by Spirit Druid\n\nSaviticus in Anthos. Whether he made the edits this version shows or copied these edits from an older account is unknown, though it is important to note that while this version speaks of the Aspects, a single Goddess reference slips through, showing it to\n\nhave been edited from the original version.\n\nFinally, we have a completely different account than the Myth of the Great War. We have some of the Writings of the Wandering Wizard, which state simply that Malin taught his children to commune with the\n\nAspects and called them druids. It is an odd claim, given that the existence of the Aspects had been unknown until the dream occurred in Aegis. It is possible that knowledge of the Aspects was lost before Respiren formed the current Order, but there is no\n\nevidence one way or the other aside from this single account. \n\nAs for who the first druid was, both accounts of the Great War make one thing clear, she was a woman. The older account states that she was human, while both the newer\n\naccount states she was elven and the writings of the wandering wizard state that druidism was first taught by Malin to his children. The claim that Malin taught his children druidism would suggest he was the first druid, which contradicts the account of\n\nthe Great War stating it was a woman. \n\nThere are no historical accounts of the Great War aside from the myth itself, though writings that old are hard to come by, and it is possible such an event has been lost to history.\n\nAssuming the Great War occurred early in the history of the world, it is possible that the first druid could have been a first generation daughter of Malin, but this is just speculation.\n\nIn the end, the truth is lost to myth, and without the\n\ndiscovery of writings from those times, it is likely to remain that way.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nemiisae", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Nemiisae\n\nThird and Final book in Intro to the Aspects\n\nby Sunshine Druid Verden\n\n**A poorly sketched woman of otherworldly beauty wrapped in shadows with spiders around her is on the first page.**\n\n        Nemiisae\n\nThe Shadow of Death\nGrandmother Spider\nWoman of The Moon\n\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Nature’s Wounding\n\nGoverning Season: Autumn\n\nManuscripts of ages long since passed describe a woman of forbidden beauty and inescapable horrors. The ancient and forgotten race of Mori’quessir once worshipped such a goddess in their caves of impenetrable darkness, where those who spoke of light were\n\nsacrificed in rituals of blood and web. She is the matron of the night and dark desires, and it is she who spins the great web of destiny. Little is known for sure of Grandmother Spider, and indeed it is difficult to speak of her at all without reference\n\nto Cerridwen and Cernunnos.\n\nWhere Cerridwen breathes life and motherhood, Nemiisae demands death and servitude. She exists as a dark mirror of all that Cerridwen stands for, and perverts her creations of happiness and love into monsters of\n\nhorror and passion. Those that find themselves abandoned by the light find their way into her domain one way or another, where they emerge changed or do not emerge at all. Though it may seem contrary in this light, Nemiisae is not an evil aspect.\n\nCerridwen provides the life, and Nemiisae evokes the evolution of that life into something new but sustaining. For many species of plant and animal, Nemiisae has been their salvation.\n\nCernunnos teaches strength, honor, and discipline in the same way that Nemiisae teaches cunning, fear, and impulse. The Woman of The Moon teaches us that these abstract concepts are all well and good, but we are better off finding our own light rather\n\n than basking in the light of others. Scavengers, luminescent creatures, and spiders are examples of guidance toward her ideals of self-enlightenment.\n\n    Rituals and Beliefs\n\n*Once knowledge of her presence was made public, several Nemiisaen witch covens began sprouting up. Currently very little is known about these reclusive groups, which is fitting considering she is the Aspect of secrets.\n\n*Giant Glowshrooms and other luminescent creatures are said to have been guided by Nemiisae for millennia, and successfully attained ‘their own light’.\n\n*The moon is often used as her sigil, and many believe it to directly confer Nemiisae’s will upon the realm.\n\n*Creatures blessed with condition of Menalinsim are said to have been touched by Nemiisae, predisposing them as dominant and frightening.", ""),
        StaticBook("BalanceOfNature", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Balance\n        Of Nature\n\n   By: Apple Druid\n    Leyun Kameki\n\nIt is on the sad occasion of the passing of Solstice Druid Callax that I look back on the things he has taught me throughout my time in the Order.\n\nI think that one of the best lessons that can be written down is one from my dedicancy.\n\nThis took place back in Anthos during some problems we had with the High Elves of Haelun’or. This was back during the waning days of Malinor, prior to the\n\n abandonment of Luminaire. Haelun’or had cut down some trees near it’s walls and many druids were upset to the point where a large number were grown outside of Haelun’or’s gate which later got cut back down again.\n\nCallax, unlike most other druids, wasn’t upset. He explained that in cutting down the trees, they had allowed a beautiful flower filled field spring up. Critters that typically didn’t live in the heavily forested region of Malinor were\n\nable to flourish there. The loss of a few trees and increased the diversity of life in the area. He said he’d gladly go out and turn a patch of forest into a swamp and would encourage other druids to do similar acts.\n\nThe point of this lesson is one that I feel many druids tend to forget. As druids, we serve all of nature, not just the trees. The grasses and flowers and shrubs and bushes and fields and mountains and deserts and everything that lives in all of them\n\nand more are all part of nature. Even people are part of nature. Our duty is to all of that, and not limited to any one small part. An act of genocide will disrupt the balance as surely as cutting down an entire forest. The removal of just a couple trees\n\nmay also have little effect or even beneficial effect.\n\nThe balance of nature can be a subjective thing to work with, and in the end, it is up to the druid to decide what is appropriate action, and what needs no action at all.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid Heal Tips", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Fundamental Lesson in Effective Druidic Healing\n\n*Sequoia Druid Lilith Winterleaf*\n\nMy name is Lillith, the Sequoia Druid, and I was taught this lesson by my Guide back on Asulon, Jena, and it seems to have been lost somewhere\n\nalong the way, so I will give this knowledge back to the Order, as is my duty. It is an effective technique that takes very little strain on the druid themselves as you only augment what is already present instead of drawing from your own essence.\n\nThis allows it to be repeated almost indefinitely without the need for resting frequently to restore oneself.\n\nIt is a simple bandage and medicine of a sort, but only in the hands of an attuned Druid is it potent enough to be an effective\n\nmethod of healing, and one does not need to be a healing-focused Druid to perform this task.\nAll you need is bonemeal and fresh lily root, and then a large leaf, vine, or lilypad. I personally have found lilypads to be most receptive and effective,\n\nbut I have used all three successfully. You must grind up the bone meal and lily root into a fine paste, add water as needed to reach a consistency of a thick paste. If using a vine as the bandage, you need to butterfly cut it down one side and fold it\n\nopen so it is large enough to serve as a bandage.\n\nApply the paste to one side of your chosen bandage and place it over the wound. You can use multiple such bandages for larger wounds, but keep them all the same plant or you will need to augment each\n\neach separately in the next step.\n\nOnce the wound is covered, place your hand upon the center of the bandage and focus on the mixture beneath, asking the nutrients and life within to mend flesh and bone. You should feel when the mixture responds,\n\nit will grow lukewarm. You then focus upon the bandage plant, asking it to aid in the mending and remain on the flesh until the healing is done. The plant should glow a faint green for a moment and then adhere to their skin, and you should be\n\nalmost unable to distinguish where the edges of the plant end and the flesh begins, they should be quite literally fused.\n\nOnce this is done, there is nothing more to do but wait for the wound to heal, this usually takes an hour or two for most\n\nwounds. If there is significant muscle damage it can sometimes take a day or two. Once the wound is healed the bandage should release itself and fall away, it is perfectly normal for the skin beneath to take on a greenish hue for a few days until a few\n\nlayers of skin have been shed.\n\nYou should be able to perform this task many times without tiring, as it requires very little from the Druid, the energy needed is drawn from the living essence of the mixture and bandage.\n\nIf you put more energy and focus into it, the speed of healing is greatly increased but this will begin to draw from the Druid and will tax you after only one to three uses, depending on the skill and fortitude of the Druid.\n\nOther substances can be used for the mixture, I have experimented with many substances and have found bonemeal and lilypad roots to be among the most potent and most readily available ingredients, and I usually use lilypads as the bandage as\n\nthe pad is left over from harvesting the roots anyway.\n\nI highly recommend having bonemeal in your mixture if possible, I have found every mixture without it to be far lacking in potency, sometimes with no healing effect at all.\n\nAlways include bonemeal when possible.\n\nOther viable substitutes to the lily root include:\n-wheat (fresher is better, over an elven week after harvest seems to reduce its effectiveness.)\n-Tippen's Root\n\n-Halfling's Grass\n-Blaze Powder (makes the mixture sting to the touch, painful to the recipient, use sparingly)\n-Cactus Green (makes the mixture odorous, strongly so, but is fairly potent.)\n\nThe only viable replacement for bonemeal that I have found to be just as effective in mixtures lacking bonemeal has been living slime, usually found in swamps. This can also be mixed with bonemeal for the most potent mixture I have found, usually halving\n\npreviously stated healing times. However, slime is rarely a readily available ingredient and so should only be used for the most dire of cases if available.\n\nThis is by no means a comprehensive list, other substances can be used and I have\n\ntried others, but they generally were not strong enough to make suitable replacements. All flowers were nearly pointless to attempt.\n\nI hope this helps many of my brothers and sisters in the Order.\n\n It is a fairly simple task and one I feel is a vital base for any aspiring healer, as I know from personal experience how taxing Druidic Healing can be and how much it can take from you if you are not careful.\n\n~The Sequoia Druid, Lillith Winterleaf", ""),
        StaticBook("Circle Splits", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Circle Splits\n   By Elf Druid Danic\n\nWhen one envisions the Druidic Order, they typically think of the singular group that has existed for centuries, united together. Although this thought is perfectly reasonable, it is flawed. In truth,\n\nthe Druids are not always united within a single Order. Indeed, in times of great stress, disagreement, or otherworldly situations, the Order will splinter into fragments, Druids turning their backs and going separate ways from one and another. These are\n\n known as ‘Circle Splits’, and their occurrences have been the cause of some of the Orders greatest changes throughout its existence.\n\nTo understand a Circle Split, one must understand a surprisingly volatile concept; Druid\n\npolitics. Yes, like all organized groups, the Druidic Order has its own form of political platforms, not too different from any other religious group. Such politics can become heated, creating fissures and cracks within the integrity of the Order.\n\nEach Druid has their own ideals, beliefs, and creeds that they follow, albeit all sharing the same underlying tenets. From this, disagreements on how the Order is built and structured arise, creating tensions between Druids. To worsen this, a Druid's\n\npreviously mentioned ideals are typically adopted from their teacher, and in turn creating many distinct groups of druids that disagree with each other on numerous topics. For the most part, Druids are able to work out their conflicts, but under highly\n\ntensioned and strained situations, some Druids believe the only course of action is to splinter off from the rest. Thus, a new circle, sovereign from the authority of any other, is created.\n\nIn reality, practically anyone could claim they are ‘splitting off’ in a new circle. The key difference between such, and an actual Circle Split, is recognition. Recognition is granted by the Mother Circle (The original Order) to the newly formed Circle,\n\nsignifying that they recognize their separation from the rest of the Order and their sovereignty from its hierarchy, structure, and rules. Such recognition is usually given when the splintering Circle shows their ability to function as an independent\n\nfigure, such as including Druids capable of attunement, Druids with previous experience in teaching dedicants, and numerous Druids who are notably skilled and can teach gifts one is unable to learn on their own.\n\nWhile typically there is no formal event for recognition, each circle indirectly recognizes each others right to exist by naming a Grand Druid. The Grand Druid is a neutral individual tasked to maintain diplomacy between Circles, with a hopeful end goal\n\nof reuniting the circles. The Grand Druid is chosen when both Circles agree on an individual, typically someone who has no involvement with either side in hopes that s/he will remain neutral for both Circles. Whenever there is something that needs to be\n\ndiscussed, debated, or decided, the Grand Druid is there to mediate any meeting to assure that the best decision for both sides is reached, and to extinguish any heated emotions from either circle.\n\nCircle Splits can be a time of turbulence and fragility of the Order itself. Many Druids harbor spiteful, angry emotions towards either those they split off from or those who split. They feel as if the event is stupid or pointless, and should never\n\nhappen. These Druids are guided purely by their emotions, and must remove themselves from their hazy cloud and look at the event from a clear perspective. A split does not warrant bitterness, but change. That change must happen for the good of the Order,\n\nand change comes from compromise. To achieve this strong armed compromise, all Druids must open their minds to alternative view they had refused to consider previously. Once the split is resolved, all would hopefully come out of it with a stronger, more\n\nunderstanding, connection to his/her brothers and sisters and a better grasp on how to handle it in the future.\n\nIn the end, no matter how one might spin it, Circle Splits are complex, and difficult times for the Order. They herald change, possibly unwelcome change, but change nonetheless. It’s easy to see how one could have prevented it after the fact, but once\n\nthey occur, the Druid must accept such an event, and look to the future to assure a better, more connected, changed, and united Druidic Order.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aspect Studies", "§bTever Softfoot", "I was walking along the river near my home when I spotted a very nice spot to sit and take a nap. It was quiet, sunny and warm.\nI lay down and closed my eys. I felt the sun warming me. Felt the wind blowing and the life all around.\n\nI began to meditate. I felt a peace that I hadn't felt before. I eventually fell asleep. I dreamed I was bathed in light. The air tasted sweet. There was music in the air that sounded like an orchestra of nature. When I awoke I was reminded of my\n\nteachings. Of Cerridwen and the light and life she brings. I looked up into the tree, upon rising from my resting place, and saw a honey comb dripping with beautiful golden honey. Like it was a gift from Cerridwen meant for me.\n\nThe honey represents\n\nCerridwen because not only is it golden, it is life sustaining, warm, sweet,and the labor of creatures of life. Nothing would be living without Cerridwens blessing of bees to pollinate things.\n\nAs a lumberjack I find myself in search of exotic woods deep in mysterious places. I heard of a source of dark oak deep within the forests of the Mali. As I traveled through the ancient forest I felt the presence of the trees watching me.\n\nIt was a welcoming feeling however. I made my way to the location of the trees and made camp. Later that night a terrible storm came upon me. My camp was blown away and I lost my way. Three days I wandered the forest with no hope.\n\nI was hungry and cold. Hope had fled from me. I finally fell to my knees and yelled defiantly at anything that could hear me. \"I will no' die tis way!\" I thought of my love. Of the future that would never be.\n\nAs I kneeled there in the damp leaves, the quiet of the forest settled around me. The stillness was tangible. There was not a sound to be heard. As I stood up and looked around I saw a pure white deer looking at me. As I slowly backed away I tripped over\n\nwhat I thought was a fallen branch. On closer examination the branch turned out to be a moss covered bow. The string was made of vines. There were also a few moss covered arrows wrapped in a sheet of old leather.\n\nAs I picked up the bow the deer bolted away through the underbrush. Being on the verge of starving, I shrugged off the despair. This was my only chance to survive. I needed food. I grabbed the Bow and arrows and started after the deer.\n\nI tracked the deer through valleys, over hills, through fields and forests for what seemed like an eternity. As i crested a small hill I came upon the deer once more. The wind was blowing torwards me. The grass was soft and silent.\n\nIt was the perfect chance and my only one. I had not the strength to track this quarry any longer. I slowly nocked an arrow and raised the bow. I thought the mossy wood would creak and give as I pulled it, but it remained silent. I held my breath.\n\nThe only sounds were the wind and the fast beating of my heart. The thrill of the hunt running through my veins. The arrow flies true and hits it's mark. A clean kill. I went to the deer and sat by his side.\n\nCrying at my salvation I began to dress the deer.I built a fire and ate my fill all the while praising my luck. As I ate I was reminded of my teachings. Of Cernunnos the great huntsman. I believe the deer and the bow where gifts from him.\n\nI sat near the fire and began to meditate on what I had learned and thanks the Horned God for his generosity and for saving my life. \nThis moss covered bow reperesents Cernunnos. It is the tool of the true hunter.\n\nOf his dedication and prowess. Of the thrill of the chase and of the taking of your prey. And of the thankfulness for a life given to save another life.\n\nI was in the far north. Conscripted to a group of explorers as repayment of a debt. After trudging the snow for several days we came upon twin mountains. Very unusual looking with they were. We decided to make camp at the base of the second with plans to\n\nexplore them the next day.\nAfter a hot meal and a few tunes from a pipe we turned in for the night. As I lay there I heard someone crying out past the ring of light made by the campfire. I rose from my bed and walked the the edge of the camp.\n\nI could make out the shape of what looked like a small child. I paused and looked back at the others still sleeping. They would be fine, and so would I as long as I kept the fire in sight.\n\nAs I stepped out into the cold dark of the night, four other figures appeared. I turned to run back into the camp when a numbing coldness came over me. I couldn't move. Last thing I saw was the ground coming at me, then darkness...\n\nWhen I awoke I was in a lightless place bound in iron. I could hear muffled voices nearby. The ground was hard and cold. Luckily they did not take my jacket or any of my things. I fished a lockpick from my boot and began to fumble in the dark to unlock\n\nthe shackels around my feet. It was very difficult to pick the locks without making a sound and alerting my captors. My previous title as Borrower helped in the task of breaking free. I finally freed my feet and began trying to free my arms.\n\nLuckily for me being a halfling meant that the chains were far to big for my small arms and with a little effort I slipped my hands right through them.\nI crept towards the voices, grasping in the dark so as not to bump into anything.\n\nThe voices grew louder and louder as I crept in the dark, until finally I could understand what they were saying. It was three females talking about what to do with me. It seemed like the consensus was to toss me down a deep hole.\n\nI began to panic and flail around in the dark for an escape but only ended up tripping and banging my head. Bright light filled my vision as I was lifted from the ground. I was carried for some distance and then wind. Nothing but darkness and wind.\n\nI fell for ages it seemed then splash. I fell into an underground lake. The darkness was so thick I could not see my hands in front of my face. I picked a direction and began to swim. Eventually I found what seemed like a bank.\n\nI was so afraid. What was I going to do? I was miles underground with no light, no food, and no hope. I sat and cried for a while. Such was my despair that I decided that if I was going to die that it would be my way,\n\nNot eaten by some unknown creature of the dark. I rose from the place I was sitting and began to wander in the dark, tripping and fumbling as I went. As I was scrambling through the dark I tripped over a loose stone and fell down a hill.\n\nI rolled and rolled and fell off of a small cliff, breaking my leg as I hit the bottom. I screamed out in pain as I pulled my leg out from under me, bone protruding from the skin.\nAll I could do was lay there in despair.\n\nAs i sat there writhing in pain my heart sunk. I was not alone here in the dark. I heard small pebbles shifting in the dark. Something grabbed my broken leg and began to drag me.\n\nPain shot through my body as the creature sank it's teeth sank into my body. \n\nI sat up from my bedroll with a scream. The pain still wracking my body as I came to terms with what had happened. Everyone looked at me in bewilderment,\n\nwondering what was going on. As I sat there the pain faded and the realization that it was just a dream dawned on me. The horror and helplessness I felt was still there.\n\nI shudder even now as I write this. The feeling of that dream will haunt me to the end of my days. I think that dream was from Niimasae. Showing me that I I should find my own light, that, like in the caverns I am helpless without it.\n\nThe object I think represents Niimasae is the enchanted stone I had made so that I would never be without my own source of light. I will not be left alone in the darkness again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Herbs 2", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Druid's Book Of\n         Herbs\n       Voldume 2\n\n**Herbs from Soltice Druid Callax*\n\n-Scribed with additions by Apple Druid Leyun.-\n\n*Malin's Heart\nA white flower that grows in plains and forests.\n\nEffects: Chewing numbers the whole body for a few minutes.\n\nOverdose: Prolonged numbness.\n\nNotes: A bit harder to find than blissfoil.\n\n*Mandragora\nA parsnip shaped herb that grows in swamps and wetlands.\n\nEffects: Revitalization and regenerative properties make the plants valuable, but the spores it release in season cause hallucinations.\n\nOverdose: Can cause growths. The hallucinogenic spores often cause choking and can be fatal.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate water symbol, often used in healing potions.\n\nNotes: Harvest 2 weeks after maturity to avoid spores.\n\n*Night Sap\nA gold sap that drips from deformed oak trees.\n\nEffects: Causes a sense of calm, safety, and happiness, along with tiredness. It usually makes for about 5 hours of sleep. After waking, though, it brings splitting headaches\n\nand paralysis for as much time as the sleep.\n\nOverdose: Longer lasting effects.\n\nNotes: Highly addictive.\n\n*Saffvil\nA sickly green grass that grows in swamps near dead things.\n\nEffects: Nausea and blood clotting.\n\nOverdose: Death.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate fire symbol.\n\n*Sagedust\nPollen from the Alabaster plant.\n\nEffects: Increased metabolism and speed, along with heightened senses and a rapid heart rate.\n\nOverdose: Possible heart attack.\n\nNotes: Incredibly difficult to harvest.\n\n*Serpent's Stalk\nA long, reed like emerald stalk that grows in forests near or in water.\n\nEffects: Cooling effect when applied with water, may cause rashes.\n\nOverdose: Mild hypothermia\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate water symbol. Typically used in antidotes, calming, and healing potions.\n\nNotes: Poisonous to bugs.\n\n*Shade Leaf\nA short, small plant with plain leaves, yellow flowers, and silvery veins that are commonly found anywhere this is grass and shade.\n\nEffects: Calming, stress relieving, bloodshot eyes, increased appetite.\n\nOverdose: Hallucinations\n\nNotes: Grows in large quantities, similar to normal weeds. Makes a popping noise when exposed to rain.\n\n*Swamp Blossom\nA purple green flower with a short, thick stem, found in the branches of swamp trees.\n\nEffects: Nectar is like honey, and the flower is nutritious, though consuming raw can gum up the throat.\n\nOverdose: Smelly breath and diarrhea.\n\n*Tippen's Root\nPurple flowers with a dark yellow speckled root that grow near fallen trees in dense forests.\n\nEffects: Stops bleeding almost instantly, but stinks to the seven hells.\n\nOverdose: Blood clots, and the dangers of blood clots.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate earth symbol, commonly used in strength and healing potions.\n\nNotes: Really seriously stinks. Really. Make sure you're prepared for the stink.\n\n*Yarrow\nA white to pink flowered plant that grows in grasslands and forests.\n\nEffects: Can reduce inflammation and blood pressure, as well as intensify the medicinal effects of other herbs.\n\nOverdose: Dizziness and fainting due to low blood pressure.\n\n*Zawabate\nA plant with a tall stalk and yellow-green curled petals that grows in a desert oasis in water.\n\nEffects: Out of body experiences, hallucinations, pain and numbing.\n\nOverdose: Can destroy a person's mind.\n\nAlchemical Properties: Moderate water symbol, used in numbing and healing potions.\n\nNotes: Nectar is the medicinal part of the plant.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sacred Stones", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Sacred Stones\n\nThe Sacred Stones of the Aspects are powerful Artifacts associated with the Druids. There is believed to be three, one for each of the figurehead Aspects and a third that binds them together. They are large, rounded\n\nsquare-ish boulders that each are a different colour; Blue for Cerridwen, Silver for Cernunnos, and black for the binder. They are very heavy, but one person could transport one stone on his own. The three together however have known to require at least\n\nthree people to carry a chest with them in it. And once the Stones are close enough they magnetize, though this effect is only present when all three are together it seems.\n\nThe stones emit a powerful aura of Druidic Energy. Together the three stones can prevent corruption of any kind from breaching the perimeter of the grove they are in. Indeed, just one has enough power to stop corruption for up to\n\ntwenty feet, but together their power is much larger. The stones have been known to give visions to those who touch them, and help aid Druids on paths to enlightenment through their pulsating auras.\n\nSymbolically the stones represent the completion of the Order. Each one represents an Aspect, and are thought to have been molded by the Aspects themselves. To possess something touched by the Aspects is beyond blessed to the Druids.\n\nEven when broken down the stones seem to emit light Druidic energy even centuries after their crumbling. It is rumored the the Order remains in possession of the Stone Dust that was once the Great Aegis Stones.", ""),
        StaticBook("NemiisaeCritique", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "A Critique of Nemiisae\nOr: The Charade of the Aspectual “Trinity”\n\n\nIntroduction:\n\nThe Druids are known for many things; Our affinity for nature, our incredibly powers of healing, but above all we are known for our reverence of\n\nbeings known as the Aspects. Until recently, the main sect of such worship has included only two pillars, The Mother Cerridwen, and the Father Cernunnos. Together, their realm’s interactions create the ebbs of Nature and the persistence of the Balance.\n\nBut this duo had recently gained an apparent third. A being known as Nemiisae. Her existence was revealed to us during the turbulent times of Athera and, while modern times her worship is not as widespread as the original two, she has settled comfortably\n\ninto the personal worship of many Druids.\n\n“The Shadow of Death, Grandmother Spider, Woman of the Moon”. Nemiisae is believed to be the patron of those that prefer to dwell in secret and shadow. Brother Sunshine writes of her in\n\n“Intro to the Aspects: Atheran Edition”, where he describes her as a being that “Demands death and servitude”, that she “perverts her [Cerridwen] creations of happiness and love into monsters of horror and passion”. Despite these rather alarming\n\ndescription, Brother Sunshine goes onto claim “Nemiisae is not an evil aspect”, and I would agree. Nemiisae is not an evil Aspect, and in reality is not an Aspect at all. In this essay, I will begin the much needed dissection of the assertion that this\n\n‘Nemiisae’ could possibly be a third Aspect, touching on topics such as the nature of her reveal, and her realm and how it is incompatible, possibly dangerous, to the real Aspects and Druidism as a whole. To begin, let us investigate the former point.", ""),
        StaticBook("Wolf Druid Tome", "§bYelfir Everna", "Tome of the Wolf Druid\n\nBy; Wolf Druid Ouity \n\nI- Power \nThe soul attracts that which it secretly harbors-that which it loves, thus circumstances do not make the Druid; they only reveal him to himself. It means that blessedness,\n\nand not wealth, is the measure of right thought; misery, not poverty or lack of family is the measure of wrong thought. A Druid will find that as he alters his thoughts towards the Nature and the Aspects, Nature and Aspects\nwill alter towards him.\n\n--you will always draw near toward that which you, secretly, most love. The Races surge with uncontrolled passion, are tumultuous with ungoverned grief,  and are blown about by anxiety, doubt, and the wars and wills of Aenguls and Daemons.\nonly the wis\n\n--Druid, only he whose thoughts are controlled and purified, can make the winds and storms of the soul, and indeed the world, obey him. The greatest achievement was at first, and for a time only a dream. Just as the oak sleeps in the acorn, and the bird--\n\n--waits in the egg, so dreams are the seedlings of realities.\n Beware therefore of what you dream of. For some dreams are given by the Aspects to inspire us by what may yet be. Others are planted within us by others, foul seeds that we harvest to our -\n\n--destruction.\n\nII- Pride\nThere is but one way to truly gain mastery over nature, and that is to realize you cannot truly master it at all. It masters you. When one attempts to force it, compel it, command it, or otherwise exercise dominion --\n\n-- over it-the power flees like a timid bird. This is because the Aspects know our innermost thoughts. They know how we intend to use Nature’s Gift. Man may deceive other men. But one simply does not deceive the Aspects. If their will sought, they will --\n\n--come. If we emulate the principals by which is thrives, they flourish in us. Pride is poison to them. In reality, there is perhaps not one of our natural passions so hard to subdue as our pride. Disguise it  struggle with it, beat it down, stifle it, --\n\n--mortify it as much as you please.  It is still alive, and will every now and then peep out and show itself. You will see it perhaps, even with in the most sacred grottos of the realm. For even I, the Arch Druid, could conceive that I had completely\n\novercome it, I should probably be proud of my humility. A desire to be observed, considered, esteemed, praised, beloved, and admired by his fellows his one of the earliest, as well as the keenest dispositions  discovered in the hearts of the Races.\n\n My advice to new Dedicants is to squelch it all their days, for those desires lead to ruin.\n\nIII- Doubt\nMany Dedicants arrive at the Groves with passion already instilled in them. They will yearn to learn, they carry drive,\n\n and zest, and youth, and curiosity. When they enter our great tree, they have nothing, and leave with everything. However, a few lose what they have. The rigorous trials of Dedicancy \nbegin to take their toll on them,  or they submit their thoughts\n\nto the subtle poison of doubt. Not even an Arch Druid’s power can cure it, for these students do harm to themselves. The mind, like the body, can be moved from sunshine into shade. They submit themselves to the slews of myriad ways of Aenguls, Daemons,\n\nand the trickery of man, and they yield before this great power to sway one’s faith in the Aspects’ might. hose of you who would stay and train inasmuch as you strip yourself from jealousies and fears, and humble yourself before the Aspects,\n\nor you are not sufficiently humble, the veil over your eyes shall be torn and you will see. See all their glory and power, and know you are beloved. A servant of the earth, and wild. Having conceived of their attunement, a Druid should mentally mark out--\n\n--a straight pathway to service, looking neither to the right nor left. Doubts and fears should be zealously starved. They are disintegrating elements which break up the straight path, rendering it crooked, ineffectual, and useless.\n\nThoughts of doubt and fear can never accomplish anything. They always lead to failure. Purpose, energy, power to do, and all strong thoughts cease whenever doubt and fear creep in. The will to do springs from the knowledge that we can do.\n\nHe who has conquered doubt and fear has conquered failure in all his endeavors, both as a Druid, and a person.\n\nIV- Blight\nIn every era there comes a moment when the collective thoughts, whims,  and motivations of arcane\ndelvers--\n\n--become so self-absorbed, so malignant, so unheeding that nature itself revolts. Man scars the land such that it finals rebels against him. As thoughts can spread despair and death like seedlings of weeds strewn by the wind, so they must eventually --\n\n--draw the Druids to pluck them out. The vetches must be pulled, roots and all. When this happens, the Aspects cease to bless, and instead they curse. Instead of healing, they spew poison. It happens so swiftly and terribly. The ancients of the --\n\n Wayward’s time gave it a name, this culling process that blackens the world. They named it after a wasting disease that occurs in once-healthy groves of trees. They called it the Blight, and the first of these, the Great War.\n\nI put this quill to paper here now so that others do not make the mistakes I did in my youth. Blinded by war and hate, my transgressions were many, and hard to earn penance for. Though I served the Aspects diligently for many lifetimes. I fear in my --\n\nheart, I have failed them. May the Aspects look kindly on me in the afterlife-\nXal ussta stath xunde mir dosst elamshinae, Nemmisae", ""),
        StaticBook("Runes of Old", "§bKefi", "Aqua Affinity:\n+--\n++-\n+++\n-++\n\nBane of Arthropods:\n?\n\nBlast Protection:\n-----++\n-++++--\n++++++-\n-++++--\n\nDepth Strider:\n?\n\nEfficiency:\n+--\n-++\n+--\n\nOR:\n--+\n++-\n--+\n\nFeather Falling:\n?\n\nFire Aspect:\n?\n\nFire Protection:\n?\n\nFlame:\n?\n\nFortune:\n++------\n--+---+-\n-+-+-+++\n+--+--+-\n\nFrost Walker:\n?\n\nInfinity:\n-+++-+++-\n+---+---+\n+---+---+\n-+++-+++-\n\nKnockback:\n++---\n+++++\n+++++\n++---\n\nLooting:\n-+-----+-\n+++++-+++\n-+-----+-\n\nLuck of the Sea:\n-++-+\n+--+-\n-++-+\n\nLure:\n+-\n+-\n++\n\nMending:\n?\n\nPower:\n++----+--\n--++++++-\n--+++++++\n++----+--\n\nProjectile Protection:\n+--+--+++\n-++++-+++\n+--+--+++\n-------+-\n\nProtection:\n+++-+++\n+++++++\n-++-++-\n--+++--\n\nPunch:\n-+++\n++++\n++++\n-+++\n\nRespiration:\n?\n\nSharpness:\n--+------\n++++++++-\n+++++++++\n--+------\n\nSilk Touch:\n+++++++++\n\nSmite:\n-++-\n++++\n++++\n-++-\n\nSweeping Edge:\n?\n\nThorns:\n+-+-+-+-+\n+-+-+-+-+\n+-+-+-+-+\n+-+-+-+-+\n\nUnbreaking:\n+-+\n-+-\n+-+\n\nOR:\n+-+-+-+\n-+---+-\n+-+-+-+", ""),
        StaticBook("Herbology", "§bVlad Shade", "Alchemy Herbology\n\nTippens root\nA alone weak herb but it is used for healing purposes and can stop bleeding effectively on its own and is very usefull in combonation with others\n\nMandragora \nA commonly used herb a very powerful base in lesser and greater form commonly used as a healing base and can be used for more destructive potions\n\nSerpents stalk\nA very good ingredient and can only be harmful if in combonation alone and in most combonations its used for its remarkable aspect of healing burn wounds\n\nFrost vine\nA very colt and blue flowered vine its only found in dwarven lands due to the temperature and is very helpful although very rare\n\nBliss Foil\nA very calming but yet un nerving herb it can numb down a area so no one feels any pain meaning who knows what they could do if applied\n\nAthin\nA herb commonly used for just its extract its very fire retardant and can be applied to non metalic armors to resist fire\nbut in combonation is a ingredient to a Greater Healing Potion\n\nCrimson Vase\nA very odd herb indeed its extract is one of its many uses this herb is a odd smelling flower its smell clouds the mind making decision making hasty but it can also be used in alchemist fire\n\nDrakes tail\nA elegant herb quite rare as well it seems that only the orcs have made its habitat their home and for good reason it only grows in barren land but it has many destructive uses\n\nDwarfs pumpkin\notherwise known as the alcohol flower it can be used to make a dwarven ale the recipie is odd and hidden away from most in oren however\n\nBlood Lotus\nA herb with flowers resembling blood can be very helpful to most peasants considering its soup has all the nutrients one wouldnt get in their daily ration\n\nFlame Tongue root\nA destructive herb that has a few medicinal uses in use with aqua vitae it can be a healing salve called flame spitter but in water becomes a painful extract\n\nLariheis fingers\na herb then when its extract is drank hightens the senses and is very helpful a new fact to the herb is its very sleek and easy to carry so not much of a problem for traveling alchemist\n\nElfs Hair Vine\nA vine that is found in dwarven lands commonly can be very sugary and used to get a mild energy rush in a split second you will be worse then you sttarted off so be warned on that\n\nCrouching feather\nA feather very commonly used in potions mostly in potions for throwing and lethal purpose\n\nHalflings grass\nA herb comonly used in weak poison but can be turned into a drug like affect if in a greater form\n\nAlabaster leaf\nCommonly used in poison but if alone in extract form and drank while the user is poisoned will counteract that of a stronger potency\n\nSaffvil\nA strong and deadly smell eminates from this poison and will kill the entire users body over time if drank\n\nClutchers straw \nthis herb has one hear it one known use and that is to paralysise the poor soul inflicted with its extract or higher potentcy poisons\n\nMetzils tangle \na herb only found in the jungle coils at every area on the herb and s used commonly in stone skin\n\nGoblins ivy\na potion used for many weak poisons and is quite rare a alchemist with their hands on this might make you sick at best", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient Hist. V1", "§bMage Baruk-Ghaz", "\"The Ancient History\"\n\n*The next lines are curved*\n\"These volumes contain the Origins of Aegis, written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Cloud Temple of Aegis.\"\n\n*The book apears to be in good shape, it must be a copy*\n\n\nThere were no planes of existance. There was nothing. Just the void. And a birght shining light. It was not a light we could see, it was not really white either, but it would be the only way to explain the existance of such a Deity in contrast to the\n\nemptiness of the Void. None knows why, how or when this Deity existed. It is in-fact, herecy to speculate on such matters. The all knowing God was this Deity.\n\nTruly none can describe the majesty of the creator, the one and only. The Merciful Creator of\n\nthe Seven Skys. With his intent, and only his intent, the World of Aegis was created. All he had to do was simply want it to occur and it happened. The rolling seas, proud mountains and vast landscapes, all in a second, created by God.\n\nThe World was not created in play or for fun, but as a theater for the inhabitants of the World to be tested in. And tested for what? Well, that is for us to speculate. Though I believe it is to test us for purity, how hard we can fight temptation, and\n\nhow clean and good can our actions be.\n\nThe first mortal being to be created was a man. This man was sculptured with clay and water, but left hollow. Soulless, God's creation lay there for the other immortal beings to see. The Aengul and The Daemon, the\n\ntwo spirits created before the mortal beings and before the creation of Aegis. Not much is known about these two spirit races, but hopefully in the future we may know more.\n\nGod then blew his own breath of existance into the hollow shell and the Man\n\nlived. God then took the man and put him in the centre of all of Aegis, the same spot where The Cloud Temple now stands. God also made the Man a partner, from the exact same clay and the exact same breath, she was the first Woman. These simple beings\n\ncould not talk, nor did thay know much about the World. They roamed the world, enjoying its pre-historic beauty. Untouched, the fresh glades and dark thick forests were a wonder to set eyes on. Soon they had four children in the four corners of Aegis.\n\nMalin was the first of their sons, born in the deep forests of Aegis, he had blonde hair and sharp ears. Standing tall, he was the father of the Elves of Aegis. Krug was born in the scorching deserts of Aegis, fierce and relentless, he was\n\nthe father of the Orcs. In the fresh meadows of Aegis was born Horen, father of Humans. And lastly, in the deep underground caves of the World, Urguan was born, master of the Dwarves.\n\n*You quickly grab the next volume*", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient Hist. V2", "§bMage Baruk-Ghaz", "\"The Ancient History\"\n\n*The next lines are curved*\n\"These volumes contain the Origins of Aegis, written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Cloud Temple of Aegis.\"\n\n*The book apears to be in good shape, it must be a copy*\n\nWe do not know how they fathered sons and how their lines continued but perhaps God created a partner for each Son to continue their lines? Soon the first Man and Woman created by God departed Aegis and climbed the Seven Skys to meet with God, their\n\ncreator, for they had achieved their goal. The Sons began to explore the World of Aegis and each was stricken with grief at the loss of their parents, seeking to fill the chasm of emotion inside them they began to create civilisation.\n\nAlthough none of the descendants of each Son had neither their long lives or splendour, they did carry their forefathers looks and traits. The Elves with Malin at their head travelled the lands and began naming all the inhabitants of this world, they were\n\n the first to create a tongue and began to name the trees and grass, the mountains and valleys across all of Aegis.\n\nAt this time, Malin, Urguan, Krug and Horen were not far apart in looks. Horen and Krug had darker hair than the other two and Krug was a\n\nbit larger in build. Malin had blonde hair and Urguan had red. They were all close companions, friends and had much love for each other and each others people.\n\nUrguan travelled into the deep underground of the earth, naming the beautifull ores and gems,\n\nsharing it with brethren. Krug travelled the harsh deserts, testing his stamina and strength against the environment of Aegis, and Horen began building the first squalid huts and villages in the valleys. The people lived for hundreds of years, in harmony\n\nand peace. Until the day where one of the great Daemons, great worshipers and squalid protectors of the Void, kin spirits to the Aengul, defected. Iblees, who was once the greatest of all of Gods worshippers was cast down into the mortal world, he was\n\nabout to destroy the tranquility of Aegis forever. He came in the shape of a man. A gentle being who loved things delicate, he was quickly befriend by Malin, Urguan and Horen. Krug had a great distrust of him but after many years of being convinced by his\n\n brothers, he finally accepted his company.\n\nLittle did Iblees know that this was all part of the Great God's plan to test the descendants of Man and Woman. Iblees wanted to destroy everything that God had designed and created, still not in a powerfull\n\nphysical form he began sowing seeds of evil in the minds of the Descendants. Prying on their needs and lusts.\n\nTo Malin, lover of nature, he promised him many children. He promised him that their laughter would resonate across their forest homes and that\n\nhe would be content. Malin did not want anything more in this world than a bountiful amount of young to occupy the barren forests.\n\nTo Urguan, he promised infinite wealth of Gems and Ores, colours and crystals he could never imagine. To this, Urguans\n\ngreed failed him. \n\nHoren, was anxious he would not live long enough to see his city completed and his lands increased. He needed more time, and Iblees struck the cord and promised immortality.\n\n*You quickly grab the next volume*", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient History4", "§bTailesin", "-------------------Ancient History, \nVolume 4\n-------------------\n\nThese volumes contain the Origins of Aegis. written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Could Temple of Aegis.\"\n\nBut.. Before they could complete the sentence, Iblees stood tall for one last time.\n\n“You believe you may simply banish me from this plane and that my taint shall not last? I have touched this world with evil and it shall forever be part of the souls that inhabit it.\n\nMalin, I curse you with sterility, you and your kin shall forever lack the children they need. May your forest halls forever be silent, and your hearts heavy with sadness.\n\nUrguan, your greed and lust shall overcome you, you are not worthy of the height God gave you, your descendants shall be short, squalid and ugly. You will always seek to find Gold and Gems in the deep underground of the earth, your hunger never satisfied\n\nHoren, you wish immortality? I will curse you with the opposite, early death for you and your kin. You shall age quickly and die before you experience the fruits of your useless labour.\n\nAnd you Krug, the most hated of The Descendants, you shall always have the lust of war. You are strong? Well the strength shall be used against your brothers,\n\nused to pillage and murder! Your lust for battle shall be unsatiated and your descendants shall grow ugly and heartless.”\n\nAs soon as his words were uttered, the world grew suddenly dark. A great roar erupted from Iblees' mouth and in a blast of fire he was banished from the Mortal World.\n\nThe robed figure stood before the four brothers.\n\n“There is nothing in my power that may undo what that evil did. There is one thing I may do however, and that is to bless you. Malin, may your forests be a sanctuary of peace and your children long lived.\n\nUrguan, may your descendants be hardy, strong hearted and strong of mind.\n\nKrug, may your people show valour and honour where none exist. And Horen, may your people one day explore the Seven Skies.\n\nI can do no more, for all my power is spent. We will go back to our master now, I believe your children may see us once more but long in the future.”\n\nAnd with that, the great host of Aenguls and Daemons dissapeared into thin whisps in the air.", ""),
        StaticBook("Birthday", "§bKira Winterleaf", "A feeling of warmth floods by body.\n\nI feel like I've grown a foot! \n\nI wouldn't care if I was covered in soot on this very special day.\n\nI want to run and jump and play! \n\nGo lots of places but\n\nnever stay!\n\nIt only comes once a year.\n\nIs yours finally coming near? \n\nKeep your heart close and never jeer on this very special day.\n\nI lay down my head tonight, thinking of happiness and not blight.\n\nI see the stars--so beautiful they are!--alined as they were thirteen years ago on my birthday. \n\n\n1556 10th of The Snow's Maiden (my birthday) \n\nBy: Kira Raine Winterleaf, recently age thirteen.", "")
    )

    private fun _ATLAS_12(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("Ancient Hist. V4", "§bMage Baruk-Ghaz", "\"The Ancient History\"\n\n*The next lines are curved*\n\"These volumes contain the Origins of Aegis. written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Could Temple of Aegis.\"\n\n*The book apears to be in good shape, it must be a copy*\n\nBefore they could complete the sentence, Iblees stood tall for one last time.\n\n“You believe you may simply banish me from this plane and that my taint shall not last? I have touched this world with evil and it shall forever be part of the souls that\n\ninhabit it.\n\nMalin, I curse you with sterility, you and your kin shall forever lack the children they need. May your forest halls forever be silent, and your hearts heavy with sadness.\n\nUrguan, your greed and lust shall overcome you, you are not worthy of\n\n the height God gave you, your descendants shall be short, squalid and ugly. You will always seek to find Gold and Gems in the deep underground of the earth, your hunger never satisfied.\n\nHoren, you wish immortality? I will curse you with the opposite,\n\nearly death for you and your kin. You shall age quickly and die before you experience the fruits of your useless labour.\n\nAnd you Krug, the most hated of The Descendants, you shall always have the lust of war. You are strong? Well the strength shall be\n\nused against your brothers, used to pillage and murder! Your lust for battle shall be unsatiated and your descendants shall grow ugly and heartless.”\n\nAs soon as his words were uttered, the world grew suddenly dark. A great roar erupted from Ibleeses\n\nmouth and in a blast of fire he was banished from the Mortal World.\n\nThe robed figure stood before the four brothers.\n\n“There is nothing in my power that may undo what that evil did. There is one thing I may do however, and that is to bless you.\n\nMalin, may your forests be a sanctuary of peace and your children long lived.\n\nUrguan, may your descendants be hardy, strong hearted and strong of mind.\n\nKrug, may your people show valour and honour where none exist.\n\nAnd Horen, may your people one day explore the Seven Skies.\n\nI can do no more, for all my power is spent. We will go back to our master now, I believe your children may see us once more but long in the future.”\n\nAnd with that, the great host of Aenguls and Daemons dissapeared into thin whisps in the air. \n\n*You finished reading about The Ancient History, it feels like you understand the world a lot better*\n\nCopies mady by:\n~Baruk-Ghaz", ""),
        StaticBook("The Apprentice", "§bKira Winterleaf", "~~~~~ The Woodworker's Apprentice ~~~~~\n\nThe Woodworker's Apprentice had long, thin hands.\n\nIn her free time she traveled the lands.\n\nTwisting and twirling her hair in seperate strands.\n\nOh, how I wonder!\n\nThrough rain and through thunder and toil and blunder she skips and skips and runs.\n\nYet, somehow, she gets all her work done.\n\nOh, I wonder, how through rain and thunder, toil and blunder, the Woodworker's Apprentice does it.\n\nShe widdles and riddles and works.\n\nBut in the shadows I lurk.\n\nWaiting for her to come play.\n\nIt could be any day!\n\nBut, no. She roams the land through rain and thunder, and toil and blunder.\n\nI would die for the Woodworker's Apprentice.\n\n\n~~~~~~~   The End   ~~~~~~\n\n\nBy: Kira Raine Winterleaf, Age 12, 1556 9th of the Snow's Maiden (the day before my birthday)c", ""),
        StaticBook("Halflings Vol. 1", "§bPetrus Frondor", "Halflings: A History\n              Vol. I\n\n\n     A History of Halflings\n      By Petyr Brandybuck\n\n    Edited by Petrus Frondor\n\nPreface\nAs is common knowledge, the origins of the halfling race reside began with couples consisting of a human and a dwarf. Nowadays, the halflings have distinct traits of their own, practically unmistakable when compared  with the modern counterparts\n\nof their progenitors. The purpose of this book is to educate the populace about the culture and history of the halflings. The information in this book has been preserved either in ruins, in literature, or in folklore.\n\nChapter 1 - Beginnings\nYears 0-300\n\nThough there are many conflicting accounts regarding the creation and following years of Aegis, most records agree that the race of humans and that of dwarves most likely had contact before Year 50. And, as when any two\n\ncultures collide, so do their blood, and so at some point in time, humans began to mate with dwarves. We can trace some traits back to this point, such as brewing and farming talents, but as we will see, other generic halfling traits developed over time.\n\nIndeed, it is hard to pinpoint the first halfling. After researching animal breeding as well as magical oddities, I would propose that, intially, the children of a dwarf/human couple would not be classified as a halfling.  You see, it would make sense if\n\nhalflings were a height in between that of a dwarf and human, but instead, they are actually shorter! This would indicate that, some time in the past, a great outer influence caused the future children of these mixed couples to have this odd height.\n\nIt is reasonable to conclude that halflings as we know them today were present by Year 300, based on folklore and literary records.\n\nChapter 2 - Early Developments\nYears 300-600\n\nA combination of small stature and inherant greed drove some of the first halflings to thievery, a very viable profession for nimble hands in the cities. They gained a reputation for being mischievous and\n\ndevious, and thus gained little respect for their brewing or farming prowess. There was some prejudice against them, no doubt; never once in history or literature does one hear of a heroic or powerful halfling. To say they were suppressed would be\n\nincorrect, but people did find them a thing to be \"tolerated\".\n\nOver time, of course, most halflings developed a sort of fear of violence and arguments, reducing the number of thieves. As well, most halflings moved to Oren to escape the war-mongering\n\nattitude of the dwarves. This is estimated to have taken place over the time span of Years 400-600 The humans were little better, though. And, over time, halflings began to move to more rural areas, escaping those that would persecute them. Small\n\ncommunities on the very border of human cities were formed, and there halflings began to develop customs to cheer them up. Indeed, good food and good times seemed to become the goal of every halfling.\n\nChapter 3 - Later Developments Years 600-1200\n\nHalflings continued migrating farther and farther out of the city.  It came to be that small communities of halflings were spread out in the vast plains surrounding Oren, with the halflings only revealing\n\nthemselves occasionally to trade goods. Over time, the negative connotation towards halflings mostly disappated, but the halflings enjoyed their semi-solitary lifestyles.\n\nThe countryside is where the art of halfling brew and cooking truly matured into\n\nthe art form that it is today. Indeed, the herbs and spices that could be found in the countryside of Oren had never been truely used to their full potential, and so halflings were the first to utilize them. Some of these herbs include the yellow\n\nwildflower, spice of apple, and fern dust. The ale, while very potent, began to develop more of a focus on flavor and aftertaste. This also was the case with halfling bread and cider.\n\nIt is important to note that there were still some halflings living in cities at this point (~Year 800). Most halflings were born of halfling/halfling, but some were still born of a human/dwarf couple. These halflings enjoyed a much easier life than their\n\nancestors.\nAround this time in history, the number of halflings had indeed grown to a point where surnames were required to identify with, as previously, only single names were used. Names, both first and last, were usually one or two syllables.\n\n Notable surnames and houses that began at this time were the houses of Took and Brandybuck, founded by Tulkan and Grombadook, respectively. The Tooks had the traits of what might be called the quintessential halfling- friendly, yet shy, of average height\n\nand demeanor. The Brandybucks, however, were a tad more adventurous, with darker hair than most halflings. Each family lived in a single burrow hill, most of them in the general vicinity of another. These family hills were the closest thing to a town as\n\nhad truely ever existed yet in halfling culture.\nHowever, in around Year 1010, some families started to break apart from population pressures. Families like the Brandybucks eventually spread all througout the lands.\n\nBy around this time, halflings had been isolated enough that, not only did this unique culture of peace and merriment develop, but the halflings actually forgot or disbelieved in the creation stories and of Aenguls, Daemons, and God. Indeed, the halflings\n\ndidn't really develop a totally obscure faith, but instead took on a quasi-religious outlook that revolved around the things they held dear: harvest and food, companionship, nature, and peace. No \"gods\" were ever named, nor was worship very common,\n\nbut there was a common belief that, if a halfling betrayed his tight-knit community, that the harvest and the tranquility of life would be lost.\n\nChapter 4 - The Rise and Decline of the Village Era\nYears 1200-1304\n\nAround the year 1200, a small group of halflings, from both Took, Brandybuck, Goodbody, and other families, decided to form a self-sufficient community apart from the family burrows.\n\nThey had decided to make it a collection of hills, hidden from society by the waters and hills surrounding it. This community eventually came to be named \"Dunwood\", and it remains the most notable halfling town to date. There are massive wheat fields and\n\na beautiful, clean lake. This town was always notable for being quite territorial, for as the humans began to urbanize more and more, family burrows were abandoned, and Dunwood was one of the last retreats for the country-loving halflings. As word of the\n\ntown's existence came to be, spread by the word of the Wandering Wizard, halflings from both cities and family hills came to try and gain a burrow here. The founders of Dunwood were initially very welcome, but soon, the onrush of want-to-be residents came\n\nto such a high number that it was impossible to distinguish which would be beneficial to the community. The aspect of companionship was always very emphasized, and as a later resident of the town, I can vouch for this fact. By the time I moved to Dunwood,\n\nit was impossible to walk through the streets of Al'Khazar without being pestered for the town's semi-secret location.\n\nEnvious of the town, many halflings and non-halflings alike began making similar towns in the surrounding hills, a more known one by the name of Thistledown.\n\nI am now qualified, at this point, to tell the history from a first-person perspective. Yes, for I arrived in Dunwood during a small population boom, when the town was thriving and more inhabitants came every day. The Elders of the town were Kip Took,\n\nLen, and Gimblo. I was first escorted to the town by Gimblo, who took Bell and I for a visit. And, as I mentioned earlier, we were pursued through the streets of Al'Khazar by nosy people! Gimblo turned a corner, snapped his finger, and suddenly Bell and I\n\nwere in Dunwood (a trick I have yet to emulate perfectly). The smell of hot bread and ale wafted from the ale garden, and I could hear a halfling singing with a lyre in the distance.  Indeed, it was truly the ideal place for a halfling. Soon, the whole\n\ntown took a trip to Al'Khazar to pledge allegiance to the newly-kinged Edmund.\n\nAfter many a year of drinking, story-telling, and visitor-shoveling, the town seemed to go into a cultural decline. The Elders grew a tad sickly, and so they appointed Owen Sturdyfoot II as the Mayor to help govern. However, even his efforts did not\n\nmaintain the vitality of the town, as Brian left to become an Ascended, Biodoc passed away, Rusty moved to Al'Khazar, and Lilly was kidnapped. This decline occured in other towns, too, leaving an area surrounding Dunwood filled with empty hill-homes and\n\nthe likes. I, too, left after a call from the Druids, though the years of countryside living would have a profound influence on me for my entire life.\n\nChapter 5 - Up to Modern Day\nYears 1304-Present Day (approx. 1351)\n\nAfter many years, around 1340, I decided along with a few relatives to restore the popularity and culture of Dunwood, and so we hosted DunFest, a large festival with much merriment.\n\nThere was drinking, dancing, singing, and the likes, of course. However, afterwards, it did not have the profound influence on the town population that I would have liked. And so, I went back to Malinor, only to try another festival later. Indeed, it\n\nappeared that our selective ways way long ago had a profound influence on the interest in the town, and so many gave up their hopes of moving and settled in the city. Many halflings have forgotten the joy of the simple and clean life, to my surprise.\n\nWhile I certainly traveled and was no typical halfling, the air off of Lake Dunwood in the early morn still stirs my soul like very little else does.\nAt the most recent DunFest, I did meet some fellows quite interested in culture, but none were halflings.\n\n I have tried to embrace the city-dwelling halflings with open arms, but very few have answered the call.\nWith the division of Oren, I as one of the only remaining Elders of the town decided to not immediately pledge our allegiance to either faction,\n\ninstead remaining independent for some time. I did, however, set up a trade caravan of goods between Dunwood and Galahar, trying to make use of our overstocked wheat.\n\nAt the time of writing this, the state of the world grows dim as the Undead continue to win victory after victory. The future will surely deserve another tome of halfling history, perhaps more in depth,as this was merely a general overview.\n\nHopefully this has been an informative and enjoyable read. \n\nPetyr Brandybuck", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Ode", "§bTerreni Decorum", "Morning springs with hope anew\n\nTo melt the chill of winters dew. \n\nThe great tree wakes from slumbers peace\n\nWhile blooms of color shows their fleece.\n\nNo more will winter's chill cause the world to still. \n\nWhy you ask , should spring flow in such a way\n\ntis the circle of which things grow, death of one another to sew\n\nThere you have the poem nature's ode \n\nSilence to follow while darkness calls, life stills beneath the shadows\n\nThe third page reached so no more shall follow.", ""),
        StaticBook("Laurelin", "§bArinislia", "Children of Laurelin\n\nI hear the song of Laurelin;\nThe children’s laugher singing;\nOf trees, and leaves of many things,\nTo many, the sound of bells ringing;\n\nI hear the song of  Laurelin,\nNow lost in the burning flames;\nThe echos whisper in our hearts,\nNow most don’t seem the same;\n\nI heard the song of Laurelin,\nof purity and rings;\nOf ones who mix of blood,\nAre met with acid that stings.\n\nI seek the song of  Laurelin,\nOf children’s laughter and song;\n The sound almost lost to me now,\nThough somehow I continue on.", ""),
        StaticBook("Zei Grie", "§bAvenel Synalli", "I\n            III\n           IIIII\n          IIIIIII\n        II       II\n      IIII       IIII\n   IIIIIII       IIIIIII\n      IIIIIIIIIIIIIII\n       III  III  III\n      III   III   III\n      III   III   III\n      ＺＥＩ  ＧＲＩＭＯＩ\n            III\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nThe mind. One of the most powerful tools one can have, given they understand how it works. The mind holds all that is dear, all that one sees, all that one has heard, remembers and has forgotten; the mind is an infinite platform for\ncountless things.\n\nThe mind however, has a major vulnerability that being the tricky archetype of the arcane known as Illusion. While our mind can play tricks on itself, it is generally more vulnerable for being played around by an outsider. Not so many realize that\n\nthe mind generates and projects various\nillusions on itself naturally. This is what the commonfolk call 'Dreams' and if the illusions have negative themes they are 'Nightmares'. While they are considered commonplace in the community that actually sleeps,\n\npeople hardly come into conclusion that they are actually naturally occuring illusions; they are so real and genuine sensations until the dreamer realizes it is nothing but a dream. That means the key to illusion is managing to keep it realistic. Making\n\nsomeone paralyzed without any kind of contact simply doesn't add up, thus a diversion must be put in place for such an exemplary illusion to actually work. The illusions are vital for the mind mages; the Mentalists.\n\nIn no way are illusions the only thing a talented mentalist can master in their collection of spells.\nThe most common spells in a mentalists' array are telepathy, imaginary projection, means to pass barriers and melding, not to mention illusions.\n\nMore talented mentalists on the other hand have knowledge of wiping memories and cataleptic illusions, but in addition to them\n\n.\n\n.\n\n.\n\n.\n\n.\n\n..\n\n.\n\n.\n\n..\n\n.", ""),
        StaticBook("IHBH:SA", "§bSer Paul Ryan", "- - - - - - - - - - - -         It's Hard\n    Being Handsome:\n\n  How to survive in \n  Athera, and other\n        life tips\n- - - - - - - - - - - - \n   By: Ser Pavl Ryan\nWith special foreword         by Emperor \n   Tuvya Carrion I\n\n TABLE OF CONTENTS: \n\n- Foreword\n- Authors Note\n- Chapters I-V\n- About the Author\n\n        FOREWORD\n-------------------\n\nMeeting Ser Paul Ryan, much less becoming good personal friends with him, will most certainly be one of the most defining moments of my life. I've seen much in my time as the Emperor of Athera's greatest nation, but\n\nnever have I seen such strict dedication to greatness as I have in Paul. Whether it was making him part of my privy council, calling upon him to lead a heroic charge in times of war, or simply asking the favor of his attendance to my young daughters\n\nnameday party, I knew I could count on a man as loyal and reliable as Paul. Reading his first book, as well as getting a sneak peak at his upcoming biography, granted me a different outlook on life, and how I live it. Never before have I experienced such\n\na thrilling page turner as I have when reading \"It's Hard Being Handsome\". I recommend this fantastic encyclopedia of majesty to all men and women, regardless of age, race or gender, who one day wish to become sucessful.\n\n     AUTHORS NOTE\n-------------------In my times of travel around the land of Athera, I've come to learn a great deal of things, both through speaking to some of the oldest and wisest masters of \nmany skills, and trial and error as I blundered\n\nmy way through city after city, town after town. I've learnt how to fight great mythical beast, how to drink a \ndwarf under the table, how to hide from halflings, sell my legitimate wares to all kinds of illigitimate folk, and a great deal of other skills\n\n. But if there's \none thing I've learned above all else, it's this; \nIt's hard \nbeing handsome. \n\nHi, I'm Ser Paul Ryan, and this is my book.\n\nMany times through the years, I've been approached by great kings and lowly merchants, all desperate for my advice. I've been the honored guest in many foriegn \ncourts, and handed out justice in my own lands.\n\n I recieved many great awards, both for my continued service to House Carrion and Oren as a whole, and for helping\nout the distressed through all kinds of situations. Yet I soon found out that, no matter how great I was (and trust me, I was great),\n\n my life always seemed....\nempty. By the time I had done all of these great things and achieved knighthood and gentry status, all at the ripe old age of 18, I decided to write down my \nadventures, and, more importantly,\n\nthe rules with which I've come to live life. In the following pages, you too will learn to live by these rules. Good luck, friend,\nand I hope you enjoy it!\n\nCHAPTER ONE (I)\n-------------------Find friends:\nOne of the earliest lessons I learned in life was this; EVERYTHING'S better with friends, friend. Becoming close acquaintance to esteemed men and women like the \nruling class of Oren,\n\nor simply the pal of the local drunk and homeless, friends help you through all sorts of trouble. Need a place to stay? Fine a friend. Need\na loan? Find a friend. Need help breaking Goldsteins' stinking knees, because he still hasn't paid you back\n\nfor that time in the brothel? Find a friend. No matter\nwhere I go in all of Athera, I can always count on finding room and board, courtesy of my good pals. If you really think you're a step above the rest, you can even\ntry to become one of MY friends,\n\nand join Paul's Pals™, my adventuring group of heroes. For better or for worse, helping out someone in need of a hand can result \nin some good luck coming your way down the road. Remember my original and trademarked saying:\n\n A friend in need is a friend indeed, who, in the future, will owe you™. \n\nCHAPTER TWO (II)\n-------------------Find fortune\n\nOne of the greatest assets any man has in lifes' adventure is his coin purse.\n\nMany a schlub can be bribed, and it never hurts to have the pocket coinage to buy that\nfancy new automatic crossbow, or perhaps donate to your favorite local political party. But how does one obtain such vast, impressive wealth like that owned by\n\nthe plutocrats of our age, such as Emperor Tuvya, or Ser Paul Ryan? Well, friend, let me tell you; hard work, creativity, and a good bit of honest borrowing can go far. I myself made my first fortune when I founded CapCo™, Oren's premier\n\nentreprenuerial company, which now is valued at roughly 500,000 minas. By working hard to achieve\nyour dream, and marketing your products correctly, you'll soon build the capital needed to further your endeavors, like\n\nperhaps launching a new, better company. Good\nbusiness partners are a must have, and I myself continue to hold close correspondance with my good friend and investor, Jacob Goldstein, of Goldstein & Company. After I safely handed over CapCo™\n\nto the care of my board of directors, my next startup companies, Paul's Pawn & Picking™, Paul's Tonics & Powders™, Paul's Ghost Hunting™, \nand Paul's Travelling Stall™all quickly flourished under my careful experience. Need some help starting down the\n\npath to vast wealth? Try finding a local investor\nto give you a startup fee, and bleed that sucker dry! If you need some immediate cash, perhaps to pay back the loan shark who threatened to break your fabulous \ncheekbones, consider\nborrwing someone\n\nsomeone elses valuables and using those to pay. Remember, friends: It's not illegal if you're not caught. \n\nCHAPTER THREE (III)\n-------------------Find your talents\n\nIt never helps to have good qualities, friend.\n\n I myself am incredibly handsome and creative, and possess a myriad of skills that would make a jack of all trades cry\nuncle. But how can you exploit the talents you have? Simple; by deceptively manipulating others. Are you a heavy hitter? Consider\n\nstrong arming your way into the \nlocal tavern, and borrowing some of his ale to sell later at a profit. Small and sneaky? Deceptively sneak into a store after hours, and help yourself to the register. \nOr perhaps you're like me,\n\na reknown inventor throughout the lands, sought after for his political savviness and charm. Remember that in this life, NOTHINGS free. Make\nsure you charge the right amount to give that little girl the location you saw her lost dog in, or to hide that\n\nhalfling running from some hungry orcs. If you don't \ncapitalize on all of your chances to make yourself more marketable, you'll never make it in our dog-eat-dog world.\n\nCHAPTER FOUR (IV)\n-------------------Find adventure\n\nSure, friend, you may be able to talk the talk, but can you walk the walk? It doesn't matter if you've accomplished all of these other steps to becoming great, if you don't USE them! Go out on the\n\nroad, and venture into lands previously uncharted. Have a drink at that edgy tavern, or consider stopping by your good dwarf friends\nto see what they've been up to lately. Seeing the world is its own lesson, one that anyone can take, and for free.\n\n Learn how to fight or talk your way out of \nany situation by haggling with the Vanirs over the price of a fish, or wrestling with Orcs in their sand pits. Awe the local milkmaid with tales of your completely\nlegitimate exploits, or spend a night sweeping\n\nat a tavern to cover your room and board. Life in our world is uncertain, friend, but it's always worth checking around the \ncorner to see what kind of trouble you can get into. Fall down and break a leg, or wake up without memory in the temple of those\n\nmonks who definitly enchanted this\nholy water I can sell you at a discount price? Get back up, and try again. If you're ever torn between relative safety, or a possible adventure, ask yourself one \nthing: What would Paul do?\n\nCHAPTER FIVE (V)\n-------------------Putting it all together, and surviving the process\n\nYes, friend, it's challening to learn how to be as great as me, and even more challenging to try, but the results will always pay off. By combining\n\nmy advice with\na little salt-of-experience you gain on your own, you'll quickly find yourself developing into the man or woman you only dreamed of becoming. Go out there, learn\nyour good qualities, get rid of your bad ones, and make\n\nsome good memories while you're at it. Work your way from the gutter to a palace, and go from using that\nrusty dagger to defend yourself to summoning the most powerful of magics. You will fail at first, friend. After all, no one is perfect.\n\n(well, actually, speak for yourself, bub)\nbut the lessons you learn in your failure will help you in years to come. Inevtiably, the great success you'll achieve will make the lesser peons\nof this great realm jealous of you. They'll wish they had your\n\ncreativity, your work ethic, and above all, your dashing good looks. There's a great deal I've learned\nmyself in my many adventures, most of all I've found that, yes, it really IS hard, being handsome. But would I ever give it up?\n\nNot for anything in this world, friend. \n\n- Ser Paul Ryan\n\nSome Quick Tips! \n\nWant to get better at those strange moments of your life, when everyone suddenly has magical symbols above their heads, and people start hitting each other? Try \npracticing in your free time with some pals and a wooden\n\nsparring sword, or, if worse comes to worse, learn how to flee effectively! To fight another day, of course.\n\nDon't try so hard to win all of the time, friend! In gambling, tussling, or even a friendly debate, losing can often yield more than winning.\n\nIf you see Paul or one of his many friends throughout the land, consider stopping for a chat or challenge with them, rather than strangely running and jumping by.\n\nPick a skill or service, become talented in it, and learn how to make a living through it. Sell your paintings or swords in a stall, or on the road, like me! Get a job as a petty laborer to garner experience, before ultimately starting a rival\n\ncompany up with stolen secrets, and crush your former boss!\n\nGo outside every once in a while, friend. And I don't just mean leaving your hovel in Athera.\n\n\nHave fun, friend. What else are we here for?\n\n - About the Author - -------------------Ser Paul Ryan is a successful warrior, statesman, and inventor of great reknown. Founder and CEO of CapCo™, and a number of other popular brands, such as \nPaul's Pawn & Picking™,\n\n Paul's Travelling Stall™, Paul's Pals™, and Paul's World Fair™, he was born in the small village of Wesconsen, before squiring under Boris Carrion III. \nKnighted by the late Emperor Peter Chivay for valor extraordinare in the field of battle.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mothers", "§bOliver", "Dedicant Name: Oliver\n\nTeacher Name: Ryn Powell\n\nDedicantcy Begun on: 20th of Sun's Smile, 1522\n\nTask One: Ode to Nature\n\nAssignment: Write a poem about Nature. It must be at least two pages and can be about anything in nature.\n\n*The following penmanship is like that of a child's. And it's in the wrong book.*\n\ndep in forests wer no men tred- born a child, a wolf in den\ncub in maw or fawn in glen\nal lifes cretures is one comon\nthred\nther eys wer closed, born to fer, lost an hunry, cold and tired\nsur to die, the world is strang\ndark, and big, but help was ner\n\nin the blindnes tim stil dos pas- mothers gid cub out of hard, cold gras\nembrac, and milc, a mothers love a thing well nown by al with warm blod\ngrat to smal, hart to ber,\neven lions hav had ther shar\n\nnon ar born without a mothers car\n\nbut still ther ar thos whos car\n\nwas bar\n\n\nOliver Gallic, dedicent", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro to Aspects", "§bAeran", "Into to the Aspects: Vailorian Edition\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nUpdated from Atheran Edition\n\nThe Druidic Aspects are an ancient and powerful duo of gods whose names are synonymous with Nature itself. Each of the Aspects maintain their respective domain over the natural world and cooperate to establish true balance, a feat which has been\n\nundermined by the efforts of mages and the greedy (Further detailed in the book, ‘The Great War’). Because of this, the Aspects have blessed their divine will upon individuals within the mortal realm, dubbing those chosen  as 'Druids.' The purpose of\n\nthese individuals was clear: eradicate the unnatural, allow life to prosper, and defend nature against unordained destruction. With this creed in mind, the Druids have propelled themselves into the the modern era under the careful supervision of our\n\nbenefactors. But, who are the Aspects?\n\nCerridwen\nGoddess of Life, Mother of The Morn, Lady of Light\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Nature’s Healing\nGoverning Season: Spring\n\nIt is said that whenever the sun rises, so too does the great Springmother. Cerridwen’s divine light breathes life into\n\nthe cold  darkness each morning, replenishing the weary with renewed strength and vigor. During the creation of the world she is said to have personally sculpted every flower and fruit, every nut and vegetable into an object of replenishment and beauty.\n\nCerridwen’s love for the world is unconditional, and she desires peace above all else.\n\nTo experience Cerridwen is to experience the purest form of bliss. You must run barefoot through a bright meadow in the spring-time, your hair down and blowing in\n\nthe warm breeze as the sunlight washes over your sealed eyelids. You must kneel at the edge of a coursing stream and stare down at your reflection, watching the myriad of colorful fish swimming by without much care in the world. You must hold an infant in\n\nyour arms, helpless, trusting, and know what it is to be a mother.\n\nRituals and Beliefs\n-When any new life is brought into the world, Cerridwen is believed to watch over them as any mother would. For this reason the phenomenon of ‘Baby Luck’ is often attributed to her.\n-Cerridwen's recognized symbol is the sun, which can\n\ncommonly be found in druidic libraries as a depiction of ‘Illuminating the darkness of ignorance’.\n\n-Small shrines to Cerridwen can be found in family gardens or farms to ward off pests and encourage a good harvest.\n\n-The officiant of a marriage often blesses the lucky couple with words of Cerridwen to encourage a long, and child-filled life.\n\nCernunnos\nThe Great Huntsman, The Green Man, The Horned God\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Control of Nature\nGoverning Seasons: Summer and Winter\n\nWoe to thee who might seek to prey upon the flock of Lord Cernunnos. Legends speak of arrows being loosed\n\nupon the skies in numbers uncountable, raining upon those that seek to disrupt the careful balance of nature. It was he who gave the porcupine it’s quills, the rattlesnake it's fangs, and he who taught the Elves of ancient times the art of bowmanship.\n\n Cernunnos waits patiently at the side of Cerridwen, guarding her peace with the vigil of a hawk and the calm of an evergreen forest.\n\nTo experience Cernunnos is to experience true challenge. You must hide yourself amidst the canopy of a great green\n\nforest, your body obscured by a cloak of leaves you stitched together with pine needles and sap, your stomach howling for the smallest hint of nourishment, your fingers wrapped tightly around a bowstring as you stalk your prey on the forest floor. You\n\nmust stand steady and vigilant at world’s end, bloody and broken, and counter the pain with courage and skill.\n\nRituals and Beliefs\n-To this day, all Elves owe their natural affinity for archery to Cernunnos. It is believed he personally taught the ancient Elven lord, Malin, the art as a means to balance the diplomatic race against more violent beings.\n\n-In times of war, it is quite common to hear calls of courage and steadiness in the name of the Huntsman.\n\n-Many of the greatest druids have taken an affinity for Cernunnos, and some believe him to be the leader of the Aspectual Trinity.\n\n-When Druids have a goal in mind, they often refer to it as a hunt in reference to the Huntsman.\n\n-The condition of Albinism is often attributed as a blessing from Cernunnos. Creatures with a pure, white coat are predisposed as honourable and majestic\n\nbeasts.\n\nNemiisae\nThe Shadow of Death, Grandmother Spider, Woman of The Moon\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Nature’s Wounding\nGoverning Season: Autumn\n\nManuscripts of ages long since passed describe a woman of forbidden beauty and inescapable horrors. The ancient and\n\nforgotten race of Mori’quessir once worshipped such a goddess in their caves of impenetrable darkness, where those who spoke of light were sacrificed in rituals of blood and web. She is the matron of the night and dark desires, and it is she who spins the\n\ngreat web of destiny. Little is known for sure of Grandmother Spider, and indeed it is difficult to speak of her at all without reference to Cerridwen and Cernunnos.\n\nWhere Cerridwen breathes life and motherhood, Nemiisae demands death and servitude. She\n\nexists as a dark mirror of all that Cerridwen stands for, and perverts her creations of happiness and love into monsters of horror and passion. Those that find themselves abandoned by the light find their way into her domain one way or another, where they\n\nemerge changed or do not emerge at all. Though it may seem contrary in this light, Nemiisae is not an evil aspect. Cerridwen provides the life, and Nemiisae evokes the evolution of that life into something new but sustaining. For many species of plant and\n\nanimal, Nemiisae has been their salvation.\n\nCernunnos teaches strength, honor, and discipline in the same way that Nemiisae teaches cunning, fear, and impulse. The Woman of The Moon teaches us that these abstract concepts are all well and good, but we are\n\noff finding our own light rather than basking in the light of others. Scavengers, luminescent creatures, and spiders are examples of guidance toward her ideals of self-enlightenment.\n\nRituals and Beliefs\n\n-Once knowledge of her presence was made public, several Nemiisaen witch covens began sprouting up. Currently very little is known about these reclusive groups, which is fitting considering she is the Aspect of secrets.\n\n-Giant Glowshrooms and other luminescent creatures are said to have been guided by Nemiisae for millennia, and successfully attained ‘their own light’.\n\n-The moon is often used as her sigil, and many believe it to directly confer Nemiisae's will.\n\n-Creatures blessed with condition of Menalinsim are said to have been touched by Nemiisae, predisposing them as dominant and frightening.", ""),
        StaticBook("The All-Theory", "§bArchibald", "The Theory of Everything, by Archibald Vallero\n-------------------\n\n    In this tome, I wish to explain to all my beliefs on why things exist with direct connection to the realm known as the Void. At times, you might be confused. This is entirely\n\nunderstandable given just how abstract this idea of mine might be.\nIf any of this offends you, I will let you know right now that I did not intend to offend or undermine anyone's religious beliefs. If that is what you believe I intended to do by the\n\ntime you've finished reading this book, then sorry. \n\nNow, let's begin.\n\n       The Void\n-------------------\n\n    Many readers probably have no idea what the Void is, which is why I've decided to begin this with an explanation so that non-mages can be at the same level of understanding with mages as to what the Void is.\n\n     The Void, in short is a dimension where everything exists simultaneously along with nothing. It is all that is and all that will ever be, but at the same time there is nothing there. One point I should emphasize while explaining this is that the Void\n\nIS another dimension. Implant this into your mind: the Void is a completely separate dimension to ours. Now, the use of the Void to mages is simply to draw the ability to create from it. Another keyword I'd like for you to remember is 'creation.' It will\n\nbe mentioned continually throughout this tome. As said before, voidal mages are able to create an array of elements and entities by exhausting their mana, which is essentially every living being's life force. Mana flows through our veins, and with it we\n\nare able to connect to the Void and bring about lightning, flesh, fire, water, air, earth, 'raw' energy (arcane energy), and the ability to alter the world or the people around us through different types of illusion or alteration. We use the Void to bring\n\nabout the CREATION of something or another that would otherwise be inaccessible to non-mages. Now, all of us have a mana anchor in the void, which is what it sounds like. Our mana, while in our bodies, is also connected to the Void, and this is what\n\nallows all non-mages to become mages. Now, with this explanation, I wish to bring up my theory.\n\n    The All-Theory \n-------------------\n\n    So, now you understand what the Void basically is. Good. Now, what if I told you that, since the Void is a dimension where anything is possible and, while it is in a constant state of balance and\n\nunbalance, it was still able to create /everything/. What if the dimension is the epicenter of ALL creations? What if it was even the creator of /our/ Creator? Think about it like this . . .  Mana can be found in all living, organic beings. This means\n\neven grass, trees, all abundances of animals, and us. This mana is what allows for there to be a mana anchor, weak or strong, to the Void for all beings. While the grass you stand on might have an extremely weak mana anchor, there is mana there\n\nnonetheless. Necromancers can drain the life from anything with mana in order to power themselves, meaning that, indeed, all things have mana. Even the stone of castles or the earth beneath us might have mana, though it might be in such an insignificant\n\namount that nothing can really be done with it. If we move up the chain and start with trees, there's more than likely more mana in them than both grass and stone, since Graven's are capable of manipulating their own mana to creator a connection or bond\n\nwith the tree that allows it to stay there. The tree is also involved in this process, though, by allowing for their to be a connection in the first place. Perhaps they have enough mana to have such a thing be done. Next is us Descendant races.\n\nWe are able to cast spells that closely resemble elements we can observe in modern day life, but with more manipulation involved. I won't go into specifics, but it requires the touch of our minds to create ice sculptures, fiery animals, and other such\n\nabnormal things. \n\n    Now, I did say that mana flows through our veins, but what if, just like stone, our skin, bones, and other bodily particles also contained mana? We are beings of mana, from the bottom up. All other things are made of mana,\n\neven if it is difficult for mages to tell. This leads me to believe that we were all created by the Void, but with middle-men inbetween the process of our creation. \n\n    Let's delve further into the nature of the Void for just a moment. We can use it\n\nto create a myriad of things. Here comes the confusing part of this theory, but I will try to summarize it as such first before explaining.\n\nInanimate materials have low mana, not able to create anything. We are animate along with animals, and some\n\nanimals are capable of being magical while us Descendant races can connect with the Void to create. Aengudaemons are powerful, capable of creating amazing and wondrous things, an example being the Arch-aengul Aeriel creating the Ascended, or Metzli\n\ncreating the entire race known as the Kharajyr. This type of creation is far beyond the Descendant races' abilities. Then there is the Creator, which many believe has created the aengudaemons and all of us. This could be true. But now, answer this for\n\nme: what created the Creator? The answer to this, I have deduced, is the only dimension capable of bringing about everything material and animate, or the Void itself. It is to my belief that the Creator is simply a powerful entity that has a immensely\n\npowerful connection to the void, allowing for this being to acquire a massive amount of mana and the ability to create the aengudaemons and us all. Essentially, the Void brought about the powerful being known to us as the Creator, which had a greater,\n\nintense mana anchor and mana pool, that this creator of ours was able to use everything available to it in the Void (which is everything conceivable) to create this world, and the aengudaemons. \n\n    There's also a pecking order, I've noticed. The\n\n Void is the creator of all, it is a dimension/entity/being capable of granting power and creating any and everything, and as it had created our Creator, since the Creator has such a close connection to this dimension, this being's powers closely\n\nresembled the Void's. Then there are the Aengudaemons, some ranking higher than others. They have an immense amount of power, but have physical restrictions. An example would be the fact that they can only shape our realm through us lower beings, as\n\nthe paladins resemble Xan's hand, or the clerics resemble Tahariae's hand, or the cultists of Ikuras resemble Ikuras's hand. Then there is us. We do have a connection to the Void, but at only at a great cost. The more we master arts that allow us to\n\ncreate wards, elements, or bring things back to life or even drain mana from other living beings, the more our muscle mass and sanity disappears. We are thinning out. We are losing ourselves as we continue. This leads me to my next theory.\n\n    The All-Pure\n-------------------\n\n    As said before, the more one attempts to bring something into this realm, the more mass, or particles of themselves, they lose. I've lost around twenty pounds while still maintaining a healthy diet as I\n\nattempted to master conjuration, but if I were to try and learn even more evocations or magicks of another kind, I would be even far more thin. Weak. Decrepit. We lose our corporeal selves as we delve into the power that allows us to create. This leads me\n\nto the other observation that the more closely connected one entity is to the almighty Void, the more of 'themselves' they lose, or, rather, the more they lose their body, the greater their ability to manipulate elements and, naturally, everthing else.\n\nWhy this occurs I'm not /entirely/ sure, but an idea that's come to my mind is that it's the cost, as everything has a cost. The cost for becoming more god-like in being is that you lose what we know as ourselves. We lose our body and our 'mind.' This can\n\npotentially explain why Aengudaemons are able to have such great power in their own realm, creating what they want at their own whim, but as soon as they enter an earthly vessel to interact with us, they only become the equivalent of a powerful mage,\n\nrather than some being with the capabilities to destroy and create. The cost for having a body like ours is that they lose some of the powers that makes them 'them.' At the same time, once they are killed in this world, they are still able to ascend back\n\nto their own and retain their original powers. All very interesting, indeed.\n\n    The real aspect of the All-Pure theory involves 'purity.' In my belief, the closer one is to obtaining the ultimate power that the Void grants, creation, the more\n\n'pure' they are. In this sense, non-mages are not as pure as voidal mages. Inanimate objects aren't entirely as pure either, and are on the bottom of the order. This does not mean that one being deserves to live and one to die. It is simply my outlook on\n\nthings. Now, with this new theory, it is fact that mages who are granted their power from an aengudaemonic force, such as paladins, clerics, and cultists, are not as pure as voidal mages. The purest one can be is when they are able to siphon\n\ntheir spells of creation from the Void, the dimension where, believe, all life truly originates! The reason why paladins and clerics are not as pure as voidal mages is due to the fact that they are drawing their power from a being lower than the Void,\n\nand rather from an aengudaemon which had been created by the Void. They are drawing their power from a weaker source. The Void is the most powerful, truly. This could also explain why they, the aengudaemonic siphoners, are able to retain some muscle\n\nmass and weight while us voidal mages are weakened. We're closer to reaching the nature of the 'nothing-yet-everything' Void, while they are granted their power from a lower and less powerful being which takes its power from the Void. If they were to cut\n\nout the middle-man in their magicks, they would end up being like the voidal sorcerers: able to create more elements and manipulate this world further, yet at the cost of them losing more and more of their bodily matter. \n\n    With this in consideration,\n\nI do believe that there is a Creator. I believe, though, that our Creator is, frankly, a being who has such a powerful connection with the Void that it was capable of bringing our kind about along with the aengudaemons. But the true Creator is the voidal\n\ndimension. \n\n    With this, I conclude my novel. I hope you, brave scholar, found this piece of literature interesting and profound in its content. Thank you.\n\n         -Archibald V.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ironguts", "§bAzkel Frostbeard", "The Irongut Clan is one of the longest living clans of the Dwarves, as well as being one of the biggest.\n\nThe Ironguts are known for their skills in magics, as they are the only Dwarven Clan that magics are frequently taught (besides golemancy &\n\nrunesmithing).\n\nThe Ironguts are also known for having many of their Kin rule the dwarves, including the current King, Ziko Irongut. The Ironguts are also usually powerful blacksmiths, and are known to make their own weapons with their symbols usually on\n\nthem. The Irongut colors are blue and white, as represented on their clothing and banner.", ""),
        StaticBook("Monk Resurrerect", "§bThurak'Yar", ">|<Monk>:<\n -=~Resurrection~=-\n\nIt is the salvaition of many lives, but I wonder why I have seen nothing written on it. The monks, as it is very well known, are able to save those that have passed away. While managing my stall\n\nat the sanctuary grounds I have been able to see for myself some of the happenings, or so I believe.\nThis magic, unlike many others, is one I know very little about. Like all holy magics it seems to\n\nbe manifested as a bright light, mostly used to heal those wounded. Besides the bright light, the monk performing it is praying the the Triumvirate. It appears to be like clerical healing, but has many other properties. Sometimes a monk arrives\n\nappears bandits and the like do not care that they are mugging what could be their savior. What I have to say about those monks, is that unlike normal folk, their wounds heal faster. This is of course, is due to their magic, and in turn the small\n\n flecks of light which I’ve seen surrounding and healing their wounds. Then the monks are able to bring back the once deceased to become fully mortal once more.\n\nThis is not to say they are necromancers. Those simply raise the dead, and they remain in a limbo state; living and being deceased. The monks are able to bring the body and soul exactly like it was before the death in near perfect condition.\n\n From those I have talked to over the years in the Sanctuary, there is an always present sensation of salvation. Present in all of those that I have seen, none have memories of their death or even of the events leading up to it. Other\n\ncommon effects are scars, sometimes showing how the poor soul met their end before, or of the tortures it passed. The ones that worry me the most are those that change after being saved. I have talked to some before after saving, and the\n\nchanges can be quite extreme. Some go crazy, others start to live in constant fear, some remain with pains in parts of their bodies. The changes they can go through are incredibly varied. I could conjure up reasons as to why this happens, all\n\nof them without means of proving so. I just believe death to be a terrible experience, that part of it continues on even after salvation.\n\nAnother interesting fact I have gathered, somehow by luck, is that the saved do not want to return to the location that they had perished. This particular thought came to me when one of the saved was hugged dearly by what I assumed was his lover.\n\nBefore that, his body had been carried by another, a friend perhaps, and he and I had a chat about what happened. I knew of the exact location of his demise, and as him and the woman were moving away, he asked if they were passing through\n\nthe area when they were returning home. With the confirmation of the woman, he started giving out other ways to reach their house. A big argument had begun when the woman just wanted to return home as fast as she could with him, and he plainly refused to\n\ngo through that area, which was the only road to their house. He didn’t know himself why he didn’t want to. Alas, he returned to normal about half a day later. Since then I started noticing other strange behaviours such as people staying\n\nin the sanctuary for longer times, not using their normal routes out of the sanctuary and returning shortly after, then leaving with a friend. I believe, once more, that the experience of death was so horrible that those saved gain a\n\nsubconscious fear of their death place. Besides, what would happen if they saw their own body?\nOn the mention of bodies, I do have to state as well, it is not necessary for the body to be brought back to the monks for the resurrection.\n\nI have seen many monks bring bodies to the sanctuary, so I believe it might be quite easier with them. This is facorable, as in the lands of Anthos there are many evils. You can be decapitated, eaten, thrown into lava, cut into little bits\n\nand drowned in the deepest of oceans. Your body may disappear and never be found, and you may this way still be saved. How they restore the body I do not know, and the monks seem not to answer when queried. They do answer a few questions,\n\nhowever. I learnt, for instance, that the monks are neutral; never taking sides in political views or wars. They also take this neutrality to those they save. I have seen those of evil whose purpose is to kill being saved together with the\n\nchildren that never made any harm. This has brought problems to the monks. Many was others to remain dead, some for good cause. \nMost of the stories of the savesd start simply with them explaining how they woke up in the\n\nmonk’s care. It would seem to always be the same as this. Some come back quite soon after their demise, already back before their friends and family run to the sanctuary to check, and others can take elven days. Yet this magic is not all powerful.\n\nAs the monks mention, they cannot save all. If the deceased does not walk out of the monk’s rooms an Elven week after their death, they are forever lost. The same happens to those that take their own life. I believe that if they think that they\n\ndon’t want to live anymore, then they wouldn’t return to the land of the living. The monks are a blessing to the mortals; they are those that save so many from their shortened lives.\n\n~Written by Rosso, 15’th of Malin’s Welcome, 1452\nRescribed by the Farseer Thurak, 1’st of The Grand Harvest, 1513.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Gardenia", "§bThe Wilting Druid", "O' what are you but,\na fleeting glimpse of \nbeauty. Whose rain;\nthe spring showers\nwash away into\n\nThe crick of mud an'\ngushing water. Your petals which once pierced night skies like a northern star.\n\nAn' that river will flow\ninto a lake of sorrow\nwhere your petals will be forgotten with the annals of time.\n\nWho am I to forget? But I do. I do forget those piercing glimpses of beauty. That you provided me\n\nO' Gardenia! You've taken so long to germinate, but now, within two dawns of eternal happiness\n\nYou are forever bygone. Why must your beauty be a fleeting image and not one of eternity\n\nOnly those unbeautified flora, like grass, daisies weeds, and chrysanthemums have eternity\n\nBut you, O' Gardenia, you are gone with that spring shower. Perhaps. It is good that I knew thee\n\nOnly till spring, for if you'd lived longer, perhaps your petals would not have wilted, but instead.\n\nWilted", ""),
        StaticBook("Elephant Inn", "§bSer Paul Ryan Jr.", "+ The Elephant Inn + -------------------\n\n       Foodstuffs:\n\nBread - 1 mina\nBaked Potatoe - 2 mina Steak - 5 mina\n\nVarious specials - ask\n\n + The Elephant Inn + -------------------           Drinks \n\nAle - 2 Mina\nWine - 3 Mina\nSpecial - Ask\n\n+ The Elephant Inn +\n-------------------         Services \n\nRooms - 10 minas \nMassage - 5 minas \nEntertainment - varies \nSpecials - ask", ""),
        StaticBook("Mr & Mrs Carrot!", "§bAylun'sae Tenna'tahu", "The Gallant Adventures of Mister and Missus Carrot!!\n\n  -Aylun'sae\n     Tenna'tahu\n\nDay One:\n The Carrot Patch\n\n The morning was a clear one, the season good for growing. Upon this fine morning, Mister Carrot stretched his roots and said to himself, \"Now isn't this a fine day!\" and with that he\n\nuprooted and went to start on his morning walk. \n  Upon this walk, he saw Mister Potato teaching his daughter how to starch. As a neighborly sort of fellow, Mister Potato called out, \"Hello Mister Potato! A fine day it is today!\"\n\n  To this, Mister Potato angrily replied, \"Yeah! A fine day to your skinny rooty self! Stay away from my daughter you orange fiend!\"\n  Mister Carrot didn't mind Mister Potato's angry outbursts. Usually they just meant he was being friendly. So on Mister\n\nCarrot went with his walk, and a fine walk it was.\n\nDay Two:\n  Mister Rabbit\n\n From time to time the garden patch would have visitors who would come from far and away to see the vegetables growing and raising their offshoots. Mister Rabbit was one of these fine fellows, and\n\nquite the self proclaimed vegetable afficionado he was!\n On one of the gallant mornings that Mister Carrot went for one of his delightful walks, lo and behold!! Mister Rabbit was meandering down his usual route of walking!\n Mister Carrot was quite beside\n\nhimself with emotion, \"What is this?? A Mister Rabbit?? In the gardens???\" It was quite the dilemma.\n Mister Carrot, being an unusually amiable fellow, of course, thought it best to give Mister Rabbit the appropriate welcome. And so, without further ado,\n\nMister Carrot found himself walking himself towards Mister Potato's usual garden bed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pine Tree Art", "§bArchdruid Gi'Garun", "*An elaborate painting of a Pine tree is here*\n\n             --\n           -----\n         ------- \n       ----------\n     ------------ \n    -------------\n   --------------\n  ---------------\n -----------------\n            [ ]", ""),
        StaticBook("Siege Weapons", "timzuiderveld", "* A fairly accurate drawing of two catapults in a forest fills this page, there is a road going between them the inscription below reads: \"Two catapults on road to Urguan, near Barbek Hold \" *\n\n* A detailed sketch of the Blackmaul crossing wall fills this page the inscription below reads \"Blackmaul crossing, ~1 or two guards\" *\n\n* A rough sketch of the Vaerhaven gate fills this page, the inscription below reads \"Vaerhaven gates, features internal drawbridge, Vaerhaven bought materials for ~4 ballistae\" *\n\n* A fairly detailed sketch of a dwarven steam ship fills this page the inscription below reads \"Soggy beard: No siege weapons on board\" *\n\n* A detailed sketch of a dwarven steam ship with a ballista on its deck fills this page, the inscription below reads: \"Frosty Axe, one ballista on top deck\" *\n\n* A detailed sketch of yet another dwarven steam ship fills this page, it has a big ballista on the front, and a smaller one on the right the inscription below reads \"Thorins wrath: Two Ballistae on top deck\"\n\n* A sketch of a sailing ship with dark sails fills this page, it has a ballista mounted on its top deck the inscription below reads \"Unknown: One Ballista top deck\" *\n\n* a vage sketch of a dwarven steam ship off in the distance fills this page, it has barely any details, the inscription below reads \"Unknown: Arnaments unknown\"*\n\n* A sketch of a ballista on the dwarven docks fills this page, the inscription below reads \"Ballista, dwarven docks\"\n\n* A sketch of a multiple decked sailing ship fills this page, it has one ballista barely visible on its top decks, and many ballista holes on its sides *\n\"Unknown ship, Ballista, 1\"\n\n* a sketch of three catapults standing neatly in a line in the snow fills this page*\n\"Kal azgoth Catapuls   One two and three\"\n\n* A sketch of 4 ballistae standing neatly in a line fills this page, the one on the right is far bigger* \n\"Kal Azgoth Ballistae one two three and four.\"\n\nSiege weapons Tally\nCatapults: 5\nBallistae: 14\n\nShip tally\nSoggy beard -0\nFrosty Aexe - 1\nThorins wrath -2\nunknown       -1\nUnknown       -?\nUnknown       -1", ""),
        StaticBook("Rain Storm", "§bTever Softfoot", "Poem titled \"Rain Storm\"\nBy Tever Softfoot.\n\nThe rain falls down from the grey skies above.\nWashing away the days weariness and adding a glow to all life.\nLeaves dancing as the water falls over them.\nEarth drinking deep the refreshing gift,\n\nsharing it with all those around.\nRefreshing creation with life giving energy.\nWashing away the troubles of the past.\nGiving way to new life and new days.\nThunder rumbles in the skies, signaling the rebirth of life. \nShaking the air with the power of\n\nnature.\nLightning crashes through the air.\nSmiting the evil in the world and returning the balance. \nWater rushes through the rivers and streams.\nWashing away the filth of those who taint it.\nThe rain falls ever harder,filling the rivers and streams\n\nbeyond holding.\nFlooding the land and washing away all signs.\nYet something remains in this place.\nThe clouds depart, the thunder ceases, and the lightning retreats.\nThe sun begins to shine down upon the glorious scene of natures power.\nThe water retreats\n\nand life from the smallest of seed begins anew.\nThe promise of beginning after beginning, thus is the cycle of nature.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old fat spider", "§b[Poet] Garry Bogger", "Halfling Poems\n\n1.           Old fat spider           spinning in a tree\n\nOld fat spider spinning in a tree!\nOld fat spider can't see me!\nAttercorp! Attercorp!\nWon't you stop,\nStop your spinning and look for me?\n\nOld Tomnoddy, all big body,\nOld Tomnoddycan't spy me!\nAttercorp! Attercorp!\nDown you drop!\nYou'll never catch me up your tree!\n\nLazy Lob and crazy Cob\nAre wearingwebs to wind me\nI am far more sweet than other meetBut still cannot f\n\nind me!\n\n\nHere am I, naughty little fly;\nYou are fat and layzy.\nYou cannot trap me, tough you try in your cobwebs crazy.\n\nMay the hair on yer toes never fall off!!!", ""),
        StaticBook("Kha Dictionary", "§bRi'Zeriko", "~ Greetings/Courtesy Words ~\n\nSa'vi - Hello/Goodbye\nMul'ta - Thank you\nTra'kul - You're welcome\n\n~ Pronouns ~\n\nPatta - Father\nMuuna - Mother\nMetz'al - Life Mate or Spouse\nBunn - Wife\nMunn - Husband\nPer - Son\nMaut - Daughter\nPer'ta - Brother\nMau'na - Sister\n\n~The Karakatuan Government~\n\nTlatlanni - A Kharajyr blessed with white fur from Metztli herself, the Tlatlanni is perfection and divinity amongst the Kharajyr. The role of the Tlatlanni is to lead the Kharajyr for Metztli.\n\nTultelkos - Aelkos of war. He/she deals with political or militaristic efforts, and leader of the Moonblades.\n\nTruluk - Aelkos of Crafts. He/she is director of the economy and building.\n\nVarkolu - Aelkos of Faith. The leader of the Ja’ priesthood.\n\n Sostelos - The Social Aelkos. This Aelkos is responsible in providing happiness to the Kharajyr.\n\nThe titles of the Kharajyr are bestowed upon them according to their place in society.  Every Kharajyr will have a title (Excluding kittens/cubs) that forms the prefix next to their name. \n\nTla' - Tlatlannti\nRi' - Aelkos\nDri' - Tultelkos\nDra' - Moonblade\n\nJa' - Priest of Metztli\nYhl' - Elder of the Kharajyr\nQi' - Hunter\nKo', Ki' - Merchants/traders\nS', Sa', So',Si' - Unemployed\n\n~The Wildlife~\n\nTykkan  - Alligator\n\nVena-vi - Fireflies\n\nZisban - Monkey\n\nTuark - Hyenas\n\nNehpu - Colourful breed of birds found on the kha island.\n\nCehkan - Blue parrots\n\nCayda - Sharks\n\nGy'Waka - The Gy’Waka are a breed of colossal majestic birds that inhabit the islandthey protect the homeland as if it were their own and are by far the lead predators of the island.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mathic Code", "§bRosso", "From a note written by a friend in code, of many pages, she had no memory of writing it, or what it did mean.\n\nI decided to try and decipher it as if it were a secret note between armies, and I came with results. The code is copied first, then the meaning of the code is told, then the translation.\n\nCode\n\n{t, ƒ:ªº£ ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t.£ ûe:ç :º e, ñ.ô, ñ.nü| ƒ'ª t, u':ô .nô t, ,ñö£:n,ºº d,i'û t, u':ô û,ª, e:º £' c'ññ.nô]\n[t,ü û,ª, ô\"º£ .nô e, û.º .º . º\"nd,.ñ| ô,ƒ:n:nø .£ i.º£ t,:ª ê.ö,]\n\n[.º û.º d,ƒ'ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º e, ñ.ô, .ø.:n| û:t t, º'ªª'û 'ƒ º\"ni:øe£ ª,ñ.:n:nø \"n£ª,.£,ô .nô \"nû,ic'ñ, £' t, ô,,ö c\"ªª,n£º 'ƒ t, º,.]\n[º\"ç ô:ª,c£:'ni,ººn,ºº :º .n.t,ñ. £' e:ñ| ûe' :º i:n,.ª]\n[t,º, .ª, £' d, E:º £ª:\"ñöeº .nô E:º ƒ.:i:nøº| ñ.nü £:ñ,º 'u,ª]\n\n{t, º,c‘nô ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ƒª.c£:‘nº ‘ƒ t.£ ûe:ç :º e, .ª, d.ªª,ô ƒª‘ñ ,n£ªü]\n[t, ‘t,ªº e.u, t,:ª ô,u:c,º ‘ƒ û.ª .nô ô:º“n:‘n| ô,ƒ:n:æ t,ñ .º ûe.£ t,ü .iû.üº e.u, d,,n| t, c.ª,£.*,ªº ‘ƒ t,:ª ƒ.£,]\n\n[t,ü ê‘“£ .nô öi,.ô .nô ‘ƒƒ,ª t,:ª u‘:c,º £‘ t, e,.u,nº| d“£ t,ü .ª, .º t, ô,,ö c.u,º d,øø:æ ƒ‘ª º“ni:øe£]\n[t, û.ü :º ê“£]\n\n{t, t:ªô ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[. ‘n, £,.ªº ƒª,, t, di:nôƒ‘iô ‘ƒ t, .düºº ƒª‘ñ t, ,ü,º ‘ƒ t, ö.ª£º]\n[:£ :º .º :£ û.º]\n[t, i:øe£ .nô i:øe£º .nô i:øe£:æ ‘ƒ t,:ª êª:n,º ƒª,, t,:ª º:øe£| t.£ t,ü ñ.ü di:nô ‘t,ªº]\n\n[û.ª .nô ô:º“n:‘n .ø.:n º“øø,º£º :£º,iƒ| .nô t, i:n, :º ñ.ô, .º .n “nc‘“n£.di, º,ª:,º ‘ƒ ö‘:n£º| .ii :n c‘nƒi:c£ û:t t,:ª ƒ,ii‘ûº]\n[.ii û:,iô:æ t,:ª i.û]\n\n{t, ƒ‘“ªt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t,:ª i.û :º t, ö‘û,ª ‘ƒ t, ñ.nü]\n[ºi‘ûiü t, i:øe£º .ª, ,æª.u,ô û:t t, ºüñd‘iº ‘ƒ t, ,nº,ñdi,º ‘ƒ t, ñ.nü]\n[t, º:øe£º ‘ƒ n,û ç.:nº .ª, ƒ.ñ:i:.ª]\n\n{t, ƒ:ƒt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ƒª.øñ,n£º .ª, ñ.ô, .º £‘ø,t,ª .º û.º d,ƒ‘ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º e,]\n[t, ñ.nü ç‘‘º, t,:ª ö.t| £‘ t, ª,ö,£:£:‘n .º t, º“n ª:º,º .nô ƒ.iiº ‘ª £‘ t, dª,.*:æ ‘ƒ :£| t.£ t, º“n ê.ii º£.nô º£:ii]\n\n{t, º:xt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ñ.nü d,c‘ñ, .º ‘n, .º û.º t.£ ûe:ç :º e,]\n\n{t, º,u,nt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[.º û.º d,ƒ‘ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º ñ.nü ñ.ô, .ø.:n]\n\nMeaning\n\n. - a\n, - e\n: - i\n‘ - o\n“ - u\n\nt - th\nƒ - f\nª - r\nº - s\n£ - t\nñ - m\nc - c\n\nø - g\nû - w\ne - h\nç - ch\nô - d\nn - n\nü - y\nu - v\nö - p\nd - b\ni - l\nê - sh\n* - k\n\næ - ng\n\n| - ,\n\n[] - sentance\n{} - title\n\nTranslation\n\nThe first mathic age.\n\nThat which is He made many, for the void and the emptiness below the void were His to command.\nThey were dust and He was as a sunbeam, defining at last their shape.\n\nAs was before, that which is He made again, with the sorrow of sunlight remaining untreated and unwelcome to the deep currents of the sea.\nSuch directionless is anathema to Him, who is linear.\n\nThese are to be His triumphs and His failings, many times over.\n\nThe second Mathic age\n\nThe fractions of that which is he are barred from entry.\nThe others have their devices of war and disunion, defining them as what they always have been, the caretakers of their fate.\n\nThey shout and plead and offer their voices to the heavens, but they are as the deep caves begging for sunlight.\nThe way is shut.\n\nThe third Mathic age\n\nA one tears free the blindfold of the abyss from the eyes of the parts.\nIt is as it was.\nThe light and lights and lighting of their shrines free their sight, that they may blind others.\n\nWar and disunion again suggests itself, and the line is made as an uncountable series of points, all in conflict with their fellows.\nAll wielding their law.\n\nThe Fourth Mathic Age\n\nTheir law is the power of the many.\nSlowly the lights are engraved with the symbols of the ensembles of the many.\nThe sights of new chains are familiar.\n\nThe Fifth Mathic Age\n\nThe fragments are made as together as was before, that which is he\nThe many choose their path, to the repetition as the sun rises and falls or to the breaking of it, the sun shall stand still.\n\nThe Sixth Mathic Age\n\nThe many become as one as was that which is he.\n\nThe Seventh Mathic Age\n\nAs was before, that which is he many made again.\n\nMathic Code\n\nCoppied and Decoded by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro to Aspects", "§bTaynuel", "Intro to the Aspects\n\nThe Druidic Aspects are an ancient and powerful duo of gods whose names are synonymous with Nature itself. Each of the Aspects maintain their respective domain over the natural world and cooperate to establish true balance, a feat which has been\n\nundermined by the efforts of mages and the greedy (Further detailed in the book, 'The Great War'). Because of this, the Aspects have blessed their divine will upon individuals within the mortal realm, dubbing those chosen as 'Druids'. The purpose of these\n\nindividuals was clear: eradicate the unnatural, allow life to prosper, and defend nature against unordained destruction. With this creed in mind, the Druids have propelled themselves into the modern era under the careful supervision\n\nof our benefactors. But, who are the Aspects?\n\nCerridwen\nGoddess of Life\nMother of the Morn\nLady of Light\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Nature's Healing\nGoverning Season: Spring\n\nIt is said that whenever the sun rises, so too does\n\nthe great Springmother. Cerridwen's divine light breathes life into the cold darkness each morning, replenishing the weary with renewed strength and vigor. During the creation of the world she is said to have personally sculpted every flower and\n\nfruit, every nut and vegetable into an object of replenishment and beauty. Cerridwen's love for the world is unconditional, and she desires peace above all else.\n\nTo experience Cerridwen is to experience the purest\n\nform of bliss. You must run barefoot through a bright meador in the spring-time, your hair down and blowing in the warm breeze as the sunlight washes over your sealed eyelids. You must kneel at the edge of a coursing stream and stare down at your\n\nreflection, watching the myriad of colorful fish swimming by without much care in the world. You must hold an infant in your arms, helpless, trusting, and know what it is to be a mother.\n\nRituals and Beliefs\n\n-When any new life is brought into the world, Cerridwen is believed to watch over them as any mother would. For this reason the phenomenon of 'Baby Luck' is often attributed to her.\n-Cerridwen's recognized symbol is\n\nthe sun, which can commonly be found in druidic libraries as a depiction of 'Illuminating the darkness of ignorance'.\n-Small shrines to Cerridwen can be found in family gardens or farms to ward off pests and encourage a good\n\nharvest.\n-The officiant of a marriage often blesses the lucky couple with words of Cerridwen to encourage a long and child-filled life.\n\nCernunnos\nThe Great Huntsman\nThe Green Man\nThe Horned God\nBestowed Druidic Ability: Control of Nature\nGoverning Seasons: Summer and Winter\n\nWoe to thee who might seek to prey upon the flock of Lord Cernunnos. Legends\n\nspeak of arrows being loosed upon the skies in numbers uncountable, raining upon those that seek to disrupt the careful balance of nature. It was he who gave the porcupine its quills, the rattlesnake its fangs, and he who first taught the Elves of ancient\n\ntimes the art of bowmanship. Cernunnos waits patiently at the side of Cerridwen, guarding her peace with the vigil of a hawk and the calm of an evergreen forest.\n\nTo experience Cernunnos is to experience true challenge. You must hide yourself amidst\n\nthe canopy of a great green forest, your body obscured by a cloak of leaves you stitched together with pine needles and sap, your stomach howling for the smallest hint of nourishment, your fingers wrapped tightly around a bowstring as you stalk your prey\n\non the forest floor. You must stand steady and vigilant at world's end, bloody and broken, and counter the pain with courage and skill.\n\nRituals and Beliefs\n\n-To this day, all Elves owe their natural affinity for archery\n\nto Cernunnos. It is believed he personally taught the ancient Elven lord, Malin, the art as a means to balance the diplomatic race against more violent beings.\n-In times of war, it is quite common to hear calls of courage and steadiness in the name of the\n\nHuntsman.\n-Many of the greatest druids have taken an affinity for Cernunnos, and some believe him to be the leader of the Aspects.\n-When Druids have a goal in mind, they often refer to it as a hunt in reference to the Huntsman.\n-The condition of\n\nAlbinism is often attributed as a blessing from Cernunnos. Creatures with a pure, white coat are predisposed as honorable and majestic beasts.\n\n*All pages after this point have been unceremoniously torn from the book, only tatters with narry a droplet of ink remaining visible. The scent of ash is nearby.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Aren'iril", "JMACKLES", "Aren'iril\n\nYou were there\n'Neath Delfyr's Sanctuary\n\nYou were there\nAs I came to be.\n\nYou learned by my side as I trained in my arts, taking a totem that was not for me.\n\nTogether we created much, spread\n\nverdure long.  The Mother of Elandriel and Swamp of Song I do not claim my power that you gave.  It is of the Aspect's will you endured, becoming more than a staff, becoming my closest, longest friend.\n\nLost to the void, I would leap in to find you.\n\nAnd I did.\n\nYou were there when the land began to fall, and Ethanriel's call grew weak, you TOOK her into you, kept her for me, till time was right to plant the seed.\n\nTogether we searched the lands for the Sacred Three.\n\nYou tried to stop me from dying, but I was so tired.  So very, very tired.....though you could not know the Wolf's plan.....\n\nAnd when forward came the SonofNone, for the second time you resisted him, and let my brothers know of his existence.\n\nYou were there through every moment, and I was not there for your last.\n\nI could not be there to stop your demise, but I promise you this....\nI will find who did this and bring justice to them.  There will be no mercy for such a pure life destroyed.\n\nOr I will die trying.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                  ~Song", ""),
        StaticBook("Fairy Ring", "§bKyral Rilann", "The following is from myself, Kyral, and my time spent meditating in a fairy circle.\n\nThese are the three days of this task.\n\nDay one\n\nSitting  in the tree when the all familiar humming noises filled my senses, it felt overwhelming, but I tried and tried to stay in as long as I could, focusing on the humming for as long as I could. Was I incomfortable? or\n\nwas it just the noise getting to me? At least this was my first time in, I had plenty of time to get used to such noises.\n\nI figure it will be easier the more I go in, but I need to pace myself, I feel a woman could go a little crazy if left inside that\n\nFairy ring for far too long. Did I mention it was hard resisting the urge to dance? I never dance and I felt myself wanting to do it more and more.\n\nI don't like dancing.\n\nDay two\n\nI easily entered the circle, finding it much easier then the last to meditate with my eyes closed. It was more amazing to focus in the humming I heard with my other senses, a whole new experience that left me more amazed at\n\nthis mystical ring, I can't comprehend the things I heard, but I think its the forest.. doing something.\n\nAs for leaving the circle, I left with only slight issues, having to get back in for this book, but I looked around the whole grove for signs of\n\npeople, and I skipped around the fairy circle.\n\nIt was amazing I felt, relieved. I hated to dance but it was just skipping, and I felt so much better.\n\nDay three\n\nThis day was one of the more amazing, I meditated after having had a long talk with one of my former Scions, talking about what I did and redemption. Having been calmed by that chat, I walked back into the fairy ring.\n\nI was not ready and i did not expect to hear what I did. It was like the Pulse of the realm was guiding itself to me, I felt everything moving and coming towards me, Meditating was hard, but i managed to keep it going, remembering my times as a Cleric and\n\nMage to keep me going, to focus on one of those feelings, one of those pulses of life.\n\nI almost did it, but I felt my thoughts filling my mind with crazy reasoning, and I needed to get out. This wasn't a safe day to be inside the circle. I feel that I am\n\ngetting closer to understanding the way the realm works, but I might be wrong, this Fairy circle continues to bring new and new things to me to realize, that the realm is forever changing.\n\nI skipped around the circle again, and stopped it at that.\n\nThis would be my final day, but I expect to be asked to meditate here from time to time.\n\n*The following pages\nseem to be sketches slid into the book*\n\n*A large Rainbow tree seems to be cut by a figure in this one, the top half of this page\ndepicts this.\n\nWhile the lower half depicts the tree igniting, leaving scrambled markings on the ground /#&!(@/ *\n\n*Another sketch seems to be the apple, blue in color being picked from the still standing rainbow tree, a hand extending to it as if this was a panel by panel sketch. but it stops there.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "t", ""),
        StaticBook("I am the Tree", "§bYelfir Everna", "I Am the Tree\n\nby:  Hierophant Callax, the Solstice Druid\n\nThe Tree is oft' taken for granted; so much more is't than a mere creation amid the Aspects' vast domain. It is a symbol which has lasted since the dawn of Nature's--\n\n--time and which will last for eternity. \n\nThe Tree assumes many elegant forms and an outstanding array of varieties;  albeit, it is stubborn and unchanging. And even while it is stubborn and unchanging, it is so forgiving and gracious.\n\nThe Tree is powerful and captivating; one cannot rid of't. No matter the force, the blight, or the raging fire, the Tree will overcome and retain vitality, spreading across the vast lands.\n\nThe Tree takes little, but gives much.\n\nDespite the actions taken upon and against it by unnatural forces or by ravaging Races, the Tree continues to provide to all with grace and plenty.  \n\nIt is the Tree by which I have life. It is the Tree by which I learn - and unto which I have- respe\n\n And it is the Tree by which I have an adoration for that I shall ne'er conceal.\n\nTimes do change -as do people and factions and nations. However, the Tree is stubborn and it does not change. It continues to devoutly serve Nature's world.\n\nI must be like the Tree.\n\nI am the Tree.", ""),
        StaticBook("What is Balance?", "§bYelfir Everna", "What is Balance?\n\nby: Solstice Druid Callax \n\nBalance, something that we all seek as Druids. And as I meditate I often wonder what one must go through to achieve this.\n\nThere are times when we are often surrounded by ideals, goals, mindsets and values that utterly contradict balance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Death", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Death\nOr: The Interactions of the Aspects\n\n~~Elk Druid Danic\n\nShining down on our world, Cerridwen’s warmth and love fosters the growth of beautiful creations throughout our world. She culticates the rolling fields, the\n\nbright flowers, and the tall patient oaks.\n\nLurking in the foliage, Cernunnos waits with his bow in hand, his hunt unending. He teaches the wild predators of this world the thrill of the chase, and lays traps of poison ivy and throny bushes within\n\nhis realm.\n\nIn every corner of this world, the Aspects bless it with their glorious creatures. From the snowy mountains, to the dank lowlands, life thrives and grows under their will. As one would assume, protecting such life from outside\n\ninfluences is one of the primary roles of the Druid. But what is Life without Death? Such an important topic rarely is brought up amongst Druids, mostly prescribing it to discussions between student and teacher. Woefully, even here the topic is bare and\n\nsentences are filled with “Um”s and “Well”s. Other times, the answer is pulled out of thin air from the teacher to sound like they know what they’re talking about. This simply will not do, for to be a respectable Druid, one must understand the worlds of\n\nLife and Death, what they are, and where they come from. This essay will hopefully assist Druids and soon-to-be Druids young and old to learn what Death is at least from my understanding and centuries of experience.\n\n**What IS Death?**\n\n   This seems like a rather simple question, what is it? Obviously we all know what it is. It’s where something or someone stops breathing, moving, heartbeat, and just generally living. Of course, while I’m sure there are a\n\nthousand arguments on what happens after, we’re going to focus on a different aspect of this question; Where does it come from? More importantly, under whose domain does it fall under? This is where, I feel, many Druids fail to truly understand. They\n\nsimply follow the train of thought “Either Cernunnos or Cerridwen”. This is not necessarily a faulty line of thinking, as it does work in most questions involving the Aspects, but it is flawed in the respect that it fails to branch out from that “Or” and\n\nenter the world of “And”. Indeed, not everything falls cleanly into one or the other, but instead emerges because of the two together.\n\nBecause most people fail to realize this, they futilely attempt to neatly place Death into either of the realms of\n\nAspects, only to struggle and ignore the numerous reasons why their placement cannot be true. Strangely, they also fail to comprehend that such a topic can be the result of both of the Aspects together. This union of the Aspects is something one could\n\ncall ‘Aspectual Interaction’ or simple, interactions between the Aspects. Many things are the result of this, the Balance being one example, Life itself being another. Death is also the result of aspectual interaction. No, it is not Cernunnos and his\n\nhunt, or Cerridwen and her seasons, but the ways their realms intermingle. How can this be? I’m sure you’re wondering, so let us begin on what Death actually does.\n\nAs stated in my previous writings, ‘Absolutism’, Death is something that should be\n\ncelebrated and protected so long as it remains natural. It’s the key mechanisms that the Natural World has to maintain Balance. Each Death has a key influence of the world around it, no Natural Death is truly wasteful. When an elder elk passes from this\n\nworld, its body will be consumed, its bones will fertilize, and its exit will give way to future elk. When the seasons change, the flowers and leaves will die and fall, covering the ground. They will remain packed underneath the snow, retaining warmth for\n\nfuture seedlings, and will decay into fertilizer for the day the sun returns and the snow melts. Death allows for the world to continue on, opening dozens of doors for life to spring forth once more in a more stable fashion. But the question is, how does\n\nthis relate to the intermingling of the Aspect’s realms?\n\nNo single Aspect can truly take credit for the Death. As if a dance, the two interact and create the conditions possible for the Death. Cernunnos may teach his children the Great Hunt, but\n\nbut without Cerridwen’s flock, no hunt would occur. One could argue that this is really just Cernunnos indirectly causing Death, which I would not necessarily disagree with, but because of that o assign Death to Cernunnos would be a grievously shallow\n\nunderstanding of the Balance as a whole. It might seem rather contradictory, but in this topic of study, non-interaction is just as key as interaction itself. Let us try a little thought experiment:\n\nAssume Cerridwen withdraws from the world, taking\n\n taking her children with her. Interaction between the two Aspects reaches absolute zero. Suddenly, Cernunnos’ children have no hunt, no shelter, nothing. His thorny traps wither in the sun, or even worse, succumb to the crippling never ending winter.\n\nDeath is rampant within the world, but is Cernunnos to blame? Of course not, he was not responsible for killing his own children. More importantly, is Cerridwen to blame? I would argue again that she would be innocent. She did not interact with the world\n\nwhatsoever, yet Death still occurred, not because of anyone in particular. This is why non-interaction is nearly the equivalence of interaction of the Aspects. Death is the result of how they interact, not because they interact. “How did they interact?\n\nThey did not.\" versus “Because they interacted, they did not” might help you grasp this concept a bit better, as it’s rather difficult to explain using the common tongue.\n\nFrom the thought experiment, we realize that in reality, neither Aspect is genuinely solely responsible for Death. It’s merely the reaction to their interaction or non-interaction. While it’s obviously easier to just claim falsely that one Aspect creates\n\nsuch a thing, it does a great disservice to the absolutely beautiful and intricate system our Blessed Ones have crafted around us. I do hope this essay will help ease the stress of understanding and teaching such a fascinating topic, and more Druids will\n\nenter their position more apt and capable than ever.\n\n****If the reader has any disagreements or alternate views, I greatly urge you to publish your own essays. Druidic Thought cannot progress within an echo chamber.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mystic Wandering", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Mystic Wanderings\n       By: *Unknown*\n\n  It was the raw and wild duo of earth and natural growth: the Aenguls Cernunnos and Cerridwen.\n\n Thes two incorporeal beings are the patrons and deities\n\nof which Druids call upon for influence over nature.\n\n With the Elven penchant for similar ideals as the Druid Aspects, the two factions frequently blend. In this, the Druids exist.", ""),
        StaticBook("Neo-Druidism", "§bTristanm1", "Neo-Druidism\nBy Salmon Druid Petyr\n\nWith some modern druidic practices wandering astray, it has been necessary for me to scribe another text - that of Neo-Druidism. Throughout my travels and my meditative studies, I have had revelations unknown to others. It is important to understand that\n\nthere is a special type of balance that druids themselves remember to study - action and non-action. Let me clarify. Action is motion, the motion of restoring balance, and of being the hand of Nature. Non-action is meditation and silence; invisible\n\nmotion. You see, this is the only Way to truly understand Nature, the Universe, the \"It\". For, through non-action you may feel \"It\", and through action you may be \"It\".  Call this \"It\" what you want; most druids will call it Nature. What is important is\n\nto understand what Nature truly is, and how it relates to you. To reveal this is the goal of this tome.\n\nSection 1 - The Unity of Nature\nThe first fault in thinking is to make distinctions - those between you and Nature, between life and death, between good and evil.  Everything is Nature, despite what any given piece believes. A druid does not use nature: a\n\ndruid is merely a piece who realizes the profound truth. Through non-action, one may truly understand this concept and feel it course through them. However, while meditating in non-action, one also separates themselves from the flow; the Way; the path\n\nthat Nature chooses for its pieces. To rejoin with Nature, one must practice action.\n\nSection 2 - The Way of Nature\nNature does what it will, and while we realize this while meditating, sometimes we forget in action. We are merely moving along the river of life, the tides of balance. While we believe that we are restoring the balance, it\n\nis balance restoring itself in an everlasting self-conflict. Thus, through action, we are the hands of Nature, of balance, of the Way. One must not think of \"using\" Nature while playing their part, but of \"being\" Nature. This is a common mistake as well\n\namong young druids and non-druids. They think to restore the world by planting lots of trees... but that is not balance, nor is it even the way to achieve balance. Action alone is not the Way, for one forgets the purpose of action. Only with the mix of\n\naction and non-action can one achieve the goal: to have your cake and eat it, too.\n\nSection 3 - The Druid\nSo as we can now see, the Druid is simply the most important hand, the piece Nature uses to govern itself. To truly try to understand is a task that lies within meditation and non-action. However, there is a limit to the mental reach\n\nof the pieces, for as no one truly knows all of themselves, Nature does not know all of itself. This is a fairly important observation: that even though Nature is unified, the unification is marked by threadlines of dubiousness and doubt. The Neo-Druid\n\nshould not fret, however. There are uneven surfaces in every river. No tree has identical leaves. Just so, nothing, no \"It\", can be the symmetrical perfection one would like to see. Randomness and uniqueness are inherent in Nature, as defining as the\n\nunity itself. Paradoxical though it may seem, through balance of action and non-action, it shall be revealed to you, and only then will you understand the phrase...\n\n\n\"All is One\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Green Children", "§bYelfir Everna", "Green Children\n\nby: Sunshine Druid Verden\n\nThe beauty of nature is something that can be compared, but never truly replicated by the four races. An artist can paint a beautiful picture that will be marveled by all for a time,\n\nbut flowers have and will always be marveled. It isn’t skill or practice that creates a flower.  It is love and attention, the same way one raises a child. They smile in the sunlight, and go to bed for the winter, only to awaken once more come spring.\n\nFlowers are the children of love.\n\nWhen one looks skyward in the dead of winter,  they do not see the same as in spring. The leaves of nature are no fools. They know when they should appear and when  they should muster their strength\n\nfor the next assault on the skies. The body which grows them is no less steadfast. Where leaves demand cunning, their trunk holds fast against invaders. The trees of Malin are children of intelligence and soldiers of nature.\n\nThe glowing fields of wheat surrounding the Vale are a sight to see for all people. Golden and brilliant, a single seed can grow into a vast plantation that not only sustains its own population, buts its caretakers as well. Wheat and its friends have --\n\n--all formed an inseparable bond with the mortal races that can never be broken. Vegetables, fruits and grains are the children of generosity and kindness.\n\nKnown as weeds to some, the hardy grasses of the lands are the precursors of natural expansio\n\nBefore trees, flowers or shrubs dare set foot in a place, the grass has already been there and prepared the soil for them. They hold the land together to prevent its shattering and falling apart, without it there is no habitable for any natural green.\n\nThe grasses are the children of adventure and courage.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dedicant's Vow", "§bYelfir Everna", "Dedicant's Vow\n\nBy Salmon Druid Petyr\n\n            ~*~\n\"I will walk the forests with my new kin. I will swim the river of truth wherever it takes me. As the trees watch over us, I shall watch over others in need.\n\nI  will serve nature and help restore balance,  , even as dusk approaches. I vow to uphold my duties as a Dedicant of the Druid Order.\"\n\n            ~*~\n\"I will walk the forests with my new kin.\"\n\nWith this statement,\n\nthe Dedicant pledges his or her official ties to be with the Druids of the Druidic Order.\n\n           ~*~\n\"I will swim the river of truth wherever it takes me.\"\n\nWith this statement, he Dedicant pledges loyalty --\n\n--to the Order and thereby promises to speak the truth and never with deception.\n           \n           ~*~\n\"As the trees watch over us, I shall watch over others in need.\"\n\nWhile under the guidance of the Aspects and the elder Druids,\n\nthe Dedicant pledges to return the favour of respect and servive.\n\n           ~*~\n\"I will serve nature and help restore balance, even as dusk approaches.\"\n\nThe Dedicant pledges devout service,\n\nThe Dedicant pledges devout service,\n\n           ~*~\n\"I vow to uphold my duties as a Dedicant of the Druidic Order.\"\n\nThe Dedicant does just that; he or she vows to uphold his or her duties and their pledge as a Dedicant", ""),
        StaticBook("Neo-Druidism", "§bYelfir Everna", "Neo-Druidism\n\nby Salmon Druid Petyr\n\nWith some modern druidic practices wandering astray, it has been necessary for me to scribe another text - that of Neo-Druidism. Throughout my travels and my meditative studies,\n\n I have had revelations unknown to others. It is important to understand that there is a special type of balance that druids themselves remember to study - action and non-action. Let me clarify. Action is motion, the motion of restoring balance,\n\nand of being the hand of Nature. Non-action is meditation and silence; invisible motion. You see, this is the only Way to truly understand Nature, the Universe, the \"It\". For, through non-action you may feel \"It\", and through action you may be \"It\".\n\nCall this \"It\" what you want; most druids will call it Nature. What is important is to understand what Nature truly is, and how it relates to you. To reveal this is the goal of this tome.\n\nSection 1 - The Unity of Nature *** The first fault in thinking is to make distinctions -  those between you and Nature, between life and death, between good and evil. Everything is Nature, despite what any given piece believes. A druid does not us --\n\n-- nature: a druid is merely a piece who realizes the profound truth. Through non-action, one may truly understand this concept and feel it course through them. However, while meditating in non-action,  one also separates themselves from the flow;\n\n the Way, the path that Nature chooses for its pieces. To rejoin with Nature, one must practice action. \n\n*** Section 2 - The Way of Nature *** \n Nature does what it will, and while we realize this while meditating, sometimes we forget in action.\n\nWe are merely moving along the river of life, the tides of balance. While we believe that we are restoring the balance, it is balance restoring itself in an everlasting self-conflict. Thus, through action, we are the hands of Nature, \nof balance,\n\nof the Way. One must not think of \"using\" Nature while playing their part, but of \"being\" Nature. This is a common mistake as well among young druids and non-druids. . They think to restore the world by planting lots of trees... but that is not balance,\n\nnor is it even the way to achieve balance. Action alone is not the Way, for one forgets the purpose of action. Only with the mix of action and non-action can one achieve the goal: to have your cake and eat it, too. \n\nSection 3 - The Druid *** --\n\n--So as we can now see, the Druid is simply the most important hand, the piece Nature uses to govern itself. To truly try to understand is a task that lies within meditation and non-action. However, there is a limit to the mental reach of the pieces,\n\nor as no one truly knows all of themselves, Nature does not know all of itself. This is a fairly important observation: that even though Nature is unified, the unification is marked by threadlines of dubiousness and doubt.\n\nThe Neo-Druid should not fret, however. There are uneven surfaces in every river. No tree has identical leaves. Just so, nothing, no \"It\",\ncan be the symmetrical perfection one would like to see. Randomness and uniqueness are inherent in Nature,\n\nas defining as the unity itself. Paradoxical though it may seem, through balance of action and non-action, it shall be revealed to you, and only then will you understand the phrase.... \n\n\"All is One\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Bardic Rite", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Bardic Rite\n \"Solace Druid\"\n\nThe tasks we set out before our charges are not tests of their character or dedication, they are lessons, first and foremost. The test doesn't come until they have breathed their first breath as a Druid,--\n\nand felt the touch of the world as they realize their new role. Druidism is the test, not the reward, and Dedicancy is the lesson, not the trial.\n\nAnd the first lesson is to open their eyes.\n\nFor if they are blind, they will trudge along\n\nthrough all the lessons you could give without being able to see what they truly mean.\n\nFor this reason, the bardic rite is no simple talent show. It is not a test. It is no pointless tradition.\n\nIt may seem like a fun exercise to draw\n\nyour Dedicant in, but it is an important lesson nonetheless. It is a way to open the Dedicant's mind to the path that lays out before them.\n\nA way of initiating them into the mindset that will be natural for them when they finally bloom.\n\nPoetry has come to be the staple, yes, but the rite makes no such requirement. At least, not by my interpretation.\n\nA poem is simply what most people assume they need to do, and the presumption has infiltrated even the Guides. Creativity is a Druid's\n\ngreatest asset, and this lie of thought inhibits it, both for those who teach it and those they teach.\n\nA bard is no simply poet. A bard is an artist of many muses. A spoken word, a poem, a melodious tone. Harmony by any means is not the sole medium\n\nwhich they express themselves. Songs without words, stories without structure.\n\nBrush, chisel, knife, or even fire. Bards are storytellers, keepers of one's culture, and weaver of feeling, not through poetry, or even just tales. At the heart of every\n\nbard is art, not simply song and dance, or even a pen.\n\nPoetry is just one avenue they pursue. Claiming to know unwavering that the only true path is down that avenue\n\nClaiming that the 'Bardic Rite' itself requires it, limits it,\n\n limits the Dedicant's ability to truly show their colours, to truly inscribe their personal dedication to nature into their work.\n\n\n**Crudely scribbled here in charcoal**\n\nPRETENTIOUS BULLSHIT", ""),
        StaticBook("Ashton: Age 9", "§bAshton", "\"Ashton. Age 9.\"\n\n\"Mister Oliver made me upset today. I feel like I'm never going to have a family. He cares for everyone equally. That's a good thing, but...I'm not special to him.\"\n\n*A drawing of a sad looking little girl is below the text.*\n\n\"I talked to Mister Aeran, and I do feel a bit better. Still, I feel like I'm not special to anyone. I just sort of exist. I feel so lonely. \n\nMister Aeran said I can talk to him whenever I feel lonely. I hope it helps.\"\n\n\"MISTER OLIVER ADOPTED ME! HE IS MY DAD NOW!\" \n\n*A large drawing of a happy face takes up the rest of the page.*\n\n\"There is a woman in the infirmary. She was kidnapped by orcs. Some Dark Elves brought her in. She had her tongue cut out. \n\nDad and Aeran are letting me watch again. They are trying to heal her.\"\n\n*A few drawings take up this page. One of a tree. Some shrubs. A few smiley faces. A butterfly. Other things little girls would draw.*\n\n*The rest of the book is blank, as if it was forgotten.*", ""),
        StaticBook("To Oliver.", "elliot5", "Dear Oliver,\nI cannot change what happened, I cannot change how you view what has happened and what you view our future to be.\n\nAll I can ask is that you have faith in not me, but us as partners.\n\n- Connor", ""),
        StaticBook("Ashton: Age 8", "§b[Child] Ashton", "On the top of the page, a scraggly text is written. \n\"My name is Ashton. I am 7 years old.\"\n\nBelow the text is a crudely drawn picture of a cactus.\n\nThe text on this page has quite a few spelling errors. However, it might be understood as the following:\n\"My grandma always told me death was inevitable. I guess that's true. A man Mr. Oliver knew died. His dad is a druid. It was sad.\"\n\nThe next several pages are filled with drawings of flowers, shrubs, trees, and other fauna. \n\nEach picture is labeled with a basic description.\n\nI'm 8 years old today. Mister Aeran (Mister Oliver called him Aeran) gave me a book. I don't think he knew it was my birthday. It was nice.\n\n\"There is a person in the infirmary. They were  badly burned. Mister Aeran made a salve for them. He told me I could watch, so I can learn.\"\n\nA hastily drawn picture of a person in bandages lies below the writing.\n\n\"Mister Muildir told Mister Aeran that a seed is a good way to stitch up a wound. Mister Aeran has to grow the seed in the wound to help it heal. He said to coat it in tipp in root salve.\"\n\n\"They made the seed grow! It looked weird. They used it to help the lady's leg.\"\n\nA drawing of a zig zag sproutling takes up the rest of the page.\n\n\"Mister Muildir said something bad has happend. That there is a disturbance in nature. \n\nHe said it's terrible, but they don't know what it is.\"\n\n\"Today, I was playing in the forest near Felsen. I saw a hare. It was pretty. \n\nBut a hunter killed her. He butchered her. It was really sad. \n\nI found her babies. I brought them back to the grove with me.\"\n\n*A drawing of three infant hares takes up this page.*\n\n\"Mister Oliver told me that Mister Taynuel is the Hare Druid!!! I took the babies to his office, but he wasn't there. So, I put them in a basket and left a letter. I hope he gets to them quick. \n\nI'll check on them until he comes.\"\n\n\"I was in Felsen today...\n\nI heard the church men yelling about Mass. I remember Mass from when I lived in Felsen. That's where I would get food.\"\n\n\"Today wasn't like the other times I was there, though.\n\nEverything was chaos. \n\nAn orc tried to take me! He told me to follow him, and he started saying weird words.\"\n\n\"Then, Mister Aeran showed up! He saved me. He told the orc I'm not a slave, and he made him go away. \n\nMister Aeran took me out of the church, and a woman accused HIM of kidnapping me!\"\n\n*A drawing of Aeran, bravely telling the orc to go away is on this page. Including a speech bubble of Aeran saying fuck off.*\n\n*On this page, a drawing of Aeran calling a mean looking woman a cunt is drawn.*\n\n\"After that ended, a woman almost got kidnapped outside of Felsen.\n\nFelsen is a scary place. I don't ever want to go back without someone.\"\n\n\"But...that means I can't see Karrow anymore. I have to go back. I miss him. He's my first friend in a long time.\"\n\n*A drawing of Ashton and Karrow playing is below the text.*\n\n\"I went to a moot with Mister Oliver today. It was neat. I liked listening to the people. Except when the people were arguing.\n\nMister Oliver got chosen to be a guide! I'm so excited!\"\n\n\"But I have to wait until I'm 16 to become a dedicant. I wish I didn't have to wait that long.\"\n\n*A picture is drawn of an older Ashton, as she imagines herself to be at 16.*", ""),
        StaticBook("ulogy for egl", "§bOliver", "*The penmanship here is incredibly dense, and like that of a child's. One could read it if they stared long enough, and accepted a certain level of insanity.*\n\n\n\nulogy for egl\n\nrin askd mi to rite a ulagy for this tini frend croning it a tasc and tretin it lik a test I rispect that but in all honesty I dont ned a asinment to spek up at a birds fewnarel after al I knew it beter then anyon her ha this bird a vidgelent berd of pray\n\nwas born with a sol redy to sor a destiny to se far horizons to swiftle div throgh tha tres unrestraned and magistec the hiest fredom mortl flesh can ateyn but it was al rested awy from him befor even the first breth he tok stilborn and the only fertil eg\n\nin his clutc peraps a yer ago I wold despis the culty of fat but now iv relizd another point of vew it is a sham yes that he levs us so yong but the circl wil start again his sol remanes his flesh is not lost he returns to the land that crafted him to be\n\nreborn and sor agen he departs for the rots that so the gras and in the stag that grazs on it to the wolfs that hunt it nd the rayvn thet stalks them it was never his desteny to sit fot of his ary in his living days he livd and he saw his lif was short\n\nbut it did pas and that is al it pasd us by\n\n*The reader now feels their eyes scream for mercy as they recover from the strain. They feel the eldritched abomination in front of them sapping their intelligence with every moment they continue staring.*", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter for Oli", "§bShawn Telvin", "\"Dear Oliver,\n\nI know I asked to speak privately on this matter, but I felt a letter would be better. I want to express myself in the most sincere way I possibly can. I do not express these concerns lightly. I thank you for your time in listening.\"\n\n*There are several words crossed out. It appears Shawn started and restarted this section numerous times.*\n\n\"I have doubts about Jesslyn. I deeply feel she should not be a Druid or even a dedicant at this time.\"\n\n\"During my time interacting with her, I have seen rage over petty things. \n\nI have been the target of her anger on more than one occassion for simply speaking in a way she deemed out of turn.\"\n\n\"She blatantly disrespects you nearly every time I see the two of you interacting.\n\nShe refuses to do assignments in the way you ask. She argues over your lessons.\"\n\n\"She insisted after quite a lengthy amount of time that she couldn't locate one specific book in the library. \n\nI have to say -- the library is not very large. She could have asked. She could have looked harder.\"\n\n\"I have my doubts that she even tried to locate the book.\n\nWhen you told her to look again, she argued and insisted you tell her instead. \n\nWhen you did, she couldn't even answer one question accurately.\"\n\n\"Her time as a dedicant has been marked with laziness, anger, and insincerity.\n\nPerhaps someday, she will be more mature and ready. Perhaps her life experiences will some day bring her the patience and respect she needs to properly be a dedicant.\"\n\n\"Until then, I would like to officially complain about Jesslyn. \n\nI want to make it known that I truly believe her to not be ready for dedicancy at this time.\n\nOnce again, I thank you for your time.\"\n\n-Shawn Telvin", ""),
        StaticBook("Grant I (1502)", "§bMenithrian", "Syndicate: 1000 Minae grant \nSun's Smile 1502\nPurpose: Fund Roopak's agricultural Valaise requires intial capital for ability for growth and purchase of inital livestock. \n\nSigned by Menithrian \nActing Chairman of the Hawkers' Club\nConsul\n\nSigned by High Overseer of the Syndicate, Tyrell Blavier.", ""),
        StaticBook("Task Tome", "§bYelfir Everna", "Task Tome\n\nBy: Lillith Winterleaf, the Sequoia Druid\n\nI endeavor in a series of tomes to give examples of tasks I gave as a Guide on Asulon, or that I have observed or had given to me in my time as a Dedicant.\n\nThese are to help both current and future Guides, but are not the only options. Be creative and - assign tasks to a Dedicant that are tailored to who they are, Be creative and - assign tasks to a Dedicant that are tailored to who they are,\n\n~ The Sequoia Druid, Lillith Wintereaf\n\n[]Poetry: A poem is an excellent way to judge one's particular affinity towards nature,  and what specific aspect of Nature they are drawn to. This also can help the Dedicant get an idea what Totem --\n\n-- they may wish to choose if they reach attunement, for they will usually write about that which they are drawn to.\n\n[]Make A Salve: There are a variety of salves able to be made from various herbs and mixtures, which can be found in documented in -\n\n--other books, I will not clutter this book with them. Choose a salve, be it for burns, wounds, pain relief, antivenom, or whatever you feel there will be ingredients readily available for. Have the Dedicant bring you the finished mixture to inspect it,\n\nhen have them apply it to a relevant affliction or wound if such is available.\n\n[]One of Every Specimen: : This is not as straightforward as it may sound.  This task is meant to familiarize a Dedicant with a variety of nature's creations.\n\n Some choose to have the Dedicant bring them 'one of everything that grows' if they are not particularly knowledgeable about plant life, while others choose to have them bring all of one or two particular varieties of natural things,\n\nlike flowers, logs and leaves, ores, mushrooms and grasses, fish meat and bones, or any variety of such things. All of the chosen items are turned in together, and should be followed by a verbal lesson, which I will explain next as a Task of its own.\n\n[]Verbal Lesson on Natural Resources: This should directly follow a gathering of a variety of natural resources. Take the Dedicant aside to a quiet place so you will avoid interruption. ake each item the Dedicant has brought you and explain,\n\nin your own words and opinion, the purpose of the item, its uses, its place in nature, and anything else you feel worth mentioning about it. Return each item afterwards for the Dedicant to study further on their own if they so desire. Ask them questions--\n\nabout what you have told them to ensure they have paid attention and learned from you.\n\n[]Into the Wilds: This Task is meant to teach a Dedicant about a druid's responsibility to seek out places where nature needs a helping hand to flourish once more\n\n Most often this involves providing them with a small quantity of bonemeal and saplings, and perhaps a tent kit as well if there are some to spare, and tasking them to venture out beyond the lands of the races and nations, to the wild lands beyond to--\n\n--seek out a place they can plant the saplings and use the bonemeal to make the ground fertile for grasses and flowers. They are to tend to the area until all the saplings grow,  for no less than one elven day. they need to keep a journal of their stay--\n\n--and their thoughts and activities, the Guide should provide a blank book for this, and once the Task is complete, they can return to the Guide and hand in their journal and report on their Task and what they learned from it.\n\n ((they should RP the entire thing once finding a location and take screenshots of performing the task, tending to it,  meditation, etc, and upload them either via Skype directly to their Guide, or preferably\nuploading to a gallery--\n\nlike imgur as a single gallery.))\n\n[]Spread of Bonemeal: This is a simple task usually given early to a Dedicant. You give them a moderate amount of bonemeal and send them out into the wilderness to spread  it out upon an empty plains--\n\n--or clearing to make the ground lush and fertile. They can also perform this Task within the grove if there is an area suitable for it.\n\n\n[]Bravery: This is not a Task that is assigned, rather it is earned by the Dedicant.\n\n If an event occurs during their training, or at the grove, nd the Dedicant shows an act of exceptional selflessness and bravery in defense of the Order, while maintaining admirable attitude and integrity,\ntheir Guide may deem to count this action as a\n\n--completed Task, as they have proven that their heart is with the Order.\n\n[]Broader Knowledge:\n This Task is given in order to teach the Dedicant that knowledge is perhaps the most important thing in the world.\n\nIgnorance can easily get you killed, and misunderstanding can easily cause offense where there should not have been. Generally this task involves going out and finding two books or tomes that contain knowledge or teachings from outside the Order,\n\nit is important they are not Druidic in content, as the entire point of this lesson is to understand something else and fill gaps in their knowledge so they can be more successful in their future dealings.\n\n[]Fairy Ring Meditation: --\n\n This is an important lesson, and you should encourage the Dedicant to return to fairy rings on their own from time to time, even after their attunement. For this lesson,  you should explain what they will experience once they enter the ring. once they --\n\n--understand, have them sit within the ring and give them the time they need to become used to the sensation, answer any questions they have. Then, have them meditate, help them find peace and balance, and let them meditate until either you feel they --\n\n-- succeeded, or the strain of the communion is causing them undue stress. Reinforce the experience with questions about what they felt, what they saw, and if they have any questions themselves.\n[]Dangers of the Void: --\n\n--This lesson is the most vital for a Dedicant to understand. Void magic, ALL Void magic, is pure imbalance.  If an attuned Druid casts Void magic of any sort, even a very simple spell, it damages their connection to the Aspects and there --\n\n--communion to nature. The Druid that attuned them will also feel when this occurs through the link they formed upon attuning the Dedicant, and Archdruids feel the Void casting as well and can sense the taint of it upon a Druid.\n\nIt is vitally important you explain this to every Dedicant so they understand the consequences of their actions and the damage they will do to their attunement.  Furthermore, casting Void magic is grounds for immediate reprimand by the Order,\n\nand further offenses will lead to unattunement and removal from the order. Void magic is a subject it pays to be knowledgeable about, ignorance can get you killed, but a Druid must never seek to use such unnatural and imbalancing magic.\n\n((if you are a Druid and you use Void Magic of any sort, you MUST OOCly inform at least the Guide that attuned you and an Archdruid,  how you approach telling them, or not, in RP is up to you. . Failure to OOC inform is considered power gaming--\n\n-- and if it is learned you have done this, you may have your attunement shattered without the chance to explain your actions. POWERGAMING IS NEVER OKAY!!!))\n\n[] Lesson on The Aspects:: This lesson teaches a Dedicant the core 'faith', if you will, of Druidism, The Aspects. While little precise information is known about them, hrough dreams and visions Druids have gleaned a little of the nature of our patrons.\n\nIt is important for the Dedicant to understand that without The Aspects, Druids would have almost none of their gifts, it is the blessing of The Aspects that we can attune to the currents and melodies of nature, , the ebb and flow of life around us.\n\nHelp the Dedicant develop a respect and thankful attitude towards The Aspects, they should give thanks to them often. Use other tomessuch as 'An Introduction to the Aspects' to aid your lesson. \n\n[]Lesson on The Balance:: --\n\n--his is a very in-depth lesson to give your Dedicants, but a very important one. You may find the 'Neo-Druidism' tome to be especially helpful in this lesson, as it gives a most accurate description if positive and negative action towards preserving --\n\n--the Balance. This is sometimes a hard concept to grasp, but a very important one for every Druid. You must impress upon them that a Druid must recognize when action is required, and when\nobservation or even lack of action is required,\n\nand understanding what these truly mean to a Druid.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pigmen history", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\nA Brief History of Pigmen: Aegis - Kalos\n-------------------\n\n~Sunshine Druid Verden\n\nThe appearance of Zombie Pigmen was first documented during the Second Iblees Invasion, several hundred years ago. These unnatural monsters were used by the Fallen Daemon as powerful soldiers during his war of conquest against the mortal races of\n\nAegis, where their group mentality and shear ferocity was crippling to the defenders.\n\nUnlike other lesser Undead, the Zombie Pigmen possessed an intellect far superior to any common Zombie or Skeleton. They would always be seen in possession of jagged, golden swords and very rarely were encountered alone.\n\nTheir speed was almost on par with that of the living, a trait that allowed few the luxury of escaping.\n\nAfter the invasion was ended and the Dark One had been sealed away for good, many strange things were observed by the population of Aegis. While the mindless Zombies and Skeletons continued to attack anyone on sight, the Pigmen did not.\n\nThey had become passive, almost completely indifferent to the world around them. Instead of mindlessly killing, they began to aimlessly roam the world, staring blindly at the things around them as if they weren't truly there.\n\nAfter Aegis had crumbled and sank into the sea, many thought they would never see the creatures again. And, indeed, they didn't. For a time. But, again, the Pigmen emerged from the unknown, and they were furious.\n\nFor many years the Pigmen were found roaming the darkest corners of Asulon and attacked anyone foolish enough to get close. The reason for this is not clear, and many theories have arisen to explain. However, none have been proven.\n\nYet another strange event was observed on the island of Elysium. A large volcano near the temporary Elven settlement erupted and spewed lava over the Malinor farms, and the Pigmen rode it down the slope.\n\nThey were, thankfully, peaceful this time; but they appeared in numbers never seen before. Hundreds of them began to blindly wander from their lair, spilling over the border into the Orcish deserts.\n\nThere they were destroyed not by men, nor by beast, but by cacti. The creatures tore themselves to shreds over the next few months as they walked through the cacti farms,\n\nand they probably would still if it wasn't for the piles of their dead brethren impeding their movement.\n\nSince arriving in Anthos there has only been one reported case of Pigmen. This was a very small issue, where only three or four Pigmen would wander around a border wall in the Wilds.\n\nA magical fountain was placed at this site, and all has been quiet since then.\n\nIf the Pigmen are here on Anthos, they are hiding very well.", ""),
        StaticBook("Seed of Laureli", "§bTailesin", "-------------------The High Prince of Malinor and The Seed of Laurelin\nScribed by Zevandir\nTold by High Prince Native\n-------------------\n\nA powerful Arch Druid from one of Malinor's eldest cities came to Aegis to help us with the erecting of Laurelin's trees. I met him at the cobblestone spires to the west of the Aegis Temple.\n\nWe set off on foot to a mountain gorge I had come across that would act as a natural barrier to our foes. Once we arrived, the Druid wandered around in the valley reaching into a pouch at his hip every so often.\n\nHe showed me the seeds in said pouch and they had tiny, intricate vines woven around them, with a shine very similar to glowstone. Whilst my brigade of builders were creating the city's defensive wall, trees began to grow exponentially!\n\n It seemed as though the trees grew faster the taller they got and soon the canopy raised hundreds of feet above us.\n\nIt was a wonderful sight, the leaves sprung out of the branches with a burst of the similar glowstone sheen, and sprinkles of metallic dust swirled down from the tree tops.\n\nThere was still, however, a large clearing in the center of the city. This of course was for the mother tree. A tree to harbor them all. He grabbed a bit of parchment from his satchel and look over at me reassuringly.\n\nThe Arch Druid then walked to the center of the clearing, and began to cast a powerful spell upon one of the seeds. Leaves suddenly spun around him wildly, and a wonderful green color radiated from him like an aura.\n\nThe tree' trunk sprouted around him, and he was no longer in view. I was rather frightened when I saw this, hoping he hadn't cast the spell incorrectly.\n\nAs the Mother Tree grew to its ultimate height, I saw a small opening half way up the tree. Low and behold, the Arch Druid stood in the opening proclaiming \"This is where the stone alter shall lie.\"\n\nOnce the trees seemed to stop growing, we climbed up the trees with packs of wooden planks and began setting down the first few platforms.\n\nIn time, Laurelin had become of the finest city's of Malinor, and to think it had all begun with just a pouch of seeds and a pinch of Druid Magic.", ""),
        StaticBook("Talar & Valah", "§bAscanius Dunlain", "When age fell upon the world, and wonder went out of the minds of men; when gray cities reared to smoky skies, tall towers, grim and ugly, in whose shadow none might dream of the sun or of spring's flowering meads; when learning stripped our world\n\nof her mantle of beauty, and poets sand no more save of twisted phantoms seen with bleared and inward-looking eyes; when these things had come to pass, and childish hopes had gone away forever, there was a man who travelled out of\n\nlife on a quest into the spaces whither the world's dreams had fled.\n Of the name and abode of this man but little is written, for they were of the waking world only; yet it is said that both were obscure. It is enough to know that he dwelt in a city of\n\nhigh walls where sterile twilight reigned, and that he toiled all day among shadow and turmoil, coming home at evening to a room whose one window opened not on the fields and groves but on a dim court where other windows stared in dull despair. From that\n\ncasement one might see only walls and windows, except sometimes when one leaned far out and peered aloft at the small stars that passed. And because mere walls and windows must soon drive to madness a man who dreams and reads much, the dweller\n\nin that room used night after night to lean out and peer aloft to glimpse some fragment of things beyond waking world and grayness of tall cities. After years he began to call the slow-sailing stars by name, and to follow them in fancy when they glided\n\nregretfully out of sight; till at length of his vision opened to many secret vistas whose existence no common eye suspects. And one night a might gulf was bridged, and the dream-haunted skies swelled down to the loney watcher's window to merge with the\n\nclose air of his room and make him a part of their fabulous wonder.\n There came to that room wild streams of violet midnight glittering with dust of gold; vortices of dust and fire, swirling out of the ultimate spaces and heavy with perfumes from beyond\n\nthe worlds. Opiate oceans poured there, litten by suns that the eye may never behold and having in their whirlpools strange dolphins and sea-nymphs of unrememberable deeps. Noiseless infinity eddied around the dreamer and wafted him away\n\nwithout even touching the body that leaned stiffly from the lonely window; and for days not counted in men's calanders the tides of ar spheres bare him gently to join the dreams for which he longed; the dreams that men have lost. And in the course of many\n\ncycles they tenderly left him sleeping on a green sunrise shore, a green shore fragrant with lotus-blossoms and starred by red camalotes.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sequoia's Tome", "§bRuby Lorseth", "I write this for whatever form my beloved Druidic Order has taken in the long...long years since my death was reported to you by my sister.\n\nI am, or rather was, the Sequoia Druid, Lillith Winterleaf.  I was guided down the druidic path by Jena, and the\n\nday of my attunement is something I will never forget, for save for the birth of my only child it was the happiest day of my life.  Nature's symphony filled my mind and I was never the same again, I was happier, better, and that joy never left me while I\n\nwas one of you.  I was kidnapped by the Wihuns, whom your younger members I hope have no recollection of, they never left Asulon, my dear husband and others saw to that after they kidnapped me, tortured me, cut out my left eye, cut my left ear in half,\n\nand perhaps the only thing they did that actually truly scarred me in any way I cared, made me infertile by means I will not repeat even now in reflection, that wound still pains me deeply, now that my only son is long lost and I have found no trace of\n\nhim in decades now and I fear the worst, but retain my hope.\n\nThat is not the purpose of this writing...the purpose of this writing is to offer reflection on what I experienced in my time as both a Druid and a Guide of the order long ago.  I never was\n\nable to compile my wisdom and experience for the Druidic Library, I was murdered before I finished them, and the incomplete tome was lost.\n\nYes, I was murdered, by one of the few remaining Wihuns at that, who blamed me and my husband for\n\nthe fall of their order.  I lay dieing in the arms of my sister, Queen of Skravia, Vailoen, and her husband and...my secret lover, whom I shall not name here, she took her life in grief of my death, I learned much later. She was a Dedicant of our order,\n\nwho I came to care for deeply, and I led her to her grave, I wholly blame myself for this.\n\nI awoke what felt like only moments after I'd felt my heart cease to beat in my chest, to a sinister laughter I had not heard in so very long. It mocked me and\n\nhaunted me since I'd first touched that cursed amulet, with a malevolent spirit bound within, the bane of the Johnson family, killer of their royal bloodline for generations.  A mere pendant, with a terrible curse, that I'd helped unbind from the Johnson\n\nbloodline and kept with me for safekeeping...and when I was stabbed my blood soaked upon it and it bound to MY family instead and held my very soul and preserved my body within the tomb my family burried me within.\nUpon awaking, it\n\nsevered my attunement to break me, and show me it had its grip completely upon me, and I despaired.  My sister felt something was wrong, dreamt of me screaming in my grave, which I was, and they dug me up to their complete bewilderment.\n\nThe rest is a long, sad tale that does not need to be told once more.  Suffice it to say that the pendant was eventually destroyed, the evil spirit within finally banished and the gemstone destroyed.\n\nWhat have I learned from all of this?\n\nLife is sacred, always.\n\nLife is temporary, always.\n\nDo with your life everything you can, live without regrets, never put off until tomorrow what you can\n\naccomplish today, for you do not know what tomorrow may bring.\n\nNature is always reaching out to us, after all of these years, though my attunement is shattered, I still feel the slightest pull of places of nature's truest beauty.\n\nIn every land we've fled through, I have found places of natural beauty and peace, completely by instinct alone, and I believe this is the Aspects trying to comfort me still, even though I can no longer hear them.\n\nI believe the Aspects love all of their children within the Order, and will never abandon us, even if we no longer can hear them.\n\nMy experiences as a Druid, a Guide, and now a broken woman faithfully staying at her husband's side even through darkness\n\nhas shown me only this, the Aspects love us, all of us, Druids and non Druids alike.  Their love knows no bounds, and they seek only to show us the way.\n\nI performed healing I never thought I could when I would listen deeply to the songs and melodies in\n\nmy mind, the harmonies of nature itself, and I believe the voice of the Aspects themselves.  I watched the wounds close, even when I could not heal my most grievous wound and could bear no children, my healing seemed to be even stronger, my devotion\n\neasing my pain by helping others.\n\nThis is the wisdom I wish to pass down, the legacy I want to leave for you all.  The Sequoia Druid is no more, I am only Lillith now, but even still I do what I believe the Aspects wanted of me, and I still praise them.\n\nAlways love, for the Aspects love all.  No one is beyond saving, beyond healing, beyond redemption.  Anyone can be healed, both body and mind.  Please, if you take nothing else from this, take the love of the Aspects into your heart.\n\nI await now only the day when I finally lay to rest and can hear the song of the Aspects in my thoughts once more.  I yearn for nothing more than that, and until then I will take the lessons they imparted upon me, and the lessons my dearest Jena taught me\n\nand continue on, guiding those who will listen to reason and love and compassion.\n\n\nI love you all and my heart is always with the Order.\n\n~The former Sequoia Druid\n-Lillith Winterleaf\n\n((A picture of a large sequoia tree is drawn upon this page, highly decorative with black and green inks, and standing at the base is a figure in a green and yellow dress with blonde hair, kneeling over a broken staff))", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Ranks", "§bEloh'ra", "The Druidic Ranks\n\n  - Eloh'ra Rohkea -\n   \n          -==<>==-\n\nThe Druidic Order consists of various ranks which are assigned  to each member as to show their aid to every person of the Order.\n\nDruid(s):\n\n A druid's main duty is to uphold the balance of nature. \nAlthough there is freedom, they musn't do anything that could harm said balance.\n\nWhen becoming a druid, you learn that patience is key and that violence should never be\n\nbe the answer. Although the same person, your views change for as you can now feel the pain of a flower you may step on; the distress from the felling of trees with no replanting occuring and mantaining the balance becomes a way of life.\n\nGuide(s):\n\nAlthough many believe the guide position serves only as a teacher of dedicants, guides also teach newly attuned druids better in the ways of druidism. Such as the proper use of their gifts and the duties required from them.\n\nThe Guide is also a member of the Inner Circle- this means that guides partake in druidic politics; making decisions regarding the order and often assist the archdruids.\n\nWhen a new guide rises to the inner circle or when a dedicant is to be attuned,\n\nguides help decide if they are truly ready.\n\nSmaller duties consist of tending to conflicts in the grove or small amounts of diplomacy. \nA guide doesn't deal with other circles much for it isn't their job to discuss other circle's issues but more theirs.\n\nArchdruid(s):\n\nThe Archdruid oversees the entire Order. They deal with the business of said Order; such as relationships with other nations or in-grove issues.\n\nAn Archdruid oversees the entire teaching branch.\n\nHowever, a system has been worked upon in order to make everything run as smoothly as possible.\n\nAlthough politics are a big part of what the Order becomes, Archdruids make sure that the balanced is mantained, no matter what.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n~ Eloh'ra Rohkea.\n~Dedicant Task 8.", ""),
        StaticBook("\"Notes\"", "§bJan C. Karlowicz", "Alchemy Book Notes\n\n\n-Fall's Book so Do Not Touch!\n\n\n*My knowledge starts on page 6*\n\n*NEW REASEARCH AND START IS ON PAGE 10!*\n\nAls start reading on page 10. \n**It's important that you copy everything from 10 and so on. That information explains the basics of starting Alchemy.**\n\nRuined potion Ingredients\n\n-wheat\n-catcus green\n-dark resin\n-flowers\n-mushroom\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nAwkward Potion Ingredients\n\n-slime ball\n-seeds\n-Bone&bone meal\n-saplings\n-Noodles\n\nBase Ingredients\n\n-Nether wart\n-Glowstone Dust\n-Redstone\n-Fermented Spider eye\n-------------------\n\nSecondary Ingredients\n\n-Magma Cream:Fire Resistance\n-Sugar:Speed\n-Glistering Melon:Instant Health\n-Spider Eye-Poison\n-Ghast Tear:Regeneration\n-Blaze Powder:Strength\n\n-Fermented Spider Eye:Weakness\n-Golden Carrot:Night vision\n-Pufferfish:Water Breathing\n-Rabbit's Foot:Leaping\n-------------------\n\nModifiers\n\n-Redstone:Increases duration\n-Glowstone Dust:Increases potency\n-Gunpowder:Turns into a splash\n-Fermented Spider Eye:Corrupts effect\n-------------------Where research starts --------->\n\nForest:\n\nAlabaster leaf\nBlazing Fern\nBlissfoil\nBlistering Toad\nBlonde Blossom\nCrouching Feather\nFaerie Truffles\nFire Flower\nFlameTongue Root\nKing's Ivy\nNight sap\n\nRed Rose\nSerpents Stalk\nShadeleaf\nThe Crimson Vase\nTippens Root\n-------------------\n\nSwamp:\n\nAqua Nymph\nBiteit\nBlistering Toad\nJailer's Moss\nMandragora\nSaffvil\nSwamp Blossom\nThe Dwarfs Pumpkin\n-------------------\n\nJungle:\n\nAtheros\nFire Flower\nSinged Kha\n-------------------\n\nThundra & Snow Lands:\n\nAthin\nDrilltongue\nElfs Hair Vine\nElrow Berries\nFrost Vine\n-------------------\n\nDesert:\n\nBlack Sun's Spinster\nBlood Lotus\nCactus\nDesert Berry Bush\nDrakes Tail\nGoblins Ivy\nGijaklul\nJaven \nZawabate\n-------------------\n\nCaverns:\n\nBronze Dust\nMiners Helmet\nWisp Breath\n-------------------\n\nOceans:\n\nJokers Scum\n-------------------\n\nOther locations:\n\nLilypad *ponds*\nMelons *Food*\nOrcs Braid *Sewers*\nPotatoes *Farms*\nPumpkins *Farms*\nReeds *near water*\nSpirit Magnolis *Spirit of Nature's body*\nWheat *Farms*\n-------------------\n\nEarth Symbol Items:\n\nAlabaster Leaf\nTippens Root\nSaffvil\nDrilltongue\nDesert Berry Bush\nGoblins Ivy\nBronze Dust\nMiners Helmet\nNight sap\n-------------------\n\nFire Symbol Items:\n\nBlistering Toad\nFlameTongue Root\nThe Crimson Vase\nThe Dwarfs Pumpkin\nSinged Kha\nElrow Berries\nBlood Lotus\nDrakes Tail\nOrcs Braid\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nPotions Made:\n\negg nog: 1 egg\npumpkin juice: pumpkin\npoppy tea: red poppy\nDandelion Tea: dandelion", ""),
        StaticBook("Home", "§bHenry Winterleaf", "Home\n----\n\nA poem by Henry Winterleaf\n\n\nHome is where you hang your hat, or so the saying goes.\nWell, I come from a far off land where it doesn't rain, it snows.\n\nHome is where you feel the safest.\nThe one place in the world that you think is the greatest.\n\n\nHome is family spending time together.\nHaving fun, without the sun, despite the stormy weather.\n\nHome is surrounding yourself with friends.\nSharing laughs together and hoping it wont end.\n\n\nHome is the where you go when you're feeling down.\nTo ease away frustration, removing all your frowns.\n\nHome. Home means different things to everyone.\nSnow, rain, wind, blistering sun.\n\n\nThe place I called home is long gone, but there's no need to cry.\nHome is where I make it, so I don't have to say goodbye.", ""),
        StaticBook("Seebo the Large", "§bFranswaar Kellian", "This is the Legend of Seebo the Large\n\n\n\n\n\n         Written By:\n            Franswaar\n\nThe raggedy old man known as Galendal the pink was in possesion of an evil artifact. Galendal knew an item such as this must be destroyed, but he alone could not do it alone. Galendal sought help from the person best suited for the task at hand, Seebo\n\nGalendal entered the young the halfings house, amazed that such a small creature could have so much clutter. Seebo was found at his desk reading poetry written by famous writer. As Galendal unfolded his tale of the artifact it filled Seebo wonder and\n\nadventure. He thought 'How dangerous could the artifact be.' 'I surely must destroy this so no harm could come to the world.' But during supper Seebo thought he could sell the artifact for quite a fortune. So in the night while Galendal slept peacefully,\n\nSeebo smuthered him whilst grining with his idea in mind. Unfortunatly Seebo made no profit from selling the ring and threw it in a now covered well. Seebo in his annoyance, proceeded to join the Orenian military, where had killed many dwarfs and orcs.\n\nThrough sheer luck and willpower (and the occasional ankle biting) Seebo vanquished his foes and earned the self imposed title of Seebo the Large, not for his size (he was quite the small halfing), but for the size of the piles of burning, rotting,\n\ncorspes in his wake. On the battlefield on brisk morning, Seebo found the small portrait of a dwarven family. Grief overcame the small halfing as he realized he had slain a member of a loving and happy family... and had not slain the rest of the family\n\nSeebo then dedicated the rest of his life to hunting the family. He deserted the Orenian military and massacre anyone that steped in his way. One such group was a tribe of Ologs. Seebo the Large came during the darkest of the blackest night and poured\n\nalchemist's fire down the throat of all the Ologs, except the young prince, Char'Goth, who had been fabled amoung the tribe as the strongest warrior they had ever seen. But with his home destroyed, his family burnt to a crisp, and his titled striped from\n\nhim, Seebo beat the Olog into eternal submission, even death shall not free him of Seebo's wraith. The 9ft Olog, Char' Goth, was taken away by Seebo to be his eternal slave.  With his new found ally, it is said that Seebo still searches for the dwarven\n\nfamily that had escaped Seebo's grasp, for now.\n\nIf you ever happen to pass the fabled Seebo the Large, be very wary, and keep your ankles covered.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pastries Anthos", "§bJistuma", "________________\n~                        ~\n\nPastries of Anthos\n\n\n~----------------~\n\nIn the mind of a child, there will always be a love for the sweet taste of cake. I have traveled the lands of Anthos after my love for pastries grew with my mother’s skill, and family’s bakery. Watching the children laugh and smile whenever they came and\n\nI served them a slice of cake, I decided that bakery would as well be my way of life. With the destruction of my home and bakery in Asulon, I had decided that I would search and try new recipes with the ingredients of all over the realm. I have collected\n\nsome to this recipe book, for others to bring a smile to other children.\n\nCheesecake of Salvus\nBefore the fall of Salvus I was able to try out their ingredients, and create a well rounded cheesecake. Sugar and wheat Dawn’s bakery, the eggs fresh from the farms, and the cheese came from a kind shepherd.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Two hand size goat cheeses\n-A glass full of goat milk cream\n-Three mugs of flour\n-Two soup spoon of refined sugar.\n-One fresh egg\n\nRemember to start heating the oven before you start mixing the ingredients. The Cheese and the cream first have to be added to the flour and mixed until fully clear. I advise that you slowly add the milk as the cheese is mixed with the flour. The sugar\n\nand eggs come after, or they would not mix very well. Mix well with the rest and Grease and flour a pan. The oven should be hot by now, so pour the mix inside the pan, and then the pan inside the oven. When you start smelling a sweet cheese aroma,\n\nthe cheesecake, and serve.\n\nHoney cookies\nI was quite amazed that the elves of Luminaire did not use sugar in their food, curious as that may be, their pastries were still quite sweet. The use of honey was their secret, very pleasant when used in cookies.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-One cup of Redbee honey\n-One duck egg\n-A bit of cinnamon\n-Two cups of forest wheat flour\n\nMakes 16 cookies.\n\nMore than difficult to make, the ingredients are quite hard to find, if you are not an elf yourself. Simply preheat the oven, and mix everything up. It will appear very hard, it is suppose to. With a spoon place on a mix in a pan, and place in the oven.\n\nKrill cake\nThe dwarves sure have some interesting tastes, the Krill cake being one of the most unique pastries I found. A pastry made of ingredients of the sea and eaten with a side mug of Ale.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Two mugs of Ale\n-A mug of Beer\n-Two mugs Whale Blubber\n-Krill, which is a paste of many small crustaceans\n\nThis recipe is quite hard to make. You will have to first preheat oil in a cauldron as you prepare the mix. In a bowl, mix half of the Krill, a mug and a half of the Whale Blubber, and the mug of Beer. Mix until even, it will appear to be a bit slimy. Mix\n\nthe rest of the Krill and Whale Blubber in another bowl, it will be able to hold its form. Place the slimy mix inside the other mix and close it. After making many of them, place inside a fishing net and deep fry in the hot oil. Let cool before you grab\n\na mug of Ale and drink another as you eat the Krill cakes.\n\nPine needle crackers\nEven those recipes that appear quite weird to ever be taught about, I have found. In the south snow mountains of the Urguan kingdom, I discovered the use of pine needles and seaweed in a result of hard crackers.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-A bowl of newborn pine needles\n-Five strips of dried seaweed\n-Two sweet potatoes\n-One cave cow mushroom\n-Two Owl eggs\n\nWithout any wheat in those cold lands, the residents of VaerHaven came up with using potatoes to create their dough. After removing the skin of the potato, cut it as small as you can before you add the egg. Let a few small mushrooms that were taken from\n\nthe back of cave cows, and pour everything into a frying pan. Mix everything up, and let it cook for a moment. When the red bits of the mushroom start turning brown, take the frying pan from the fire and let cool over snow. Not many pine needles are\n\nneeded, only a few newborns. Cut them very small as well and add to the dough. Mix everything up and place them seaweed around the dough. They need a few hours freezing outside, but to my surprise it came up quite sweet.\n\nCactus on a stick\nI would feel bad if I did not add anything about the orchish culture. They do not cook a lot, and it was quite hard to find this recipe, but it was the only one I found.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Long needle cactus\n-A stick\n\nNot a hard recipe, just find and cut a long needle cactus, they are the sweetest of them. You remove the needles and cut the cactus into bits, and stick them in a stick. Then you place over fire and let cook.\n\nCarrot bun\nThe Halflings are exceptional farmers, and what I enjoyed the most about them was the carrots, they were quite delicious. I created a simple recipe while using it.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Three hand sized carrots\n-A glass of milk\n-A few strips of sugarcanes\n-Two fresh chicken eggs\n\nPreheat the oven with Birch coal, which will give a mild aroma of the tree to the buns, but don’t let it heat too much. Skin and chop the carrots into a bowl, and add the milk. Crush some of the sugarcane juice as you stir, the eggs coming at the end.\n\nWith a soup spoon, take the dough and place over the leftover sugarcanes, which are now juiceless. Place everything in a long plate and place in the oven.\n\nCrab pie\nFor last, I present the Kha treats, made of the crabs caught in the waters around their island, and featuring cocoa from their jungle.\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Long Pincer crab\n-Five big Cocoa beans\n-Two mugs of Jungle wheat flour\n-Three parrot’s eggs\n-Blue silk seaweed\n\nA pie fully done with jungle ingredients, for a seafood pie, it is quite lovely. Pre-heating the oven is always a necessity. The Crab and the Blue silk seaweed are found at the shores of the island, the crab having a very long pincer, and the seaweed a\n\ndeep blue, with the texture like silk. The crab is hard to prepare, the shell needing removing so that the soggy meat can be taken. Cut to bits and mixed with the flour from the wheat that naturally grows in jungles. The eggs have to be taken from\n\nparrot’s nests, and they are what is added next. Then mix, it will be very hard. The seaweed needs to be made into the form of the pie, in the oven it will become crusty, so after the form is done, place the dough in it. While the pie is in the oven, pick\n\nup the Cocoa beans and start making it into powder. As soon as you take out the pie, place the cocoa powder over it, the smell will be wonderful.\n\nI hope that many smiles and giggles come while enjoying my recipes.\n\n          Mrs Ole\n\nPastries of Anthos\n\nWritten by Mrs. Ole\nCoppied by Rosso\nCloud Temple of Anthos", ""),
        StaticBook("Tajo's Adventure", "§bTajo S. Torto", "(( https://www.lordofthecraft.net/forums/topic/157569-tajo-s-tortos-amazing-druidic-cave-adventure/ ))", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Fiandria", "§bRindir", "Ode to Fiandria\n          by: Rindir\n\nOde to the great white stag adorned here.\nAnd to the wonderful marvel of those without fear.\n\nOde to the bears, and elk, and 'coons.\nAnd to all the marvelous life that looms.\n\nOde to Fiandria.\n\nOde to the enormous trees so tall.\nIn hopes that they will surely never fall.\n\nOde to the great mystical forest.\nAnd to the flowers loved by a magnificent florist.\n\nOde to Fiandria.\n\nOde to the wildlife that comes and goes.\nAnd to the many plants that grows.\n\nOde to the friends that we may make.\nIn hopes we will always cherish their wake.\n\nOde to Fiandria.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dedicant's Path", "§bAlerad", "Among ice an' fire\nMay be one's deepest\nDesire\n\nTo be one with nature\nA've it protect me\nAnd me protect it\n\nTo live among the moor\nTo sail to every shore\n\nSearching for one to guide\n\nAnd a place to ide by\n\nFor many years they learn\nTo open up ther' ears\n\nTo the sweetest words\nThat nature does say\n\nThey are taught the ways of nature\nWhether they are destined to er not\n\nToo see the meadows\nToo meet new fellows\n\nToo enrich nature\nNot to become rich\n\nTo live in seclusion\nMay just be a delusion\n\nA wave of relief to a young seeker's heart\nOr a moment where the world just seems\n\ntart.\nThis is the path of'a dedicant\n\nOr atleast it was mine\nJust remember, if you want to become un, it better not to waste time!\n-------------------Alerad Geirren,\n1st of Sun's Smile,\n1556", ""),
        StaticBook("Dedicancy", "§bCalius Terin", "Dedicancy Started:\n12th of Grand Harvest\n\nDedicant:\nCalius\n\nTeacher:\nGildun\n\nFirst Task:\nHerbalism Trial\n\nNature comes in many varieties, collect one of each thing that grows in the wilds. Experience and appreciate nature.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nSecond Task:\nGathering the Wool\n\nCollect three bundles of wool, remember to think about which way you do it, and why it is important.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nThird Task:\nBardic Rite\n\nWrite a poem about nature, and how it has a personal connection to you.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nFourth Task:\nThe Aspect Cycle\n\nRead a book on the Cycle of the Aspects, then find an item for each part of the cycle and explain the importance.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nFifth Task:\nAnimal Companion\n\nFind a Companion for life.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nSixth Task:\nThe myth of the Great War\n\nRead the book and interperate how the event effects nature and the world.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nSeventh Task:\nKeeping the Peace\n\nGo two Elvish days without dawning armour or raising a sword, solve problems peacefully.\n\n*Gildun's Signature*\n\nEighth Task:\nGrowing the Tree\n\nContinue to bond with nature by taking a sapling and meditate as it grows.\n\n[!]Gildun's Signature[!]\n\nNinth Task:\nRite of the Staff\n\nMake your staff your own, customize it how you like.\n\n[!]Gildun's Signature[!]\n\nTenth Task:\nLearning the Hierarchy\n\nAsk a Druid, Guide, Archdruid and a Hierophant. Ask why they joined the order then what their position entails.\n\n[!]Gildun's Signature[!]\n\nEleventh Task:\nThe Naming Rite\n\nGo about nature and find a totem for yourself.\n\n[!]Gildun's Signature[!]\n\nTwelfth Task:\nMaintaining the Balance\n\nFind an area that has to much life and find and area that has to little life and fix the imbalance.\n\n[!]Gildun's Signature[!]\n\nThirteenth Task:\nNeo- Druidism\n\nRead Neo-Druidism and make an interpretation.\n\nGrand Task:\nComplete Grand Task\n\nGrand Task is to complete the Druidic Library.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Break", "§bMonk Scribe", "Nature's Break\n\nAs the sun arises,\nthe moon falls,\nas the warmth starts,\nthe cold fades,\nas the leaves are bore,\nthe frost thaws,\nAegis rotates.\n\n- Written by LaTerre Amant in Aegis.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Myths", "§bWayward Druid Respiren", "* The Myth of the *           Great War\nMany moons ago a terrible war broke out between the two mighty guilds of wizards -one good and one evil- that controlled great empires. Wizards on both sides vowed to fight until  utterly triumphant\n\n Seeking to purge their rivals from the land. With fearsome magic and dragon armies they battled for centuries, neither side winning final victory . Through their warfare, the wizards wrought vast devastation on the world,\n\nForests blazed up, islands sank into the sea, entire races became extinct. Eventually, the the great goddess of Nature awakened from her sleep to witness the savage conflict.\n\n Shocked by the destruction, the Goddess sent a vision to a single woman who would become the first Grand Druid. Through the vision, this chosen figure saw that she must found a druidic order to preserve the fragile remains of her world's ecology.\n\n\n\nWith the guidance of her goddess, the  Order grew in strength Until finally it had the power to intervene in the wizard war...\n\nThe force of young druids pooled  their powers and together vanquished the members of both battling guilds, transforming the combatants into innocent wild beasts.\n\nOnce the former wizards now unable to fathom the concept of good versus evil - slithered, bounded, loped and crawled off into their ruined habitats, the Order began to heal the lands.\n\nSince then, the druidic order  continually works to prevent such destruction from ever occurring again. Druids pledge to make sure the wars of good  and evil no longer mar the precious land.\n\n But, the Order also has bitter enemies in the ancient remnants of the  guilds of warring wizards- those good and evil mages who luckily escaped the fate of their fellows.\n\nEach guild claims it had been on the verge of victory and would have won, had druids not interfered. \n\n-As recounted by Hierophant Pulver, Master of Dungeons", ""),
        StaticBook("\"Juicy\" - Poetic", "§bImmortal Cole", "You know very well who you are, \n\nDont let 'em hold you down. Reach for the stars. \n\nYou have a goal,\nbut not that many.\n\nCause you're the only one, \n\nI'll give you good-\n\n-and plenty.\n\nI made the change from a common thief\n\nTo up and close with the eldest elf. \n\nThinking back on my one-room shack.\n\nI still have that old hat -\n\n-With the fat red and black lumberjack, all to match.\n\nNo heat, no wonder nights were cold. \n\nBut I did not give up and I grabbed ahold.\n\n-Cole", ""),
        StaticBook("Shamanism 2", "§bEirik Ireheart", "Notice: These books are rushed copies of tomes in the Library of the Orkish capital, as made during the raid of the 16th of The Grand Harvest, 1532. The following is in no way a complete nor perfect copy of said book.\n\n\"The more they do this, the more word of their feats will spread among the Ancestral plan and more likely it is for these Spirits to acept their deals. Witch-healers go about things differently. During rituals, they'll present certain objects which hold\n\nrelevance to spirits they are trying to evoke. Should they be trying to create a potion that causes insatiable hunger, they would need something akin to the stomache of a large animal, like a bull, or burn and char large amounts of 'savories' in hopes of\n\ngluttonous and avaricious spirits. should they do so, they'll begin to call to their desired spirits in the tongue of Old Orkosian, asking for their aid and primising the representative attributes will be more promenent somehow after the usage of potions.\n\nShould the spirit agree, the mix will be cursed, and the Witch-Healer can make use of it at-will on his victims. However, these Shamans must take care, as it's sometimes not apparent how the spirit cursed the potion, and unintended affects are rampant in\n\nnovice brew. Finally, farseers blessings are similar to that of the Youhumans in the way that it requires bladed tongue an wit to convince the spirits to give them their boon. When Farsers decide he wishes to bless something, he must travel to the spirit\n\nrealm and call the name of the spirit with the representative attribute he needs for the blessing. Should it answer, the Farseer must convince it that the blessing will benefit it. If it fails, the farseer must fight the spirit to make the blessing. Very\n\ndangerous, done only in desperate times, as when a shaman dies in the SPirit realm, he dies in real life. Once he's convinced the Spirit of the need of the blessing, or bested him in single combat, it will then begin to take over his body. Back in the\n\nmortal realm, his body will move on its own, and words in Old Orkosian will be said to give the blessing direction. a mist, hued the color representative of the spirit, will poor from all orifices to accompass the individual to be blessed. When finished,\n\nthe shaman need only to return to his body and admire the handiwork, the fabric of reality being shifted in his favor, albeit very little.\"\n\n[!] *A small ink drawing of an Ork vomitting clouds has been comically drawn here.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Greed", "§bBernard", "~~~~~Greed~~~~~\nBy: Dedicant Bernard\n((http://tinyurl.com/BernardPoem))", ""),
        StaticBook("A Dedication", "§bHreidmar Strongbrow", "-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n Carve for Me a Rock\n \n      A Dedication      \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHreidmar Strongbrow\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nI want to build a hearth\n\nA tribute for my new kin\n\nI need your help,\noh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve again.\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nI need a place of shelter\n\nBeneath the skies of blue\n\nI need desire,\noh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve for you.\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nI want to keep my craftsmanship\n\nWithin your holy\nfount\n\nI need the strength,\noh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve your mount.\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nI have to keep my heart\n\nSecured in my new home\n\nI need the will,\noh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve a dome.\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nI need to feed my\nmind\n\nAnd ensure it is overfilled\n\nI need knowledge,\noh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve the hill.\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\nI need to build a door to you\n\nAnd hold it with a\nlock\n\nI can't do it without you,\n\nCarve for Me a Rock.\n\n -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-", ""),
        StaticBook("Flower Language", "§bElorna Avern Lle'hileia", "The Language\n       of Flowers\n\n\nA guide to the secret meaning given to each flower by the wood elves of old.\n\n    \n      In memory of\n      Mayilu Avern\n\n            ~*~\n\nWhen I was a girl I remember my mother walking with me through fields of flowers, pointing to each variety and describing what they symbolised. I do not know how commonly used this secret language was, but I will always remember the solemnity of my\n\nmother's voice as she told me the meaning behind each poppy and rose. Of course, the meanings given in this book do not describe the actual traits of each flower, simply the cultural meanings they are used to represent. One does not have to use this guide\n\nbut I hope it may inspire you while selecting a bouquet for a lover, picking a gift for a friend, or simply decorating your home and garden with life and color.\n\n\nThe Dove Druid,\nElorna Avern Lle'hileia.\n\n     The Dandelion\nThis simple flower is sometimes considered a weed, yet even that which is small and unassuming can hold hidden significance. \n\nMeanings: Fortitude, Constance, Faith, Wishes Coming True.\n\n        The Poppy\nThis deep red flower is often used medicinally to relieve pain or aid in sleep. It is often associated with both dreamers and the dead.\n\nMeanings: Dreams, Imagination, Mourning, Eternal Sleep, Relief from Suffering.\n\n       The Orchid\nThe orchid is as elegant as it is exotic, and its blue coloring is rare in the natural world.\n\nMeanings: Beauty, Refinement, Elegance, Fragility, Femininity, Rare Riches.\n\n       The Allium\nThese are a very unique looking flower, each one made up of thousands of tiny purple blossoms that form into a large, perfect sphere.\n\nMeanings: Perfection, Royalty, Many Children, a Prosperous Family.\n\n    The Azure Bluet\nSometimes called Eyebrights, they are a pale blue that often appears white. They are sometimes considered a symbol for the Druidic goddess Cerridwen.\n\nMeanings: Fertility, Motherhood, Peace, Cleanliness.\n\n      Oxeye Daisy\nWhen you see these daisies - actually chrysanthemums - in bloom it is always a sure sign of summer.\n\nMeanings: Innocence, Simplicity, Modesty, Shyness, Youth, Summer.\n\n        The Lilac\nLilacs are clusters of tiny purple blossoms that grow on leafy green bushes, and are quite popular for their aromatic scents.\n\nMeanings: New Love, Sweetness, Affection, Flirtatiousness, Spring.\n\n     The Sunflower\nThe sunflower is said to always point toward the sun. We could all learn something from the sunflower about optimism and looking on the bright side!\n\nMeanings: Hapiness, Sunshine, Good Luck, Good News, Friendship, Cheer.\n\n        The Peony\nThese flowers come in soft, pastel hues, their blossoms made up of many layers of petals. They are often associated with the wealthy and high-born.\n\nMeanings: Riches, Honor, Pride, a Carefree Life, Greed, Extravagance.\n\n       The Tulip\nTulips come in many varieties and can mean different things depending on color.\n\nGeneral Meaning: Fame.\nRed: Declaration of Love.\nOrange: Hope, Bright Smiles.\nPink: Purity of Love\nWhite: Honesty, Sorrow.\n\n        The Rose\nThe rose is one of the most iconic flowers of romance and love. Depending on how the rose is presented, it can have many meanings.\n\nFull Rose: True Love, Sincerity, Respect.\nRosebud: Innocent Love, a Pure Heart.\n\n        The Fern\nThe fern is a simple, green plant that bears no bright colors nor scented blossoms. Its reserved simplicity is used as a contrast to dazzling flowers.\n\nMeanings: Old Age, Wisdom, Mystical Knowledge, Sages.\n\n     The Ivy Vine\nIvy is another green plant that, while not a flower, holds significance in the language of flowers. \n\nMeanings: Wedded Love, Fidelity, Faithfulness, Loyalty, Eternal Ties.\n\n     The Mushroom\nMushrooms are not often used in bouquets or arrangements, yet they hold strong symbolism, especially in Druidic circles.\n\nMeanings: Magic, Prophecy, Visions, Virility, Masculinity, Long Life, Spiritual Journeys.\n\n        The Leaf\nThe symbolism and meaning behind each tree would take another book itself, but here are a few meanings associated with tree leaves:\n\nOak: Strength, Nobility, Virtue.\nBirch: Healing, Rebirth, Spirits of the Dead.\nPine: Ancient Wisdom.\n\nAnd so concludes this book, may it broaden your understanding of the natural world and its many hidden intricacies. May your life be long and happy, and may the Aspects guide your path.\n\n\n           ~*~", ""),
        StaticBook("Civility", "§bArial Meadowbloom", "\"They think it is game,\nTo soil the Aspect's name,\nBut little do they know,\nthat the more that they show,\nThe less, in life, they grow.\n\nThe nature of this land\nmust recieve a helping hand\nYet so many times\nNature falls to the crime\nof the people and their slime.\n\nMany people act as though\nthey are in charge, they can go,\nBut what they always forget,\nare the things that they most regret,\nand where civilization and nature met.\n\nThese people must realise\nthat it is the Druids who equalize\nwith all of their precision,\nfor it is always their mission.\nTo save nature is their ambition.\n\nHowever, some certain,\nmay see past the curtain,\nand help the Druids in their quest.\nThese people have passed the test;\nto the Aspects, above the rest.\n\nYet most people in this nature,\nonly bring themselves to danger\nwhen they intend to good,\nand do all they could,\nbut not at all what they should.\n\n\nSomeone should walk around\nto tell of what the Druids found,\nso that most will have respect\nfor the plants that were wrecked\nand those that get neglect.\n\nEveryone could be a Druid if they wanted,\nso that the Druids would not be so haunted\nby plants they could not see,\nby the people they could not be\nand by the sky they could be free.\n\nThough in this day and age,\nthe people are filled with either rage\nor the warmest hearts anywhere,\nso no one knows what is there\nor even what might be fair.\n\nJust because a tree cannot move,\ndoes not mean it does not need room.\nIf only the people knew this,\nlife would surely be bliss,\nand there would be nothing to miss.\n\nTreat nature with honesty is all one asks,\nin life there have been much worse tasks.\nJust water a flower, a decree, \nDo not pick rare herbs for herbal tea\nand once and a while... hug a tree.~\n\n\nThat is the balance of nature, \nuse it well to make the world better.\nSo from this day forth,\nwith or without the North,\nlive with nature in merry mirth.", ""),
        StaticBook("Laurelin", "§bTailesin", "-------------------          Laurelin\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Arinislia\n\nChildren of Laurelin\n\nI hear the song of Laurelin;\nThe Children's laughter singing;\nOf trees, and leaves of many things,\nTo many, the sound of bells ringing\n\n\nI hear the song of Laurelin,\nNow lost in the burning flames;\nThe echos whisper in our hearts,\nNow most don't seem the same;\n\n\nI heard the song of Laurelin,\nof purity and rings;\nOf ones who mix of blood,\nAre met with acid that stings.\n\nI seek the song of Laurelin,\nOf children's laughter and song;\nThe sound almost lost to me now,\nThough somehow I continue on.", ""),
        StaticBook("You are", "§bTailesin", "-------------------           You are\n-------------------\n\n~By Arthur\n\nYou are a tumour, taking me out of my perfect life.\n\nYou are a fire, forcing me out of my home.\n\nYou are at thief, stealing all of my fun and joy.\n\nYou are a killer, killing the immersion.\n\nYou are a fly, around my food.\n\nYou are a parasite, leeching onto my head.\n\nYou are my only clothes, disgusting and ripped.\n\nYou are a sword through my still beating heart.\n\nYou are an ass, dirty and unwiped.\n\nYou are a spider, driving everyone way.\n\nYou are a current, desperate to pull everyone down to your level.\n\nYou are a guard, closing down my party.\n\nYou are an aggressive Orc, capable of only one emotion.\n\nYou are porridge, bland and dull.\n\nYou are the crayon nobody uses.\n\nYou are a made up language or fictional history.\n\nYou are a lump in a comfortable pillow.\n\nYou are an obscenity shouted at a deaf man.\n\nYou are a greedy beggar at my door all day.\n\nYou are the corpse of a king who demands a temple for a resting place.\n\nYou are a pot with no soil that demands a flower.\n\nYou are the loose string that undoes a shirt.\n\nYou are the thunder I hear while trying to sleep.\n\nYou are the crack in a large expensive window.\n\nYou are the loose screw in a banks vault door.\n\nYou are a spot on a fresh piece of paper.\n\nYou are a creaky step in a silent house.\n\nYou are a moth among butterflies.\n\nYou are water extinguishing a torch.\n\nYou are dried up ink.\n\nYou are wilted soil in a drought.\n\nYou are a church without a religion.\n\nYou are.", ""),
        StaticBook("Moonlit Shore", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\n      Moonlit Shore\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Kira Winterleaf\n\nMoonlight on the Shore\n\nThere is moonlight on the shore.\n\nForever more, forever more.\n\nKnocking on the ocean's door.\n\nForever more, forever more.\n\nDancing and prancing and sharing lore.\n\nForever more, forever more.\n\n\"What we we even here for?\"\n\nForever more, forever more.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem", "§bAravis", "The birds chirp, and they sing\nThe blue sky is a beautiful thing,\nThe trees, everlasting, live so long,\nI'd hate to see it when they're gone.\nRabbits, foxes, robins too,\nThe beauty of nature will forever be true.", ""),
        StaticBook("Wolf Watches", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Wolf Watches\n *Unknown*\n\n*The first page of the journal is covered in spots of blood and botches of ink. The ink botches have seemed to have bled over certain sketches around the page. In the center a string of words is underlined\n\nand hastily scribed. \"The Wolf Watches\"*\n\nThe lone wolf stalks away from his packs, away from those whom abandon him, and those who gawked.\n\nThis lone wolf seems to lie upon the minds of sheep, what does he do here, what does he wait fro while his pack members weep?\n\nO' one of the Wolf, O' one whom hunts. Who will claim your ancient pelt, which is sought by those whom wish to cut.\n\nThe hunt, the boon of the hunter, the hunted get no forgiveness, nature's cruel intent striking those whom fall behind.\n\nThough the lone wolf now hunts, as he hears the members of his pack stray away, breaking off from those who lead from the front.\n\nBlood stains the rivers, the grounds, and the hunters. Blood paints in this world as a saunter.\n\n*Sketches litter this page, shapes of blades and of branch. Somehow having diagrams of merging the two, or shaping the said staff with a blade?\n\nIt is the only two things which can be assumed.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Acorn", "§bGil Oronar Sagehand", "-------------------           Acorn\n------------------- \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy: Gilondir Oronar\nSagehand Frostbeard\n\nDown from the sky\nLeft with no clue why.\nThe acorn fell\nUnder gravity's spell.\nOn the grass it landed\nForever stranded.\nLeft there to rust\nIn the earth's crust.\n\nBut then it gave life\nFor the next to thrive.\nIt's roots spreading\nLeaves soon heading\nUp into the sight\nOf the sun's light.\nThe aspects saw down\nNo peeve nor frown.\nFor this young tree\nWas there, was free.\n\nMany years passed\nYet still steadfast.\nThe small oak grew\nInto the skies so blue.\nAnd then one day\nIt's aker couldn't stay.\nAnd another one fell\nunder gravity's spell.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lovers of Strand", "§bTailesin", "===================\n\n   Lovers of Strand\n\n===================\n\n  ~By Freinus\n\nThe slow waves sliding\nover each other,\nPlaying a melodic tune,\nOne after another,\nthey've hewn.\n\nGlinting in the last \nlight of day,\nThen the peaceful\nlight, they betray.\nWhispers turn to\n\nRolling turn to\ncrashing,\nOceans throw walls of\nwater at unyielding\ncoasts.\n\nAnd in this natural\nstuggle, only time\nprevails.", ""),
        StaticBook("Inaam's Poetry", "§bInaam al-Filani", "Falling honey from \nThe comb, sticky and golden.\nthanks to the bees\n\nBlack rose, tall and dark\nMist tangles in thy thorns to \nImpress the watchers\n\nFox is quick as the\ncrow flies, with a snake's cunning\nand rabbit's quick step.", ""),
        StaticBook("Green Guardians", "§bKaitlyn", "Green Guardians }=-\n\nI just love the Trees \n\nThey stand tall, hold     Homes for bees,\n\nWhat a selfless act!\n\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-", ""),
        StaticBook("Waiting", "§bTailesin", "-------------------           Waiting\n-------------------\n\n~By Kira Winterleaf\n\nSometimes I felt like I should be faster, stronger.\nI feel like this no longer.\nMy path is set in stone.\nMy life I would loan to be a Druid.\n\nAttuned to the trees, animals, and bees.\nA protector of nature I want to be.\nBut for now I must wait.\nWith a patient gait.\n\nFor my time to come.\nBut for now I enjoy childhood.\nFor an adult I will not become for a while.\nForever more will not be a bore.\nI will help all those I see!\n\nAnd a Druid I will be.\nI practice writing for Dedicancy", ""),
        StaticBook("Ebony Tree", "§bTailesin", "-------------------        Ebony Tree\n-------------------\n~Author Unknown\n\nThat old ebony tree, beauty to its core.\nIt's withered, decrepit, and wonderful bark peeling upwards \ntowards the firmament of blue and white clouds\nThe old ebony tree yields to an axe, stone, iron, or\nIt is old, strong, but yea, it is old.\n\nIt cannot stand against the sharpness of the cutting tool.\nIts bark gives way to a grit-surfaced black pillar, ready for molding.\nOnce extracted that old ebony tree does not die, oh no, it does not die\n\nThough the ebony tree may be a treasure, its black ebony core is its soul, its very essence\nIt is a gift for those with the skills to carve it and polish it and fletch it into a creation.\nSword handles, tables, brushes, and flutes.\n\nThe smoothness of the wood would remind someone of an elder tree and its resilient wood.\nThe old ebony tree, not the easiest to identify.\nA natural treasure hunt, ay, but the journey is worth the treasure.\n\nHarder than oak, more pulchritudinous than diamond.\nUmbral.\nAwe.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mountains", "§bVosirk Blackaxe", "The Mountains\nWith their bodies of stone,\nAnd their hearts of ore.\nThey sustain us with their bones,\nAnd their hearts ensure ours stay beating.\nThe Mountains\nDwarf and Beast alike call them home,\n\nThey dwell above and below,\nAmong the spruce,\nAnd among the caves.\nDwarf and Beast alike,\ncall the Mountains home.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Druid's Life", "§bTailesin", "-------------------     The Druid's Life\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nThe Druid's Life\n\nEnjoy some stew \nTalk with friends \nWatch the stars \nDozes off to sleep \n\nThe Druid's life, that's for me", ""),
        StaticBook("Dreams of Lilies", "§bTailesin", "-------------------     Dreams of Lilies\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nMay you dream the dream of Lilies, where all your worries cease. Whilst you sleep the sleep of the oak tree full of love, contentment, and peace.\n\nBraids of vines tumble from the hair of the ancient walls. Flowers sprout in fertile places whilst the living pond look like friendly faces.\n\nThe singing sound of the stream rolling over the stones. Beauty in the way the butterfly flutters. The soaring of the great eagle sailing on the great blue waves of the sky.\n\nWeaving through the white peaks and the horse galloping through amber waves of grain. Natures music the whistle of the wind, the rustle of the branches, the hoot of the owl, the river rolling.\n\nMay you dream the dreams of Lilies, where all your worries cease. Whilst you sleep the sleep of the oak tree, full of love, contentment, and peace.", ""),
        StaticBook("Gwendolyn's Poem", "§bGwendolyn Alistar", "How can I repay you, brother mine?\n How can I expect you to forgive?\n Clinging to the past, I shed our blood.\n And shattered your chance to live.\nThough I knew the laws, I payed no heed.\n How can I return your wasted breath?\n\nWhat I did not know has cost you dear,\n For there is no cure for death.\n\n Beautiful mother, soft and sweet,\n Once you were gone we were not complete.\n Back through the years we reached for you,\n Alas 'twas not meant to be.\n\nAnd how can I make amends?\n For all that I took from you?\n I led you with hopeless dreams,\n My brother, I was a fool.\n\n Don't cry for the past now, brother mine.\n Neither you nor I are free from blame.\n\nNothing can erase the things we did,\n For the path we took was the same.\nMy dreams made me blind and mute.\n I long to return to that time.\n I followed without a word,\n My brother the fault is mine.\n\nSo where do we go from here?\n And how to forget and forgive?\n What's gone is forever lost.\n Now all we can do is live.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem of my Life", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\n    Poem of my Life\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nI mine for gold\nI mine for silver\nI swing my arm\nI fish all day\nI fish all night\nI am a dwarf\n\nI am the one and only one of which can fish like me \nI am the one and only who can mine ores like me.\n\nI travel and travel and yet get nowhere but I travel for many years. \nI walk up a road and come back the same.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Aspects", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n       The Aspects\n             by\n          Gi'Garun\n    --------------\n\nFar away in lands afar,\nThe Aspects began their work.\n\nLong ago in times that passed,\nOur Nature began to stir.\n\nThe Green Lady's radiance,\nBlesses the crops.\n\nThe Green Man's strength,\nBlesses the worthy.\n\nFlowers and Fruits and Seasons from her.\nPredators, tigers, hunters from him.\n\nSaviours of the Races,\nCreators of Nature.\n\nThe children respect them,\nThe children shall praise them.", ""),
        StaticBook("Xavier's poem", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n    Dedicant poem\n        by Xavier\n   --------------\n\n\n ~By Dedicant Xavier\n\nThe meadow sits in the forest,\na sea of light in the trees.\nThe flowers wave in the moonlight,\nand dance among the breeze.\n\nAutumn brings colors of fire,\nthough without the flame.\nThe meadow is showered with wonders\nnever to look the same.\n\nSnow rests upon the meadow,\ncovering it’s delicate face.\nBut when springs come again,\nit comes with wonderful grace.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Apprentice", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\n     The Apprentice\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Kira Winterleaf\n\nThe Woodworker's Apprentice\n\nThe Woodworker's Apprentice had long, thing hands.\n\nIn her free time she traveled the lands.\n\nTwisting and twirling her hair in separate strands.\n\nThrough rain and through thunder and toil and blunder she skips and skips and runs.\n\nYet, somehow, she gets all her work done.\n\nOh, I wonder, how through rain and thunder, toil and blunder, the Woodworker's Apprentice does it.\n\nShe widdles and riddles and works.\n\nBut in the shadows I lurk.\n\nWaiting for her to come play.\n\nIt could be any day!\n\nBut no, she roams the land through rain and thunder, and toil and blunder,\nI would die for the Woodworker's Apprentice", ""),
        StaticBook("Empty Bench", "§bAshton", "The empty bench\nMy biggest fear\nWhen will that day come\nThat you're no longer near\n\nYou take my hand\nAnd promise me you'll stay\nBut in my heart\nI'm scared you'll go away\n\nNo one wants you in this place\nExcept for me\nI don't see you as a waste of space\nBut I'm not enough\n\nOne day you'll go out on your own\nNever to return\nAnd I'll be left alone\nWith the empty bench", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature", "§bTailesin", "-------------------           Nature\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Ierven Camoyrn\n\nNuts and beans\nAcorns and leaves\nTree-pecking birds\nUrchins with spines\nRhinos with horns\nEveryday!\n\nTrees with apples\nRocks with moss\nEvery tree has a leaf\nEvery rock is stoney\nGrasshoppers\nRabbits\nAcorns\nSnakes\nSnails\n\nShrooms red\nHow brown?\nRed I said!\nOr brown?\nOr red!\nMaybe both.\n\nSpiders are cool\nPrickly sometimes\nI know this for sure\nDirt is their friend\nEveryone misunderstands\nRory the poor spider", ""),
        StaticBook("Abandon", "§bTailesin", "-------------------          Abandon\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Chirr Mantisuku\n\nYou left me when we were young.\n\nLeft me with my falling tears.\n\nI felt like I was no one.\nFor so many painful years.\n\nI cried at night wishing you were there.\n\nHow could you do this to me?\n\nDid you not see how that was unfair?\n\nLife was a horrible mess.\n\nMy heart so bad I couldn't rest.\n\nFalling tears that fall.\n\nShow that I've been through it all.\n\nThe cause you leaving me.\n\nWe are together now but it isn't the same.\n\nHeart aches and you are to blame.\n\nIf you saw me crying maybe you'd regret what you've done\n\nRight now I will live my life as no one.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Watcher", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n     The Watcher\n  ---------------\n\n ~By Dedicant                  Thickfeet\n\nNight falls and a watcher awakens\n\nFor soon she will find one to be taken\n\nTime always manages to fill its toll\n\nBut or the watcher, it merely plays a role\n\nHow can such a creature be so ancient?\n\nIt must be ever careful, forever patient\n\nThere is no protection, not even for kings\n\nFor those that scurry beneath the raptor's wings\n\nThroughout the world there is so much clatter\n\nBut the watchers wait for a very specific patter\n\nEyes of gold sweep through the trees\n\nSeeking out the creatures, that which they will seize\n\nThere are many possibilities, but it must be right\n\nBefore this hunter can truly take flight\n\nA very slight stirring, and then an exposure\n\nThe watcher will soon have in it's grasp full closure\n\nA push and lift, she takes to the air\n\nA target is found, they left themselves bare\n\nShe swoops forth, like a flash of lightning\n\nA sight so elegant, but so ever frightening\n\nThe distance shinks as she glides almost to totter\n\nMoving so swiftly, on a mission of slaughter\n\nWithout a sound, claws snatch it away\n\nSuccess is achieved, the watcher took its prey\n\nReturning to the branches, a home so secure\n\nShe feasts on her victim, a reaping so pure\n\nNight after night the watchers ever prowl\n\nLiing up to high fame, of the great horned owl.", ""),
        StaticBook("Helios' Poem", "§bTailesin", "-------------------        Helios' Poem\n-------------------\n\n~By Helios\n\nO, They say,\nThat under the fae,\nExist a group of individuals,\nThat serve beatles,\nAnd hug trees.\n\nO, Morons you say,\nMaybe we are,\nBut before you draw conclusions,\nYou should know,\nWe ain't a random bunch.\n\nO, Most of time anyway,\nWe have our rules,\nAnd our rulers,\nOne's quite the lass,\nLady of the Harvest.\n\nO, That'd be Cerridwen,\nBut there's another,\nCernunnos,\nYou heard me right,\nDeity o' the hunt.\n\nO, Now you know our lords,\nAspects wee call 'em,\nTreat 'em right,\nAnd they'll be kind in return,\nSupply you with the good harvest.\n\nO, And hides,\nTo keep yours warm,\nAnd that's all folks,\nIf you've changed your mind about us,\nThat's lovely,\nBut if not...\n\nO, You can piss off!", ""),
        StaticBook("Thalon's Poem", "§bTailesin", "-------------------       Thalon's Poem\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Thalon\n\nAll through the cold,\n\nNever gone and never old,\n\nIt creeps like the men of earth,\n\nWho hide deep within their holds,\n\nIt grows slowly like a newborn fawn,\n\nAnd extends its grasses at the break of dawn,\n\nTo soak in light to help it grow,\n\nSo it may live on the tree like so,\n\n*A picture of a tree is sketcher here with a bit of moss growing on one side of the tree.\n\nNever gone... Never old...", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Ode", "§bTailesin", "-------------------        Natures Ode\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nMorning springs with hope anew\n\nTo melt the chill of winters dew.\n\nThe great tree wakes from slumbers peace\n\nWhile blooms of colour shows their fleece.\n\nNo more will winter's chill cause the world to still.\n\nWhy you ask, should spring flow in such a way\n\nTis the circle of which things grow, death of one another to sew\n\nThere you have the poem nature's ode\n\nSilence to follow while darkness calls, life stills beneath the shadows\n\nThe third page reached so no more shall follow.", ""),
        StaticBook("Civility", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n         Civility\n  ---------------\n\n ~By \n   Arial Meadowbloom\n\nThey think it is a game,\nTo soil the Aspect's name,\nBut little do they know,\nThat the more they show,\nThe less,\nIn life,\nThey grow.\n\nThe nature of this land,\nMust receive a helping hand,\nYet so many times,\nNature falls to the crime,\nOf the people and their slime.\n\nMany people act as though,\nThey are in charge, they can go,\nBut what they always forget,\nAre the things that they most regret,\nAnd where civilization,\nAnd nature met.\n\nThese people must realise,\nThat it is the Druids who equalize,\nWith all of their precision,\nFor it is always their mission,\nTo save nature,\nIs their ambition.\n\nHowever some certain,\nMay see past the curtain,\nAnd help the Druids in their quest.\nThese people have passed the test;\nTo the Aspects,\nAbove the rest.\n\nYet most people in this nature,\nOnly bring themselves to danger,\nWhen they intend to good,\nAnd do all they could,\nBut not at all,\nWhat they should.\n\nSomeone should walk around,\nTo tell of what the Druids found,\nSo that most will have respect,\nFor the plants that were wrecked,\nAnd those that get neglect.\n\nEveryone could be,\nA Druid if they wanted,\nSo that the Druids,\nWould not be so haunted,\nBy plants they could not see,\nBy the people they could not be,\nAnd by the sky,\nThey could be free.\n\nThough in this day and age,\nThe people are filled with either rage,\nOr the warmest hearts anywhere,\nSo no-one knows what is there,\nOr even what might,\nBe fair.\n\nJust because a tree cannot move,\nDoes not mean it does not need room.\nIf only the people knew this,\nLife would surely be bliss,\nAnd there would be,\nNothing to miss.\n\nTreat nature with honesty,\nIs all one asks,\nIn life there have been,\nMuch worse tasks.\nJust water a flower, \nA decree,\nDo not pick rare herbs,\nFor a herbal tea,\nAnd once and a while...\nHug a tree.~\n\nThat is the balance of nature,\nUse it well to make the world better,\nSo from this day forth,\nWith or without the North,\nLive with nature,\nIn merry mirth.", ""),
        StaticBook("O Nature", "§bTailesin", "-------------------   O Nature, O Nature\n-------------------\n\n~By Dedicant Felix\n\nNature, Nature, Ode to Thee\nIt is only you that I can see.\nNature, Nauture, Tell me Why.\nHow can you create such Blue Skies?\n\nNature, O Nature,\nTell me Right.\nSo that you many cleanse my Impure Sight.\nNature, O Nature,\nLend an Ear.\nSo I can hear what you say Most Clear.\n\nNature, Nature\nshow me How.\nTo properly honor\nyou with a Bow.\nNature, Nature, Tell me Now.\nSo I may recite\nyour solemn vows.\n\nIn the Aspects,\nI shall Pledge.\nFrom Here and Hereafter,\nI shall be walking on the edge.\nFor you alone,\nMy Soul is yours.\nThe Aspects have called,\nLet us open the Door.", ""),
        StaticBook("Neutrality", "§bTailesin", "-------------------         Neutrality\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Dedicant Aeran\n\nThe Trees fall\nBabies crawl\n\nTreaties exchanged\nNature is unchanged\n\nFor it does not care\nAs it would Appear\n\nNature does not care\nabout your quarrels\nNor your battles\n\nIt cares not about pride\nIt has no side\n\nAs long as Balance remains\nDruids Need not complain\n\nNeutrality", ""),
        StaticBook("Hedgehogs", "§bTailesin", "-------------------         Hedgehogs\n------------------\n\n\n~By Andria Barbu\n\nHedgehogs are great\nHedgehogs are neat\nThey are cute when they want something to eat.\nHedges hogs are fun\nHedgehogs are small\nHedgehogs comes when you give them a call.\n\nHedgehogs are the best\nHedgehogs are better than all the rest\nHedgehogs looks cute hiding in a chest", ""),
        StaticBook("Feather Sonnet", "§bTailesin", "-------------------  Sonnet of a Feather\n-------------------\n~By Dedicant Tin\n\nSo free I fly through the night and the day.\nSo I may see all that would pass by me.\nAnd so I carry on, the breeze my way.\nAs always remaining utterly free.\n\nI remember a time, on a white bird.\nI flew onward with my very own kin.\nBut at the time, I was justly conferr'd;\nIt was that I recieved my freedom then.\n\nNot being on the bird is a blessing.\nI may go on my own way at all times.\nThough, with others there is much less stressing.\nAnd I wouldn't face alone, Death's bell chimes.\n\nIn the still of night, I sometimes still pray;\nthat I may be back on that bird someday.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature Poems", "§bTailesin", "-------------------       Nature Poems\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Mith\n\n     -=The Pond=-\n\nA light pitter patter\nBreaks the night.\n\nA dragonfly,\nit rests for flight.\n\nThe Bullfrog croaks its final song,\nAs the moon begins to yawn.\n\nAll is quiet, all is still.\nFor the pond has had her fill.\n\nThe crane, it rests, its weary head, \non the makeshift water bed\n\nEven the lark,\nits chest so bright,\nquiet its song,\njust for the night\n\nAll is quiet, all is still\nFor the pond has had her fill.\n\nBut suddenly a sharp \"HOO-WOO, HOO-WOO\".\nA flash of wings,\na tiny squeak.\nThe Owl grasps the mouse, so meek\n\nThe Bullfrog lets loose a croak,\nbut soon falls down, but soon falls down the old Crane's throat!\n\nThe meadowlark, it takes to flight, away and deep into the night.\n\nThe dragonfly it zips away\nTo be seen another day.\n\nNone is quiet, none is still.\nEven the pond can't have her fill\n\nFor each night brings,\na cacophonic storm,\nfor that is Nature,\nin its purest form.\n\n      -=The Wind=-\n\nA measly plant, so small in grain, begins the lively train.\n\nThe sets they blow, across the field, a crop that only a mouse can yield.\n\nThe mouse swallows the seeds, and runs, for he is not the only one,\nthat heeds the winds of old\n\nThe Owl, he rides as darkness drops, the wind his only ally throbs,\nand covers the noise his kill spills.\n\nBut back to the seed, for it grows so tall, crop for one, start for all.\n\nThe Deer, it grazes on the grass, grown by wind and rain, at last.\nThe wind carries the scent to his nose, a scent he all too well knows\n\nThe Wolf, it hunts the little field deer.\nRelying on the wind for him to steer,\nin the right direction for his meal.\nHe lays down at night, his muzzle bloodied, thanking the wind, his only buddy.\n\nFor this is how the wind is here,\nand there,\nand far,\nand near.", ""),
        StaticBook("Beeutiful", "§bTailesin", "-------------------          Beeutiful\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author unknown\n\nIn their small home they will live and die.\nA pointless life,\nWhere they work and they lie.\n\nWho knows why they work all day\nSooner or later,\nFor in the ground they must lay.\n\nDespite the sad circumstance,\nThey are unmoved.\nUnafraid, they will never lower their stance.\n\nIn such tiny bodies, there is something to show.\nHidden from most,\nThere is a great power that we must know.\n\nThe lesson is that of great willpower,\nWithout rhyme or reason,\nThey will never give up, it’s true, they never cower.\n\nNot a wise decision for one to provoke\nFor in their hearts,\nthere’s much power they invoke.\n\nLive life simply and you too will be strong.\nFor in your heart,\nSometimes you must decide what is right and wrong.\n\nLife is not about achievement, nor glory.\nRemember that,\nSooner or later none will remember your story.", ""),
        StaticBook("Friendship Leap", "§bTailesin", "-------------------    Leap of Friendship\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nTwo frogs leapt,\nWhile many slept,\nThe lone lilypad floating,\nOne frog over the other,\nOne just after another,\nThe lilypad held them without gloating.\n\nMany days pass,\nThe frogs increase in mass,\nLone lilypad weeps sinking more and more,\nThe froggy pair frown,\nNot wanting lilypad to drown,\nThe creek soon turns to silence.\n\nMany days pass,\nThe frogs increase in mass,\nLone lilypad weeps sinking more and more,\nThe froggy pair frown,\nNot wanting lilypad to drown,\nThe creek soon turns to silence.", ""),
        StaticBook("Seasons", "§bTailesin", "-------------\n        Seasons\n    -------------\n\n  ~By Dedicant Sevisa\n\nHere I stand among the blooming meadows\nYour touch is fleeting,\nlight and playful.\nIt runs through my hair \nand across my cheeks.\nI can barely feel your touch, love.\nLooking skyward towards your pale blue hues\nI reach giving, lo\n\nMoments past and I continue to implore\nand eventually you comply your touch\nbecomes almost tangible\nleaving me breathless and in awe.\n\nI look to your ever deep blue hues\nand watch as they darken,\nflashing dangerously and accompanied\nby a deep thundering growl.\n\nTaking shelter from the sudden flurry of emotion, I watch as your tears water the earth, nourishing your brothers and sisters.\nAnd I think to myself, love.\n\nHow much you give and never ask.\nI revel in the strength of your emotions, dear.\nYour power never ceases to amaze me.\n\nIt bothers me not to stay by your side.\nTo comfort you until the very last deluge.\nYour eyes change now, I can see it. From dark blue to hazy grey.\n\nYour touch still remains as always. Though now it is tinged with a sadness. A cooler touch, and I know, soon something is coming.\n\nYour hair begins to change, now.\nFrom the deepest of greens, to fiery reds, oranges, and yellow. I watch in reverence, as these tones shower down around me, turning brown and withering away to dust.\n\nThis change does not scare me, darling. I’ll stay here forever more.\nA shiver runs through my being, as your touch turns frigid, your hair now blanketed in white.\n\nHolding my arms out for that last embrace, I watch as that light goes out in your eyes, and pledge that I will be there, evermore. Among the blooming meadows,\nwaiting for your light hearted touch, and the warmth to return to your eyes.", ""),
        StaticBook("Descending Leaf", "§bTailesin", "-------------------   Descending Leaves\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nAs the tree grows older, its roots wither and from the lack of nutrient the tree, too, withers. The tree, stiff and hollow, is parched and barren. Its leaves descend, becoming fewer and fewer. Like the leaf, I descend.", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Balance", "§bTailesin", "-------------------     Natures Balance\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nAlike the six descendant races,\nThere exists six trees to hold their places:\n\nOak,\nBirch,\nSpruce,\nJungle,\n\nAnd according to the tale\n\nAcacia,\nAnd dark oak are said to hail\n\nThe trees can be appointed to the six,\nAnd with that nature will achieve it's balanced fix:\n\nOak to Human,\nBirch to Elf\nSpruce to Dwarf,\nJungle to Kha',\nDark Oak to Orc,\nand Acacia to Halfling\n\nAnd with these six beautiful trees,\nThe rest of the world will see,\nThe wondrous forest created by thee", ""),
        StaticBook("Potato Poem", "§bTailesin", "-------------------        Potato Poem\n------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nOh potato, I love you so.\n\nCan be boiled, mashed and put in a stew. \n\nSo many choices, it's hard to know what to do!\n\nI dream of you often, which always makes me hungry.\n\nSo I wake up and eat you for my tea.\n\nOh potato, please never leave me.\n\nSigned, the halfling with the big tummy.", ""),
        StaticBook("Asulon Meadows", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n   Meadows of Asulon\n   ---------------\n\n ~By Dedicant \n    Anthony Hearthal\n\nIn the meadows of asulon\nThere are many plants and trees\nAnd most of the grass grows up, passed your knees\nIn the deepest sea of the world\na massive fish did wonder\n\nwhy men would dive so far down under\nWell the truth is simple they just wanted to plunder\n\nLike magic trees and humming bees the world also has many secrets, where it hides treasure, the beauty of nature and soulmates that one desires.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Ocean Ode", "§bTailesin", "-------------------      The Ocean Ode\n-------------------\n\n\n~Unknown author\n\nOh Blessed Aspects, you take us on the sea.\nFrom this land to the next, protect us is my plea.\nThe breeze ruffles by me, and by my allies too.\n\nOh Blessed Aspects, I beg you to guide us through.\nAnd though the journey is hard, by day and night we go!\nThe waves are gentle, the wind is subtle, all to flee the snow!", ""),
        StaticBook("A Journey", "§bTailesin", "-------------------         A Journey\n-------------------\n~By Dedicant \n       Navarion Calm\n\nAs my feet take me to the forest's edge,\nAway goes my worries and feelings of dread.\nBirds chirp above while they soar through the sky,\nBehind me lay the fields of wheat and rye.\n\nI travel over to a tall and mighty oak,\nWho've I known to have not one branch broke.\nTracing a bare hand alongside its bark,\nMy eyes glancing up towards the hidden branches in dark.\n\nBeginning my ascent , I treat the oak full of care,\nAnd pull myself up to the branch, my feet in midair.\nI balance myself, but almost slip,\nNearly striking the branch's fresh lip.\n\nA moment is taken to regain what is lost,\nMy hands grasping the next branch, but crossed.\nI pull myself once more towards the sky,\nWith no more intentions of my route going awry.\n\nAs I reached the top of the faithful tree,\nLooking around at the vast land before me.\nThe view, stunning, the sky could leave one in awe.\nSurrounded by only the farmland's maw.\n\nI stayed for what felt like days.\nMy eyes grown tired, my mind in a haze.\nA voice rings out from a nearby home,\nCalling for me to no longer roam.\n\n\nI climb down from the forest's top,\nGoing from branch to branch with but not one stop.\nMoon replaces sun, now dark that was once bright,\nNature's majesty now glowing in the night.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Calling", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n    Nature's Calling\n   --------------\n\n ~By Dedicant \n         Llumaria\n\n\nNature calls through the wind and trees,\nIt calls with every buzzing bee.\nIt is to me as gulls to the sea.\nA most basic attraction, a harmony.\n\nThe rabbit grows by eating grass,\nBut grows to feed the teeming mass,\nOf predators that roam the ground,\nWho will someday become a mound.\n\nAnd from the fertile soil and dirt,\nNew life will surely come to spurt,\nTo help again, the rabbit grow,\nThus keeping on life's constant flow.\n\nThe way of life and death goes on.\nIt is natures call. It is nature's song.\nIt is to me as leaves on a tree.\nNature is a living part of me.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dragonfly", "§bTailesin", "-------------------         Dragonfly\n-------------------\n\n~By Dedicant Daern\n\nI watched a simply Dragonfly,\nWatched it as it grew,\nI then began to realize,\nWhy it was blue.\n\nA tone darker than water,\nUnlike all its friends.\nThe summer day grew hotter,\nMuch like the sun it began to ascend.\n\n\"Fly Dragonfly, Fly!\" I cheered,\n\"Grow and Be safe!\" I hoped,\nWith a blink of an eye, it appeared,\nAnd on my nose it began to cloak.\n\nA sign, a gift, from my friend,\nOn this summer day,\nI hope this day will never end,\nSomehow I think it may.\n\nThe Day ends, the Dragonfly leaves,\nOur hearts connected as one,\nI pick myself up on Night's Eve,\nHaving had my fun.\n\nIt's not until I realize,\nThat I was not alone.\nFor on my shoulder was a set of eyes,\nA crystal-like it shone.\n\nI walked off once more,\nWith my new friend.\nAway from the shore,\nThe day had come to an end.", ""),
        StaticBook("Stone and Claw", "§bTailesin", "-------------------     Stone and Claw\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nOf Stone and Claw\n\nI have walked the mountains in my anger.\n\nI saw nothing, and everything, and became aware.\n\nI have walked the forests in my sorrow.\n\nThe sounds gave me peace, and a path to follow.\n\nI have followed the rivers in my confusion.\n\nThe babbling streams washed all my illusion.\n\nI have walked the plains in my grief.\n\nThe animals accepted me, in my respect and belief.\n\nI have walked the caves, deep and cold.\n\nI found things wonderous, ancient, and old.\n\nI have walked the valleys with my kin.\n\nI will not forgive the deeps ones for their sin.\n\nI have walked the hunter's path, the grizzly found me.\n\nWe fought, we fell. I used it to survive, showed respect and humility.", ""),
        StaticBook("Phaedrus' Poem", "§bTailesin", "-------------------Phaedrus' Dedicant Poem\n-------------------\n~By Dedicant \n       Phaedrus\n\nI know a world of iron and coal, \nAt times battles and their blood, \nCommanding archers upon the grassy knoll, \nOr traversing trenches filled with mud,\n\nOnly fitting since my trade, \nFuels industry and war, \nBut what's changed my life most was not a blade, \nInstead it was a mere door,\n\nWithin the Grove I met someone, \nWho sought my more peaceful skill-set, \nAnd when the job had been done,\nI found myself not wanting to leave yet,\n\nShe shared with me a patient ear, \nA gentle voice and a soft hand, \nMy usual worries would disappear,\nDuring my time on that unscathed land,\n\nAs time went on I came to know peace,\nAmong trees and grass and shade, \nAs time went on I came to know love,\nDuring a dance after the masquerade,\n\nAs we grew closer I came to learn, \nHer love of Nature and Life, \nShe made my heart leap and burn, \nSo I asked her to be my wife,\n\nNow I have grown used to, \nWearing cloth instead of plate,\nWaking up besides eyes a deep blue, \nRather than the side of a gate,\n\nNow comes a new chapter, \nWithin my long-lived life,\nTo try and promote and spur,\nPeace instead of strife,\n\nMy love of Nature steadily grows, \nIn likeness to any plant,\nNow this writing has come to a close, \nThank you for listening to my poetic rant,", ""),
        StaticBook("Smurgla's Poem", "§bTailesin", "-------------------      Smurgla's Poem\n-------------------\n\n~By Smurgla\n\nEveryone has a purpose. Love yourself.\n\nEveryone have a trooth. Believe yourself.\n\nEveryone have a voice. Listen to yourself.\n\nEveryone have a friend inside. Trust yourself.\n\nEveryone have a path to walk. Follow yourself.\n\nEveryone have a purpose. Stick to yourself.\n\nEveryone have a reason. Do not listen to no one else for yourself is all that matters.", ""),
        StaticBook("Stormy Days", "§bTailesin", "-------------------       Stormy Days\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nRainy days\nStormy days \nLightning crashes overhead\nWind whips around\nThunder blasts down\nGales roar\nPanic soars\nNature clashes with itself\nTime will calm this war\n\nSee the sky\nHear it's cry\nTeardrops falling to the ground\nColor has fled\nGreyness has spread\nFlee this place\nFlee this place\nSadness leaking overhead\nTime will soothe this war\n\nRaindrops fall\nRaindrops fall\nClouds are bawling endlessly\nDarkness has spanned\nFlooding this land\nStanding tall\nThrough the fall\nLight is peering through the clouds\nTime will calm this war\n\nMirthful tears\nMirthful tears\nSunlight beams through lawlessness\nRaindrops still fall\nSoothing this brawl\nHigh above\nUp above\nSpread across the gleaming sky\nTime will end this war", ""),
        StaticBook("Autumn", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\n         Autumn\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Hare Druid\n      Taynuel\n\nThe leaves are losing shade again\n\nFading to golds and reds\n\nThe flowers have all gone to sleep\n\nSweet nothings in their heads\n\nThe wind blusters o'er hill and dale\n\nThe weather quickly chilling\n\nPreparing for the winter's dawn\n\nAnd frosty cold unyielding\n\nThe bears and squirrels make final moves\n\nPrepared for their long slumber\n\nFinding their caves and nests in trees\n\nIn which all will be warmer\n\nYounglings laugh and play together\n\nIn piles of fallen brush\n\nAll bundled up in coats and scarves\n\nNoses and cheeks all flush\n\nIt's time to say goodbye to summer\n\nThe warmth, the growth, the sunshine\n\nAlthough the clouds gather overhead\n\nWhat's coming can be divine\n\nSnow gathers on each rooftop once\n\nThe coloured season is through\n\nTime for loved ones to cuddle up\n\nTogether till spring starts anew.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Dedication", "§bTailesin", "-------------------       A Dedication\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nCarve for Me a Rock\n\nA Dedication\n\nHreidmar Strongbrow\n\nI want to build a hearth\n\nA tribute for my new kin\n\nI need your help, oh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve again.\n\nI need a place of shelter\n\nBeneath the skies of blue\n\nI need desire, oh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve for you.\n\nI want to keep my craftsmanship\n\nWithin your holy fount\n\nI need the strength, oh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve your mount.\n\nI have to keep my heart\n\nSecured in my new home\n\nI need the will, oh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve a dome.\n\nI need to feed my mind\n\nAnd ensure it is overfilled\n\nI need knowledge, oh Aspects,\n\nI want to carve the hill.\n\nI need to build a door to you\n\nAnd hold it with a lock\n\nI can't do it without you,\n\nCarve for Me a Rock.", ""),
        StaticBook("To Mommy", "§bTailesin", "-------------------        To Mommy\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nMother, I will be there before the break of light.\nLife's a hard journey full of noise and fright.\nBut like a warrior, we all must fight.\n\nLet your ideas soar and take flight.\nLife won't let us slack, not even in slight.\nBut we're still together in spirit, and that's alright.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Will", "§bIthuriel", "A wolf displays the fact it found a hare;\nThe unlucky rabbit will be consumed.\nA spider weaves its web in hope of fare;\nthe fly avoids what it knows is its doom.\n\nThe rabbit had produced a group of young;\nthey breed with speed, these hares stalked by the fox.\nThe flies naturally lay eggs in dung;\nor a body left by the river's stalks.\n\nAll these are part of the Aspects' good plan,\nthat I, a dedicant, would see maintained.\nTo serve nature and to be its guardsman,\nthese things that the Aspects did preordain.\n\nThis cycle, death and life, will come around;\nin time, the author, resting, will be found.", ""),
        StaticBook("Under the Willow", "§bLarry Shortoak", "§    §I  A Poem\n     \n     Under the\n        Willow\n\n  By Larry Shortoak\n\nDo you remember\nwhen we named each other love\nbeneath the willow?\n\nWe taught each other\nto believe in forever\nand even longer.\n\nWe knew this would never end,\nwe could evade time,\nUnder the Willow.\n\nUnder the summer sun, we shared tales from time long since faded away. \n\nYou asked what I believe in, I told you the Aspects, beneath the willow.\n\nWe found answers to all of our greatest questions in each others arms.\n\nWe called it our own happily ever after, beneath the willow.\n\nThen Malin's Welcome sun began to set and the leaves of the willow, yellow and faded.\n\n\n\n\n      For my love\n      where ever\n      you may be.\n\n        Miss you.", ""),
        StaticBook("Voyage", "§bTailesin", "-------------\n         Voyage\n    -------------\n\n   ~By Griffith \n         du Mont.\n\n      -=Foreword=-\nThis book is originally from Asulon, and was first found on the Island of \nArmaluna, in the Bards College's Library. It was restored and brought to\nAnthos by Elorna Avern.\n\n       -=Voyage=-\nUnfurl the sails and off we go,\nDivine wind will gently blow,\nStirring waves t'rock us to and fro,\nGuiding us the stars that glow.\n\nShining brightly with the moon in tow,\nTowards the horizon they'll come to show,\nA land where times ever slow,\nThat eases your heart and quells your woes.\n\nBut for now we rest in the deck below,\nWe'll reach paradise before you know!", ""),
        StaticBook("Adunian Poems", "§bHamish", "Open your eyes and watch the sky,\nto see the blue and the white,\nand view the stars that roam up above\nand watch the birds in flight.\nTo see the seasons change with time\nand the people grow and age,\n\nto see the kindness in their hearts\nand their dark eyes that hold their rage.\nOpen your eyes to notice the difference\nbetween the rumours and praise,\nfrom the people who honestly care\nto those that could not be more blasé.\n\nTo notice the change from black to grey\nfrom purple to blue, orange to red,\nand see green turn to yellow,\nthe rich comforted and the hungry get fed.\nOpen your eyes to watch the pain\nthat others hide from the rest.\n\nThey might be the man on the street\nor the masquerade, best dressed. \nNotice the seasons wash the air new\nto make room for their colours, \nand make the people change their clothes\nas well as watch out for others.\n\nOpen your eyes to their lives going by,\nto their hopes flickering and fading,\nthe things they want most leaving\nand their dreams gone from trading.\nWatch out for a puddle of tears\nnear a gravestone of the loved,\n\nand see those who longed to be touched\nas they keep their fingers gloved.\nOpen your eyes to see the compassion\nthat seems to flow in this realm,\nfrom the caring and the kindness\nto the comfort that can overwhelm.\n\nBut also take note of the depression\nthat sits at the bottoms of the lakes, \nthat rots on the tree branches\nand in the corners of the crates.\nOpen your eyes to see what I see, \nthe final realisation;\n\nthat with every new begining\ncomes yet another civilisation.\nBut just rest and take it all in,\nwith your eyes wide open\nand see this shattered realm like me,\nwith my heart completely broken.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nShattered,\nThe fact that I am broken,\nBroken,\nEven with what I said;\nTattered, \nMy heart is thankful for use,\nSmashed,\nEven though all out my heart bled.\nHalting,\n\nMy world, but seems to keep spinning,\nCrushed,\nAs my mind searches for support;\nImperfect, \nHe is gone forever as they said,\nUneven, \nAnd I did not have the energy to retort.\nDisturbed,\n\nThey told me, when I asked what happened,\nSnapped,\nThey said, when he fell with my heart,\nDefeated,\nHe was, as he was carried outside,\nCracked,\nThey described he was not that smart.\n\nStopped, \nas I cannot hear it anymore,\nDestoryed,\nmy heart to the very core,\nAltered, \nas I think its beat has changed,\nDefective,\n\nand it has made me beyond poor. \nIs,\nwhat a time that this has been,\nWhat,\nI have been through is too much,\nI,\nonly wanted to see him again,\nFeel,\n\nhis heart and his touch.\nMy,\nThree, dear Three, bless him,\nElton,\nfor he was my net under the ledge,\nIs,\nhe really, truly with you now?\n\nDead,\nand gone, straight over the edge?\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nHis green eyes never leave my gaze\nand his beard will always be brown,\nhis kilt never lost its colour\nas he kept not a trace of a frown.\nHis high stature has yet to be forgotten\nwhat with his boom laughter and grin,\n\nand his highspirits not only with his family\nbut also in the tavern with his gin.\nHis comforting words kept me going\nas his form took shape in my mind,\nfor it was I who kepts his memories,\nit was I that he never left behind.\n\nI cannot say I enjoyed all of it\nfor that man saw too many wars,\nbut he also had his moments of triumph,\nand those he loved dearly of course.\nHe kept his mind straight and level\neven under the most pressure,\n\nhe always knew where his place was\nand respected those who were lesser.\nSometimes he forgot right from wrong,\nbut then again, we all to at some point,\nand yet he knew how to keep people motivatied\neven though he was hard to dissapoint.§\n\nAnd though I miss him still to this day,\nwith years gone since he passed,\nI still wish Hanrahan was here with me,\nbut for now his memories will stay glassed.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nI tried to paint you with the finest paints,\nto bring your green eyes and brown hair,\nbut the picture simply slipped from my mind,\nand on the canvas you were not there.\nI had the colours perfectly mixed,\nmy brush poised and ready,\n\nbut when it came to shaping your face,\nI could not keep my hand steady.\nI ended up painting a landscape;\nit was one that I knew all too well,\nit had trees in the back and buildings around,\nbut little did I know that I fell.\n\nI watched my hand with curiosity,\nnot knowing what it was doing, \nand suddenly I forgot where I was,\nand even the tea I had brewing.\nI forgot my life and all my memories,\nI forgot who I had lost,\n\nI only knew about the place at hand,\nand I could not see the lines I crossed.\nI backed into my own painting\nof the trees and of the buildings,\nand backed right into the old Clinic of ours\nand I found the stash of old kindlings.\n\nI relit the fire just behind the counter,\nand leaned on it as I looked towards the door,\nI had washed the floors and that silver bell,\nand waited for your arrival once more.\nAs I stood in the Clinic of Memories\n\nI seemed to trick myself,\nbut at the time I could not see you\nas I restocked the shelf.\nYou walked in quietly as you always did,\nand I hung my coat on a hook.\nBut I could tell that I was not alone\n\nas you stood in that corner and looked.\nI turned around to see your expression,\nthose green eyes sunked into your face,\nand when you told me simple words that calmed me,\nthey helped me find my place.\nYou stepped closer and hugged me,\n\nyour words were soft and kind,\n\"You will be all right, I promise,\" you whispered,\nand suddenly it was I who was hard to find.\n\"Can't I just go back to the Clinic?\"\nI almost cried as you came near,\n\n\"Yes, anything you want, I promise,\nbut for now, Arial, you must stay here.\"\nSomething happened just then, \t\nwhen you promised I would be okay.\nI knew that the person I was then\nwould not be the person I am today.\n\nFor now I know that Arial\nwas the one who you wanted to protect,\nso she took her things and kept them safe,\nleast she be the one I forget.\nAnd now your dreams can come true,\nfor your daughter now walks these lands,\n\nand I can assure that Arial will be all right, \nbecause she is in your own hands.\nShe may call herself Annabelle,\nbut that is only for your sake,\nbecause she knows your daughter is in her\nand her soul was always yours to take.\n\nBut I did lose myself in the Clinic that day,\neven though it was just a painting.\nAnd even though it was all in my mind,\nI know it was real and Arial is still waiting.\nShe will wait for you until the end of time,\nuntil the brush is stea\n\nuntil your face can be painted again\nand until you think she is ready.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nPostscript.\n\nA collection of poems\nwritten by a famous Adunian poet in the late Anthosian period.\n\nLet her memory be charished by all those who read her delightful words.\n\n-Hanrahan Brae", ""),
        StaticBook("Haikus by Ana", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n    Haikus By Ana\n  ---------------\n\n   ~By Dedicant\n         Anarane\n\nThere is a sketching of a fox.\n\nYour coat is pretty,\nColors of orange, black and white,\nYour cuteness hides strength.\n\n\nThere is a sketch of a tree\n\nYour hands are green\nYou wave hello and goodbye\nYour roots are endless\n\nThere is a sketch of an owl\n\nYou are large and wise\nSleep at day, hunt in the night\nYou don't give a hoot", ""),
        StaticBook("~Wheat Poem~", "§bDedicant Renn", "Renn's Poem\n\n~~\n\nA golden glisten of summer sea,\nGraceful arms waving to and fro.\nA warm sensation runs through,\nA feeling of protect and peace bestow.\n\nSoft movements from a gentle breeze,\nGrace, prosperity is of Nature's own.\nThin straws of strength and vigor,\nA sense of light against the unknown.\n\nLand's children of grain,\nTreated as a gift that provides life.\n\nA gift of quelling formidable hunger,\nA might savior of times of strife.\n\nA life of giving, a seed to a stalk,\nO' Nature, grant me day and week.\nThat the grains retain their numbers,\nFor a life without wheat shows bleak.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cactus Poem", "§bEnar Oakseed", "Dark, swaying\nthe night, watching\nthe needles, pinsers to passers by\n\nThe roots embedded \nin the crimson sand,\nanchors in the ancient sea of dust and time\n\nDying, contracting, it dies and releases its\nseeds, a desperate plee\n\nAll is done and the swaying consumes it \nNo time, no space, just the desert and the dead", ""),
        StaticBook("Pumpkin and Rat", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n   Pumpkin and Rat\n  ---------------\n\n ~By Dedicant\n   Popo Sandybanks\n\nYay it was,\nThat Cerridwen would make\nSomething so sweet,\n'twas\nA pumpkin! Pumpkin!\nSo orange and round,\nWhoever saw it made a happy sound!\n\nThe pumpkin spread until one day,\nCernunnos created a creature,\nThat filled the pumpkin with dread.\nA rat! A rat!\n\nIn its hole it sat,\nWatching the pumpkin,\nHis tail thumpin'\nWaiting for his chance\nTo Strike!\n\nThe rat came out,\nNo people around to make a shout,\nAnd it burrowed its way into the Pumpkin.\nThe rat ate and ate,\nHis hunger unable to sate.\n\nThough the pumpkin was not sad.\nThe pumpkin did not cry.\nThe pumpkin was actually glad!\nThough the pumpkin dies,\nThe rat lives.\n\nAnd through the rat,\nMore seeds will spread,\nAnd with more pumpkins\nMore rats will come,\nTheir tails thumpin'\n\nAs such is the way of the Green Man and Lady,\nTo bring life from death,\nA balance made through equality.\n\nEvery living creature must know one simple truth,\nLet there be life when you cause death!\nLet the balance be held!", ""),
        StaticBook("Hiakus of Nature", "§bJesslyn", "Willow\nA willow tree stands\n\nIts green leaves sway in the wind\n\nRoots, deep in the ground.\n\n        Pond\n\nThe pond is quite green\n\nCreatures lerk in the water\n\nlilypads afloat\n\n         Spider\n\nSpider, In her hair\n\nGrows comfortable in there\n\nNo strings attached\n\n        The Sun\n\nThe sun, gives off light\n\nPlants bask in the bright sunlight\n\nGrows, into blooming flowers\n\n         Bloom\n\n\nPetals, captive in \n\nPetals, forming to flowers\n\nPetals, wilt to soil\n\n    Fawn and Doe \n\nFawn and doe laying\n\nThey lick water from the pond \n\nThey hide under shade", ""),
        StaticBook("Cycle", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n          Cycle\n  ---------------\n\n ~By Dedicant\n       Daneard\n\nA deer’s frozen breath in winter\nHunter crouching low in the snow\nHungry family waiting at home\nBlood seeps into the ground\nReturned to Nature\n\nThe calming breeze of a spring’s day\nFlowers shyly blooming after a long sleep\nYoung fawn nibbling on the soft grass\nReborn", ""),
        StaticBook("Wonderful Books", "§bKira Winterleaf", "Books, books, wonder after wonder!\n\nFinish one, pick up another!\n\nShare with a sister, share it with a brother! \n\nWe can all be book lovers!\n\nPaperback or hardcover! \n\nWe've got adventures to recover!\n\nLet's go to a place\n\nWhere free books are!\n\nC'mon, it's really not that far!\n\nJust a hop and a skip and a jump away!\n\nYou could go anyday!\n\nSo set the date, don't be late!\n\nGoing to the Library is our fate.\n\nBy: Kira Raine Winterleaf, 10th of the First Seed, 1556, Age 13", ""),
        StaticBook("Love in nature", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n      What I love\n            in\n         nature\n   --------------\n\n  ~By Arbor Druid               Thudrin\n\nThe trees be wise\nThe trees be old\nThe trees be tall\nThe trees be bold\n\nThe water be crisp\nThe water be clear\nThe water be fresh\nThe water be near\n\nThe grass be soft\nThe grass be green\nThe grass be long\nThe grass be clean\n\nThe flowers ne nice\nThe flowers be pretty\nThe flowers be soft\nThe flowers be witty\n\nThese be me favorites\nI love them the most\nI hold them so dear\nSo I raise a toast\n\n\nTo you, Nature\nYou, above all\nYou keep us alive\nSo we will not fall.", ""),
        StaticBook("Natural Poem", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n     Natural Poem\n   --------------\n \n    ~By Dedicant                   Virella\n\nNature's Poem\n\nThe peaceful grass.\nThe blowing wind.\nBlooming flowers.\nAnd the tall trees.\nThe matters of them connect to one.\nNature, it's nature, for us all.\n\nLinked Together\n\nA Druid serves nature.\nLinked to one.\nSame as the roots with trees.\nThe sun with plants.\nA family with support.\nAll is linked together.\n\nDandelion Fuzz\n\nBlooming with the wind\nNothing is sinned.\nThe fuzz flies and plants.\nNewcomers join to sprout and dance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Balance", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n         Balance\n   --------------\n\n  ~By Dedicant\n    Arial Meadowbloom\n\nBalance is responsibility,\nbalance is helping gain,\nbalance is keeping nature \nin step and free of pain.\n\nWhether it must be stopped\nor the cycle must be helped,\nnothing more can be done\nunless something is felt.\n\nThe unbalance will continue\nunless something is learned,\nunless everyone realises that\npeople scream but cannot be heard.\n\nThat sometimes the worst choice\ncan be the greatest one of all.\nthat sometimes to meet someone, \nthey must break down a wall.\n\nThe balance in nature\nis clear to everyone.\nwhether Druid or Mage,\nwelcomed or shun.\n\n(Un)balance goes on its way,\nthrough the day and night,\nwithout one whimper, one cry,\nwithout one branch giving a fight.\n\nWhich is why we must help\nin nature's time of need,\nfor when it calls to us,\nto its voice we must heed.\n\nWe must be grateful for all,\nfor the life which we work for,\nfor the things of which we own\nand the greatest thing at our core.\n\nWe must thank nature no matter where,\nno matter who and no matter how faulty,\nwe must thank nature for being\nso utterly sweet and yet so salty.\n\nLike the fire may destroy\nand yet help a forest grow,\nhow a book can ruin a life\nand yet help someone to know.\n\nThis balance goes on,\nforever and forever more,\nfor however long that must be,\nno one knows for sure.\n\nBut for now we continue\nto give thanks each, every day\nand to help nature and its parts\nin every single way.\n\nBecause...\n\nbalance is responsibility,\nbalance is staying in time,\nyet it is also keeping\nnature in its prime.", ""),
        StaticBook("Withering Flower", "§bTailesin", "---------------\n   Withering Flower\n  ---------------\n\n  ~By Unknown\n\nWithering flower, upbraid me not!\nWhy cast on me that look so pale?\nWhy bow thy head?\nWhy bend bend thy neck?\nWhy look so drooping, wan and cold?\n\nGiving my careless thoughts a check,\nAnd tell me I am getting old!\nFading flower, upbraid me not!\nStill nodding with the gentle breeze...\nOr dost thou think I have forgot,\nI too am wasting by lives?\n\nFor scarce can I believe my sight,\nWho lately saw thee fresh and gay;\nThat beauty could so early blight,\nOr such fresh colors fade away.", ""),
        StaticBook("The first song", "§bTailesin", "The first song will be played again.\n\nYou can hear it, if you listen.\n\nThe question is in the divinity.\n\nOur key can fit the lock, if you want it.\n\nThe skies were always to blame.\n\nFor I’m the breathe and life of home.\n\nLight is the sun, dark is the grain.\n\nAnd men grow tall from all but dust.\n\nThe will sees metal turn to rust.\n\nRoaming astray my endless garden.\n\nFruits of life do beg my pardon.\n\nCrops of life are what I sow.\n\nBlessed to the Gods below.\n\nFear the individuals, love the masses.\n\nStrum on the strings of yesterday.\n\nOur instruments differ, but the Melody’s the same.\n\nWhatever happened to the sun?\n\nOur is the purest sanctity.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fairy Ring", "§bTailesin", "-------------\n        Fairy ring\n             by\n          Heroxis\n    -------------\n\nSearching the night,\nNight quietly green.\nDeep in the forest,\nNo one has seen.\n\nThe circle of fungi,\nI enter you now.\nA force I sense,\nGreeting with a bow.\n\nTo sing in the rain,\nTo write in the sun.\nTo draw in the clouds,\nAttuned as one.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pine Trees", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n       Pine Trees\n   --------------\n\n  ~By The Pine Druid\n\nWhen one be walking bout thee's lands.\nOne may wander dunes of sand.\n\nOr maybe even caverns deep,\nAnd view the beauty found beneath.\n\nWhen one begins to feel cold,\nThey start to miss the sands of gold.\n\nBut when one thinks to open eye,\nThey see the beauty way up high.\n\nTrue beauty lies above you see,\nIt stands as such, a mighty tree.\n\nA towering canopy tall and fine,\nThe tree I speak of is the Pine.\n\nWhen time is right and tree is sound.\nIt drops it's pinecones to the ground.\n\nThe needles that it holds above,\nAre there not to be grabbed or shoved.\n\nBut rather be a tool to use,\nAnd not a weapon to abuse.\n\nThe tree I speak of is no quarry,\nSo harm it and you shall be sorry.\n\nSo when thou next goes near a Pine,\nBe sure to heed these words of mine.\n\nRespect the tree so tall and fine,\nAnd feel the kindness of the Pine.", ""),
        StaticBook("Listen", "§bTailesin", "-------------------           Listen\n-------------------\n\n~Meiala,\nCircle of the Tide\n\nListen.\n\nListen to the chirps.\n\nListen to the peeps.\n\nListen to the howls.\n\nListen to the creeks.\n\nListen to your heart.\n\nListen to your mind.\n\nIt'll all be better.\n\nJust give it...\n...some time.", ""),
        StaticBook("Birthday", "§bTailesin", "-------------------         Birthday\n-------------------\n\n~By Kira Winterleaf\n\nA feeling of warmth floods my body.\n\nI feel like I've grown a foot!\n\nI wouldn't care if I was covered in soot on this very special day.\n\nI want to run and jump and play!\n\nGo lots of places but never stay!\n\nIt only comes once a year.\n\nIs yours finally coming near?\n\nKeep your heart close and never jeer on this very special day.\n\nI lay down my head\n\nI see the stars-- so beautiful they are! aligned as they were thirteen years ago on my birthday.", ""),
        StaticBook("Cornfield song", "§bJudas Windsor", "________________________ \n\n        Song: I've been working on the cornfield\n\n-Judas Windsor.\n\n________________________\n\nI've been working on the cornfield all the live long day.\n\nI've been working on the cornfield just to pass the time away, can't you hear the bell ringing? Rise up so early in the morning.\n\nCan't you hear the captain shouting Dinah Blow your horn.\n\nChorus:\n\nDinah wont you blow?\n\nDinah Wont you blow\n\nDinah wont your ho-ho-horn. repeat x2\n\nSomeones with dinah, someones in the kitchen I kno-ow-ow singing Fee Fi Fiddly Aye o, fee fi fiddly aye o. Someones in the kitchen with Dinah!\n\nStrummin on the Ol Banjo...\n\nRepeat top paragraph x3", ""),
        StaticBook("Be Calm", "§bTailesin", "-------------------          Be Calm\n-------------------\n\n~By Kira Winterleaf\n\nWater ripples under the moon.\nMaybe th sun will be here soon?\nFrom the cry of the lonely loon, to the caterpillars in cacoons.\nThey are all calm.\n\nResting in nature's palm.\nLet your anger simmer away.\nDon't be angry today.\nIs all peaceful and all is right.\nJust be calm with all your might.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem of Calethel", "§bTailesin", "----------------\n    Poëm of Calethel\n  ----------------\n\n  \n ~By Dedicant \n          Calethel\n\nThe roots starts from below, as it the roots are starting to get thicker when they go up, to a stem, going up and up, from thick to thin, as it is starting to show leaves, with branches, the wood color of dark brown, leaves of dark green,\n\nThe wind blowing in the eastern horizon, as it blows the leaves with it, hearing the birds chirping and chirping, flying around the tree, as they nest in the branches, and little baby birds being born, chirping for their parents, as they are hungry.\n\nThe grass around it growing and growin, with the flowers, as the insects crawl around the ground, looking for food, the grass starts to spread at soil, growing more and more, as fallen branches on the ground starting to grow to trees.\n\nThe animals walking around happily, the cow eating the overgrown grass, chickens eating the seeds, and the sheep eating the grass also, as further into the grassland a thick forest starts to appear, starting from thin, to thick, the with growing bigger\n\nas the lenght doesn't stop, starting from oak trees, to birch trees, then to pine trees and then to acacia trees, and then to dark oak, as thick as they can get, as huge as they can, the leaves growing on the branches,\n\nas they grow more up, higher in the \n\nsky, reaching the clouds, the sun shining on the leaves,the wind quietly humming around the forest, as it is silent, with only the chirping songbirds to hear.", ""),
        StaticBook("Howard's Poem", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n    Dedicant poem \n       by Howard\n   --------------\n\n  ~By Dedicant\n           Howard\n\nOvernight, yet very white,\nVery discreet...yet quiet as most make of them,\nNor parasites nor plague.\n\nThey float in the midnight air,\nThey grow in caves,\nThey flourish on the mycelium.\n\nThese are the fungi,\nThey grow on corpses,\nAnd they grow on cave walls.\n\nThey are everywhere,\nYet they are not,\nWhite and blue, red and green,\n\nThese are the force of nature,\nThat recycles all things...\nDead or alive.\n\nMold, toad stool, mushroom, truffle, and bitter cap,\nYou all grow in mycelium and the caves below,\nYou grow on food and you grow on grains.", ""),
        StaticBook("Petrichor", "§bTailesin", "-------------------          Petrichor\n-------------------\n\n~By Endvani Ildres\n\nPetrichor, Petrichor\nSpice and Season Petrichor,\nMay the sky kiss your cheeks,\nthe rain wash away your weeps,\ntill I am at your side\n\nPetrichor, Petrichor\nSweet perfume of petrichor,\nRainsong soft with thunder's beat,\nbring your dreams,\ntill we meet.\n\nPetrichor, petrichor\nWind and waves petrichor\nMoonlight will my promise keep,\nlike the bond of stubborn, faithful sheep,\n\ntill I am at your side.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fair Ethanriel", "§bTailesin", "-------------------       Fair Ethanriel\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Song Druid\n\nEthanriel\n\nIt was the perfection of the Aspects that afford me her prospects.\n\nEven as I saw her for the first time I did blush soft, as each time she held me aloft.\n\nAncient wisdom hanging as vines, set apart from the bird and the pine.\n\nMother and lover, mistress and wife, bark and leaf-love of mine life.\n\nI stand living while she on her side, as if a mere tree fallen by the tide.\n\nElandriel gives her please, come back, save us.\n\nTarry not, give no sign of hesitation, only obedience, only inclination.\n\nBlessed Mother, home to our brothers, lives on in some regard.\n\nSacrifice greatly rendered, a loss mourned and no payment tendered.\n\nThe Druids can never recover, from a loss like no other, but memory does push onward and strength does persist.\n\nEthanriel, Ethanriel, Mother Tree of Elandriel... keep watch from the Aspects' favour.", ""),
        StaticBook("Paths", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\n          Paths\n-------------------\n\n~By Gwendolyn Powell\n\nOne step after another\nI found myself walking a path, a path laden with roots\nA path breathing and living beneath my foot\nSilent at my touch\n\nAs I wandered, on and on\nI took a turn\nA turn down a darker road, and one hidden\nA path dusty, dry, and lifeless\n\nThis path I walked for many a year\nLost amidst a sea of dark\nLost, on a path that turned slowly upwards, and soon become too difficult to walk\n\nAnd there I sat, pondering my\nOut of the corner of my eye, I glimpsed the sun\nBrilliant, bountiful, and beautiful\n\nI stood, and began to trudge slowly towards the light\nHearing birdsong, hearing the rustle and movement of leaves\nFeeling warmer and\nThe loneliness seeping from my bones\n\nThe dark shrank away in the light\nThis new path, was one brimming with life\nMuch like the first\nBut now, I began to hear something\n\nA quite breath, tiny beneath the sounds of chirping\nThe hoof beat of a running deer, and the great sway of an oak tree\nSo I listened more closely\nImmersing myself in the slow and steady beat\nOf true world\n\nThe growing, giving, lovely world\nThat I had once ignored\nAnd had now, finally\n\nFound a place in my heart\nA place, where it began to beat within me\nA place, that I hope to see flourish\nTo see flourish, like the flowers and astoundingly wonderful world around me", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature is lovely", "§bTailesin", "-------------------     Nature is Lovely\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Kira Winterleaf\n\nFireflies glow.\nTrees grow and \nrivers flow.\nNature is lovely,\nnature is strong.\nNature is where I truly belong\nDesert sands and icelands.\nAll part of nature.\n\nRabbits burrow\nBirds fly\nBugs buzz\nAnd mice hide.", ""),
        StaticBook("Light of Life", "§bTailesin", "-------------------       Light of Life\n-------------------\n\n~By Tippen Oaklen\n\nThe wooded ways shine with light.\n\nThe light unspoken from times of old.\n\nA light unlike the sun and stars.\n\nA light no man can measure or count.\n\nThe light that shines from shore to shore.\n\nThis light which speaks only in hushed tones.\n\nThis light burns light in all that grow.\n\nOn Light of Life should guide your path.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem", "§b[Dedicant] Anthony Hearthal", "In the meadows of asulon\nThere are many plants and trees\nAnd most of the grass grows up, passed your knees\nIn the deepest sea of the world\na massive fish did wonder\nwhy men would dive so far down under\n\nWell the truth is simple they just wanted to plunder\n\nLike magic trees and humming bees the world also has many secrets, where it hides treasure, the beauty of nature and soulmates that one desires.", ""),
        StaticBook("Perspective", "§bTailesin", "=================== §0\n§0      Perspective§0\n§0\n§0===================§0\n§0\n§0-By Dedicant Kianna\n\nPerspective§0\n§0Everything can be good or bad§0\n§0Calm or raging§0\n§0Few things or people just have one side§0\n§0Birth and death§0\n\nBirth can be the entering into a good life, or a life of pain and suffering§0\n§0Death can be a young life cut brutally short, otr the calming end to a life lived well, passing on.§0\n§0Everything and everyone has a point of view§0\n\nSome wildly different from others§0\n§0Some alike§0\n§0Some conflicting, some working together§0\n§0Everything has a perspective.§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Post-Dream", "§bThe Pine Druid", "History\n     - Post-Dream\n\nThe First Age of Dreaming\n\nFor a long time it seemed that Nature was silent. For a great length of time, none had seen any feats spoken about in the ancient Druid tales.\n\nBut then, as if a seed had been planted, nature began to speak, and each Druid began to dream mysterious dreams. It was the Aspects of nature, Cerridwen and Cernunnos, calling to their newly found children. Wayward Druid Respiren called a Moot to discuss\n\nthese dreams, leading to...\n\nThe Age of the Aspects\n\nThe Moot decided to embrace the two Aspects as their primary deities.\n\nThe Age of Little Occurrence\n\nNothing happened for a while, except for a few little things. there was a small Hierarchy change; The Archdruid position was given to three people and the Grand Druid's description was changed. John and\n\nMaiavel were promoted to Archdruids but Maiavel soon retured. Her spot was filled by Petyr. Cleric Druid Mahten was also promoted to the rank of Guide. A small recruiting strategy was initiated.\n\nThe Second age of Renovation\n\nDuring this age, the Laurelin Grove experienced a number of additions. A wall was created around the grove with a retractable gate. This was done to keep out a number of citizens that were harrassing\n\nthe Druids. In addition, all the entrances were covered up so that one must always use the front gate. More additions to the grove included: a kitchen, a melon and pumpkin farm, the cultivation of the Grand Tree's roots deeper into the ground, a tree farm\n\nan elevator system and Druid dwellings at the top of the Grand Tree.\n\nThe Age of Poor Judgement\n\nA lot of inner-Order arguments and bad feelings culminated in this Age. Multiple parties were involved and no side was completely right. The end result was the leaving of Seeker Druid Deltaro from\n\nthe order. The bad taste left in the mouth of many Druids caused Apollan to create the Druid Code.\n\nThe Second Age of Little Occurrence\n\nDuring this age, as its name would imply, not many things happened. The grove was given another renovation, mostly for aesthetic purposes. In addition, a Druid Order forum account was created as well as a blog.\n\nUnfortunately during this time, Archdruid John's mental health was also starting to slide.\n\nThe Second Age of Dreaming\n\nDuring this age Salmon Druid Petyr had a vision in which the creation myth was shown to him by the Aspects. It foretold of the time before the four races and a great exodus to come. A second Moot was\n\ncalled and all in attendance agreed to accept the vision as truth.\n\nThe Age of Mourning\n\nUnfortunately during this time, John's mental state grew worse. One day, John snapped. Among a number of things he attacked the Dusk Druid. He then fled to his soul tree (which he named Marcus) located in the pond outside the\n\nLaurelin Grove. There he made his last stand. The Druids present at the time were Dusk, Mahten, Apollan, Respiren, Saviticus and Thork the Betrayer (Thork the Blood Druid). In addition, several princes and bystanders were also there. John stood on the\n\nroots of his soul tree and attacked anyone who came near. There was a standoff for some time until all at once the Druids charged the tree. While some traded blows with John, Apollan worked to purify the water around the tree for he sensed that there was\n\na taint at work. Suddenly the Dusk Druid produced an axe and chopped at the soul tree until only one column was left. It was then that; with the ability to see inside the tree, Apollan discovered the taint in the form of a block of netherrack. Before\n\nApollan could share his discovery, Thork went into a blood lust. He cut down the last column of the tree rendering John unconscious. Then Thork stabbed John's body killing him. In the days that followed, Thork was shunned for violating Druid Code. John's\n\ntree was turned into the place of his burial where his body was burned and the ashes scattered around Laurelin. His will named Mahten as his replacement as Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Expansion\n\nDuring this age, the Nature's Bounty chain was expanded by one new store on the Kings Road. The Order was also expanded by one new member. Zannith, the 10 year old son of the Dusk Druid and an unknown Elf moved into the grove.\n\nMuch to the dismay of one Bircalin. Zannith later left the Druid grove to join the wardens and later committed suicide by jumping from the Grand Tree.\n\nThe Second Age of Mourning\n\nDuring this age Archdruid Mahten transformed his entire being into a tree in the grove. As a result of his sacrifice, Druid Dusk was named (in his will) as successor to the position of Archdruid.\n\nIn this time she began to bring new life to the Druids tree, ushering forth growth and spread of new branches, and brought a new generation of Druids into the Dedicant rank.\n\nThe Second Age of Expansion\n\nDuring this age, the Druid Order and the Grand tree grew exponentially.\n\nThe Third Age of Mourning\n\nDuring this age, Archdruid Apollan and Dusk were killed under mysterious circumstances. Apollan's position was given to his close friend Horizon Druid Makia and Dusk's to the Fox Druid.\n\nThe Age of Poor Logic\n\nDuring this age, everything the druids knew was proven to be incorrect. Petyr proved that wood doesn't exist, then proved that it does. He also proved that magic isn't real, and that water is fake. Unable to hold all this\n\nnonsense, Archdruid Darrian was convinced that he had gone crazy and was only dreaming. After jumping to his death from the top of the Grand tree, Faey Druid Vanyariel took his position as Archdruid.\n\nThe Age of Activism\n\nDuring this age, many Druids had visions foretelling a coming destruction of all of Aegis. The Druids took it upon themselves to find and recover all ancient artifacts of Aegis so that so that they may be saved from the end and\n\nbrougt with them to new lands. With the help of High Princess Indelwehn, the Druids were lead to the Druid tree at the docks. There she showed them the Shadow Circle, a compound beneath the tree created by Apollan and Respiren to store the first Staff,\n\nSeed, and Sapling. It was later learned that Indelwehn believed the artifacts to be fake and intended to fool the Druids. But they were real and were taken back to the Mother Grove. Next, the Druids taveled to Galahar where they valiantly defended the\n\ncity from an attack by bandits. They then asked the King for permission to excavate under the city in search of another artifact which they believed to be stored under the city. The King agreed but only if the Druids agreed to teach him magic. They also\n\nagreed. After many weeks, in which the Druids found a number of things, they broke into a grand cavern with a sandstone pyramid in the center, Inside the pyramid contained the Golden Mushroom and the Crystal Mushroom. These two were taken back to the\n\nMother Grove. Next the Druids travelled to the Mages Guild House in South Laurelin. Inside the Mages kept an artifact, an enchanted sword called the Bane of Arthropods. The Sword was rightfully the Druids' but the Mages refused to relinquish it.\n\nNegotiations were made with a mage, Deltaro Black but they did not come to fruition. Finally using the combined power of eight Druids present, the force field surrounding the chest containing the sword and it's locks were forced open. The Druids then took\n\nthe sword, bidding a hasty retreat and brought the sword back to the Mother Grove.\n\nThe Age of Treaties\n\nDuring this age, peace was finally made between the Druid Order and the Mages Guild in the form of a contract written by the Druids. Although people tend to forget that the treaty was basically and utterly forgotten within the\n\nnext five minutes of it's creation.\n\nThe Age of Hasty Movement\n\nDuring this age, the Order moved around quite a bit. First, they left the Laurelin Grove due to the fact of a rumor that all of Laurelin would explode after Vardak opened a portal at the Gauntlet. The Order took up\n\nresidence in the Rose Grove for sometime after the miasma engulfed it. It took the combined magic of several Druids to push it back to the grove's boundaries, however. The Order then had to return to Laurelin after they were given word that Malinor wished\n\nto reclaim their grove. Afterwards, when the Cloud Temple fell, the Order (artifacts and all) packed up and moved to the Verge. This age ended when Aegis was overtaken by the Undead and all of the inhabitants of Aegis traveled to the new lands of Asulon.", ""),
        StaticBook("Roll-Roll-Roll", "§b[Poet] Garry Bogger", "Halflings Poems\n\n   Roll-Roll-Roll-Roll\n\n1. Roll-Roll-Roll-Roll-Roll-Roll-rolling down the hole!\nHave ho!Splash plump!\nDow they go, down they bump!\nDown the swift dark stream you go,\nBack to land you once did\n\nknow!\nLeave the halls and caverns deep,\nLeave the northen mountains steep,\nWhere the forest wide and dim, stoops is shadow grey and grym!\nFloat beyond the world of trees.\nOut into the wispering breeze,\nPast the rushes,past the ree\n\nds,Past the marsh's waving weeds,\nTrough the mist that riseth white\nUp from mere and pool of night!\nFollow, follow stars that leap\nUp the havens cold and steep;\nTurn whan dawn comes over land,\nOver rapid, over sand,\n\nSouth away! And south away!\nSeek the sunlight and the day,\nBack to pasture, back to mead,\nWhere the kine and oxen feed!\nBack to gardens on the hills,\nWhere the berry swelles and fills\nUnder sunlight, under day!\nSouth away! And south aw\n\nay!\nDown the swift darck stream you go\nBack to lands you once did know.\n    \n         The End\n\n-May the hair on yer toes never fall off.\n\n- Book by Garry Bogger.", ""),
        StaticBook("Rebirth of Evil", "§bLilyana", "The Rebirth of Evil\n\n          By\n\n        Lilyana\n\nShe awoke in dire pain and confusion as the livelihood of the Crimson Fist’s purpose and existence, literally, weighed heavily upon her. The Champion’s mind sporadically showed images of a battle that just collapsed the temple containing most, if not all,\n\nof the Sanguine Hand, sending them to an early demise. Shouts meant to be full of victory turned into blood curdling screams of pain and death as they were overrun by their attackers. As she clawed at the gravel and dirt that trapped her, she tried\n\nlistening to her surroundings for other survivors calling out to her. But all her ears could hear were the memories of the stone walls around her cracking, sliding, grinding against one another, as they began to collapse inward on her and the others, and\n\ntheir screams. Oh how the memory of the screams tortured her mind, forcing her bloody and gashed hands to sift quicker. Her hands digging and sifting faster, trying to escape the memory,  but the images and sounds only seemed to come faster as she tried\n\nto flee, keeping in tempo with her movement. \n\nWith a heavy grunt and thrust, she thrusts her arm through a small lit-opening, finally feeling the cool air touch her skin.  This bolstered her will, giving her the strength to push herself through the\n\nsmall opening and back into the outside world. The elven woman lay there in the cool morning fog for what seemed like an eternity,  time completely lost to her, breathing in deeply the fresh air.  Slowly, from the dense fog a shadowy figure approaches her\n\nexhausted self and shoves a bow and arrow into her face. Panting, the woman looks up at the figure as he pulls back his hood, revealing the rugged features of a hunter. The man pulled back the weapon slightly from her face and asked her for her name.\n\n“M-my name…”, the elven woman spoke between her labored breaths, “ My name is Cora.”\n\nSeeing Cora’s condition and state, the hunter took pity on her that fateful morning and led her to his dwelling in the forest.  There, he fed and dressed her wounds\n\nnursing her back to health. Cora slowly came around to the man, knowing all too well how easily it is to lower ones guard when they begin to trust someone, but never spoke about the Crimson Fist. When he did question her about her past, she simply said\n\nthat she was a servant to the people that owned the, now, pile of rubble that he found her at. The man nodded, knowing it rude to question a person’s past, and went about his business. However, no matter how hard he tried to focus on cleaning his\n\nmorning’s kill or divert his gaze, he was mesmerized by Cora’s beauty and soon felt for her. Cora, knowing that the Crimson Hand’s was either utterly destroyed or scattered to the winds and that her previous life was too dangerous to try to start again\n\non her own, accepted the hunter’s affection with open arms and soon gave birth to a child of a rare complexion. The child’s skin was pale like snow, his hair like fine straws of white gold, and his eyes….crimson like blood. Because of his defining eyes,\n\nCora named him, Haemon, the Blood Child. \n\nHaemon’s features were the most exciting thing of his life as he lived a dull life in the forest. His father taught him how to string a bow, to catch a fish, to chop wood, and skin a kill. He promised to tak\n\nhim hunting when he was rightfully a man and left Haemon in charge of keeping order at home when he left on his numerous hunting trips. Cora took to motherhood surprisingly well. Her swift and accurate hands that once sliced a man’s throat before they\n\ncould blink now directed a needle and thread through cloth; her strength and determination that kept herself alive during fights with armed guards now carried in loads of firewood or cleaned the wash. She watched over Haemon throughout the years grow into\n\na fine young man, but also watched herself age. She knew that with her death, the Crimson Fist died as well, as did its unfinished work. So, as her husband left on one of his hunting trips, Cora brought Haemon into the cellar of their small home.\n\nThere, she had him swear to keep this information from his father, and showed him the history of her people, of his people. Haemon was fascinated by the information, rituals, chants, and stories his mother knew and begged to know all of it.\n\nSo, every time her husband would venture out into the woods, Cora would teach Haemon the ways of the Crimson Fist.\n\nThis continued for many years until the night before Haemon’s eighteenth birthday, when everything changed.\n\nThe hunter returned home earlier than expected, bringing with him a new bow to give to Haemon as a gift to use on their first hunt together. However, when he approached the home, all he could see was darkness through the windows. He quickly entered\n\nthe home, noticing that the fire and candles had been empty and snuffed for quite some time. The darkness however, did allow the light from the under the cellar door to draw his attention. He opened the door quietly, filling the room with light,\n\nand was met by the voices of his family, however could not make out what they were saying. Descending the staircase he saw his wife and son, dancing while around an open fire. An animal’s carcass and bloodied knife lay next to the flame and\n\nits blood was painted on their naked skin in weird designs and letters that he could not understand. Haemon was the first to notice his father’s presence, quickly dropping his hands arms, breaking the dance, and backing away from the cellar door.\n\nCora noticed the Haemon’s fear and quickly turned, meeting the horrified gaze of her husband. He, softly, began to question Cora and Haemon, but neither responded, both afraid to unveil the lies they have been keeping from him. The hunter became angry,\n\nhis voice raising as he stepped down the stairs, approaching his wife. Cora remained speechless, not knowing what to say or do, she simply looked at the floor. In a fit of tears and rage the man grabbed Cora’s arms and forced her against the wall,\n\nshouting at her, begging to her, for answers. Haemon watched in horror and fear as his father slammed his mother against the wall across the room, shouting at her, asking her questions, spewing lines of secrets, lies, and betrayal. But it was only Haemon\n\nthat noticed the fire in the center of the room grow larger and only Haemon that heard the flame telling him to stop his father. Haemon tried to cover his ears to escape the raspy voice but the flame’s voiceresonated inside of him, demanding Haemon to\n\ntake action.\n\nHaemon closed his eyes and screamed out, trying to smother the flame’s voice with his own, but when he stopped screaming, all was quiet. Haemon opened his eyes to see his hand firmly around the bloodied knife that had sat in the\n\ncenter of the room, and the knife he held was deeply embedded into his father’s back. His father twisted around to look at Haemon’s eyes as he desperately tried to reach for the knife in his back. Blood began to leak from his mouth as he tried mouthing\n\nwords, words that Haemon would never hear. His father fell towards the center of the room, his head cracking on the hard stone floor, blood leaking out of his body towards the fire. Haemon backed away from his mother who now shriveled into herself against\n\nthe wall, staring at her dead husband, at the pool of blood forming around his body.\n\nThe blood of the innocent man pooled and grew, eventually reaching the fiery claws of the flame. With an immense heat and power, the flame jumped high and turned\n\ncrimson, sparks flew from it in all directions. Haemon now stared at the fire, mesmerized by the dancing of its flames and heard the voice that spoke to him earlier, this time much clearer. He could not disobey it if he tried, the pull it had on his very\n\nbeing was too strong, so he walked into the flame. Cora tried to reach out to her son, but her body was frozen, whether in fear or by the flames own power she did not know, but when she called to him nothing escaped her throat. Haemon did not feel\n\nthe flame burn his skin, but felt it burn his insides, as if it had reached into his heart and soul and burnt them to ash. He could not scream out nor did his body react to the pain that his mind was feeling. His vision slowly became dark and blurred, his\n\nsense of feeling seemed to numb away, and his mind began to close in on itself.\n\nWhen Cora woke the next morning in her bed she quickly wretched onto the floor. The nightmare was so real to her, the heat of the fire, the smell\n\nof her husband’s blood, the image of her son walking into the fire, all shook her deeply.  She struggled out of bed and walked down to the kitchen, finding it cold and dark as it was every morning. She started a fire and hung a pot of water to boil while\n\nshe called out to Haemon to wake. However, he did not return her call. When she checked his bed, he found it vacant and undisturbed from the previous morning’s making. Fear struck at her as she turned to look at the closed cellar door. Slowly approaching\n\nit and wrapping her hand around the latch, she closed her eyes as she opened it and was greeted by nothing but the smell of burnt flesh. At the bottom of the staircase she could see her husband’s burnt skeleton in the same spot it was\n\nleft in her nightmare. She cried out and slammed the cellar shut and turned to run from the house, only to see a figure standing in the doorway, shadowed from the sun coming in around the figure. Its glowing, deep crimson eyes\n\nwere its only distinguishable characteristic.\n \nThe figure spoke to her with two voices, both familiar but from different lives.\n \n“Hello, Mother”", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost in Soul", "§bTaynuel", "<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nSo here again I sit alone\n\nMy thoughts my only company\n\nHow long now have I been here?\n\nI listen to the symphony\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nThe forest speaks in silent shrieks\n\nYet only few can hear\n\nTheir desperate and lonely cries\n\nOf hate and war and fear\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nTis not my choice for solitude\n\nBut here once more I stay\n\nThe one place I feel I belong\n\nWhere other places my patience decays\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nThe knife cuts sharp and deeply so\n\nThe blade of metal black as night\n\nYet blind is my attacker here\n\nNot recognizing my plight\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nMisinterpreted are my words\n\nAnd thrown back in my face\n\nBeaten down with ignorance\n\nIn the home I called this place\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nI'm sure he'll feed her poison words\n\nTo make me the villain now\n\nThe refusal to speak and turned away\n\nAm I each time I visit town\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nSo leave, I feel, is my best choice\n\nEscape this discrimination and hate\n\nBefore my heart can take no more\n\nAnd met am I with dismal fate\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nTo a new home, one free of strife\n\nWhere harm comes nevermore\n\nRun away, a coward I\n\nAvoiding the heartache at my door\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nBut can I speak to anyone? No\n\nFor who would listen to me\n\nI'm just an orphan darkie boy\n\nThat begs to be set free\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nMy dreams are filled with pain and loss\n\nThe nightmares everlasting\n\nA monster am I? Or something worse?\n\nA viper with deadly sting\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nWhat would they do if things were different?\n\nIf I was the one in power?\n\nWould I be challenged? Tossed away?\n\nBrought the high elves for acid shower?\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\nDoomed am I to a broken heart\n\nCannot restore what was never whole\n\nA fractured man now lost in mind\n\nBut moreso lost in soul\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>", ""),
        StaticBook("The Pumpkin Lord", "§bA Ravenous Adventurer", "So Hungry.. so very, very hungry I was... lost in the wilderness I was, oh yes, lost I was.... no food... just filthy water... tasteless water...\n\n And then... I saw him... A walking pumpkin... pumpkin pie... food... precious, precious food...\n\nI lunged at it... I chased it.. oh, it smelled so sweet... I chased it away from the city... the foul, filthy human city.... until an elf... cruel, disgusting elf...\n\nThe elf approached the delicous, precious, pumpkin man... he wanted to kill it...\n\nI wished the same.....\n\nWe fought him.... the pumpkin man.... then the treacherous, filthy pumpkin man offered a trade.... his body to the elf.... for my arm...\n\nThe treacherous, filthy elf.... he took my arm... but not before the evil pumpkin man....\n\ndecided he wanted the elf's arm instead....\n\nThe evil, pumpkin man... he cut off the filthy elf's arm...\n\nThen... we fought him... and finally... the filthy elf got his finger.... and ran off...\n\n Then.... I got one of his.... other fingers.... Then.... the pumpkin man shouted....\n\n\"I am Knox, the Pumpkin Lord!\"\n\nThen... he ran off.... and I did as well.... limping towards the moat.... pumpkin finger in hand... only one arm....\n\nHungry...\n\n\n\n\n        So Hungry....\n\n\n\n\n\n       But so much pain....\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n   Can't write... any more...\n\n\n\n *This page is soaked in blood and smells faintly of pumpkins*", ""),
        StaticBook("The Republic II", "§bAmbros", "The Republic\nVolume II\n\nBy Ambros\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Five Government\n\nTimocracy\nThe timocracy is a war-loving state.  A nation which has lost its wise leaders and, in turn, its loves of wisdom.  All that remains is justice, temperance and courage.  Without the sober-minded leaders, the courageous and passionate will\n\ncome to power.  The timocratic state holds honor and victory above all else, a state deeply entrenched in tradition.  The timocratic state is the result of poor education of future leaders, and thus, the victorious and charismatic military-men win over\n\nthe hearts of the citizens.  Of course, justice and temperance will still find a home in the timocracy.  Food will be directed towards the military, men who have become not guardians and keepers of peace, but soldiers and raiders.  The timocratic\n\nindividual is, as stated earlier, an individual who holds honor and victory to the highest regard, the timocratic academic will argue for the sake of conflict, not for the sake of extracting truths and knowledge.\n\nOligarchy\nA warring state, when successful, will inevitably come upon a great deal of land, a great deal of resources, and thus, a great deal of riches.  When this occurs the leaders and soldiers of the state will no longer hold to the values of\n\ncourage and justice, but begin to acquire property and wealth.  They will hoard such things for themselves and forget the old and honorable traditions of war.  When the timocratic state has forgotten this, and the leaders of the state are selected not\n\nupon military prestige, but rather, physical property, they will convert into an oligarchy.  The oligarchic state is a machine of profit, the military becomes a tool of profit, and the citizens become laborers of profit.  More and more will the rich\n\nbecome bloated with their wealth, and the poor will grow sickly in their labor.  However, the oligarchic state is not a state of gluttons, nae, the state still embodies temperance.  The oligarchs epitomize efficiency, they have worked hard to acquire\n\ntheir wealth, whether through war or commerce.  They are fully aware of the benefits of hard work, and the botherations of waste.  They live simply, but hoard their wealth as personal satisfactions opposed to the benefit of the state.\n\nThe oligarchic individual is a falsely modest and and sober-minded one.  In public, they will carry themselves will all amounts of politeness and agreeableness as not to lose their status.  They will live simply and only act upon the most necessary of\n\nurges, as they are quite stingy with their wealth.  However, they are hardworking individuals, capable of great feats if entirely selfish.  The oligarchic is akin to the son of a military hero who has fallen to poverty in civilized society.  The son,\n\ndisdainful of his father’s blind service to military efforts for no other reason than praise, will mark his own way, preferring to remain in the confines of the society, laboring, and growing rich.\n\nDemocracy\nAs the chasm between the rich and the poor within the oligarchy grows ever wider, the poor will begin to despise the rich for the wealth they hoard.  The poor then, being greater in number, will inevitably overthrow, exile, and execute\n\ntheir oligarch leaders and place themselves in their place.  The government will be ruled by many and all, opposed to the affluent few.  With the wealthy individual leaving the state, so too does the remaining virtue of temperance.  The democratic state,\n\nwhile being exceptionally diverse with the alluring promises of freedom, is naught but anarchy disguised is “liberty”.  The masses have remained uneducated since they lost their love of wisdom and become lazy and indulgent with their loss temperance.\n\nThey will not have the sober-mindedness of the oligarchs, and will act upon their urges without question, considering all desires, necessary or not, to be necessities.  Put simply, their will be equality for equals and unequals alike.  The democracy, left\n\nunchecked, will transform into something akin to a madhouse of lazy, uneducated citizens acting upon temporal urges and floundering in their ignorance.\n\nThe few “leaders” within the democracy, mayhaps representatives or bureaucrats, will be forced to appease the masses opposed to benefit the state as a whole.  The citizens hold all power, and thus, all leaders and government officials must remain\n\non acceptable terms with these citizens to maintain their status.  The leaders will be led by the citizens.  The citizens, in their willful ignorance, will think little of the state, of course.  All of their decisions will be based upon individual\n\ndesires, and the leaders will be forced to appease them.  It is as if a child told their parents how to raise them, or if a patient told their doctor what they thought the most effective treatment for their ailment was.  Leadership is a profession, and\n\nshould be carried out only by those who have been educated in the fine arts of leadership.\n\nThe great peculiarity of democracy is its fluidity, as it sits seemingly in the middle of this civic spectrum, capable of teetering towards oligarchy or tyranny.\n\nThe democratic individual is like the rebellious youth of a wealthy entrepreneurial father.  The father is a self made individual who rose from the droves of society via his own weathered hands.  The child, who has grown weary of their father’s\n\nfrugalness, will turn to frivolous indulgences, having no worldly knowledge and mistake their narrow opinions as experience.  Of course, with the freedoms of democracy there will be a select few who rise above, a select few of individuals, perhaps still\n\nholding the values of the old oligarchy will take advantage of the collective ignorance and indulgence of the masses to become excessively wealthy.  They will form the new wealthy class.  The indulgent and more ignorant general populace, of course, will\n\ngrow disdainful of this new upper class as it reminds them of the old oligarchy.\n\nTyranny\nWith the freedoms of democracy will come deviants, individuals who will intentionally distance themselves from the chaos and disorder of the democratic society.  Self-educated academics, scholars, advocates and philosophers who will fancy\n\nthemselves as “rogues” who are above the common “ignorant” citizen despite their idle laziness and extravagant, alluring, and romantic lives.  These individuals are emphatic will spew their beliefs and theories upon the masses, those particularly\n\ncharismatic or influential individuals will grow themselves a following.  The ignorant citizens will consider them “refreshing”, a beacon of escape from the bustle of democracy.  Some of these deviants will, of course, become ever more popular and serve\n\nas the people’s hero, organizing rallies and potentially violent acts against the shameful state they were raised in.  They will acquire a desire to push against the state and transform it, assembling their followers behind them, giving promises of\n\nredistributed wealth as they believe the democratic state to be biased despite its uncaring anarchy.  Finally, the individual will overthrow the democratic state, placing themselves sole executive and assemble a personal guard force as they, in their\n\nself-educated wisdom, believe their enlightened philosophies and theories to be the one true path to a new and prosperous state.  Of course, the new leader has not the wisdom, or courage, or temperance of their predecessors  (as is the way with democracy)\n\nand will ultimately fail to lead the state.  The state will grow poor, as leader attempts to do away the infrastructural economy for communal wealth, and the masses are little more than slaves.  To distract their now dissatisfied citizenry, the leader\n\nwill wage war, brandishing nationalistic dribble and simultaneously stimulating the economy which will function solely on the needs of the military.\n\nTo conclude, none of the above faculties of government are sustainable, and only the state which holds all four virtues to the highest esteem will prove fruitful for centuries following, a state ruled by philosopher kings, protected by guardians raised\n\nfrom birth, and lived in by citizenry of temperance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mana Gem S/R-1", "§bTyrell Blavier", "Mana Gem Research:\n\n-A mana gem is part of enchanting, and supplies mana to an enchantment. It must be charged by an enchanter. \n-After awhile it loses it's charge, and needs to be charged with mana again.\n\nI talked to Mark Volaire xXMark123Xx about these gems and the theory of mana gems and being used for energy distribution back into us. He suspected this would not be possible, however suggested the idea of using them on Golems.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Sages entry.", "§bSage Ju'Mukar", "----------------------------------------\nThe diary of Mukar\n---------------------------\nFor Mukars eyes only, should non-mukar eyes lay upon this book, close your eyes and return to Mukar or may Metztlis wrath be upon you.\n========================\n=======\n\n- 22nd of The deep cold, 1530.\n----------------------------------------This was a strange day that Metztli provided for Mukar. For rumours spread of a sky-rock falling onto the druidic grove many years ago. But the foolish druids never informed the Fylax.\n\nThey hidden it from us, for this could be the event we forsaw. \n\nMukar sent Ja'Ketzawl to investigate these rumours. Though they were true the druids refused to give any detailed explanations of the event that transpired there on that moon. The Fylax will\n\ncontinue to investigate these events, for they could be the bringer of something gravely evil.\n\nSa'vi Metztli.", ""),
        StaticBook("Impure Love", "§bHileia'elr", "----Impure Love----\n\nWritten by Hileia'elr\n\nA romance novel of two Mali, coming from two very different backrounds.\n\nI was ready to leave Haelun'or for the first time in my life. Through my childhood, my parents kept me hidden from the outside world. In uncommon circumstances, I would be allowed to travel to the docks to view the ocean, but that was it.\n\nMost of my time was spent with my teachers, and I learned about science. I didn't dislike the subject, it just didn't quite suit me. Instead, I wanted to be one of the guards, as a sword in my hand was one of the best feelings I've ever experienced...\n\nHowever, once I was old enough, I wanted to explore the realm.\n\nI walked up the steps, towards the large gate. A Mali'ame stopped me. \"State your name, race, and business, Mali.\" he said. He was dressed in a green armour, with yellow trims.\n\n\"I am Elsil'ceru. I come from Haelun'or, and I come to Laureh'lin seeking supplies.\" My answer seemed to satisfy the guard, and he let me through. As I looked around, I saw Mali'Ame everywhere. I wasn't used to large crowds, so I rushed over to the side.\n\nI just simply wanted to escape the large, noisy streets filled with Elves and other beings. As I attempted to sit down, my foot caught on the corner of a nearby table, and I fell over. With me now on the ground, the large group of people stared at me.\n\nThey all looked at me as if they've never seen a boy on the ground. I stood up, and I felt fine... but my clothing was dirty. This was my largest concern. What if another High Elf was here? I would become a laughing stalk.\n\nI quickly ran into a nearby tavern. As I walked in, the girl behind the bar counter greeted me with, \"Karin'ay-\" but stopped short when she saw how dirty I was.\n\n\"Do you have a washroom available?\" I asked her. She pointed to a door in the back.\n\nNow cleaning up my clothes in a cauldron, I was looking around for something to dry off with. Turning around, I saw that the door was open. Another girl with purple eyes glanced at me, surprised, and she asked, \"Why are you in MY washroom?\"\n\nI didn't know what to say. I was in this girls private living space, using her supplies.\n\n\"Well, you see... the girl at the counter allowed me to clean up in here...\" She gave me a half smile, and allowed me to continue.\n\nUpon re-entering the tavern, I apologized to both girls, and explained the situation. \"That's fine,\" the girl at the counter said. \"We're happy to do whatever we can...\" She then proceeded to examine my hair and eyes closely. \"You aren't from Laureh'lin.\"\n\n\"No...\" I said. \"I'm from Haelun'or, this is my first time outside the walls.\" The second girl with purple eyes raised her eye brows. \"That makes two of us.\" she exclaimed. \"My name is Sioln'ker, and my friend is Talia'lir. She runs this tavern, I just\n\nfetch supplies for her.\"\n\n\"So you're a High Elf, then?\"\n\n\"Yes...\" she replied, \"but I left the Mother City when I was young to come here, and live with the Mali'Ame.\"\n\nI began to introduce myself as well.\n\n\"I am Elsil'ceru. I'm visiting this city in search for supplies not found in Haelun'or.\"\n\n\"What might you need?\" Asked Sioln'ker. \"We might have it in the storage room.\"\n\n\"I need some sticks, iron, and paper, if I can find them.\"\n\n\"Well, I know we have sticks somewhere. Follow me.\"\n\nAs the two of us entered the basement, there were boxes scattered around the floor. While she looked for my requested supplies, I began to ask, \"Why flee the Mother City for this tavern?\"\n\nI expected her to change to some dramatic, or nostalgic expression, but she remained neutral.\n\n\"Not enough living space there.\" She replied. \"While I did get a good education there, and I was very happy, it was far too chaotic there for me. The small\n\nrooms became an issue. I'd much rather live in an actual house. Plus, all the Mali there aren't accepting of anyone shy of perfection. So, I moved here, where I didn't have to follow proper manners all the time.\"\n\nI began my counter argument.\n\n\"But why live with another race? Surely, one of your status deserves to be with other Puritans, and not... Wood elves.\"\n\nShe raised her brow at me. \"Ah, I see... you're one of those ignorant and closed-minded Mali...\"\n\n\"Well, that's only because we ARE superior to every other race. I wouldn't say such things if it weren't true.\" I replied. \"We look beautiful, and we're well educated, unlike some of the other barbaric races.\"\n\nSioln'ker sighed at Elsil'ceru's response. \"Here, I found some sticks. Take them and go.\" she said. She then forced a bundle of sticks into my hands.\n\nWhile in the High Elven culture, it is normal to declare your own race\n\nsuperior to others- Yet, it also wasn't a young man's place to upset a lady such as herself.\n\nRunning up the stairs, sticks still in hand, I opened the door at the top of the small staircase and witnessed an\n\nalmost empty tavern. The only ones in the room were two guards sipping their drinks in the back, and Talia'lir, standing at the counter. She glanced at me and said, \"Sioln stormed out. What happened in the basement?\"\n\nTrying to think of a response, I quickly lied. \"She tripped, and stubbed her toe. I offered to take a look, but she said that she would go to a nearby doctor.\"\n\nTalia glared at me. \"Liar.\" she declared. \"There's no doctor on this side of the city.\"\n\nBlushing, I then told the truth. \"Ok, so I may have insulted her unintentionally, reguarding her... race...\"\n\n\"You brought up her leaving the Mother City, didn't you?\" she replied. \"You should at least have more manners than that, being a Puritan...\"\n\n\"I know...\" I responded. \"I want to find her and apologize.\"\n\nTalia forced a half smile. \"That wouldn't be a smart idea. She tends to be quite violent when upset. And she keeps a steel dagger in her boot all the time for protection, so you might want to\n\nstay clear of her until she's recovered.\"\n\nI just stood there, unsure of what to say. \"When can... Uh...\"\n\nTalia looked at me, confused.\n\n\"When, uhm... When can I apologize to her?\"\n\nTalia placed her elbow on the table, and her chin resting on her hand, smiling. \"We're closing in an hour, but come back by sunrise. She'll be here, ready for work. We won't be open, but I'll let you in before work hours.\"\n\nI let out a long sigh and\n\npacked my belongings.\n\n\"I suppose I'll be off, then.\"\n\nExiting the tavern, I noticed a sign on the side of it.\n\n\"Welcome to the Emerald Crescent!\"\n\nRight. The Emerald Crescent, I thought to myself. I'll return here after sunrise.\n\nAs it was now night, the streets were almost abandoned, with few night dwellers roaming the streets. I had on my expensive High Elven travel clothing, so I needed a roof to\n\nsleep under. But I wondered where that might be... I noticed several small shops outside the tavern, one was marked, \"Under renovation.\"\n\nThe tarp that was meant to go on the top of the shop lay there on the ground, so I used this as a blanket.\n\nDrifting off into sleep, I hoped that I wouldn't be mugged in the night.\n\nThankfully, the next morning, I woke up, unharmed and with all my belongings. I proceeded to walk the path to the tavern. As I was walking along the road, I figured that this city\n\nwasn't as horrible as I made it out to be.\n\nNow, almost to the tavern, I began climbing the stairs. In the window, I saw Talia cleaning the counter. That's when I heard a scream.\n\nThe scream came from behind the tavern. With no time to think, I\n\nsprinted around the side of the building to see two Valah and Sioln- One was holding her, and the other had a dagger out.\n\n\"Stay still, you greedy Mali!\"\n\nThey were obviously mugging her, simply because she was a High Elf.\n\nI was skilled with a sword, but had little to no training with hand to hand combat. However, it was my only option.\n\nSprinting into action, I went for the one with the dagger first. I hooked his ankle with my foot, bringing him to the ground.\n\nI stole the dagger, and gave him a boot to the face, rendering him unable to fight properly. With his ally on the ground, the other thug released Sioln and drew his own dagger.\n\n\"Kid, why don't you piss off before you get hurt?\" He said in a raspy voice.\n\nI smiled. \"I'd much rather take my chances.\" I said as I lunged towards his chest.\n\nHe failed miserably at blocking my blow, and fell to the ground, a dagger now in his torso.\n\nSioln rushed over to me with a smile.\n\n\"Thanks... they were trying to take my necklace.\" she said.\n\n\"Enough of that!\" I screamed back. \"I have to apologize to you... I can barely see as to how I could have been so ignorant. You were right, other races are intelligent... in fact, my own race is\n\nprobably the most stupid, for being so unaccepting and ignorant of foreign ideas...\"\n\nSioln stood there in awe. She didn't expect this type of reply at all.\n\n\"However... I don't take back what I said about High Elves being the\n\nmost beautiful.\"\n\nSioln began to make a pouting face.\n\n\"Because...\" I began again, \"you're the most beautiful person I've ever met, and no other race is superior when it comes to your attractivness...\"\n\nShe blushed, and took my hand into hers...\n\n\"If you could forgive me,\" I stated, \"I want to be just like you, and leave that dreaded city. This life seems far more like the one I would favor, over my life back in the Mother City.\"\n\n\"So, what you mean is...\" she began. \"What you're implying is that... you want to be wed?\"\n\nAll I did was smile. I said nothing, but she understood completely.\n\n\"Ok then, we'll get married soon...\" she said.\n\n\"Why not right in the tavern?\" I said with a sarcastic tone. She chuckled. \"That's not quite romantic enough, is it, Elsil?\" she replied.\n\nI swooped her off her feet. \"There's only one way to find out.\" I said.\n\nThe very next morning, it seemed as if the entire tavern was filled with not only Wood elves, but my own friends who had come all this way. Talia was in a long, flowing white dress. She leaned over and whispered to me, \"You look rather dashing. Nervous?\"\n\nI chuckled. I looked down at the blue robe I was wearing, along with the red undershirt. \"Of course I am.\" I began. \"But I'd bet anything she's just as nervous as I.\"\n\nJust then, a tall, marvelous figure walked through the door of the tavern.\n\nA tall, beautiful, slender, marvelous figure indeed. Sioln had on a long flowing green dress that didn't match my outfit at all.\n\nIt had just occurred to me that this was not a traditional wedding at all. But I didn't care. I was still getting married.\n\nStanding at the front of the tavern, we joined hands, and the ceremony had started.\n\n\n\nAnd so did our new lives.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Origins", "Finalhazard", "Arlen Teharissa's Compendium on:\n\nThe Druids\nThe Creator\nThe Aspects\nNature\n\nIn Loving Memory Of:\n\nMy Teacher: Salhassan\nMy Best Friend: Fawynn\nMy Father: Aphoros\n\nTable of Contents\n\nChapter 1: Page 5\nThe Creator\nChapter 2: Page 17\nOur Realm\nChapter 3: Page 21\nThe Druids\n\nAuthor's Note:\n\nThis book isn't for the ignorant. Nor is it for whom believe their God of choice is the only God in existence. This book is for the thinkers, the inventors, and those who wish to seek knowledge. Do not be afraid to interpret the-\n\n- teachings of your Archdruids, Paladins, Preachers, Popes, Kings, Queens, and your parents differently. You're not going against their word. You're expanding your knowledge by proceeding and thinking differently.\n\n\nChapter 1:\n\nThe Creator\n\nThe start of all lies. While I do not doubt \"The Creator\"'s existence, I doubt his ability to interfere to directly with the mortal realm. That is, if he is even still alive, or-\n\n\"Active.*\"\n\n[*]Active; I'm led to believe that Aenguls and Daemons can, or are forced to enter a dormant state where they are unable to directly influence the world, mortals, or their own patrons. Being 'Active', is when an Aengudaemon is-\n\n- whenever they are not dormant.\n\n\nThe Creator[Cont.]:\n\nThe Creator was the first to breathe life into the world. I believe that The Creator had many failed experiments in order to create-\n\n- some sort of Primordial being similar to himself. With this, I believe he was the first alchemist. \n\nWhile this may seem trite, I believe The Creator tried to create a wife, or a husband. I really don't know as The Creator may not even have a-\n\n- true gender. Perhaps The Creator was genderless. Anyways, I digress.\n\nThe Creator tried to create a companion in order to keep himself company. If his experiment succeeded or not, I do not know. Yet I believe that something tragic occured -\n\n- hence why we hear nothing of a second Creator-tier being.\n\nThen he created nature. By nature, I don't mean the grass, trees, and animals. I mean he invented a complex force (Similar to that of Mana, Life-Force, and Olive Oil) -\n\n- that cannot be governed, or maintained by any Aengul, Daemon, group, or individual.\n\nHowever, he tried to maintain it. Through this, he created The Aspects. Cerunnos, and Cerrwiden.\n\nThe two then began to maintain nature. At -\n\n- the time however, nature was contained within a garden, or a 'Grove' of sorts.\n\nEventually, nature grew rampant and spread to the nearby lands outside The Grove. The once dry deserts turned into a lushful oasis, and the lifeless tundra sprouted -\n\n- greens and turned into a dense forest.\n\nIt was beautiful.\n\nBut it all had to die.\n\nThe Creator saw all the life that had been created, and decided that there must be a counterweight in order to ensure that nature did not -\n\n- overprogress. \n\nSo, he allowed another being to roam the land. Yet, this woman was cursed. Everything she touched that was outside 'The Grove' would perish. The flowers would wilt and turn black and the dirt would lose its fertility faster than an elf.\n\nThis woman would be come to known as Nemiisae.\n\nAs time began to pass, Cerunnos, Cerridwen, and Nemiisae knew that the three of them alone could not maintain nature, so instead they passed on the art that they knew onto the ancient descendents, thus -\n\n - creating the 'Second Generation' Druids.\n\nThe Aspects themselves, being the 'First [or Primordial] Generation Druids.'\n\n\nChapter 2: \n\nOur Realm\n\n\nI'm led to believe that the realm we live in cannot be directly touhed by any Aengudaemonic being. The Creator was no fool, I imagine he setup a barrier that is -\n\n- impenetrable to specific beings. This would include the Shamanic Greater Spirits.\n\nWhy this barrier was setup, I do not know. I do have my theories, however.\n\nPerhaps The Creator forsaw himself using his powers to -\n\n - directly influence us. \n\nOr maybe, he realized that his future-kin would try to intervene in our mortal affairs. \n\nWhatever the case. I've come to this conclusion due to the fact that if the Gods could truly step down upon this realm they-\n\n - would have a long time ago, and this very land that sit on would be a warzone for the Aenguls and Daemons to fight on. \n\nInstead, they choose to have us follow beliefs, religions, and guided-mindsets in order to speak to them.\n\n\nChapter 3:\n\nThe Druids\n\n\nI say this as the son of a Druid, and as someone who has switched between the Naelurir and the Mother Circle.\n\nA catastrophe is a naturally occuring event that occurs whenever the balance is disrupted to the point of no-return. \n\nThe freezing over of Thales.\nThe drought in The Fringe.\nThe Bull Worm of Athera.\n\nAll these moments of loss is a product of our own misconduct.\n\nTo be blunt. A Catastrophe is nature's way of resetting itself from the damage that we let occur, whether it be overgrowth, or the entire destruction of nature. Do remember that nature itself-\n\n- is a primordial force that the Aspects do not control, but help maintain nature, and simply represent specific aspects of it.\n\nWhile I do not believe that the destruction of nature is the way to obtain 'balance', on the contrary I also do not -\n\n - believe in the idealogy that planting trees and having each dedicant create a grove as a Grand Task is optimal for the balance.\n\nA cruel type of irony it is that we as Druids are supposed to aid in maintaining the balance, when a large portion of\n\n- us refuse to change our lives in order to fit the balance we're meant to uphold. \n\nBut, I stray from my original topic. \n\nThe Druids as a collective whole are a unique group of individuals whom work both tegether and seperately in order-\n\nto serve the Aspects, and therefore nature in order to 'protect the balance'.\n\n\nFinal Notes:\n\n\nVol. 2 Coming Soon[TM]\n\n\n20th of The Grand Harvest, 1561.\n\nAuthor:\nArlen\nTeharissa", ""),
        StaticBook("Malinor's Fall", "§bLucion Sullas", "A Treatise upon the\nCollapse of the Unified        Elven State\n\n\n  -By Lucion Sullas\n\n\n\n\n\n\n ~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~\n\nThe ancient land of our forebears, Aegis, is historically the only period of a singular Elven state ruled over by a united authoratative body. One can cite multiple reasons for the success of said state, and few would be able to refute their truthfulness;\n\nthe success and power of the Warden military, the stability of governance brought by Prince Native and many of his subordinates, and the political respect afforded to their nation by the world-at-large to name but a few. Furthermore,\n\nand most importantly of all, the elven population had a single cultural heritage from which to draw upon--the father Malin at it's head, and his protective forests that embraced our ancestors. With the fall of Aegis and the migration to Asulon,\n\nthe remnants of Malinor did what they could to emulate the nation they lost. Their arrival on the Emerald Peninsula had them seek out what was familiar, that is, a grand forest to protect them. In this they founded their first settlement of Elandriel they\n\nthey were driven from it by the pressing of the sinister Mori. After this reversal of fortunes, they took to living in the nearby city of Normandor, and named it their capital. It was in this isolated pocket of Asulon where Malinor was broken, due to six\n\ndegenerations that occurred within a relatively short period of time.\n\n1. The Secession of\n       Haelun'or\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~With the settlement of ancient ruins at the far west of the peninsula, the high elves preferred to rule themselves. This was generally accepted by the Malinor High Council, and no official\n\ntreaty was ever deemed necessary. The high elves themselves held a referendum to gauge the desire for true independence, and the result was overwhelmingly in favour, despite the grumbling of a small number of loyalists. Thus Malinor lost a\n\nlarge number of its high elven population.\n\n2. The Hostilities of \n       the Mori\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~The natives of the land were openly hostile to all Elven elements save for those who served them. Slavers, murderers, and open degenerates in both mind and flesh, they attacked the elven colonists\n\nwith unbridled fervour. Their religious institutions may have been the cause of this, but the purpose of this aggression has never been confirmed to any degree of satisfaction.\n\n3.   The Dark Elven\n        Servitude\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Many dark elves left Malinor to join their supposed ebony-skinned brethren. Considering themselves kin to the Mori creatures, many of the dark elves wished to learn from them, and adopt their\n\nways. Not only did it lose Malinor a swathe of its dark elven population, but it also made these departed dark elves actively hostile.\n\n4. The Departure of\n      the Druids\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Displeased at the felling of trees for construction, the druids left Malinor lands in protest of the 'humanised elves.' Many have refuted that the druids were ever a true part of Malinor, as they were\n\nnever exclusively elven themselves, though the seats given to them on Malinor's High Council suggest that they had influence in its affairs despite themselves. Regardless, the loss of this link to the natural world left a cultural gap in the nation.\n\n5. The Isolation of the\n    Elven Population\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~So far away from the conflicts of the humans, dwarves and orcs, Malinor never truly needed to test themselves upon the battlefield. The population of dedicated guardsmen (already in decline\n\nduring late Aegis) dwindled significantly with personal conflicts settled by individuals amongst the population. Raiders were avoided through a closed gate, or simply by being ignored. With no threats, what martial legacy they had died away.\n\n6. The Degeneracy of\n     the Malinorian\n        Population\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Though not truly flowering into its most putrid form until mid-Anthos, Malinor's population turned often to lawlessness. Though this was abated late-Asulon somwhat with a\n\npopulation boom in civilians and guardsmen, the seeds of chaos were already planted. And an increase of political refugees from the growing Oren Empire simply made an unstable situation worse.\n\nAsulon, overall, was an abysmal time for the old Princedom, and these issues never were addressed with any real success. It finally succumbed to the Conclave of Malin and collapsed close to the end of Anthos. The cultural divides within the Elven populous\n\nare so wide that any unified state that wished to exist would require significant co-operation and trust between the Elven factions--a trust that does not exist--to stave off inevitable despotism. Many would tout compromise as a way to bridge gaps,\n\nbut one must only look upon the Conclave of Malin to see the success of compromise on a vastly diverse state.\n\nThere will never be a true 'Malinor' again. It will be for future historians to argue the implications of this.\n\n     \n\n\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~  Maehr'sae Hiylun'ehya\n\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~", ""),
        StaticBook("Normandor", "§bAzkel Frostbeard", "This is the story of how the Great Elder Tree of Normandor formed. For generations, a powerful dynasty of Elves held the seed. However this was no ordinary seed, this was an Elder Seed. It is said that of all the great trees in Aegis, only the Elder Seeds\n\ncould produce the finest and most magical entities. These seeds were tainted by both light and dark magic and would yearn to reside in the heavens but also in the void. The last known holder of this seed was Bravepaw, now chief of Normandor, founder of\n\nthe Elder Tree. Having little understanding of the seed's power, the gods truly had guided him to unleashing its full potential. xOver the long elvish years and with the considerable gaps between generations, the purpose of the seed had been somewhat lost\n\nAll that survived was the tradition to pass the seed on to the new eldest son with the simple words, “Light conquers evil, consumes darkness, embraces life and takes root even in the most darkened of places.” Bravepaw had indeed always been baffled\n\nby these words, but wore the seed around his neck, instilling in him the protection and guidance of his ancestors.\nOn his father’s deathbed, Bravepaw was told, “When the Void surrounds you, look to our greatest source of light,” with those words\n\npassing him into the beyond. The Elder Seed became incredibly sentimental to Bravepaw, he clutched it in remembrance of his father and his fathers before him; it guided him and comforted him, leading him to the beyond.\n\nSeasons passed, Bravepaw grew mature and wise. He became very close to his elvish companion Genriel, and formed a strong pact with two elves named Orlanden and Travathian. They eventually came to settle south of Laurelin,\n\nin a place called Valenwood. For a while Bravepaw found peace there, but the seed would always distract him and push him, he was never truly at home. Following a peace treaty struck between the Elves and the Orcs the four companions were given\n\nthe opportunity to run an Elvish Outpost and have much autonomy. Bravepaw accepted this position in order to expand the frontier of the Elvish power. He always remained suspicious and vigilant of the new Orcish landlords. A price of 50,000 Minas was put\n\non the land, Bravepaw set about to gather the necessary funds, the Orcs giving him one week to do so. However, the Orcs in their treachery, arrived earlier than this to collect it. They wanted a reason to ransack the place and remove the Elvish\n\npresence. On arrival, Bravepaw had managed to raise the necessary amount and offered it willingly. The Orcs, defeated in their dim-witted plan, turned it down and retreated, attempting to salvage what they would call Orcish pride. He left two younger\n\nelves to pay the orcs on their return. Supplies were running low and this hunt would be critical to their survival. Fate it would seem has a wicked sense of irony.\nThe Orcs were watching; they watched Bravepaw lead his people into the wilds to hunt.\n\nThey watched the two elves that were left behind and they watched the sun dip behind the mountains until it was day no more. That night the Orcs charged the outpost, slaughtering the two elves, taking the money and setting the place on fire.\n\nThe outpost had no hope and was scorched from the very surface of Aegis. When Bravepaw returned with his companions, they were met with smouldering ruins, the air thick with smoke, the ground covered in ash. The orcs there were\n\ntold Bravepaw this was the action taken for not paying. Bravepaw used his elvish wisdom to try and reason with them, he had given the opportunity of payment and even left the money. “Cunsidda it compens...compen..compensation”. The Orc growled;\n\n looking very pleased with himself for mustering the necessary vocabulary.\nBravepaw felt a rage build inside of him; he wanted to strike these foul orcs down now where they stood. He reached for his bow, but something stopped him.\n\nHe felt the seed beating, beating as if it were his own heart. It calmed him, it filled him with warmth, a vision flew into his head, the void, an island, a distant land, unlike anywhere he had seen in Aegis.  Was this to be his home, was his father\n\ncalling him or was this the magic of the seed driving him insane? He pondered on this for a moment. An orc seeing that his hand had reached for his bow, lunged forward, piercing his dagger into Bravepaw’s chest. His companions gasped, reaching for their\n\nswords, fearing the worst. However, Bravepaw had not been harmed. The Dagger struck the seed and the seed had erupted. The orcish metal dissolved into nothing, corruption spread through the body of the Orc, he squirmed and\n\nscreamed as he turned into Ash, a breeze rolled past, taking with it the orcish ash. The Elder Seed had awoken! The rest of the Orcs seeing the raw power of whatever this was rushed away, leaving Bravepaw and his companions\n\nin awe of the events that had just transpired. The Elder Seed was glowing now, it had been given its sacrifice, the glow of the Seed was pulling them, leading them, guiding them. The group would follow its light, to the very end of Aegis\n\nif they needed to. They made a pact that day, to never rest until they had found the home the seed was leading them to. The group played their part in the evil times to come, the rise of the Undead and the Torment of Iblees.\n\nBut this, like many things, is a different story. The companions boarded an Elven Ship on the shores of the verge. The seed had brought them here, evil had driven them here.Throughout all they had suffered the seed still shone bright.\n\n. It was drawing them across the perilous ocean; it was calling them to Asulon. On reaching the shores of this new land the seed seemed to glow brighter than ever. They knew after all these years, they were close.\n\nBravepaw and his companions were loyal to the Elves. They stayed with them to protect their people until the Elves stopped in what later would become Malinor and where the Elvish City of Elandriel was founded. For a while the seed dimmed,\n\nthey stood at the foot of the tree the Elves of Elandriel named the Mother Tree, Bravepaw buried the seed here, not understanding what he should do. They remained there for Many elven weeks, a busy bustling city sprung up around them as the Elves\n\nsettled into their new home and an Elven prince oversaw the creation of a new Elven safe haven. Bravepaw never left the site where he planted the seed at the foot of the Mother Tree. Until one fateful night he awoke.A blinding light erupted from where he\n\nplanted the seed. The light sent sparks far to the south and he heard a distant explosion. Bravepaw ripped the Seed from the earth. To his amazement, it had sprouted! (The mages of Normandor believe this to be a simple explanation:The seed\n\n had simply forgotten how to be a tree. The mother tree reminded it of this, however this will be explained in another Chronicle.) Bravepaw woke his companions and they ran in the direction of the sparks. The Elder Seed glowing  brighter than ever before\n\nemitting a single melodic tone the closer they got to the site the sparks had landed.\nThey came to the sea; across they saw a shoreline, the top smouldering, glistening in the darkness, surely the site of the explosion.\n\nThey swam across the shore, they were close now, and they knew it. They came to the top of the smouldering mass and stared down into the abyss. There a temple lay, surrounded by the darkness of the Void. (The mages of Normandor believe\n\nthis to have been the site of a Mori ritual and sacrifice to the Void.) Darkness filled their hearts, evil filled their minds; they all dropped to the floor, and the Void was consuming their very souls. As Bravepaw’s mind became dark, his vision blurred\n\nand his warmth removed, he clutched the seed tight. In his head he heard the saying of his family he had always kept true, “Light conquers evil, consumes darkness, embraces life and takes root even in the most darkened of places.”\n\nBravepaw’s mind cleared, he saw... the impossible.. the ghost of his father.. and his dying words, “When the void surrounds you look to our greatest source of light,” Bravepaw’s mind cleared, only for an instant, he knew what he must do.\n\nHe mustered the last of his strength, and casted the Elder Seed into the depths of the Void. Darkness filled him. Bravepaw and his companions were but lifeless corpses on the fringes of this foul temple and her Void. As they lay there, something never\n\n seen before happened. Shoots, roots, branches, leaves and vines sprouted from the all consuming darkness of the void. Several of the vines carried Bravepaw and his companions upwards,beneath them a tree like no other was growing.\n\nThe Elder Tree had been born in Asulon!\nIf you remember, “The seeds were tainted by both dark and light magic and would yearn to be in the heavens, but also in the Void.” It had found it’s perfect home; its roots sprawled into and filled the Void, while\n\n its branches and trunk reached out for the heavens beyond the clouds. This was truly the perfect site for the Elder Seed, and this is how the first and only Elder Tree came to Asulon.\nBravepaw and his companions awoke, gasping for air.\n\nThey were astonished; they had awoken to find themselves on top of the world. Beneath them the Elder Tree creaked in the Asulonian winds and the leaves roared. It is not known how long the tree took to sprout, but Bravepaw and his companions\n\nwere sustained by its light and had been released from the clutches of the Void. They began to climb down its branches and used her vines as ladders. When they reached the foot of the tree, they looked at each other... they had been giving their\n\n home. Travathian looked to his companions, “Normandor... Above sacred land.”\n\nThe Elder Tree was visible from much of Malinor.\n\nMany journeyed to its shores, drawn by its light. Bravepaw, Genriel, Orlanden, and Travathian appointed themselves as Chiefs and Chiefesses of all of Normandor, founders, protectors, and rulers. They created the first High Council of Normandor\n\nto oversee its development. High Magister Kyrenn, High Commander Ithil, and High Lord Thrase now sit on the council and oversee many different aspects of its development. They work with the chiefs to ensure harmony\n\nand prosperity across all of Normandor and the Elder Tree. The inside of the Elder Tree and indeed the very top became space for many homes and buildings and is home to some of the finest Elven Architecture in the lands.\n\nOn one fateful day the High Prince of the Elven Lands of Malinor decided to journey to Normandor. He had heard the many tales of its beauty and tranquility. He had been busy securing the future of Malinor and it was not until\n\nnow he found the time to observe the Elder Tree in all her glory. On arriving at the bridge and meeting Bravepaw, he could not believe his eyes. This truly was the place of elven might and power.  Its beauty and nature overwhelmed him.\n\nHe consulted the High Elven Council and talked to the Princess of Malinor. In his wisdom he decreed that the Elder Tree City of Normandor was to become Capital of All Malinor.The City of Elves, a bastion of Elvish power, home to the High Council Chambers\n\nand would house Malinor’s central government. The citizens of Normandor rejoiced and celebrated. Bravepaw was appointed Prince of Malinor and joined the other Princes and Princesses in the Malinorian Government.\n\nHis loyal companions and rulers of Normandor were declared as Lords and Lady of the Elven Realm. The Elder Tree, reborn, revitalised and rejoiced.\nOver the years as the Elder Tree has grown, its roots plunging ever deeper into the\n\nVoid and its branches ever further into the heavens the tree’s arcane aura has expanded significantly. Many mages have been drawn to this raw arcane infusion and believe tremendous power can be drawn from it.", ""),
        StaticBook("En Sapientia", "§bAmbros", "En Sapientia\n\nBy Ambros\n\nHe who is deemed a sage does not speak it so, for only an internal awareness can recognize itself as a sage.  The outward conscience does not know such things, only the soul.  Thusly, it is not the mortal body that is a sage but the immortal and\n\ntranscendent mind.  So often do we look to the Ascended Sages, for there has not been a sage since such archaic times.  But what sages were they?  Worshippers of a venerable and apathetic deity so many have forgotten that which we call Aerial.  These\n\n“sages” were not but elevated priests, given this divine right by by their almighty.  Nay!  For the true sage need not assistance from higher functions to elevate their soul.  The sage need only his own mind, through effort and inquiry does the sage\n\nelevate their soul.  Though the sage may worship their alphas, he dare not put his own reasoning into the hands of such abstract powers.  The philosopher is not wise, but possesses the self-awareness of lacking wisdom, and thus pursues it.  In tandem, the\n\nsage lives according to an ideal which transcends the everyday, and wishes to share this wisdom.  However, the sage would never engage in any system-building or reputation nor engage in its cumbersome analysis. Nor do they write obscure texts, and much\n\nless do they dream up a philosophical system. What they write down is moderate, peaceful, and non-pretending, and everyone understands their thought. After reading it, our eyes shine bright and blister, our minds are clear and free of confusion, and we\n\nrealize: Yes, this is how things truly are.\n\nIt is the view of sages and their stoic followers that there are two races, that of the worthwhile, and that of the worthless.  The race of the worthwhile employ the virtues through all of their lives, while the race of the worthless employ the vices.\n\nHence the worthwhile always do the right thing on which they embark, while the worthless do wrong.\n\nWhile the sage is ever a student, the sage is the eternal teacher.  He who has found peace of mind in his studies and wishes only to spread his knowledge, for it is the duty of the sage to take on disciples.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipies", "§bVlad Shade", "Alchemist Fire\nMandragora\nSerpent stalk\nDwarf pumpkin\nDrake tail\nFlame tongue\nCrimson vase\nCrouching feather\n\nStone Skin\nMandragora\nSerpents\nDwarf pumpkin \nDrakes tail\nFlame Tongue\nCrouching feather\nalabaster leaf\nClutchers straw\nMetzils tangle\nAlabaster leaf\n\nHealing \nAthin\nFrost Vine\nCrouching feather\nElfs hair\nTippens\n\nSmoke\nBlood lotus\nDrake tail\nFingers\nCrouching feather\n\nFrost oil\nSerpents stalk\nAthin\nFrost vine\nCrouching feather\nElfs hair\nTippen\n\nMinor healing \nTippen\n\nBurn healing\nTippen and serpents\n\nLesser Regen \nTippen and mandragora\n\nStrong Regen\nTippen mandragora serpent", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide,3-2", "§bDaniel Nodal", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 1 – Alchemy Potions\nPart 2/3\n\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\n Potion of Celerity\n\nAlso know as potion of agility and potion of speed, the potion of celerity is the one that raises the movement speed and reflexes of the body. Normally wanted to be used for combat, many find it better to use when following\n\nprey while hunting, or even by thieves to quickly escape when seen. The side effects happen as all do when the body is changed temporarily, the body is not made to move faster than it does, and forcing it to do it may bring extreme pain to the muscles\n\nafter the effects are done.\n\n Recipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n3 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n\n Potion of Accuity\n\nAnother air based potion like the potion of Celerity, the potion of Accuity is an amazing potion to create and use. This potion provides the drinker with heightened senses, and may be prepared to all senses, or to specific ones for\n\nmore potency. Often times uses for hunting or for taking a job in hard tasks with require great vision, hearing or touch. In a competition of cooking for example, the judges might drink this potion beforehand for greater ability to judge the taste,\n\nand it is said that with it, someone might smell poison in their drink when there is no smell to sense. The result of enhancing the senses for a short while, is the worsening of them afterwards for a short while.\n\n Recipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbol of Air (Perception)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n3 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n Potion of Wizard’s        Wisdom\n\nEver wanted to feel like the great scholars of old? Then this potion is the one to use. A potion able to enhance the mental capabilities of the drinker temporarily. Fantastic even in it’s purple color, it enhances\n\none’s problem solving, reasoning, and general thinking ability. Of great use to alchemists as well, especially when formulating new potions and you find yourself stuck on a problem. This potion will aid you, not by giving you ideas as soon as you drink it\n\nbut by clearing your mind, making you think fluidly, and giving you a new perspective on the matter. It’s reagents are many, so to create them you would take quite the lengthy time, and the migraines it creates are long, yet if you enhancing it with\n\npurer ingredients will diminish some of the migraines. Another point about this potion could perhaps be it’s effects when dealing with memory loss, it might make the person have an easier time remembering, mind invasion from mental magic, becoming fully\n\nin control of your own mind placing the mage outside of control, and while being interrogated or lying, you will appear far more truthful in your responses.\n\n Recipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n1 Symbol of Fire (Power)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Air (Perception)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n3 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n2 Symbols of Fire (Power)\n3 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n2 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n5 Symbols of Water (Balance, Clarity)\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power)\n3 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n3 Symbols of Air (Perception)\n\n Potion of Smoke\n     Whispers\n\nLike in the magical stories where the hero throws something into the floor and a cloud of smoke is formed, this potion creates the same as the vial shatters. Creating black smoke which releases fast,\n\nit allows the thrower a distraction or the ability to temporarily incapacitate the adversaries. Brewed over the course of four weeks, this potion is literally smoke in a bottle, and like it, it creates irritation when breathing and to the eyes.\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Air Symbols (Flight, Transport)\n1 Fire Symbol (Combustion)\n\nPotion of Sleeping\n\nUsed by people of all kinds, this potion’s objective is placing someone to sleep. It might act fast, it might act slow, it might be to help with sleeping at night, or it might be to knock someone out. The use  will dictate the powe\n\nthe potion has to be made. Minor potions will have more of a relaxing result, making resting far more easier to achieve. Lesser ones have to be drank, and soon after the potion will take effect and the one to drink it will not even noticed he passed out.\n\nGreater potions on the other hand, might not even need to be drank, only smelled. This is particular good for doctors when needing to operate, as it does not force the patient to drink something. To force it on someone might not have some good results,\n\nas the potion does take it’s time to give it’s effect, enough to fight back. It is worth noticing, that if the recipe is only read by the symbols needed, the lesser potion of sleeping is the same as the lesser potion of mending.\n\nAlways be careful with the representations of the symbols you use.\n\n Recipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation) \n1 Symbols of Air (Speed) \n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n2 Symbols of Air (Flight and Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n Freezing Potion\n\nA peculiar potion, which it’s only intent is freezing or cooling something, or perhaps to just keep cold. I invented one of the potions, the potent one which quickly freezes water on contact, making it something impossible to\n\ndrink and dangerous to the touch. This potion must actually be kept in a thick glass vial, and it then must be surrounded by fur, or something of the like as to keep it your hands from freezing and stopping water from getting stuck to the vial. Another\n\nrecipe was made by my student, one far weaker, and more for a use in someone’s body. To put it simply, it is a protection of a person’s cold, which seems quite horrible for most times. If you’re travelling into very hot climates, it could save one’s life.\n\n Recipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbols of Water (Cold) \n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Ice, Cold) \n1 Symbol of Earth (Protection)\n\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Hardiness)\n\n  Waterbreathing           Potion\n\nA remarkable potion which allows someone to breath underwater for long periods of time. The biggest side effect of this potion thought, is that you stop breathing air. Made of rare ingredients, mainly the liver\n\nand gills of a mermaid, it turns someone temporarily into a sort of human fish. Your skin between the toes and hands starts webbing, only falling out a few hours after the effects of the potion are gone. Being too long inside of the water also makes it so\n\nthat when you finally leave, your ears seem to pop, and you can not hear for a few minutes. The most important part of the potion, is the creation of gills on the side of your neck, it is these that allow you to breathe underwater, yet not above.\n\nFor the first few moments of the transformation, someone can not breathe in or out of water, it may create panic in some people, yet they must remember to go into the water, because they will not be able to breath out of it.\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae mixed with a bit of seawater\nMermaid’s Gills and Liver\n1 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n\nContinues in part 3\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe First Seed, 1501", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide,3-3", "§bDaniel Nodal", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 1 – Alchemy Potions\nPart 3/3\n\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\n      Frost Oil\n\nAs an oil, this concoction has an external use and is not to be drank. As I move on to the oils, the type of base fixes itself to only lard. Lard has a more viscous consistency which allows it not to drip when coated on something.\n\nThe Frost oil, or Oil of frost, is the first I will speak of. An oil which has similarities with the potion of freezing, but whose effects last longer, enabling it to be coated in weapons, armor or clothes. It’s effects are quick cooling what it touches,\n\nbut it reacts weakly to air, meaning it’s effect happen mostly through contact. Using such an oil on clothing or armor might make the wearer cold, but such could be the objective if one is travelling into hot deserts or inside volcanos. On the other hand,\n\nwhile it might make the cloth or armor it was placed cold, it also offers a protection to the same cold, as something already cold, can’t get colder. As such, if one would use the oil of frost in cloth, he would find himself getting colder, yet if the oil\n\nwas used on the outer layer of plate armor, it would assist with protecting against cold winds. If placed on weapons, it has the most use on swords. A slash of the weapon on someone’s skin would slightly freeze the area. You might think a frozen cut would\n\nmean that the blood loss would be lower, and it’s true, yet freezing the area will make the skin turn slightly blue, and the opponent will have difficulties moving the area, this way slowing him down. The cut makes the cooling enter more into the flesh,\n\nperhaps even forcing someone to have to remove the frozen flesh affected, perhaps even amputating a limb. The biggest worry of using it on tools and armor is that it also cools it down substantially,\n\nand when badly brewed will make metals very easily shattered, when well brewed they will still suffer some problems in lesser effect.\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Water(Cold, Ice, Frost)\n2 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Fortitude)\n\n      Flame Oil\n\nThe Oil of Flame does almost the exact same as the Oil of Frost, yet instead of cold, it works with burning. In contact with the skin it will leave huge burns, but of course you do not apply it to the skin. On weapons, when striking\n\nthe enemy, it will cause great pain a kind to burning. The flesh will become red and start to create puss. While the Oil of Frost might slow the opponent, the oil of Flames inflicts greater damage on him. The weapon does not escape damage, while with the\n\nFrost Oil the weapon may shatter, with the Flame Oil it corrodes it. While the effects do seem like those of a flame, it does not actually heat an object up. It actually make it more flame repellent and heat resistant. Of course the resistance isn’t\n\nperfect, and a wooden sword coated with the oil will still catch ablaze, it does tarden the effects. This means if applied to armor or cloth that is not touching the skin, it creates a sort of heat and flame protection. In the recipe it is found the use\n\nof a water symbol, and one might ask why such a representation is required in such a fire centric concoction.\n\nThe explanation is rather simple, it calms the fire symbols of destruction and power, balancing it to not create an extreme reaction, which normally is represented as an explosion while brewing.\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power, Destruction, Burning)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Water (Calmness, Balance)\n\n    Fortitude Oil\n\nQuite a simple oil to explain with the phrase “Armor for your Armor”. An oil which holds material together and makes it last longer or resist further violent means against it. The problems with this simple oil aren't many,\n\nas it has about the same effect with weapons, armor, or even special goods that one might want to protect. While armor will resist greater forces on it, as soon as the limit is reached, the oil would be as not there, even making the armor break more\n\nsubstantially. This makes the armor ideal for arrows, sword cuts, piercing, and jabs, yet if a warhammer or battle axe hit full on, it would make the armor shatter. For weapons this problem does not exist, as the weapon is made to hold and last great\n\nforce, if being used correctly. As such this oil is ideal to prolong the life of the weapon it is used on. Just remember to never mix any of these oils in armor or weapons, as the consequences would be quite high. From rusting, fracturing, to even melting\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Earth (Endurance, Fortitude, etc.)\n2 Symbols of Fire (Potency, Power)\n\n Hair Rejuvenating\n          Oil\n\nAn oil of my own invention, and one that I am quite pleased with. Simply be, depending on what ingredients you use, it rejuvenates hair, grows hair that was falling, and grows hair where the oil touches.\n\nThe weaker version will simply make the hair stronger and shinier, while the middle one helps hair grow back and grow faster, if someone is going bald, or lost hair due to a prank, or accident. The stronger version is quite dangerous.\n\nWhile it’s main purpose is to grow hair, it also grows anything else it touches. As such, the area it is used in must be cleaned, clean gloves must be used or you risk gaining furry fingers, and a clean cloth must be used to spread the oil.\n\nIf anything contaminates the oil, it is more likely that fungus or bugs start growing instead of hair, and if you drink it… lets just say I’ve seen someone puke a moving fish after drinking it, and the tongue looked quite furry.\n\n Recipe:\n\n- Minor\nBase: Lard\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed/Grace)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Hardiness) \n1 Symbol of Water (Rejuvenation)\n\n- Lesser\nBase: Lard\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed/Grace)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Hardiness and Regrowth) \n1 Symbol of Water (Rejuvenation)\n\n- Greater:\nBase: Lard\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed/Grace)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Hardiness and Regrow)\n2 symbol of Water (Rejuvenation and Life)\n\n      Glue Oil\n\nA simple oil of my own creation after discovering the ingredient which I named Jailer’s moss. It is a weak air symbol representing travel, but it has the property of glue as well. Through testing I realised that I could\n\nstrengthen the glue properties, and make them react only when movement is happening. Something interesting to use when fixing machinery or to make shoes that do not slide in ice, but allow you to walk on it easily.\n\nWithout using that particular ingredient, and using an air symbol representing speed, then the effects change and the glue becomes one that hardens quickly after applying, especially good in construction.\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Lard\n1 Symbol of Air (Travel/Speed)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Hardiness, Connection)\n\n  Alchemist’s Fire\n\nOne of the greatest means of attack from an alchemist, their creation of fire. Many times referred to as ‘fire in a bottle,’ a more correct term would be ‘liquid fire.’ The alchemist is able to access the properties of fire\n\nand add them into a liquid, making it highly volatile and dangerous. Getting hit by a bottle of it would result in being completely covered in flames. Of course it’s not always used for combat, and many times it is used to cleaning metal utensils,\n\nsuch as cauldrons and knives from contaminants and impurities. Some may say it’s a waste to use such a powerful potion on cleaning, but it’s use that way may also be associated with it’s danger. Alchemist’s fire is highly dangerous, both while brewing and\n\nafter. To brew it, not only must the temperature of the concoction remain constant, that temperature must be almost freezing. The room must be made almost specifically to create it, as it must be freezing cold. Some alchemists have started using Thanhium\n\nto have a cold room in their labs instead of heading to the tall mountains, or the cold Tundras. In those conditions the alchemist’s fire is made, and even brewed in freezing temperatures, it still can burn your hand. The bottles must be made of glass,\n\nand to fill them, thongs of sorts must be used, as if your hand touches the liquid it will be burnt, even will gloves. Never should an alchemist fall into the mistake of using a wooden cork, as the liquid will burn right through it and most likely blow\n\nin the hands of the handler, they must always be made of glass. Now onto the dangers of the bottles. If they are kept stationary, they won’t be dangerous, it’s problems start when they are moved quickly or hit. Some alchemists keep them in Athin made\n\nsacks to prevent some of it’s force if the bottle is broken, yet even so the alchemist would be burnt. Falling while carrying these bottles, or being pushed, punched, thrown to the ground may result in a bottle breaking. For them to be useful they must\n\nbreak, but that is the bane of the carrier as well. For each bottle a man carries, when subjected to what was mentioned above, there is a 30% chance of it breaking and setting the man on fire. Throwing the bottle hard will have 30% chances of blowing up\n\nin the thrower’s hand, and even if thrown underarm for more safety still has 15% chances of blowing when thrown. The safest way to use the bottle is to drop it, it results in it blowing up when hitting the ground in almost all cases. While it does\n\nexplode, it’s explosion is not one like tnt, but more of many tiny shards of glass and fire sent flying everywhere. Normally for cleaning that I spoke above, the explosion is avoided by opening the bottle and pouring it out fast enough so that it doesn't\n\nbreak the bottle. Alchemist’s fire releases air as it burns, meaning it can’t be easily extinguished and will continue to burn until it’s gone. There are only a few specific ways to stop it from burning, normally requiring an alchemy potion to counter\n\neffect it, a freezing potion for example could work. The way it behaves is almost like burning oil, a liquid aflame.\n\n Recipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Symbols of Fire (Destruction, Power, Consumption)\n2 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe First Seed, 1501", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Dangers", "§bVlad Shade", "Alchemy Dangers\nAlthough alchemys art is of pure bueaty it has a dark side a very dark side the dangers are very high considering that all the potions could literally blow up in your face some potions shouldnt even be touched\n\nmost potions are of good nature but some have a volitile or poisoness effect\n\n let us use the commonly known alchemist fire for example its a dealy volotile potion and explodes on air contact\n\nthis potion should be trusted not for its nature is of destruction and if you are experienced should know not to be used unless dangerous\n\nThe there are the poisons these are made by the fools in the alchemy world they only act on impulse and destruction and all they want is someones death this is why sometimes even i am ashamed to be a alchemist but i am a master and lover of the art", ""),
        StaticBook("AlchemyGuide 2-3", "§bDaniel", "\\_+*+_/\n   Alchemy Guide\n\n\n\nVolume 2 – Reagents\nPart 3/4\n\n\n\n\n-By Daniel Nodal\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    Water Symbols\n\n           Weak\n\nName: Blissfoil\nRepresentation: Calmness\nDescription: Blissfoil is a plant which has more applications in healing medicine than in alchemical healing potions. While it has been found to have weak potency in\n\nalchemy, it’s effects are quite used in healing. I do not believe many good doctors exist which have not heard of the use of this plant, for it is one of the best numbing agents out there. The greatest problem with it, is the fact that overuse of it\n\ncauses permanent numbing to an area, and with the power it holds, it happening by someone who does not know of it’s effects is quite possible. The plant itself looks like a combination of many small blue flowers with a thick and flimsy stalk.\n\nIt’s harvesting is as easy as collecting a flower, and it is born and grows under birch trees near grassy areas.\nLocation: Under birch trees in grassy areas.\n\n         Moderate\n\nName: Serpent's Stalk\nRepresentations: Life, Rebirth, Calmness, Fluity\nDescription: This plant is one of the easiest to find in the forest of the realm. If one knows where there are small ponds and perhaps streams of\n\nwater, they would find the Serpent's Stalks. Long Emerald hued stalks, with stripes and ridges of a lighter green and brown along its length, almost like a serpent. Sometimes simple mistaken by normal sugar canes by those unknown of this plant, and to\n\ncollect it is the same as well, cutting it at the bottom. Another attribute it holds similar to a serpent’s it’s the poisonous nature it has. Specific insects that come in contact with it are normally found dead all around them, yet from all it’s use, no\n\nother creatures seem to die from it. It is actually used many times as a healing agent, specifically to heal and treat burn wounds. A normal oinkment from simply crushing the plant and mixing it with some water may smooth the pain and keep the wound\n\nsafe. It is as such a good water symbol that can be used for antidotes, oils, balms, healing potions and potions of calmness, as well as fire extinguishing potions. Those are good to have when preparing alchemist fire.\n\nLocation: Grows near small bodies of water in forests.\n\nName: Mandragora\nRepresentation: Rejuvenation, Life, Rebirth, Regrowth\nDescription: This is one of the reagents most alchemists use when creating more powerful healing potions. It’s meat rejuvenating traits makes it so that it’s very well\n\nsought by many alchemists. No good ingredient comes without its dangers thought, and Mandragora is one that can bring a collector to a very humiliating death. It is observed growing into the shallow waters of swamp lands, with thick green leaves flowing\n\nout of the water. Such leaves are quite delicious as garnish for soups and to make a quite rejuvenating tea. It is highly nutritious as well, filled with nutrients. It is not the leaves that one uses as a reagent thought, but the “meat” of the plant.\n\nThe part of the plant normally underwater is shaped like a parsnip. It is this “meat” that needs to be collected and prepared to use by the alchemists. Collecting it it’s not as easy as it sounds. This plant creates highly narcotic and paralyzing spores\n\nwhich are released quite easily when the plant is moved. Inhaling them brings the humiliating death of drowning in a foot high water level, as you simply fall on it and are unable to move any further. As such, one has to known when the spores are fully\n\nreleased, and only then retrieve them. Normally simply a group of at least two set out to find them, and one pulls the other from death if the spores are still with the plant.\nLocation: Low waters in wetlands.\n\nName: Zawabate\nRepresentations: Life, Calmness, Rebirth\nDescription: Zawabate is the only reagent I know of water representation in the desert lands. Of course it does not grow in the middle of the sand, it grows in the rare oasis one might\n\nfind. There is it protected from the strong heat and low humidity, in the fertile grounds around the waters. It is identified by it’s prominent colors of green and yellow, with short curvy petals in a long stalk. For those that live near them, especially\n\nthe orcish shamans, it is a plant which brings a higher connection with the spirits and out of body experiences. To say more simply, Zawabate is a hallucinogen, creating great comfort and sensations, which are further enhanced if made into a stong tea.\n\nThese flowers are certainly an amazing addition to potions designed to numb pain or heal because of their home in such a lush landscape among a wasteland of aridity. The amount to use however, should be greatly controlled, due to its hallucinogenic\n\nproperties. Too much, and the healing potion might cause delusions and mental problems, which means it can also be used for to create those same poison effects if wanted.\nLocation: In the fertile lands of an oasis\n\nName: Frost Vine\nRepresentation: Life, Calmness, Ice\nDescription: Frost vine is a blue vine which grows in very cold climates. It seems to have a need for shade, the most common of places being around trees, leaving long blue spirals in their\n\ntrunks and falling from their branches. The plant is a very good numbing agent, one which can easily be made into a sticky clear substance which numbs pain and loosens stiff muscles. Too much of it and it may affect the limbs, making them completely numb\n\nfor some time. One must always be sure to wash the area after applying the substance. It is by some considered a good alternative to a stronger but also more dangerous rubbing agent, Blissfoil, and with some other good qualities in the mix.\n\nThe vine is quite cold to the touch, which means it may also be further used to better the effect of numbing and treating wounds and pains. With these traits, it is no wonder that this plant represents life, calmness and cold.\n\nLocation: Shaded areas in cold climates.\n\n          Strong\n\nName: Athin\nSymbol: Freezing, Ice, Life, Balance\nDescription: Athin is quite a potent Water Symbol that one finds in ice lakes and rivers of fresh water, which are normally frozen. It is a blue algae, sometimes quite hard\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nto find if the ice is too thick or the snow stops vision to the unfrozen water under the ice. One particular difficult to harvest plant, not due to the how you take it, but because of the danger associated with it. It is taught to all alchemists that it\n\ntakes two people to harvest Athin. This is because it grows under ice, on very cold water. The ice breaking and someone getting stuck is a high probability, but either way you will have to get into the water to collect it. Sometimes people get stuck\n\nunder the ice, not knowing where the hole they did was, sometimes they do not take precautions with heating before entering the water and freeze in the cold wind when out. Such dangers do come with reward, as Athin is one of the materials there are that\n\nare fireproof. It has a great representation of ice and freezing, and an alchemist that carries around alchemist fire, or simply makes it, always have some athin around with them as well.\n\nLocation: Under the ice of frozen lakes and rivers.\n\nContinues in part 4\n\nWritten by Daniel Nodal\nThe Grand Harvest, 1480", ""),
        StaticBook("Lean", "§bThurak'Yar", "LEAN\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=Gifted to Thurak'Yar by the Eternal Librarian\n\nThe origins of Lean, or more commonly known as Purple Drink Precede man himself according to the legend of Lawrence Lean. Back in the First Era, a would-be herbalist was delving far into the reaches of Aegis, looking to change the face\n\nof the herbal game forever. It was after thirty years of searching that his thirst was quenched. He had discovered the Chrono root.\nIt glowed when he unearthed it on accident on a hot day in the wastes. Shocking and\n\nmagnificent, he gently uncovered it without severing it from its host. A perfect extraction was followed by mass excavation. Before too long Lawrence Lean had a nice stockpile of Chrono and made preparations to head back to the heartland\n\n of Aegis to share his findings. Upon a relatively short trip back to his lab the herb fiend got to work. Mashing, grinding, and boiling, Lean went wild trying to find a use for his life’s work. It was in a workplace rage that he discovered the\n\nChrono root actually grey out of root form and created a dense bush of sweetly scented buds and flowers. When a mature plant’s roots are grinded and boiled in cheesecloth Lawrence Lean found himself with a powerful elixir. It’s effects are\n\nknown to slow down perceived time in the user’s mind and induce an intense warped effect about the user. When dried out the buds made for great pipe tobacco substitute. Chrono’s use as a smokable remedy has recently been made\n\nfamous by Hansetians, notably adiv Chrono user the late Darius “Lion”. Lawrence Lean proclaims his elixir Lean to be the key to immortality. It has been described as a mixture that looks like the deep heavens in colour and enthralling in nature\n\nwhen staring directly into a fresh mug of Lean.\n\nAfter note:\nTexts recovered from a Human library prior to its closure.\nOriginal texts indicate the author was one Lion III of the Holy Oren Empire from the era of Asulon.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Bonds", "§bVlad Shade", "The True Art of Alchemy:\n\nAlchemy isnt just a passion or a carrier\nits a art beyond that of anyother such as one might call cooking a culinary art alchemy is one of its own beyond any limit it is seen as a art beyond our human eyes\n\nWe see alchemy as a combo of herbs to just benifit us but the truth is alchemy isnt just that it is the blessed art of making something from once useless materials you can even spring forth the life of your own body with them\n\nWe are but the  essential elements of these potions mixed togther to do nothing but fix our inperfections but it can be so much more we can be of the greatest kind of race working togther we can become what we deisre by one bond", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy In. V.II", "§bLibrarian Azkel Frostbeard", "Alchemy Ingredients Volume II: Fire Symbols\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Desert Bush Berry:\n\nRepresentations: Empowerment, Overwhelming\n\nLocations: Desert Cactus Forests in full moon days\n\nDescription: withered rose bush with brownish-gray stalks\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Flametongue's Root:\n\nRepresentations:Heat, Anger, Destruction\n\nLocation: Forest hillsides and burnt forests\n\nDescription: Bright. red colored roots\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Dwarf's Pumpkin:\n\nRepresentations: Rage,Envy,Bitterness, Power, Strength\n\nLocations: Swamplands next to muddy waters\n\nDescription: odious orange-leafed shrub\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Blood Lotus:\n\nRepresentations: Hope, Courage\n\nLocation:Entrance of caves in hot desert lands\n\nDescription: rusty- colored vine\n\nElrow Berries:\n\nRepresentations: Rage, Anger, Burning\n\nLocation: Bushes in cold climates\n\nDescription: Unedible Berries\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_\n\nCrimson Vase:\n\nRepresentations: Heat, warmth, love, passion, compassion\n\nLocations: At the base of young trees\n\nDescription: A crimson flower with large petals\n\nDrake's Tail:\n\nRepresentations: Heat, Destruction, Chaos\n\nLocation: In deserts close to  oasis\n\nDescription: A red-dusky colored plant", ""),
        StaticBook("Intro. Alchemist", "§bRosso", "Forward:\n\nGreetings, my dear reader. Within the pages of this tome I shall be explaining to you what it takes to be a true Alchemist. I shall run you through our mindset, qualities, and traditions.\n\nChapter I: \n\nThe Recipe for a Good Alchemist.\n\nIt takes a special breed of person to be an Alchemist: a rare combination of patience, creativity, intellect, enthusiasm, and of course, an unusual resistance to explosions. For those who wish to follow the noble path of an Alchemist, you must first\n\nconsider if you possess these key traits, for if you do not, much like a potion made with a flawed recipe, you will be ineffective.\n\nDo not mistake what I say for supremacism, but rather realize that it is simply an effort to save you.\n\nIf you do not possess these qualities, dear reader it is quite possible that you, in the attempt to follow our path, may indeed end up harming yourself and others. Now, allow me to go more in-depth about what these traits mean, in order of their\n\nimportance to an Alchemist: Patience. Alchemy, I'm afraid, is not a fast nor particularly interesting process. An Alchemist will spend most of their time for their elembic to boil so that the extract of your ingredients may be collected for use, or simply\n\nwaiting for a potion to brew. Very little of the time it takes to create a potion is actually spent creating the mixture, although this time is indeed the most interesting part of Alchemy in my opinion. Because of all this, dear reader, Patience is vital\n\nto an Alchemist, as it would be, in my opinion, apt to give Alchemy the formula consisting of 25 \"parts\":\n\n>7 parts waiting (brewing, waiting for the elembic's water to boil.)\n\n>5 parts setting up, cleaning up, and inspecting your equipment.\n\n>5 parts testing (making sure your potion works.)\n\n>3 parts failure (realizing that your recipe is bollocks and starting over.)\n\n>3 parts extracting (boiling down the ingredients and collecting the condensation using an alembic.)\n\n>1 parts explosion (surprisingly common.)\n\n>1 parts mixing the bloody potion.\n\nIntellect. You may be wondering why this is not the first on my list... Well, someone whom is completely dull, yet is willing to give up hours upon hours to make up for it, is much more likely to succeed than someone brilliant who will become frustrated\n\nand rushed the second they see that their brew will be taking a very long time to finish. So, while this trait is near-vital, it can be made up for by a massive amount of patience and dedication.\n\nCreativity. Please note that I am writing here to explain what it takes to be an Alchemist, not a good Alchemist. Creativity is something completely vital to anyone who wishes to be a master alchemist... but it is not to those whom only wish to dabble in\n\nthe art. Creativity is essential for those who wish to truly excel at the art, because one cannot just follow recipes forever. There comes a time, when an apprentice Alchemist must take these matters into their own hands. No more books, no more\n\nexplanations as to why these particular ingredients must be used... there comes a time, when every Alchemist must learn to rely on their own extensive knowledge of herbs and ingredients, of what they represent... To craft a potion of their very own. This\n\nis much more difficult that it may seem... but I shan't go into that, as this is no true Alchemical textbook, but rather, an introduction to the science.\n\nLastly, Enthusiasm. This trait may seem unusual to list, as most would automatically think of someone who is impulsive and stupid... 'overly' enthusiastic. No, Alchemy requires a special type of enthusiasm. One tempered by logic and patience.\n\nEnthusiasm is needed because without the drive to learn more, to improve your skill and to create potions of wonder... you are nothing, and shall go nowhere. The reason this is listed at the bottom of the list instead of the top, is simply because by\n\nreading this far, you have shown an ounce of this quality, and as such, you may now take pride in knowing that you have the potential to learn this art.\n\nChapter 2:\n\nThe Life of an Alchemist\n\nThe Lifestyle.\nThe life of an Alchemist, despite its labours, is a rewarding and interesting one. Many nations, many companies, and many guilds need Alchemists, which means that an Alchemist is very rarely without options. Indeed, one of the\n\nmost fulfilling parts of being an Alchemist is the satisfaction of knowing you are needed and wanted. An Alchemist rarely stays in one location whilst unemployed, often traveling from kingdom to kingdom, seeking a guild or company that has use for them.\n\nIn my experience, this does not take a long time... Although it is important to measure one's options before taking a job. This is not to say that an Alchemist /must/ be employed, gods know many Alchemists choose a life of reclusion and\n\nintroversion--preferring the company of their ingredients and their equipment to the company of people or currency. As an Alchemist, there are many different paths one may choose from; be it creating poisons or creating potions to heal the ill and\n\nwounded. These paths will play  large role in deciding how easily one can get jobs, and from whom. For example, one who makes poisons may find it harder to get a job than one who creates healing serums as poisons are hard to mass-produce, which means are\n\nof no real use to an army, and they are certainly of no use for a peaceful company of a guild. This is much the opposite for healing potions, as oddly enough they are some of the simplest to make, and can be made in quite large quantities without losing\n\nmuch of their effectiveness, so all the aforementioned employers could have a use for an Alchemist of healing. Do not think that these are the only two paths that an Alchemist may follow, however--and certainly no Alchemist sticks only to one branch of\n\nthe science--think of it more as a specialization. I, for example, specialize in incendiary and explosive concoctions. Alchemist Fire, if you will. This means that I am often asked to work for militaries of small offensive guilds… even research companies.\n\nIn conclusion, the lifestyle in terms of business, for an Alchemist, is much dependent upon what they specialize in, and regardless of their business opportunities the life of an Alchemist is rarely full, and in my opinion, very fulfilling.\n\nThe Morality of an Alchemist.\nMany jobs seem to have a set \"morality.\" Soldiers are expected to be lawful and trustworthy, Mages to be intellectual and neutral... the Morality of an Alchemist is much the same as that of a Mage, and is, much like\n\none's business opportunities dictated by the Alchemist's specialization. To use the same examples as before, it would be more likely for an Alchemist specializing in poisons to fall into a darker, more \"evil\" morality, as they would likely find their work\n\nwith small groups of assassins of the like. Whereas an Alchemist specializing in healing, would be much more likely to fall into a \"good\" morality, as by nature, their job of choice is to help people. There are not set rules, of course, a healing\n\nAlchemist may very well choose to assist an unlawful organization, and an Alchemist of poison may choose to supply a small vigilante group with his poisons. In essence, the Morality of an Alchemist is neutral, and is dictated completely by the individual.\n\nThe Living Conditions of an Alchemist\nThis section will be somewhat small, as there is very little that can be written about this. An Alchemist's abode will generally have only one constant: a lab, usually located in a basement. This lab would contain\n\nan Alchemist's supplies, equipment, and is generally kept locked, as much inside would be fragile or dangerous. As for why it is usually found in a basement… Well… The earth seems to be a bit more resistant to explosions than wooden or cobblestone walls.\n\nFarewell:\nI thank you, reader, for taking the time to read my small introduction to the Alchemist. I bid you good ever, good day, and farewell.\n\nAn Introduction to the Alchemist\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Journal", "§bLion", "[!] Seemingly, this was the start of a discontinued journal. The author didn't appear like much of a writer.\n\nDay --\n\nMy dear Fox has given me this gift of a sentient statue; one carved from the shape of a lion, with part of my aura inside of it.\n\nThough I did have to give up my claw, which now sits within the statue's hollow underbelly. I think-\n\n-we should of gone with a less cliche weak spot maybe.\n\nI can't attack it while it's on duty mode, though thankfully my dear Fox set up this game to keep it occupied. If I ever need my weapon back, I can just finish this little puzzle thing and reclaim-\n\n-it after the statue 'sleeps'. How does it even sleep. Lifeforce, I think. Strange.\n\n- Bruum, the Lion Druid.", ""),
        StaticBook("On War", "§bAmbros", "On War\n\nBy Ambros\n\nHE who serves a ruler of men in harmony with virtue will not subdue the Empire by force of arms. Such a course is wont to bring retribution in its train.\n\nWhere troops have been quartered, brambles and thorns spring up. In the track of great armies there must follow lean years.\n\nThe good man wins a victory and then stops; he will not go on to acts of violence. Winning, he boasteth not; he will not triumph; he shows no arrogance. He wins because he cannot choose; after his victory he will not be overbearing.\n\nWeapons, however beautiful, are instruments of ill omen, hateful to all creatures. Therefore he who has virtue will have nothing to do with them.\n\nWhere the princely man abides, the weak left hand is in honour. But he who uses weapons honours the stronger right. Weapons are instruments of ill omen; they are not the instruments of the princely man, who uses them only when he needs must.\n\nPeace and tranquillity are what he prizes. When he conquers, he is not elate. To be elate were to rejoice in the slaughter of human beings. And he who rejoices in the slaughter of human beings is not fit to work his will in the Empire.\n\nOn happy occasions, the left is favoured; on sad occasions, the right. The second in command has his place on the left, the general in chief on the right. That is to say, they are placed in the order observed at funeral rites. And, indeed, he who has\n\nexterminated a great multitude of men should bewail them with tears and lamentation. It is well that those who are victorious in battle should be placed in the order of funeral rites.\n\nA certain military commander used to say: \"I dare not act the host; I prefer to play the guest. I dare not advance an inch; I prefer to retreat a foot.\"\n\nThere is no greater calamity than lightly engaging in war. Lightly to engage in war is to risk the loss of our treasure.\n\nWhen opposing warriors join in battle, he who has pity conquers.", ""),
        StaticBook("Gemstones", "§bcjmate8", "This book has been created in order to spread the cultural significance of gemstones. It is my hope that this book will lead to increased insight when crafting jewelry for a partner or friend.\n-------------------Ruby- Gives people increased wisdom.\n\nIt is particularly useful for rash, or less intelligent beings.\n\nEmerald- Increases the fertility of a person, both during conception and bearing. \n\nDiamond- The most powerful of all gems.\nIt amplifies all other\n\ntraits.\n\nAmethyst- Calms the nerves. Useful for study or meditation.\n\nSugilite- The healer's stone. It is used in the healing arts, both magic and item.\n\nCarnelian- Improves analytical skills.\n\nGarnet- The bloodstone. It is used to draw toxins from the body and aid in blood clot.\n\nOnyx- Used to obtain enlightenment.\n\nHematite- Reduces stress.\n\nJasper- Protects the\n\nwearer from mind invasion.\n\nSapphire- Invokes peace and happiness.\n\nTopaz- Increases both metal and physically stamina.\n\nTiger's eye- Destroys anxiety. It promotes non-reckless\n\ncourage.\n\nPeridot: Used for growth. Commonly given to small children to hasten or improve growth. It can also aid handicapped children who are recovering.\n\nPearl- Used in chakra. Improves the connection with both\n\nmind and body.\n\nSoladite: Absorbs negative thoughts from both the wearer and those around them. This negates the thoughts.\n\n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("The Republic I", "§bAmbros", "The Republic\nVolume I\n\nBy Ambros\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Virtuous State\n\nThere exists a perfected nation, a city which functions as though it were a finely tuned engine assembled for the sole purpose of meeting the needs of its residents.  However, before one might examine the perfect state, they must first examine the\n\nperfect man, one who embodies the perfect state in all of its functions and humors.  Thus, the perfected man is virtuous, emanating the four cardinal excellences of wisdom, courage, temperance and justice.\n\nFirst, we examine wisdom.  It is the trait of the scholar and the academic.  He who studies logic, rhetoric, and dialectic so that they may both come to thorough conclusions and communicate said conclusions without falsehood.\n\nSecond, we examine courage.  It is the trait of the warrior.  Those who study gymnastics and athletics so they might hone the full potential of the mortal form.  From this anatomical tuning, they develop passion.  Granted, untamed passion is prone to\n\nviolence and fruitless aggression.  Nae, those who have courage must also studies poetry, art, and music so tame their passion.  Granted, he who study only poetry, art and music will be equally flawed as they are prone to effeteness and fragility.  It is\n\nwhen athletics and art are combined does one find courage.\n\nThird, we examine temperance.  Temperance is not found in one individual, but all individuals.  It is the virtue of controlling one’s desires, determining what is necessary and what is indulgent.  The temperate individual wastes not, and wants little.\n\nFinally, we examine justice.  There are those who will claim justice is receiving and giving what one is due, whether it be praise or punishment.  This is a falsehood.  While it is true that justice deals in what is owed, if one does their job then they\n\nare just and, as a result, give to those what they owe.\n\nWe may then compare the virtuous man a virtuous state.  Much like the man, the virtuous state is one which embodies wisdom, courage, temperance, and justice.  Where are these virtues found then?  Let us first explore what a city requires to function.\n\nFor certain a city or state is in need of workers, those who will plow the fields and mine stones.  However, the city also requires artisans to transform these raw goods into consumable and profitable materials.  We shall call this class of people the\n\nworkers.  Now, with all of these goods, for certain foreign nations and states will desire these goods and the land from which they are stripped.  It is necessary, then, that the city has guardians to protect both the workers and the goods they produce.\n\nFinally, there must be those who are knowledgeable enough to lead these guardians and we shall call these the leaders of our fair state.\n\nFirst, we examine the working class.  The working class have no need of courage or wisdom, but must still show temperance and justice.  Thus, they alone may own wealth and property within the state, trading amongst each other.  No worker should do the job\n\nof another, however.  Bookbinders should not build houses, and farmers should not bake bread.  As a result, all people of the state or necessary and no task is left undone.\n\nSecondly, we examine the guardian class.  It is of great importance that they are raised with particular temperaments, molded from birth.  They must, first and foremost, serve as beacons of courage so their education must include both the athletics and\n\nthe arts.  One to produce passion, and one to tame it.  These guardians should hold no property or wealth of their own and live communally.  For if they are given wealth and property, then they will be tempted to use their power over the working class to\n\nobtain said wealth.  Thus, the guardian should also be banned from practicing any craft beyond guardianship.  The guardian should not farm, or mine, or build houses.  Nae, all mental and physical capacities should be spent on protection of the city,\n\nfor the lungs will do no good at the job of the heart.  In this way, they are giving what is owed to the city, and in return receive what they owe from the city, this is the essence of justice.  Thus, they will also not be able to produce their own wealth\n\nand must rely solely on the citizenry for their upkeep.  As a result, the guardians will practice temperance to the highest degree.  The workers will give the guardians yearly rations of food.  Enough such the the guardians do not become malnourished, but\n\nlittle enough such that the guardians do not grow lethargic and indulgent.  Considering the guardians shall not own property, they will be given communal living spaces by the state, and will not be given private storage space since they shall have nothing\n\nto store there.\n\nAn assessment of the leader class will come next.  They are much like the warriors, unable to hold property or wealth of their own and exist to serve the state and its citizens.  However, not only will they have the courage of the guardian and the\n\ntemperance of the entirety, but they will have wisdom necessary to lead.  They will be trained in rhetoric, dialectic, and logic, thus, they will be philosopher kings and lead the state on a basis of wisdom and truth.  Granted, not all truth is fruitful,\n\nthus the leaders will be granted the right to lie to the citizenry if they see fit, and they, in their wisdom, will know when it is wise to lie.  The citizens, however, must never lie and such an offence will be punished.  The role of a leader is a\n\nprofession, a duty and a job.  What good would it do for a patient to lie to their doctor?  How may a leader help their citizens if they are lied to?\n\nThere comes a pressing question plaguing our virtuous state: who shall be of the class of leaders, guardians and workers?  This is not a government of birth-right, but rather merit.  From birth, all children should be separated from their parents so that\n\ntheir parents temperaments due not tarnish the pure ivory of the youthful mind.  Then, all of these children will undergo the same form of education.  As the children age, certain humors will arise and the children will discover what they are best suited\n\nfor.  First, the children who lack courage and wisdom will be given to the class of workers.  The remaining children will begin their training to become guardians, first learning the arts and then the athletics.  Many of these children will continue on to\n\nbecome guardians, but some will show particular fortitude and wisdom.  These children will be removed and undergo extensive training.  They will be tempted with indulgences and power.  Should they resist, they will be deemed suitable for the noble class\n\nof leaders where they will hone their wisdom, learning logic, dialectics and rhetoric.  Note, however, that the arts of dialectic and rhetoric will only begin when the young leader is of a mature age.  As those of arrogant youth will use these arts as a\n\nform of academic competition, arguing with each other to claim intellectual victories for its own sake and not truth or wisdom.  This philosophical training will continue until they are at the age of thirty accompanied by a few years of service as a\n\nguardian.  From here, they will be allowed to lead the guardians into battle, practicing their skills as both leaders and lanterns of wisdom.  After twenty years of guardian leadership, at the age of fifty, they will be wise enough to finally lead the\n\ncity.\n\nIt is paramount that the classes do not cross.  Leaders and guardians must never take up a craft and workers must never serve as guardians or leaders as they have not the virtues suited for it.  The virtuous city is like a body, and organs have specific\n\nduties and would fail at the duties of other organs.\n\nThis, then, is the virtuous state, one which embodies temperance through its casting out of overindulgent and rich materials.  For if the state is “rich” and overindulgent, it will trade in its guardians for armies and raiders as it will not have the\n\nmaterials necessary to sate the desires of the extravagant citizenry.  The city will embody courage through these guardians, and embody wisdom through its leaders.  Finally, the state will embody justice by not allowing its citizens to do the job of\n\nothers as justice is giving what is owed and receiving what is owed in return.", ""),
        StaticBook("Guide to Insects", "§bCir'dian", "~Insect~\n     ~Compendium~\n\nBy Sen Nymphea\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n\n\n Stag Beetle\n(Lucanus cervus)\n\nAfter much close studying and examination, I have concluded that this is definately an insect. Its four hind legs arch backward and are located on the\n\nunderside of the abdomen while the two front legs arch forawrd and are located on the underside of its midsection. The carapace of this insect usually varies from chestnut brown to jet black. Its head adorns two large, but seemingly harmless\n\nhorns and a pair of outward stretching antennae. Their horns seem to be for nothing more than asserting dominance over other males when fighting for a mate and its wings barely keep it aloft. I doubt I could find a use for an insect like this.\n\n\n\nAzure Beetle\n\n\nThis beetle can easily be distinguished by its glossy, vibrant blue carapace. Even its bodily fluids share this distinct blue hue and I actually discovered this insect\n\nwhen I found one squished to the front cover of this book, its body and insides blending right in with the books blue coat. Like most beetles, it has four legs located on the underside of the abdomen and two located on the underside of the midsection. Its\n\nantennae stretch out around 2 inches from the front of its head and end in tiny, barbed rolls. I have a feeling that I can harvest the liquid from these insects, albeit miniscule amounts. I may very well have discovered a way to end the blue dye poverty.\n\n\n\nSawbug\n\n\nThe forward-most legs of this insect bare a line of sharp 'teeth' with which they cut leaves and other small plantlife, similar to how a carpenter saws wooden planks. It\n\nuses the pieces of vegetation it cuts to build simple structures which it uses for storage and housing. Much like ants, these insects tend to live in large nests of five hundred or more and usually possess a queen. The Sawbug's carapace is usually a\n\nrustic brown, though notable differences between genders include slightly brighter colouration in females as well as just seven teeth along the arms as apposed to a male's nine.\n\n\n\nGluebug\n\n\nThis strange insect is filled with a very sticky but acidic liquid that it shoots at its prey through a hose-like appendage above its mouth. The liquid is a very pale,\n\nslightly orange colour, quite similar to puss. It is so tightly packed with this substance that its even visible through its shell, giving the carapace a similarly pale colour. These large, fat beasts tend to prey on clusters of smaller insects such as\n\nants and larvae nests by spraying them with its sticky foam and eating them at its own leisure. If I could find a way to neutralise the acids in the liquids this bug creates it would probably make a good adhesive.\n\n\n\nCreeping Spider\n\n\nA common household pest. These black, hairy spiders don't usually grow much larger than a small mina coin by leg span, but they tend to cluster together in\n\ncorners of cielings, making them one of few non-territorial species. Their name comes from the way they creep toward prey caught in their webs rather than frantically rush toward it, unlike most other species.\n\n\n\nClashbug\n\n\nThis crazy insect likes to jump about madly and crash in to things. I haven't found any logical reason for this behaviour, though I think it may have something to do with\n\nthoughening the exoskeleton on its forehead. Its strong hind legs allow it to jump over three feet heigh, making it one of the strongest jumpers in the insect kingdom. The carapace is usually deep, vibrant orange with a stronger hint of yellow in females.\n\nIts antennae only reach a mere quarter of an inch, probably due to its head banging nature. Because of the sheer uselessness of this bug, I honestly can't say there's any use for it.\n\n\n\nInfernal Mantis\n\n\nThese dangerous predators prefer the harsh environments of deserts and other hot locations. Not only does it possess deadly claws and a sandy cammoflauge\n\nbut it also secretes a liquid from beneath its carapace in dire heats which is best described as 'Flame retardant'. All of these weapons and defences make for a deadly combination for what is already one of the most dangerous stealth-killers in the insect\n\nkingdom. This predator hunts slow, unsuspecting land dwelling insects, but is known to attack predators even larger than itself. I could definately use its secretionary liquids for something useful.\n\n\n\nGreen Cicada\n\n\nThese large bugs spend most of their lives as nymphs, buried beneath trees in hives and only live for a couple of weeks as fullfledged adults. Once they reach\n\nmaturity, their body takes a leaf green colour. This helps them blend in with their environment as they spend a lot of their time feeding on trees and plants during the spring, when they finally mature from nymphs. Females will lay their eggs shortly\n\nbefore death and the young normally hatch two months after incubation, then living as nymphs for a further nine before finally maturing in to adults. These life cycles are unusually short for Cicadae, especially for those of a larger size.\n\n\n\nSen Bee\n\n\nApparently, nobody's ever given these adorable creatures a name before so I've decided to name them after myself! These friendly little helpers are so docile that\n\nI've let one sit on my hand for ten minutes and it did nothing but sleep. They can easily be distinguished by their fat fluffy bodies and peach pink bands as opposed to an ordinary bee's yellow bands. These little wads of cute make very sweet honey in\n\ntheir nests that they feed to their young larvae, like most species. I could probably use this heavenly substance in cooking, and their honeycombs may have some secondary purpose.\n\n\n\nGreen Ladybug\n\n\nThis species of Ladybug is native to Anthos and are a common sight in thick wooded regions, such as Malinor. They're somewhat smaller than other species, sizing\n\nat not even a quarter of an inch in width. As well as their distinct grass green wings this species can be distinguished by the nine black spots on their wings, though they appear to be slightly larger in males than females.\n\n\n\nMetal Wasp\n\n\nThis is a truly nasty species of insect. They possess some of the toughest jaws in the insect kingdom and are able to spray a very strong acid from their mouths, capable\n\nof destroying the density of materials as tough as iron. These two nasty weapons combined allow them to make short work of even the toughest of prey. They usually make their nests deep within thick tree trunks and occasionally the walls of homes. They\n\nseem very aversive to strong citrus fruit such as oranges and lemons. Because of their hostility and natural strength, containing these things is very difficult, but if I could keep them under control then I'm sure I could put their acidic saliva to use.\n\n\n\nTitanous Caterpillar\n\n\nThis enourmous species of caterpillar may seem intimidating, but like most other species it is completely gentle. It can be most easily distinguished by its red, black and\n\nyellow band pattern. This beast's diet consists entirely of plant life greenery, which unfortunately makes it effective at destroying gardens if left to reproduce long enough. After six months of maturing, they veil themselves in a cocoon which they\n\nremain inside for a further six months before emerging as butterflies of the same species name.", ""),
        StaticBook("Politica", "§bAmbros", "On the Politica\n\nBy Ambros\n\nNOT exalting worth keeps the people from rivalry. Not prizing what is hard to procure keeps the people from theft. Not to show them what they may covet is the way to keep their minds from disorder.\n\nTherefore the Sage, when he governs, empties their minds and fills their bellies, weakens their inclinations and strengthens their bones. His constant object is to keep the people without knowledge and without desire, or to prevent those who have\n\nknowledge from daring to act. He practises inaction, and nothing remains ungoverned.\n\nHe who respects the State as his own person is fit to govern it. He who loves the State as his own body is fit to be entrusted with it.\n\nIn the highest antiquity, the people did not know that they had rulers. In the next age they loved and praised them. In the next, they feared them. In the next, they despised them.\nHow cautious is the Sage, how sparing of his words! When his task is\n\naccomplished and affairs are prosperous, the people all say: \"We have come to be as we are, naturally and of ourselves.\"\n\nIf any one desires to take the Empire in hand and govern it, I see that he will not succeed. The Empire is a divine utensil which may not be roughly handled. He who meddles, mars. He who holds it by force, loses it.\n\nFishes must not be taken from the water: the methods of government must not be exhibited to the people.\n\nUse uprightness in ruling a State; employ stratagems in waging war; practise non-interference in order to win the Empire. Now this is how I know what I lay down:--\n\nAs restrictions and prohibitions are multiplied in the Empire, the people grow poorer and poorer. When the people are subjected to overmuch government, the land is thrown into confusion. When the people are skilled in many cunning arts, strange are the\n\nobjects of luxury that appear.  The greater the number of laws and enactments, the more thieves and robbers there will be. Therefore the Sage says: \"So long as I do nothing, the people will work out their own reformation. So long as I love calm, the\n\npeople will right themselves. If only I keep from meddling, the people will grow rich. If only I am free from desire, the people will come naturally back to simplicity.\"\n\nIf the government is sluggish and tolerant, the people will be honest and free from guile. If the government is prying and meddling, there will be constant infraction of the law. Is the government corrupt? Then uprightness becomes rare, and goodness\n\nbecomes strange. Verily, mankind have been under delusion for many a day!\n\nGovern a great nation as you would cook a small fish.\n\nIf the Empire is governed according to virtue, disembodied spirits will not manifest supernatural powers. It is not that they lack supernatural power, but they will not use it to hurt mankind. Again, it is not that they are unable to hurt mankind, but\n\nthey see that the Sage also does not hurt mankind. If then neither Sage nor spirits work harm, their virtue converges to one beneficent end.  In ancient times those who knew how to practise Tao did not use it to enlighten the people, but rather to keep\n\nthem ignorant. The difficulty of governing the people arises from their having too much knowledge.\n\nIf the people do not fear the majesty of government, a reign of terror will ensue.\n\nDo not confine them within too narrow bounds; do not make their lives too weary. For if you do not weary them of life, then they will not grow weary of you.\n\nIf the people do not fear death, what good is there in using death as a deterrent? But if the people are brought up in fear of death, and we can take and execute any man who has committed a monstrous crime, who will dare to follow his example?\n\nNow, there is always one who presides over the infliction of death. He who would take the place of the magistrate and himself inflict death, is like one who should try to do the work of a master-carpenter. And of those who try the work\n\nof a master-carpenter there are few who do not cut their own hands.\n\nThe people starve because those in authority over them devour too many taxes; that is why they starve. The people are difficult to govern because those placed over them are meddlesome; that is why they are difficult to govern. The people despise death\n\nbecause of their excessive labour in seeking the means of life; that is why they despise death.\n\nA Sage has said: \"He who can take upon himself the nation's shame is fit to be lord of the land. He who can take upon himself the nation's calamities is fit to be ruler over the Empire.\"\nWere I ruler of a little State with a small population, and only\n\nten or a hundred men available as soldiers, I would not use them. I would have the people look on death as a grievous thing, and they should not travel to distant countries. Though they might possess boats and carriages, they should have no occasion to\n\nride in them. Though they might own weapons and armour, they should have no need to use them. I would make the people return to the use of knotted cords. They should find their plain food sweet, their rough garments fine. They should be content with their\n\nhomes, and happy in their simple ways. If a neighbouring State was within sight of mine--nay, if we were close enough to hear the crowing of each other's cocks and the barking of each other's dogs--the two peoples should grow old and die without there\n\never having been any mutual intercourse.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Forest Poem", "§bTailesin", "-------------------      A Forest Poem\n-------------------\n\n\n~By Nivndil\n\nPuerith'leyu\n\nThe trees they stand,\nA clamor of souls.\n\nTheir voices born on\nWinds, are old.\n\nBoughs up high,\nFairy halls.\n\nRoots below,\nSilent foot-falls.\n\nThey stand and wait,\nFor times unborn.\n\nTo whisper secrets,\nBefore words were forged.\n\nAmongst their trunks,\nBeneath canopy dome.\n\nTo bathe in twilight,\nThe trees my home.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Forest Poem", "§bNivndil Duskhollow", "Puerith'leyu.\n\nThe trees they stand,\nA clamour of souls.\n\nTheir voices born on\nWinds, are old.\n\nBoughs up high,\nFairy halls.\n\nRoots below,\nSilent foot-falls.\n\nThey stand and wait,\nFor times unborn.\n\nTo whisper secrets,\nBefore words were forged.\n\nAmongst their trunks,\nBeneath canopy dome.\n\nTo bathe in twilight,\nThe trees my home.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dedicant Poem", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n    Dedicant Poem \n           by\n         Veren\n   --------------\n\nThe leaves rustle as the wind blows strong,\nFlowers blossom and trees grow tall.\nThe Druids protect the beautiful place,\nWhile the Aspects guide with strength and grace.\n\nThe Druids watch the void grow strong,\nTrying to protect the things we love.\nAs nature listens so subtle and quiet,\nThe world grows on, forever strong.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lovers of Strand", "§bFerinus", "Untitled\n\nThe slow waves sliding over each other,\nPlaying a melodic tune,\nOne after another,\nAgainst the rocks they've hewn.\n\nGlinting in the last light of day,\nThen the peaceful light, they betray.\nWhispers turn to roars,\n\nRolling turns to crashing,\nOceans throw walls of water at unyielding coasts.\n\nAnd in this natural struggle, only time prevails.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fairy Ring", "§b[Dedicant] Heroxis", "Fairy Ring\n\nby Heroxis\n\nSearching the night.\nNight quietly green.\nDeep in the forest.\nNo one has seen.\n\nThe circle of fungi.\nI enter you now.\nA force I sense.\nGreeting with a bow.\n\nTo sing in the rain.\nTo write in the sun.\nTo draw in the clouds.\nAttuned as one.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dedicant Poem", "§bUnknown Dedicant", "Dedicant Poem\n\nNature is beautiful, quiet and serene.\nNature is the forest, with its many shades of green.\nNature is the birds, singing in the dawn.\nNature is a calf, trying to walk after it is born.\nNature is a salmon,\n\nswimming against the stream.\nNature is a geyser, blowing off steam.\n\nNature is a beast, kicking up a storm.\nNature is the trees, broken and forlorn.\nNature is lightning, striking at the ground.\nNature is a fire,\n\nconsuming all around.\nNature is a tornado, screaming out a roar.\nNature is a wave, washing things ashore.\nNature can be a beauty, and nature can be a beast.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Nature", "§bTailesin", "--------------\n       On Nature\n   --------------\n\n ~By Dedicant Elorlas\n\nTake some time to sit and notice.\nThe beauty of the trees.\nTake a moment to admire, the dancing of the leaves.\nNote, Mali'i, playing with your toys, so full of hungerous joy.\n\nNot to crush the insects, who are a thousandth of your weight.\nYou may be a tenant of nature, but do not seal their fate.\nLife is about enjoyment, not creating storms.\n\nSo head this warning now, before all Anthos is torn.", ""),
        StaticBook("Isabellas Poem!", "§b[[Child]] Isabella Carstais Ibar'Ker", "*The poem seems to be written in childish handwriting*\n\nFlower Flowers! Pretty flowers\nHow do you like to grow?\nIn the rain the flowers reply, up in the druids grove\n\nFlowers flowers\nblooming flowers\nWhy are you colored so?\nCause we spread the joy, the flowers sing, all around the world\n\nFlowers flowers dying flowers\nWhy do you look so gloom?\n\nOur time has come, the flowers cough, and its time to go below.\n\n\n*There seems to be another page!*\n\n             ,-. \n    ,     ,-.   ,-. \n   / \\   (   )-(   ) \n   \\ |  ,.>-(   )-< \n    \\|,' (   )-(   ) \n     Y ___`-'   `-' \n     |/__/   `-' \n     | \n     | \n     |    -hrr- \n  ___|_____________ \n*a childs drawing of a flower!*", ""),
        StaticBook("Herb", "§bTailesin", "-------------------            Herb\n-------------------\n\n\n~By \nThudrin Treebeard\n\nStuff it in your pipe and light\n\nSmoke all day, smoke all night\n\nRoll on, maybe two\n\nSend them clouds up into the blue.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Night of Ice", "§bTailesin", "-------------------    The Night of Ice\n-------------------\n\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nThe isles were home to many\nA peaceful message it tried to send;\nA distant memory it has become\nSo many lives about to end.\n\nMany more came along to join with each other;\neven I found myself falling for another.\n\nMany years we spent living without fear;\nBut none of us ever knew\nWhat was so very near\n\nMany lands fell before the wood started to ache;\nEvents were about to unfold\nAnd the ice began to break.\n\nIn the end it sank and save the isles I did try;\nIt sank into the abyss and so to did I.", ""),
        StaticBook("You are", "§bArthur Caulfield", "You are a tumour, taking me out of a perfect life. \n\nYou are a fire, forcing me out of my home.\n\nYou are a thief, stealing all of my fun and joy. \n\nYou are a killer, killing the immersion.\n\nYou are a fly, around my food. \n\nYou are a parasite, leeching onto my head.\n \nYou are my only clothes, disgusting and ripped.\n\nYou are a sword through my still beating heart.\n\nYou are an ass, dirty and unwiped. \n\nYou are a spider, driving everyone away.\n\nYou are a current, desperate to pull everyone down to your level.\n\nYou are a guard, closing down my party. \nYou are an aggressive orc, capable of only one emotion.\n\nYou are porridge, bland and dull.\n\nYou are the crayon nobody uses.\n\nYou are a made up language or fictional history. \n\nYou are a lump in a comfortable pillow.\n\nYou are an obscenity shouted at a deaf man.\n\nYou are a greedy beggar at my door all day.\n\nYou are the corpse of a king who demands a temple for a resting place. \n\nYou are a pot with no soil that demands a flower.\n\nYou are the loose string that undoes a shirt.\n\nYou are the thunder I hear while trying to sleep. \n\nYou are the crack in a large expensive window.\n\nYou are the loose screw in a banks vault door.\n\nYou are a spot on a fresh piece of paper. \n\nYou are a creaky step in a silent house.\n\nYou are a moth among butterflies.\n\nYou are water extinguishing a torch.\n\nYou are dried up ink.\n\nYou are a chicken without a head.\n\nYou are wilted soil in a draught.\n\nYou are a church without a religion.\n\nYou are.", ""),
        StaticBook("Paths", "§bGwendolyn Powell", "One step after another\nI found myself walking a path, a path laden with roots\nA path breathing and living beneath my foot\nSilant at my touch\n\nAs I wadnered, on and on\nI took a turn\nA turn down a darker, road, and one hidden from the sun\n\nA path dusty, dry, and lifeless\n\nThis path I waled for many a year\nLost admist a sea of dark\nLost, on a path that turned slowly upwards, and soon became too difficult to walk\nAnd there I sat, pondering my loneliness\n\nOut of the corner of my eye, I glimpsed the sun\nBrilliant, bountiful and beautiful\n\nI stood, abd began to trudge slowly towards the light\nHearing birdsong, hearing the rustle and movements of leaves\nFeeling warmer and warmer as I went\n\nThe loneliness seeping from my bones\n\nThe dark shrank away in the light\nThis new path, was one brimming with life\nMuch like the first\nBut now, I began to hear something\n\nA quiet breathe, tiny beneath the sounds of chirping\nThe hoof beat of a running dear, and the great sway of an oak tree\nSo I listened more closely\nImmersing myself in the slow and steady beat\n\nOf the true world\nThe growing, giving, loving world\nThat I had once ignored\nAnd had now, finally\n\nFound a place in my heart\nA place, where it began to beat within me\nA place, that I hope to see flourish\n\nTo see flourish, like the flowers and astoundingly wonderful world around me", ""),
        StaticBook("Helios' Poem", "§bGigarun", "Helios' Poem\n\nAs told by Helios\n      &\n   Scribed by \n         The Pine Druid\n\nO, They say,\nThat under the fae,\nExist a group of individuals,\nThat serve beatles,\nAnd hug trees.\n\nO, Morons you say,\nMaybe we are,\nBut before you draw conclusions,\nYou should know,\nWe ain't a random bunch.\n\nO, Most of time anyway,\nWe have our rules,\nAnd our rulers,\nOne's quite the lass,\nLady of the Harvest.\n\nO, That'd be Cerridwen,\nBut there's another, Cernunnos,\nYou heard me right,\nDeity o' the hunt.\n\nO, Now you know our lords,\nAspects we call 'em,\nTreat 'em right,\nAnd they'll be kind in return,\nSupply you with good harvest.\n\nO, And hides,\nTo keep yours warm,\nAnd that's all folks,\nIf you've changed you mind about us,\nThat's lovely,\nBut if not...\n\nO, You can piss off!", ""),
        StaticBook("Natural Poem", "§bDraoen", "Nature's Poem                                   The peaceful grass.\nThe blowing wind.\nBlooming flowers.\nAnd the tall trees.\nThe matters of them connect to one.\nNature, it's nature, for us all.\n\n   Linked Together\n\nA Druid serves nature.\nLinked to one.\nSame as the roots with trees.\nThe sun with plants.\nA family with support.\nAll is linked together.\n\n    Dandelion Fuzz\n\nBlooming with the wind\nNothing is sinned.\nThe fuzz flies and plants.\nNewcomers join to sprout and dance.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Sonnet", "§bFranswaar Kellian", "A Sonnet\n    Written By:\n      Franswaar\n\n\n\n\n     Dedicated to:\nThe Memory of someone you have lost\n\nShe left me in a hurry without thought\nLeft alone mourning a memory\nCries from a sinner gave heaven no fraught\nEven the wisest oak men shall fade suddenly\n\nHonour shattered, respect lost, and heads turned\nStanding slightly ever straight though dwarfs looked upon\nTwo mountains over looking the edge of the world\nOne stands while the other crumbles toward the fading sun\n\nThe voice of the fallen echo through out\nVisions of haven through branches of the williow\nTombs sit empty while the bodies of the living prowl\nThough gone the sight of her to my life I owe\n\nJust one last glimmer I wish I could see\nRosalina, you were my queen.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature is Lovely", "§bKira Winterleaf", "Fireflies glow.\nTrees grow and rivers flow.\nNature is lovely, nature is strong.\nNature is where I truly belong.\nDesert sands\nand icelands.\nAll part of nature.\nRabbits burrow\nBirds fly\nBugs buzz\nAnd mice hide.", ""),
        StaticBook("Moonlit Shore", "§bKira Winterleaf", "~~~~ Moonlight On the Shore ~~~~\n\nThere is moonlight on the shore.\n\nForever more, forever more.\n\nKnocking on the ocean's door.\n\nForever more, forever more.\n\nDancing and prancing and sharing lore.\n\nForever more, forever more.\n\n\"What are we even here for?\" \n\nForever more, forever more.\n\nBy: Kira Raine Winterleaf, age thirteen, 9th of Malin's Welcome, 1556", ""),
        StaticBook("Song Anthology 2", "§bAeran", "Song Anthology: Volume 2\n\nThe Lame Drunk of Abresi\n\nThe Day the Spit Flew\n\nA poor man came to a fair maid/\nSaid ‘I need some work, I need to get paid?’/\nShe said that’s a damn shame/\nFor, he was lame!\n\nThe Lame Drunk of Abresi/\nHe ate messy/\nHe lvied poorly/\n\nAnd used to love a girl named Jessie!\n\nHis beard was down to his tits/\nHe smelt like uncleaned pits/\nBut he said I'm rich, geez/\nIf you counted fleas!\n\nIf you counted teeth,\nnah.\n\nThe Lame Drunk of Abresi/\nHe ate messy/\nHe lived poorly/\nAnd used to love a girl named Jessie/\n\nOne day he saw a great knight/\nHe challenged him to a fight!/\n\nThe great warrior prepared with sword and shield/\nAnd as he took the field.../\n\nThere was the Lame Drunk/\nHe smelt like a skunk/\nFor his smell was his shield/\nHis breath, what he would weild/\n\nThe knight fell, had no choice but to yield/\nHe could not take the field/\nHis helmet was filled with puke/\nHis dismissed the drunk as a cuke!/\n\nThe Lame Drunk of Abresi/\nHe ate messy/\nHe lived poorly/\n\nAnd used to love a girl named Jessie!/\n\nThe Lame Drunk of Abresi/\nHe ate messy/\nHe lived poorly/\nAnd used to love a girl named Jessie/\nAnd damn was he crazy!\n\nThe Day the Spit Flew\n\nIt was a day unlike today/\nExcept, nothing like today\nThe atmosphere was the opposite of gay/\nThe atmospher was yag/\nThe Snow Elves had kidnapped a princess! Or something!/\n\nBut it did not matter, the pale devils would get it!/\nAnd by it I mean completly disproport-\nionate aggression/\nBUT I'LL BE DAMNED IF THEY DIDN'T GET IT!/\n\nTwo kingdoms met infront of another kingdom/\n\nOren, half of their court had come/\nBut all of Snelvedom showed up; Tundrak!/\nIt was a standoff to be sung about maybe when the singer was really drunk or out of songs!/\n\nThey fought with words/\n\nEmperor Chivay drowned out by his court/\nTundrak drowned out by his nation's silence/\nBut alas, no solution came/\n\nSo, with a great 'HORK'/\nAnd a loud 'POOT'/\nHead to toe/\n\nTundrak was covered with spit/\nAnd damn, wasn't it an improvement!", ""),
        StaticBook("Dedicant Poem", "§b[Teen] Dedicant Veren", "The leaves rustle as the wind blows strong\nFlowers blossen and trees grow tall\nThe Druids protect this beutiful place\nWhile the Aspects guid with strength and grace \nThe druids watch the void grow strong Trying to protect the things we love\n\nAs nature listens, so sutle and quiet\nThe world grows on forever strong\nBy:\nDedicant Veren", ""),
        StaticBook("A stray flower", "§bHileia", "A stray flower\n\nDaughters of light, who never repine,\nThough high your birth,\nIt is yours in humble life to shine\nLike modest worth.\n\nArrayed in robes of heavenly hue\n\nyou come and go,\nto drink the nectar of the pure,\nNot the taste of woe.\n\nIt is yours a language pure to teach,\nand share his heart\nWho seeks in Vailor\nhigh aims to reach\nEre he depart.\n\nAnd yours the whisper which, I throw,\nI hear at eve,\nand in the morning's roseate glow-\nHear and believe:\n\nBelieve the gospel of your lips\nspoken to man,\nnor heed the coming frost that nips\neach hope and plan.\n\nFor, if my life in Vailor is true,\nI yet, on high,\nShall wear a glorious robe like you,\nAnd never die.\n\n             -By Hileia\nDedicant of the mother\nCircle", ""),
        StaticBook("Like the Tree", "§bCallax", "-=-=-=-=-=-\n            I Am Like\n             the Tree\n           =-=-=-=-=-=\n\n\n\n\n\n\nScribed by Hierophant Callax, the Solstice Druid\n\nTrees are taken for granted; so much more is the tree than a reason for Druids to spread vitality. The tree is a symbol which has lasted since the dawn of time and which will last for eternity.\n\nThe tree assumes many elegant forms and an outstanding array of varieties, but it is stubborn. It is unchanging. And even while it is stubborn and unchanging, it is so forgiving and gracious.\n\nOne cannot rid of the tree. No matter the force, the blight, or the raging fire, the tree will overcome and retain vitality, spreading across the vast lands.\n\nThe tree takes little but gives much. Despite the actions taken upon it by unnatural forces or by ravaging races, the tree continues to provide with grace and plenty.\n\nIt is a tree with which I have life. It is a tree which I shall not leave the side of. And it is a tree with which I have a love I shall never hide.\n\nTimes do change, as do people and factions and nations. However, the tree is stubborn and it does not change. It continues to devoutly serve the natural world. I am like the tree.\n\n[!] On the final page, an intricate image of an oak tree with a wizened face on its trunk is sketched.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter", "§bAshton", "\"Dear Mister Muildir,\n\nThank you for serving as a wonderful archdruid and for always helping maintain the balance. \n\nI appreciate all that you do for the grove and our people. Please accept this pie as a thank you.\"\n-Ashton", ""),
        StaticBook("Moot 8th FS 1606", "§bAiden", "Moot\n8th Of The First Seed\n1606.\n\n-Recorded By\nPhoenix Druid Aiden.\n\n-Toren's Speech-\n\nWhen the explores found Bertha, and spoke about her at the previous moot we left out some important information. When we arrived the grove was already the way it is, and was named the wandering grove. The addition of many homes\n\nwere not us, all we did was place the aspect stones in their place. When we did arrive here, two strangers were already living upon Bertha, we quickly recognised them as unknown druids but when we asked them their totems and names it was as if they spoke\n\na completely different language to us. However it was clear the aspects brought us to them, as they already know of our needing of a new home and upon completion left, this is the reason why I refer to them as the two wandering druids. Those who were good\n\nat spotting things, would notice two statues at the entrance both in honour of the nameless wandering druids. I just felt it was appropriate to inform you all of such, and respect them for their devotion.\n\n-Renn's Opening Speech-\n\n\nAs many of you can see we've arrived within our new grove, hopefully this place will be a good place for our kin and new members of the order, we've already gathered quite a lot of outside interest.\n\nIt may take time to get accustomed to the new grove. We've continued the deathsbane tavern, labeled \"Lion's Hearth Inn\" Ask Elly for a job. If you need a home speak with one of the running Archdruids, we're limited on space, so you may need to bunk with\n\nanother druid, though we're always open to switch things about, so don't be stressed. Any non-druids needing housing seek out a tavern worker, they'll be maintaining a lot of the rooms located to the left of the entrance.\n\n-Questions About The New Grove-\n\nZa'Kabar-\nWhat's to stop spooks from just waltzing in?\n\nRenn-\nBertha's shell is next to impossible to climb, but also out of reach from sea level. The only way for that to be possible is to go\n\nthrough the farm island, which is also Bertha's feeding ground on occasion. Currently we're working to fix that, but its not a main priority until we see an active presence of whatever spooks throw themselves at us.\n\n-No more questions-\n\n-Renn, Concerning Elly's Temporary Archdruidship-\n\nElly will no longer be acting as a temporary fill in for Lilliana. Instead she'll be replacing her and will become an offical archdruid.\n\n-Renn Guide System-\n\nA lot of traditions have changed since our past. Some for better, some for worse. Though change is always the way of nature. And thusly expect some necessary changes to the guide system over the course of the next elven month.\n\n-Elly Guide System Further Explanation.-\n\nThe idea is quite simple. A mentor system. Those who show interest in becoming a guide, could talk to an archdruid. They'll go through training, instead of just being thrown in after begin voted in.\n\nThe druid would come to find an archdruid, learn what they need to learn. Learn how to make proper tasks, learn how to teach a dedicant, their interview process, everything. They could shadow an experienced guide, which will give both of them extra\n\nexperience. When deemed fit by the archdruid overseeing their training, they could be voted in at the next moot. It'll help the newer guides train our next generation of druids. \n\nNo objections to new guide system, thus passed and implemented.\n\n-Renn Removing Inactive Guides-\n\nThis is more of an unfortunate topic, though we'll be removing two acting guides currently. This being both sister Holly Ashton, and brother Buffalo Sena. They've done the circle, and the order well. But it seems as t\n\nthe aspects call them elsewhere for the time being. If they choose to return, I'm sure their continued efforts would be appreciated. \n\nNeither were nominated for Hierophancy.\n\n-Renn Implementing Head Teacher-\n\nAs per the renewal of a more fluent guide system, we think it necessary to reimplement the head teacher role. Refefined it as well perhaps. It would entail assisting in teaching new guides, alongside Elly's system.\n\nBut also maintaining documents to ensure guide fluidity, and maintain consistency in rites and tasks themselves this dosen't mean tasks will be set, they'll still have their appropriate styles of the respective guide of course.  This role would also be\n\nworking alongside both the scribes of the library and the inner circle to ensure things run smoothly. Though! We are at a problem who to pick?\n\nPhoenix Druid Aiden-\nNominated Bull Druid Toren.\n\nToren Received The Role.\n\n-Renn Interview Before Grand Task-\n\nNow next, little tid-bit. I'll be personally enforcing an old tradition on that I believe was necessary for the guide system. A guide must contact an archdruid prior to their dedicant doing their grand task. They'l\n\ninterviewed promptly to ensure that they've been taught correctly, and are ready to preform their grand task.\n\n-Questions-\n\nGi'Garun: Can Hierophants interview and inform the archdruid, if they aren't present?\n\nRenn: Preferable, it'd be best to seek out an archdruid, if at all possible at the time. But if not, then I don't see a problem with allowing the hierophant to interview. It just means we'll also have to send a rough outline of what the interview should\n\nentail to all the heirophants. Though it shouldn't be much of a problem.\n\n-Renn Library-\n\nAnywho onto the topic of the library. We'll be somewhat reviewing how the library works. We want it to have a more profound impact on the grove and the order itself. Literature and history have always been a large part of who we are.\n\nso we've allowed a head scrube to manage that whole area. He would also be in charge of ensuring scribes stay on their toes, and all moots are documented for those that can't attend. Currently we have Aiden, Brother Phoenix acting as head scribe.\n\nHe's been documenting this moot so far, and hope it turns out well, and I'm sure it will.\n\n-Renn Artifact Collection-\n\nWe'll also have an artifact collection within our library. These artifacts of course relating to druids, and our culture and history. As for artifacts currently being used or kept in secret, replicas would be\n\nmade of them. So if you have an artifact you would like to donate, have documented, or perhpas have a replica made, seek out Aiden.\n\n-Renn Recruiting-\n\nWe'll also be encouraging more recruiting now, with the new grove and all. The naelurir are almost beating us, and that quite frankly isnt acceptable. So, I encourage you all to get out there, get students, or direct them to a\n\nguide. We'll also probably have an influx of non-druids wandering the grove. It seems to be a hotspot recently. So I'd encourage you all to make friends with our visitors. Though to add onto the potential influx of new visistors, we also wish to hold more\n\nfestivals and the likes. Sometimes we'll encourage our druids to get out there into other places, recruit and offer possible blessings. Other times, we'll encourage visitors to a variety of events! Whether this be a flower dance of sorts, some sort of\n\nsummer-type festival, or feast, or even a carnival!\n\n-Renn Sharr's Topic-\n\nI don't know the entirety of what it is\nSharr wished to speak about, though from what I was told he wishes to flourish an elder tree near the dwarven capital.\n\n-Renn Sharr's Attack On Sena-\n\nDuring the times of our Savannah grove, an incident occurred between both Brother Buffalo, and Brother Metal. One that almost resulted in the loss of Buffalo.\n-Renn Calls Za'Kabar To Speak-\n\nFrom what I've gathered, Sharr and some runelords were trying to kill the jackal. Oo'ri and Sena were nearby. Sena tried to prevent them from fighting. Runelords and Sharr were still trying to kill the jackal, nearly killing Sena in the process.\n\n-A vote for a trial against Sharr, for his chrimes against Sena.-\n\n-Trial Vote Passed. Sharr will be put to trial-\n\n-Elly Natures Bounty-\n\nA bonus note on our nature's bounty shops, if you have an interest in helping run or create one, please contact an archdruid and we'll get you set up. These shops are little stalls we have set outside the tavern. As well I'm lo\n\nfor little helpers in the tavern if anyone would like to help.\n\n-Elly Ending Note-\n\nOur last little blurb before everyone can go, please be aware we will be having moots quite regularly, so we can have smaller amounts of topics to go through, and in ture shorter moots!\nIsn't that lovely?", ""),
        StaticBook("15th S.S 1613", "§bTailesin", "-------------------         Moot log\n15th Sun’s smile, 1613\n-------------------\n\n\n~Scribed by \nTailesin, Lynx Druid\n\n-Topic 1: Nominations-\n\nFirst for nominations was for the next archdruid. The three who were nominated for the position was; Shark Druid Za’Kabar, Lynx Druid Tailesin & Illarun.\n\nTailesin was voted into the position with six votes to her name where as Za’Kabar & Illarun had zero\n\nTopic 1 b:  Hierophant nomination for Metal Druid Sharr Irongut\n \nSharr was voted into the position of hierophant with four votes, blessed be brother Metal.\n\nTopic 1 c:  Guideship \nMouse Druid Adyalyn was nominated for guide and was voted in as guide with four votes, blessed be sister Mouse.\n\nTopic 2:  The unshunning of Tajo\n \nThe topic of unshunning of the ex-dedicant Tajo was brought up as he requested for the order to talk about it.\n\nIt was quickly agreed that his status as a “kill on sight shun” wouldn’t changed due to his dangerous past.\n\nTopic 3: Uruks & their tribute\n \nUruks of the war nation came to us asking for tribute which we agreed to do.\nThe tribute is as followed.\n\n“Toh puzh the grynd deepever into the nubdeadh, the frozt witchz and thoze who wish to dishonor our name.\nKrugmar wihll take a portion ofh latz minas ahnd casihngs of the armah needed to rain boltz upon the wickedh.”\n\nHowever it was stated that our current mina available wasn’t the greatest so the Rex continued.\n \n“ Ifh layx do nub haveh the minaz to offer toh the Warntation. Latz wihll be a part of the Baalak-hai.”\n\nThe “Baalak-hai”  Is explained by another uruk, Gorkil, as followed: “Agh armi agaynx da Nub’hozh, uphuldin da hozhest ideylz of Krug hymzellf. Nub da zpiritz ur da Rex. Azh hai det focuziz un da deztruktuhn uf da Nub’hozh ayn buurzzhomoz ayn buurzmojoz.”\n\nWe managed to agree on that we’d help with the “Baalak-hai” against the undead and also pay the tribute. The tribute being a variety of armaments and weaponry, including Alchemist’s fire.\n\nTopic 4: New roles within the circle\nThese new roles would be a part of the Inner Circle.\n \nThe roles being: Storage manager, Events Manager & Steward for druid and non-druid housing.\n\nTrout Druid Kasfer volunteered to be the storage manager, with no one else challenging him.\n\nShark Druid Za’Kabar was nominated as events manager, with no one else challenging him\n\nThyme Druid Rhova’dir volunteered to be steward for non-druid housing, with no one else challenging him\n\nMeldramiriel Druid Nynaeve & Trout Druid Kasfer volunteered to be stewards for druid housing, with no one else challenging them.\n\nWith Toren going off on a journey he has stepped down from being head teacher and Archdruid Elswyen taking his place.\n\n\n-End of log", ""),
        StaticBook("14th GH 1637 Pt1", "§bTailesin", "===================         Moot log\n14th Grandharvest 1637\n===================\n\n-Scribed by Tailesin, Lynx druid\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Alright! Can I have quiet PLEASE! The Moot is starting! Kasfer would you like to do our Moot call?\"\n\nThe moot call happens\n\n Indra: \"Thank you,\" she'd exhale, glancing about the circle. \"I see we have some old face in the crowd. Thank you Brothers and Sisters for coming.\n\nOur first order of business is concerning. Guideship. I've spoken to Brother Bluejay and though he is Arch of the Cerulean circle, he's offered to help this circle with Guides. It is only until this Circle has picked up on their own guides.\n\nAs of right now, We have Tailesin, myself, Za'kabar and Kasfer picking up dedicants.\"\n\n[Hare Druid] Taynuel clears his throat, raising his staff slightly.\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Yes, Brother Hare?\"\n\n[Hare Druid] Taynuel: \"Sister Veil was a guide previously. Though it has been a long time, she would be an excellent candidate for guideship. I can honestly say she has a spotless record.\"\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Alright! Well! Thank you for the nomination! Let me just get this next announcement out then we shall hold votes.\n\nDuring the last Arch meeting myself and the other Arches have started to review how our Guides are chosen and the work load they take on.\n\nWe've deemed it best that in order for one to be nominated for Guide they should be attuned for no less than four elven months. It will help us see who has clear understanding of the balance and Aspects.\"\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Now. We can hold a nomination. Sister Veil has been nominated. Would anyone else like to nominate?\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Willow, though he's about as long in the attunement as me, he's offered to assist Guiding as well.”\n\n[Willow Druid] Guyden Aureon shrugs his shoulders \"I still hold ties to the Naelurir, yet such should never get in the way of cultivating the youth.\"\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Two nominations, going once.\"\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Twice....\"\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Three times. Sister Veil and Brother Willow, I'll speak to you after this Moot.\"\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"Next topic is from Brother Willow actually, he came to me about one of the Groves. Guyden, would you like to stand and tell us more of what you found?\"\n\n[Willow Druid] Guyden Aureon glances towards Jere for a moment, only to face forward once more. Gripping his staff and rising to his feet \"Aye. Appreciated. I'll get right down to the point.\n\nIt is to my belief that the remains of the old Savanna Grove are haven to the Jackal. Who- while under his influence- has torched much of the surrounding flora, and spread death to anything in the vicinity.\"\n\n[Ram Druid] Jeremiah: \"It isn't even corrupted, it's just burnt land. There is ash, which is right fertile. Ah bet only a few of us could heal the whole thing.\"\n\n[Willow Druid] Guyden Aureon: \"Myself and Brother Ram-,\" He gestures towards Jere \"Attempted healing the land, yet what efforts we managed were promptly undone.\n\nNow... is it not our duty to correct such blatant disrespect to nature and the Aspects?\"\n\nKaryssmov Faroe: \"My issue with healing it... is that if people don't live there to keep it healed, how are ya gunna stop it from happening again, unless you station Druii' there to stop the Jackal from returning, as it seems he lives there.\"\n\n[Willow Druid] Guyden Aureon: \"Ahernan, Hareven.\" Gently bobbing his head, he continues \"There are more than enough Drui' present at this moment in time to heal the Grove thrice-over. Yes-,\" He looks towards Kary\n\n\"The Jackal could try to corrupt it again. However, without the issue of dragonflame, I doubt he'd be able to do nigh as much impact.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"There is also the chance his phylactery might be hidden there. Which makes to prospect of being able to explore it once healed a pleasant idea.\"\n\n[Wolf Druid] Ouity raises a hand slowly. \"If the soul of the Jackal were to be found, I would wish it that this Circle would summon me at once.\"\n\n[Willow Druid] Pausing for a long moment, Guyden would tap his staff... before shaking his head \"No, that will be all. Ahernan.\" He bows his head.\n\nIndra: \"Very well. Brother Bluejay, you're up.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Alrighty. .\"He'd rock himself up, taking a stand ,\" Just two or three things. First, a concern regarding weapons spreading around.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"As Karyssmov has fought as well, as well as Toren and perhaps others. The Judges from Devirad, strange Abyss men whom are only half dead that are gifts dunnae work on, and suck out life.\n\nAll whom have ran into them here by raising of hands?\"\nHareven Lorenthus pulls a leather bound sword off of his back, a massive blade, slowly undoing the bindings around the single edge of the long blade as people raised their hands\n\n\"I believe weapons of their nature are spreading around. This was picked up by another druid with no idea of its nature until it made contact with the ground. It saps life.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus actually hands the Blade to kary to destroy, since he's so eager.\n\n[Exemplar] Karyssmov Faroe proceeds to set the blade upon his lap, making sure it is upon plate and not skin, before his eyes begin to smolder\n\nwith an amber glow and he gets to work.\n\nLaethesia: \"Can whatever that is, be done with any sort of weapon or item? Or just swords and things.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Gauntlets have been reported to do the same. So I'd assume so.\"\n\n[Willow Druid] Guyden Aureon: \"Those... Judges of Devirad have proved to be quite the wanderers. Actively seeking out and slaying anyone of faith, no exceptions.\"\n\nHe expels a soft sigh \"Measures should be taken against them soon lest we wish to fear their arrogance for longer.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Yes, they are not weak, but a word for those who are reliant on your gifts to fight. Do not fight them with your gifts. You will fuel them and lose, learn to swing a sword and axe, anything like you would have used before attunement.\n\nOtherwise you will die.\"\n\n[Ox Druid] Popo: “How would using our gifts fuel them?”\n\nHareven Lorenthus: “They suck the life out of anything they touch, that’s how Ox.\n\nAnd typically, they are clad in bronze or silver armors, preaching of the Old Dark. Which brings me to my next topic. Who all here can even fight worth a damn,\n\nand state as much with confidence without using roots and other such methods with our gifts?\n\nA few members at the moot raise their hands, not all however.\n\nHareven Lorenthus: “Good. It comes to attention that some of our kin can’t for the life of them, is the problem and those who cannot need to learn.\n\nWe’ve had endless issues with people using gifts in petty fights, many can recall off the the top of their heads the last Raven (Raven druid) doing so too many fucking times,\n\nand now with these Abyss knights, those who don’t know need to learn. Seek our another who can and please educate yourself as soon as possible.\nLastly. Someone started a fire in the Dominion not long ago.\n\nI know a lot of those here don’t care about the Naeluir but this almost spread throughout a fruit grove and into the main forest itself. I’d like to keep an eye out for possible suspects to who started this.\n\nIt was barely by the intervention of Sister Mint, a red headed male druid and myself, as well as a water evolutionist known as Richard, the fire was stopped.”\n\n[Trout Druid] Kasfer: “What if someone just left the oven on? Why are we looking for suspects?”\nHaveven Lorenthus: “No clues of who started it have been found so far, and the damage wasn’t terrible thankfully,\n\nbut it remains that someone almost caused a massive issue for us, for if those forests caught on fire, it would be beyond hard to stop. Signs point to arson.”\n\n[Wolf Druid] Ouity: “If I may present a topic of mine own?”\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: “Come forth, Brother.”\n\n[Wolf Druid] Ouity: “I am here to hold a vote. I would appreciate a lack of interruption.\n\n would like to nominate the Song Druid, Arik, to the position of Notable Druid. His deeds may have been lost to time, I intend to let you know of his action before a vote is cast.\n\nIn the realm of Aegis, the first home of the Descendants, Song was an Archdruid, and retained his title through Asulon. He split the first circle and would go on to document many of our ways.\n\nI would nominate him as ‘Scribe’ For it was he who created convention from chaos. He detailed meticulously every gift a druid can possess and did so well enough that to this day his teaches are used to train dedicants and druids alike.\n\nHe served faithfully within the order all the way until Athera, many elven years, before eventually fading into the wild as many elder druids often do.”\n\n\n\n-------------------Continued in part two\n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("14th GH 1637 Pt2", "§bTailesin", "===================Moot 14th Grandharvest 1637\nPart two\n===================\n\n-Scribed by Tailesin, Lynx Druid.\n\n[Calendula Druid] Mare: “Not only did Arik bring forth balance both within nature and the order, he managed to sustain the belief within those who once lacked it;\n\nHe brought hardship and passionate fury just at the right of times. Something worth bringing to mind when aiming to nominate him for such a role. He was akin to stone,\n\nunable to rock into another direction even during our darkest of of times. Genuinely, I think he earns it.\n\n[Wolf Druid] Ouity: “Unless anybody else has something to add I would like to cast the votes. I would say this. I nominate Song because truly I believe that his contribution should be remembered. Then shall we?”\n\nThe vote was passed. Song Druid, Arik, has been granted the title of Notable Druid. Blessed be, Brother Song, the Scribe.\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: “Now. I am still young compared to many of you, though I know well enough the feats of our older druii.\n\nOne in particular s Brother Ram. He has been an excellent source of healing blight since I was a dedicant, and I am sure you all have fond memories of things he has done.\n\nSo with that, I would like to nominate Brother Ram for heirophant.”\n\nThe vote was passsed. Ram Druid, Jeremiah, has been granted the title of Hierophant. Blessed be, Brother Ram.\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: Brother Pine wanted to issue a rather specific apology as well as lay out a few other topic, but since he is not here. I cannot do his words justice. I shall be asking him to write a letter so that I may hand out.\n\nBut the gist of his apology is this.\nHe apologizes for not consulting the Inner circle about the Aspect Stones. He felt he was right in moving them however.\n\nI do want to point out that, this situation could have been avoided. I do not like seeing my Brothers and Sisters mad at each other. Myself, Kasfer and Za’Kabar are doing everything in our power to make sure your voices are heard.\n\nSo please am begging. If you have an issue, seek one of us out.I am more than happy to listen to your woes and try to help you.\n\nAlright. Now for the fun part. The season of giving thanks is upon us, and soon after that it will be Krugsmas. I want to try something.\n\nI haven’t seen this done before but I hope all of you will enjoy it. We will be doing a Secret Krugsmas gift exchange.\n\nNow. for our next festival we wanted to hold a feast in honor of giving thanks to pretty much all of you. Does anyone wish to suggest any other activities for this festival?\n\nGeneral gist of suggestions.\nCandy, pie eating contest, Pin the tail on the dedicant (with or without padding), Mazes,  Pie launching contest?, Archery contest, wreath making contest, shogging, after party, drinking contest, offer bake goods to others.", ""),
        StaticBook("Moot TFS 1640 P1", "§bTailesin", "===================          Moot Log\n The first seed 1640\n===================\n\n-Part 1\n\n-Scribed by\nLynx Druid Tailesin\n\n[Cosmos Druid] Indra: \"There's really not much for me to discuss.\" she looks up and around the group. \"So I'll just state my few reminders- after we're all settled.\n\nVery well. So the only reminder I have is for the Secret Krugsmas gift giving. Some of you have turned in your gifts and others are waiting, just a reminder that we'll be exchanging during the Festival.\n\nAny topics? Aye nay? If not I'm calling the moot to a close. Speak up now.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Oh. One thing to simplify something.\"\n\nRenn: \"New system of prizes - based on token or ticket count rewarded by festival games.. Beanstalk or tree race climbing, feasts, shogging, contests, HOCKEY, ice fishing, ice skating, snowball stuff.. Mistletoes.\"\n\nRenn: \"Do we have any other topics? I know Hareven was going to bring up a major topic, but he's fallen asleep for a bit.\"\n\nKaryssmov Faroe: \"Oh, uh.\" he raises a hand. “No, no. It's about the Jackal's grove thing, in case it wasn't already reported. Alright, so. Savannah grove.\n\nPeople are a bit in conflict about it, but whether or not you think the tree is dead is not the point here. There are growths beneath the tree.\n\n A sort of red crystal, growing and breathing like some sort of unholy mineral heart. It, I think, is continually damaging the land around it.\n\nMy Light as you know, burns taint. It lit the dust that the crystals exhumed up like oil to flame. Along with it, choked some of us to half-death.\n\neremiah nearly keeled over trying to heal it. Inhalation of the stuff, whilst not as bad as thanium, will definitely kill you.\n\nIt had to be purged with Clerical spells to save the lives of those who encountered it. So I say you lot should go and scout it, figure out what to do, and organize a mass purging of the stuff. Bring respirators and other supplies to deal with it.\n\nEither way, the crystals are spreading like a plague, and must be stopped lest the land around it would be destroyed. Either they need to be sealed and removed, or something. I'm not the land-taint expert, I'm the person-taint expert.\"\n\nRenn: \"I suppose I'll toss up a topic. It's somewhat urgent and only popped in my head just then. When expeditions are made to the Savannah Grove, notify the Order.\n\nGoing on your lonesome, or with very little company will only end out worse for ware. I don't want to see any more dead druids arriving at my doorstep.\n\nHareven had a topic I originally intended on writing a small speech for this. Though I entrusted this 'project' with Hareven. A druid who I believe holds an excellent amount of potential.\n\nOur Order is founded off of unity. It would not be here today if it did not triumph through its many trials of rising and falling. Though what has always been consistent is that we can be brought together when the call is made.\n\nWe are druids. Alike in mind and spirit. This topic isn't something I bring lightly, nor as something that should be dismissed at the mere mention.\n\nAll I ask is that you please think on it. The project of rejuvenation and restoring the Order to its roots. That we rid of our divided ranks and disconnected beliefs. I am proposing that we draw ourselves to one another once more.\n\nNot as any pre-existing Circle, but rather as one larger Circle that we may call home. Have your own groves, have your own beliefs, teachings, and opinions. But we should no longer shy away from one another.\"\n\nViltaren Lorenthus: \" I am a dedicant, and It isn't my place to speak here. That being said, what Renn is talking about is being worked at by Hareven.\n\nIt is why he wishes to talk to all of you. So please do when you all have a moment. He is putting a lot of effort into ensuring everyone's thoughts and feels are taken into account.\n\nThis will not move forward until everyone has had a chance to speak with him. That is all.\"\n\nRenn: \"Mhm. What Viltaren spoke rings true. This is something I have spoken with some of you about - and something I have spoken with Hareven extensively on.\n\nIt's not something I wish to take from him in pursuing - though he.. fell asleep. I thought this would be a better time than any to address it. Kary, you wanted to speak?\"\n\nKaryssmov Faroe: \"Aye.\" he rises, clearing his throat.\n\"The concepts that you Druii' have are wondrous ones, ones that I myself in my youth lived. The entire time,\n\nI would drift between the Mother Grove that was then in Sutica to the Naelur'ir, to see what was different about it, the disconnect between the two that had become so obvious that even a mere dedicant would see and feel the repercussions of such.\n\nThe foundations upon which each grove is made is one of thought. The ones they got from their teachers, who taught their teachers, so on so forth But there is a crucial part of it all. It's all different.\n\nWe've seen time and time again, even now, how those who dare to speak out against the 'mass' are punished with either a shun, disconnection, or with a dismissal of their words or actions.\n\nThe concept of multiple circles, each with their own brand of thought and what makes them unique... prevents this sort of mass hatred. If a Naelur'ir was decried from their own circle because they thought different,\n\nthey could simply go to the Mother Grove and be welcomed. But if we were to do as you said, and bring everything under one order and one 'government' so to speak, how would we be able to stop something like that from happening?\n\nA Dismissal of those who think different? Democracy is an important factor. \nWhile circle splits are an issue as it draws Druids from the 'main order', it is something that would happen anyway.\n\nA different druid wouldn't agree with the 'main' druids order, and simply work on their thoughts in secret. Democracy and free thought are what the Circles are offered as the system is now. I'm not saying it's a perfect system, but it's not broken.\n\nAn authoritarian hand upon the Druii' is not what you lot need. Imagine, for instance, and sorry I'm taking so long with this. If the High Elven Clerics and the Sutican Clerics were forced to work together, despite our innumerable differences.\n\nIt would be bloodshed. We do our mission in our way, and they do it in theirs. They think we're wrong, I think they're wrong. And as it is, the system works. We simply don't interact with them, but His Mission is still completed.\n\nAs it is today, with your Circles. Balance is still maintained. To bring everyone under one banner would do naught but destroy free thought and the power of choice.\"\n\nRenn: \"Those words inspired by doubts. Doubts that this Order must be ridded of. In the past, the Order has held traditions that silenced outsiders, silenced dedicants. And even silenced its own brethren based on age.\n\nWe have come a long way with the ability to provide concise and free word from everyone. No one has been silenced or dismissed. No one has been shunned or disconnected for speaking out. These are misconceived.\n\nThough your words do ring true to an extent, Karyssmov. \n\nEach Circle is different. Unique in its own right. This new reformation would not want to silence or oppress any beliefs or ways of thinking.\n\nNor any individuality or uniqueness. Rather, it aims to promote these. It allows everyone's voice to be heard on an equal level. \nThere would be no discrimination as there currently is.\n\nDiscrimination in believing that one that isn't of your Circle is out to get you. These seeds of hatred and doubt are what caused this divide. This is a choice. It is not something that will be forced,\n\nnor will you be ridiculed on your decision. We are past those days. \nA decision to be made by yourself. A druid, a dedicant, a wanderer. This reformation would provide a larger council of Archdruids to appeal to the pre-existing CIrcles.\n\nThese roles would be redefined, not as leaders - but as those that serve to guide and maintain the Circle, the Order, and its kin.\"\n\nOwl Druid: \"I wish to speak if I may.\"\nRenn: \"Go ahead, sister.\"\n\nOwl Druid: \"Ever since I had returned from my wandering, I have seen many things. Some good, yet for the most part, disheartening. What you speak of unity is something all of us should strife for and see each other,\n\nno matter our teachings, as brothers and sisters.\nWe cannot dismiss facts of how fellows druids have been treating others. To say disillusion on brothers and sisters going after each other isn't something to merely state.\n\nEveryone here has seen quite a few example of druids doing just that. \nInsulting. Beating, fighting, punishing, and even killing one another while others aren't punished at all. A Lot do not feel safe and are wary.\n\nIn the letters we between you and I when the other two circles formed, I even warned of this and now it's come to pass. We need to focus on mending and acknowledging this before trying to merge back together again.\"\n\nRenn: \"Of course. This is not a choice that anyone asks of any of you immediately. All is wished is that you think upon it.\"\n\n[Trout Druid] Kasfer: \"I personally believe that the best way to mend the wrongs within t' order would be to merge circles like this. Of course, not immediately, but over a long period of time. Being separated only increases the already existing tensions,\n\n and draws them out further by being divided. Only by being together, and talking, can we mend the wrongs we grant upon each other.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Moot TFS 1640 P2", "§bTailesin", "===================         Moot Log\n The First Seed 1640\n===================\n\n-Part 2\n-Scribed by\nLynx Druid Tailesin\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"So, for those not acquainted, I'm Brother Bluejay. I'm t'at one druid dwarf who keeps wandering into bullshit and getting out without dying. Hi. \nBy logic that escapes me,\n\n I've been chosen to gather the opinions and wants of the entire Order in regards to attempting to restore its status. \nOf course, t'e first option to trying to achieve this is a merge.\n\nA lovely idea in theory for the reasons Kasfer brought up. But Kary also brings up valid points. \n\nAs does Owl. And t'is is where this long walk begins.\n\nWe don't listen to one another cause most of us aren' willing to talk. We aren't willing to talk because people lash out. We're finally at a point in the Orders existence again where we can\n\nfeasibly see Order-Wide cooperate feasible again. \nBut I cannae see t'is through without everyone speaking. Renn asked me to help her fix the order.\n\nA True Circle would be grand but that isn't possible if everyone doesn't want it. We also don't want to be an autocracy. Which is why I ask you. \n\nTell every drui you know to find me.\n\nTo speak to me. I don't care if you piss on my shoes to get your point across, I want your opinions. I want to know your beliefs. If a merge isn't possible, we need to still focus on sealing the cracks.\n\nMembers being absolutely crass to one another . Communication not going through . \n\nWe can fix these but not if you sit silently. Be loud.\n\nBe loud at me. Be like Kary, Viltaren, be loud and unafraid to tell me what you think and what you want. I will see my task through one way or the other but this task affects the entire order.\n\nThis Order is a family at the end of the day, but we have to try and do something. Or does anyone not agree?\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus motions a hand to Karyssmov \"You were a dedicant once, you know how we work, so please speak.\"\n\nKaryssmov Faroe: \"Aye, I was once. I know how it works. I know that there is a divide, one that simply can't be fixed by lumping everyone into a pan and saying to 'play nice'. \n\nWhile I agree, there needs to be more cooperation\n\nbetween Orders, a merger is not a good idea. To force each other to interact, to force each other to put up with each other, that'll only cause issue. Holy Orders. Great idea in concept, yeah?\n\nNever works out, and not because of one faction fucking up or anything. Such things fail because they are, intrinsically, different. We need to not ignore this important fact!\n\nWe are all different, everyone here has some different idea of how to go about Druidism, how they wish to preserve the balance. \n\nTo standardize things would only remove this freethought,\n\nthis value of creativity that allows your magic to flourish as it does.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Conflict of ideals, aye.\"\n\nKaryssmov Faroe: \"Precisely, it is a conflict.\n\nWhile yes you should work together, having a merger won't work out. \nYou instead, should focus on attempting a mass moot instead,\n\nthat I what I think you should do. It would complete the ideals you are trying to set forth by having folk talk to each other, without forcing them to be there and deal with those they don't like.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"And amongst all the ideas I have been drawing up, I have considered ways to alleviate that, but aye. You make a valid point. Which is what I need. From everyone. Please don't be afraid to talk.”\n\nKaryssmov Faroe: \"If you think about it, if you cannot even make a mass moot happens, what makes you think that people would accept to be merged with folk they don't like? I say, take baby steps.\n\nTry to have everyone work together first. As a merger won't do anything but cause strife.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"The long path. .\"He'd nod,before waving a hand at Kasfer to speak.\n\n[Trout Druid] Kasfer: \"It won't be an instant thing, obviously, Kary. It'll take.. years, decades maybe, to get to the point that ah merge even becomes viable. But' it is not only ah goal we can all work towards,\n\nit will help us do our duty better. Being within the' same circle does not' mean you must' all conform to the' same way of serving nature. It simply means working together in different' parts t' form the whole,\n\n in which we all seek to partake in. It just simply takes seeing all of our own prides.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"I know. I know not everyone likes one another. But Wisteria seems to believe most of you like me.\n\nEnough to talk to me and see if this is feasible. Listen. I don't want to be liked. I just want to do my duty. I can't do it without communication. So even you make a point.\"\n\nRenn: \"Thank you all for listening. I heavily encourage you to seek out Hareven if you wish to add anything more.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"Final words then.\n\nPlease seek me out at any time. I will listen and work with you and your ideas. We can figure out a way to bond our Brothers and Sisters in the Order again. I'll be damned before I let the current state continue,\n\nmerge, no merge, even if I have to use Kasfer to cork shut the flow of bullshit that works its way into our midsts sometimes. Please talk to me. You know where I am.\n\nI've never turned down a Brother or Sister in need before. I don't intend to now.\"\n\nRenn: \"One more thing. If you have any ideas for the upcoming Krugsmas festival\n\nin six elven days, please do tell myself or Indra! We'll have ice hockey, and a new prize system with unique prizes! That's all.\"\n\nHareven Lorenthus: \"MOOT OVER.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Moot 8th FS 1606", "§bAiden", "Moot\n8th Of The First Seed\n1606.\n\n-Recorded By\nPhoenix Druid Aiden.\n\n-Toren's Speech-\n\nWhen the explores found Bertha, and spoke about her at the previous moot we left out some important information. When we arrived the grove was already the way it is, and was named the wandering grove. The addition of many homes\n\nwere not us, all we did was place the aspect stones in their place. When we did arrive here, two strangers were already living upon Bertha, we quickly recognised them as unknown druids but when we asked them their totems and names it was as if they spoke\n\na completely different language to us. However it was clear the aspects brought us to them, as they already know of our needing of a new home and upon completion left, this is the reason why I refer to them as the two wandering druids. Those who were good\n\nat spotting things, would notice two statues at the entrance both in honour of the nameless wandering druids. I just felt it was appropriate to inform you all of such, and respect them for their devotion.\n\n-Renn's Opening Speech-\n\n\nAs many of you can see we've arrived within our new grove, hopefully this place will be a good place for our kin and new members of the order, we've already gathered quite a lot of outside interest.\n\nIt may take time to get accustomed to the new grove. We've continued the deathsbane tavern, labeled \"Lion's Hearth Inn\" Ask Elly for a job. If you need a home speak with one of the running Archdruids, we're limited on space, so you may need to bunk with\n\nanother druid, though we're always open to switch things about, so don't be stressed. Any non-druids needing housing seek out a tavern worker, they'll be maintaining a lot of the rooms located to the left of the entrance.\n\n-Questions About The New Grove-\n\nZa'Kabar-\nWhat's to stop spooks from just waltzing in?\n\nRenn-\nBertha's shell is next to impossible to climb, but also out of reach from sea level. The only way for that to be possible is to go\n\nthrough the farm island, which is also Bertha's feeding ground on occasion. Currently we're working to fix that, but its not a main priority until we see an active presence of whatever spooks throw themselves at us.\n\n-No more questions-\n\n-Renn, Concerning Elly's Temporary Archdruidship-\n\nElly will no longer be acting as a temporary fill in for Lilliana. Instead she'll be replacing her and will become an offical archdruid.\n\n-Renn Guide System-\n\nA lot of traditions have changed since our past. Some for better, some for worse. Though change is always the way of nature. And thusly expect some necessary changes to the guide system over the course of the next elven month.\n\n-Elly Guide System Further Explanation.-\n\nThe idea is quite simple. A mentor system. Those who show interest in becoming a guide, could talk to an archdruid. They'll go through training, instead of just being thrown in after begin voted in.\n\nThe druid would come to find an archdruid, learn what they need to learn. Learn how to make proper tasks, learn how to teach a dedicant, their interview process, everything. They could shadow an experienced guide, which will give both of them extra\n\nexperience. When deemed fit by the archdruid overseeing their training, they could be voted in at the next moot. It'll help the newer guides train our next generation of druids. \n\nNo objections to new guide system, thus passed and implemented.\n\n-Renn Removing Inactive Guides-\n\nThis is more of an unfortunate topic, though we'll be removing two acting guides currently. This being both sister Holly Ashton, and brother Buffalo Sena. They've done the circle, and the order well. But it seems as though\n\nthe aspects call them elsewhere for the time being. If they choose to return, I'm sure their continued efforts would be appreciated. \n\nNeither were nominated for Hierophancy.\n\n-Renn Implementing Head Teacher-\n\nAs per the renewal of a more fluent guide system, we think it necessary to reimplement the head teacher role. Refefined it as well perhaps. It would entail assisting in teaching new guides, alongside Elly's system.\n\nBut also maintaining documents to ensure guide fluidity, and maintain consistency in rites and tasks themselves this dosen't mean tasks will be set, they'll still have their appropriate styles of the respective guide of course.  This role would also be\n\nworking alongside both the scribes of the library and the inner circle to ensure things run smoothly. Though! We are at a problem who to pick?\n\nPhoenix Druid Aiden-\nNominated Bull Druid Toren.\n\nToren Received The Role.\n\n-Renn Interview Before Grand Task-\n\nNow next, little tid-bit. I'll be personally enforcing an old tradition on that I believe was necessary for the guide system. A guide must contact an archdruid prior to their dedicant doing their grand task. They'll be\n\ninterviewed promptly to ensure that they've been taught correctly, and are ready to preform their grand task.\n\n-Questions-\n\nGi'Garun: Can Hierophants interview and inform the archdruid, if they aren't present?\n\nRenn: Preferable, it'd be best to seek out an archdruid, if at all possible at the time. But if not, then I don't see a problem with allowing the hierophant to interview. It just means we'll also have to send a rough outline of what the interview should\n\nentail to all the heirophants. Though it shouldn't be much of a problem.\n\n-Renn Library-\n\nAnywho onto the topic of the library. We'll be somewhat reviewing how the library works. We want it to have a more profound impact on the grove and the order itself. Literature and history have always been a large part of who we are.\n\nso we've allowed a head scrube to manage that whole area. He would also be in charge of ensuring scribes stay on their toes, and all moots are documented for those that can't attend. Currently we have Aiden, Brother Phoenix acting as head scribe.\n\nHe's been documenting this moot so far, and hope it turns out well, and I'm sure it will.\n\n-Renn Artifact Collection-\n\nWe'll also have an artifact collection within our library. These artifacts of course relating to druids, and our culture and history. As for artifacts currently being used or kept in secret, replicas would be\n\nmade of them. So if you have an artifact you would like to donate, have documented, or perhpas have a replica made, seek out Aiden.\n\n-Renn Recruiting-\n\nWe'll also be encouraging more recruiting now, with the new grove and all. The naelurir are almost beating us, and that quite frankly isnt acceptable. So, I encourage you all to get out there, get students, or direct them to a\n\nguide. We'll also probably have an influx of non-druids wandering the grove. It seems to be a hotspot recently. So I'd encourage you all to make friends with our visitors. Though to add onto the potential influx of new visistors, we also wish to hold more\n\nfestivals and the likes. Sometimes we'll encourage our druids to get out there into other places, recruit and offer possible blessings. Other times, we'll encourage visitors to a variety of events! Whether this be a flower dance of sorts, some sort of\n\nsummer-type festival, or feast, or even a carnival!\n\n-Renn Sharr's Topic-\n\nI don't know the entirety of what it is\nSharr wished to speak about, though from what I was told he wishes to flourish an elder tree near the dwarven capital.\n\n-Renn Sharr's Attack On Sena-\n\nDuring the times of our Savannah grove, an incident occurred between both Brother Buffalo, and Brother Metal. One that almost resulted in the loss of Buffalo.\n-Renn Calls Za'Kabar To Speak-\n\nFrom what I've gathered, Sharr and some runelords were trying to kill the jackal. Oo'ri and Sena were nearby. Sena tried to prevent them from fighting. Runelords and Sharr were still trying to kill the jackal, nearly killing Sena in the process.\n\n-A vote for a trial against Sharr, for his chrimes against Sena.-\n\n-Trial Vote Passed. Sharr will be put to trial-\n\n-Elly Natures Bounty-\n\nA bonus note on our nature's bounty shops, if you have an interest in helping run or create one, please contact an archdruid and we'll get you set up. These shops are little stalls we have set outside the tavern. As well I'm looking\n\nfor little helpers in the tavern if anyone would like to help.\n\n-Elly Ending Note-\n\nOur last little blurb before everyone can go, please be aware we will be having moots quite regularly, so we can have smaller amounts of topics to go through, and in ture shorter moots!\nIsn't that lovely?", "")
    )

    private fun _ATLAS_13(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("SongDruidJournal", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "*You discover a small book. There aren’t many pages....perhaps there are more like this?*\n\nFrom the Journal of Dedicant Arik, SonofNone\n\nDay 1\nI admit, I was nervous about beginning my training, but I know that the Aspects guide me. The Rose Druid requested I log my progress to serve for the great library. Perhaps this will serve for future generations.\n\nMy attunement was only a few days ago, so I am having trouble adjusting to all these new feelings. Where there was solitude in my mind I now feel companionship with everything around me. It is difficult to put into words.\n\nThings that I saw previously before I was attuned have now become sharpened and clear-those insignificant blades of grass now seem as individual, and I am aware of an ebbing life force coming from everything. From what I understand of this Order,\n\n it is unnatural to be attuned as a Dedicant. The Rose Druid did not seem to mind, though, but it makes me wonder if I am ready to receive this knowledge, this enlightenment. Still, I relish the opportunity to learn alongside nature.\n\nDay 2\nI have taken to meditating within the sanctuary. I find that if I keep my mind at peace, the world opens itself to me, on a whole new level. It is as if my new connection becomes something else entirely; a pair of spectacles through which\n\nI see with my mind’s eye. I still however feel as though I am unable to to breach the veil I feel. I can only describe it as if wearing a watertight glove, and submerging my hand in water. I feel the weight of the water, the pressure, but there is\n\nsomething separating me from it. Perhaps another example would be a pig stomach filled with water. As much as I squeeze or stretch the water escapes and moves to another bubble. Following this I believe Druids learn to work without the glove, or to breach\n\nthe stomach.\n\nAfter hours of meditating it has come to a fruitless end. But, I am not discouraged. I have seen great works from the likes of the Wayward Druid; indeed, the Rose Druid has done wonders that perhaps I will never achieve.\n\nThe leaves and petals of roses shift him from place to place. Such a technique can only be granted by the Aspects. I digress however, I must rest now.\n\nDay 10\nIt has been many days since my last entry. It is something to behold, though, the progress I have made! It is small, it is little, I know, but each step I take feels as though a great stride along a staircase that goes on forever. I can only go\n\nhigher. And if I ever reach the end it will be Cerridwen and Cernunnos that greet me.\n\nMy meditations at the Druid’s Grove of Laurelin have given me new perspective as well. The Aspect stones seem to have given me a greater\n\nunderstanding of my abilities. Though I do not have the stomach for the Elven City, I think the sacrifice is worth making to experience such encompassing strength from the Aspects. Though I should note-there wasn’t much more I could do,\n\nI just felt so much more! As if my own meditations were overlooking a puddle of water caused by rainfall, and my meditations by the stones saw me looking out over a pond, or lake!\n\nStill, I should speak on my progress. I discovered that\n\nsmaller things are slowly responding to my own presence. Yes! Presence! I see that each thing is its own presence, it is just how we learn to identify them. I meditated within the Sactuary, I felt the presence of the Rose Druid and even Shoi. It was awe\n\ninspiring. This realization should help me in my training, I am certain of it!\n\n\nDay 15\nNot much progress as far as my abilities themselves go, but I am learning more and more about the nature of my connection (no pun intended).\n\nAs I meditate, and commune with nature, I am as if one presence that moves about, tethered to my own body as if by a cord. Can this cord be broken? Or is that unhealthy? Perhaps it can be lengthened....\n\nI read a tome in the library, describing how\n\nthe Aspects do not offer us control, but cooperation with nature. That my ability to grow a plant on the windowsill is determined by if the seed respects my request. What determines this respect? What gives me this respect? Or must I earn it? Is that\n\nwhat dedicancy is? Earning the respect of nature, so that it will become my friend?\n\nDoes nature judge? If so, does it judge us by our past? How will it judge me....?", ""),
        StaticBook("Aspect Essay", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Aspect Essay\n\n*by Riley Powell*\n\nThe assignment presented to me was to give examples on how the Aspects help to preserve the balance.\n\nThey do this in many ways but the main way is through Druids. Druids are directed to preserve and protect the balance of nature.\n\nThey can do this through many ways. One of the main examples that I remember is when we first were coming to Vailor.\n\nI was a little kid then, but I remember the Mother Grove becoming overrun with rabbits. There were so many rabbits you could hardly walk for stepping on one. My big sister Gwen even started naming them all and tried to create a family tree for them all.\n\nBut the rabbits were suffering. There wasn't enough food there for all the rabbits, even with people feeding them, and there definitely wasn't enough room for them and us. So a few Druids relocated a bunch of rabbits to another area in Vailor, so the\n\npopulation could spread out there.\n\nAnother example of this can be through the Aspects using nature itself to preserve the balance. Areas in the world are sometimes overgrown with trees and plants, or overpopulated with animals.\n\nOne way that this can be solved through nature would be natural events, like a forest fire, or hurricane. \n\nIn an area with too many trees, a strong wind of a hurricane would blow down trees with weaker root systems and\n\nmake room for the other trees to expand their roots. Fire can also help with this. When the trees and plants burn, their ashes make the soil rich, so that new life can bloom forth with time.\n\nThe other way could be through an event, like a natural sickness and the changing of the seasons.\n\nSometimes a natural sickness, like rabies, will become rampant in an area.\n\nThose that get infected die off and leave more room for those that survived to thrive.\n\nThe same goes for harsh summers and winters. Weaker animals succumb to the elements, while the stronger ones survive.\n\nThose that die, their bodies go on to join the natural cycle and provide nourshiment for the other animals and plants.\n\nSo there are many ways that the Aspects can preserve the balance of nature. Through Druids, weather,\n\nnatural events, and nature itself, the balance will continue to be preserved and the natural cycle maintained.", ""),
        StaticBook("Finding Nature", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "How Did I Find Nature?\n\n*By: Lillith Winterleaf, the Sequoia Druid*\n\nI found the Order by mistake, oddly enough. It was only about four years since Aegis had fallen and I had been married for about a year.\n\nI was five months pregnant, and stubbornly travelling to Salvus to trade some of our wools and leathers for food supplies, as we had not built our farms yet.\n\nOn the crossroads leading towards the Salvus farmlands a man drew a knife on\n\nme, and proceeded to steal my leathers and wools, and what little minas I had. He then decided he was going to take me to sell as a slave, so I started struggling. He sliced my arm and side as I broke away from him and fled across the outskirts of Salvus.\n\nI did not know what was beyond Salvus, I fled into a dense area of oak trees and ended up stumbling upon a coastline with a long wooden bridge that almost looked grown out of the trees, spanning the waters to an island of dense trees with a huge tree in\n\nthe center. I held my bleeding side and crossed over the bridge, pushing vines and leaves out of my way.\n\nI stepped off the bridge into a small clearing, a small shop stall on the right, ahead a root gate leading inside the great tree,\n\nto the left a pathway led into the trees and I could see a small wheat farm along the path. Standing in the root gateway were two robed figures, one male and one female, both elves.\n\nThey turned and saw me, the woman stepping towards me after a\n\nmoment. She wore red and yellow robes, and her eyes were fake, made of gold, but they moved and she seemed able to see, a worried look on her face as she saw me, asking if I was okay. I told her I was attacked and she took me down the path toward the\n\nfarm, sitting me down next to the small stream to relax while she tended to me.\n\nI asked what this place was, it was beautiful. She smiled and told me it was the Druid Grove, and introduced herself as Affection Druid Jena, a Guide of the Druidic\n\nOrder. We spoke for a long while, my wounds taking little time for her powers to heal, but finding pleasant conversation in each other. She told me of the Order and answered all my questions. When we grew hungry, she excused herself to get us a snack.\n\nWhile she was away, the man from the gate approached, asking if I was okay and introducing himself as Ice Druid Jameson, one of the Archdruids. We spoke for a short while, then two Orcs with staves ran by, one wearing a pumpkin on his head, yelling out\n\n for the 'bangaloos', hitting my head grazingly with his staff as he ran by. Jameson excused himself and ran after them, yelling at them to be silent. Jena returned shortly with a plate of bread slices, a cream spread on them which she told me was made\n\nof bonemeal, and sliced apples.\n\nWe talked through the night, both of us napping serenely under the canopy and stars. In the morning I asked if I could return after having my child to join the Order, she smiled and said that I was welcome to return\n\nwhenever I wanted and she would be happy to instruct me.\n\nI had my son four months later, Aeran, and I returned to the grove four months after that with him in my arms, answered Jena's questions and became a Dedicant.\n\nJena instructed me, with advice along the way from Archdruids Jameson and Saviticus, as well as Mystery Druid Thomas. Once I became attuned as the Sequoia Druid by Jena, I began learning all forms of medicine and healing that I could,\n\nfrom Jena as well as a little from Saviticus, but mostly taking their lessons and experimenting on my own to refine the techniques, and some lessons on bandages and remedies from both the Monks and some Clerics.\n\nThe rest is a much longer story for another time, but this is how I found my calling in nature.", ""),
        StaticBook("TasksFromVerden", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Tasks from Verden\n\n*Apple Druid Leyun*\n\n(( X-1162 / Z-427 ))\n\nHead to Darkhaven and question the population about any strange occurrences or sightings in the nearby forest.\n\nBring back your findings to me directly.\n\n((X -1059 / Y -291))\n\nTravel to the Infested Forest and survey the area. Bring back any information of the undead roaming there, and some proof that you were there.\n\n((X -425 / Z -331))\n\n*A simple map has been dawn over the page, leading to a specific point of interest somewhere on the continent*\n\nI would like for you to take Tony, the lakefish, back to his home at Highland lake. The lake has been having\n\ntroubles recently with an invasive parasite, and Tony here is our solution. Also, please don't eat him.", ""),
        StaticBook("Verden's Review", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Verden's Review\n\n*written by Pine Druid Gi'Garun, rescribbed from old texts.*\n\nI sent Gavin and Plonas to survey and lessen the presence\nof undead in a forest that had become infested with them.\n\nThey both brought back a specimen of an undead spider,\nand provided information vital to restoring the area.\n\nI taught Gavin about the dangers of the unnatural, and\nhow we as druids not only look after nature, but also seek to remove that\n\nwhich is detrimental to the balance of\nthe world.\n\nPlonas was asleep somewhere. You may want to give\nhim a recap.", ""),
        StaticBook("Smurgla's Task", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Smurgla's Task\n\n*rescribed from old texts*\n\nSmurgla has gone to the Mali'Aheral city and scattered four good amounts of bonemeal about, being accompanied by a guard, of course.\n\nSmurgla has allowed for more flower and grass to appear!\n\nSmurgla has visited Aesterwald now, planting a flower every so often, but not in the snow! Smurgla met a few nice people and taught them about nature's important.\n\nSmurgla planted regrew some dirt back to crass and Smurgla cleaned up some yucky grass near Orcies' cities. Smurgla had no one to sign off on this one.\n\nSmurgla made some grass grow in yucky cave near D'orf city. D'rofs try to kill Smurgla though, so Smurgla had to do Drood task in cave. No one to sign off on this one.\n\nSmurgla used the last few pieces of bonemeal in the Drood's grove, itself. Smurgla thinks it is best to give back to where your roots are from. Smurgla planted a few flowers near the gate.\n\nSignatures:\n\nMali'Aheral:\n--Talimeracahe\n\nAsterwalkd:\n--Haleth MII", ""),
        StaticBook("Homework", "§bKira Winterleaf", "Ask three people of all the following ranks why they became druids, learn their reasons and gain knowledge.\n\nDruids:\nAeran the Donkey Druid:\n\"Well...first I, uhm, wanted to make Lillith proud. But then it quickly became me wanting to help the world, maintain balance, fix everything, y'know?\" Elyswyn the Rose Druid:\"I was raised in the Grove.\"\nZa\n\nGuide:\nOliver the Solace Druid:\n\"I felt something awaken inside of me that I hadn't felt since I was a kid in the backwood. I tried to subdue the feeling, but I failed, and now I'm a Druid.\" (Not a exact quote. Just a summary)\n\nArchdruid:\nHare Druid Taynuel:\n\"I became a Druid because I lacked purpose and direction. Druidism gave me both.\"\n\nHierophant:\nMuildir: \"I was raised to respect and cater nature.\"\nWhat made you think that?: \"I follow the Aspects closely.\"\n\nWhy do you follow the Aspects?: \"Because they deserve loyal people.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Elinor's Trial", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Elinor's Trial\n\n*Oine Druid Gi'Garun, scribed from old texts*\n\nTravel to the three groves and inquire about the life and\npersonalities of the Druids. Ask about their totem, and how\nthey serve Nature and the Aspects. Be kind, courteous, and\nfriendly; this is your future family after all.\n\nThe Malinor circle sits comfortably at the back of the city.\nJust look for the absurdly large tree. The Wayward circle\nsits outside Rosewyn Hollow, outside former Salvus. It's\nvery secluded, so you might want to ask for directions.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tree Task", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Task Notes\n\n*rescribed from old texts*\n\nI have found a calm area to plant the sapling, just outside of Lillith's Grove. Firstly I have set up a camp, a fire, and a tent (all I need for now).\n\n I have also planted the sapling close by, so I can keep an eye on it. I will start caring for it in the morning, now I will rest.\n\nI have brought certain equipment with me to help with the trees growth, I will list them on the next page.\n\n10 bags of fertilizer\n\nA bucket, for water\n\nFencing, for protection\n\nI estimate using 2 bags of fertilizer every 2 months, with maybe some left over. I will begin logging how I care for the tree in the morning.\n\nThe First Month\n\nStarting off, I have planted the sapling just outside of where I have set up a tent. For the first few days I have started to sprinkle some Fertilizer around the sapling, along with pouring a little water in the soil.\n\nI even told it a story. I am not sure if it would help or not, but, I haven't spoken to anyone in days, it was nice to speak.\n\nLightning had struck close by in the night, it woke me up. A fire was close, small and easy to put out. It wasn’t too close\n\nThe sapling seems to have properly rooted itself into the ground, it’s sturdy and I can see some change in its growth.\n\nI have gotten into the routine of giving the sapling a little fertilizer and a little water every two days, this seems to keep\n\nthe sapling growing at a steady rate. The first month is almost over and the sapling is slowly showing good results in its growth.\n\nThe Second Month\n\nSince the sapling has now rooted into the dirt I have stopped giving it water as the roots should be able to pull water from the soil, I will continue to spread fertilizer every two days however.\n\nA few more days have passed, the sapling is showing rather promising results in its growth, the fertilizer is helping greatly.\n\nI sat by the sapling for awhile, humming a song that my mother used to sing to me when I was a boy.\n\nIf it could hear me, I hope it enjoyed it.\n\nI have gone through my second bag of fertilizer. I sprinkled the last of the bag this morning. The tree is making good progress and I find the area very peaceful and relaxing.\n\nI find myself fending off a few monsters in the night; They seem to have increased in number. They get close to the tree but are more interested in me, so I fight back.\n\nThe Third Month\n\nThe tree is making great progress now. I would say it is around the same size as me now and I feel a sense of accomplishment whenever I tend to it. I have a few more bags of fertilizer on me which should last the remaining time I\n\nhave. Spreading the fertilizer has also caused some grass and plants to grow in the area. Along with the tree, the area is starting to look rather nice.\n\nI am spending most fighting off monsters now. They come in small waves and are easy to handle.\n\nBut with the increase in the amount it is getting a little hard to sleep. Something is causing more to come, maybe me being here is causing more to come, but it’s best to not think upon this and focus on the tree.\n\nI have a few bags of fertilizer left and I think it may be time to stop spreading it. I think the tree can go without it now. I will let a few weeks pass and then look at the results.\n\nThe Fourth Month\n\nThe tree has grown taller than me now, it is nice to see it growing without fertilizer. I think this is the final month I will need to be here. I will spend some time here and talk to the tree. If it can hear me, I hope it enjoyed\n\nmy company for the time I have been with it.\n\nI have watched over the tree now for a total of just under four months. I am going to spend just a few more days with the tere, and then leave to speak with Arin to tell her I think I have completed my\n\ntask. All in all, this has been a nice experience and, to be honest, I am a little sad to see it come to an end. This will be the last thing I write before heading out to speak to Arin. I hope the tree enjoyed me here as much as I have enjoyed caring\n\nfor it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Verden's Summary", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Verden's Summary\n*rescribed from old texts*\n\nI sent Bartley to Gllowshroom cave to collect spores from\nthe rare breed of mushroom that grows inside. His small and quiet stature made short work of sneaking past the spider nest located\n\ninside.\n\nHe did well.\n\nThe point of this lesson was how we, as druids, must\nunderstand nature before we can make any attempt to\naid it. This was couple by also learning the proper technique to observe\n\nspiders in their natural\nhabitat without interfering more than needed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sav's Journal", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Saviticus's Journal\n*Rescribed from old texts*\n\nDay 1: I've moved into the temple, but the library isn't finished so I must wait to do my work. Also the Undead attacked Krugmar while I was there and I barely got away with my life. I am worried\n\nfor the safety of Kal'Urguan.\n\nDay 2: The library was finished and I have been working on filling it up. The Arch-Bishop said he was pleased with my progress. I went to the Elven lands to seek becoming a druid, but they said there was no one to teach\n\nme and they would get back to me. I doubt that they will. \n\nDay 3: I talked to the Wayward Druid himself today, and he took me with him as he went about his druidic tasks. It was an honor, but he had left early. I intend to seek him out as soon as\n\n possible to ask him to take me in as a druid. There was an emergency in Kal'Urguan today, as the crag swowed up the outer city. It was completely destroyed, and they are working on rebuilding it.\n\nDay 4: Great newsh! Oashish Druid John brought me into the order, and I have begun my teachingsh. He sheemsh to be a mosht capable druid, and I am eager to learn from... hic! him. Unfortunately, Priesht Kane died today deshpite Johnsh besht effortsh, and\n\nArch-Bishop Hoggarth wash removed from office. I no longer have any reashon to... hic! shtay in Kal'Ulguan and I dishagree with King Agrim, sho I have moved to Laurien and... hic! purchashed a nice home there with help from Hoggarth. I am eager to invite\n\nhim to come live with me. Alsho, Krumgar wash attacked again by the undead, and I was caught in the middle of it. We won, but their w was destroyed and many lifes were... hic! losht. I pray to the crafter that they don'y attack my new home.... hic!\n\nI 'ave decided to start this journal as a record of me life. I looked around me, and saw that I looked around me the other day, at this world which was changin' so fast. Towns are born and empires crumble, all while the undead plot against us.\n\n I 'ave decided to start this journal, so that one day people may read it and learn about what went on in me lifetime.", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations v1", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Observations v1\n\n*recorded by Dedicants Eldon and Beth, rescribbed from old texts.*\n\nOn their travels to the Witch Woods of Lenfarthing, the\nsubject of many tales of magical anomalies, the Dedicants\n\nmentioned came upon a strange sight. This create\nappeared to the Dedicants as a faintly glowing blue orb\nwhich floated and could vanish at will.\n\nAlthough one could be commonly seen, the Dedicants also observed a group\n\n hovering around one larger orb.\nThe Dedicants can only assume these were unnatural\nbeings created by magical events. \n\nMore records may\nbe taken at a later date.", ""),
        StaticBook("Interviews", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Dedicant Task: Interview\n*rescribed from old texts*\n\n*Dedicant Aeran Winterleaf*\n\nI was given the task to interview some Druids on what they do. I decided to also make this a bit of a candid interview series,\n\nso maybe someone in the future can read this and think 'Wow, that Aeran guy is a jerk.'\n\nMirabelle\n~~~~~~\nA: Ok, specifically, as clear as you can say it, in less than three sentences, what do you do in the Order?\n\nM: One: I learn from Miss Izzy how to use my gifts of healing.\nM: Two: I use my gifts to purge the taint and hairy ticks.\nA: What are hairy ticks?\nM: Oh, uhm. They're, y'know, hairy ticks. They commit heresy and sacrilege against nature and the\n\nbalance! Like necro-spooks and void slingers.\nA: I never got the spook meme. CONTINUE!\nM: Three, I do the sweeping and tend the shrines here in the Mama Grove.\n\n*Translated from Haflingese\n\nBarry\n~~~~\nA: What do you do?\nB: The rank of Druid has the duty to uphold the balance of nature and help wherever needed. That's the most basic description.\nA: And you specifically?\nB: I myself, as a Druid, I travel, help people observe\n\nothers, and protect those in danger as well as helping within the grove.\nA: So, just general helping, Barry?\nB: I also help with taint removal.\n\nKefi\n~~~\nA: What does your rank do for the order, and what do you specifically do for the order?\nA: In the simplest way you can explain it, of course.\nK: Hmm...\nA: Hmm isn't acceptable, I think.\n\nK: I'm just a normal Druid. I go about daily business, making sure order is kept and help other Druids when needed. Like today I helped heal the tree.\nA: Affirmative. And, do you do anything special specifically?\nK: Nope!\n\nA: So, do you think I write down 'lame as a mule' or...?\nK: I think that would be bad...\nA: Me too. Do you... I dunno, smuggle cactus green in from the Orcs, or... Subvert Orenian power by slipping peasants with 'Praise the Aspects' fliers?\n\nK: Nope. I talk to flowers and grow trees.\nA: Do you ever talk to flowers and grow trees, or specifically in that order?\nK: Both ways.\n\n*Kefi may or may not have been high during this interview, depending on who's asking.\n\nGi'Garun\n~~~~~~\nA: So, uhm, what do Archdruids do, and what do you do specifically, unique to other Archdruids?\nG: Well... Archdruids are considered head of the Order, and head of the inner circle. They head teaching, upkeeping the grove,\n\nmaintaining relationships with other organizations yet always maintaining our neutrality. We deal punishments in the order where it is needed, which is rare, and are in charge of pushing the Order forwards.\n\nA: Segwaying a bit, but is hanging Dedicants from vines acceptable punishment?\nG: Hmm? If the situation called for it...\nA: We'll assume it didn't and put that as a 'No.'\nG: Anywho, Archdruids are the only ones who may call moots or shuns,\n\nand are the only members of the order who many interact with a shunned person.\nG: For my specific role... I'm currently heading the upkeeping of the grove, as well as working on spreading the teachings of the Aspects about Athera. I'm also a Hierophant\n\nalthough I am unsure if you want to hear about that position from me as well.\nA: That answer was much better than Kefi's 'I dunno.' It's alright, I dunno if I could handle you talking for much longer anyways. Joking, joking.\n\nG: I could say the same about you. But anywho, that everything?\nA: Uhm, also, when was your last bowel movement? For the task, of course.\nG: I'd say... About five decades ago.\n\nTaynuel\n~~~~~\nA: What do Druids in general do, according to you, and what do you specifically do?\nT: Druids in general work to maintain and protect the delicate balance of nature.\nT: As a Druid, as in my duty, I watch over the highly\n\nvolatile balance of the world that Cerridwen and Cernunnos have been gracious enough to provide us with, ensuring that it does not fall to such a state of disrepair and pandemonium that the Druids are unable to save it from destruction by the ill-advised\n\nor unknowing hands of the mortal races. Put simply, I keep people from f**king nature up.\nA: Eloquent.\n\n- Dedicant Aeran Winterleaf", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid Task I", "§bSun Druid Alyain", "Alexander,\n\nAs a human, many of the other dedicants may look down upon you. And rightfully so -- the life of a human is so short that they, you included, can barely understand the impact descendants make upon nature, let alone try to bring balance to it.\n\nYou must prove the others wrong. I have faith in you: from the day I first saw you come to our grove, I felt the fires of the Aspects burning inside you.\n\nTravel north, to the deserts of Tahn, where your kin make their homes. Find their fields, and go out\n\ninto the middle of one. Sit, and meditate, eating and drinking as little as possible, for one elvish week. Understand what they take from nature. Then, return, and we will see what you have learned.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid Task IV", "§bVine Druid Turelin", "Nifin,\n\nYou performed your last task admirably -- that family would have lost everything. It's not so often a dedicant is called upon so suddenly, but you have displayed a courage few can compare. I'm proud of you.\n\nThe great ent Kharpando has requested to speak with you. The wisdom of the ents far outstrips even my own. Absorb every word. And make a good impression!", ""),
        StaticBook("On Pigmen", "§bTristanm1", "A Brief History of Pigmen: Aegis - Kalos\n\nBy Sunshine Druid Verden\n\nThe appearance of Zombie Pigmen was first documented during the Second Iblees Invasion, several hundred years ago. These unnatural monsters were used by the Fallen Daemon as powerful soldiers during his war of conquest against the mortal races of Aegis,\n\nwhere their group mentality and shear ferocity was crippling to the defenders.\n\nUnlike other lesser Undead, the Zombie Pigmen possessed an intellect far superior to any common Zombie or Skeleton. They would always be seen in possession of\n\njagged, golden swords and very rarely were encountered alone. Their speed was almost on par with that of the living, a trait that allowed few the luxury of escaping.\n\nAfter the invasion was ended and the Dark One had been sealed away for good, many\n\nstrange things were observed by the population of Aegis. While the mindless Zombies and Skeletons continued to attack anyone on sight, the Pigmen did not. They had become passive, almost completely indifferent to the world around them. Instead of\n\nmindlessly killing, they began to aimlessly roam the world, staring blindly at the things around them as if they weren't truly there.\n\nAfter Aegis had crumbled and sank into the sea, many thought they would never see the creatures again. And indeed, they\n\ndidn't. For a time. But, again, the Pigmen emerged from the unknown, and they were furious. For many years the Pigmen were found roaming the darkest corners of Asulon and attacked anyone foolish enough to get close. The reason for this is not clear, and\n\nmany theories have arisen to explain. However, none have been proven.\n\nYet another strange event was observed on the island of Elysium. A large volcano near the temporary Elven settlement erupted and spewed lava over the Malinor farms,\n\nand the Pigmen rode it down the slope. They were, thankfully, peaceful this time; but they appeared in numbers never seen before. Hundreds of them began to blindly wander from their lair, spilling over the border into the Orcish deserts. There they were\n\ndestroyed not by men, nor by beast, but by cacti. The creatures tore themselves to shreds over the next few months as they walked through the cacti farms, and they probably would still if it wasn't for the piles of their dead brethren impeding their\n\nmovement.\n\nSince arriving in Anthos there has only been one reported case of Pigmen. This was a very small issue, where only three or four Pigmen would wander around a border wall in the Wilds. A magical fountain was placed\n\nat this site, and all has been quiet since then.\n\nIf the Pigmen are here on Anthos, they are hiding very well.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lesser Dragons", "§bOrithur", "There are two species of dragonkin known to have descended from Dragur, though corrupted and defiled forms of both have formerly existed. Dragur, the Daemon of Knowledge, oftentimes appears as a dragon in his true form. Dragur’s chief descendants,\n\nthe Dragaar, are sentient “greater dragons” that seek to collect and share knowledge with mortals. They can conceal themselves under the guise of mortals and other creatures, though it is a rare occasion to meet a Dragaar in person.\n\nDragons, on the other hand, are more common -- though proportionately rare compared to other living beings -- than greater dragons. They are generally referred to as dragons, though the proper classification is “lesser dragon.”\n\nThey are non sentient, and are commonly viewed as natural predators that roam our world. However, like all living beings, they are susceptible to taint and corruption. Though a rare occasion, dragons have been known to alter their very nature\n\n into a twisted, foul portrayal consumed by hatred and rage for the mortal races. The outcome of this calamity is referred to as a drake. Little is known about the purpose of the dragonkin as a whole, except that each species’ intentions vary greatly.\n\nDRAGONS:\n\nThe dragon (or lesser dragon) species is beastlike in nature, and generally accepted to be non sentient. Though they cannot process thought or comprehend concepts, they are highly intelligent in regards to instinct.\n\nThe lesser dragon was once a rare sight to mortals, but they have appeared to exist in greater numbers in Anthos. Particularly, dragons roam the frozen wastelands of the North. Though they live in other regions of Anthos, the frigid north is home to many\n\ndeep caves and caverns, which they prefer for nesting purposes. The region is cold, secluded, and tribesmen allow the food supply to remain steady. Dragons may roam the land of Anthos in search of cattle or mortals if they grow tired of pork flesh,\n\n and they continue to hunt until they are satisfied. Although lesser dragons have been frequently encountered in recent times, their numbers are slowly dwindling as they have begun to retreat to the deep, cold North.\n\nDRAKES:\n\n“Do not underestimate the allure of darkness, mortal. Your kind has yet to discover that even the purest of souls are drawn to it.” -Aruzond. Through the malevolent desires of twisted corruption, the dragon has\n\nthe potential to unwillingly alter its very existence. When a dragon degrades into a drake, its body decays into a shadowy skeleton of its former self. The scales of a drake turn black as midnight, and their fangs cloak themselves in putrefying poison.\n\nWhile a drake does not develop sentience, its desires can be manipulated to follow any malignant alignment. Drakes forfeit any connection to their former selves, and their lust for eternal damnation of life causes them to instill fear in those that would\n\noppose it. The drake is slightly smaller in size compared to a dragon, but what it lacks in size and physical strength is compensated by maneuverability and lethality. It is rare to encounter an evil powerful enough to twist and taint the mind of a mighty\n\ndragon, but these influences communicate through our world more readily than we may often realize.", ""),
        StaticBook("Greater Dragons", "§bOrithur", "There are two species of dragonkin known to have descended from Dragur, though corrupted and defiled forms of both have formerly existed. Dragur, the Daemon of Knowledge, oftentimes appears as a dragon in his true form.\n\ndescendants, the Dragaar, are sentient “greater dragons” that seek to collect and share knowledge with mortals. They can conceal themselves under the guise of mortals and other creatures, though it is a rare occasion to meet a Dragaar in person.\n\nDragons, on the other hand, are more common -- though proportionately rare compared to other living beings -- than greater dragons. They are generally referred to as dragons, though the proper classification is “lesser dragon.”\n\nThey are non sentient, and are commonly viewed as natural predators that roam our world. However, like all living beings, they are susceptible to taint and corruption. Though a rare occasion, dragons have been known to alter their very nature into a\n\n twisted, foul portrayal consumed by hatred and rage for the mortal races. The outcome of this calamity is referred to as a drake. Little is known about the purpose of the dragonkin as a whole, except that each species’ intentions vary greatly.\n\nDRAGAAR:\n\nBestowed with the gift of immortality and sentience, the Dragaar exist chiefly to seek and gather knowledge. Each Dragaar is assigned the role of gathering a distinct aspect of knowledge, and this role defines their characteristics and demeanor.\n\nHowever, they do not solely desire knowledge and wisdom. The greater dragons, or Dragaar, act as ever watching wardens over the mortal races. However, most prefer to live in seclusion in deep thought and meditation, only to emerge when the races call upon\n\nthem in their time of need. The Dragaar can and often disguise themselves under the guise of a mortal or creature. This is their preferred method of watching over mortals, as it is both subtle and inconspicuous.  There are only two Dragaar that have\n\nrevealed themselves to the mortal races, and the decision to do so was not lightly concluded. Though the Dragaar seek to protect the mortal races, they often decide to do so by living in secrecy. Many discern that if they chose to associate\n\nthemselves with mortal affairs too frequently, dire consequences would arise. The primary concern held by Dragaar, in regards to involvement in the matters of mortals, is that the races would eventually resort to relying on them to protect\n\nmortals from any form of evil. However, the greater dragons aspire to teach the mortal races self-preservation, without them depending on the intervention of immortals.\n\nDRAKAAR:\n\nTo attempt to bend the will of a Dragaar would be all for naught, and thus what can cause a Dragaar to devolve itself into a twisted abomination of hatred is unknown. The ancient ancestors of the four mortal races once believed that the choice\n\n for this horrendous transformation to occur was their own, though the aftermath of such a decision would contradict their entrusted duty. The greater dragon itself is a terrifying opponent, but a Dragaar that feels no remorse and fears no consequence?\n\nThat is the true fear of corruption; the perversion of the mind and distortion of one’s morality is a dreadful circumstance. In past times, the outcome of such an event would often be referred to as a “greater drake.” It is the combination of a Dragaar's\n\n sentience and a drake’s all-consuming hatred. The likelihood of a Dragaar devolving into a being tormented by rage is next to none, as most would, in their right mind, turn themselves into stone to prevent the conversion. The only verifiable event\n\nrecorded of a Dragaar turning to stone is that of Velketzar. The Guardian Velketzar, consumed by self-condemnation and despair due to his failure, dramatically transformed into a stone statue to prevent the inevitable calamity.\n\nIf a Dragaar were to fall under the hand of corruption, it would hold the power to politically corrupt nations with ease. Additionally, detecting and pursuing such a foe would be toilsome, all due to its shape shifting ability.\n\nFortunately, deep meditation and concentration can inhibit this rare occurrence, along with immersing themselves in their aspect of knowledge.", ""),
        StaticBook("Uruk Culture", "§bPiercingDarkness", "Culture of the Orcs\n-----------------\nBook I, written by Ves\n\n\"May this book serve as a guide to any brave enough to venture into Orcish land. Or any scholar who may be curious of the hostile land to the far south.\"\n\nChapter 1:\nThe orcish homeland\n------------------\nAs most will know, the territory of orcs is the harsh sands of the desert. They have adapted to survive in such an unforgiving environment, so unless you are an orc born upon the sands, it is\n\nadvisable to be prepared before heading into the desert terrain, orcs are not the only threat there, thier land is just as harsh and unforgiving as they are. Many foolish adventurers have perished because of lack of preperation.\n\nThere are very few resources out there in the desert, so it is best to bring what you need to survive, water food and clothing to protect you from the effects of the strong desert sun. If however you are stranded without the supplies necassary\n\nthere are a few ways of obtaining them. Dehydration may be the most prominent killers in the desert, it is important to drink a lot and drink regularly, finding a stream is the most obvious source of water, however if you cannot find one,\n\nanother way of obtaining water is digging near vegitation such as the bushes or cacti that are found there, if you dig you will almost certainly find some water, but remember to never drink the sea water of surrounding oceans, that will only make\n\nthings worse. As for food, you may find small critters or bugs amongst the bushes or under rocks, so long as you cook them, most should be fine to eat, just to not eat the cacti as they are poisonous. Another very important thing is finding some shelter\n\nin the day, shelter will keep you cool and at night it will keep you warm, the days are hot and nights are cold so it is important to find a place to stay out of the harsh weather as it can have some pretty nasty effects if you are exposed for too long.\n\nbut is important to remember, that if you are in the desert, you are considered an intruder and will be treated as such, so do not step into orc land without expecting to be approached with a certain amount of hostility.\n\nChapter 2:\nOrc Clans\n------------------\nOrcs divide themselves into different groups called clans, the best way of explaining what a clan is, is that they are like families. That is why orcs will show such fierce loyalty to thier clan and will\n\nprotect thier clans with thier lives. Since the orcs left thier city there are too many clans to count, more and more sprout up constantly these new clans are often approached with hostility from the older and more respected clans such\n\nas Gorkil, Lur, Braduk and Ugluk. Each clan has thier own fort and land, which they develop and protect. Land is often the cause of war between clans, whether it be because one intrudes on another's land, or one clan wants the land owned by a\n\ndifferent clan. Orcs can either be born into a clan, or inducted by completing some kind of trial, which is different for each clan, often this is followed by some kind of ritual to welcome them to thier new family. Although the clans tend to stay\n\nout of each others business, they will often meet to discuss important matters and rally together in times of war against a common enemy, such as intruder in the desert or factions that oppose thier race. Each clan has a leader and various\n\nranks stemming down from there, orcs will earn thier rank by showing promise in battle or through loyalty, another less common way for an orc to gain a rank is for them to challenge another for thier possition, they will then fight for the rank.\n\nWhen on orc land, it is a smart idea to avoid setting foot in the city or on clan land, as for most clans this is a crime punnishable by death and sometimes something worse.\n\nChapter 3\nOrcish anatomy\n------------------\nWhen a orc is born and up until it is about eight to thirteen years old, it will look much like the child of any other race, of course larger and with a different coloured skin. It is when an orc\n\nreaches it's age of development that it takes the form that most people will recoginse. It is at this age of development that orcs take their adult form; generally described as the rapid growth of thick, ivory tusks, darkening and thickening of skin, and\n\nA greater tendancy to be thrown into Bloodlust. Along with these attributes, Orcs generally become more determined and 'Wise', compared to the cubs. Wise being a very widened term, with Orcs it can be used to tell of their inner sense of 'rightness'.\n\nBefore the age of thirteen is generally when most Orcs become whitewashes. The cubs are much more thick headed and rash compared to their older brothers (Though these are still much more easily angered than most other races).\n\nAfter this age, most Orcs tend to bond with their family and clan, and only tend to leave in much more serious circumstances, such as being banished. \nAn Orc's skin is generally thicker than most other races, Kha's included. The rough sands bite their\n\nHides and slowly but surely make them as tough as leather. Not unsurpisingly, Orcs hate colder climates. Perhaps it's their choice of clothing, or their tendancy to live in the hot badlands, but at any case, a shivering Orc is the only one you'll see in\n\nThe colder climates of the North and South, usually dressed in a somewhat amusing choice of thick, wooly, bundled clothing. Orcs come in a variety of colours, the most common being green, the rarest being black or even blue.\n\nChapter 4\nOrcish Customs\n-------------------As a race, the Orcs have certain customs that are shown by all clans. All Orcs hate Whitewashes. Save for those who are banished themselves, whitewashes are hated by all clans and all Orcs, and are hunted\n\nAs a sport by the race as a whole. Orcs are commonly superstitious, believing the spirits of their dead ascendants watch over to them this day, judging their honour watching their choices with approval, or disapproval, depending on the situation.\n\nThey see the Humans, Elves, Dwarves and Kha's as lesser races in general, though they have a certain mutual respect for the Dwaves' sturdyness. Various theories have arisen to propose why this is so, with the most generall accepted one being extracted\n\nFrom an Orcish fable, common to that of the Wandering Wizard. At the time of the four brothers, each had green skin. As the years of war against Iblees raged on, and the bad attributes of each race was shown, slowly the Elves, Humans and Dwarves' skin\n\nTurned pink; the colour of dishonour and taint, the same of that of the fungi which grow between their toes, often. Black, they see as trecherous and unstrustable, while colours such as purple is the colour of the spirits, and green the colour of life and\n\nPurity.\n\nChapter 5\nWhitewashes\n-------------------Whitewashes are somewhat uncommon in today's Orcish culture, though nonetheless hated by the race as a whole. A whitewash is not, as some believe, a nomadic Orc; one without a clan who roames at his free\n\nWill. A whitewash Orc, commonly known as Wiydwazh by the Uruks, is an Orc that has forsaken his or her own kind, broken their traditions and disregarded their god and beliefs, which generally revolve around Honor and Krug. A Whitewash Orc is hunted his or\n\nHer whole life (In most cases), by Orcs, and shooed away from any permanent home by any others due to the 'trouble' they bring, trouble being the Orc hunters intent on mounting the Whitewash's skull somewhere impressive.\n\nThey don't vary at all physically from other Orcs, though it is common for weaker Orcs to be banished from the Uzg, or their clan, due to losing an honor klomp, or something similar. In several cases, Whitewash Orcs can make their way back into the Orcish\n\nCommunity, usually by varying means, most involving klomps. Additionally, any Orc Shaman that becomes Whitewash loses their powers, since it is foremost fueled by the ancestors and spirits, which generally look down on Whitewashes.", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on Inwe", "§bTristanm1", "Notes on Inwe\nAuthor Unknown\n\nJoma, 20th of The Second Seed, 1316: \n\nThe Original Dark Elves of Inwe seemed to live within the forest, having trees for homes. In conflict with their orc neighbours, they hid themselves amongst the thick darkwood trees and found shelter and\n\npeace here. \n\nTheir houses are made of simple wooden constructions, preferring to live in the inside of thicker trees. Despite this preference, they were not keen on climbing, and dug cavernousdwellings below their houses as\n\npossible shelter and storage.\n\nThe grove within Inwe was the reason the Dark Elves split from Malinor. They worshipped the moon over the sun. This is evident in seeing their sacred pool, as it was built so moonlight would reflect in it during\n\ntheir nightly gatherings. \n\nIn time, Dark Elves learned to weave forests and perhaps even draw the moon's power into magical abilities. Their chantings of old seem to echo within the pool still. It was possible that the Dark Elves\n\nhad an order much like the Druids, but this is unconfirmed.\n\nTolta, 24th of The Second Seed, 1316 : \n\nThere is a strange sort of energy wafting this place. It surrounds the pool like mist. I feel it strongest in the mornings, as the dew hangs from the leaves and the moon's touch is still recent upon\n\nthe pool.\n\nIt is revitalizing, like waking from a long sleep. Some traces of the Mali'Ker magic might still linger here.\n\nIrba, 4th of Sun's Smile, 1316: \n\nI have found an old tome amongst the ruins of the Dark Elven settlement. It denotes that the Dark Elves in Inwe were hunted by a people they called 'Marauders', a group that finally found and attacked the village.\n\nThis may have been the cause of their disappearance, but that's anything but confirmed. \n\nThis book is not written in old elvish, but in common Aegis tongue. We might be able to derive a time period from this, or a taboo for using elvish in\n\nwritten work. Either way, the journal uses the word 'Velulaei', which possibly means 'Moon'. They speak of the moon as if she is an entity, like a goddess or a spirit. I wonder if this could be more than silly superstition of a primitive race.\n\nSabet, 28th of Snows Maiden, 1316: \n\nWe are starting to uncover multiple grotto's that may have served as cave dwellings and hiding spaces for the dark elves. These caverns seem damp and cramped, and I would abhor spending just\n\none night in one of them. The original inhabitants must have used them out of necessity, perhaps a fear of being found if they spend too much time above the surface.\n\nOne of the caverns is situated directly below the largest tree of\n\nthe village, baring its roots and the water that feeds it. I wonder if the elves tampered with this pool of water in some way, in order to have the tree grow to its unusual vastness.\n\nSpending too much time in this grotto makes me ill at ease, my senses\n\nurging me to leave.. This feeling might be the remnant of an ancient spell ment to keep intruders out. If this is true, I can only assume that within this grotto is something the former owners did not want to be found.", ""),
        StaticBook("TeethOfHell", "§bTristanm1", "Through the Teeth of Hell\nAuthor Unknown\n\nHarold Fergensun, an Orc, was walking down a stone road, pretending he was innocent. You see, Harold was a robber, rapist, and murderer, just to name a few. Then he was shot by guards. Arrow in his back, Harold dropped to his knees, and died.\n\nSwirling Shapes and figures rushed around him. He landed in the stone of Hell. Crying, yelling, death, all 'round. He saw above him the teeth of hell. He started climbing, and saw his friend Lucas, burning, pleading for him to help, but he said, \"I cannot\n\nhelp.\" He climbed further. He then saw his father, begging for salvation. Harold again says, \"I cannot help.\" He climbs almost to the top. Almost there, and... his wife. His dead wife. \"Help me Harold, I love you!\" Harold couldn't resist. \"I love you too,\n\nbut...\" he looked down. \"I must help you all.\" He drags all the earlier mentioned friends through the teeth of hell. He asks them, \"Why were you there?\" They replied, \"Because of you...\" He told them he was sorry, and he knew what to do.\n\nHe jumped back in He saved his friends from hell, and sacrificed himself. He heard the voice of God calling him up while he was grinding in the teeth of Hell. God said, \"You have sacrificed yourself for others. You are redeemed.\"\n\nHe was saved from the Nether and brought up to heaven. There he saw the people he just rescued, and many more. The Cathaldus family, the Harronis family, and many more. He was at peace with God and Aegis.\nThe End", ""),
        StaticBook("Tree", "§bThickfeet", "A Tree\n            Grows in\n      Willow Hollow\n\n\n  The Journey of the first\n        Herb Druid\n\nAbove Thickfeet's Rest, a rather run down burrow, was a tree.\nNot much of a tree as it was dry, thin, and leafless. It was a sad tree. It seemed to be dead and any passerby would expect it to fall over at any moment. It was a sad sight.\n  \n         ----<@\n\nChoking against the endless spreading of fungi, the tree was truly dying.\n\nThat was until the studying druid living below took notice...\n\n\n          ----<&\n\nWinter was settling in and snow had already begun to drift into the town. For weeks he spent his time clearing the area around its roots, finding a delicious but infectious collection of mushrooms  eating away at the very trunk.\n\n\n          ----<@\n\n\n\nOnce the mushrooms had been cooked and eaten, he then tended to the dried and brittle dead branches that hung pathetically from the aged limbs.\n\n\n         ----<\$\n\nBy mid winter in the deadest of cold it had appeared to be completely cleaned. Although missing the lush green leaves usually associated with life, it still seemed quite hopeful that the abandoned woodland friend would return.\n\n\n          ----<&\n\nSnow was already beginning to melt and the sounds of life had returned to the world. The end of winter was coming and already the droopy tree had regained much of its strength.\n\n\n\n           ----<*\n\nThe canopy had already began anew as the quit of green was starting to spread. \n\n\n\nLife had returned.\n\n\n           ---<@\n\nIt wasn't long before other green kin joined and soon vines and mosses were fastening themselves to this new bringer of shade.\n\n\n\n           ----<*\n\nIn full force of spring the world was awake and vibrant and this tree was no different.  Amongst the base was now a small haven for mediation and sitting. \n\n        \n\n           ----<@\n\nBefore long a small statue of Cerwidan sat at the base and looked quite at home among the revived new friend of Willow Hollow.\n\n\n\n          ---<&", ""),
        StaticBook("Forest Dwarves", "§bTailesin", "-------------------The True History of the Forest Dwarves\n-------------------\n\nBy Thudrin Treebeard, Druid of Oak\n\nIn the early years of Aegis, a single son of Urguan dared to venture into the forests outside the mountain, calling the trees instead of caves his true home. Gloin, as his father named him, took a stand against his brothers and his kingdom to be free.\n\nOnce his brothers learned of his ways, he and his followers were exiled, left for dead outside the cold mountain gates. They were given but two options - sit in the freezing cold and starve, or venture forth and make themselves a kingdom of their own.\n\nThe Treebeards, Gloin called his loyal followers. They took to the deepest forests of Aegis to live in the true serenity of nature. Hunting, farming, woodcutting and woodworking became prized skills of the Treebeards, and with these skills, they thrived.\n\nBut then the transformation begin to take place.\n\nThese dwarves were bestowed a gift from nature itself, the final blow to truly separate the Treebeards from their mountain-dwelling kin.\n\nTheir skin tanned to an earthly brown, their beards dwindled, extending no further than their mere chins. Their hair grew long and fine, their bodies became slim and agile.\n\nThis mixed clan of cave and mountain dwarves was to be no more. They were united once and for all under their new appearance. Gloin then declared his people dwarves of the forest, bound by honor and companionship.\n\nIn their true home, the forest dwarves lived happily, never looking back at the lives they once lived inside the mountain", ""),
        StaticBook("Ashes of Doom", "§bRaine", "When dreams of soot and ash invade,\nHow is one's doom delayed?\nCan you dodge arrows forever?\nCan you block blades without end?\nCan you swim 'cross any river?\nCan you wade out of the quicksand?\nEventually, you will die,\n\nYou may be buried in tomb on high.\nBut know this, and don't despair,\nThat the ash and soot will surely be there,\nAnd when you descend to the hell you deserve,\nSo will you be worn and frayed of nerve,\nAnd eventually, your soul dies, too,\n\nAnd you'll fade from existence;\nNo one will remember you.", ""),
        StaticBook("Blood of Many", "bluefire514", "The Blood of Many: \nThe Heart of One\n\nWith great might and ideals men have raised themselves to fight. Throughout the sands of time we have seen gigantic armies fall and tyrant kings still stand. And yet, where do these ideals, these admittedly impressive albeit\n\nunsuccessful ventures lead? Death and destruction. Decay of rebelions, and the downfall of freedom. \nAnd yet where many have failed, it takes but one to succeed. Need one be reminded of any of the ancient kings and rules who have failed, it takes\n\nbut one to succeed. Come to it, shall we say and abrupt end?\nIndeed, assassins shape the tides of history more than we know. And here you, reading, seeking, learning. What is it you wish to know I wonder? ::It is true that we have our governments, it\n\nis true they are what hold us together \n_ And yet it is also true that we have the power to sway the tide! Move battlefields to our advantage! And even, end the rule of the horrendous! The power in the minority is greater than we think. And it\n\nis why today we live in a world DEFINED by the assassinations of yesterday! And were these the greatest historic decisions? Perhaps not. But while some where not the best, others changed history for the better, bringing Aegis to the place it is today\n\nor was yesterday rather. Grand armies once again amass in a hopless effort to combat the Undead forces. It makes one question where the world will be left, with such meek enemies yet so strong. It is high time we accept our history's motive and ally\n\nourselves with Iblees\nto bring salvation to our world. This is where out powers are tested. We must remain resolute in the times\nto come if we are to become united. Otherwise we might as well let our blood splatter against the stone.\n\nRewritten by\nLeyu'Maehr\n\nCopied in Wondrous Writtings, Vear'Haven by Shen'in \n\n*Strange Numbers\n(5/25/2013)", ""),
        StaticBook("Gi'Garun Journal", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Journal and Notes of Gi'Garun Irangornson\n\nrecorded by: Gi'Garun\nSon of Iragorn, Son of Araya.\n\nKarin'ayla! I see that you have discovered and decided\nto read my book. I hope you find it of interest.\n\nMay the stars watch over you, and the Aspects guide\nand protect you. Van'ayla.\n\nMay this book be forever kept in the hands of\nthe Druidic Order, never to fade and be bound to the land of Anthos and all the worlds to come.\n\nAn introduction of brief description.\n\nI was brought up in the forests of Malinor by my parents\nIragórn son of Meúh, and Aráya daughter of Lillieth. My father began training me in the art of Archery from a very young age of only 10 years.\n\n My father said that I\nhad an aim that far exceeded his expectations, and that\nhe was proud to call me his son. My mother, would teach\nme how to craft different objects of different shapes and\nsizes using wood. She was strict about the fact\n\nthat\nI should never hurt nature to collect the wood, and\nthat I should only use wood found on the ground. I was\nof course very careful when finding wood, and did as my\nmother taught me.\n\nMy father worked as a farmer and my mother made \npies and various baked items to sell at the market.\n\nBy the time I had reached the age of 6 and 30 years,\nmy skills with a bow had increased dramatically, and I\n\nrarely missed a target. A year passed, and in that time,\nmy dearest mother and father passed into the void, long\nbefore their time. It was later discovered that an\nunknown illness had been given to them, by an assassin from the North.\n\nThe reason for doing so, is still to be\ndetermined, and I shan't rest until I find out why.\nWhether I accomplish this task, is up to fate, and I now\ntravel the land of Anthos.\n\nEntry #1\nI have been on a journey of great adventure! I had\npassed the Cloud Temple and was heading through the\ngate when I met an elf by the name of Indriil, a Dedicant of the Druids of Malinor. He was headed to Abresi, the capitol of Oren,\n\nand I was to go with him it seemed.\n\nWe had not walked more than a few steps before we\nencountered another elf, by the name of Rosemarie. I\nshalt never forget the name. Rose, as we called her, was also headed to Abresi, so she joined us.\n\nAlong the Anthos Highway, we encountered many\nstrange people, including a human wearing nothing but\na pair of tattered underwear. We travelled the highway\nuntil we finally arrived at a bridge. We crossed the bridge and walked along the bank\n\nwhere we came\nacross an Orc. He went by the name of Orlik, and was a\ngroundsman of the nearby town. we asked him if he\nknew the way to Abresi, and he told us that he did.\n\nOrlik let us stay the night in a tavern he owned, and the next\n\nmorning we set off once more. Orlik and Lorik, a\nhuman that worked in the tavern, led us to a narrow\nchasm which led straight to the road that led to Abresi.\n\nWe thanked Orlik and Lorik and later arrived at the city.\n\nA peculier elf with a strange name, offered to buy us\ndinner from the local inn. We were most grateful. I then\nbooked Rose, Indriil and I 3 rooms from the innkeeper\nwhom went by the name Tuv.\n\nWe stayed in the inn for a total of 4 days, and in that\ntime, I had an unfortunate incident with a fireplace. I\nwas walking towards the inn's stairs, when I stumbled\nfor an unknown reason and fell onto the fireplace. The pain, as you can\n\nquite imagine, was unbearable. I\nsprinted out of the inn and dived into the fountain\noutside. A few minutes later I passed out in front of the\ninn. I spent 2 days in bed, in which time, Rose had found\nherself a job.\n\nIndriil and I had been discussing that we would return\nto Malinor, as I wished to become a Druid. On the fourth\nday, Indriil and I left Abresi, and left a message for Rose,\ntelling her where we were headed. We travelled long\nand far, and\n\nfinally reached Malinor. We made our way\nthrough the beautiful forest of Luminaire, and \napproached the Druids Grove. \n\nIndriil led me up to where the Dedicants are homed. We\nspoke with a few of the other\n\nDedicants, and then the\nHead Teacher returned. Indriil asked the Head Teacher\nabout whether I could become a Dedicant, and she\nreplied that they were currently full. I tried to persuade\nher, which was somewhat of a foolish move on my\n\naccount, but she replied with the same answer each\ntime.\n\nDismayed that I were unable to become a Druid, I set\nout to leave the Grove. I wandered the paths of\nMalinor, and was approaching the main gate when all\n\nof a sudden, I spotted a young female elf, being\nsupported by a tall hooded figure. I greeted them and\nasked if they needed help. The tall figure explained\nthat the young elf had fallen afar and had badly\nbroken her leg.\n\nHe said that he was looking for a healer.\n\nI knew that the Druids were skilled in the art of Healing,\nand offered to show them the way. The pair were very\ngrateful, and I led them to the Grove. We arrived in the Dedicant housing, and I\n\nshouted for help. A Druid by the\nname of Frosty, helped to fix the young elf's leg, and I\nlater discovered that her name was Leyu.\n\nWe had to lock her in a vacant Dedicant Room as she was persistant in getting straight back out into the land.\n\nWe had to make her sleep twice, which she did not like\nin the slightest.\n\nAfter a few days, Leyu left the grove, and I stayed a \ncouple of nights in the sanctuary of the Druids. The next\nday, I awoke and Indriil and I were to head to the\n\nOrcish deserts, for reasons I will not reveal for\nit is private. We reached the desert after days of\ntravelling, and stayed for a mere moment, before\nreturning to Malinor (to my utmost surprise and\nannoyance).\n\nWe arrived back in the forest, and Indriil returned to\nthe Grove. I on the other hand remained near the\ngate as I had nowhere to go, nor a house. I spent\nthat night on the side of the road, sleeping under\nan overhanging branch of a tree.\n\nThe next morning, I awoke and found the face of the\nhooded stranger staring at me, the same stranger that\nhad been helping Leyu. His name turned out to be\nMiles Lionheart, another name I shall never forget. A few moments later, I heard\n\n a commotion over near the main\ngate. Miles and I went over to investigate.\n\nWhat we saw still haunts me to this day. The young elf,\nLeyu, lay sprawled on the ground, covered in a vast\narray of different wounds, all designed to torture\n\nthe\nvictim to a slow and painful death. After a brief\nconversation, we agreed Leyu's body should be taken\nback to the Druids. Miles picked up the lifeless body\nand we made our way to the grove. After a quick discussion\n\nwith Indriil and another Dedicant, it was\ndecided that we would take the body of Leyu to the\nCloud Temple.\n\nWe travelled for many days, and finally arrived at the\nTemple. We took Leyu down into the depths of the Temple,\n\nand finally came across the pool of Life. After a \nwhile and some strange events, Leyu was brought back\nfrom the dead and we returned once more to Malinor.\n\nEntry #2\nIt has been many a month since my last entry. Not much\nhas happened since. I managed to locate myself a\nhouse within Malinor, and finally a place to call home.\nIndriil seems to highly dislike me, for reasons I do not\nknow.\n\nEntry #3\nToday many strange things occurred. I went to the\nDruids Grove and I entered to find Iler,\nanother Dedicant, and Frosty. \nNot a moment had I entered the room, the air around me froze. What happened next I cannot explain in words.\n\nBut I awoke on the floor, my\nhead throbbing with icy pain. It seemed that\nthis...apparition had affected Frosty and Iler also. Arial,\nanother Dedicant entered the room, and not a moment\nhad she put her hand on Frosty's shoulder, the\n\napparition consumed her. It dragged her down a wide\nwinding passage.\n\nNaturally, Frosty, Iler and I followed. The passage led\nto an open cave, and we piled in to see Arial the apparition standing nead an opening in the rock face.\n\nThe apparition threw Arial through the opening, and we\nheard a splash and a scream. Frosty ran to the opening\nand dived in after her. A second splash was heard. I\nran to the edge but realised that the apparition was\nmoving towards Iler, who\n\nappeared to be in a trance. I\nran to him and grabbed his shoulder, pulling him out of\nthe room and up the passage. \n\nWe came out the other side and I sat him down in a\nchair. I then pulled out my sword and headed down\n\nthe passageway, emerging into the room, the apparition\nnowhere to be seen. I rushed to the opening and saw\nFrosty dragging Arial onto the shore of the lake. A\nmoment later I felt a shove on my back and fell into the lake. I emerged from under\n\nthe water and turned to\nsee Iler jump in after me. I carried swimming to the\nshoreline, picking myself up and turning to where Iler\nstood upon a lilypad. He let out a gurgle of some\nsorts, obviously possessed by this apparition. The\n\nDedicant began to mumble in the rough tongue of\nthe Orcs. What he was saying, I could not understand. I then-\n*A large smudge of ink is here, covering the rest of\nthe sentence...*\n\nEntry #4\nToday many a strange thing has happened. A letter\narrived from Ralof, a friend of mine. The note read\nas thus: \"Gi'Garun, I told them you had the Staff. They\nare coming to kill you. The Cardinal is coming to kill you.\n\nDon't go anywhere, stay in Malinor and hide. ~ Ralof\"\n\nOf course this was a shock. I am staying inside my\nhouse, with my sword loose in it's scabbard. I shall\nfinish this entry on this note, if I do not survive the\n\nevents that shall occur, this book shall end here.\n\nEntry #5\nWell it seems that this...guild has ceased hunting me,\nand I am glad to say the least. I have been accepted\ninto the Druidic order, and have been given a task by\n\nthe Arch Druid. I am to write a poem on the Aspects.\nStrange things have been happening in Malinor. \nHeads have been delivered to the High Prince, along\nwith demands for an elf each week, otherwise a head\n\nshall be delivered each day. I am not frightened, but a\nsense of uneasiness fills my heart...", ""),
        StaticBook("Stones Journal", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Journal of the Scared Stones\n\n*A tome written by the Song Druid*\n\nOur most sacred order of Drui stretches back farther than any guild that has ever been. We have maintained stability despite those around us crumbling.\n\nHowever we are not without our own losses. Since the fall of Aegis centuries ago we lost something that leaves a hole in our order. The Sacred Stones of the Aspects. The most coveted artifacts that have ever been, and ever will be.\n\nWhat were the Aspect stones? Three stones, mounted within the Mother Grove, one a shining blue, another a glinting silver. Between the two sat a dull black-together they make the Sacred Stones. Each stone represents an Aspect of Nature. The left one,\n\nthe blue one, represents Cerridwen, our great Mother. The left, silver, speaks of Cernunnos, the mighty Hunter. Bound by the neutrality of the black stone they come together to form the Three stones of the Aspects.\n\nAlas, my memory of the Aegis stones wanes with each day, but I do remember their size. Rounded square in definition, each stone resembled the size of an Orc's head, and just as heavy! When all three were together nobody dared steal them.\n\nThe three of them seemed to attract one another with some form of natural magnetism....yes it was truly remarkable. You'd have to move the three at that point, to steal them! Heavy indeed!\n\nStill, I digress. What became of these stones? As Iblees began his final vengeful strike against Aegis the Druids fled alongside the other citizens to the Verge. The Fox Druid Valas did lead the transport of the stones through the portal-\n\nbut something occurred when the chest passed through the portal; the stones cracked and broke up into dust! This being at the end of my own Dedicancy, I took special interest. As I stepped into the title of \"Soul Druid\" on the shores of the Verge\n\nI examined the stones remnants.\n\nThe Aspects have often given me whispers, indeed it was one of the reasons I rose so quickly, they spoke to me like they would no other, but yet where others would receice visions from the Aspects I did\n\nso less frequently. But one such vision does exist within the recesses of my mind to this very day....I saw the stones, but they were different....newer. Remade, for this new world. Little did I know that the world was much larger than just Asulon. It\n\nbecame my mission to find the stones and restore the Order to its full power.\n\nIt was in Asulon that I discovered the first stone. I found it within the city of Menocress, home to the Mori'Quessir. But what use, I rationalized, did one stone that\n\ndidn't represent a known Aspect have? I secured the stone and continued my search. But it wasn't for many decades that I would find the second stone, the stone of Cerridwen. With the help of the Arum Druid I was able to secure it, but upon contact with\n\nthe stone we somehow triggered the Third Great Dream of the Druids. It was a time of rejoicing. One more stone left! Where could it be....?\n\nAnd now I watch these troves of ships gather and clouded whispers from the Aspects become clear to me. The final stone is somewhere beyond this horizon, that much I am sure. Shall I find it before I finally join my brethren? Still. It is clear now what\n\nmy true calling as a Druid is and has always been-to reunite the Aspect Stones and finally restore our Order to it's mighty power.", ""),
        StaticBook("Misunderstanding", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Fear and Misunderstanding\n\n*Unknown, scribed from old texts*\n\nI was taken from my home.\n\nIt was the middle of a perfectly normal day in Asulon, there was a light rain in the early\n\nhours that left the air clean and fresh, and the birds were singing happily outside my Inn. I ran an Inn about a day's horse ride from Skravia. My husband had build a large Fortress there and I built my Inn within the upper courtyard next to the stables,\n\noverhanging the cliffs above our farmlands. It was a popular stopover for travelers from Skravia heading out into the wilds to hunt, gather lumber, fish, or simply travel. Some, including a few of my fellow Druids, and members of Skravia's royal family,\n\nenjoying coming to my Inn for leisure, to get away and relax, so we were accustomed to having people stop by frequently. \n\nOn this day, the Inn was empty, which was unusual, but I was taking the opportunity to sweep and clean the inn, and as I began\n\nwiping down the bar, a man came in holding a piece of paper and wearing a friendly smile. He came over and sat on a stool at the bar, ordering a mug of ale and placing a gold coin on the counter. I gave him his ale and we began talking.\n\nHe asked about the Inn, if there was anyone else there, then if I knew a Druid named Lillith Whisperblade, me, and I was beginning to grow wary from the rigid he was speaking, so I said she was not around. But he looked back at his paper, then slid it\n\nover to me, it had my name, a physical description, and the word DRUID written on it, and he told me there would be 'no more games' and that I was coming with him.\n\nI backed away but I was trapped behind the counter, and he stood and came around,\n\npulling out his blade. As I spoke to try and talk reason with him, he backhanded me, telling me to be silent and come with him, or else. I refused, and he slashed at me suddenly, cutting off half my left ear. I jumped over the counter and reached the door\n\n as one of the guards from Skravia came in for his regular drink.\n\nHe saw my ear and tried to defend me, but my attack yelled after me as I ran, calling me a witch and stabbing my would-be rescuer, leaving him to bleed out as he caught me.\n\nHe knocked me out and I awoke in a cage, built on a raised platform in a room designed like a chapel, pews facing the cage. My attack was next to me, laying out tools of torture on a small table. He introduced himself as Tyret Wolfhank Farfor, and asked\n\nif I knew why I was taken. \n\nI knew the name, my and teacher, Jena, had been tortured by the same man before escaping him. The discussion was calling me a heretic and a witch because I wielded 'magic' as he called it, and would not listen when I\n\ntried to explain the difference between nature's gift and void magics. He and his followers all cursed me and any other wielder of any sort of power, even the temple monks, of being the cause of Aegis' destruction, and that they would 'cleanse' us.\n\nThe torture began by slicing and peeling away small portions of skin on my arms and legs, then using a red hot poker to burn the wounds and cauterize them. He took a small pocket blade and had one of his men grab my head and force me to look upwards as\n\nhe pushed the knight through my lower eyelid and behind my eye, seeking to pry it out intact, but his hand slipped and I saw a black line spread through my vision as his blade sliced through my eye, blinding it.\n\nHis hand slipped because of a commotion upstairs, he dropped the knife and left one of his younger followers to watch me while he dealt with it, and I later found out that it was my sister and the King and High King of Skravia with a few of their men.\n\nThe guard at my inn had survived and reported what happened and me being called a witch, so they had little doubt who had taken me. The Wihuns had more men, but apparently Tyret said he had not taken their Druid, and they had never mentioned exactly who\n\nwas taken, so they departed to get more men. \n\nWhile all of this happened without my knowledge, the young man I had been left with had come beside me, telling me that all the pain could be over if I renounced my Druidic path and joined their cause,\n\nthat I was too beautiful to suffer like this, and I should be with him, he would make them stop and protect if I would only turn away from my mistakes. I looked him in the eyes and weld my kindest smile as I told him my husband would come for me,\n\nand long after the Wihuns were made to answer for their crimes, I would live happily with my husband and our children as a Druid.\n\nThe young man frowned deeply at my response and walked over to the brazier, taking the poker right from the heart\n\nof the coals, the tip white hot. He came back and held it in front of my face, I could feel the heat and I was terrified, but not nearly expecting what came next.\n\nHe lowered it to my waist, telling me \"No, there will be no children for you,\n\nwitch.\" Then stabbing me just above the thigh, pressing it through my womb and moving it in circles, I screamed.\n\nI could feel the fire burning me from inside, and he poured a healing potion down my mouth and held his hand over it, making me choke it\n\ndown through my screams to keep it from killing me, holding the hot iron within me for what seemed an eternity of pain, but must have been only a minute, then removed it right as the healing potion's effects wore off.\n\nI screamed and cried as Tyret returned down the stairs, asking what was wrong, the young man saying he had simply continued. They continued on as before, my body going numb on me, my mind crying out for my husband.\n\nAfter another few hours of physical and mental torture, the base shook from a violent explosion, and men screaming the front wall had been breached. They closed their inner gate and grabbed their weapons as a second explosion shook us, much closer,\n\nsending rubble down the stairs, and I heard my husband shouting commands to his men as they rushed down the stairs. The fight held at the final gate for many minutes, but my husband and his men broke through and slew many, though Tyret escaped out a\n\ntunnel with many others. I was released into my husband's arms, only managing a weak smile before finally succumbing to my agony, passing out.\n\nThe rest is history, I awoke at home, a temple monk beside my bed trying to mend me, but he could not,\n\ntheir powers at the time were greatly weakened, and he could not save my womb. I believe the Wihuns could have been a force of good, but they allowed fear, prejudice, and misunderstanding to rule their actions and became a force of great evil in the\n\nworld, harming many innocent and few of the 'dark mages' they claimed to target.\n\nI did recently however meet the son of Tyret, and learned the group had disbanded upon his father's death, and he did not agree with the targeting of Druids, among\n\nothers. I have lived with the injuries and mental torment of that dark, horrible day, for over a century, never able to bear another child, but blessed with my only son before that terrible day. I devoted myself to the Druids, turning my past into my\n\nreason, my drive.\n\nI want to bring love and peace and balance back to the world, and let others see that any pain can be endured, and still work towards a better tomorrow for everyone. Prejudice only divides and weakens us against the darkness that\n\nfollows us from land to land, and now, more than ever, we stand prepared to fall for the last time if anything threatens our world with peril once more.\n\nDo no let anger and pain from the past ruin our chances to make this a better world for our\n\nour children, our grandchildren. We must unite and set out differences aside so we may stand against the darkness and prevail, lest Iblees and the Betrayer prevail over the descendants.", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidism", "§bLeyu", "Druidism\n\nIs this what the druid college is like...? Being idnapped and forced to write books? Er, kidnapped. Not idnapped. They think I'm a druid for some reason... It's as if they're brainwashed.\n\nThey think I'm an elf, too! I'm a damn dwarf! They trapped Bell in here, too. She's also forced to write. He just said to write..anything. I guess this will be okay to write, maybe...? I'm just jotting down notes... And my thoughts... And why is htis\n\nhappening!? So, what do write now. I don't even feel like making paragraphs. Hmm. Well, since I last wrote in a journal, Many things have happened. For one, I've been indulged in a quest to find an ancient artifact... It's a magical artifact from\n\nWinterfall that the undead want. Now it's in the hands of one, named Hwaldar, who hid it. I rea some clues. It's from the Kar'Lani tribe, and is hidden in the \"fake father of Al'Khazaar\". It's cold there, too. I find the whole quest odd, found by books\n\nwritten by Hwaldar, who is actually an undead himself, yet HE hid the artifact. I've been living in Al'Khazaar and Laurelin latly. I left Al'Khazaar because the rulers were stupid and no one even realized it, causing a bad monarchy. I went\n\nto Laurelin, where it turns out a friend did too. Nothing much happened for a while. But then, a few days ago, I found some lvers near a pumpkin and was playing around with them, when a druid invited me in his house to give me a book. I accepted, of\n\ncourse. I like books. But when I came in, the druid blocked off the door, and gave me enough books to fill my backpack. He commanded me to write and hit me a few times. My friend I mentioned earlier, Bell, was there. The druid was trying to brainwash us\n\nor something. He kept on saying we were elves and druids, which neither of us were. Bell's human and I'm a dwarf. And I don't think either of us would want to study druidism after this.... Anyways. Thankfully, a guard found it. Er, not it. us. He tried\n\nto break us out, when the druid teleported Bell and I to the treetops on top of his house . We found a way back down, and I hit him a few times. He then ran away. So pretty much, I think I'll go back to Al'Khazaar... but I'll write about the next\n\nadventure . I went to go to Al'Khazaar with Bell after being kidnapped. On the road, we met two men who pleaded for Bell's help to heal a man named Ademas. We went to follow, but Bell dissapeared. I still don't know where she is. We continued on\n\nthe path, along with an orc shaman who tagged along. Once we got there, the shaman accidentally activated the demon inside him while trying to destroy it. The demon went mad. I wrote about it in a different book. He eventually jumped off a cliff and\n\ndied. A while later, the Oren carnival opened. It was fun at first. I won 100 minas at an archery tournament, and watched others fight in the Fight Pits . The Wizard gave me a fortune. He said I would be either a great protector of good or an evil\n\nbandit. It's obviously a load of crap. But then, something actually happened at the carnival. Undead attacked. A man revealed himself to be undead and lit the place on fire. The king of Al'Khazaar didn't even notice. He, along with the rest of human\n\nroyalty, is just STUPID. There's no other way to put it. Then everyone just continued playing games, even after a few men died. They didn't even CARE. The people of this world are fools. Maybe the undead do have a point...no. Untrue. Just untrue.\n\nAnyways, Brum, the first undead I ever met came. veryone thought he was a pale orc, even though he's admitted to be undead many times . The fools... the idiots, the fools... I went to watch one fight. A man that looked very similar to an earlier undead\n\ncame. He even used the same teqnique; He set the ground on fire wherever he walked to kill the opponets. The fools.. no one noticed, even when I said it. I am surrounded by oblivious morons. No one understands what is going on... I would write\n\nmore about my life, but I realize I just don't know who will get this book. I might die and good looted. I mean, get looted. Anyways, I can't let people know of my feelings or all of my thoughts. That's pretty much all of my life so far that I care to\n\nshare.\n\nOriginally written in Aegis by an author Unknown.\nRe-scribed in Vailor by Leyu'Maehr from memory.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sequoia's Tome", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Sequoia's Tome\n*By Sequoia Druid Lilith Winterleaf*\n\nI write this for whatever form my beloved Druidic Order has taken in the long...long years since my death was reported to you by my sister.\n\nI am, or rather was, the Sequoia Druid, Lillith Winterleaf. I was guided down the druidic path by Jena, and the day of my attunement is something I will never forget, for save for the birth of my only child it was the happiest day of my life. Nature's\n\nsymphony filled my mind and I was never the same again, I was happier, better, and that joy never left me while I was one of you. I was kidnapped by the Wihuns, whom your younger members I hope have no recollection of, they never left Asulon,\n\nmy dear husband and others saw to that after they kidnapped me, tortured me, cut out my left eye, cut my left ear in half, and perhaps the only thing they did that actually truly scarred me in any way I cared, made me infertile by means I will not repeat\n\neven now in reflection, that wound still pains me deeply, now that my only son is long lost and I have found no trace of him in decades now and I fear the worst, but retain my hope.\n\nThat is not the purpose of this writing...the purpose of this writing is to offer reflection on what I experienced in my time as both a Druid and a Guide of the order long ago. I never was able to compile my wisdom and experience for the Druidic\n\n Library, I was murdered before I finished them, and the incomplete tome was lost.\n\nYes, I was murdered, by one of the few remaining Wihuns at that, who blamed me and my husband for the fall of their order.\n\nI lay dieing in the arms of my sister, Queen of Skravia, Vailoen, and her husband and...my secret lover, whom I shall not name here, she took her life in grief of my death, I learned much later. She was a Dedicant of our order, who I came to care for\n\ndeeply, and I led her to her grave, I wholly blame myself for this.\n\nI awoke what felt like only moments after I'd felt my heart cease to beat in my chest, to a sinister laughter I had not heard in so very long.\n\nIt mocked me and haunted me since I'd first touched that cursed amulet, with a malevolent spirit bound within, the bane of the Johnson family, killer of their royal bloodline for generations. A mere pendant, with a terrible curse, that I'd helped unbind\n\nfrom the Johnson bloodline and kept with me for safekeeping...and when I was stabbed my blood soaked upon it and it bound to MY family instead and held my very soul and preserved my body within the tomb my family burried me within.\n\nUpon awaking, it severed my attunement to break me, and show me it had its grip completely upon me, and I despaired. My sister felt something was wrong, dreamt of me screaming in my grave, which I was, and they dug me up to their complete bewilderment.\n\nThe rest is a long, sad tale that does not need to be told once more. Suffice it to say that the pendant was eventually destroyed, the evil spirit within finally banished and the gemstone destroyed. What have I learned from all of this?\n\nLife is sacred, always.\n\nLife is temporary, always.\n\nDo with your life everything you can, live without regrets, never put off until tomorrow what you can accomplish today, for you do not know what tomorrow may bring.\n\nNature is always reaching out to us, after all of these years, though my attunement is shattered, I still feel the slightest pull of places of nature's truest beauty. In every land we've fled through, I have found places of natural beauty and peace,\n\ncompletely by instinct alone, and I believe this is the Aspects trying to comfort me still, even though I can no longer hear them. I believe the Aspects love all of their children within the Order, and will never abandon us, even if we no longer can hear\n\nthem.\n\nMy experiences as a Druid, a Guide, and now a broken woman faithfully staying at her husband's side even through darkness has shown me only this, the Aspects love us, all of us, Druids and non Druids alike. Their love knows no bounds,\n\nand they seek only to show us the way.\n\nI performed healing I never thought I could when I would listen deeply to the songs and melodies in my mind, the harmonies of nature itself, and I believe the voice of the Aspects themselves.\n\nI watched the wounds close, even when I could not heal my most grievous wound and could bear no children, my healing seemed to be even stronger, my devotion easing my pain by helping others.\n\nThis is the wisdom I wish to pass down, the legacy I want to leave for you all. The Sequoia Druid is no more, I am only Lillith now, but even still I do what I believe the Aspects wanted of me, and I still praise them. Always love, for the Aspects\n\nlove all. No one is beyond saving, beyond healing, beyond redemption. Anyone can be healed, both body and mind. Please, if you take nothing else from this, take the love of the Aspects into your heart. I await now only the day when I finally lay to rest\n\nand can hear the song of the Aspects in my thoughts once more. I yearn for nothing more than that, and until then I will take the lessons they imparted upon me, and the lessons my dearest Jena taught me and continue on, guiding those who will listen to\n\nreason and love and compassion.\n\nI love you all and my heart is always with the Order.\n\n~The former Sequoia Druid\n-Lillith Winterleaf\n\n((A picture of a large sequoia tree is drawn upon this page, highly decorative with black and green inks, and standing at the base is a figure in a green and yellow dress with blonde hair, kneeling over a broken staff))", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid of Clouds", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Druid of the Clouds\n\n*Unknown, scribed from old texts*\n\nBroll stewed up a rumbling grunt as he meditated on the perfect set of words, but of no avail did they come. The tempo of the forest sucked in his frame more than the book, which had\n\nmany chants and tunes; to name them all would be like reproducing the alphabet many times. He was sentimental and mushy character, currently residing by the tree he bonded with. Broll ingrained letters sprucely into the tome that lays\n\nuntroubled in his lap.\n\n\"I remember the day like the back of my pal. I say that in the utmost honesty. It was the day when Druid of the Song and I collided worlds, met each other, and forged a band as teacher and student. Druid of Song is, and was, a\n\ngood teacher-- like a father.\n\nThe two of us bonded that day, a sign of the Aspects I bet, but it wasn't about powers or seeping into the knowledge of the Aspects... I mean, don't get me wrong, I'm the next guy who wanted it to play a part in my\n\nlife, though I didn't let it consume me like a flame. Instead of worrying about all of that, I looked for quality time, going on quest and adventure.\"\n\nHe etched the quilt a more curvy and harsher into the paper, tears soaring through the air below\n\nhis eyes.\n\n\"The true virtue I've received is a mentor and times to reflect on as the good old days. For that I will always be thankful to the Druid of the Song. He taught me many new sensations, like a fairy ring. This is something that you should\n\nexperience yourself, for sure.\"\n\nA whole new world around me I've never seen before! Still, I know my true vision lies within serving the Aspects. All I can do is serve them and the realm, perserve Order, and heal the shards\n\nbroken by the Voidslingers for the Aspects.", ""),
        StaticBook("Frum'Staaz", "§bThurak'Yar", "[!]               [!] \n \nIt seems to be a small, wrinkled scrap of paper, torn from what must have been a great tome. The writing is faded, though easily made out against the yellow parchment.\n\n\n\n [!]               [!]\n\n“… all forms one could imagine. They gathered about the great Traf accompanied by their Shamans as Frum’staaz; they obeyed, but only in companionship, for these beasts were never to be controlled.\n\nBy one Uruk’s side only a flat, grey shadow could be seen, trailing from his feet and blinking back at those that glared…”", ""),
        StaticBook("Plant People", "§bOo'ri Earthbreaker", "Plant People\n-----------------------Plant people were once people, infected by some of parasite. Not much is known about said parasite, but puts the person infected through a process, irreversable at the moment, and turns their whole body into\n\nplant matter and plant life. \n\nOne said person was asked about how they became a plant person, and was relatively unknown that the change occured. Apparently the person fell asleep and then awoke as a plant person.\n\nAs a druid in training, I had to ask if they felt nature the same way that druids do after attunement and they answered that they did not. They did mention feeling an 'odd' feeling when in or around a fairy ring. Not sure why, need to ask more.\n\nAs stated early in this book, a parasite was labeled to be the cause of such transformation. A druid from Anthera looked at a plant person and found such a thing within them. They also stated that it was 'learning' emotions and feelings.This is unnerving.\n\nThe process, as stated before, changes the persons body and thus changes what they body can do. They can regrow limbs at a rate, and only eats vegitables and bread, no meats. \n\nOne odd trait is that they need to take a walk once a year, no reason why.", ""),
        StaticBook("Salum's Story", "§bTaynuel", "<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n\n    The Story of Salum\n\n        A short story\n    written on the 17th\n   of Amber Cold, 1486\n\n     by Taynuel Faerin\n\n\n<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>~<>\n\n     She was a very young hare when she found herself hopelessly lost, her mother gone from sight, her father long since dead, killed by hunters for his pelt and left for his family to find frozen in the snow. The sight scared her deeply, and she saw she\n\nwas more frightened of the two-legged monsters her mother called 'people' than she had ever been before.\n\n      It was late at night, the moon high above her head, when she finally admitted it to herself. She had no idea where she was or which direction\n\nher home was. The echos of wolfish howls rang through the air, chilling her  to the bone. She had to find shelter, and fast.\n\n     And so, she ran, tearing through the woods as fast as her powerful legs could carry her. Faster than any rabbit, she was\n\nsinuous and strong for her age, her silver fur rippling with each motion of her legs. Chocolate eyes wide with fright, she surged through the bushes, looking for something, anything, anywhere to call shelter until the sun kissed the skies once more and\n\nshe could find her way home.\n\n     It was then that she spotted it - there, off in the distance, was a small hut, crude and fashioned from fallen pine boughs. She didn't have much of a choice, so she darted in to escape the wintry cold and the gnashing of\n\nthe wolves, starved and needing to feast.\n\n      What she saw inside the hut both startled her and sparked her curiosity. Inside was a boy, an elf of deep gray skin, wrapped tightly in blankets and shivering. The only light in the room came from the\n\npitiful embers of the hearth in the center, casting an eerie glow all around.\n\n     She gulped, shivering as the boy stirred from his slumber. He groaned, roused from his sleep likely thanks to the rush of wind that accompanied her entrance. The shrieks\n\nof the blustery currents of air outside reminding them of the impending storm.\n\n     He opened his eyes slowly, revealing them to be the same hoary hue of her pelt. He made a small sound in his throat and turned toward the entrance of the makeshift tent,\n\nblinking at the sight. \"A hare...\" he whispered hoarsely, though she could not understand him.\n\n     No, what she felt was something different. She didn't know what she said, but she could somehow /feel/ it. She knew in some way that he would not, no,\n\ncould not harm her, and so she approached him, oh so cautiously.\n\n     Her triangular nose twitched slightly as she came closer, lowering her ears and stretching her neck out to sniff at him.\n\n     A gentle hand held itself out,\n\nnot to touch her, but to let her sniff and see that he was no threat to her.\n\n     For a fleeting moment, she considered turning and running away, but the scream of the wind and the snarl of the wolves close behind made her think twice. With a nervous\n\ngulp, she approached him, watching in fear as he lifted the blanket...\n\n     ...to reveal he had no weapons of any sort. He was as defenseless as she was out here, and needed shelter just as much as she. A strange feeling in her gut arose, telling her\n\nthat this elf could be trusted, at least for just one night. Nervously, she crawled under the blanket, curling up as close to his belly as she could.\n\n     Shortly thereafter, she felt a light weight on herself. He was hugging her, swaddling her in the\n\nheat she would need to survive the bitter cold.\n\n     \"If I have done nothing in this life, at least I will have saved yours, no matter how small,\" he whispered, closing his eyes with a shiver. Thus they rode out the violent storm, sharing each other's\n\nbody heat and cowering together.\n\n     The following morning, she found that he was not where she last remembered him to be. Instead, he sat across the small room from her, the only thing separating them being a small pile of greenery.\n\n     \"Eat,\" he said. \"You will need your strength in this cold.\" She did not quite understand what he said, but she knew what he was trying to convey by the gesture he made with his hand.\n\n     So began their friendship. The elf came to call her 'Salum',\n\nthe Elvish word for 'to know, or to sense,' an homage to her intuition.\n\n     Bound by an invisible bond, one that neither could see or touch, but both could feel.", ""),
        StaticBook("Acerbus' Journal", "§bKoraz", "Amber's Cold, 1476\n\nI've begun this journal as a way to record the tales of the strange events happening with the beginning of the Wars with Iblees. Already the Dwarves have opened a gateway to release the dreaded Daemon, and already many skirmishes have\n\narised, but none so great as this day. Today the Undead legions have launched their first true assault upon the Descendants by assaulting the Dwarven city of Kal'Agnar. I and my brothers have already stationed inside the walls despite our bad relations\n\nwith the Dwarves. All aid is accepted in this siege, for all have the same purpose- to bring death to Iblees and his minions.\n\nThe Seers claim there to be a devastating end to the Dwarves to come, yet under their direction we arrived to aid in the\n\ncombat eitherway. Kaegaz'arai claims it to be worth it for the defeat of as many Undead as could be slain. He and I have been hunting the Daemon's creations since their beginning. We've become quite too used to the smell of miasma by now, but it was our\n\nduty.\n\nFew of the dreaded Aengul's chosen ones have shown themselves, and if they have they would not stay long. Clerics, Paladins, Ascended. None do well in battles. Not that it matters- More Holy taint will only add to the fire.\n\nSnow's Maiden, 1476.\n\nThe Seers were correct. Despit all attempts at defense, the Dwarves have lost their Kingdom to the Undead General. My brothers and I escaped along with several others through the mountains after the brunt of our forces were\n\ndefeated. This merely meant the Undead were closer, and easier to reach.\n\nThe First Seed, 1479.\n\nThe Dwarves have reclaimed their home in a non-satisfactory battle. The kindred of Iblees have begun to disappear as they lose more and more skirmishes. This means our attention may turn to the Ascended and their elks. A few clerics\n\nhave begun to sprout up around the region. Kaegaz'aria and I have located one amongst the Dark Elves. \n\nThe cleric was alone when we arrived, but as blades were drawn his fellow Dark Elves began to appear one by one. Soon we were outnumbered\n\nseveral to just two of us. We were separated and fighting in a mere defensive operation, until finally a scream arose from those not near me.\n\nKaegaz's master had arrived. He had no true form to show, merely sound and power. He aided us in bringing\n\nthe Dark Elves to their knees until the cleric was butchered. Never had I seen such power, and I believe I never will again.\n\n1513, Date Unknown.\n\nThe remaining years of the Iblees War led to his inevitable downfall. Our Order has adapted to change, and now makes for the land known as Vailor.\n\n*Several pages, previously ripped from earlier chapters, are stuffed here. They're water logged and crumpled, unable to be read.\n\nThe Deep Cold, 1516.\n\nOur Guild, formerly known as Acerbus, has formed to become the New Order. Kaegaz'arai still holds reign, and I have risen, along with several new members, to a higher position amongst the ranks. We have grown in numbers with the\n\narrival in Vailor.\n\nThe First Seed, 1528.\n\nOur ranks have grown to never before seen numbers. Kaeg, although going by a different name, has become a Necromancer of great skill and has included the aid of Darker beings for our battles against the 'Holy' beings that still\n\nplague the realm. Our members hold high positions in numerous nations, and control more men than that of the Elves combined. Kaeg speaks of Xionism, though few know what it is.\n\n1540. Date Unknown.\n\nOur peace with the Clerics is beginning to recede as new beliefs arise. Xionism becomes well known by all for its Anti-God belief. Several Clerics attempt to reason with the leaders, but they have begun to hide in the cities of others\n\nI've begun delving into the Dark Arts, though the idea of being crippled stuns me. Kaegaz'aria seeks to search for ways to enable the Dark Arts without crippling our states. The belief comes from Dragon blood.\n\n1547, Deep Cold.\n\nThe Sunless Sanctum has been opened to all believers of Xionism, as a safe haven for dark beings. They say a beast of Old walks there, and we now serve him. Though I've seen not, I simply believe.\n\nThe Deep Cold, 1575.\n\nThere's been little to no action in Axios. Xionism is rarely spoken of, and Kaegaz'aria is rarely seen. I've adapted to the life as a Wood Elf, yet my hope for the Dragon Blood work does not end.\n\nSnow's Maiden, 1576.\n\nI've rarely slept for days, yet I find myself in a state of mind greater than any I have experienced before. My swordsmanship has rosen far pass that of the average Man or Elf. Several times I fell upon groups of bandits only to come\n\nout with their heads piked before the Wood Elf gates, whom, after my entry into their city, have grown more familiar with me. I've joined them in the defense of their lands on numerous occasions. With them I have also grown more acustom with Sutican\n\nlife, though as a guard it can get rather boring. Sutica was merely a stepping stone for me as I rose to my full strength, but provided a way to speak with the clerics that reside there and learn of their ways.\n\nAfter communing with the Clerics and Druids I once fought against. The ideas they hold beneath the layers of disgust are not to the point of disbelief. It is to my knowledge that the only real use of both are to protect the Descendants, which is fine by\n\nitself, yet both still wage war against the scum of Iblees. I find very strange of them to continue their reign in the mortal realm despite Iblees' passing.\n\nDate unknown.\n\nThough I've never been able to meet the fabled man, I've spent numerous hours reading what he's written in his own library, and mark his words. It is a truely interesting to read of a Harbinger, brought to his lowest,\n\nwho would later rise to the position as a peaceful Librarian. If this is indeed the end, I write this to mark the end of a leaf, and the beginning of a new.\n\nI, Koraz, Hand of Kaegaz'arai, past General of Acerbus, and later known Xionist under the false Old Lord, now end my story.", ""),
        StaticBook("Harbtale Vol.I", "§bOrithur", "A Harbinger's Tale\n\nVolume I\n-------------------\n\nBlessed we were, the brothers and sisters of the Order of the Golden Lance. Our spirits were mighty, our friendship like no other. We served proudly under the\n\nAengul, Xan, and it was our divine duty to seek out the dark creatures of the world that threatened the order and sanctity that we lived to protect.\n\nOne such creature, was the Drakaar known as Setherien. A Drakaar is the\n\ncorrupted version of a Dragaar, a greater dragon. These dragons are capable of thought, speech and extreme power... As we were soon to learn.\n\nSetherien had a sister, and the pair of them resided quietly in a Dwarven ruin. When the dwarves of Asulon\n\nreleased the pair, they managed to slay the sister, but Setherien himself, as a fledgling, escaped. Our Order recognised the threat and sought to end it before the corrupted greater dragon could bring further harm to the world.\n\nNine. Nine of the Order were chosen to track down the beast. One of these nine being myself. We hunted the creature across many lands and encountered many struggles along the way, years passed before we finally discovered the hiding place of the Drakaar\n\nin the frozen North of the land soon to be known as 'Anthos'. We waited, observed, and planned carefully, we could not feel more prepared to attack, and so we did...\n\nThe Drakaar slew us all within minutes. I watched the determination fade\n\nfrom the dying faces of my brothers and sisters as the unholy creature laughed over our bloodied bodies. Our long journey seemed over with such a hopeless anticlimax, and if we could have known what was to come, we would all have gladly fallen upon our\n\nown swords before the search for Setherien even began.\n\nFor you see, Setherien did not kill us. He enslaved the Nine of us as his immortal servants. We became Setherien's Harbingers... And soon the mortal races found Anthos, and\n\nSetherien's revenge was to be dealt through us, the people that had sought to protect them.\n\nUpon the corruption of our bodies and minds, we became imbued with a power over dragonfire and frost, as well as the ability to corrupt the\n\nfallen into withering, charred skeletons to act as Setherien's footsoldiers, and we his Dark Leuitenants.\n\nBut we did not see ourselves as slaves. The Harbingers gladly obeyed their master, and all memory of our past lives became but a distant dream,\n\nblurred and unclear. We were taught to ignore it, but I didn't. I became obsessed with these memories that didn't seem to belong to me. I served loyally under Setherien, but the doubt was planted.\n\nEach of us bore the same black and blue robes of a\n\nfreezing aura, with only our differing helms and visors to identify us as individuals.\n\nWe became willing icons of fear, and the more we served under Setherien, the more distant our past selves seemed to become...", ""),
        StaticBook("The Matriarch", "§bThurak'Yar", "The Matriarch\n\nFirst born of the brood of Dragur, a female, molded in his image, and adopting his unquenchable thirst of sheer knowledge. “The Sun will awaken each new day, followed by the rise of the moon, that will light\n\nthe seemingly endless nights, and during these nights it will be the Stars which will guide you. This land in which you were born in will teach you many things. And as you come to learn, you will share the knowledge which you have come to acquire in the\n\nhopes that the ones you have come to share with learn from it. This is my dream.”\nAnd so the Daemon fell into a seemingly eternal slumber. Many centuries have come to pass since that day, and he collection continued to grow.\n\nUnfortunately, some had used bits of knowledge she had shared with them for their own greedy purposes. They didn’t believe in her ways of “Shared knowledge for knowledge”, and wanted it without giving anything in return.\n\nThey found the place in which she kept all her books, scrolls and scrawlings, and began to steal whatever they thought would bring them wealth and power. Upon returning, she felt a sense of violation and mistrust, but knew and understood that\n\ntime itself would heal those feelings. She began to scrawl everything she could remember from what was stolen from her, but they were merely tattered fragments compared to what she once had. Hope had reached her a few years\n\nlater when she found one of her books amongst some ruins and rubble. She began to hope that the others will come to reveal themselves once more.\nThus, the Matriarch wanders the land, in search of the items that were taken from her, but also\n\nstill learning what she can from her travels and from the land. By merely observing and keeping scrawls of what she has come to witness, to finding books, novels and scrolls from past and present civilizations of the land.\n\nThis is her passion… And her only purpose.", ""),
        StaticBook("Eldest Brother 1", "§bTailesin", "The Eldest Brother: A History of Malinor\nVolume I\nThe First Sky\n\nBy Ebs Telrunya, Former Fourth Prince of Malinor\n\nMalinor as we know it began after the Collapse, an obscure event which lead to the collapse of any civilization that may have existed before 1300.\n\nWhen one of the symptoms of the Collapse, the erasing of the collective memory of all who survived the Collapse, wore off,\n\n Hight Prince Native banded together what was left of a wood elven colony, as well as the dark elves and high elves in the region surrounding Laurelin.\n\nHe set about consolidating his position in Laurein, assisting many elves in setting up lives in the capital.\n\nVery few elves are left from this time, almost all have gone missing at some point during Aegis, so accounts of the early days are secondhand at best, many times having been passed from pointed ear to pointed ear until reaching the writer.\n\nBy most accounts, Native picked anarray of Princes to help him govern the growing elven population, some notable ones being Prince Toren the Farmer, Prince Flefal the Engineer, Princess Arianna, and PRince Lafthi the Diplomat.\n\nThe mining paradise of Ravenhold was founded, home to such notable elves as Khel Oussana, Haelphon Adecchio, and future Prince Ebs Telrunya.\n\nAfter the initial years in Laurelin, most of the High Council, including High Prince Native, were barely ever seen in Laurelin, and Malinor suffered.\n\nThe Gauntlet, and obstavle course that offered a large minas reward, was opened rarely, and almost every elf immediately entered the Wardens of the Druidic Order upon entry to Malinor.\n\nThe only functioning part of Malinor's government were indeed the Wardens, the elite defenders of the forests of Malinor. Lead at first by the ruthless Blackthorn, they protected the forests while the High Council squandered away the Princedom.\n\nNatives only notable action during this time was nearly destroying Ravenhold when he caused the mine to collapse.\n\nWhen Kal'Bryst was destroyed during the onset of the Undead invasion, the High Council's reaction was nonexistent. During the great northern wars, in which the humans struggled for survival against the undead invaders,\n\nthe elves hid in the south, with very few participating the defense of Alstion. The Wardens instead focused on removing Wrath from Wrath's Clutch, an Undead citadel in the South that threatened to cut off Alras and Al'Khazar from Laurelin and Kal'Urguan.\n\nCountless wardens and other elves perished in attacks on Wraths Clutch, which was repeatedly assaulted so that prisoners being held there, most notably Princess Indelwehn, could be freed.\n\nNo matter how many times the walls were breached, the forces of the United Aegis Coalition (UAC) could not dislodge Wrath until very late in the war.\n\nIndelwehn became notable for leading the wardens in the defense of Sanjezel, leading to the signing of the Malin-Krug pact, a long-lasting defensive treaty between the Holy Princedom and Krugmar,\n\nwhich was signed by Princess Indelwehn and Wargoth Mogroka'Gorkil.\n\n\nMalinor had taken presence in the Verge, it's official settlement, overseen by Princess Lirinya, lacking utility and security. More enterprising elves and a few humans united under Viscount Ebs,\n\nwho founded the city Fionn, which flourished for a time, until all but the mentally unstable abandoned the Verge during the massive slime and spider invasion.\n\nAt this time, Laurelin abandoned it's old Council Chambers to allow the Mages Guild o have a place to set up away from the unstable north.\n\nFor a time, malinor had a High Princess, Sorious, the wife of Native. After her death, the High Council passed a law stating that being the spouse of a Prince does not inherently make you a Prince.\n\nNative later fell in love with the owner of the Treetop Inn, which flourished in the trees North of Malinor. The owner, Lirinya, quickly rose to prominence in Malinor, gaining the title of Princess quite quickly.\n\nHer down to earth nature and her humbleness disguised that she was Undead. Gaining trust from a besmitten Native, she betrayed Malinor, destroying Ravenhold during the rule of High Princess Indelwehn.\n\nWith the appointments of Princess Indelwehn and Prince Mylas, a new generation of Elven leaders took the helm of the nation. Ebs, a high elf, and Sythra, a dark elf, convinced High Prince Native to disband the corrupted Wardens,\n\nwho were being used by the cruel Prince Quazar as an aggressive force, sparking conflict with the other nations. The Vanguard was formed, with Sythra in command.\n\nWith the appointment of Prince Ebs as High Prince Natives's final act, the High Council, guided by the still mostly absent Princes Toren and Lafthi elected High Princess Indelwehn to the highest position in Malinor.\n\nThey then added then Treelord Haelpon and Sythra to the Council, and removed the Arch Druids from the Council, as in that time, the druid's grove was a pit of filth and crime, all but sanctioned, and most certainly indulged in by Respiren and Dusk.\n\nThis was the Golden Age for the elves, as their international power and influence waxed to the point where it could have been argued, after the fall of Al'Khazar, that Malinor was the stablest and most powerful state in Aegis.\n\nUnknown at the time of her election was the deep tain hidden in the heart of Indelwehn, installed during one of her stays in Wrath's Clutch. Her second husband, Bircalin, and her friends watched in horror as she changed.\n\nIt is still not clear if she was aware she was tainted, however, she broke the Malin-Krug pact, refusing to aid Krugmar in their war against Urguan and Oren, and made a series of disastrous mistakes.\n\nShe attempted to have Prince Ebs assassinated for questioning her, however he barely survived, and after a year recovering in Hanseti, he returned when Indelwehn was charged with the murder of a small child.\n\nThe High Council attempted to cover up her crimes, but realized she had to go, and voted unanimously to remove her, revealing to the public only that she had resigned. In a near tie, Haelpon was elected High Princem with Sythra losing by one vote.\n\nBy this point, Prince Mylas had been killed during an undead attack, and his body had been laid to rest outside the Sister City, a suburb of Laurelin.\n\nAt this time, the High Council met in the Sister City, as it was too easy for Undead spies to listen in on proceedings in Laurelin.\n\nMalinor established good relations with the Kingdom of Renatus after it emerged from the Phoenix Rebellion, but the final days of Aegis were characterized by fear among the High Council.\n\n After the falls of both Al'Khazar and Alras, refugees flocked to Malinor. Prince Ebs set up the Alrasian refugee camp east of Laurelin, but the Alrasian officers and Vanguard struggled to keep order in the once peaceful city of Laurelin.\n\nDuring a magical experiment by the druids and mages, Arkelos, an Undead, destroyed the Gauntlet, spreading miasma around Laurelin. As elves prepared to flee the city, the druids managed to restore the air, however the damage was staggering.\n\nThe High Council, despite the pleading of a few members, refused to build additional fortifications around Laurelin, instead opting to give Deltaro Black, and elf who thought he was much more competent than he actually was,\n\npermission to remodel the front entrance to make it more \"naturey.\" Laurelin did not stand a chance in the final battle, and as the dragons leveled the once proud jewel of Laurelin, the elves fled for the Verge, many of which were openly weeping,\n\nwhere they were met by chaotic conditions, as the elven government has fallen apart. Treelord Alan managed to build an elven ship, but travel conditions were horrid, and many elves were lost in the voyage.\n\nAnd so the elves set out for Asulon, to brave a strange new world full of dangers they could not have possibly conceived as they left Laurelin.", ""),
        StaticBook("Blood of Many", "§bTailesin", "-------------------The Blood of Many, The Heart of One\n-------------------\n\n~Author Unknown\n\nWith great might and ideals men have raised themselves to fight. Throughout the sands of time we have seen gigantic armies fall and tyrant kings still stand.\n\nAnd yet, where do these ideals, these admittedly impressive albeit unsuccessful ventures lead? Death and destruction. Decay and destruction.\n\nDecay of rebellions, and the downfall of freedom. And yet where many have failed, it takes but one to succeed. Need one be reminded of any of the ancient kings and rules who have failed, it takes but one to succeed.\n\nCome to it, shall we say and abrupt end?\n\nIndeed, assassins shape the tides of history more than we know. And here you, reading, seeking, learning. What is it you wish to know I wonder? ::It is true that we have our governments, it is true they are what hold us together.\n\nAnd yet it is also true that we have the power to sway the tide! Move battlefields to our advantage! And even, end the rule of the horrendous! The power in the minority is greater than we think.\n\nAnd it is why today we live in a world DEFINED by the assassinations of yesterday! And were these the greatest historic decisions? Perhaps not.\n\nBut while some were not the best, others changed history for the better, bringing Aegis to the place it is today or was yesterday rather. Grand armies once again amass in a hopeless effort to combat the Undead forces.\n\nIt makes one question where the world will be left, with such meek enemies yet so strong. It is high time we accept our history's motive and ally ourselves with Iblees to bring salvation to our world.\n\nThis is where our powers are tested. We must remain resolute in the times to come if we are to become united. Otherwise we might as well let our blood splatter against the stone.", ""),
        StaticBook("Herbalism vol.01", "§bElder Elf", "~*~*~A GUIDE~*~*~*\n~*~*~*~TO~*~*~*~*\n~*~*HERBALISM*~*~\n~*~*~*vol. 01*~*~*~\n~*~ALOE   PLANTS~*~\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*\n~*~*~*~*A*~*~*~*~*\n~*~*~DRUIDIC~*~*~*\n~*~*~*ORDER*~*~*~\n~*~*~*TOME*~*~*~*~\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*\n\n\n          .-. .-.\n         (_ | _)\n          (_|_)\n             |\n             |\n             \\\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~#########################Written By####\n##The Druid of Song#\n###########################♫#################♫♫♫#########\n\n✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿FOR BELERAN✿✿\n✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿✿\n\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪The aloe plant is an age old plant that is famous for its healing abilities.  It can be prepared a number of ways.  This tome will explain those ways.\nAloe can be ingested,\napplied to the skin, or ground and combined\n\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪with other herbs to create an even more potent healing salve.\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪\n\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪\n1: INGESTION\nIt is very potent to\ndirectly ingest, so cut out the flesh and brew it into a tea.  It can be used to treat...\n✿Constipation\n✿Hard of breathing\n✿Colds\n✿Partial blindness\n♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪\n\n♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ ♪ 2:DIRECT APPLICATION\nThe cut plant can be applied directly to the skin for...\n✿Sunburn\n✿Frostbite\n✿Open Wounds\n✿Burns\n✿Sores\n✿...and flaky skin!\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪\n\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪3: Combination\nThe plant can be combined with other herbs.\n\n✿Goldenrod\n✿Blissfoil\n✿Cactus Green\n✿Kolagotu\n✿And many more!\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪\n\n♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪♪Please be sure to read our other volumes for more information on creating salves and poltices.\n\n♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣THANK♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣YOU♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣FOR♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣SUPPORTING♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣♣THE DRUIDS♣♣♣♣♣♣", ""),
        StaticBook("Fi'hiiran'tanya", "Toodles", "Fi'hiiran'tanya\nOriginally written by\nValia'laure Faeifeth\n\nTranscribed for the Library of Dragur by\nRevion M. Tathron\n\nThe following is a transcript of a book found in the ruins of a house in Celein.\n\nThe Smile of Larihei Heals the Mind Like Milk Heals the Body.\nThe Lifting- Sacrifice\n\nThe Ritual of the Lifting is different from that of Untempered Fi, for it is the stringent conditions which make for the true, Tempered Fi's creation.\n\nThe ritual of the Elibar'acal follows, and yet it is sufficient but not necessary to create a Tempered soul.\n\nThe sacrifice is tied to the Altar, imbued with the magic of Fi. The room is lit only by the light of a Fi' orb in a specially made lamp.\n\nGold dust is scattered upon the sacrifice as the following is read. Each in attendance may bless themselves as they so desire.\n\n<Transcriber's note:\n\nBoth the Elvish and common versions of the Ritual have been removed, to deter those who seek to practice this vile art.>\n\n<Basically, it detailed how the Golden Owl is saving everyone from monsters. It wasn't worth reading, believe me.>\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nMilk is passed between those in attendance in a Silver Chalice. It is drunk by each in turn, symbolising they are worthy of the Milk of Larihei, and therefore her smile.\n\n\n\nYour body unfit for the milk, Your mind unfit for the smile of Larihei, \nYour very essence forfeit.\n\nThe Milk is refused to the sacrifice, symbolising they are unworthy of it. A blade imbued with Fi' energy (read: Fi'chant, just as the altar is)\n\nis used to cut the sacrifice's throat and as this is done droplets of blue will pool on the blade's tip.\n\nBlood is collected in a clay pot. The droplets will spin and form a blue light which will become stable (PK)\n\nor will explode in a flash of light (no PK, soul escapes). It is required an orb be present to power both the altar and the blade.\n\nFi'hiiran'seth\n\nThis subtype of the magic focuses on the negation of Arcane Magic. Whether Evocation, Conjuration, Illusion, Runes, or Alteration, if can either send a forming spell back to the void or eat away at a created spell.\n\nThe magic will also steal the aura needed for Shade magic, but has no affect on existing tendrils.\n\nFi'hiiran'acaele\n\nThe second subtype of the magic can stop all Deity Magic. (For example: Clerical, Druidism, Shamanism, Monk, Muun'Trivazja, and Ascended). Unlike its brother subtype, which funnels mana away into the void,\n\nFi'hiiran'acaele disrupts a connection, interfering with someone's ability to connect with their chosen deity. This can be small, when they cast a spell, or large, severing a connection for a time.\n\nStrangely, neither Fi'hiiran'acaele or Fi'hiiran'seth affect Necromancy.\n\nThe mage must be a first-time magic user, never having connected to anything, Deity, Void, or otherwise.\n\nThe mage must be of good condition. Fi'hiiran'tanya corrupts the body twice as much as a normal magic, and the mage's increased metabolism may wither them away without proper care.\n\nThe mage must perform the Ritual.\n\nThis Ritual, developed by the Elibar'acals, is the source of the mage's power. They much find a sacrifice (a character willing to be permanently killed) that they know well, and kill them on the altar.\n\nIt is in this moment, when the soul is leaving, that the mage will connect to the Void for the first time. The soul will be returning to The Cloud Temple,\n\nand the mage will grab it, trapping it in a state of Limbo. A state of half-death, forever caught in the mage's grasp. The soul will become a tool,\n\nand will eventually forget its old self, becoming more and more compliant. There is a small chance, in the beginning years, that the soul will attempt to wrest control, though no soul has ever gained full control.\n\nPhysical and mental strain\nLoss of weight and muscle mass\nIncredibly realistic lucid dreams and nightmares, leading to a confusion on what is real and what is imagined.\n\nEnchanted areas will leave a Fi'hiiran'tanya user fatigued if they stay in there for too long a time period.\nProblems with breathing, blood loss, internal bleeding, and the rate at which wounds heal.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fred the Panda", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Fred the Panda\n\n*Bardic Rite, Unknown*\n\nOnce upon a time their was a little panda bear named Fred. Fred was a typical little girl panda, that grew up in a grove full of beautiful bamboo,\n\nwith the elder pandas that cared for it. Her entire life, she admired these elders and wanted to grow up to be one of the caretakers of her beautiful home.\n\nHer life was far from easy though. She spent a lot of time roaming the bamboo grove with her twin sister, learning about the lands, plants and herbs. Nights were spent studying and listening to stories from her family. Then one day, she was old enough to\n\nbegin down her path.\n\nThe elder she spoke with told her that since she had never left the safety of the bamboo, she should strike out with her sister to explore the world outside. Fred was very afraid to do this, but trusted the elder’s judgement.\n\nSo she and her sister struck out on their own for the first time.\n\nThe road was a hard one. The two of them had no idea how the outside world was. Days were spent walking and trying to find their way through the odd places that fill the world. The\n\nThe one comfort was the nights, where the familiar feeling of nature surrounded them while camping.\n\nShe took joy in the sound of crickets chirping and whippoorwills singing their songs. The grass and leaves were her blankets, the trees were her bed.\n\nWhen she was hungry, nature provided a variety of food for her. Things were starting to not look as bleak.\n\nThen one day, the two were traveling down a forest path when a group of people tried to grab the two little pandas. Fred’s sister was killed.\n\nThe little panda retreated from everyone, weeping for her loss. She went down a darker road, one filled with hate and rage that was not her own. She was tricked into hurting those she cared about.\n\nFred’s heart hurt for everything that had happened. One night, she lay in the forest and realized that she shouldn’t feel this amount of pain. The loss was great, yes, but her sister had become part of the natural cycle. She was a part of nature now,\n\nand nature was still around her. In every choice she seemed to make, her path led back to nature and the bamboo grove.\n\nNature meant everything to her. Through her childhood and through life, it had always been there for her.\n\nEven when she pushed everyone else away from her, and let pain consume her heart, it was there. Fred returned home.", ""),
        StaticBook("Grasshopper&Ants", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "The Grasshopper\n    and the Ants\n\n-Mother Mouse- ~(,_,@*\n\n*The front page has a sketch of a grasshopper holding a fiddle with a few ants standing around him. A small mouse is in the corner of the page.*\n\n*A sketch of ants walking in the grass, carrying grains of wheat on their backs, the sun shining brightly*\n\nOne bright day in late autumn a family of ants were bustling about in the warm sunshine, drying grain they had stored during summer....\n\n*A grasshopper with a fiddle stands infront of the ants, the grain behind the ants.*\n\nwhen a starving grasshopper, his fiddle under his arm, came up and humbly begged for a bite to eat.\n\n*The Grasshopper holds out his hand in a begging way as the ants seem shocked.*\n\n\"What!\" cried the ants in surprise, \"haven't you stored anything away for winter? What in the world were you doing all summer?\"\n\n*It shows a picture of the grasshopper standing on a treestump, playing his fiddle in the sunshine happily*\n\n\"I didn't have time to store up any food,\" whined the Grasshopper, \"I was so busy making music that before I knew it...\"\n\n*It shows a new picture of the Grasshopper on the same tree stump, the tree leaves now changed to oranges and yellows.*\n\n\"... the summer was gone.\"\n\n*The ants seem to not care, turning away from the Grasshopper.*\n\nThe Ants shrugged their shoulders and turned away from the grasshopper in disgust. \n\"We will not give food to a lazy grasshopper!\"\n\n*The sketch shows the Grasshopper holding up his fiddle, ready to play, the ants looking over curiously.* \n\nThe Grasshopper held up his fiddle and smiled at the Ants, \"If I make music for you while you work, may I have but a bite?\"\n\n*The picture shows the ants looking to eachother while the grasshopper waits, fiddle ready.*\n\nThe Ants turned to one another and whispered for a moment before they nodded in agreement. They went back to work, carrying grain around.\n\n*The picture shows the Grasshopper playing his fiddle as the ants work, smiles on their faces.*\n\nThe Ants worked hard but the Grasshopper worked just as hard, playing his fiddle and tapping his feet to the beat of his music.\n\n*The picture shows the sun setting, the Ants offering some grain to the Grasshopper with smiles on their faces.*\n\nAs the sun began to set, the ants had finished their work. They gave the Grasshopper some grain, thanking him...\n\n\"Thank you, Grasshopper, you made our work more fun with your music.\"\n\nThe Grasshopper smiled to them, \"I'm always pleased to make music, thank you Ants.\"\n\n*The Ants are waving as the Grasshopper leaves towards the sunset.* \n\nThe Ants waved farewell to their new friend, calling to him, \"Come again, Grasshopper!\"\nThe Grasshopper waved back with a smile as he left, \"Farewell, my friends!\"\n\n*The back cover has a silly sketch of a mouse with spectacles on its nose, sitting under a tree with a book open in its lap.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient History3", "§bTailesin", "-------------------Ancient History, \nVolume 3\n-------------------\n\nThese volumes contain the Origins of Aegis, written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Cloud Temple of Aegis.\"\n\nKrug was the last to be tricked, Iblees tempted him with wealth, with food, with power. None interested Krug. Iblees was furious, he then challenged Krug in open combat in fury.\n\nThe whole world witnessed Ibleeses transformation into a hideous beast of the Void. His skin turned into scales, fangs erupted and dark horns exploded from his head. He grew large wings and began to breath terrible fire.\n\nAll stood in shock and horror. The beast terrified all inhabitants of Aegis, animals fled and the Sons of the Descendants were too weak of blood to stand infront of such evil fury.\n\nOnly Malin, Urguan, Horen and Krug stood infront of the terror.\n\nIn Iblees' right hand, a sword wrought in flame. Krug, naked and unarmed charged at The Daemon, throwing himself against the scalding scales of Iblees. He screamed in agony as his skin burned and decayed, becoming green and molten.\n\nIblees was knocked off his feet, for the last time. He stood up, heaving in rage and fury, flames exploded from pores of his skin, he roared in anger and where he struck his sword the land collapsed and the ground erupted.\n\nAnd Lo, great was the Valour of Urguan and the Strength of Krug as they fought the beast. Powerful were the arms that carried Krugs Axe and strong was Horens heart as they fought in open combat upon the plains of Aegis.\n\nMalins sword was fast and swift, smiting Iblees upon his breast and sides.\n\nFor thirty years The Descendants fought Iblees, the world became a war torn mess. The dead were defiled, summoned from their graves to follow Ibleeses mastery.\n\nThe more mortal descendants died, the greater Ibleeses armys grew. It was a sad chapter in the World of Aegis, the brothers threw up their hands to God in prayer and in agony. Their creations destroyed, their children massacred.\n\nWoe to the Sons of Man and Woman. Krug grew bitter, his resentment of his brothers increased for befriending such a beast. Urguan grew greedy, he fought valourously but began to hide his treasures underground.\n\nMalin feared for the lives of his children and did not leave his forest sanctuary. Horen was too ambitious, too weak of a leader, many of his people fought for Iblees and many of them died of recklessness. It seemed the world was destined for ruin.\n\nIblees grew in strength and The Descendants weakened. Until one day.\n\nOne day where the sound of horns could be heard from the far east. Beautiful horns echoing all across the skies of Aegis. God had heard their prayers.\n\nAn army of Aengul and Daemons from the Seven Skys had appeared on the grand shores of Aegis. Their majesty and beauty so strong that all The Descendants could see was a flash of power white light erupt from the east.\n\nOnly Malin with his strong sight could see the figures of light as they sped across the skies towards the armies of Iblees.\n\nIblees' soldiers burned where they stood, The thousands of Skeletons and Zombies burst aflames and his living servants fled. There in the right middle of the world, he was cast down and chained by The Aengul and Daemon.\n\nA robed figure with brown hair and a black wreathed apparation stood before him. They were a terrible image to look at, all shyed down and bowed in their majest and glory.\n\nThey stood before Iblees, knowing he was immortal and could not be killed, sentencing him to banishment to the void where his dark soul would be forever trapped in torment.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tahn X", "§bMarch Ash", "It is amazing that not a kilometer from here the world has been consumed by the void, yet in this place nature has recovered. Despite all of Iblees' machinations, the resilience of nature is more powerful.\n\nSo long as a single glade survives, the world might recover. I hope the Aspects are patient enough.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tahn VI", "§bMarch Ash", "The swamps have always been a place for reclusive druids to go and meditate. The muck holds thousands of years within it, an ancient story of the land. Corpses of ancient men and women, who will tell me more about the history of this place\n\nthan books ever could.", ""),
        StaticBook("Asul III", "§bMarch Ash", "Underneath the mushroom-caps, the smell of the spores fresh in my nose, it feels easier to breathe. It reminds me so much of the Great Rot. Miles of mycelium, stretching all the way to the horizon. A ground alive with ten trillion hyphae,\n\nsplitting and merging like living rivers, a chaotic order. The memory is as sweet as slime mold.", ""),
        StaticBook("Asul VII", "§bMarch Ash", "In many ways, the mangroves are alive. Acting collectively as a single organism, many pieces of one whole. But they are also individuals. Communities are not only made of people. They can be made of any creature, alive or otherwise.\n\nWe are a community of neurons, after all.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ceru I", "§bMarch Ash", "It is visceral. I smell the dry earth, the rind of the roots, the bitter air of tangled moss. The shade is cool. But then I exhale, and I am pulled back. I think I would be just as happy were I born a tidal wave or a breeze.", ""),
        StaticBook("Worn Scroll", "§bRenn", "Copy of an ancient Elven scroll, recovered from the depths of Malin's Seed Well.\n\n\"Ullral iyl'fin narneyae...  kaean'leh thilln chul'okarae... Larehan ito kae'leh.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Kyra Leigh", "§bKyra Leigh", "Warmth, tranquility. \nI am surrounded by the spirits of the forest.\nThese magnificent creatures, tower over me, as if they were protecting me, as I do for them.\nI am in darkness, sitting in the folds of the roots of the trees. Everything is moving.\n\nThe moving water off the cliff, the wind blowing through the trees, rustling the leaves. \nAs I sit in the meadow, I see a beautiful old Elk, resting next to a large oak tree. The image is unbelievable. He lays there still, slowly breathing, in and out.\n\nI am now lost. It is dark. The sounds of the waterfall have since faded away. All is quiet now. All I hear are the chirpings of the small creatures in the flora.\n\nI have discovered...\n\nPerfection.", ""),
        StaticBook("Hardened Book", "§b???", "*Various drawing illustrate the layout of the tomb. However, a lot of the drawings have faded with time, or have been torn out.*\n\n*This page details how a pit of snakes lay below rugged sand.\nIt goes into more detail about snakes.*\n\n*The page continues on about snakes, and traps. However, one entry intrigues you. It tells of how the author of this journal had fed one of the snakes a key to a door.*\n\n*A mammoth statue was drawn onto this page.\nIt seems peculiar though, because indecipherable symbols litter around the drawing.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Ruin Translation", "§bLeyun Kameki", "\"Elsanan Malin ito narne.\"\n\n___Malin walks within here.\n\n\"Elame ito narne.\"\n\nThe forest walks within here. (?)\n\n\"Elokarir'ame ito narne.\"\n\nI must take a break from translating, for it is of the upmost importance!  I have gained entry into the ruins! I nearly killed myself in the process, but it was worth it!\n\nWhen I entered I...began to hear whispers, no doubt in Elven. Where I entered there\n\nwere two levels of partially dilapidated bookcases. Unfortunately, there were no ancient texts within them. Upon further exploration, more bookcases were located on an upper level, howver they procured no texts either.\n\nThe ruins themselves are not that large, however there is something rather odd about the ruins. \n\nIt would seem there is an altar of sorts. Whether it has a sinister nature, I cannot yet tell. I shall further examine it.\n\n9th of the Deep Cold, 1468\n\nI have studied the altar to...Some discomfort. As I approached the altar the whispers became...Painful. It felt as though they were boring into my skull. Yet the search for knowledge is more important than\n\npain.\n\nthe altar itself is of a different stone than the rest of the ruins, mayhaps it was a temple of sorts in ages long past? Elaborate would be one of the words I could use to describe it. Quaint, would be another, and mayhaps it is a tad bit\n\neerie. Sinister would-\n*The writing stops, cut off*\n\nI had to move away from altar a good bit. The whispers were getting to a point where I could barely withstand it. The altar itself has a...Glowing, a pulsating spike of blue impaled into it.\n\nWhether it was there originally or forced upon the altar for some...More sinister purpose is unclear at the moment.\n\nI believe I shall stay here for the next few days to further study it.\n\n10th of the Deep Cold, 1468\n\nI made my way up onto the wall once more from the inner sanctum of the ruins. It seems there is an inner section past the blocked entrance, and it has more Elven inscriptions within. I can barely make out some of the\n\ninscriptions and shall write them down for translation.\n\n\"Elokarir'ame evare eltalareh'sirame onn'elbarbu ullral'kina 'ehya seth.\"\n\nThe ____ forest will protect the honorable stone (?) from the dark, murdered/murderous\n\nmonsters/beasts and the void.\n\nIt would seem...There might be a connection to the floating islands and these ruins. I do not believe it speaks of the Void, in which mages draw their power from. No, I believe it speaks of the void that is at the edges of\n\nthese lands. I must make another journey over there soon.\n\n\"Elnaeri'maehr elsanan'mali uhiereyae elsan'thill.\"\n\nThe wise Cleric/devout one shall speak/tell the elves to seek the pure word.\n\nMayhaps it speaks of returning to our roots, finding and furthering the speech of Elven?\n\n\"Elame Elnaeri'ame\"\n\nLiteral: The forest, the devout forest. \n\nThe forest is devout to the forest? Mayhaps it is just a name.\n\n\"Elker Elnaeri'kina.\"\n\nLiteral: The dark, the murdered devout one. (?) \n\nMayhaps it is the devout one who will be murdered by the darkness?\n\n\"Malin elayla\"\n\nMalin is good\n(Obviously)\n\n\"Larihei ellierir\"\n\nThe sturdy voice is a deciever\n\nIf that goes with the previous translation, as they are inscribed next to each other in the ruins, would it mean\n\nMalin is a good deceiver with a strong voice? This is...Unsettling at the very least.\n\n\"Eldrui eltilruir' talareh'sirame.\"\n\nThe Druid/druids serve/will serve the stone tied to the forest.\n\n\"Elaheral elnaeri'maehr.\"\n\nLiteral: The blessed, the devout wisdom\n\n\"Elasimlum uhiere elhael yuln'sirame.\"\n\nThe sanctuary seeks three gemstones connected to the forest.\n\nIl'yuln'oem ito elonoh narna taliame tahu' elanoh sinyuera tal' taliame'eyha uhier elyuln. Elyuln'niut ito elaneh narna talareh'tanya 'eyha taelua elmaehr ito iyatha elareh. Elyuln'hael ito elanah narne eltalareh' miruel'eyha narne ito elres'nor.\n\nWith gemstone one ____ within walk to the high tree to silence the tree to seek the gemstone.\n\nGemstone two is within the right walk of the life rock (?) to learn wisdom and to think of the mountain. \n\nGemstone three walks within the body of the\n\nred stone and walks within the root lands (forest?).\n\n\"Uhiere elhael yuln'sirame.\"\n\nSeek  the three gemstones tied with the forest.\n\nLariehei is the progenitor of the Mali'aheral", ""),
        StaticBook("Mysterious Badge", "§bTyrell Blavier", "*A large and decorated \"V\" lies in the center of the page. Around it lies repetition of the phrase \"Pax et Gloria. Safe.\"*\n\n       Agent One\n         P-APP\n          -Q\n          -TB", ""),
        StaticBook("Malin II", "§bＭａｒｃｈ Ａｓｈ", "Ｈｅｒｅ Ｉ ｓｔａｎｄ ａｇａｉｎ. Ｒｅａｄｉｎｇ ｍｙ ｐａｓｔ ｗｏｒｄｓ... ｉｔ'ｓ ｆｕｎｎｙ. Ｍａｙｂｅ ｔｈｅｙ ｈａｖｅ ｃｈａｎｇｅｄ, ｊｕｓｔ ｔｈｅ ｓｍａｌｌｅｓｔ ｂｉｔ. Ｆｒｏｍ ｔｈｅ ｅｎｌｉｇｈｔｅｎｅｄ ｆｅｗ, Ｉ ｈａｖｅ ｇａｉｎｅｄ ｍｕｃｈ. \n\nＩ ｆｅｅｌ ｉｔ ｉｎ ｍｙ ｆｌｅｓｈ ａｎｄ ｓｉｎｅｗ, ｓｏｆｔ ｓｋｉｎ ａｎｄ ｈｏｔ ｂｌｏｏｄ.\n\nＷｈｅｎ Ｉ ｃｌｅｎｃｈ ｍｙ ｆｉｓｔｓ ａｎｄ ｃｌｏｓｅ ｍｙ ｅｙｅｓ, ｎａｔｕｒｅ ｈｅｅｄｓ ｍｙ ｍｉｎｄ ｌｉｋｅ ｉｔ ｗｅｒｅ ａｎ ｅｘｔｅｎｓｉｏｎ ｏｆ ｔｈｉｓ ｂｏｄｙ. Ｗｈａｔ ｗｅｒｅ ｔｈｅ ｗｏｒｄｓ ｓｐｏｋｅｎ ｆｏｒ ｈｉｇｈ ｄｒｕｉｄｓ? \n\n\"Ｔｈｅｙ ｗｉｌｌ ｈｕｎｔ ｙｏｕ ｉｎ ｙｏｕｒ ｄｒｅａｍｓ, ａ ｂｅａｒ ｔｈｅ ｓｉｚｅ ｏｆ ｔｈｅ ｓｋｙ ａｍｏｎｇ ｔｈｅ ｒａｉｎ-ｓｌｉｃｋｅｄ ｐｉｎｅｓ.\"\n\nＡｎｄ Ｉ ｗｉｌｌ ｂｅ ｔｈａｔ ｒａｉｎ.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aphidylytes", "Anonymous", "Scribed from the last words of Urangoi Ballenz, fifth descendant of Urguan’s generations-\n\nI am stuck. I ventured too deep into the coil of the Motley Ravine, and after a wrong turn in one of the\n\nabandoned mines I suppose I was knocked out. I’ve awoken to be affixed to a wall of some gooey, thick sludge. It binds me to the wall, but luckily I have my journal wedged beside me, and close enough to write on. Nearby I\n\nhear a woman, I can barely make out her lectures, but it sounds like she is preaching in a far off cavern… I fear for my life here.\n\nDeep underground, beneath the ridges of the Motley Ravine, lay a muddied labyrinth\n\nmarbled in stone and the hardened byproduct of ant-like construction. Riddling the maze of tunnels and crevices once scuttled swarms of uncountable hordes, writhing masses of legged, exoskeletal shells\n\nand tibia, clusters of wriggling bugs formed out of coxa and spinnerets. With thick armor of biomass from their antennas to their thoraxes, in the pitch abyss roamed the aphids, grand and gargantuan in both number and size.\n\n\nBut now, all have vanished. Not a single critter may be sought scuttling about in those barren halls, decrepit with time’s withering. That being due to collapse. The Deep now only consists of a chamber; the\n\npith. The lifeless bluff of the overworld resides without motion while all of the Aphidylytes are compacted into their own deposits and veins of sand and sediment. Except for one.\n\nIn the core of one\n\nmesa’s knoll, wedged down below in a sliver of openness, lies the central crux. It too lies in stillness, but not in death, but in concealed ambush. The ancient Hive Queen rests in unconscious slumber, awaiting the day for\n\nexcavation of a foolish kind before her automaticly reactive limbs may draw in the prey and feed upon it to revive her, and thus the Hive.", ""),
        StaticBook("Documentation", "§bDraoen", "Documentation- I found a Void Mage who says he is Fire Evocationist. His name is Cirimas Elendil. We went traveled next to the manor into an open field where he could use the magic safetly.\n\nHe begins to extend an arm with an open palm facing upward. He seems to be inhaling sharply. Cirimas looks like he is focusing, a pool of sweat begins to roll down his face. A small fire erupts just above his palms. It grows bigger as he breathes.\n\nThe flame grows in size, about the size of an apple. After he was done the fire fades and nothing was left. The man looks tired and has fallen into the grass. The man was very polite and I thank him.", ""),
        StaticBook("Age 13: Part 1", "§bAshton", "\"My name is Ashton. I am starting this journal at the age of 13.\n\nIf found, please return to the Oasis Grove in the Caliphate.\"\n\n\"Today, I'm a bit sad. \n\nAeran will never view me as anything more than a friend or a child. To him, I will always be the little girl he's watched grow up. \n\nI suppose that's proper and all, but it saddens me.\"\n\n\"I care very deeply for him. I'm not entirely sure in what way. He is the one person I can count on. He's always been there for me. \n\nI wish I could make him truly happy. I wish I could make him feel joy.\"\n\n\"It's not enough for me to just make him smile. I want him to feel like life can be happy for him. I want him to feel like he's as great as I think he is. \n\nBut I don't think I'll ever be able to. I don't think he'd ever let me have that role for him.\"\n\n\"I suppose all I can do is hope someone comes along that can do that for him.\"\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n\"So far, things seem peaceful today. I hope they stay that way.\"\n\n*a few drawings of local plantlife lay below the text.*\n\n\"I feel sad today. I don't know why. It's like a darkness pulling me down. I just feel so...I don't have a word for it.\"\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n\"Aeran and I talked for the longest time. He made me feel so much happier.\"\n\n\"I'm glad to have him as a friend.\n\nHe has a bit of a crush on Kyrah, the half-orc dedicant. I'm not surprised, and it was so cute! He got all red in the face. Aeran can be quite adorable. It's fun to tease him.\"\n\n\"He knows now that I have a crush on someone in the grove. He guessed a few people, but I wouldn't tell him.\n\nI made a promise to tell him the night before my attunement.\n\nI wonder if he'll be surprised to learn it's him.\"\n\n\"I wonder if he was just playing dumb. I don't know. He seemed like he really didn't know. \n\nOh, well, it's fun to tease him! I'll drop hints here and there, but I doubt he'd ever guess himself.\"\n\n\"I know it'll never be anything more than a crush. I know he'll always see me as that little girl. That's okay with me. I don't want to ruin our frienship. That's why I couldn't tell him anything. \n\nI don't want him to feel weird about talking to me.\"\n\n\"I can only hope these feelings fade by the time I reach attunement, or that'll be awkward.\"\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n*Several drawings of insects fill up the rest of the page. They appear to be common bugs from the forest near the grove.*\n\n\"Why are boys my age so annoying and stupid?\"\n\n*There is no elaboration on this entry. The rest of the page is filled to the brim with creative drawings of trees, flowers, and bushes. None of them are realistic in nature.*\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n\"Why do I have to be 13? Why can't I just grow up right now? I'm so sick of these feelings and stupid GIRL THINGS!\n\nI love Aeran. I'm SURE of it! But I know that if I say anything, it'll make him feel weird about me.\"\n\n\"I don't want him to hate me. I wish he'd notice me as more than just a little girl. But I don't want him to at the same time! I don't want him to know how I feel. \n\nWhy can't I just skip ahead five years and be done with all this stupid teen stuff!?\"\n\n*The handwriting for this entry seems a little heavy handed. As if he was angry or quite irritated while writing.*\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n\"I wish I was as pretty as some of the women I see in the grove. I'm so average and buh.\"\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n\"I was playing with Kira for a while today. We played hide and seek (which she is much better at than me) and Truth or Dare. \n\nShe got me to admit I have a crush on Aeran. I can't wait to tell Aeran that she knows.\"\n\n\"I think it'll prove most hilarious!\"\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*\n\n((It's too bad I know the book won't let me name it 'An Ode to Teen Angst'.))\n\n\"I need to stop fooling myself into believing I could ever be something special.\"\n\n\"I'm just some girl. Nothing more. I'll never be anything more than just some girl.\"\n\n*The words on this page are somewhat smeared. One might gather that she was crying while writing the text.*\n\n*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*", ""),
        StaticBook("Druidic Studies", "§bAria Lanyathir", "=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=Ariadni Lanyathir's\nDruid Studies.\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\n=-=-=- Index -=-=-=\n\nCh 1: Aspects\nCh 2: Blessings\nCh 3: Balance\nCh 4: Cycle of Life\nCh 5: The Order\nCh 6: Totems\nCh 7: Powers\nCh 8: Responsibilities\nCh 9: Ranks\n\n=-=-=-Aspects-=-=-=\n\nCerridwen: Mother of the Harvest.\n\nCernunnos: Father of the Hunt.\n\nCerridwen is the Mother of the Harvest, the Green Lady and the Woman of Morn.\n\nCernunnos is the Father of the Hunt and the Great Huntsman.\n\n=-=-=Blessings=-=-=\n\nCerridwen's blessings cover all harvests and marriages. Newly-weds may also receive the blessing of many children.\n\nCernunnos' blessings are used upon armies and honourable fighters.\n\n=-=-=Balance=-=-=\n\nThe balance of nature is one in which every life that lives is fair and just. None shall perish, both plants and animals, without a natural end. If their end is not natural, then a Druid is able to interfere to save the nature and\n\nwildlife from their unfateful end. However, is a forest is alite due to a natural storm or weather, then a Druid musn't interfere, but rather let nature run its course. This promotes the natural life cycle of all organisms.\n\n=-= Cycle of Life =-=\n\nThe Cycle of Life has 4 phases. Birth, Maturity-Growth, Reproduction and Death.\n\nBirth:\nBeginning of a New Life.\n\nMaturity-Growth:\nGrowth Stages into Adulthood.\n\nReproduction:\nFinding a Mate to create a new life.\n\nDeath:\nLoss of life.\n\nThe Food Chain is the order in which animals eat one another; the\n\n'Chain'.\nDecendants don't have a place in this chain, but would be near the Top, otherwise.\n\n=-=- The Order -=-=\n\nThe Druids are dedicated to preserving the balance of nature. Druids undergo learning and studying in order to establish a strong link with nature; a bond.\nThe Order is open to all races.\n\n=-=-= Totems =-=-=\n\nA Totem is a title that defines and identifies who a Druid is, representing their history, personality and approach to life. Only one Druid may possess a specific totem at one time.\n\n=-=-= Powers =-=-=\n\nThe extent of a Druid's powers are unknown, though they are some of the best healers and are deeply connected with nature. They serve nature, often using their powers in the form of favours.\n\n=- Responsibilities -=\n\nA Druid is a keeper and protector of nature. A Druid works to maintain the balance and peace of nature, possibly allowing the removal of a tree of forest, but expect each tree to be replanted elsewhere in return; balance.\n\n=-=-=- Ranks -=-=-=\n\nDedicants:\nThe students and learners of Druidism, under a Druid Guide.\n\nDruids:\nThe main rank, the middle position of the Order after Dedicants and before the Inner Circle.\n\nDruid Guides:\nThe teachers and 'newbies' of the Inner Circle.\n\nArch-Druids:\nThe heads of the Inner Circle. They maintain all affairs and have direct contact with the Aspects themselves. This is how most orders from the Asoects are\n\nconveyed.\n\nGrand-Druids:\nMiddleman for if a split were to occur.\n\nHierophants:\nRetired Inner Circle members, though still extremely strong. Respectable Druids.\n\nNotable Druids:\nUsually deceased\n\nDruids who have made such an impact on the Order and nature that they're kept on record as High Status Druids.\n\nDemi-Aspects:\nDeceased Druids who have passed on to join the Aspects; One of the highest honours the Order can bestow.\n\nSeekers:\nThe first rank amongst Druidism; People that have taken an interest in the Aspects, the Druids, and their practices.\n\nAcolyte:\nOne who is learning Druidism outside of the Order.\n\nLost Druids:\nDruids that have disappeared without a trace of explanation. Their return may result in punishment.\n\nForgotten Druids:\nDruids that have been removed from the Order, whether it's by death or unattunement, but despite being 'Forgotten', many of\n\nthese Druids are unforgotten due to their accomplishments.\n\nShunned Druids:\nDruids that have been shunned by an Arch Druid, due to their actions that have threatened the protection or peace of the Order. No Druid of the Order is peritted to speak with\n\none, but if a Shunned approached them, they must only say:\n\"Much wind pours from your mouth\".\nIf the Shunned persists, Druids should either ignore them or warn them that defensive action may be taken. This action should be carried out if the Shunned does\n\nnot heed the warning. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=By:\n    Ariadni Lanyathir", ""),
        StaticBook("Sunshine Letter", "§bGrand Druid Verden", "Dear Arch Druid Leyun,\n\nI'm sending this as a confirmation of my mailing address in the event that anything needs brought to my attention. My mail is kept confidential and locked tight, so feel free to let me know of any developments.\n\n                 ->\n\nPO Box 24 Equinox Island Rd.", ""),
        StaticBook("Yearn to Burn", "§bThickfeet", "A Yearn to Burn\n\n\n\n  The Journey of the first\n        Herb    Druid\n\nAmong the flora of the realm\nSomething is stirring\nA feeling of full whelm\n\nNot for food or fair\nNot for water or air\n\nBut something fresh and fine\nSomething for all, yours and mine\n\nHidden between rock an tree\nGrowing on a stalk wild and  free\n\nIs an old good friend\nOf which this poem is penned\n\nIn many variations\nIn many wide nations\nWithout regard to any stations\n\nYou can find this friend\nA friend I strongly recommend\n\nThis friend you see\nProvides many with subtle glee\nAnd including you too\nRegardless of taboo\n\nIn all of our lands\nIf you search with your hands\n\nWith a grind and a flick\nAnd a good leaf pick\nBreathe deep take a toke\nAnd enjoy your own smoke\n\n\n\n\nWith the simplest of type\nAnd a fine long pipe\nYou can soothe this yearning\nWith the right herbs to be burning", ""),
        StaticBook("A Dangerous Art", "§bJaskier of Krinfrid", "Not of This World\n___________________\nA summary of why druids oppose the use of voidal magic.\n-------------------------------------By Jaskier\n\n\"A druid's role is to commune with the aspects and protect their creations of nature.\"\n\nFounded upon principles of preservation, the druidic order aims to protect all that is of the aspects' creation.\n\nOne might note, that druids may appear to use magic in their protection of nature. However the abilites of druids differs to void magic\n\nA druid's influence over nature stems from their attunement with the aspects, a druid does not control nature but rather asks favours of the aspects in order to manipulate nature.\n\nWhereas the work of void magicians is the unnatural control of other-world\n\nelements and is done by pulling them into our own.\n\nWith this in mind, it is quite clear why the druids prohibit the use of voidal magic. As commanding unnatural elements into our own world might throw off the balance, that it is a druid's goal to\n\nmaintain.\n\nIt should be said, that a druid's role is commune with the aspects and protect their creations of nature. Which is why it is imperative that the use of void magic be prohibitid, to protect the cycle and balance of nature.\n\nFor is it in the best interest of the living world and is the will of the aspects that the seperation of our world and the void be maintained.\n\nKeep this in mind, the next time you see void magic used. So that you might recognise the harm it might do.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Kidnapping", "§bHenry Winterleaf", "The following is a true story. This book has, however, been written many years after the story it tells, all from memory. Therefore, some details may be slightly innaccurate.\n\n\nMy name is Henry Winterleaf and this is the story of the time I was kidnapped by the kha's of Asulon. The story takes place over the space of two elven days. It begins with me sitting at home by the fire. Back then, I was a prince of a minor city and I\n\nlived a happy life. This was not one of those days, however. I received a note by a large hawk. It was from the Leader of the Khas telling me that my fiance, Kirra, had been kidnapped.\nThey told me to stand by for demands but I would not wait. I rushed\n\noutside, quickly putting on my coat to protect myself from the snow.\nI headed to the cloud temple, hoping I could find my way to rescuing my fiance. I didn't even know where she was. All I knew was that I had to find her. And I had to find her now.\n\nI began asking around the temple for anyone who knew where the kha people lived. Eventually, I came across a halfling who offered to show me the way. After all this time I've forgotten his name, i'm afraid but he was a kind man. Brave enough to lead me\n\neven though he knew it would bring only danger upon himself. I had explained my situation to him and along the way we were joined by an old dwarven warrior with a hatred for the Khas. The three of us travelled together all the way to the wood elf capital.\n\n\"The boat to the Kha's island is just beyond the wood elf city\" said our halfling guide. However, we would not make it that far today. Along the path, just past the city, we were ambushed. A group of 3 large Kha warriors blocked our path. They were led by\n\na giant black-haired beast of a Kha. He was nearly 2ft taller than me and the others weren't much shorter. We had no choice but to stand and fight. Unfortunately, we were no match for them, with both the halfling and the dwarf getting knocked out, leaving\n\nme all alone against the three of them. I held my ground for as long as I could but it was no use to fight any longer. I managed to crawl under the leaves of a nearby tree, just out of reach of the Khas. They clawed at the ground trying to get\n\nme. Every scratch getting inches closer. It looked like all hope was lost when suddenly a patrol of elf city guards emerged from the treetops. We had been lucky enough to survive long enough for the army to come back from training.\n\nThey drew their bows and the Kha trio was forced to retreat. We were safe. For now, at least. We were escorted back inside the city where we were given three beds in a nearby tavern to recover in. With that, the first elven day was over.\n\nThe next elven day I awoke feeling healed. However, my companions had not been so lucky. Their injuries were much greater and they could not travel with me any further. Not wanting to waste time, I thanked my companions for their bravery and set off.\n\nI did not have to go far before I received another message from a familiar bird. It told me to come to the Kha boat with 10,000 minas or else Kirra would die. I suspected a trap, so I contacted a couple of my friends - Helgar and Arthur. We met up and\n\nheaded towards the meeting point. \"Hide here and if i'm not back soon, come after me.\" I said, cautious not to let the kidnappers see I had brought company, despite their orders. We met on the boat and I saw a few familiar faces. The Khas from yesterday,\n\nall three of them, but most importantly, my fiance, Kirra. She was bound and gagged but mostly unharmed. Uneasily, I handed over the ransom money and waited for them to hold up their end of the deal. They did not. I watched as my fiance was thrown\n\noverboard, still with her arms and legs bound, and I was grabbed by several Khas. I was now their captive and could only watch helplessly as Kirra began to drown. We set sail for their island, with me furiously trying to fight my way free.\n\nWe arrived on the island and I was immediately dragged into a pit in the ground. With its sand floor and high walls it looked more like an arena than a cage but the bars on the roof were the give-away. I cursed loudly at my captors but I was met with only\n\ninsults and taunting. Suddenly, all the Khas rushed away from the cage. There was a situation at the docks. I turned to find the door of my cage opening and a medium sized Kha in robes greeting me. He told me that my friends had arrived in time to save\n\nKirra and were now fighting their way through the docks. This Kha was different from the others. There was no word for it at the time but I believe this was the first ever case of an 'ApeKha'. I couldn't believe it. I was a free man once more but still\n\nnot safe yet. With this 'ApeKha's' help I fought my way to the docks where I found Helgar, Arthur and Kirra along with two orc warriors fighting the Kha's from earlier. Some how, my friends had enlisted the help of these two orcs to free me. We managed to\n\nmake our way onto the boat, along with our new 'Apekha' friend, with the orcs choosing to stay behind to fight. Battered and bruised but still alive, we sailed off into the distance.\nDespite all our misfortunes, we had come out on top. We were free.", ""),
        StaticBook("De Shroom Guide", "§bMadpen Brookflint", "De Purpose of this book is te ca'alouge any an' all fungi, fungus, shrooms an' differen' 'ypes of Mycelium. This lis' will undoub'udly be expanded as Me colec's more specimens. Each Specimen will be ge'ing a pic'ure an' description. -Madpen\n\nDe \"Shroom\" clasification be given te a fungi when i' meets cer'ain cri'eria. -------------------I' mus' 'ave a stalk of some sor', an' a distinc'ive cap. This 'oo par' structure is wha' seperates it. Shrooms are usualy found in groups, or clus'ers.\n\nBoomafool Shroom or\nBooming Fowl Shroom\n-------------------This Shroom is fairly common, i' grows on rocks, s'eep 'ills or under 'rees. It has a large pocket of a very ligh' gas in i's stalk. This pocket is esential. When de Boomafool begins to spore, i'\n\nde'aches from de ground and floa's upwards. It will either hi' a tree and de'ona' or i' will get blown away. When i' takes a blow, de top section explodes releasing the spores. De spores of de Boomafool are very small and liable to ge' stuck in ye eyes.\n\n\n\n_______________/~~_________\n            \\____\n                    \\____\n\n\n\n  __\n/##\\\n_||||||________________The Boomafool -Maddy\n\nRepiluca or\nCreeping Glow Shroom\n-------------------This Shroom is fairly common in caves, generaly on limes'one it grows in spall 'oles in de wall. A Faint glow is produced by spots on i'. De Repiluca is often used in mixings of Acui'y.\n\n_____________\n                   \\\n                     \\\n                      |\n                      |\n                      |\n      /++\\    ___/\n_____||||||___/\n\nThe Repiluca\n-Maddy\nNote: The Repiluca clones ocasionaly.\n\nBlack Capped River Shroom or Umbra-oom\n-------------------The Umbra-oom is much larger then previous Shrooms. It 'as a long an' curved s'alk with a bell shaped hood. I' is normaly found next to rivers as i' consumes river bir's tha' crash in'o it. The Umb\n\nra-oom can be harvested in large chunks, and will consume an' disolve anythin' tha' falls in'o i'.\n-------------------I' is safe less you fall on de 'op. Then ye should be screaming.\n\n    __________\n   /              \\\n   |                |\n  /___________\\\n        ||||||||||||\n         ||||||||||||\n          ||||||||||||\n            |||||||||||\n             \\|||||||\\\n            /||||||||||||||\\\nThe Umbra-oom\n-Maddy\n\nSafron Tree Shroom\nYellow Cap Shroom\n-------------------This Shroom be of'en confused with a Frazzlebush when i's small. I' is covered in a thick layer of vines and pe'als. Should any par' of the pe'als be eaten de consumer will become very agressive and\n\nwill have a craving for more. De pe'als are a mix of safron an' a bright white.\n\n        #######0##\n      ###0#########\n      ########0####\n      0############\n      #############\n      ####0########\n          |||||||||||||||||||||||\n          |||||||||||||||||||||||\n          |||||||||||||||||||||||\nThe Safron Tree Shroom\n-Maddy\n\nFireball Shroom\nSpotted Orb Shroom\n-------------------There is nothing much to say about this shroom. Despite i's name i's no' flamable. I' is of'en made in'o soup or use' in a salad.\n\n\n\n       _________\n     /              \\\n     |                |\n     |                |\n     \\_________/\n         |||||||||||||||||\n         |||||||||||||||||\n         |||||||||||||||||\nFireball Shroom\n-Maddy\n\nDining Plate Shroom\n-------------------This Shroom comes in many sha'es and sizes. It 'as a long stalk and a reli'ivly flat and 'ard cap. I' is of'en used as a pla'e when a good one is no' available, as i' 'as no effec' on de consumer.\n\n\n\n___\n  |\n\n        __________\n        |_________|\n              |||||\n              ||||| \n              |||||\n              |||||\n___     ___\n  |         |\n\nGiant Cluster Shroom\n-------------------Las' bu' no' leas': This Shroom is a sigh' te be'old. Much like de Spotted Orb Shroom bu' this one grows in massive pods. I' 'as no inherent use bu' 'as been used as 'ousing an' is considered sacred by many.\n\n    _____________\n  /_____________\\\n           ||||||||\n ___      ||||||||\n/__\\     |||||||||\n  ||||       |||||||||\n           |||||||||    ___\n           |||||||||   /__\\\n           |||||||||     ||||\n           |||||||||\nGiant Cluster Shroom\n-Maddy\n\nOther Tomes of de Fungi by \nMadpen Brookflint:\n\nA guide te infec'ions, fungus an' Myceliums\n\nRiver Fish o' Maliner\n\nA Guide te 'oilet makin'", ""),
        StaticBook("The Golden Pepe", "§bAsher Highwind", "The Legend\n         of the\n      Golden Pepe\n\nThe forgotten history of the descendants of the four races the great war of the pepe. \nAfter Ibeless had been casted into the firey pits of the nether, the world lay in ruins after the great horde of undead Ibless unleashed upon the world.\n\nA great power known only as \"Tythus\" decided to bless the descendants with the greatest gift of all. A way to rebuild, and thus the first pepe. Was born, he sent 4 to the pvp defaulters of the plains of Oren, 3 to the cyber dagger ears within the forests,\n\n4 to the squeakers under the mountain of Urugan. But Tythus needed to mantain power over the races, thus he created One Pepe to Rule them All, from the fires he forged, The Golden Pepe\n\nSeveral summers passed and the descendants lived in\n\nharomny with the pepe's at there side. But the power of the Golden Pepe had consumed Tythus and he wanted more power. Power that the Pepe was as if speaking to him, whispering into his ear evil thoughts to take what the descendants had that he was so\n\nmuch greater than all of them. And thus the whispering of the Pepe sepped into his mind, it turned the once peaceful Tythus into a Steam buying evil doer. He waged a bloody war against the descendants with the power of the Pepe at his side none could stop\n\nhim until a young hero by the name of Billy Bob cut the Pepe from Tythus's grasph without the pepe to sustain the evil that was inside him he faded into the air, but instead of casting the Pepe back into the fires from wince it came ((wait this sounds-\n\nfamiler)) He chose to weild the power of the Pepe for himself until he was slain in an ambush but before he took his final breath the Creator smiled down upon his actions to defeat the evil Tythus and brought him into the Seven Skies as his farmer. To\n\nharvest the fields for all enterinty ((it was awful, pretty sure he went crazy from looking at all the taters)) Yet the pepe was lost no one is quite sure what became of it the story of the pepe drifting away throughout the generations now simpily ghost\n\nstories that parents tell there children to keep them doclie. But the Pepe is out there somewhere looking to whisper into the next ear to start the next great war of the descendants... Lying... Waiting...", ""),
        StaticBook("Forbidden Part 1", "§bAshton", "Forbidden Romance\n\n\n\n\nAshton Gallic\n\nAshton invited Aeran to her dedicant room to celebrate. Tomorrow would be her attunement. She would become the Swan Druid.\n\nHer bedside table was topped with a few wine bottles and two glasses.\n\nShe paced impatiently as she waited for him. She shook nervously.\n\nShe knew what was coming.\n\nThat was the night she had promised long ago to tell Aeran about her 'crush'.\n\nHe had figured it out some time ago, but she aimed to keep that promise.\n\nOn this night, she would tell him that she still loved him more than anything. She would tell him that she thought only of him, day and night.\n\nShe was ready to be rejected again. She knew it was coming, but still, she had to tell him.\n\nShe uncorked one of the wine bottles and poured it into the glasses. Drinking hers before filling it again. And again. She needed to calm her nerves.\n\nA knock came at her door. Ashton could feel her heart pounding wildly in her chest. Her breathing became labored and her whole body felt heavy, sweaty.\n\nShe opened the door, and there stood Aeran, a genuine, happy smile on his face.\n\nHe walked in and wrapped his arms around her.\n\n\"Congratulations, 'Ton! I knew you'd be great.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Aeran.\" Ashton smiled and held him tight in her arms. The feeling was like a million fireworks shooting off inside her body. Being close to him was magical for her.\n\n'He doesn't know what's coming...' She thought.\n\nSuddenly, that feeling of deep seated pleasure faded, and she let go of the hug.\n\nFor a brief moment, her smile was gone. Her face was overcome with a pout and her eyes glazed over with sadness.\n\nFearing Aeran would know something was wrong, she quickly threw a cocktail party smile on and walked over to the wine.\n\nShe picked up the full glasses and walked over to Aeran.\n\n\"Here! I wanted to invite you for a toast.\"\n\nAshton handed him his glass. They raised them, clinking them together, and said a cheer in unison.\n\nDrinking the wine made her feel more at ease. Her previous glasses were taking effect and helping quell any nerves that were left.\n\n\"Please, sit.\" Ashton said as she motioned to a small guest chair in her room. \n\nAeran smiled once more and sat in the chair.\n\n\"You've come a long way, 'Ton. I'm proud of you,\" he said.\n\nAshton took one of the bottles of wine and poured more into Aeran's glass before pouring more for herself.\n\nShe then climbed into her bed across the small room -- really only a few steps away from the chair.\n\nSitting there, she just smiled at him, not saying a word for a moment.\n\n'This is perfect,' she thought, 'Nothing exists except us here. Why must I ruin it now?\"\n\nShe let out a sigh and closed her eyes. SHe breathed in deeply. Holding her breathe for a moment, she calmly cleared her mind. Slowly, she exhaled. Surely, some sort of meditative thing she learned from her father.\n\n\"Aeran, there's something I want to speak with you about.\" Her words were said in almost a joking tone, a smirk on her face.\n\nJoking was the only way she could think to handle this situation.\n\n\"Oh, and what is that, 'Ton?\" Aeran asked as he sat back in the chair. He sipped on his wine. \n\n\"Well, you see, several years ago, I made you a promise. I was going to tell you who I had a crush on!\" She exclaimed.\n\n\"Ashton...we've been over this already. It was settled back then. You don't have to do this now.\" Aeran's tone went from matching Ashton's joking manner to one of senceritity and concern.\n\nAshton grinned wide.\nShe laughed.\n\n\"Oh, hush, you! I made a promise, and I intend to keep it.\" She chugged back the glass of wine. Reaching over, she grabbed another bottle and struggled with the cork for a moment.\n\nAeran's brows furrowed. \"How much have you had to drink exactly? I think maybe you should stop. Tomorrow is an important day.\" He was very worried about Ashton now. What feelings had she been dwelling on to reach this point?\n\n\"Stop. I'm fine. Thank you for worrying about me, really, but I want to keep my promise. I don't intend to break any of my promises to you.\" She stuck her tongue out at him.\n\nAeran sighed as he rubbed his face with his hands.\n\nThe room fell silent for a moment. Neither of them really wanted what was coming next.\n\n\"I still love you, Aeran. Despite all the things I said in the past, I do. I think of you...always.\" She laughed. \"You are my one and only love.\"\n\n\"Ashton...\" He tried to stop her from saying more, but she held up her hand.\n\n\"No, Aeran. It's okay. While you may be my love, I know I'm not your's. It's okay. I've always known that. I had to say it, though. I promised.\"\n\nAeran looked into her eyes. He looked so tired. He seemed so saddened by this. Once again, it was all coming up. He could feel her heart breaking all over again despite her sincere smile.\n\n\"Ashton...I don't know what to say.\"\n\n\"There's nothing to be said. It's all been said before. There's nothing more to add. It's alright. I kept my promise. I'm happy. It will always be okay, Aeran. I promise.\" Her voice was shakey.\n\nDespite her smile, she could feel herself holding back tears.\n\n\"Aeran. There is...one thing. One thing I've always wanted. Could you...do something for me? Just this once. Perhaps...as a gift for my attunement?\"\n\nAeran's brows furrowed once more. He didn't like her tone or the nature of this request.\n\n\"Ashton, please. Don't ask for anything more than I can give. You know where I stand on this.\"\n\nAshton stood and walked over to him. He straightened up in the chair. His eyes were locked with hers.\n\nShe placed her hands on his shoulders.\n\n\"Ashton, please.\" He tried to plead with her. \"Don't do anything or ask of anything you'll regret.\"\n\n\"Shh...\" She whispered.\n\n\"Just one kiss.\" Her tone was low. Her voice still shakey.\n\n\"Ashton, no. I won't do this. It will only hurt you more. Please. Stop.\"\n\n\"Just one kiss. I know how you feel. We never have to speak of it again. Just one. I've always wanted to know what your lips would feel like against mine. I need to know.\" Her breathing was deep and heavy again. Her heart was pounding harder than ever.\n\n\"Ashton...\" His voice was heavy with grief. He felt so bad for his little 'Ton. He placed his hands on hers and removed them from his shoulders.\n\nPushing her back gently, he stood from his chair. Once again, he looked into her eyes.\n\nThe silence hung in the room for several minutes. \n\nFinally, Aeran sighed. He was pained from this situation. He wanted nothing more than for Ashton to be happy, but he knew this wasn't the way.\n\nAeran knew he'd regret it if he gave in. He knew he'd beat himself up for it. He knew she'd do the same to herself.\n\nBut...it was just one kiss. Just one. That's all she asked for. Was it really too much?", ""),
        StaticBook("Eldest Brother", "§bAzkel Frostbeard", "The Eldest Brother: A History of Malinor\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Malinor as we know it began after the Collapse, an obscure event which lead to the collapse of any civilization that may have existed before 1300. When one of the symptoms of the Collapse,\n\nthe erasing of the collective memory of all who survived the Collapse, wore off, High Prince Native banded together what was left of a wood elven colony, as well as the dark elves and high elves in the region surrounding Laurelin. He set about\n\nconsolidating his position in Laurelin, assisting many elves in setting up lives in the capital. Very few elves are left from this time almost all having gone missing at some point during Aegis, so accounts of the early days are secondhand at best, many\n\ntimes having been passed from pointed ear to pointed ear until reaching the writer. By most accounts, Native picked an array of Princes to help him govern the growing elven population, some notable ones being Prince Toren the Farmer,\n\nPrince Flefal the Engineer, Princess Arianna, and Prince Lafthi the Diplomat. The mining paradise of Ravenhold was founded, home to such notable elves as Khel Oussana, Haelphon Adecchio, and future Prince Ebs Telrunya.\n\nAfter the initial years in Laurelin, most of the High Council, including High Prince Native, were barely ever seen in Laurelin, and the Malinor suffered. The Gauntlet, an obstacle course that offered a large minas reward, was opened rarely,\n\nand almost every elf immediately entered the Wardens or the Druidic Order upon entry into Malinor. The only functioning part of Malinor's government were indeed the Wardens, the elite defenders of the forests of Malinor.\n\nimmediately entered the Wardens or the Druidic Order upon entry into Malinor. The only functioning part of Malinor's government were indeed the Wardens, the elite defenders of the forests of Malinor. Lead at first by the ruthless Blackthorn,\n\n they protected the forests while the High Council squandered away the Princedom. Native's only notable action during this time was nearly destroying Ravenhold when he caused the mine to collapse. When Kal'Bryst was destroyed during the\n\nonset of the Undead invasion, the High Council's reaction was nonexistent. During the great northern wars, in which the humans struggled for survival against the undead invaders, the elves hid in the south, with very few participating in the defense of\n\n Alstion. The Wardens instead focused on removing Wrath from Wrath's Clutch, an Undead citadel in the South that threatened to cut off Alras and Al'Khazar from Laurelin and Kal'Urguan. Countless wardens and other elves\n\nperished in attacks on Wrath's Clutch, which was repeatedly assaulted so that prisoners being held there, most notably Princess Indelwehn, could be freed. No matter how many time the walls were breached, the forces of the\n\nUnited Aegis Coalition (UAC) could not dislodge Wrath until very late in the war. Indelwehn became notable for leading the wardens in the defense of Sanjezel, leading the to the signing of the Malin-Krug pact, a long-lasting defensive\n\ntreaty between the Holy Princedom and Krugmar, which was signed by Princess Indelwehn and Wargoth Mogroka'Gorkil.\n\tMalinor had a token presence in the Verge, its official settlement, overseen by Princess Lirinya, lacking utility and security.\n\nMore enterprising elves and a few humans united under Viscount Ebs, who founded the city of Fionn, which flourished for a time, until all but the mentally unstable abandoned the Verge during the massive slime and spider invasion.\n\nAt this time, Laurelin abandoned its old Council Chambers to allow the Mages Guild to have a place to set up away from the unstable north.\n\tFor a time, Malinor had a High Princess, Sorious, the wife of Native. After her death,\n\nthe High Council passed a law stating that being the spouse of a Prince does not inherently make you a Prince. Native later fell in love with the owner of the Treetop Inn, which flourished in the trees North of Malinor. The owner, Lirinya, quickly rose to\n\nprominence in Malinor, gaining the title of Princess quite quickly. Her down to earth nature and her humbleness disguised that she was Undead. Gaining trust from a besmitten Native, she betrayed Malinor, destroying Ravenhold during the rule of\n\n High Princess Indelwehn.\n\tWith the appointments of Princess Indelwehn and Prince Mylas, a new generation of Elven leaders took the helm of the nation, Ebs, a high elf, and Sythra, a dark elf, convinced High Prince Native to disband the corrupted Wardens,\n\nwho were being used by the cruel Prince Quazar as an aggressive force, sparking conflict with the other nations. The Vanguard was formed, with Sythra in command. With the appointment of Prince Ebs as High Prince Native's final act, the High Council,\n\nguided by the still mostly absent Princes Toren and Lafthi elected High Princess Indelwehn to the highest position in Mainor. They then added then Treelord Haelphon and Sythra to the Council, and removed the Archdruids from the Council, as in that time\n\n the druids grove was a pit of filth and crime, all but sanctioned, and most certainly indulged in by Respiren and Dusk. This was a Golden Age for the elves, as their international power and influence waxed to a point where it could have been argued,\n\nafter the fall of Al'Khazar, that Malinor was the stablest and most powerful state in Aegis.\n\tUnknown at the time of her election was a deep taint hidden in the heart of Indelwehn, installed during one of her stays in Wrath's Clutch.\n\nHer second husband, Bircalin, and her friends watched in horror as she changed. It is still not clear if she was aware she was tainted, however, she broke the Malin-Krug pact, refusing to aid Krugmar in their war against Urguan and Oren\n\nand made a series of disastrous mistakes. She attempted to have prince Ebs assassinated for questioning her, however he barely survived, and after a year recovering in Hanseti, he returned when Indelwehn was charged\n\nwith the murder of a small child. The High Council attempted to cover up her crimes, but realized she had to go, and voted unanimously to remove her, revealing to the public only that she had resigned. In a near tie, Haelphon was elected High Prince,\n\nwith Sythra losing by one vote. By this point, Prince Mylas had been killed during an Undead attack, and his body had been laid to rest outside the Sister City, a suburb of Laurelin. At this time, the High Council met in the Sister City, as it was too\n\neasy to Undead spies to listen in on proceedings in Laurelin.\n\tMalinor established good relations with the Kingdom of Renatus after it emerged from the Phoenix Rebellion, but the final days in Aegis was characterized\n\nby fear among the High Council. After the falls of both Al'Khazar and Alras, refugees flocked to Malinor. Prince Ebs set up the Alrasian refugee camp East of Laurelin, but the Alrasian officers and Vanguard struggled to keep order in the once peaceful\n\nity of Laurelin. During a magical experiment by the druids and mages, Arkelos, and Undead, destroyed the Gauntlet, spreading miasma around Laurelin. As elves prepared to flee the city, the druids managed to restore the air, however the damage\n\nwas staggering. The High Council, despite the pleading of a few members, refused to build additional fortifications around Laurelin, instead opting to give Deltaro Black, an elf who thought he was much more competent that he actually was, permission\n\nto remodel the front entrance to make it more\"naturey.\" Laurelin did not stand a chance in the final battle, and as the dragons leveled the once proud jewel of Laurelin, the elves fled for the Verge, man of which were openly weeping, where\n\nthey were met by the chaotic conditions, as the elven government has fallen apart. Treelord Alan managed to build an elven ship, but the travel conditions were horrid, and many elves were lost in the voyage. And so the elves set out for Asulon, to brave\n\n a strange new world full of dangers they could not have possibly conceived as they left Laurelin.\n--Ebs Telrunya, Former Fourth Prince of Malinor", ""),
        StaticBook("Needing a Task", "Lego_XBOX", "\"Good whatever time...\n\nI'm Vel'Aryla Kameki and my teacher has asked me to get a task from you. Whenever you have a moment... could you give me a task to do...?\"\n\n~Vel'Aryla Kameki", ""),
        StaticBook("You tried!", "§bTailesin", "Oh hey! Good job on§0\n§0getting into the §0\n§0restricted section!§0\n§0\n§0Too bad the books §0\n§0you're looking for §0\n§0are not in here!§0\n§0\n§0And to think of all the§0\n§0trouble you went through to break into here!§0\n§0-Love Tailesin, Lynx Druid.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient History2", "§bTailesin", "-------------------\nAncient History,\nVolume 2\n-------------------\n\nThese volumes contain the Origins of Aegis, written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Cloud Temple of Aegis.\"\n\nWe do not know how they fathered sons and how their lines continued but perhaps God created a partner for each Son to continue their lines? Soon the first Man and Woman created by God departed Aegis and climbed the Seven Skies to meet with God,\n\n their creator, for they had achieved their goal. The Sons began to explore the World of Aegis and each was stricken with grief at the loss of their parents, seeking to fill the chasm of emotion inside them they began to create civilization.\n\nAlthough none of the descendants of each Son had neither their long lives or splendour, they did carry their forefathers looks and traits. The Elves with Malin at their head travelled the lands and began naming all the inhabitants of this world,\n\nthey were the first to create a tongue and began to name the trees and grass, the mountains and valleys across all of Aegis.\n\nAt this time, Malin, Urguan, Krug and Horen were not far apart in looks. Horen and Krug had darker hair than the other two and Krug was a bit larger in build. Malin had blonde hair and Urguan had red.\n\nThey were all close companions, friends and had much love for each other and each others people.\n\nUrguan travelled into the deep underground of the earth, naming the beautifull ores and gems, sharing it with brethren. Krug travelled the harsh deserts, testing his stamina and strength against the environment of Aegis,\n\nand Horen began building the first squalid huts and villages in the valleys. The people lived for hundreds of years, in harmony and peace.\n\nUntil the day where one of the great Daemons, great worshipers and squalid protectors of the Void, kin spirits to the Aengul, defected. Iblees, who was once the greatest of all of Gods worshippers was cast down into the mortal world,\n\nhe was about to destroy the tranquility of Aegis forever. He came in the shape of a man. A gentle being who loved things delicate, he was quickly befriend by Malin, Urguan and Horen.\n\nKrug had a great distrust of him but after many years of being convinced by his brothers, he finally accepted his company.\n\nLittle did Iblees know that this was all part of the Great God's plan to test the descendants of Man and Woman. Iblees wanted to destroy everything that God had designed and created,\n\nstill not in a powerful physical form he began sowing seeds of evil in the minds of the Descendants. Prying on their needs and lusts.\n\nTo Malin, lover of nature, he promised him many children. He promised him that their laughter would resonate across their forest homes and that he would be content.\n\nMalin did not want anything more in this world than a bountiful amount of young to occupy the barren forests.\n\nTo Urguan, he promised infinite wealth of Gems and Ores, colours and crystals he could never imagine. To this, Urguan's greed failed him.\n\nHoren, was anxious he would not live long enough to see his city completed and his lands increased. He needed more time, and Iblees struck the cord and promised immortality.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Mori", "§bThurak'Yar", "The End of the Mori\n\nWhen the murders and attacks on the city occurred, many speculations arose within the city itself of whom was to blame and who should be slaughtered for such acts against their kind – many claimed it was the work of the\n\nsurfacers, others believed that such things were only possible by those living within the city.\nIt was declared and enforced that all living Mori were to return to the bosom of Nemiisae and remain within the sanctum of Menocress; behind\n\nthese stragglers to return the horror unfolded, each path and passage that journeyed through only hours before could be heard crumbling under the weight of explosives. The people were brought each in turn to the Temple of the\n\nMother to be cleansed of their taint, the sickness of the surface that had infested them. The final act of the Valsharess and her Yathrins before the war broke out.\nMany that had broken loose from such rigid traditions fought against this cleansing\n\nand became quickly enraged, spitting in the faces of the priestess’ whom dared demand their obedience and silence. It was not long before two factions emerged; the devout and the rebels. Beneath the surface of the world, sealed off from\n\nthe blinding damnation of the sun’s light, the Caverns of Menocress’ streets were littered with crude barricades, strewn with the deceased and wounded – The once marvelled city now rots with death and destruction… crumbling under the civil war.\n\nBathed in life blood of their kith and kin, the rebellion breached the Yathrins’ sacred enclave; banners raised in revolt, their armour and bodies marked by their treason. Once considered mere common rabble; these denizens now slaughter\n\nblessed noble born and their ilk standing guard at the doors of the Temple. From behind the sealed doors both rothe and Mori alike stand in the un-ease union against the threat of tait to its own city – this seeded by those turned to the\n\nhubris of their Ilharn Zanunder.\n\nRescribed by the Farseer Thurak’Braduk, 14th of the First Seed 1484.", ""),
        StaticBook("Personal Records", "§bTailesin", "-------------------The Governance of Rivel\n-------------------\n\n~By Artorius Thannius\n\nThe city-state of Rivel has been described by foreigners as a 'mageocracy.' Such a term is clearly tautological: it is an obvious fact to all citizens of Rivel that skill in the arcane is a requisite for the foresight required for governance:\n\nhowever could one control and lead a populace without the wisdom of the arcane and the insight into the thoughts and actions of the populace offered by mental magics and scrying?\n\nTherefore, Rivel is organized into a two-tier society: the enlightened citizens such as ourselves skilled in the arcane are led by the Circle of Governance and its leader, the Spellcrafter Archon.\n\nThe Enlightened Caste of Spellcrafters can be identified by our pointed ears. The round eared, magically blind and short-lived Lesser Caste of Labourers act as workers and servants.\n\nThey own property and instead live in worker quarters in the homes of their Enlightened betters. Obvious we do not allow them to take positions of authority and responsibility as their magical blindness means they would do a poor job indeed;\n\nbetter to not allow them to try. Those who display some magical skill are given directorial positions over the rest of the Lesser Caste, but they do not live long enough to achieve the true arcane mastery required to join the ranks of the Enlightened.\n\nThe city also makes use of semi-intelligent pack animals known as Orcs, which once broken can be taught basic speech and simplest tasks.\n\nThey are incredibly useful as beats of burden but-- despite the word of what are clearly rumours and the of agitators-- they are not capable of higher thought any more than cattle are.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ikuras", "§bThurak'Yar", "“And so it was that (Ikuras) was imprisoned, entomed by his rothers in fire and earth.” -Diary of Grigory Adeveci\nThere are few things in Ancient History more mysterious, more dark, than the tale of Ikuras, the Daemon of Fear.\n\nWhat little we know is written in the Diary of Grigory Adeveci, a monk who was by all accounts insane. However, it is the belief of some that Ikuras is very real, and that his story is true.\n\nThe following is an analysis of Grigory’s Writing.\nThe journal begins fairly orginarily.\n\n“Greetings, my name is Grigory Adeveci. I am writing this in my little room in the abbey. All is quiet now here at night and I believe it is safe to bring the artefact out. Allow me to describe this artefact to you; it is a small key, appearing as\n\na dagger. It looks to be forged of a crystal material, and shimmers darkly when I turn it towards the light. Tomorrow I will study it more.”\nRecords suggest that Grigory was a part of the abbey’s archaeological studies, and collected\n\nthis artefact, this key, on a dig. The next passage is where Grigory begins experiencing odd dreams.\n\n“Last night I had a vision. The world was drenched in a shadow. It looked like a damp, heavy shadow. I could no see through it, only hear the mad laughter from underneath. I do not think the artefact is safe to show anyone else. I will have to see\n\nwhat happens upon my sleep tonight.”\nThe next day the journal goes on.\n\n“My nightmares last night were nothing short of disturbing. I was the land, and I could not see. But I felt things, crawling on my back. Hairy, gaunt, rotting. I must destroy the artefact or I will be unable to sleep.”\n\nThere are varying arguments addressing why Grigory did not simply remove the artefact from his presence. The most prevalent belief is that he was already turning mad. This is Grigory’s last sane entry.\n\n“My dreams were good, heavenly last night. I dreampt that a thousand lights came down from the heavens and sucked away the darkness. I do not know where they imprisoned it, but the darkness was locked away somewhere. The fear was locked away.\n\nHere Grigory appears to have gone completely mad. This is several weeks after his sane entry. His writings get longer and longer periods of time between them.\n\n“I don’t know why I tried to break myself. He told me it was bad but I did it anyway. I had to try and escape from myself. I should have known I should have known I should have known I would shatter. The fear is eating me now.\n\nI see my blood on its teeth teeth teeth.”\n\nThe repeating of phrases is very present in the insanity writings. Always he repeats something three times.\n\nThe second line in this next entry is oddly out of place, it is assumed that this obscure prophecy came to him in a bout of sanity.\n\n“The fear fear fear is eating me and I don’t know where the lights made it gone the light made him gone gone gone. And so it was that he was imprisoned, entombed by his brothers in fire and earth. Ikuras. From the same divine spark\n\nas Iblees and Aeriel. Creator preserve me it said the name is said the name name name it must never say the name.”\nAt this point Grigory constantly refers to himself as “It.” This next entry is the final one, written one month after the last.\n\n“They crawl all over it. The wolf, the spider, the corpse. It is the First Continent, and it feels them on its back back back. Now it is the Second Land, and it is free free free of the fear fear fear. But now it is the Third Place, and it is\n\nback back back. It is feeling the wolf the spider the corpse again again again. The key is the prison. The key is the prison. The key is the prison.”\n\nDisturbingly, the last three sentences, the repeating of “The key is the prison” is written in Grigory’s own blood. Records show that he committed suicide after this last entry.", ""),
        StaticBook("Terafil's Demise", "§bThurak'Yar", "Being no strangers to conflict, political or otherwise, it seems to be a miracle that one house, the Miiystra, has ruled over the other noble clans of the Menocress for so long. Nearly every other noble clan has shifted power and positions\n\nat some time in history, but never have the Miiystra given up their seat of power at the very top. That is not to say attempts to overthrow the Miiystra have not occurred before, however. In fact, it is due to the very nature of the\n\nMori’Quessir, of viciousness, ruthlessness, and an always-competitive urge that has kept the Miiystra in power for so long. Coups have been attempted, but all have crumbled into dust before fruition. The one which got closest to victory, closest\n\nto the complete annihilation of House Miiystra, is perhaps the best to look at when considering how the realm of politics functions in Menorcress.\nThe name of the Hosue responsible for this is no longer spoken in good company, lest you wish\n\no lose your head. We record it here at great risk for our well being and have even gone so far as to translate it into a more accessible version of the common tongue.\nThat being said, we feel obligated to warn you that being caught\n\nreading of this atrocious event (Which, we, as the authors, in no way condone any sane being to attempt to replicate) in the company of the Mori’Quessir, or in any location under the control of House Miiysta, you may find\n\nyourself unable to read anything.\nEver again.\nWe also request that you use this document only for learning of history- not for attempting to repeat it. There are many other things which we, as a group, should be investigating. Things far\n\nless lethal than the Mori’Quessir and their history. As such, we implore you to take this moment so that we may be on our way. Quickly. Please. We also do not wish for our names to be known, for all of the above reasons and many others.\n\nIf you’ll excuse us, we have some nice Dwarven Ruins to attempt to translate now.\nAt least the worst danger in Dwarven Territory is collapsing rocks and lava…\n\nForty-something years ago, in the city of Menorcress, existed 6 houses- 5 Nobles and 1 Head. These noble houses were:\nMierillis, Mages of great renown.\nZaurret, peddlers of services and entertainment.\n\nMalachai, founded and headed by the ledendary warrior Elrodon’Malachai.\nCresa’mtor, merchants responsible for the flow of goods throughout the city,\nAnd finally, the Terafil.\n\nThe Terafil were an odd bunch, run by Matron Mother T’risskiira, there were a clan of workers for hire. Mercenaries, miners, messengers and the like, they had no specialty besides being hard workers. There was also the Head House, the Miiystra.\n\nThe give a quick overview of the Miiystra and their claim to power, we must take a brief look at their ancient history. All of them are descended from the Eldest Daughter of Nemiisae, their Goddess, and can trace their bloodline\n\nfirmly back to her. The Miistra are not only in charge of the other houses, but also rule over the Cleric College and all aspects of religion, being the most “Holy” as it were, in the eyes of Nemiisae.\n\nThis displease T’risskiira. In their eyes, they were the most devout, the most holy, and the most deserving of power and praise. They wished not only to be the head house, but also the closest to their Goddess. It was with this in mind that they\n\nbegan their plot to overthrow the Miiystra and take their place as the favoured bloodline. They amassed weapons, gathered allies and were soon ready to throw their full force against the Miiystra. They marched suddenly and without\n\nwarning into the halls of the Palace, shedding blood as they went and sparing no one. Slaves, warriors, servants and blood members all fell. The other houses quickly came to action, but not to assist the Terafil. No, you see, the way of Menocress\n\nis in an old saying. “If your enemy has power, take it. If you cannot take it, remove it from him so that no one else may take it.”\nYou see, if the other noble clans could not take the Miistra’s position and power, they did not\n\nwith for Terafil to have it. House Mierillis rose immediately, marching into the warzone that was the palace in an attempt to save the High Matriarch and as many of her kin as they could. House Malachai marched as well, eager\n\nto fight and slaughter house Terafil. In but one day, dozens of House Miiysta fell. High Matriarch Veylna was slain and only a few of her hcildren survived. At first, it was believed only two of them survived; the Eldest Daughter and the Eldest Son,\n\nboth saved by House Mierillis. They retreated then, creating a new base at the Cleric College, still safe from the Terafil-Traitors. There they stayed for several weeks to regain their strength while house Mierillis held off the attackers.\n\nThen they marched on the palace, another blood bath ensuing, cleansing the walls of Miiystra in blood with the blood of traitors.\nIn but a few short weeks, the Miiystra clan was devastated and the Terafil clan was annihilated.\n\nThe Eldest Daughter mournfully took her place as High Matriarch and the last of the females of the bloodline. That is, until Elrodon’Malachai arrived to recognise her as High Matriarch, as all of the House Heads were required to do.\n\nThe High Matriarch was none too pleased to see this man; while he had aided greatly in the fight by slaughtering the Terafil, he had not come to her to pledge his undying allegiance while she was in exile at the Cleric College. He had done the right\n\nthing by helping slaughter the Terafil, but for what reasons, precisely, had he done it? THe ambiguity of his ambitions made her wary of him and what he did next made her even more so. For from behind himself appeared a young woman,\n\n perhaps 70 years of age, cloaked in a robe. House Malachai had saved a few of the younger ones, namely the only other female to survive the slaughter. WIthout demanding power, money, or anything else in exchange, in an uncharacteristically\n\ngenerous gesture, Elrodon’Malachai handed over the younger sister to the new High Matriarch. He then performed his duties as a House Head, recognising the High Matriarch as the rules of Menorcress, then left, as quietly as he\n\nhad came. It was in this way that the Miiystra bloodline was nearly destroyed; that a traitorous clan was destroyed, and how stability was returned to Menorcress. Mierillis would remain a strong ally of the High Matriarch for their\n\nassistance in the revolt, while for many years rumours would spread through the city as to Elrodon’Malachai’s ambitions. In the end, he went to his grave with not another soul knowing how or why he had\n\nrescued the youngest daughter. But the fact remained that he had, and for the stability of Miiystra and the maintaining of the status quo, most of Menorcres was grateful. Some forty years after the fall of House Terafil, the mentioning of its name\n\nhas been banned from Menorcress. It has been purged from most of the record books and its old clan home left to stand, burned and rotted, as an example to all. Not a drop of the Terafil blood still remains, and if it does, such blood\n\n is sure to keep their mouth shut, lest they tear open old wounds and cause new ones to themselves.\n\nRescribed by the Archmage Crumena V. Ilwindior on the 2nd of THe First Seed, 1473.", ""),
        StaticBook("Kha' Childhood", "§bThe Tultekos Inaijo", "FROM CHILD TO ADULT\n\nA Kharajyran book surrounding the events of a Kha’s childhood.\n___________________\n                            Everyone remembers their childhood, or is still experiencing it. No matter who you are, childhood will be a\n\ndifferent and unique experience for yourself. Even more so as you look between the races. But one question decided to rise, “What is Kharajyr childhood like?”\nMany know little to none on this topic as the Kharajyr do not write down their ways of life and\n\nhistory into books or tomes for the public eye to see. But that is soon to change...\n___________________\n                            Chapter 1:\n0 - 5 Years\n\nThe wonder of Birth, a new life with a new soul. Not for the Kharajyr however,\n\nfor the Kharajyr birth is simply a new life, the soul is that of a previous Kharajyr, passed on from one body to another. As such when they are taken into the world they are given a small ceremony in the Muunpool of Metztli. Here it is found out what this\n\nnew Kitten’s place among their civilization will be. (See ‘Basic Va’Kharjiran’ for more on Kharajyr jobs.) At this early newborn stage, nothing much happens in their lives apart from learning a few basic words and ways of general life.\n___________________\n\nChapter 2:\n6 - 10 Years\n\nAt this age the Kitten is reaching the end years of younger life and is reaching the stage of a cub. They are however now able to speak few words and very rarely structure a sentence and as such are begging to take an\n\ninterest in learning about the world as they are much curious, they will tend to ask their parents/guardians for guidance now. But are still walking on all fours (However they can rarely stand on their hind-legs.) They will begin to help out with their\n\nfamily life at this point, performing chores and carrying a various array of objects, perhaps a small blade but rarely a weapon. \n___________________\n\nChapter 3:\n11 - 15 Years\n \nAt this age they are becoming more mature\n\nand can now choose to stand on their hind-legs, occasionally returning to all fours. They are learning to form sentences and fight. They will be a lot more independent at this point and their parents will be giving them more mature goals and they will be\n\nlearning how to cope with the real world as the age of maturity for Kharajyr is at fifteen.\n___________________\n                            \nFinal notes:\n16+ Years\n\nNow the Kharajyr is an official adult among the Kharajyr, he/she is respected and\n\ntreated as one, he is bestowed with a title and is expected to find his own path in life (Within the area of job he is given at birth.\n\nThis brings the book to an end, we hope you had an interesting and informative read.\n\nSigned, Inaijo.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient History1", "§bTailesin", "-------------------  The Ancient History\n-------------------\n\nThese volumes contain the Origins of Aegis, written by the \"Wandering Wizard\" in the second seed of year 84 at the Cloud Temple of Aegis.\"\n\nThere were no planes of existence. There was nothing. Just the void. And a bright shining light. It was not a light we could see, it was not really white either, but it would be the only way to explain the existence of such a Deity in contrast to the\n\nemptiness of the Void. None knows why, how or when this Deity existed. It is in-fact, heresy to speculate on such matters. The all knowing God was this Deity.\n\nTruly none can describe the majesty of the creator, the one and only. The Merciful Creator of the Seven Skies. With his intent, and only his intent, the World of Aegis was created.\n\n All he had to do was simply want it to occur and it happened. The rolling seas, proud mountains and vast landscapes, all in a second, created by God\n\nThe World was not created in play or for fun, but as a theater for the inhabitants of the World to be tested in. And tested for what? Well, that is for us to speculate.\n\nThough I believe it is to test us for purity, how hard we can fight temptation, and how clean and good can our actions be.\n\nThe first mortal being to be created was a man. This man was sculptured with clay and water, but left hollow. Soulless, God's creation lay there for the other immortal beings to see.\n\nThe Aengul and The Daemon, the two spirits created before the mortal beings and before the creation of Aegis. Not much is known about these two spirit races, but hopefully in the future we may know more.\n\nGod then blew his own breath of existence into the hollow shell and the Man lived. God then took the man and put him in the center of all of Aegis, the same spot where The Cloud Temple now stands.\n\nGod also made the Man a partner, from the exact same clay and the exact same breath, she was the first Woman. These simple beings could not talk, nor did they know much about the World.\n\nThey roamed the world, enjoying its prehistoric beauty. Untouched, the fresh glades and dark thick forests were a wonder to set eyes on. Soon they had four children in the four corners of Aegis.\n\nMalin was the first of their sons, born in the deep forests of Aegis, he had blonde hair and sharp ears. Standing tall, he was the father of the Elves of Aegis.\n\nKrug was born in the scorching deserts of Aegis, fierce and relentless, he was the father of the Orcs. In the fresh meadows of Aegis was born Horen, father of Humans.\n\nAnd lastly, in the deep underground caves of the World, Urguan was born, master of the Dwarves.", ""),
        StaticBook("Essential Oils", "Nyxxaa", "Essential Oils - A guide to theraputic uses\n\nBy Nyx\n\n\n((ooc disclaimer: this is written mostly from Modern Essentials: A Contemporary Guide to the Therapeutic use of essential oils isbn 978-1-937702-04-5))\n\nquick Definitions:\nEssential oils: substances created inside aromatic plants that are both volatile (they evaporate) and oil soluble (they easily mix with oils and fats).\n\nAromatic: Referring to plants that have an aroma.\n\nTopical Application: Placing an essential oil directly on the skin or other surface of the body.\n\nAromatic Application: Often referred to as aromatherapy, aromatic application refers to inhaling an essential oil or it's aroma.\n\nInternal Application:\nInternalizing an essential oil, typically through the mouth.\n\nCarrier oil: A carrier oil refers to a vegetable oil, wax, fat, or other oil that an essential oil is mixed with. The carrier oil \"carries\" the essential oil and dilutes\n\nit so it's effects can be spread over a larger area.\n\nNeat: refers to applying an essential oil by itself, without diluting it in a carrier oil.\n\nDilution: refers to mixing an essential oil in a carrier oil so it's effects are not as\n\nconcentrated in one area.\n\nVolatile: a substance that will evaporate when exposed to air is referred to as volatile. Volatile substances are typically light substances with small parts.\n\nSingle oil: A single oil is one type of oil by itself.\n\nOil Blend: An oil blend is a mixture of two or more single oils.\n\nTable of Content\n\nWhat are essential oils?--------see p.9\n\nTopical application p.13\n\nAromatic p.28\n\nOils p.\n\nWhat are Essential oils? Essential oils are the volatile liquids that are distilled from plants (including their respective parts such as seeds, bark, leaves, stems, roots, flowrs, fruit, etc.). One of the factors that determines the purity and\n\ntherapeutic value of an oil is it's constituents. These constituents can be affected by a vast number of variables: the part(s) of the plant from which the oil was produced, soil condition, fertilizer, geographic region, climate, altittude, harvest season\n\nand method, and distillation process. For example common thyme produces several different effects depending on the conditions of it's growth, climate, and altitude. If it is distilled during mid summer or late fall, the oil with be more caustic or\n\nirrritating to the skin. Low pressure and low temperature are key to maintaining the purity, the ultimate fragrance, and the therapeutic value of the oil.\n\nTopical Application\n\nTopical application is the process of placing an essential oil on the skin, hair, mouth, teeth, nails, or membrances of the body. Applying eddential oils directly can sometimes be harmful  so usage of a carrier oil is needed\n\noften carrier oils are coconut oil, almond oil, olive oil, jojoba oil, etc. Several topical application methods are outlined below.\n\nDirect application:\nTo achieve best results use 1-3 drops of oil.\n\ntips\n\n- The feet are the second fastest area of the body to absorb ouls because of the large pores. other quick absorbing areas include behind the ears and on the wrists.\n\n- When massaging a large area of the body, always dilute the oils by 15% to 30% with coconut oil.\n\n- When applying to infants or small children always dilute oils using 1-3 drops of the oil and about a spoon full of coconut oil.\n\n- use caution when creating blends. oil parts can be altered and cause undesirable reactions.\n\n- Layering individual oils is preferred over mixing your own blends for topical use. Layering refers to the process of applying one oil\n\n, rubbing it in, and then applying another oil. There is no need to wait more than a couple of seconds between each oil.\n\nMassage:\nTo create a simple massage oil that includes the benefits of oils, combine 3-10 drops of your oil\n\nwith a full spoon of coconut oil. apply a small amount of this mixture on the massage location and massage it into the skin using light to medium-light strokes of the hand of fingers.\n\nContact point:\nIs a simple method of appying oils to contact points in the feet or hands. A series of hand rotation movements at those control points create a vibrational healing energy that carries the oils along the pathways. The oils either help remove\n\nany bloackage along the pathways or travel the length of the pathways to benefit the particular organ. Refer to hand and foot charts on the following pages for more details.\n\nCompresses:\nBasin. Fill a wash basin with two buckets of hot or cold water, and\n\nadd the desired oils. stir the water viforously; then lay a towel on top of the water. Since the oils will float to the top, the towel will absorb the oils with the water. after the towel is completely saturated, wring out excess water, and place the\n\ntowel over the area needing the compress. for a hot compress, cover with a dry towl and a hote water bottle. For a cold compress, cover with water treated leather. finally, put another towel on top, and leave for as long as possible (1-2 hours)\n\nMassage. Apply a hot, wet towl and then a dry towel on top of an already massages area. The moist heat will force the oils deeper into the tissues of the body.\n\nBaths:\nbathwater. begin by adding 3-6 drops of the oil to the bathwater\n\nwhilst filling the tub. soak for 15 minutes.\n\nSoaps. during the creation process add 3-6 drops to 1/2 an oz of the base.\n\nCharts\nhttp://i58.photobucket.com/albums/g261/senactra/20160712_161828.jpg\n\nhttp://i58.photobucket.com/albums/g261/senactra/20160712_161856.jpg\n\nhttp://i58.photobucket.com/albums/g261/senactra/20160712_161847.jpg\n\nFor those that would wish to make blends themselves a chart for you.\n\nhttp://i58.photobucket.com/albums/g261/senactra/20160712_161948.jpg\n\nAromatic\n\nDirect inhalation: is the simplest way to inhale the aroma of an oil. hold bottle under nose and breath alternatively place 1-2 drops in your hands and cup over nose and mouth before breathing.\n\nOils:\n\nhttp://doterra.com/US/en/pl/singleoils\n\nhttp://www.sustainablebabysteps.com/uses-for-essential-oils.html", ""),
        StaticBook("Missive", "§bTailesin", "-------------------           Missive\n------------------=\n\n~By Artorius Thannius, Exalted Spellcrafter, Archon of Rivel\n\nMissive to the People - The Revolt\n\nThe Circle of Governance would like to inform all citizens of the Enlightened Caste that the city is now back under control; the revolt of the lesser castes has been suppressed.\n\nI would like to formally dispel the rumours that this revolt was led by one of the orc beasts. It is laughable that a beast of burden could conceive of a plan of revolt, instruct the lesser caste in the use of tools of labour as weapons,\n\nand lead the lesser caste up in arms against the Enlightened. It is obvious that this must have been a coup attempted by on of the enlightened, and we will spare no expense in hunting down the leader of this insurrection and reeducating them.\n\nI also wish to assure the enlightened people of Rivel that the recent mass euthanasia of the orc beasts is to prevent the spread of disease amongst them, not, as some had the lack of wisdom to suggest, to prevent further revolt.\n\nOrcs do not think, this is a clearly established fact. They are simple beats possessing no capacity for higher thought nor magical skill.\n\nLikewise, the recent introduction of reorientation of members of the lesser caste via mental magic is simply to improve their efficiency and well being.\n\nThe idea that we are attempting to turn them into 'drones' is equally unfounded, and I would request such rumours are ceased; we do not wish to agitate the Lesser Caste and reduce the efficiency of their service to us.", ""),
        StaticBook("Unison", "§bShagarath", "-_~_-\n          Unison\n        -__~__-\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWritten by Shagarath\n\n9th of The First Seed, 1580\n\nThe past few weeks has been a busy one, for the sighting of an orc in the grove was well discussed among animal life around. The message spread and spread of an... appointment... a deal struck between druid and shaman.\n\nA Shaman and Druids strode into the Savannah Grove, a meeting of sorts between two rivaling groups for some time. The meeting was one of understanding and peace between the two group\n\nas the Warlock Shagarath began to explain the common ground between the two groups. In great trust, He called upon the spirits to show the binding unison of the groups and the Spirits answered.\n\nQiouvor, Lesser Spirit of Trees was summoned by his master Freygoth. He appeared in his avatar: an Ent. A light flash shone, and soon the goliath of leaf and bark stood before them.\n\n[!] The image of an Ent, a Goliath being of bark and leaf is drawn in great detail. The figure appearing aged, leaning on a tree-sized cane.\n\nThe spirit then inquired from the shaman, in the Spirit Tongue of Old Blah.“Why did you call for my. Why was I summoned?” Followed by “Do you wish me to kill these?”\n\nPointing at the children of the forest. The shaman then quickly shouted in reply, putting emphasis on the aspect of learning. For that was the purpose for which he was summoned. To teach… to make understand the ways of the spirits.\n\n“I have… communed with… your companions..” Qiouvor spoke in common tongue, looking upon the trees of the grove. A halfing named Jeremiah spoke “Wha’d they tell ya?”\n\n“We will… work in… unison…” Qiouvor spoke as it looked upon the group\n\nThe spirit then disappeard as it came, leaving but the two roots that were his legs. From then shone a bright emerald hue. For in each of these two roots… seeds were placed.\n\n“A gift…”\n“A gift from spirits….to aspects”\nShagarath spoke, offering one to the Druids, keeping one for the Shamans of the Angathgûl.\n\nA gift was given, and the branch of an alliance was received.\n\nA branch of alliance is once again shared upon the two groups, knowing full well the common ground they stand upon, and in hopes to flourish together rather than tear each other apart.\n\nUpon departing, the two groups blessed one another:\n“Aspects guide you, Shagarath” Spoke Oo’ri\n“Afar-Ilzgûl, Lûp-Ilzgûl.” Shagarath replied.\n\n\n\nFear the Spirits, Praise the Spirits", ""),
        StaticBook("Legend of Ents", "§bYelfir Everna", "Legend of the Ents\n\nTranscribed by Hierophant Arik, the Song Druid\n\nThe Ents are mysterious creatures that were first found within the forests of Aegis. Little is known about them, but they are often found near\n--\n\nsacred groves or ancient trees, keeping them protected and free of disruption. But the tale of the first Ent is interesting in itself. \n\nIt is said that the Aspects granted ancient and wonderful power to a certain tree older than time itself.\n\nThis tree, far wiser than the entire forest, yearned to protect his kind, but could not do much from within the confines of his roots. So, he waited for the stars to align, and produced a seed of gold, and let it fall to the foot of his gargantan trunk --\n\n--to sprout.\n\nThrough the course of many moon, this seed grew until it became a tree half as large as its father. However, it found that its roots were not bound in the soil, and so it walked. It roamed the forest,\n\nfinding purpose under the gentle guidance of the Eldertree.  The Ent soon found humans attempting to fell an ancient oak, which cried out in agony. Letting out a roar, the great being assumed its role as guardian of the forest,\n\nand tore through the men with unforgiving vengeance. And such is the story of the first Ent.", ""),
        StaticBook("Books - High Elf", "§b[Apple Druid] Leyun Kameki", "The following books come from The Eternal Library in Tahn'siol\n-Druidology, by Respiren\n-Wolf Druids Tome, by The Wolf Druid\n-The Aspect Tomes, by then Dedicant Elizabeth\n-Elinor's Trail, by then Grand Druid Verden\n\n-The Fundamentals of Alchemy, copied by Rosso\n-Alchemy Recipes, copies by Rosso\n-Elements of Alchemy,\ncopied by Rosso\n\nI ordered the copies of these books for two reasons, one to spend knowledge to the world and, two to\n\nGet some of our lost books where they belong.\n\nIf there are any other books you want in regards to nature or the world let me know.\nAlso they looking for any book of value, so if you have any spare copies of our books\n\nplease send it to me by my things in the grove ((x=331, y=111 z=-794)).\n\nIf there is anything else let me know.\n\n-Mist Druid Earendur", ""),
        StaticBook("Fe Ari Fleyja", "OwynMorin", "*The pages below are covered in neatly written plain text. It would seem as if the letters were written with a simple charcoal pencil.*\n\n     Journal of the Eagle\n           Property of:\n        Ruari of Orvar\n\n\"I see as Argal, his gaze and mine, as one.\"\n\n9th of Malin's Welcome,\n1562\n\nI start my journey from the towering pines of the new Grove. I will venture outwards and seek a suitable place to survive for the three days I have been given. These lands are unfamiliar to me, but their type does not differ-\n\nmuch from home. A challenge.\n\n*Below the entry would be a small drawing of an Eagle, it's wings spread over the backdrop of clouds and sky. Pigments would give the drawing colour.\n\nEntry 2\n\nI have stumbled upon a small wooded area. There-\n\nappears to be a stream of fresh water nearby. I am quite far from the grove by now, perhaps half a days travel. This seems a good place to stop.\n\nIf I am quick, I can erect some form of shelter and stave off the cold. If nothing ill happens, perhaps I-\n\nwill stay here for the full three days. A thought.\n\n*As the text ends, another picture comes into view. This time portraying a thick bolder, five times the size of a man. Beside it a small stream flows down steep cliff faces and many dozen trees border-\n\n*-the drawing, as if depicting a clearing.*\n\nEntry 3\n\nI have woven some vines and leaves together with fallen branches, creating a nice thick roof for my shelter. The rocks form a sort of V shape, which I can easily cover above with my creation. The-\n\npotato given to me has yet to be touched, as I am only feeling slightly famished. Perhaps next I will seek out some sharpened rocks and sharpen myself some spears. Skildir may not be here, but I do not need rely on it's steel to put food in my belly.\n\nEntry 4\n\nI have wittled down three sturdy spears, their points sharp enough to do the job. Next, I have masked my scent with the aid of some mud and droppings nearby. Elk droppings, if my senses are not failing me. Perhaps the beast is nearby. Until-\n\nthen, I will have to rely on the handful of wild onions I managed to scavenge. Not a bad treat.\n\nAmong my few possessions I packed when coming out here are the two idols of Cerridwen and Cernunnos that I aquired. I use them to meditate and communicate-\n\nwith the life around me. I only hope that the mother and father can hear me, and know that I am worthy of the bounties I rely on to survive. I have bound together a small wreath, for Cerridwen, as thank for the onions I took.\n\n*A drawing of the wreath is-\n\n*promptly displayed below, comprised of bound together evergreen branches. At the center is a small pink flower, nestled comfortably in the lower part of the wreath.*\n\nEntry 5\n\nMy hunt yielded nothing this day. I managed to-\n\ntrack the beast that left the droppings a slight ways from the clearing before I lost the trail. Gorund's great eye now descends on the Horizon, and night comes. May the gods and the aspects keep me safe from the evils that lurk in the dark.\n\n10th of Malin's Welcome,\n1562\n\nEntry 1\n\nI heard something lumbering in the forest. Not sure what. Could have been an Ufiik, perhaps even a Jotuun. Wouldn't fadir have loved to see our giant-kin ancestors once again? Nonetheless, I-\n\ndid not lay any eye on the disturbance, but sat up half the night on alert. I doubt these spears would protect me half as good as Skildir.\n\nEntry 2\n\nDawn has settled and Gorund's gaze shines on the world once again. I am ready to resume my hunt. May-\n\nCernunnos guide me hand in this endeavor, and may Beira bless me with her presence.\n\n*A drawing of Ruari, his usual garb covered in bland leathers and furs, would follow. His attention seemed to be focused to the right of the page, through a pair of-\n\n*towering pines, his makeshift spear held up in alert.*\n\nEntry 3 \n\nSuccess. I picked up on the beasts trail once again, about three miles away from where I made camp. It fought a hard battle, but in the end I am the victor. I made sure to end-\n\nit's suffering with a quick stroke. While I gave the proper thanks to Cernunnos for his assistance, I have also given offering to Beira and her children. Some Druii may frown that I hold belief in my own gods alongside the Aspects, but I will not-\n\nfalter. Both are beings I respect and serve, now. No one is more important than the other.\n\nEntry 4\n\nI have returned to my camp with some of the Elk's meat. A pack of wolves drove me off of my prey, I had known them to be stalking the same as I did.-\n\nIt was too dangerous to stay and finish using the beast, but I am sure the pack will enjoy the meal they have found. Perhaps I will visit it's resting place before I leave and retrieve it's remains.\n\nSivvee could use a new skull for ritual.\n\nEntry 5\n\nMy fire roars, my belly full. Dusk descends upon the realm of man. I will sleep easy this night.\n\n*A drawing of the moon is present, several dozen stars flanking it's pale visage.*\n\n11th of Malin's Welcome,\n1562\n\nEntry 1\n\nWhen I awoke, I was greeted by a familiar face. Fylkir sat perched upon a nearby pine, gazing down at me. I sense he may have been hurt, or perhaps just disappointed I did not-\n\ninvite him along. I told him that Muildir wanted me to survive in the nature, on my own accord. He may understand.\n\nI will allow him to stay, if he wishes, but will accept no aid from my avian friend.\n\n*Below is yet another drawing, this time of-\n\na crowned eagle perched atop a thick branch, it's intense orangish eyes staring back out from the book. Atop it's head stuck out several thick feathers, a feature which gave it it's namesake. It's chest feathers were black and white, while it's wingspan-\n\nwas covered in bristled feathers, each various shadings of brown.*\n\nEntry 2 \n\nI collected enough meat from the Elk to feed me for more than a week, so food is no longer an issue. I have decided to dedicate the rest of this day to meditation.\n\nTo feel the-\n\nthrows of nature. To suck in the fresh air. When one sits, relaxes, and takes in the wild around them. They never feel more alive.\n\nEntry 3\n\nThe sky has filled with a tinge of orange. Gorund's eye will be setting soon.\n\nBefore it does, I think I will-\n\ndye decorate Fylkir's tail feathers, if he lets me. When Jeremiah gave me the task to seek out a companion, he gave me a gift he will never know. I had been avoiding finding another Eagle, one I could truly bond with, because of what happened to Danslr.-\n\nI think Fylkir and I will be good friends.\n\nOrvar bond closest with the Eagle. Argal and his kin are as one with us, for we return the protection he gave us so long ago.\n\nThe final night will set, soon, and I find I have no more will to write in these-\n\npages. I will go and make the most of the rest of my time out here.\n\n*A drawing of a gargantuan eagle is seeped into the remaining blank page, it's feet dug into the earthly ground beneath. It's wingspan clearly dominated the treeline around it,-\n\nappearing as if a hundred times larger. Several small humanoid figures huddled at it's feet, while still more seemed to be climbing down off of it's back.*\n\n\"Fleyja sevlla, fleyja fjarri.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("A Dream", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "Dream\n-L. Thickfeet\n*scribed from old texts*\n\n\"Take this seed and find the right place. You will know it when you find it. Plant it without any help from any source. Meditate until it grows tall. Do not return until it is done.\"\n\nThis was my fifth task as a Druid dedicant.\n\nWhat kind of task is that for a hobbit of my age? Some of the very saplings I remember as a child still are barely able to bare fruit. This was he day I realized the Druids were truly masochists.\n\nWhat had I gotten myself into?\n\nSo, of course, without much hesitation I gathered just a few things. I may or may not have emptied the entire stock of Elvish lembas bread that I have ever owned. I carried with me enough pipe weed for the next century\n\na couple of water skins. and just the seed I needed to plant.\n\nI set off early morning to the north hoping to find a quiet place to start the task that would probably be the end of me. A few days past when I found myself crossing the Orcish desert.\n\nI knew full well the danger of someone of my stature entering lands of danger, however I took great care to only about at night.\n\nI found a rather easy to climb rocky hill that would allow for a much larger view of the land.\n\nI made my way up and as expected I gained a grand view. At night the desert can be a very beautiful place. The moon at its fullest sent its brightest light that shimmered off the sand dunes below.\n\nI should remember to appreciate all the world offers\n\nAfter a few moments and realizing there was no greener lands to be found nearby, I turned to leave and tripped over myself. Before I knew it I had spilled most of the contents of my bag. Lost in a sea of sand is exactly what I wanted.\n\nThere I was, shaking sand off of food and luxuries when I realized the seed had gone missing. The panic that rose in me was like no other as I hurriedly checked the hill for it.\n\nA single seed in an ocean of death. Fantastic.\n\nI started digging into the sand to see if it had simply been covered and found something intriguing.\n\nDirt.\n\nIt was just under a short layer of sand and a small bit of stones.\n\nI am no expert on dirt but I was truly astounded to find it in such a barren place as that. Time passed and I had forfeited any chance of finding the Archdruid's gift.\n\nI had failed so soon into my journey that I frustratingly fell back and fell\n\nasleep to the stars.\n\nI woke early dawn with a sudden jolt. Once my old eyes had cleared I stared blankly at the stick in the ground.\n\nTime passed and I began to realize that I was staring at a sapling. Had that damned thing been there\n\nthe entire time? Had I finally gone blind? Did I possibly reveal it in my seed searching in panic?\n\nIt couldn't possibly... It couldn't possibly have been the very seed I was sent to plant. I couldn't have slept for that many days without some sort\n\nof hunger!\n\nAt the very least in my old age I'd have a sore back by now.\n\nNo such luck.\n\nAs if the situation hadn't been any more wild, I decided the best course of action was to do what my trachers had always\n\ndictated. I meditated.\n\nSee what I mean?\n\nI meditated next to the impossibly growing and arguably the thirstiest sapling I would ever encounter. I cleared my mind as usual, began to control my breathing, and felt the regular soothing calm that\n\naccompanied me each time.\n\nExcept this time something had changed. I felt as if I had opened my eyes. Had I ever used my eyes before?\n\nI couldn't see anything but darkness.\n\nI was completely relaxed yet aware of all the nothing that surrounded me. I was everywhere and nowhere and just as I started to analyze the situation a faint glowed appeared and grew all around me.\n\nI then realized how cold I had been. Had I ever\n\nfelt coldness on my skin before? The darkness faded swiftly and all was engulfed in light and pure warmth.\n\nI then felt a stirring around me.\n\nA wind blew softly between my hands and a long string of orbed lights fluttered and danced before me.\n\nWhen did I learn to feel something like the wind? My focus changed and I looked around to see that I was now sitting in an open meadow.\n\nThick soft grass leaned to-and-fro and wild flowers of every colour bloomed in the moonlight. When had I ever\n\nseen colour before? The wind picked up lovely scents and I was no aware of my sense of smell. Had I ever smelled ever before in my life?\n\nAs if trying to tell me not to waste time, the wind picked me up and I found myself up on my feet. I realized\n\nhow it felt to use my legs.\n\nWhen did I obtain a body?\n\nI could have sworn that I had grown quite a bit. I could have been as tall as a human now. Maybe I was always that tall?\n\nI had no memory of it.\n\nThe orbs spun around me and then headed onward. They were calling to me. I heard faint whispers but could not understand them.\n\nWhen did I ever have hearing?\n\nI walked for an age.\n\nThe meadows stretched endlessly and the night lasted for an eternity.\n\nIt was an ocean of life.\n\nAfter a while I began to notice a rather large glow in the distance. No matter\n\nhow I looked at it, I could not get a clear sight. Something inside of me told me 'No.'\n\nIt was wrong and shouldn't be there.\n\nThe orb almost reacted to my thought and swiftly flew through me. They had gone but I felt a strange\n\nresence within. \n\nThey were with me now.\n\nI felt myself grow taller then. Was I as towering as an Orc? Maybe taller than a castle wall. I couldn't remember how large that was.\n\nI ran.\n\nI sprinted towards the blur in a rage. I ran for hours, days, months, years towards the very thing that must leave this place. The blur remained and I could not pick out what its figure was. It was greater than I could have imagined but it had to leave!\n\nThere I was before it. It was monstrous and mysterious. The blur remained and I could not touch it. Did I even try or did fear hold my hands?\n\nI could not remember how to use my hands.\n\nA great anger boiled and I screamed at it to leave. It not\n\nbelong here. This was my world and it was not welcome. Nothing happened and my anger turned to rage. A single orb fled my body and went deep into the ground.\n\nI believe the earth itself responded to my rage.\n\nThe foundations of the world were shaking. And so was I.\n\nWhy was this thing here? Why didn't it just go home? It never asked to be in this place and I knew it was going to hurt everything around it. It had to leave immediately!\n\nAnd endless stream of fury erupted from within and the earth ripped apart. It was as if a hand of the land itself reached out and pulled the phased phenomenon deep into the reaches of the world. It had gone and the land swallowed it whole.\n\nI fell back into the grass but felt no weight. The flora beneath me cradled me down. I felt the roots of the flowers hug me and I was pulled deeper and deeper into a dream. I noticed that I had grown but not in a way I felt before.\n\nGone was my hearing, my feeling, taste, smell, and my sight.\n\nWhat then was I?\n\nI was everywhere.\n\nAll at once.\n\nI had grown again.\n\nI was at peace.\n\nSunlight touched my skin softly and the warmth woke me smoothly. I sat up and recognized the salty scent of the desert. I rustled my hands and felt the soft dewy grass beneath me. Startled I was at the conflicting feelings I stood up in shock.\n\nBefore me stood a fully grown oak wood tree. Beneath me was an area of healthy grasses and flowers. Nearby as a small pool of water.\n\nSurrounding all of this beauty was a barren and lifeless desert.\n\nI ran to the water and splashed my face. What had happened and how long had I been asleep? Nothing about anything made sense and I know without a doubt that nobody would ever believe this story.\n\nI then headed to the grove of the Druids.\n\nI hoped I hadn't slept through the end of the world.\n\nOr lost my mind in the process.", ""),
        StaticBook("1st Explanation", "§bDouglas", "Learnings on \"The\nMyth of the Great\nWar\" by Douglas\nHolger, Dedicant of\nZa'Kabar, written on\nthe 10th of the Amber Cold, 1571.\n\n*something was\nscribbled on the\nfront* No\nreferences this time;\nas this is all\n\npersonal knowledge\nand opinion, and\nexplains what I have\nlearnt. \n\nThe Myth of the\nGreat War is, with all\ndue respect, a\nglorified piece of\nliterature meant for\nthose that are\nspoon-fed myths full\nof hyperboles.\n\nHowever, to a Druid\nor studious Dedicant,\nthey can see a fable\nbeneath the flattery;\na topaz within the\ngold. \n\nThe purpose of this\npiece of literature\nwas to sketch out a\nframe for the\nbeginnings of the\nDruid Order; and\n\nthus, their reason of\nexistence. The story\ntells simply of two\nempires at war, with\nmages on both sides\nwith dangerous void\nmagic fought each\nother, but was dumb\nenough not to aim\nproperly and\nwrecked the forests,\nwrecked some races\nplus a couple of\n\nislands. Their\naffiliations are not\nrelevant currently,\nbut will be touched\non later. Now their\nlack of aim is not only\nto laugh and scoff\nat, but to learn from.\nOften we hear the\ngreat feats of\nmassive infernos and\nrolling thunder by mages, but do the\n\nmyths account for\nthe collateral\ndamage? No, but\nstrangely this myth\ndoes. The nature of\nmages is the path of\nknowledge; and\nthrough that they\nunderstand by\nopening up their\nsenses and\ncomprehending the\ngreat Void, and draw\n\ntheir power from the\npowerful and perfect\nentropic disorder;\nthey draw on this\ndisorder with mana\nanchors and summon\nthem into the mortal\nrealm. Now with the\nstory's antagonists\ndown, let's move on\nto the next point.\n\nWith this chaos\n\nunfolding, the\nGoddess of Nature\nwas awakened from\nher slumber and sent\na vision to a single\nhuman, and she was\nthe first Grand Druid.\nFirstly, the\nrepresentation of\nNature as a Goddess\nis too damn clich'd\n(Could be the Green\nLady, but the Horned\n\nMan was not\npresent.), but her\nactions as a powerful\nbeing spoke\nsomething. She was in\nslumber. Why not\nroam around,\ngathering followers\nin the mortal realm?\nOne of my guesses is\nthat it represented\nthe neutrality of\nNature and the flow\n\nof balance within it;\nindicating the nature\nof the Druii in turn\nas well. So when the\nbalance (nature) was\nbeing terrorized,\nthere must be\nintervention.\n\nWith the birth of the\nfirst Grand Druid, the\nvision showed her the\nneed to found a\n\norder that protected\nthe balance in the\nstead of the\nGoddess. While I like\nto point out that this\nshowed that Druii\ncommune to the\nAspects with visions\n(such as during their\nattunement), it was\nthe founding of the\nDruidic Order, and\nthey together turned\n\nthe mages into\ngrazing beasts.\nWhile I would like to\npoint out that this\nmyth just described\nshapeshifting \ninto animals, I would\nprefer not to be kicked\nin the posterior by\nmy guide, and instead\npoint out that it\nshowed an important\naspect of Druidism;\n\nand that is only\nthrough unity that\nthe Druii show their\nmight. If you think\nabout it, Nature could\nhave simply blessed a\nchampion to enforce\nthe balance, and or\nmade a Order after\nthe chaos was\nsettles by said\nchampion; but instead\nit showed an Order\n\nworking cohesively to\nkeep the balance and\neliminate disorder.\n\nThen to the very\nimportant point,\nrepresented by this\npitiable quote: \"Once\nthe former mages -\nnow unable to fathom\nthe concept of good\nversus evil -\nslithered, bounded,\n\nloped, and crawled\noff into their ruined\nhabitats.\" While it\nlooks like a sentence\ndemeaning aimless\nmages, anyone within\nthe Order would have\nlaughed as well as\nunderstand it's hidden meaning. Originally the\nmages were fighting\nbased on affiliation;\n\ngood vs evil. Usually\nthe good side wins,\nbut now they are\ngrazing like animals?\nSo who won? As the\nmyth pointed out\nearlier, the neutrality\nof nature. Mages are\ndescribed here as\nselfish and teeming\nfor their own\ndesires; hurting\nothers even if they\n\nwere they shiny good\nside. Both parties\nwere depicted to\ncause enough chaos\nto dwarf a Halfing's\nbirthday party, but\nsome would recognize\na reference to the\nVoidal magic they\nwielded. See, the Void\ndoes not cater to\nonly one side; its\npower is distributed\n\nto those who\nunderstand it's\nproperties. Whether\nyou are good or evil,\nyou still need to be\nsupervised when\nwielding a dangerous\ntoy. Thus gleaning\nboth the nature of\nthe Void and the\nimportance of\ndiscipline within the\nDruii ranks; the Void\n\nliterally represents\ndisorder, drawing\nfrom it and using it in\nthe realm will tilt the\nbalance; it grows\nrampant if not\nstopped; thus you\nneed to be disciplined\nnot to do the same as\na Druid and\nrecognize anything\nthat disrupts the\nbalance at a glance.\n\nThis was represented\nby the words \"fathom\nthe concept of good\nversus evil\" and\n\"slithered,\nbounded...into their\nruined habitats\".\nBeing neutral will\nalways guide a way of\nrationality and\nnature, and will\nalways provide\nsafety unlike the\n\nanimals whose past\nselves destroyed\ntheir homes.\n\nSo Voidal magic was\ndeemed a threat and\nwhatever the mages\ncaused, Druii\nrepaired. They even\ninterfered with the\nGreat War, 'good and\nevil' fighting again,\nbut the Druii made\n\nsure that none won;\nno matter how close to victory.\nAnother point of\nthe nature of\nneutrality, but\nshowed the authority\nof the Druii then with\ntheir disallowance of\na whole empire from\nclaiming victory. A\nsubtle hint that the\nDruii and Mages will\n\nnever get along as\ntime passes was\ndropped as well,\nprobably a reminder\nto a Druid and a\nwarning for a\nDedicant. Now enemies\nwith both good and\nevil, the Druids stand\nto be known as an\nOrder that whose\nbeliefs are not to be\ntrifled with; if they\n\nare challenged, they\nsuffer the poor\nmisfortune of the\nwarring mages. So\nwhat did you learn\nthrough my eyes?\nHopefully something\nyou perhaps didn't\nsee yourself, or saw\nsomething I didn't\nsee. If that's the\ncase, find me and tell\nme; I always seek to\n\nbe wrong than right.\nBut there's one thing\nthat the myth\ndescribes that we\ncan all settle on;\nmages cannot aim at\nanything unless it\nwas a donkey's arse.\n\n*you find another\nnote, this time\nevidently the writer\nhad a blunt quill. In\n\nthicker writing it\nread* Don't kill/shun\nme for calling the\nbook over-glorified.\nPlease.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Dryad's Tale", "§bToriel", "The Dryad's Tale\n\n              By Toriel\n\n\nOnce, there was a Dryad.\n\nNo, no. That will not do.\n\nDryads? There was Once. Once, by all standards,\n\nof which for Dryads are there many, was not a very good Dryad. She often forgot to tend to her Tree, choosing instead to frolic in the glades of the forest with the many animals that dwelt within - nearly all of them friendly, except for the Rocklice, who\n\nwas very selfish and liked to stay on his own inside of his cave.\n\nOnce - the Dryad, that is - decided one day that her tree was not colorful enough, and thought \"I should go get some ribbons for my tree!\" , so off she went on her new quest for\n\ncolourful strips of cloth.\n\nOnce travelled across many leagues and miles of Anthos searching for a tailor with ribbons most beautiful and most colourful, but none caught her eye.\n\nBeginning to feel like her journey was in vain, she decided to turn back and return to her tree.\n\nHowever, fate had other plans in store for her. \"Psst!\" she hears, off down a darkened alleyway. Being a curious Dryad,\n\nOnce decided to investigate. She stepped cautiously into the dark path, peering into the murky gloom ahead. She spots a pair of eyes in the dark, peering back at her. \"I see yer lookin' for something. I'm sure I can cut a deal wid ya.\" says the\n\nstranger, tipping his hat at her politely, although his grin unnerved Once slightly. \"W-well... i'm looking for some beautiful ribbon...\" she replies in a nervous tone, her hands clasped behind her back. \"Well... ah believe I 'ave just what yer-\n\nlookin' fer...\" the stranger replies, as he reaches inside his long, heavy cloak. \n\nWith a flick of his hand, he pulls out a ribbon of the most beautiful, purest cloth you could ever imagine. It seemed to shift from vibrant scarlet to chilling\n\nsapphire-blue, to verdant forest-canopy green... and back again! It seemed to glow in the gloom, the rainbow of light reflecting in Once's Greedy gaze. \"I want it!\"  she exclaims, reaching for it. The stranger withdraws his hand and shakes his head.\n\n\"Nu-uh. We gotta make a deal, lass.\"  he says, nodding once. \"I'll trade it fer somethin' of equal value. 'ow's that sound?\" he proposes, flashing his too-white grin oncemore. Once gulps, and thinks to herself - \"I don't have anything of equal\n\n value to this! He's getting a terrible deal! Ha, he's silly!\". She grins back, and nods. \"Deal!\"  she exclaims, reaching her hand forwards, the stranger eagerly shaking her hand. He hands her the cloth and steps back into the gloom, fading into\n\nthe murky darkness, disappearing in a matter of moments, only his white grin visible for a while until that too disappears.\n\n\nOnce feels a sinking feeling in her stomach. Like something is wrong, but her body\n\ndoesn't quite know what. She clutches the ribbon and swiftly walks away, returning to her tree with a quickened stride and nervous looks over her shoulder.\n\n\nOnce returned to her Tree.\n\n\n\n  Her Tree was dead.\n\n The limbs of the tree were blackened and dead, naked of leaves, which are strewn across the forest\n\nfloor like discarded toys, wilted and brown. \nA soft breeze blows through the bare branches, and Once hears a pained groan as it sways in the breeze. \n\nTears come to her eyes as she collapses onto her knees.\n\n\n\"Why!?\"  she exclaims, tears flowing down her cheeks in streams as she sobs quietly.\n\nWhy had they taken her tree?\n\nThe only thing that equalled her greed was her love for her tree,\n\nWhich she took oh so for granted.\n\n\"Do not, in your eagerness to improve and better yourself, forget who and what made your existence possible. Without them, you are nothing.\"\n\n           - Toriel", ""),
        StaticBook("Practitioners", "§bGrungron Irongut", "Dark Arts\n      Practitioners\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=- Gareth\nAsher\nCorvo\nStella\nNicholas\nArtius\nFaenor\nMithrion\nAsarielle\nAdniel", ""),
        StaticBook("Cheza's Smok Mix", "§b[Mouse Druid] Aydalyn", "~The Creation o Cheza`s Smoking mix~\n\n10th, Snows Maiden 1550\nScribed by Dedicant Dlûn the Elder\n\nEffects (When made Correctly):\n-Removal of Headache\n-Relaxation of the Mind\n\nIngredients:\n-Frost Vine ~Cold to the Touch\n-Shade Leaf ~Numbing effect upon Touch\n-Sage Dust (Optional) ~Speeds up the Effects\n\nAddition: Blessing\n\nThe creation of this herbal mix:\n\n~ Both dried herbs are to be blessed individually\n\n((A roll determines succession:\n< 10 enhanced bad features\n\n11-15 nothing\n16-20 removal of numbness of shade leaf))\n\n~ Both dried herbs are put in a mortar, to be grinded into a fine mixture, after which this mix is once more blessed\n\n((A roll determines succession:\n< 10 enhanced bad features\n11-15 nothing\n16-20 removal of numbness of shade leaf))\n\n~ This blessed mixture is then optionally covered with a small amount of Sage Dust to speed up the Effects. This mixture is put into a pipe and smoked.\n\n((A roll determines succession:\nThe effect is determined from previous rolls. Bad rolls result in a numbing potion, good rolls give an all-time High experience complete with hallucinations.))", ""),
        StaticBook("The Druid Guide", "§bYelfir Everna", "The Druid Guide\n\nTranscribed by Hierophant Callax, the Solstice Druid\n\nThe Order\n\nThe Druids are a group dedicated to preserving the balance of nature.\n\nAll Druids undergo years of learning and studying in order to establish a strong link with nature.\n\nAlthough some are known to walk the land, spreading the teachings of Druidism, the Order officially resides in their Grove.\n\n Despite the misconception that only Elves can be Druid, the Order is open to all races, as it welcomes all who wish to follow the way of the Aspects.\n\nThe Druids do not recognize those who proclaim to walk the way of the Druids--\n\nyet do not take the time to go through the studying. That is to say, only those who have been promoted to the status of Druid by one of the Guide rank or higher will be acknowledged for their wisdom.\n\nTotems\n\nAll of those who --\n\n--are full-fledged Druids possess a title, sometimes referred to as a totem.  This totem represents their history, their personality, their approach to life.\nIt is up to the Druid to identify themselves with an appropriate title.\n\nThe time at which a Dedicant becomes a Druid is up to both those higher in the Order and the Dedicant themselves.\n\nPowers\nIt is well known that Druids are some of the best healers in the land, yet the true extent of these powers is unknown.\n\n Druids have been known to bring people back from the brink of death and restore sight to the blind.\n\nThe Druids are deeply connected to nature, yet do not control nature, but serve it. \nTo try to have power over something without understanding--\n\n--is against their ways. It is more similar to asking nature for favours, and then doing nature favours in return. Though nature will sometimes do very large favours, if the situation calls for it.\n\nResponsibilities\nThe Druids pride themselves with being the keepers and protectors of Nature. They will allow the chopping down of a tree,  possibly even the removal of a forest, but in return they expect each tree to be replanted elsewhere.\n\nDruids are non-violent, and due to their long periods of studying in their libraries, are known to be fantastic sources for information. However, despite their non-violent tendencies, Druids will do whatever it takes to preserve the safety of Nature --\n\n-- when it is threatened, and will resort to fighting if the situation demands it.\nThe responsibilities of the Druids are vast. They teach their ways to all those willing, and sometimes unwilling, to listen,  serve Nature by adhering to the Natural Cyc\n\nand provide their healing hand to all land and creature which has been unnaturally harmed in some way. In oversight, the responsibilities of the Druids are innumerable and great.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Biography", "§bAeran", "A Biography of the\n          Song Druid\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWritten after talking to the Song Druids and others in the Order\n\nI did not probe the Song Druid for stories of his pre-Druidic Order days. From my handful of talks with him, he seemed to regret actions when he was young, going so far to no longer go by the name he was born with. He once mentioned that him joining the\n\nDruids was a way to seek pennance for his past, but has never gone deeper than that. \n\nHe joined the Order in Aegis, shortly after the Rose Druid Delfyr became Archdruid (which was near the end of Aegis, if my Druid history is accurate). This was a\n\ntime when the Order was still fleshing out it's identity, it's practices.\n\nThe Order was one a hunt for various Artifacts of Aegis, and did not pay much respect to even city walls. From all accounts, the Order used violence to seize artifacts that they\n\nfelt belonged to them.\n\nThe Song Druid, contrary to modern practices, was attuned at the beginning of his dedicancy. I have heard Dedicancy described as 'opening up a hole' and attunement as 'opening that hole up.'\n\nThe Song Druid\n\ndescribed his first attunement as 'making a hole,' so perhaps it is a similar process. From what I understand people who have completed Dedicancy don't seem to be any closer to the Aspects than someone who was never a Dedicant. Atleast, they have no extra\n\npowers or control, they are just more knowledgable. So I do not know the extent of this first attunement; The Song Druid did not dwell on it, and the only reason I know of this was because I found an old journal of his that mentioned this, which lead\n\nme to asking the Song Druid about it, which then lead to me hearing his life story. And writing this biography.\n\nThe Song Druid was mentored under Delfyr, where he spent time at a 'Sanctuary' Delfyr had made. There he read many\n\ntomes, discovered an affinity to music, and worked as a half Dedicant half Druid.\n\nSometime during this period he met a woman, whom he described as a 'beatiful dark elf' named Faeyin.\n\nShe possessed a magic which augmented the power of other magic\n\nusers around her. The Song Druid went on to stay in close contact with her throughout his Dedicancy, protecting her from others that wanted to exploit her powers.\n\nHis Dedicancy seemed to pass swiftly, completing all of his tasks quickly. Yet, he\n\ndecided to wait for the Aspects to show him a Grand Task, and did not decide one on a whim like so many do today.\n\nBefore any task appeared to him, the Descendants were forced to flee to the Verge, through a portal.\n\nAt this time, the Order was paying the consequences  of their mad hunt for 'Artifacts.' They were forced out of Laurelin, and there were very few left. The Song Druid was still a Dedicant, but on the Verge his Grand Task revealed itself to him.\n\nHe had found Faeyin, the woman with the powers, dying. Her magic was 'too powerful to be destroyed by death,' so it was forming into a crystal. Arik took the crystal after he death, and protected it. This became his Grand Task.\n\nHe implied that he was\n\nclose to Faeyin, although he called her a friend. In my discussions with him, he has only ever mentioned one relationship with a tree (to be discussed later), but never with any other Descendants. This leads me to believe he has put that to the side. It\n\nis only a guess, but perhaps the passing of this woman affected him more than he thought.\n\nHe made his way to the Druid's boat, presenting the crystal to the Fox Druid Valas, an Archdruid at the time. From there he was attuned the second\n\ntime, completing his connection to the Aspects.\n\nUpon arrival to Asulon, the Song Druid set off with the crystal and a far diferent totem; 'Soul Druid,' to reflect his Grand Task.\n\nHe went past the Caverns of\n\nMenocrss, away from all civilization. He stumbled upon a budding elder tree in the middle of a marsh, and began the construction of a small swamp, which became known as the 'Swamp of Song,' due to The Song Druid's singing.\n\nHe remained there for a time,\n\nprotecting the crystal from the various groups that wanted it.\n\nHe made occasional outings, helping to heal taint or discuss things with others in the Order. On one such outing he met a man named 'Lucas Black,' whom sought to take the crystal and use it\n\nfor himself. The Song Druid was having none of that, fought gainst the man, eventually causing the destruction of the conduit of power in their duel. He went unconscious as a result of this, and awake ot find Lucas Black gone, emptyhanded, which the Song\n\nDruid claims was quite a feet. I have searched the Cloud Temple library, aswell as the Druid library, but have found no mention of Lucas Black. From my understand, though, he was powerful.\n\nThe Song Druid returned to the Order at this time, finding\n\na Druid which he calls the 'Useless Druid Falco' (for he is the only Druid he ever forgot the totem of) appointed as an Archdruid. This new Archdruid was unaware of the practices of the Order, and had no regards for any others. This initiated the first\n\nsplit, with the Song Druid and the Rose Druid Delfyr creating the Cerridwen Circle, opposite to the main Order which was called the Cernunnos Circle.\n\nThe Cerridwen Circle set up home in the Swamp of Song, but it was clear that the Order did not have the\n\nnumbers to sustain a split; Both circles faded into inactivity.\n\nBut, one day, the Song Druid felt a draw to Dawn's Bakery in Salvus. Here he met no less than 8 other Druids, who felt the same draw. There they agreed the rejoin the Order, given a small\n\nisland to create a Grove.\n\nAround this time the Song Druid was tasked with investigating a disturbance at the roots of the great tree of Normandor. There, he found the roots extended into a bottomless pit of Void. In his infinite wisdom, the Song\n\nDruid decided the best way to fix this was to jump into the Void to speak to the oldest part of the tree.\n\nAfter becoming trapped in there for a while, and presumably fixing the disturbance, the Song Druid returned to the Grove in Salvus, where he found\n\ntaken by the Witch Queen of Salvus, our very own Scroll Druid Kyral.\n\nThis was long ago, in Asulon. She has attoned for her sins.\n\nBut she had burned down the Grove, pillaging it.\n\nThis caused the\n\nOrder to return to Elandriel, the forests by Normandor.\n\nHere he spent much time meditating, leading the Order.\n\nHere he told me a rather funny story of the Elves of Normandor; they had begun to felling the ancient trees in\n\ntheir forest, creating stone houses.\n\nThis thoroughly pissed the Song Druid off.\n\nHe went to the center of Normandor, and told the leaders that until they learned the error in their ways, they would be known as 'Human Elves.'\n\nAnd this name stuck. I remember hearing about this when I was young-- I would have been not even 30 Human years.\n\nAfter pissing off the Wood Elf leaders, the Order created a grove within the Mother Tree Ethanriel. And it was with this tree that the Song\n\nDruid bore a child.\n\nAn Ent, from my understanding. I do not know any specifics, and nor did the Song Druid, but he had an Ent with the Mother tree Ethanriel.\n\nFrom the end of Asulon to now, Vailor, he has served many roles in the Order, but\n\nhas not been as much of an influence on the Order as he was in Aegis and Asulon. He was a Head Teacher, has been an Archdruid a number of times, has taught many of even the greatest Druids, including Callax.\n\nThere is no doubt he is one of, if not the,\n\ngreatest Druids to have ever walked. He knows the best way to restore balance, he maintains a level headed approach when dealing with all Aspects of the Druids, and one of my biggest regrets is never being able to live up to what he thought I could be.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n~Aeran", ""),
        StaticBook("Voyage", "birdwhisperer", "Foreword\n\nThis book is originally from Asulon, and was first found on the Island of Armaluna, in the Bards College’s Library. It was rest- ored and brought to Anthos by Elorna Avern.\n\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~\n\nUnfurl the sails and off we go,\n\nDivine wind will gently blow,\n\nStirring waves t'rock us to and fro,\n\nGuiding us the stars that glow.\n\nShining brightly with the moon in tow,\n\nTowards the horizon, they'll come to show,\n\nA land where times ever slow,\n\nThat eases your heart and quells your woes,\n\nBut for now we rest in the deck below,\n\nWe'll reach paradise before you know!\n\n\n\n\n\n~Griffith du Mont, Grand Maestro", ""),
        StaticBook("Theatre Guide", "§bAzoth Hawksong", "FOREWORD\nIn this constantly changing world we currently exist in, the mundane day and labors of all reach a climax. It is here that people desire an escape. A new world. The theatre.\n\nA. Hawksong\nBedevere Theatre Owner\n\nACTING TIPS\n1. Be sure to say your lines out to the audience. Do not speak with your back to them or in a space where your voice cannot carry.\n2. Always speak loudly, but not enough to deafen those attending. (/rp)\n\n3. When backstage, always keep out of sight and silent. (/sneak)\n4. Always strive to do more than the line tells you. Be dramatic, you're on stage! But don't butcher the scene or ruin the tone.\n5. Know some will be rude. Just accept it and move on.\n\n6. Have fun! You may be new or a veteran, but you will never be perfect. But with this company, you'll come as close as you can.\n\nTHEATRE PROCEDURES\n\n1. Actors are paid 20 minas per performance, regardless of role.\n2. Most shows at the Bedevere Theatre are colorblind. This means that we do not discriminate race per role. You a dwarf and want to play an elf? Go for it!\n\n3. Always listen to the Director. They want to help you, even if they get heated. Just try your best to be respectful.\n4. Having been in a play before does not mean you'll always be cast. Some roles need different things.\n5. Be kind to your fellow actors!\n\n6. During intermission and after the show, actors are asked to use the side door on stage right to visit the VIP guests before leaving or speaking with the other audience members. Be kind and be happy!\n7. We rehearse a play at least twice before.\n\n8. Each actor gets one complimentary ticket per show. This means they can invite one guest who does not have to pay. Simply ask the Owner to add them to the Comp List. Tickets will be at Will Call.\n9. Patrons can drink during shows. Expect outbursts.\n\nThat is all I can think of, lads and lasses! I hope you enjoy your time with the Bedevere Theatre and we are so blessed to have you all here.\n\nThank you all.\n\nA. Hawksong, Owner", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "miss i need to talk :)", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "If found, please return to Bat Druid Oropher Deathsbane.\n\nI have found an odd chunk of white material, piercing the sky from the ground. It hums with some form of pulsing energy. I must research it.§0\n§0\n§0*Instructions on how to find the object are here.*§0\n§0\n§0((3240, 1692))\n\n*A drawing of a group of people from long, long ago. Isabella, Ouity, Clara, Elswyen and Oropher Deathsbane. Stella Evaglno is there, as well. Beneath the drawing is a simple phrase.§0\n§0\n§0\"Do not forget.\"\n\nI do not feel good at all anymore. It feels so cold. The nightmares have not faded. I cannot let the children see this, I must protect them. If only Haelun was still here to help. She would know what to do. She was better than I.\n\n*Scrawlings of runes, carvings and various abstract images of animals. Below them, are scrawling of runes, carvings and various images of plants.*\n\nTHEY ARE COMING THEY ARE COMING THEY ARE COMING THEY ARE COMING REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN THEY ARE COMING THEY ARE COMING REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN REMAIN THEY ARE COMING REMAIN REMAIN THEY ARE COMING REMAIN.\n\nYoung. Young. Young. Young. Young. Young. Young. It was him. It was him. IT WAS HIM IT WAS HIM IT WAS HIM IT WAS HIMITWASHIMITWASHIMITWASHIMITWASHIMITWASHIM§0\n§0\n§0...why§0?\n\nWhy? Why? Why? Why? Why would he do such a thing? Why? WHY? WHY? WHY WOULD HE DO SUCH A THING? WHYWHYWHYWHYWHYWHWYWHYWHWYWHWYWHWYWHWYWHWHY?\n\nskin skin skin skin skin skin skin skins ksin§0\n§0\n§0and so they betrayed and so they betrayed and so MY§0\n§0OWN§0\n§0FLESH AND BLOOD§0\n§0WHYWHYWHYWHYWHYWHYWHWYWHYWHY§0\n§0\n§0WHY WOULD YOU DO THIS TO ME?!\n\ndrip§0\n§0drop§0\n§0dry§0\n§0\n§0drip§0\n§0drop§0\n§0dry§0\n§0\n§0drip§0\n§0drop§0\n§0dry§0\n§0\n§0drip drop dry\n\nbring me something vitriolic§0\n§0\n§0bring me§0\n§0\n§0bring me to her§0\n§0\n§0her eyes are so pretty§0\n§0\n§0SHE IS NOT HER SHE IS NOT HER YOU LIE YOU LIE YOU LIE\n\nnothing nothing nothing§0\n§0\n§0I love you.§0\n§0\n§0nothing nothing nothing§0\n§0\n§0I love you not.§0\n§0\n§0nothing nothing nothing §0\n§0I love you.§0\n§0\n§0nothing nothing nothing i love you not\n\nTHE HEART IS THE VESSEL OF THE TRUTH§0\n§0\n§0YOU HAVE NONE§0\n§0YOU§0\n§0HAVE§0\n§0NO§0\n§0TRUTH§0\n§0\n§0the eyes are the windows to the soul§0\n§0\n§0you have no windows. you are sealed away.\n\nSUMMIT ONE§0\n§0endure§0\n§0SUMMIT TWO§0\n§0learn§0\n§0SUMMIT THREE§0\n§0carve§0\n§0SUMMIT FOUR§0\n§0empower§0\n§0SUMMIT FIVE§0\n§0create§0\n§0SUMMIT SIX§0\n§0sacrafice§0\n§0SUMMIT SEVEN§0\n§0hunt\n\nSEVEN summits to reach the peak. There IS no way to avoid. THE path to virulence is long and arduous. When one has scaled seven NUMBER of peaks, one will have nothing but heat and acid and rage left within them.§0\n§0\n§0THAT IS WHY WE WILL DESTROY THEM.\n\nO, Cerrunos, Horned Lord, another corpse for your mound, rotten and festering.§0\n§0\n§0O, Cerridwen, Green Lady, forgive me for this violence I am about to inflict.§0\n§0\n§0O, Aspects, Old Gods, fill me with heat, and acid, and rage.\n\nit hurts it it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it hurts it   hurts  it hurts it hurts it hurts §0\n§0\n§0it hurts\n\nsoon§0\n§0soon§0\n§0\n§0time moves inexorably forwards§0\n§0\n§0soon§0\n§0\n§0\n§0soon§0\n§0\n§0one day, you will walk these grounds without really knowing why\n\na new order, found upon sanguine ground, the bones and sinew and cartilage of the rotten as its mulch and fertiliser. a new grove, made up of hate and acid and rage.§0\n§0\n§0The Carmine Pact", ""),
        StaticBook("To Toren", "§bLord Azoth Hawksong", "Toren.\n\nWe often find ourselves afraid of what we cannot see. We fear the dark for we believe it to be full of monsters that we dare not even imagine. This is the folly of us all. The real monsters are the ones which sit out in the light of the sun.\n\nYou, Toren, are a monster. What you have done is unforgivable and sets you as one of the most vile mali I have ever met. You often attempt to set yourself apart from your father, but you are no better. Maybe even worse.\n\nI say this with most sincere of meanings: I hope you learn very soon what your idiocy has cost you. Your mind was so riddled with impulses from your manhood that you could not even see what you were doing. I don't know whether to hate you or feel pity for\n\nyour obvious lack of character and foresight.\n\nWhat comes in the next few days is by your creation, Toren. I will do my duty to protect the Laws and People of this land.\n\nI hope that you burn.\nA. Hawksong", ""),
