        StaticBook("Mesia's D'cy 1", "§bNivndil", "Dedicant Nu'Mesia\n\n                    ~*~\n\nNu'Mesia came to me after her prior Guide was unable to continue to take her through Dedicancy. She expressed initial frustration with this, but seemed pleased by the opporunity to take Dedicancy again.\n...\n\nI have decided to trust in the interview afforded to Nu'Mesia by her prior Guide, the Hawk Druid. I will take her through the Dedicant's Vow.\n\n12th Sun's Smile, 1666\n\n                   ~*~\n\nTask 1: Meeting the Order\nNu'Mesia has been tasked with finding seven Drui of the Order. She will record their names, Totems and how being Drui means to them.\n\nUpon her return, she has presented me with a journal containing the seven Drui\n...\n\nas per the task. I have accepted her submission, which will be moved to the library upon completion of her Dedicancy.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nTask 2: The Code if Conduct I have asked Nu'Mesia to familiarise herself with the Order's Code of Conduct, in light of the recent decades of transgressions within the Order.\n\nNu'Mesia was unable to recall specific sections of the Code at the end of her\n..\n\nfirst lesson, claiming only to have read it once. I then instructed her to study it again and that I would be testing her knowledge of it.\n\nFollowing further study, Nu'Mesia was able to recall several articles of the Code word-for-word. I am pleased to\n..\n\nsee that she put in the effort to learn it correctly. I hope that she will continue to heed it as she walks the path of a Dedicant, and as a Drui afterward, if it comes to that.\n\n                   ~*~\n\nTask 3: The Bardic Rite\nNu'Mesia has been tasked with writing a poem about her relationship with nature.\n\nHer submitted poem was both enjoyable and clearly this task has been a thoughtful exercise for Nu'Mesia. I have accepted her submission\n...\n\nfor her Bardic Rite and the poem will be submitted to the library to be shared with Dedicants and Drui for the years ahead.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nLesson 1: The Order\nThis lesson immediately followed Nu'Mesia's first task. It detailed and explained the Order's hierarchy and how it provides structure, and in turn how this enables us to organise ourselves to pursue our duties.\n\n\n...\n\nThe positions from Dedicant to Demi-Aspect were covered, as well as their roles and expectations, how they serve the Balance and provide support to the Order as a whole.\n\nNu'Mesia appeared engaged throughout.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nLesson 2: Druidic Gifts\nFollowing the submission of her Bardic Rite, Nu'Mesia joined me for a lesson in which we discussed Druidic Gifts.\n\nShe was able to name all major Gifts, including Shapeshifting. Emphasis was placed upon the importance of not\n\n...\n\nrelying entirely upon our Gifts, that our minds and bodies remaining intact after Attunement means that we can use them to serve the Balance as well.\n\nWhen asked what sort of situation would be appropriate to use Blight Healing, Nu'Mesia responded,\n...\n\n\"To heal taint.\"\nI then gave her a hypothetical question, which required her to think beyond simply using Gifts to resolve it. She came up with a number of suggestions, which demonstrate she needs further educating upon the Balance, but they were\n\n...\n\npractical solutions.\n\nI also explained that there was joy to be found in our Gifts, that we must never abuse them or make a spectacle of them, but that we could make and do many beautiful things with our Gifts and that they should be celebrated.\n\nTask 4: The Sapling Task\nNu'Mesia has been instructed to take a sapling into the wilds of the world and to plant it, carying for it for four Elven Days before she may return. When asked if she required a sapling, she suggested that she would\n\n...\n\ngather a sapling from the tree which grows in her and the Bone Druid's garden and would use that.\n\nThis is quite a sentimental and meaningful gesture, as this tree has significance to Brother Bone's own Dedicancy. I have thus allowed it.\n\nNu'Mesia has brought me to see the young tree planted out in the wilds. It is healthy and growing steadily out in the forest. The soil is moist and its leaves have turned in accordance with the Autumnal season, I look forward to seeing it renew again.\n\n'Mesia has provided me with her journal from the task, wherein she details the care and nurturing she provided for the sapling. When asked, she elaborated that she found it difficult to be away from her family, but distracted herself with her task\n\n...\n\nand sewing, and that otherwise she encountered few problems.\n'Mesia even explained that she feels the beginning of a connection with nature beginning to form, at least in part due to her camping out in the wilderness while nurturing her sapling.\n\n\n...\n\nI have passed Nu'Mesia upon her Sapling Task. Her jounral will be submitted to the library shortly, for other Dedicants and Drui to draw inspiration from.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nLesson 3: Bond with Nature In light of her response during the Sapling Task, I felt it an opportune moment to explain to Nu'Mesia that she was beginning to understand the role of a Drui, in spending her own time, energy, care and consideration to\n\n...\n\nbring natural life into the world and see it thrive. She understands that growing trees is a matter of more than simply planting  sapling and that it requires thought and physical labour, and how this relates to the work of Drui.\n\n...\n\nThough it is later, that she will come to understand how this fits into the grander scheme, I feel. 'Mesia is keen and engaged in her Dedicancy, and it is enjoyable to teach her. I will however, be sure to temper her enthusiasm with patience.\n\nTask 5: The Symbols Task\nNu'Mesia has been instructed to gather objects which come to represent the Aspects. Which is to say that she will gather one object for each Aspect.\n\nShe returned with an egg, the eyes of an enormous spider and an arrow.\n\n'Mesia explained that the egg symbolised Cerridwen, in that it was life in its purest form, ready to spring forward, the beginning of all nature.\n\nThe arrow symbolised Cernunnos as the Father of the Hunt, for it was the means by which we Descendants\n\n...\n\nsurvive within the wilds,  our tools, where the lion has claws, the wolf has teeth and the serpent has venom.\n\nLastly, the spider eyes represented death. That the spider itself must have died for them to be taken. It symbolised the end of things,\n\n...\n\nbefore new life could take hold. That the spider's cunning, how it entraps prey within a web, represented the Aspect of nature that is less savoury, which many are woe to acknowledge.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nLesson Four: The Balance\nFollowing Mesia's Symbol Task, we discussed the Balance of Nature. She should now understand that the Balance encompasses the cycle of life and death, that without birth the world is still and silent;\n\n\n...\n\nwithout survival, life cannot spread or reproduce, there is no fire or drive or passion; without death nature will grow beyond its ability to support itself, plants will drain the soil of nutrients, animals will become overcrowded.\n\n\n...\n\nFurther, 'Mesia should also understand that the Balance refers to the movement of other forces within the natural world. That deserts grow and mountains shrink, but that we cannot allow deserts to consume the world, for example. That the world needs\n\n...\n\nall these things in careful balance of each other, else the world falls totally into chaos.\n\nThat sometimes we must allow a fire to take hold of a forest, or allow one species to consume another and for new ecosystems to emerge from the old.\n\nLesson Five: The Fae Ring\nThis lesson comprised of introducing 'Mesia to the Grove's Fae Ring. She was made aware of its dangers, but also how it enables the non-Attuned to experience something akin to our Attunement.\n\n\n...\n\nShe was given the opportunity to step into the Ring under the supervision of myself and the Bone Druid Keir. 'Mesia was able to remain within the ring for several moments, before she passed out from the overwhelming sensation.\n\n\n...\n\n'Mesia was then taken home by Keir, to rest and recover from which since, she has.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nTask Six: The Hunting Task Nu'Mesia has been instructed to find a quarry she determines appropriate. She will hunt it and make a quick, clean kill and then use all of the body. Leaving nothing to waste.\n\nMesia has returned a number of seeds later.\n\n...\n\nHer chosen quarry was a rabbit, her kill was quick and clean. From the pelt she has fashioned swaddling, as she and the Bone Druid are trying for a Malii. The bones have been turned into a necklace for him also, as a gift, while the skull serves\n\n...\n\nas a sentimental trophy of the task. Lastly the meat and organs have been used for food.\n\nWhen asked, 'Mesia explained that she encountered difficulty in making the kill, something which one might expect from someone with a gentle Spirit.\n\nLesson Six: Voidal Magicks\nIn this lesson, we covered how magicks of the Void require the perversion of the natural veil between it and our world. How our world is all of existence, all that is, and how the Void is an infinity of potential, how\n\n...\n\nthe two are not meant to touch or interract. She also understands that Conjurers for example, require the butchering of animals in order to hone their craft. How they have little regard for natural life in to persuit of personal goals and power.\n\n...\n\nWhen asked if it was then wrong for Water Evocationists to save a burning forest she replied that it was not, for they were helping the Balance.\nAfter I reminded her that sometimes forests needed to burn, I asked if then we should also allow Fire\n\n...\n\nEvocationists to burn forests as well, or Conjurers to summon their familiars in Order to combat undead. 'Mesia appeared to appreciate the nuance of the situation then, suggesting that no, it was not acceptable, that they were interfering and\n\n...\n\nthat nature should be allowed to function upon its own accord. I am pleased that she was engaged in the lesson enough to give a thoughtful response.\n\n                     ~*~\n\nTask Seven: Animal Companion\n\nNow that Nu'Mesia had proven that she can hunt with respect and reverence  for her kill, she will now demonstrate that she has the patience and gentleness to befriend a creature of the wilds. She is to earn the \n...\n\ntrust of a wild animal and to return to the Grove with it, so that I may ensure she has done as her task requires.\n\nMesia has returned with her new companion accompanying her. A grey fox from the South of Atlas, which she encountered as a lone pup\n\n...\n\noutside Haense. The young fox was healthy and was comfortable around Mesia. Upon Communing it had an appreciative regard for her and was comfortable.\n\nWhen asked what she had learned from this, Mesia explained that she was forming a stronger\n\n...\n\nrelationship with nature, and that she better understood a Drui's role in guardianship.\n\nI'm pleased with her submission for this task.\n\n                    ~*~\n\nTask Eight: The Staff Rite\nMesia has been instructed to return to the tree she'd grown for her sapling task, to take a branch and create a staff from it. She knows that her efforts and creativity will be judged.\n\nMesia has returned with her staff.\n\n..\n\nFrom examining it, I can clearly see that she's put effort and thought into its creation. I feel that she now better understands the reciprocal relationship we have with nature, because of the significance of her staff and the tree from which it comes.\n\nLesson Seven: Meditation\nThis lesson instructed Mesia in how to meditate effectively. She meditated under my instruction and understands that occupies an important place in a Drui's life, and the development of our Gifts.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mesia's D'dcy 2", "§bNivndil", "Contiuation of Dedicant Nu'Mesia's Record.§0\n§0\n§0Task Nine: The Sound of a Moth's Wings§0\n§0\n§0Following our lesson on meditation, I have asked Mesia to make more time for it in her everyday life. This I feel will prove challenging, as she is currently raising§0\n§0...\n\nher young son. She will however, have to understand that we Drui must make time for our duties as well as our personal lives.§0\n§0\n§0For this task, I have instructed Mesia to find a quiet place to meditate, where she will do so until hearing the§0\n§0\n§0...\n\nbeating of moth's wings.§0\n§0\n§0Mesia has returned a few months later, reporting her success. She elaborates that she enjoyed the opportunity to simply listen to the sounds of nature around her and to lose herself amongst it. She also explained that her§0\n§0\n§0...\n\nyoung son, Kaemir, sat quietly within her lap through her meditation.§0\n§0\n§0We discussed how meditation is important for we Drui to understand when inaction and action are necessary for our duties.§0\n§0\n§0                    ~*~\n\nMesia's Dedicancy draws to a close. As such I have asked her to begin thinking of a Grand Task, as an appropriate final achievement to her studies. §0\n§0\n§0She requested to consult with her husband, the Bone Druid, to which I suggested she could also speak§0\n§0\n§0...\n\nwith other Drui concerning their previous Grant Tasks as well.§0\n§0\n§0I will have Mesia interviewed after a tenth task. My intention is to have an Archdruid other than I conduct the interview, to avoid bias.§0\n§0\n§0                    ~*~\n\nTask Ten: The Garden Task§0\n§0I have instructed Mesia to create a garden in the wilds of nature. The requirements were such that it could be used as a shelter for small animals.§0\n§0\n§0Mesia's garden was beautiful. Grown among the savannah of the Pridelands§0\n§0...\n\nit blended easily with the natural landscape so as not to disrupt the ecosystem.  She'd assembled it around a large tree, which had a hollow dug beneath it. Mesia had filled the hollow with hay for creatures to rest within.§0\n§0\n§0She'd also placed a trough§0\n§0...\n\nto allow animals access to water.§0\n§0\n§0I was pleased with the results of her task and have passed it.§0\n§0\n§0She is now ready for interview.§0\n§0\n§0                    ~*~\n\nArchdruid Ithuriel was kind enough to oblige my request to interview Mesia. §0\n§0\n§0She passed the interview and then discussed her idea for a Grand Task with Sister Sable and myself.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0                     ~*~\n\nThe Grand Task§0\n§0\n§0In light of the encroaching threat of magical blizzards from the Vaeyl, Mesia has chosen to travel South and to assist the animals which have been displaced or otherwise disoriented from this occurence.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0...\n\nUpon her return, Mesia detailed that she was able to assist with a few migrating species, as they fled the snowstorms and darkness swelling up from the Yatl Wasteland.§0\n§0\n§0During this time, she also attended he wounds of a snowy owl, which had been §0\n§0\n§0...\n\nshot out of the sky by arrowfire. Whatever blundering hunter attempted such, did not reveal himself.§0\n§0\n§0I was pleased to know that she followed the path of a Drui, in answering the call to aid nature in its time of peril and confusion.§0\n\nIn light of Mesia's completed Grand Task, I have made the decision to Attune her.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Blessed be, Sister Reindeer.  §0\n§0\n§0\n§0                     ~*~", ""),
        StaticBook("Niv, please read", "§bEndvani", "Niv,§0\n§0\n§0I went to Caras Eldar and ran into Hareven and Quillian on their way into the city. Quillian had decided to turn herself in to the militia for the things she's done.§0\n§0\n§0I accompanied the party to the jail cells below the city to stand\n\nas witness to the event. Almost as soon as Quillian was behind bars she began to deny everything that was brought up against her. Hareven, though he did admit she had done certain things, kept interrupting the process and saying she's learning.\n\nThat she should be excused of kidnapping the Moonlight Druid, that yes she did it but she shouldn't be punished for it.§0\n§0\n§0Kairn repeatedly asked Hareven to stop interrupting. Hareven continued to berate those bringing up accusations.\n\nKairn ordered Hareven to be knocked out.. But when that failed, he smashed him with a hammer.. Turned him into mulch.§0\n§0\n§0I thought they would kill Artimec. He denied Quillian had ever done anything wrong. He tried to run the show like it was a court trial.§0\n\nArt refused to stand down. He was crying by the end of it. Niia nearly killed him. Kairn did something... Strange. He made a weird motion at Art and gave him a nosebleed without even touching him.§0\n§0\n§0Renn taught Awaiti unattunement.\n\nThere was a stranger with Quillian when she showed up to the city as well. An Andunian. They were asked to wait outside but refused. They verbally and physically assaulted the guard and spat at them... They were killed.\n\nArtimec is most definitely going to despise me now. I was asked to report anything at all that I knew about Quillian's misconduct. I mentioned the usual things. Most of which Artimec denied or said she'd already served time for.§0\n§0\n§0It was when I mentioned\n\nher relationship with Hareven that he was really upset.§0\n§0\n§0You know I don't personally have anything against interbreeding. But I remember Art telling me almost specifically, back in Vailor, that it doesn't matter what my opinion is on that matter.\n\nIt doesn't change the law.§0\n§0\n§0He's such a hypocrite.  I have witness, first hand, Artimec slaughtering Dominion Citizens in the tavern in Caras Eldar for so much as just mentioning magic. He has a no excuse, no mercy policy towards\n\nanyone else breaking his precious laws.. But when Quillian breaks them? He begs for mercy. It was a mistake, give her a chance, let her attone. I've never seen him afford any of that mercy to anyone outside his family. He bloodies his sword on a whim if\n\nhe so much as thinks someone has something to do with magic. Quillian? She's definitely been up to some 'shady' shit. He's ignoring the problem.§0\n§0\n§0He even defended her having sex with a Shade. She herself said she led it to the light by sleeping with it.\n\nTheir grounds for forgiving that is that it was then made into a druid.§0\n§0\n§0I didn't realize that sleeping with shades was allowed to pass so easily, be forgiven so lightly, as long as you get them to attune afterwards.§0\n§0\n§0Apparently she's seen\n\nas being righteous and self sacrificing for having fucked a shade.§0\n§0\n§0She claimed she kidnapped and drugged her brother for his health benefit as well. Said it was the loving sisterly thing to do.§0\n§0\n§0Artimec also said that Quillian was no worse than anyone\n\nelse in the order.§0\n§0\n§0I certainly don't believe that. No one else I know has ever drugged and kidnapped a druid. She's the only one I know who fucked a shade, or went feral inside city limits, or uses thuleanism in outbursts of public rage on her own home..\n\nKairn finally gave in to Artimec's crying. He's postponed a judgement and given the final verdict to the Naelurir circle to decide.§0\n§0\n§0So of course Quillian will probably be off scot free. Again.§0\n§0\n§0And with a vengeance against everyone who brought things\n\nagainst her.§0\n§0\n§0I wanted to write this all down and make sure you got to see it. Make a record of it.§0\n§0\n§0I'm not entirely sure what happened lastnight but it wasn't good.§0\n§0\n§0I had nightmares about Kairn's eyes..\n\nPerhaps I should have refused to speak.. But there were swords out and people were being killed for not cooperating.§0\n§0\n§0Maybe a part of my testimony was tainted as well, by my irritation with Art and Quillian.. Especially Quill and Hareven.\n\nI don't know if I did it for some kind of payback for Ele's sake,§0\n§0\n§0or just to prove what a hypocrite Artimec is on the matter.§0\n§0\n§0Maybe I was wrong.§0\n§0\n§0Or maybe staying quiet would have been more selfpreserving and selfish.\n\nI'm not sure.§0\n§0\n§0All I'm really sure about is that I'm sick of seeing Artimec and Quillian march around as if they are untouchable and untied by the rules they use to slaughter or abuse others.§0\n\n\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0       .:E:.", ""),
        StaticBook("Letter to Koi", "§bNivndil Duskhollow", "ay'salum Brother Koi,§0\n§0\n§0Your recent writings certainly make for an interesting read. I am woe t opoint out however, that several things appear to have escaped your attention if only through the deepness of your thought. §0\n§0...\n\nFor you are correct that Drui must not fear extremes, nor violence and that there is both much joy and energy to be found in passion. I fear however that your concentration upon passion alone has led you to a misguided conclusion.§0\n§0...\n\nFor us to guard the Balance, we must first understand it. That there is passion in nature yes, but also peace. After the thunderstorm comes the quiet and stillness, rejuvenation after destruction. After the passion of the hunt, there is§0\n§0...\n\nThe Peace and reverence. When the quarry escapes the hunter, it retreats and knows peace again. This peace soon gives way to passion once more.§0\n§0\n§0There is chaos, the fight for survival is a tangled web of predators and prey, but there is order in§0\n§0...\n\nNature also. We need not look far t osee the way in which ants or bees organise themselves, the wolf and its pack, the meerkat who rear their young collectively. Chaos and hunger are not constants, we can see this in the lion or the wolf who does no§0\n§0...\n\nHarm to the deer or gazelle when its hunger is sated. Order is born from chaos, and will be consumed by it, only to rise again.§0\n§0\n§0Least of all, would I dissuade you from the power of your emotions. Among my Seed, we know of the torture surpressing ...\n\nOne's emotions inflicts upon the Spirit, but that discipline is also necessary. Our Gifts respond well to the invocation of our emotions, but without the serenity and calm afforded to us by meditation, we could not develop our Gifts at all.§0\n§0...\n\nWe Drui are all wardens of the Balance, and we must therefore understand the Balance within ourselves also.§0\n§0\n§0There is passion, yet peace§0\n§0There is serenity, yet emotion§0\n§0There is chaos, yet order§0\n§0\n§0...\n\n    ito igne, ehya hileia§0\n§0    ito leyun, ehya ayilu§0\n§0    ito Tiuth, ehya Heial§0\n§0\n§0You are quite right, that to fall to stagnation is to surrender to the comforts of a city, where nature struggles to dwell. That to protect without teaching one to protect§0\n§0...\n\nThemselves is irresponsible, is to teach that there will always be help when this is not always possible. We must also remember however, that there is a diversity of strength. That your strength is my strength, and my wisdom is your wisdom.§0\n§0...\n\nCooperation is natural also and to forsake it for individualism alone is short-sighted. We are to serve, tht is the purpose of our Gifts and Attunement, and to pursue victory without the guidance afforded us by peace and serenity is to forsake the§0\n§0\n§0...\n\nPeace that the ASpects grant us also.§0\n§0To listen only to the absolute primal. When there is no absolute in nature.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0...\n\nFlowing through all, there is Balance§0\n§0There is no peace without a passion to create§0\n§0There is no passion without peace to guide§0\n§0Knowledge stagnates without the strength to act§0\n§0Power blinds without the serenity to see§0\n§0There is freedom in life§0\n§0...\n\nThere is purpose in death.§0\n§0\n§0Nature is all things, and we are Nature.§0\n§0\n§0                    ~*~§0\n§0\n§0I would not have you walk from the path you have made for yourself, but I would have you meditate upon it.§0\n§0...\n\nTo open your mind to the passion /and/ the peace, the serenity /and/ the emotion, chaos /and/ order.§0\n§0\n§0ilhileia maehr'ehya§0\n§0Sister Autumn,§0\n§0Nivndil Duskhollow.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "I'm supposed to be using this to write down exactly what happened for Archdruid Indra... For when they confront Kasfer.§0\n§0\n§0Tay was getting his soul puppetry link removed by a cleric, and Kasfer approached me asking to talk for just a minute. I followed him\n\nand he offered me a drink of something before we started speaking.§0\n§0\n§0My memory is blank, after that. The next thing I remember is waking up. I was... Maimed. He harmed me badly. Elly had to powershare with me so I could heal. Though... I hardly\n\nremember how I found Elly... I was walking through the city, in deep amounts of pain... She found me, along with Harlan, an Ascended... From there on out, I was fixed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "17th Elesul'leh Liewyn, 1650§0\n§0\n§0Kae'leh Ciyutan ehya'Liyuran kinanal ito. Liyuran Lomilumr'leh kinah, kae'leh taeleh lluman myumiera.§0\n§0\n§0Kae'leh Ficiyutan ehya Filiyuran Kae marwyn illere. Eleheial'drui hiyluan ito ehya naelueyae.§0\n§0Kae lin ito.\n\neleasimulum marleyuan ito. eleasimulum'Laurehlin leyu itoal, ehya iyloem mamarn.§0\n§0Kae'leh Ciyutan ehyaLiyuran iyatan bilokir ito.§0\n§0\n§0Kae anniler chul ehiere, iyul Kae'leh illernan'drui lente ehya Kae Eletaliinan tilrueh.§0\n§0\n§0Kae'leh mayilun kae wynan myumiere.\n\n12th Elemarfeta, 1650§0\n§0\n§0Kae'leh Ciyut Hareven sanerale, karinte. Lae Kae naelu halerala! marwyn ito Kae'leh.§0\n§0\n§0Kae Eletaliiynan tilrer erne, y'talikalem kento. Lae Kae biloke heilal ilito Kae'leh taeleh, naeluala kente.\n\nehya Kae'leh taeleh chule avere ilheialsirui, il'Kae§0.§0\n§0                   ~+~§0\n§0\n§013th Elemarfeta, 1650§0\n§0\n§0Kae ilama iyatsaeale... iyul Kae heya sanere maelune, karinito. lomi'tayna Kae ahn'sae ito, ehya'iyl marleyan ito... Kae vallumale kente.§0\n\nmarmateluan'vihaian il'Kae'leh iyatan. oem'marmatelu sulii ehya lomi'tayna! Eletaliiynan'leh ahernan marleyan ito.§0\n§0\n§0Kae annilererih.§0\n§0                   ~+~§0\n\n17th Elefeta'Yulnayl, 1650§0\n§0\n§0Mal'onn Ikurn kae puerala, lae Marfin'Malin atansae ito sanerala karinte. kae Syrex'ehya narnalae. y Caras Eldar narnae, oem iler'sith iluigne'seth mahnih ito thruerala. igne ito, valluman'ilcruan'ehya karinte.\n\nKae Elevirarim uriar'ilcruan ehya'ignera sallume. Ciyut Ikrun ito, karinte. Igneal ehya'kinah. Kae walehne. Nersine, eletal'uruk drui'taeluir, medin uhierala karinte. Oem Mali Vosirk Lin'Waleh ito myumierala, ehya Kae Asimulum'Naelurir'onn vallei myumiera\n\nKae medine. Kae'leh taeleh diiyatan, kae ito hiuwne. Kae ikru sallume.\n\n*This page appears to have§0\n§0several smudges and scrawlings across it, likely used to quickly jot down notes*§0\n§0\n§0telu ehya'banih irrini'taynei§0\n§0niut'telu ehya'esun irrini'taynei§0\n§0kreyn banih'telu ehya'vailu mataliame\n\n13th Asul'leh Liewyn, 1652§0\n§0\n§0marwynan ito Kae'leh. Elemardrui Hareven Kae sanere ilutaliamean naeluala, karinte. Kae ilKae'leh eletaliame salume, ehya'eletaliame Kae saluma. §0\n§0\n§0Hareven y Kae, hiuwa taliame adontere, sanerala.\n\nKae adonterale ehya'eletaliame welierala. eletaliame'leh anuh'wy hiuwal'anoha ehya'Kae'leh iyl salume, karinte. leyan ito iyl.§0\n§0\n§0eletaliiynan Kae illern illeraleyae.\n\n7th Oemir Lentonn, 1653§0\n§0\n§0Liyur Elswyen kinal ito, karinte. lluman ito Kaean'leh. elasimulum'haelun khel lenta, ehya Kae'leh taliiynan'mayilu divu itone. Cruan ehya'llumn iyul Kaean'leh myumiereyae, ehya marenne ohn Liyur Kyral, kae welierala.\n\niyul miarsae taliiynan'mayilu ito igner biloka, ehya Kae'leh Ciyutan rhil ito. y'iul Kae'leh iyatan divhiuwan.§0\n§0\n§0                   ~+~\n\n5th Elemarfeta, 1655§0\n§0\n§0martareman ito ethn§0\n§0heya kae ethne?§0\n§0\n§0elannilir ito kae'leh. Taynuel ay'kae ito'eleheial sanerala, karinte. Sirame ito kae'leh ay'iyl.§0\n§0\n§0Anorak ito kae'leh eloem taeluir'drui. ayla Mali ito kae'leh.\n\nehya, lae'leh ante parmaehrn kae'leh lluman myumierala, karinte. lae niut turran'ehya oem metta, ito'leur iylahnala.§0\n§0\n§0kae arhil narnsaer ay'taynansaer lae annilerale, karinte. kae lae peritha iyl andriae.§0\n§0\n§0Anorak'leh saluma siol kae sanerala..\n\nlae lliran taeluir'drui onn adontera. ohn iyulan itoal kae'ehya Endvani, karinte.§0\n§0\n§0kae nae medina, karinto.§0\n§0       §0\n§0                   ~+~\n\n12th Elefenn'leh Larin, 1656§0\n§0\n§0kae maliearaln sulierale, kae iyathe karinte.§0\n§0\n§0ito'Kaz'Ulrah, kae Quillian'ehya oem Uruk marhierne sulierale, karinte. kae lae'leh iyathalan ay'iyl maelune. iyl oem vulln ito kae, kae sallume.§0\n\noem talikae'onn salume, Mal'onn Vilut'mal iyl adonterala.§0\n§0\n§0mardiln'ehya alliman ito iyl elkreyn.§0\n§0\n§0                  ~+~\n\n10th elefeta'yulnayl, 1656§0\n§0\n§0kinal ito Mal'onn Vilut'mal.§0\n§0elasimulum kae'leh ciwn etheral, il'iyl lluman'ehya valluman kento.§0\n§0\n§0heya iyul kae'leh crun myumiera? ikrunan ito lae'leh ti...§0\n§0*an ink blott suggests hesitation*§0\n§0ehya kaean'leh Mal'onn.\n\nheya Quillian sallume? kae erne parsaere. ordivuparsaerne chul ito hae'leh vallumn.§0\n§0\n§0hileia ito nae'leh, Mal'onn Toren. kae nae'leh tillunan adontere, ay nae ahrilne iyathale.§0\n§0       §0\n§0                   ~+~\n\n15th elemarfeta, 1656§0\n§0\n§0iyulan miarsaenan kae'leh llumsaera. kae Quillian omentale, hae'ehya elillern'orlluman illerale. kaean vallumeriialae, karinte.§0\n§0\n§0heya kae iyl chul sallume? ikrunan ito hae'ehya Mal'onn Vilut'mal ti,§0\n§0ehya...\n\nkae marn Larionn'ehya Mal'onn kina oerne suliera karinto.§0\n§0\n§0Vilutmal'ohn ito Mal'onn Tahorran, dilan'ehya alliman iyl niut.§0\n§0\n§0ehya kae lluman cruan'ehya sallume.§0\n§0\n§0heya ito kae'leh? heya ito kae'leh Talonnii?§0\n§0                   ~*~\n\n15th Malin'leh Ahern, 1658§0\n§0\n§0elemardrui ito kae'leh.§0\n§0\n§0Lari'onn Elswyen ay'kae sanerala. Kae ay'Mal'onn Ikurn sanerale, ehya eleheial kae iyl illeraleyae karinte.§0\n§0\n§0iyul kae Sirame myumiereh. §0\n§0\n§0kaean il'ahril tilruerae'ehya eleheial...\n\nhiylunan, hileia Meracahe'ehya  myumierae, karinto.§0\n§0\n§0                    ~*~\n\n7th Elefenn'leh Larin, 1659 §0\n§0\n§0Nesrine'leh orikrunan'ehya orllyt hae llumn myumierala. hae Elefin'Malin'ito chulne narna, iyl Mal'onn Khairn sanerala, karinte.§0\n§0\n§0hae kae'leh mayilun llumn'ehya cruan illerna'ehya, iyl kae'leh cruan myumiera.\n\nmaraetahii ito hae'leh, kae ito iyal suliera. aetahn'ehya hiylun'orlentin ochul ito Nesrine'leh, karinto.§0\n§0\n§0kae nae otillunan adontere, Nesrine.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0                    ~+~\n\n4th Elefeta'Yulnayl, 1661§0\n§0\n§0malieral ito kaean. oem malieir ito tosaeiran myumierla karinte.§0\n§0\n§0kae Elenora'ehya malier ochul uhiersae, iyl ochulne ahrilerneh.§0\n§0\n§0iyl kae'leh igne cruan'ehya myumiera.\n\n17th Elemarfeta, 1665§0\n§0\n§0enet ornarne karinto. fi'elemardrui ito kaean, Ithuriel halerala karim'ante. hae wyn kae'leh myumiere, ut hae orchul Meracahe tilruer, kae'ehya lae'leh lle salume.§0\n§0\n§0kaean'leh elheial lente, wyl kae'leh taeluiran eth narnsaen narnae.\n\nHileia Meracahe'ehya kaean'ito narnhyeme. ahernal ito.§0\n§0\n§0Lari'onn Celia il'kae Endvani'ehya cinheral, ayiyl llun ito kae'leh karinte. lae kae'leh lle wyn myumierala, ehya..§0\n§0iheiuhii narne narneyem'ehya.\n\n14th Elmarwehnsae'tehral, 1673§0\n§0\n§0Hareven Quillian'ehya yallreyae. ullralan lae'leh lin llyteran, ehya kae'leh Mal'onn Lari'onn'ehya yallreyae. Ahernal ito.§0\n§0\n§0kaean orchul medier ullralan aeloran'ehya uhierae, thillereyae'ehya kaean'leh Eleheial.\n\nHareven y'maliearn ito Eleheial sanerale.. iyl kae'leh hiylun llum'saere, karinte.§0\n§0\n§0kae orchul viran ito kae'leh karinto. eletaliynan kaean evarerih.§0\n§0       §0\n§0                   ~*~\n\n9th Oemir Lentonn, 1677§0\n§0Uell kariman narnale'yem§0\n§0\n§0kaean orchul kaean'leh hiylunan ay'ignesaen cerurae. September, iyal ilueth, ciwn lenterale'onn karinte.§0\n§0\n§0malierier ullral'ehya ito lae'leh, lae'ehya Fae Drui, Taliynan'ehya luere il'omarlienean.\n\nkaean orchul anoh lae llytherae, iyl'ehya ullral kinarae. kae ay'eletaliynan'leh Mani'ehya ahernan adontere, ut kae iyal div'cerun ito kae'leh virare.§0\n§0\n§0eletaliynan kaean annilerieh.§0\n§0\n§0                    ~*~", ""),
        StaticBook("Van´ayla", "§bKella", "§2 I seem to avoid§0\n§2  What´s seen§0\n§2  As a void§0\n§2  Of things§0\n§2  I have brought§0\n§2  With the ink§0\n§2  That I bought§0\n§2  With memory.§0\n\n  §2Therefore§0\n§2  I am here no more.§0\n§2  I´ll run to the fields§0\n§2  The rest I´ll store§0\n§2  In a book.§0\n§0\n§0\n§2  I am glad to have met these roots.§0\n§2  But I´m not to stay.§0\n§2  I move.§0\n§2  Like the pages§0\n§2  I tore away.\n\n §3 So yeah§0\n§3  I´m leaving§0\n§3  I´ll learn to remember with age.§0\n§3  I´ll forget I am trapped in this cage.§0\n§3  Or I´ll seek any way to ease rage.§0\n§3 §0\n§3  Just know§0\n§3  You were the first name§0\n§3  I ever wrote in my page,", ""),
        StaticBook("Red Rose Card", "§bNivndil Duskhollow", "~+~ <> ~+~§0\n§0\n§0      §0\n§0    *The card is decorated§0\n§0     with the image of a§0\n§0      tree with pink fuschsia§0\n§0     blossoms cascading to§0\n§0     form a thick blanket§0\n§0     at its roots. It appears§0\n§0     to be hand-drawn*§0\n§0\n§0                   ~+~\n\nay'Endvani§0\n§0\n§0\n§0     Happy Red Rose Day§0\n§0\n§0        To My Lentin'Ker§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0il'mayileronn,§0\n§0With love from§0\n§0\n§0    Niv", ""),
        StaticBook("Words I cant say", "§bKella", "Blunt wood\nWould move if it could\nI see not why it should But I know others do.\n\nThat´s why I look\nI guess life´s like \n                   a book\nI´d read if I could\nBut can´t yet see\n              it through.\n\nSo I know.\nI know I don´t know.\nI don´t know where\n                 to go\nI seed doubt ´till\n               I´m whole\nI don´t look for a home\n\nBut I hope.\nI hope I´m not alone.\nAnd I hope when\n               I´m gone\nI´´ll leave a trail\n                in stone.\n\nSo yeah\nThat´s that.\nI seek for a path\nI wish to stop seeking\n\nI like your hand too\nReally. It´s really cool\nInspired the first verse and all\n\nI can handle it.\nNot the hand,\n     your guidance.\nThat´s what I feel I need, I don´t know why\n\ni´m learning not to be rude\nSorry we started on the wrong foot\n\n\n\n\n\n                 -Kella", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Skale, §0\n§0\n§0You're to travel to various parts of the land. You must never reveal you are associated with Druids while doing this. §0\n§0\n§0Find a person of each race. Dwarf, Orc, Elf, Human. They must not be associated with Druids either.\n\nStart a conversation with them somehow. And during the conversation find a way to bring up Druids as a whole. §0\n§0\n§0Find out their opinion on the Druids.§0\n§0\n§0When finished talking, thank them for the conversation and offer them something.\n\nThis could be food, drink, or just a trinket. Then be on your way. §0\n§0\n§0Record every response you get.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0ALSO§0\n§0\n§0Ask them what they know about the Aspects. Do not attempt to educate them, though.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Vivec: Human§0\n§0\n§0Vivec believes the Druids are okay. In his words:§0\n§0\"They don't fuck with me, so I don't fuck with them.\"§0\n§0Also in his words:§0\n§0\"I love me some Aspects.\"§0\n§0Claims to be the Silverback Gorilla Druid.\n\nMaxistraw: Orc§0\n§0\n§0Maxistraw cannot speak, therefore this interview was conducted with me reading his thoughts from his book. He claims that:§0\n§0\n§0\"Druids are selfish and he doesn't much like them.\"\n\nWhen asked about the Aspects, he had a very similar response.§0\n§0\n§0\"Not much like them, I hear others speak bad about them.\"§0\n§0\n§0An interesting individual, perhaps I'll return and shed some light on both in the future.\n\n???: Dwarf§0\n§0\n§0The dwarf appeared to be a mountain dwarf, with a thick black beard and long black hair, braided into his beard. It was a very interesting look, I will admit. He didn't particularly care for me and refused to give his name, though\n\nhe was willing to answer my question. When asked about Druids he said:§0\n§0\n§0\"I don't particularly care for them, worse than the elves really.\"§0\n§0\n§0He appeared to not really be fond of things aside from stone and metals.\n\nWhen asked about the Aspects he stated:§0\n§0\n§0\"Bunch of bullshit if you ask me.\"§0\n§0\n§0He was an interest man, I kind of want to return to him at some point and perhaps learn about his own lifestyle.\n\nRhillen: Elf§0\n§0\n§0Rhillen is a mali'ame within the Dominion. He was inquisitive on why I wanted to know his opinion on Druids overall, I just let him know I was researching the public opinion on them. When asked about Druids, he had this to say:\n\n\"I agree with the Aspects but do not personally follow the more fantatic folk, who wish to purge that which is different.\"§0\n§0\n§0Apparently there are those within the Dominion of Malin who hate the druids because they are allowed to kill those they consider\n\nheretics within the city.§0\n§0\n§0Rhillen himself considers himself an Aspectist, having a more progressive view of them himself.§0\n§0\n§0He wishes to see the druids not murder others and hopes to see an overall change in the way crime is handled by druids.\n\nEveryone took the baked potato I offered.§0\n§0\n§0As it stands, it appears the public opinion of druids has dropped considerably. With the apparent 'degeneracy' of the Mother Grove and the strict, pagan-like actions of the Dominion's grove, we\n\nneed to strive to show the world we are not their foes. While we serve the balance, public opinion isn't really important. But I think being accepted and appreciated by the public will greatly help with our work.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Today is the day, I march towards the ruins of the high elves.  The Semptember Prince's horde must be stopped before it proceeds further south. It is not a bad life, though I may take up fishing.\n\n9th of The First Steed. I have left the Kingdom of man kind, and will likely be called a traitor. I am a coward after all it seems. The Empire and Fenn had a fight right before the gates. The human called for me to kill the High Elf. I ran away.\n\n10 of The First of Seed. 1689. The human returned. I seek to join the guard of Fenn, in the hopes of citizenship. the human has demanded a price be paid.\n\n11th of the first Seed. 1689§0\n§0\n§0I have moved into Fenn. And plan to join the guard shortly after.§0\n\n12 of The Amber Cold,1698.§0\n§0\n§0I have grown out my hair and started the trade of hunting once more, selling pelts here or there for food.\n\n16 of The Amber Cold, 1689.§0\n§0\n§0I have joined the Ivae'Fenn today, I have myself a new set of full plate. I am keeping my hair grown out however.\n\n18th of The Amber Cold, 1698.§0\n§0\n§0A Contient wide war has almost certainly broken out. Where my role in this will be is beyond me for now.\n\n6th of The Deep Cold, 1689§0\n§0\n§0Fenn has treated me well, for an elf that is. I have traveled back to the cloud Temple once more, to see fresh faces and bits of the country side.\n\n7th of the Deep Cold, 1689§0\n§0\n§0I have gone hunting for some boar, spotted near Warhawkes. A long way from Fenn. In my travels, I happened upon a destroyed fort. It is a breath taking sight, I shall have to mark it upon my map. §0\n\n10th of Snows Maiden, 1690.§0\n§0\n§0The dawn of a new year comes to Fenn. It seems the city has a bit of life back to it. A breathe behind those tall towering walls.§0\n§0\n§0I find myself looking back to my father in times like these. I would love to hunt with\n\nhim once again..\n\n11th of Malins Welcome. §0\n§0\n§0I will be reciving training in due time, and one of the cat folk may join the forces of Fenn. An interesting prospect.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "~Tree Journal~§0\n§0\n§0Day 1§0\n§0The", ""),
        StaticBook("Dear Reader", "§b'", "§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\n§0              ᶠᵘᶜᵏᵧₒᵤ", ""),
        StaticBook("Jobs (draft 1)", "§bQuavinir", "Orchadist§0\n§0Herbalist,§0\n§0Florist,§0\n§0Farmer,§0\n§0Bogger,§0\n§0LAddersmith§0\n§0Metalsmith§0\n§0(Bronze)§0\n§0(Iron)§0\n§0(Steel)§0\n§0(Silver)§0\n§0(Gold)§0\n§0(Copper)\n\nCooper(Barrelmaker)§0\n§0Glassworker§0\n§0Glasspaner§0\n§0Glassblower§0\n§0Clay-moulder§0\n§0Clay-baker§0\n§0Ash-maker§0\n§0Mudder§0\n§0Brickmaker§0\n§0Stonemason§0\n§0Stonecutter\n\nstonecarver,§0\n§0Gemworker§0\n§0Gemcutter§0\n§0Gemcrafter§0\n§0Ore-smelter§0\n§0Gem-Refiner§0\n§0Muscian,§0\n§0Portrait Painter§0\n§0Landscape Painter§0\n§0General Painter§0\n§0Weaver\n\nWicker-weaver§0\n§0Basket-maker§0\n§0Brewer§0\n§0Vitner§0\n§0Alchemist§0\n§0Mage§0\n§0Druid§0\n§0Cleric§0\n§0Paladin,§0\n§0Ascended§0\n§0Tailor\n\ncobbler§0\n§0glovemaker,§0\n§0hat-maker§0\n§0hunter§0\n§0fur-trapper§0\n§0fisher§0\n§0whaler§0\n§0shepherd§0\n§0animal herder§0\n§0animal keeper§0\n§0breeder§0\n§0leather tanner\n\nleather tanner§0\n§0leather worker§0\n§0leather sewer§0\n§0Boatmaker§0\n§0Housemaker§0\n§0architect§0\n§0builder§0\n§0engraver§0\n§0inscense maker§0\n§0healer§0\n§0bottler§0\n§0preserver", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid Trial", "§bAmaron", "Name: Kyral Winterleaf§0\n§0Totem: Scroll§0\n§0Rank: Archdruid§0\n§0Kyral says that being a druid to her goes all the way back to why she originally became a dedicant. She claims she was not a good person, and wanted to serve a power greater than herself. She\n\nsought purpose, service, and redemption\n\nName: Bairre§0\n§0Totem: Shepheard§0\n§0Rank: Regular druid§0\n§0Being a druid, to Bairre, means to serve the Aspects and protect nature. He seeks to provide service and charity.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Silent Beast", "§bGuyden Aureon", "-=< Amaethonn >=-\nand The Silent Beast\n\n\n\nA story passed down amongst the tribal seeds...\n\nIn a time when the mali'ame were young, but the forests already ancient, there lived two beings.\n\nOne was Amaethon, the Great Mani Prince of the Cervidae. Amaethon was immortal and ethereal, and ruled over the stags, deer and moose\n\nof the land. He had a fondness for the Wood Elven seeds who roamed about his domains, who paid him tribute when they hunted and revered him as a demigod of the wild. It is said that he took peculiar delight in the music the elves produced.\n\nOne was was a beast named Silence- Lyu. He once had another name, now lost to time, when he was a mortal elf of the Yelvairene Seed. He was an man with no morals, whose violent nature led to the death of his son. He hunted beasts for sport\n\nand did not kill cleanly, taking pleasure in their suffering. As a punishment, the Great Mani of Lupines, Morea, cursed him to be among the realm’s first ferals.\n\nLyu was trapped permanently in his wolven form, living as a monster. It is said\n\nPrince Morea bound his soul to a sacred tree, which reformed his body whenever he died, foredooming him to immortality, an eternity to devolve into savagery. Lyu would soon lose all the mental cognition he had as an elf, remembering only his\n\nhatred for mali kind.\n\nOn the first Winter's Solstice after the transformation, the beast named Silence snuck like a shadow into the camp of the Yelvairene Seed, his former kin. There, fueled by his hatred, he killed all of the Seed's\n\nchildren while they slept. The Yelvairene were distraught, and in their mourning, prayed for a hundred days and nights to the Aspects for answers, to know who would do this to them.\n\nThe orison of the Yelvairene was answered\n\nby Amaethon, who appeared to them in the form of a ghostly stag. Who spoke unto them these words.\n\"That which did this to you was once one of you, but who has become a demon twisted by malice. The Prince of Wolves is the son of the Father,\n\nand embraces the death caused by his punishments. I, however, am a child of the Mother. I am benevolent. Play your drums and flutes for me, children of Malin, and I will come to protect you.\"\n\nIt is said that the next year,\n\non the night of the Winter's Solstice, the entire Yelvairene seed played their deerskin drums about a big bonfire all night, none pausing to rest nor sleep. Amaethon took notice of this, and when Lyu appeared in the shadows once more,\n\na mighty stag with a pure white mane came to challenge him, piercing the monster in the heart with its horns and banishing it back to the tree far away where it would regenerate.\n\nThis miracle was quick to spread. Soon every\n\nwood elven seed had heard of the tale of Amaethon and the Silent Beast. It became tradition among all the tribes to spend the entire night of the winter's solstice playing their drums and praying to the great Prince of Cervidae,\n\nso he would come protect them from Lyu, who they say to this day is still bound to his tree, regenerating every winter's solstice with malice in his heart, looking to slit the throats of children whose seeds are not protected by Amaethon’s grace.", ""),
        StaticBook("Meaning of Druid", "§bKeir Elwing", "What it means§0\n§0     To be a Druid\n\nName: Quillian§0\n§0Totem: Azalea§0\n§0Meaning of being a druid: She believes being a Druii is to see everything natural prosper. To stand fearless in the eeys of those whom would dare temper with it.\n\nName: Abelas§0\n§0Totem: Caribou§0\n§0Meaning of being a druid: He believes that spreading the faith of elnaelurir, upholding the balance through spread of an organised faith around the Aspects.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature Rules All", "§bLeomenor Sarfina", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Nature Rules All§0\n§0- Leomenor Sarfina§0\n§0~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~§0\n§0The World, baren and dead,§0\n§0The power of the Aspects spread,§0\n§0This created all the life, the plants,§0\n§0The animals, even the ants.§0\n\nThe animals began to roam,§0\n§0The mali’ame observed,§0\n§0And the aspects came of worship in their home,§0\n§0And so to nature the Druids serve.§0\n\nThe magical prowess of the Druid’s are unmatched,§0\n§0Their great Grove’s unscratched!§0\n§0They guard the Natural world, §0\n§0With its beautiful hills furled!§0\n\nNature has power of its own,§0\n§0Summoning lightning to burn the highest empires!§0\n§0The great forest-fires being shone!§0\n§0The great towers of the empires falling, their spires…§0\n\nThe power of nature is unmatched, it is loving and harsh,§0\n§0The murky, soft marsh…§0\n§0The dark, moist caves…§0\n§0And the power, crashing waves…§0\n§0\n§0Nature rules all, over time.\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Nature Rules All§0\n§0- Leomenor Sarfina§0\n§0~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~", ""),
        StaticBook("Druid task #2", "§bAmaron", "Each of the four biomes I'd encountered each presented a vast majority of conflicts, though despite the many problematic situations that confronted me, if one were to look close enough; there was beauty in everyone of nature's conflicts,\n\nblessings, and environment. I visited the Ocean, Taiga, Arctic, and Swamp biomes, and each one was unique.\n\nThe Taiga Biome:§0\n§0The Taiga was the first environtment I'd approached, and was considerably easier to thrive in than each other biome I had visited in my adventures. I still had a full supply of food and the only formidable animal I had to avoid were\n\nbears. In the Taiga were many everygreen treens, specifically spruce and pine. These two trees provided me support in solving the first major problem presented to me: The cold. At night, and during the day, this biome was unbearbly cold. I took shelter in\n\nthe hallowed trunk of a large spruce tree, and gathered a fair bit of lumber to create fires to prevent freezing in my thin robes. I ended up only needing chop down one moderately sized tree and replanted a seed immediately after. The lumber was for fire.\n\nThis environment bore rocky, infertile soil which could not provide shelter for many different plants. I found various mosses, one of which left me with a brutal rash, and a few blueberry bushes scattered about supplied me with food so I could save\n\nmy bread for upcoming, more difficult climates.§0\n§0I had little interaction with animals, considering I visited the Taiga in the midst of Winter; I saw a few small birds, and there were few insects to prey upon my skin because of the freezing temperature.\n\nBears were in hybernation, and wolves were sparse, though there was a point in which I saw a small pack of Dire Wolves from about one hundred yards away. I avoided the gathering all together. Eventually, I used my remaining supply of spruce would to craft\n\na crude, but functional, raft. I was preparing for an upcoming environment: The ocean.\n\nThe Ocean§0\n§0the Ocean was a formidable environment for its lack of. . . just about anything except lots and lots of water. The waves of the ocean were formidable, and the reefs were beautiful, housing many different species of fish such as barricudas\n\nsmall sharks, parrot fish, etc. The ocean lacked plants aside from kelp and bits of seaweed. I fortunately encountered no storms out in the high seas, but the salty waves were powerful, causing my to fall off my raft on multiple occassions, leaving the\n\nremainder of my bread soggy. In the mornings, massive whales and curious dolphins broke the surface of the water, and in the evenings the fins of sharks trailed through the waves. After much effort, I found my way back toward land using the guidance of\n\nthe gulls and moved on toward my next location: The Swamp.\n\nThe Swamp§0\n§0By the time I reached the Swampland it was the spring time, which brought forth many, many problems. Gators and Water Moccasins lurked within the murky water, and should one dare go in it, liches would feast upon there flesh. For this reason I\n\navoided these small bodies of water. It rained often in the swamps, and there were many insects, most of which left nasty bites in my skin. One spider left such a painful sensation in my calf I had to seek out blissfoil, which can be found in the swamps\n\nthough it is quite rare, to numb the area. The mire induced an odd shortness of breath, as a result of the thick, almost palpable, humidity and heat. Plants in the swamplands ranged from beautiful Cypress trees to resilient lily pads\n\nwith frogs resting upon them. The plant life is comprised mostly of trees and holds rich, damp soil. This environment was the most difficult to thrive in thus far as a result of the insects and dirty water. I'd been forced to leave my waterskin hanging\n\nfrom a tree branch over night with the hopes that it'd catch rain. Insects swarmed my skin, leaving my with anything from a small bump to large, oozing welts. However, none of this compared to the experience I'd faced in the Arctics.\n\n*Each page in the rest of the book has blotches of blood dotting them, and they are slightly soggy.*\n\nThe Arctic§0\n§0With my time in the arctic came one of the most dreadful experiences of my life, yet I was enlightened by gaining an understanding of nature's power. §0\n§0By this time I was forced to hunt for food, having eaten very sparringly up until this point.\n\nI tied a sharpened stone to a moderately lengthed, straight tree branch to create a makeshift spear. I began to search, and after around two hours I came across a rabbit all on its lonesome. I harvested its meat using my spear after muttering a brief\n\napology, then ventured to a hallowed tree trunk and started a fire to cook it, similarly to the Tundra. When I began to eat in the evening, however, a grizzly bear ventured toward my tree, peering up at me and tilting its head slightly. there was a long\n\nmoment of stillness, my hands trembling slightly before the bear reered up onto two legs and released a thunderous roar, before charing forth and smashing it's paw into the side of my face. One of its claws, unfortunately, caught my eye, completely\n\ndistorting it. I lost all vision in my left eye, and had a large scar running down my face. The bear peered down at me as I curled up in a ball and covered my head, before snorting and walking away. It did my best to prevent bleeding and the immense pain,\n\nbeginning my trek home. I could not research much regarding the plants, aside from the fact that Arctic regions are nearly inhabitable for all plant species, though it is dense with evergreen trees. The soil is covered in snow, and is stiff and rocky.", ""),
        StaticBook("First Task - Leo", "§bLeomenor Sarfina", "Circle: Naelurir§0\n§0Name: Lyeathri§0\n§0Rank: Acolyte§0\n§0Totem: Heron Druid§0\n§0What does it mean to be a Druid, answer:§0\n§0To be a druid is to uphold the balance and beauty of nature as Cernunnos and Cerridwen's children. By following in their path we\n\nrecieve a gift that is Druidism.§0\n§0\n§0~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Circle: Mother§0\n§0Name: Elswyen§0\n§0Rank: Hierophant§0\n§0Totem: Rose Druid§0\n§0Answer: Eternal Servitude (No really, that's what she said.)§0\n§0\n§0~Leomenor", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Poem", "§bAvor Forel", "Winter wonderland thawed away to silver winds and pristine greens.§0\n§0Wooded shading hides luscious prairies and babbling brook.§0\n§0Verdant shades mask the underlying visage of death and decay.§0\n§0Reach beneath these vibrant hues to take a look.\n\nPlunging from the darkness to the light come the tendrils of new beginning,§0\n§0Blight and bereavement hold no candle to the coming growth,§0\n§0Pushed aside with colossal trunks and unfurled petals,§0\n§0Holding true to the Gods’ uttered oath.\n\nSoaring above the bird calls down to meet the whispered cries,§0\n§0Consciousness ceasing to be and returning again and again, §0\n§0Demise brings forth the next chain of being, §0\n§0Never can one be without the other’s dark stain.\n\nWithin the soil beneath the next generation springs forth,§0\n§0Carried on wings, legs, hearts built with strength and valor,§0\n§0On and on they carry, without pause, without cowardice,§0\n§0Never hidden away in darkness and pallor.\n\nGrassy and fresh, the winds blow home, curving amidst stately oaks,§0\n§0Razing leaves from their divaricate homes,§0\n§0Tumbling rapids across timeworn rock and soil,§0\n§0Of this one could write a thousand tomes.\n\nBut here, dear reader, we must part,§0\n§0Of this story there is no elated or melancholy conclusion,§0\n§0For years and years it has maintained its course,§0\n§0So let us make haste and end our intrusion.", ""),
        StaticBook("Riv's First Task", "§bAvor Forel", "Druid name: Calius, The River Druid, Currently a Hierophant.§0\n§0\n§0To Calius being a druid means to serve something above yourself, seeing Nature in a higher regard than yourself and dedicating your life to the continuation of the balance.\n\nAnd apart of how he serves the aspects is teaching younger druids and some older in ways, as well as combing the land for blight and other such things.§0\n§0\n§0Calius Task:§0\n§0\n§0He asked me where grass fits into the balance in Nature.\n\nMy response was:§0\n§0\n§0In my eyes, It's food for many creatures, they also use it for bedding for their homes for warmth. It has many features for animals of this world. §0\n§0\n§0He commended me on my response.§0\n§0\n\nSecond interview With Harold.§0\n§0\n§0Rank Druid. Totem is a Hedgehog.§0\n§0\n§0Harold serves the aspects by keeping an eye out for landscars and unnatural things, To fix them is he is capable. To him, being a druid is keeping the balance and fix things\n\nHis task for me is to find catus green in the wilds. So I headed out to the Jungles near the Orc's in search for some Natural growing Weed.§0\n§0\n§0Dried out the weed to make it smokeable. Then gave Harold the Green", ""),
        StaticBook("Task One", "§bHorace", "Indra, Totem Cosmos, Hierophant.§0\n§0Means to hold respect for nature above all else, family, friends, and lovers. Nature and the aspects should come first in your life and everything you do should to aid in keeping the balance, to restore balance.\n\nTo be a Druid is to undo much of your learning. It means sometimes to forgoe acting, when you yearn to act and other times act when part of you begs for you not to. We are stewards of nature and servents of the Aspects. We are tools, their hands, in the\n\nworld around us, to restore Balance. §0\n§0\n§0Autumn Druid Nivndil, eleanilir.", ""),
        StaticBook("First Task", "§bLia", "Task: Get two druids names, order, totem, and rank within the order. Ask what it means for them to be a druid§0 and how they serve the aspects.\n\nTaelisin, Lynx, Hierophant. Being a druid means serving the aspects to defend the balance and nature. Serves the aspects by defending the balance and nature, like she said.§0\n§0Assigned Task: Hunt a worthy creature and use all of it's resources that it\n\nyields.\n\nSkale, Humming Bird, Mother Circle, Guide. For him, to be a druid means he can join his wife in the afterlife. His service is to the Earth, rather than the aspects.", ""),
        StaticBook("Theory Infinity", "§bJaya Jayamen", "A Theory of Infinity\n\n        ~Originally by\n      Geedin Jayamen\n\nLately I have begun to think about how things are connected. Take a rug for example. We see it as just one rug, but that one rug is made up of smaller things. Wool or string, which in turn is made of smaller fibers.\n\nI think that things get smaller, maybe even infinitely.\n\nJust as things can be made of smaller things, so can smaller things make up one big thing. Again with the rug example. All of those fibers make up wool or string, which make the rug.\n\nYou may think it stops there, but what is the difference between fibers to a rug then to a rug being a part of a house? The rug isn’t physically sewn into the house, yet it is part of it,\n\nthe same as the fibers not being part of a rug until it is sewn into one. \n\nThis begins a theory of Infinity. Let me give another example. Apples and oranges. They are different, right? Well that is all a matter of perspective.\n\nApples may be different then oranges, but they can still be grouped into what we call fruits. Even everything in the world, with its infinite different parts and pieces, can be grouped into what we call, well, the world.\n\nWe may seem to think of the smaller things as making more sense when we should really be looking towards the bigger.\n\nNow let’s get a bit deeper.\n\nWhat is the difference between yourself and someone else? Well, there are differences, but there are also similarities. Well let’s think, what would make you two the same?\n\nWell the thing is, you are part of something that doesn’t make you the same, but groups you into one thing.\n\nNow for the trick. Say we are seeking all knowledge of things. We learn more and more, yet never are satisfied with what we know,\n\nbecause there is always more to know. Well we are probably looking for some way or some knowledge that can make us content. I think I may have realized what that something is. It is something complex,\n\nyet simple at the same time. It is everything, and it is also one thing. Imagine everything, every thought, every physical and non-physical thing connected into one, single, thing. That is Infinity.\n\nYou may be asking, how are we connected? I may ask you the opposite, how are we disconnected? As a rug is made up of fibers, so are you made up of flesh and bone.\n\nIf everything can be made up of infinitely smaller things and infinitely bigger things can be made of those smaller things, what does that leave, but the fact of infinity?", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "I would like to buy a home here in Holm.§0\n§0\n§0One of the chests in my room is locked.§0\n§0\n§0So do you have a lead on where these September Prince trees may be?§0\n§0\n§0What dangers come from the South?\n\nLet's head back to the Dominion then... Would that put us closer to the Wonka Wood?\n\nHow do I become a citizen of Holm?§0\n§0\n§0Yes. I was given a resident home but now I'd like a more official one in the city. Please.\n\nI'm just looking for books about the Veil.§0\n§0\n§0I'm just curious about them. I've heard that they have tormented the Frozen South, and I wanted to learn more about them.\n\nI'm just curious about the glowing blade that that person holds.\n\nChallenge them to a duel. Prove your honor that way, not by thr\n\nAs the bitter snow flows down, outside the gates of Caras Eldar, a shrouded and armored figure of white and black, bearing standards of colors same, approached on horseback.§0\n§0\n§0We stood in bewilderment, approaching carefully as it spoke its foreign tongue.\n\n\"Tae vand?\"§0\n§0\"Sahar vol.\"§0\n§0\n§0It says these words.§0\n§0Then, with the same tone, it says, \"Low chant?\"§0\n§0\n§0I will offer my journal to the Vaeyl. Perhaps it can write?\n\n\n§0Taefyinn vyrt hae dril un oir ys ydyr§0\n§0\n§0[The above was written in a flowing, cursive language. Below it, something much clumsily was scrawled.]§0\n§0\n§0reds§0\n§0\n§0endmoor\n\nEndmoor. Portal. Caution. Darkways. Reds.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "\"Aye, so... what is the best way to put this... I may have taught your daughter how to say \"feckin'.\" I am greatly sorry about this, but lil lass would have never had known if the father did not panic at the word. Regardless, I am still sorry.\"§0\n§0 -Urdreth", ""),
