        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Orrar'sair Quota Ledger. Write your name and the date of your deposit.§0", ""),
        StaticBook("\"Cringey note", "§bThorvn Bronwyn", "Dear Nathan,\nI am writing this because Im am not really sure how to tell you this in person but i-i really like you and i want to be with you but im scared to tell you because you might reject me and im scared if that happeneds.\nI wish i could ask you\n\nwhat you though of me or even if Karina will ever come back.\nDue to this i wanted to tell you something i was scared to tell you i dyed my hair blue because i was picked on by children and adults in the orphange and i hid my ears more as time passed.\n\nBut when i met you made me smile and you also make me very happy at how you never screamed and hurt me because i was a elf.\nThat was the best thing that ever happened to me then i slowly feel for you and i really wanted to make you happy even\n\nif it was without me in your life i beleved you loved Karina and i tried to give the two of you a chance to talk about everything that happened between you two and she gave up, like i did years ago and Im worried about how she is doing now because\n\nshe came back to see me and then left after asking me to go with her but saying i was able to still see you and see her all the time but she wouldn't ever come back im not sure what to do anymore im so worried and i wish i knew what to do but i do love\n\nyou.\nBut its your chose to accept this or not to except my feelings for you.\n\ni may not remeber what this is about because i wrote this well i was drunk", ""),
        StaticBook("Hunting Codex", "§bThorvn Bronwyn", "\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 The Beginners Guide§0\\\n§0        §0\\\n§0      To Hunting In §0\\\n§0\\\n§0           Atlas,§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0  By Thorvn Bronywn§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Table of Contents:§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Introduction§0 Pg 3§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Gear Pgs 4-6§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Beastiary Pgs 7-21§0\\\n§0\\\n§0General tips Pgs 22-27§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Minas! Pgs 28-32\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------        Introduction§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 If you are reading this, then you wish to     be a hunter nae? §0\\\n§0\\\n§0 Tired of getting yer arse handed to you on  every single hunt?§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 THEN LOOK NAE FURTHER THAN THIS!§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------      Gear consulting§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 For fighting most within Atlas, one must be very careful of ones surroundings, yer armor must not be too light to take blows while nae heavy to prevent mobility, one must find a balance with medium styled -\"\n\n\"armor (iron armor for example)§0\\\n§0\\\n§0And a well shaped shield to defect the multitude of projectiles beasts may have at their disposal, be it poison, barbs, bladed quills, feathers, even arrows from fellow man. One must be prepared properly to survive-\"\n\n\"this cruel world we call Atlas.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Blades are standardly kept sharp and without rust, to survive this harsh world one must be at top shape, this also applies to their weapons, don't be afraid to take extra, theres no shame in being prepared.§0\\\n§0--------oOo----\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------        Beastiary§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Boggens PG 10§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Bog Beasts PG 11§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Bilespitters PG 12§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Boars PG 13§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Bandits PG 14§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Direwolves PG 15\"\n\n\"--------oOo-------- Gravens PG 16§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Harpies PG 17§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Minotaurs PG 18§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Nullers PG 19§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Rogue Golems PG 20§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Mirror Slimes PG 21§0\\\n§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------         Add future §0\\\n§0 Content here: Beasts\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------          Boggens§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 These creatures are deceptive, use wisps to lure in prey (exp) and attack with no warning, best bring a friend, because these are near lethal to any unsuspecting target, caution advised.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------         Bog Beasts§0\\\n§0  §0\\\n§0   Near undead like swamp creatures, upon death they release a toxic mist, avoidable with enough caution, bring the beasts within the water to make the situation managable, low threat.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0        Bilespitters§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 Spiders that entrap their victims within webbing and shoot toxic substance to wear them down from afar, these beasts are to be fought with ranged weaponry, beware their numbers, moderate threat.§0\\\n§0--------oOo--\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------        Boar/Treeboar§0\\\n§0\\\n§0   Piggies, in short these mounds of bacon are fast and hit swift, bring them over river to get an edge, tree boars are to be strictly used with arrows, they, are, DEADLY. Moderate threat.§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------           Bandits§0\\\n§0   §0\\\n§0    Man gone sour, these pilfering humanoids use axe and bow, bring your own friends to combat these evil briggands, search for the fabeled berserker axe.  Moderate threat §0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------         Direwolves§0\\\n§0  §0\\\n§0    Violent pups that depend on swarm tactics to overwhelm their target, fast, hard hitting, and deadly, these beasts need to be in water to cripple, beware the vibrant howl, high threat.§0\\\n§0--------oOo--------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------          Gravens§0\\\n§0    §0\\\n§0    Spectral beings of bone and corruption, shields are useless against piercing arrows, usually found in pairs, these beasts spawn vex's upon death, beware of their fast movement as well. Moderate threat.§0\\\n§0-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------           Harpies§0\\\n§0  §0\\\n§0   Humanoid avian shaped creatures with a desire for flesh, they bolt upon being hit, soar into the air and shower their target with bladed feathers, shield HIGHLY advised. Moderate threat.§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------          Minotaurs§0\\\n§0   §0\\\n§0     Hulking bovine humanoid hybrids that overwhelm with charging force that launches targets into the air, shield advised to block the hit, along with a-lot of men.§0\\\n§0 EXTREMELY HIGH THREAT§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------           Nullers§0\\\n§0\\\n§0       Small, but make up in modest number that use neurotoxin to take down their targets, armor is useless against them,  beware stage 3 of toxin, reccomend kiting tactics, low-moderate threat.§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------       Rogue Golems§0\\\n§0 §0\\\n§0 Constructs gone Awol, these beings are to be put down with modest ease, multiple blades reccomended, so is armor, beware of seeping magic that causes you to levitate on hit, seek higher §0ground.Modest§0 threat§0\\\n§0------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------      Mirror Slimes§0\\\n§0     (Spiegelschleim)§0\\\n§0  §0\\\n§0  Germatic myth, these slimes upon death mimic their killer be it full armor or not, bring friends, they reflect full damage upon their image if struck by them.  Modest-high threat.§0\\\n§0-----\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------       General advice§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-When fighting nullers, encircle the swarm and use swords to chip them down, they're much weaker taken in that manner.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-When fighting golems, kite downhill compared to upward, if struck by magic, you'll have a -\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------much better time surviving, bring multiple swords too, they go surprisingly fast.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-When fighting Direwolves bring them to water, their numbers don't mean shite when they can barely swim as a pack.§0\\\n§0--------oOo--------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-When fighting Bandits, theres a rare chance to get a berserker axe as a drop, this does more damage than an iron sword, yet lowers your armor, it's a double sided weapon.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-When combating Gravens, consider-\"\n\n\"--------oOo-------- using arrows against them, they have a habit of moving away in Melee distance.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-When fighting boars, beware of the trees, they tend to hide up there.. for some odd reason.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-Fighting bilespitters is as simple to fighting -\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------spiders, only they spit toxins and are much more suspectable to ranged fire.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0-Consider using different tomes at Cloud temple, Huntress's codex for more damage while luck be a lady for better drop chance  -§0\\\n\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Along with Kips lucky pebble, it'll make rare drops that much more common, T'was a fleshwound for extra bleedout time, 20 seconds makes all the more difference.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0(Not dying for dummies optional.)§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------       Making Minas§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 Aye, the real part of the guide, one doesn't become a hunter without knowing what's the most profitable aye?  In short, theres only a specific list of profit with hunting, the rest are insignificant.§0\\\n§0--------oOo---\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------  §0\\\n§0   -Nullers §0\\\n§0\\\n§0   Their drops are normally very profitable and common,  8 minas per common drop and 150 per rare gland, they're awfully nice, for such a low threat monster, beware of crowding!§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------           §0\\\n§0\\\n§0   -Bandits§0\\\n§0\\\n§0   Usually with cordinated effort their drops are 'normally' profitable, one can sell or buy a berserker axe within Haense for 750/1000§0\\\n§0Minas respectively.§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------      §0\\\n§0\\\n§0    -Rogue golems§0\\\n§0\\\n§0    Their main drop being more rare than secondary, 100 minas is no joke, along with the bundles of iron (16) and scrap additions, this is a secondary profit from nullers. \"\n\n\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0     -Direwolves?§0\\\n§0\\\n§0   Subjective, but when lead to water these puppies are helpless, be sure to collect EVERY drop, heads are a good sum, so are teeth. 80/10 pelts are only 2 but come with great abundance.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0--------oOo-------\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------        Conclusion§0\\\n§0\\\n§0   If followed many steps, one should be rolling in Minas as a successful hunter, the tomes may be expensive but in the longrun that much more worth the risk.§0\\\n§0Remember, friends make every hunt better!§0\\\n§0--------oOo----\"\n\n\"--------oOo--------§0\\\n§0\\\n§0 Book by: Jdesarno/§0\\\n§0\\\n§0   Thorvn Bronwyn§0\\\n§0\\\n§0    Original topic: §0https://www.lordofthecraft.net/forums/topic/175132-feedback-on-hunting-and-its-issues/§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Made 8/11 !\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Bronzesmithing 1", "§bQuavinir", "\"    Bronzesmithing:§0\\\n§0      Volume 1§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0by Quavinir Twiceborn\"\n\n\"Bronzesmithing, or 'bronze casting' is the art of creating objects made of the metallic alloy made of bronze. Hopefully, this book series should give the reader enough knowledge on the subject of bronzesmithing that the reader, if they so chose, would be\"\n\n\"able to bear enough knowledge as to be able to take up the trade themselves. The writer of this book has been a bronzesmith for decades, and due to consistant requests to be a mentor or take on a apprentice as to share his knowledge on the subject, \"\n\n\"has chosen to instead write a series of books containing all the prerequisite knowledge of bronzesmithing. The writer of this book does not claim that this book will give the reader all the needed skills and abilities to be a bronzesmith, but rather\"\n\n\"that this book contains all the knowledge needed in which for one to begin practicing bronzesmithing themselves.§0\\\n§0With that out of the way, lets begin.\"\n\n\"       Chapter 1:§0\\\n§0   What The Heck is§0\\\n§0         BRONZE§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0The alloy known as bronze is a mixture of two somewhat common metals, copper, and tin. Copper is a brownish red metal, that is notably weak, yet light. It is considered by many\"\n\n\"to be a trash metal, incapable of making anything of value aside from ornaments. And for the most part, that is a correct judgement. Copper is a relatively soft, and light metal. Things that are made of pure copper tend to bend excessively easy, though\"\n\n\"they do not rust, rather taking on a light greenish coating called a 'patena' (though there will be more on that later.) §0\\\n§0\\\n§0Copper for the most part is not very useful on it's own (aside from the claims of 'conductivity' that many redstone workers\"\n\n\"claim it bears) and alone is not very useful.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Tin is a light, silvery metal that can bear a notable shine. Tin, like bronze is not a exceptionally useful metal either. Though it is somewhat harder than copper, it still is not hard enough to be very\"\n\n\"useful in the construction of tools and armor. Tin unlike bronze can rust, and it's rust can corrode whatever it infects. §0\\\n§0\\\n§0Both copper and tin alone are not very useful. But together, they're fantastic.\"\n\n\"Much as a touch of yeast can cause dough to rise, a small amount of tin mixed with copper causes its potential to explode. When mixed together, the alloy formed is extremely durable, yet malleable. The standard allocation for the creation of bronze is\"\n\n\"1 part tin, for every 9 parts of copper. Though, this allocation can be tweaked as to allow higher quality bronze. §0\\\n§0\\\n§0The standard I myself use, is 12% tin and 88% copper. This allocation in my opinion is the most suited for the casting of tools and\"\n\n\"weapons, and the bronze produced from such is notably more hardy than the standard allocation. §0\\\n§0\\\n§0For armor, which must be beat into shape, the most advantageous allocation is 6% tin, and 94% copper. Any less copper and the metal shall be notably \"\n\n\"weaker, any more tin and the bronze shall be too rigid to effectively beat into shape, and upon eing beat into shape will form corners and ridges that will ruin the consistancy of the metal, and lead to imperfections and weaknesses.\"\n\n\"The standard I use for the allocation of metals is by weight. I weigh each of the metals as equal and allocate them as if they weighed the same for the parts. If I were to make 100 pounds of casting bronze, I would allocate 88 pounds of copper and \"\n\n\"12 pounds of tin. For sizing however, many may see this as odd. Tin is lighter than bronze by a small margin, and I have observed other smiths attempting to allocate the amounts of tin and bronze by total area of said metal. This as far as I can tell is\"\n\n\"folly, and I advise the reader to allocate their bronze mixtures strictly by weight.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Though the mixture of tin and copper is what is widely considered to be 'bronze', there are various other copper based alloys that are considered a type of 'bronze'.\"\n\n\"Arsenical Bronze is a alloy made between copper, and the dangerous stone known as 'arsenic'. It produces a black metal that is quite durable, and able to be cast efficiently. However, the smelting and mixing of these two metals is liable to cause toxic \"\n\n\"fumes that are able to poison, and kill those who inhale such. The Kharajyr who used such bronze in times past claimed it as some form of judgement from their god Metzli, viewing any who caught the sickness from the fumes or died from such judged as \"\n\n\"unworthy to work the metal by Metzli.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Another alloy with copper is that whhich is known as 'brass'. Brass is a alloy between copper and the mineral known as zinc. It is notably less hardy than standard bronze, though it notably presents very\"\n\n\"little friction with other pieces of brass, making it a commonly used material for those who make machinery or contraptions. Brass likewise bears a notably acoustic property to it, making it one of the more ideal choices for the use of musical instruments\"\n\n\"that one blows into to make sound. Brass however is somewhat weaker than bronze, and the mineral zinc is far more difficult to properly refine, hence the smithing of brass weapons and tools is not exceptionally viable.\"\n\n\"        Chapter 2:§0\\\n§0    What You'll Need§0\\\n§0\\\n§0         /---\\\\§0\\\n§0      _/   0   \\\\_§0\\\n§0  §0\\\n§0 =]      <====[____]§0\\\n§0 ||   i^i__________§0\\\n§0 ||   = ----------§0\\\n§0 ||   VV§0\\\n§0===================\"\n\n\"The art of bronzesmithing, despite ones initial impressions can be somewhat complex, and there are many tools and skills needed in which to do such effectively. §0\\\n§0The most important 2 items you shall need in which to start casting bronze is a \"\n\n\"furnace, or kiln, and than a smelting bucket.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0A smelting kiln, or furnace, is what you shall use to to heat the metal that you shall be working with. Given the standard means of making things out of bronze is casting, the process of melting the\"\n\n\"metal so you may be able to pour it into a mold is essential, and the tool used in doing such is your most valuable tool.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0The most effective means of making a furnace is to make one out of clay. \"\n\n\"A clay furnace is somewhat simple, yet its construction may well not be. I advise speaking with an accomplished builder, (due to the construction of a clay furnace not being too far away from that of a small building) \"\n\n\"One must first bear a suitably large area, a small clay furnace will usually be a bit over a meter wide, and around 2 meters tall if done properly. You will start with making a roughly 15-20 centimeter thick-§\"\n\n\"wall of clay, around a§0\\\n§045-to-60 centimeter circle. You will begin building up, keeping the wall consistant for roughly 60 centimeters, taking a break after about every 10 centimeters to light a fire in the center as to bake the clay to being hard. \"\n\n\"Depending on the make up of the clay in your area this may take as little as an hour, though much clay may take longer. I advise keeping a fire lit for roughly 4 or so hours, as to ensure that each section of the wall is fully baked.\"\n\n\"After the first 10 centimeters of construction, you will need to make a small arch shaped wooden frame, roughly 28 centimeters high, and place that onto the clay wall as for where your opening for the furnace may be. Likewise, I advide making 2 small \"\n\n\"circular wooden frames, (no larger than 5 centimeters in diameter) and placing them at the sides of the furnace walls, right as you begin contruction, as for holes for a billows, or furnace blower. (Though, I know a few others who prefer to drill holes\"\n\n\"in the kiln at a later time for the forge blowers, though I prefer to have them built into the furnace.)§0\\\n§0\\\n§0At about the 60 centimeter mark, you will want to lower the thickness of the walls gradually, lowering the thickness by roughly 1 centimeter\"\n\n\"for every 10 centimeters you build up. You shall do this as you build up the clay walls for roughly 50 centimeters (making so upon completion of this step, your furnace is 110 centimeters tall, and the walls between 15-10 centimeters thick.)\"\n\n\"After that, you will need to start building inward slightly, this is diffcult, and I advise one to do such carefully. You will need to build with the walls curved inward,  creating an internal dome. Moving roughly 3 centimeters inward for every 10 you \"\n\n\"build upward. This will take time, and many choose to use a wooden lattice, or frame to aid in this, though it is well possible §0without.You§0 will do this for an additional 60 centimeters upward, going 10 centimeters at a time.\"\n\n\"Upon completing this, you should bear a 160 centimeter tall kiln, with a 24 centimeter large opening at the top. Before continueing, coat the inside of the  kiln with roughly one centimeter of clay (or less, it is difficult to measure such at this \"\n\n\"juncture) and ensure that the inside of the kiln is consistant and smooth, with as few ridges or abnormalities as possible. Afer doing the inside, do the same with the outside, ensuring the kiln is smooth.\"\n\n\"After this, you will need to fasion a cone of clay separately, being atleast 7 centimeters thick at the bottom, with a bottom opening at about 24 centimeters in diameter. Make the cone atleast 30 centimeters tall, with atop opening atleast 16 centimeters\"\n\n\"wide. Attach the cone to the top of the kiln with wet clay, again bake the clay into place. Remove the wooden frame for the door, and the frames (if you chose to make them) for the holes for the forge blowers. Take the frame you made for the door, and \"\n\n\"make a flat clay door, of atleast 6 centimeters in thickness in the dimensions of the door frame, as to fit in the entrance. Cut an L shaped wooden stand for the door, and press it into the wet clay before baking. The L shaped stand should\"\n\n\"be roughly as tall as the door, and the L shaped wooden stand should be placed into the clay --> L flat side first.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Fire up the kiln again, using any forge blowers, or billows you're able to puchase. Coals should be sufficient in which\"\n\n\"to gain the heat capable of melting bronze.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Place in the center of the kiln a small clay circle, (probably only about 5 centimeters in height, and about 18 centimeters in diameter) as for you to place your smelting bucket for your bronze.\"\n\n\"When firing the kiln for use of smelting bronze, ensure that you do not place the coals on the stand for the smelting bucket, as accidentally leaving such on the stand when placing the bucket may cause the bucket to tip over, and fall down, spilling\"\n\n\"the contained alloy all over the hot coals.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0As for the smelting bucket, one can fashion one out of clay much in a similar way the kiln was made out of. The size of the bucket can vary madly, and I advise the smith to experiment with different sizes.\"\n\n\"If one has the availability of such, I would advise having a smelting bucket carved from soapstone. Soapstone is an exceptionally good material for bronze casting, as it is both resistant to melting, and capable of transmiting heat through itself.\"\n\n\"Soapstone is a blackish grey stone, with a decent hardness. It bears veins of black and white through it. Most miners worth their pickaxe should be able to identify, and procure soapstone for you, should it be available in the area of their work.\"\n\n\"Casting molds, like the bucket, can likewise be made of clay, or more preferably soapstone. However, most clay molds, even if well made, will not be able to be used more than once, given that once solidified, bronze tends to stick to the clay, requiring-\"\n\n\"one to break the mold the item is cast in after the bronze has cooled.§0\\\n§0\\\n§0Soapstone, on the other hand, may have molds carved out of them, and when cooled, the bronze does not stick to the stone as much, if at all. Allowing the bronze casts \"\n\n\"to be removed easillY (Especilly if in a two part mold that can be opened up.).§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0\\\n§0CONTINUED IN VOLUME                2\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Payed Tax -Joker", "§bHekkaes 'Anvilaxe' Goldhand", "\"Payed the tax for you bro, i saw you werent active in a lot of days so i payed you the ct tax, Merry Christmas§0\\\n§0                    -JokerLow\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Tenent One:§0\n§0\n§0Protect Brothern.§0\n§0\n§0Assisting other clerics, despite emotions and other drama is important.\n\nTenent Two:§0\n§0\n§0Do not stray from the faith.§0\n§0\n§0You can only pray and worship to Lord Tahariae. If you faith wavers, he will know.\n\nTenant Three:§0\n§0\n§0Guide the Lost§0\n§0\n§0This holding to those who have not found the light yet, roaming between in the gray.\n\nTenant Four:§0\n§0\n§0Do not harm, kill, or shed blood of innocents.§0\n§0\n§0Walk a thin line, and always try to handle situations without violence.\n\nTenant Five:§0\n§0\n§0Do not abuse his gift§0\n§0\n§0Do not misuse the gift given to you by asking for money for it, or otherwise. Your gift is to help people.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Ryzar's Edge§0\n§0       Record Keeping§0\n§0\n§0Co-Owners§0\n§0  Sathoro S.§0\n§0  Nailyn R.§0 (Former)§0\n§0  Yven S.\n\nSale No. 1§0\n§0\n§0Unnamed [mcname]§0\n§0\n§0- Conditioning (35m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 35m + Tip\n\nSale No. 2§0\n§0\n§0Azoth [Zilldude]§0\n§0\n§0- Quick Trim (25m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 25m + Tip\n\nSale No. 3§0\n§0\n§0Vladimir [mcname]§0\n§0\n§0- Raevir Fade (150m)§0\n§0- The Duke (50m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 200m\n\nSale No. 4§0\n§0\n§0Timothy S. [melvinola]§0\n§0\n§0- Raevir Fade (175m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 175m + Tip\n\nSale No. 5§0\n§0\n§0Maevin [mcname]§0\n§0\n§0- Tribal (150m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 150m + Tip\n\nSale No. 6§0\n§0\n§0Aewion [MadYeMagician]§0\n§0\n§0- Hunter (200m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 200m + Tip?\n\nSale No. 7§0\n§0\n§0Osbert [June8th793]§0\n§0\n§0- Duke (50m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 50m\n\nSale No. 8§0\n§0\n§0Smano [MyNameIsMason]§0\n§0\n§0- Noble Cut (150m)§0\n§0- The Duke (50m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 200m\n\nSale No. 9§0\n§0\n§0Lithaeril [Lithaeril]§0\n§0\n§0- Hunter (180m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 180m + Tip\n\nWeek No. 1§0\n§0\n§0Total: 1215m§0\n§0\n§0This Week's Tax: Paid!§0\n§0\n§0Net Total: 1215m§0\n§0\n§0300xp Earned...§0\n§0   Level 2 Unlocked!§0\n§0\n§0\"Nice First Week!\"\n\nSale No. 10§0\n§0\n§0Magnus [Jondead]§0\n§0\n§0- Dye Removal (75m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 75m\n\nSale No.11§0\n§0\n§0Elora [UndoneGravy]§0\n§0\n§0- Length Trim (50m)§0\n§0- Conditioning (100m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 150m\n\nSale No.12§0\n§0\n§0Thillna [Anna_Lynx]§0\n§0\n§0- The Maiden Fair (150m)§0\n§0\n§0- Hair Dye (75m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 225m\n\nSale No.13§0\n§0\n§0Arnorian [Radahan]§0\n§0\n§0- Beard Weed (20m)§0\n§0- The Duke (10m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 30m\n\nSale No.14§0\n§0\n§0Quavinir [Auric_Saint]§0\n§0\n§0- Tribal Cut (200m)§0\n§0- Hair Dyeing (50m)§0\n§0- Bear Weed, Goatee (50m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 300m\n\nSale No.15§0\n§0\n§0Rycel Aquille [AwkwardQueen]§0\n§0\n§0- Quick Trim (25m)§0\n§0\n§0Total: 25m\n\nSale No.16§0\n§0\n§0Ithuriel [the_sacred_text]§0\n§0\n§0- Hair S