        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Rulan's Mixtape #1§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Straight outta Khel'Seth§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Produced by Erolas Bai'kana\n\nTrack Listing§0\n§0\n§01. Bok Bok§0\n§02. Lapis Gold§0\n§03. R.U.L.A.N. Freestyle§0\n§04. Khel' Air§0\n§05. Trial§0\n§06. Astro§0\n§07. High§0\n§08. R.U.L.A.N. pt. 2\n\nBok Bok§0\n§0\n§0Flexing with my mina§0\n§0Jumpin' on a Beamah§0\n§0Water goin' to a stream ah§0\n§0Running so fast you cant see us. §0\n§0My Bok Bok so fast§0\n§0leave yo' ass in the grass§0\n§0so fast like a flash§0\n§0Leaving Warhawkes in the past\n\nMy Bok Bok so fresh§0\n§0Rep Goldar till death§0\n§0He my boy, yeah he flesh§0\n§0Its more man he the best.\n\nLapis Gold§0\n§0\n§0Mining through the sheets§0\n§0Wool turning into fleece§0\n§0Gold falling from the trees§0\n§0Metoers fall with ease§0\n§0A gift from the gods§0\n§0Falling like a rock§0\n§0But we can't stop §0\n§0Lapis Gold on the drop. (skrrt skrrt)", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "§oA young man walked through the forest, with his quiver and hunting bow.§0\n§0\n§0§oHe heard a young girl singing, and followed the sound below.§0\n§0\n§0§oThere he found the Maiden, who lives in the Willow.\n\n§oHe called to her as she listened, from a ring of toadstools red.§0\n§0\n§0§o\"Come with me my Maiden, come from thy willow bed.\"§0\n§0\n§0§oShe looked at him serenely, and only shook her head.\n\n\"See me now...\"§0\n§0§oA ray of light in the Moondance.§0\n§0\"See me now...\"§0\n§0§oI cannot leave this place.§0\n§0\n§0\"Hear me now...\"§0\n§0§oA strain of song in the Forest.§0\n§0\"Dont ask §0me...to§0 follow where you lead.\"\n\n§oA young man walked through the forest, with a flower and coat of green.§0\n§0\n§0§oHis love had hair like fire, her eyes an emerald sheen.§0\n§0\n§0§oShe wrapped herself in beauty, so young and so serene.\n\n§oHe stood there under the Willow, and he gave her the yellow bloom.§0\n§0\n§0§o\"Girl my heart you've captured, oh I would be your Groom.\"§0\n§0\n§0§oShe said she'd wed him never, not near, nor far, nor soon.\n\n\"See me now...\"§0\n§0§oA ray of light in the Moondance.§0\n§0\"See me now...\"§0\n§0§oI cannot leave this place.§0\n§0\n§0\"Hear me now...\"§0\n§0§oA strain of song in the Forest.§0\n§0\"Dont ask §0me...to§0 follow where you lead.\"\n\n§oA young man walked through the forest, with an axe sharp as a knife.§0\n§0\n§0§o\"I'll take the green-eyed fairy, and she shall be my wife.§0\n§0\n§0§oWith her I'll raise my children, with her I'll live my life.\"\n\n§oThe Maiden wept when she heard him, when he said he'd set her free.§0\n§0\n§0§oHe took his axe and used it, to bring down her ancient tree.§0\n§0\n§0§o\"Now your willow's fallen, now you belong to me.\"\n\n\"See me now...\"§0\n§0§oA ray of light in the Moondance.§0\n§0\"See me now...\"§0\n§0§oI cannot leave this place.§0\n§0\n§0\"Hear me now...\"§0\n§0§oA strain of song in the Forest.§0\n§0\"Dont ask §0me...to§0 follow where you lead.\"\n\n§oShe followed him out the forest, and collapsed upon the earth.§0\n§0\n§0§oHer feet had walked but a distance, from the green land of her birth.§0\n§0\n§0§oShe faded into a flower, that would bloom for one bright Eve.\n\n§oHe could not take from the forest...§0\n§0\n§0§oWhat was never meant to leave.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "§oA young man walked through the forest, with his quiver and hunting bow.§0\n§0\n§0§oHe heard a young girl singing, and followed the sound below.§0\n§0\n§0§oThere he found the Maiden, who lives in the Willow.\n\n§oHe called to her as she listened, from a ring of toadstools red.§0\n§0\n§0\"Come with me my Maiden, come from thy Willow bed.\"§0\n§0\n§0§oShe looked at him serenley, and only shook her head.\n\n\"See me now...§0\n§0A ray of light in the Moondance.§0\n§0See me now...§0\n§0I cannot leave this place.\"§0\n§0\n§0\"Hear me now...§0\n§0A strain of song in the Forest.§0\n§0Dont ask me...§0\n§0To follow where you lead.\"\n\n§oA young man walked through the forest, with a flower and coat of green.§0\n§0\n§0§oHis love had hair like fire, her eyes an emerald sheen.§0\n§0\n§0§oShe wrapped herself in beauty, so young and so serene.\n\n§oHe stood there under the Willow, and he gave her the yellow bloom.§0\n§0\n§0\"Girl my heart you've captured, oh I would be your Groom.\"§0\n§0\n§0§oShe said she'd wed him never, not near, nor far, nor soon.\n\n\"See me now...§0\n§0A ray of light in the moondance.§0\n§0See me now...§0\n§0I cannot leave this place.\"§0\n§0\n§0\"Hear me now...§0\n§0A strain of song in the Forest.§0\n§0Dont ask me...§0\n§0To follow where you lead.\"\n\n§oA young man walked through the forest, with an axe sharp as a knife.§0\n§0\n§0\"I'll take the green eyed fairy, and she shall be my wife.\"§0\n§0\n§0\"With her I'll raise my children, with her I'll live my life.\"\n\n§oThe Maiden wept when she head him, when he said he'd set her free.§0\n§0\n§0§oHe took his axe and used it, to bring down her Ancient Tree.§0\n§0\n§0\"Now your Willow's fallen, now  you belong to me!\"\n\n\"See me now...§0\n§0A ray of light in the Moondance.§0\n§0See me now...§0\n§0I cannot leave this place.\"§0\n§0\n§0\"Hear me now...§0\n§0A strain of song in the Forest.§0\n§0Dont ask me...§0\n§0To follow where you lead.\"\n\n§oShe followed him out the forest,§0\n§0§oand collapsed upon the earth.§0\n§0\n§0§oHer feet had walked but a distance, from the green land of her birth.\n\n§oShe faded into a flower, that would bloom for one bright Eve.§0\n§0\n§0§oHe could not take from the Forest, what was never meant to leave.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Name:§0 Felix§0\n§0\n§0Dedicated his life to serving an Aengul called Tahariae§0\n§0\n§0Is a cleric and helps heal and protect the inoccent§0\n§0\n§0Is homeless but hangs around Sutica and Dominion (I thought he was from the\n\nDominion at first because that is where I found him)§0\n§0\n§0Was born on a continent that was destroyed§0\n§0\n§0Race: Elf", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "*The small booklet here has a few scribbles, drawings and such, and a couple of actual leaves plastered to it. Between it's folds are written words, rather neatly drawn out.*\n\nThe reason I, Saoirse af Orvar,§0\n§0wish to be a druid is because I want to connect better with nature than I already am.§0\n§0\n§0The search, understanding, and bond with nature I wish to obtain is strong within me. Ketiley Strong-Heart af Orvar and Ruari Hoarfrost\n\naf Orvar are both the Direwolf and Eagle druid. Ketiley is my Grandmother, and Ruari my Father. So I have been raised around nature and the love it brings me my entire life.§0\n§0\n§0Even when I was just six, I knew I wanted to fllow in my Father's footsteps,\n\nbecause to be a servant of nature, of Andarta, the children of the forest, and the Aspects. It is a humble one. One I want to be a part of. A path I have always dreamed of.§0\n§0\n§0I have more animal friends than I do people, but that's not really an issue.\n\nAnimals, and their well being, are my thought. High on the list of importance to me. It's the one thing I keep striving for. The one thing that keeps me going.§0\n§0\n§0Druidism is something I've dreamed to be a part of. And I'm willing to bind my soul with the\n\ngods and the Aspects to be a part of it.", ""),
        StaticBook("To My Chieftess", "§bRulan Ryvienne", "YEEEEET NIGGGGAAAA", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "[!] The writing is slightly scruffy, though neat enough to read.§0\n§0\n§0\n\nWhat has happened these lowly years I situated myself behind these walls. It was once a place I took so much pride in.. So much love and warmth for the people around. §0\n§0\n§0But now even the walls whisper, people plot, scheme and lash out. Some of the people\n\nI called family I barely even recognize. The circles think of me as powr hungry, as the true enemy. But they have no idea.. How much I literally bleed and shed tears. How much I fight when there's none left in me.§0\n§0\n§0But maybe I am the problem? There's\n\nso much uncertainty, so much I am trying to take on. But I cannot see my wrongs, what could I have possibly done to deem being schemed against...§0\n§0\n§0My sons, dead. Yet I continue to serve my duty to the circles. To both.. Yet, of course it's never\n\nnoticed. All my hard work and goodness is shoved to a dark part of one's mind and instead they seem to feed from the mistakes I make. And yes, there are many of them.§0\n§0\n§0But whom doesn't?§0\n§0\n§0It's all like a loud voice screeching down my ear.\n\nConstantly and on repeat.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "A Bard's Observations§0\n§0\n§05th Sun's Smile§0\n§0\n§0Looks like a wood elf lad, a highlander fella, and a woman have gotten into a tiffy at the cloud temple. Something about disrespect and robbing the dead and all. Could make for a nifty story or poem!\n\nTurns out the highlander fella's a slaver! Oh I can bet the woodelf ain't gonna like that. Oh he's redder than a ripe apple on harvest day.§0\n§0\n§0Well the slaver lad talked a big talk but ultimately exited stage left in a bit of a hurried fashion. The elf\n\nis right angry, looks like he clenched his fists so tight he drew blood. Poor fellow, can't imageine something making me that angry. The lady is trying to calm down the woodelf, bless. Seems an important lass, might wanna follow these two, find a story.\n\nShe's a noblewoman! And the marshall of Aldenburg! Good grief and that boy done come out as a slaver right to her! Good luck to him trying to get through the Empire unscathed!§0\n§0Seems woodelves feel things more than strongly than I once thought. Interesting\n\n14th Sun's Smile§0\n§0\n§0Big crowd in the capitol, had an easy enough time getting in tho, innocent looking little me. I heard a rumor of a lich bears investigating? Figure they're using hyperbole but you never know do you?§0\n§0\n§0I seen a giant lumbering around\n\nthe capital! Looks like the drawings I seen of Ologs, but not quite so big, ugly as sin but I think thats just all of them. Love to be proven wrong. Imagine? Little ol' me and a Olog lass? I'd get lost! \"The Ballad of The Tall and the Short?\" eh, rubbish.\n\nWell now this is interesting. I'm sitting here on a ledge writing down some old drinking songs when in marches a whole band of farlanders! They say they're on the way to Sutica, ask for conscripts! A green banner marks them, and they're shouting the glory\n\nof Allah. I'm certain no good can come og this politically. But my craft is driven by war and glory and romance, and I suspect I may find all of these in this campaign! I'm going to follow them.§0\n§0\n§0I've followed the group up the hill to another plaza, where\n\nthey appear to be talking to some noble type. I'll try to keep my distance.§0\n§0\n§0They're on a peace mission to Holm now it seems, after talking to that Nobleman looking type. One called him Imperial Highness. Must be important, I should learn more!§0\n§016th\n\nThree bloody days I been running all over Atlas cxhasing these folks! I been to Hols, The Empire, and now Sutica! Where they aren't een here! But here is excitement! They'r eplanning an attack! One fellow, scary looking one, claims he killed all of Axios\n\nwith a plague! A PLAGUE! I need to get back to the EMpire and warn them! They mean to make war!§0\n§0\n§0I need to get to the Empire before thgem... I only hope my feet don't give out! That knight gave me the willies! §0\n§0\n§0This sure isn't what I expected to find\n\nwhen I left the shire.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0I made it back to the capital ahead of some of those Crimsons, but that red armored knight was already here, he says hes a clergyman, I need to figure out how to get the truth to the right people, but if I speak up I'll surely be\n\nsilenced or killed! Think Quinry...§0\n§0\n§0\n§0I don't know whats happened. The Suticans showed up, and then left, was that their raid? Is this war? I feel I should still show my notes to the Caliph or the guard captain at least. But they're all been running\n\naround with these Suticans at the door and now they're all sat in another room, I managed to get into the palace, I will try to get someone's attention when they've finished their meeting. I don't know what to think about that fellow in red, saying he\n\nkilled ALL of Axios... Hyperbole? Myth making? Or horrid truth... I ought to find a history book and se what I can dig up.\n\n11th The Amber Cold§0\n§0\n§0I met another halfling today! First in weeks it feel like.  She told me about the Halfling communityt in Axios, and about some druid folk I could get in contact with. She asked me to play her a song, so I played her \"I Met a Friendly\n\nHalfling\" and did a little jig ands she paid me in fish! Fish! I haven't had lakefish since leaving Dunshire! Oh today was a good day!\n\n15th of Amber Cold§0\n§0\n§0WHat a lovely week I've had. After meeting Miss Daisy Applefoot in the capital, I decided I'd like to visit home. Upon arrival in Dunshire a day or so later, I began to write more songs in my book. As I did, who else but miss Daisy\n\nshould call out in greeting. But she tripped comming down the Inn Hill, and cut her leg something awful as it began to rain. Now suddenly her husband shows up all furious, and this other lady starts instigating that I must have been trying to steal his\n\nwoman! Maddness, I'm just a lad still! But he starts huffin and puffin for an hour about me bein a woman stealer and I'm just stood there trying to bandage her leg and help her. Eventually he took her to the BooBoo Tent.\n\nHe comes right back and is suddenly as po9lite and courteous as you like! His names Rollo, hes the sherrif around here but since the elders have all dissappeared or are banished, he's taken charge of Dunshire in a way. But then one of them elders showed\n\nup, and the three of us had a nice talk. They teased me about finding a lady (gross!) and offered advice on my journey to becomming a druid, then a couple more halfling fellas showed up. It was the most of my kind I'd seen since I left home! Almost a full\n\nyear ago now, I can't believe it. After the conversation died down, I went to the inna nd finally finished my first book. a book of Halfling Drinking Songs! I'm excited to show Mister Rollo and Miss Daisy and Elder Petrus!\n\n11th Deep Cold§0\n§0\n§0Today upon the road I happened across a fellow musician! A human troubadour named Egil SilverTongue! Him and the bonnie lass he was schmoozing made for fine company, and a lifelong friend I do believe I have made!  Said he'd be opening for\n\na new tavern some dwarf lord is building, so he'll send me a raven when that is an' I'll travel with him to the dwarflands and play! A bards life is always exciting!\n\n14th Malin's Welcome§0\n§0\n§0Today I woke late to findan orc and a wood elf on my fuckin lawn about to rip the gizzards out of one  another! I sent Ferrus to get an elder but weren't none around. §0\n§0\n§0They began to fight so Ferrus and I sat down to watch.\n\nThe orc lad swung, seemingly to cut the lad in half, but must have tripped or soemthing and only cut his arm. The elf rolled away and another swing kept him from attacking again. Another cut from the orc wounded the elf''s same arm. Then he went a wee bit\n\nmad and laid into the orc lad with his mace at his head! §0\n§0\n§0A solid hit but not a great one. Seems to have disoriented the big armored orc. §0\n§0\n§0 After the scuffle, the orc were victorious and the elf lay in the grass. It has hereby been wittnessed and\n\nrecorded by Quinry Lumberseed on this the 14th of Malin's Welcom, 1690.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Foreward§0\n§0\n§0    In writing this book, I hope to provide a means for others to indulge in the more whimsical and poetic aspects of life. A Bards life is not always glamorous, playing for ballrooms full of nobles, or making merry with barmaids.\n\nI have spent weeks trudging through mud and snow following Kadarsi warriors, spent nights in the streets of Holm, and freezing in the rain along the road. There have been those who tell me to stay put, find a job in an inn or with some noble house. Well\n\nwhile that life has it's  advantages, those nights in the mud and cold are  part of what make thisa life so worth living. The thrill of finding the stories and songs from the battlefield, getting drunk with wayyward dwarves, these are the songs and\n\nstories we remember, the ones that we think about after they're gone. So, without further ado, I, Quinry Lumberseed, halfling bard extrordinaire, present... A Bard's primer.", ""),
        StaticBook("Teh ; Petrus", "§bRosetta Puddifoot", "Dear Petrus,§0\n§0I thank you kindly for your generous shop offer! You're just too kind, so stop making me blush.§0\n§0\n§0I think I'll bake some wonderful pastries at the shop, (while of course, you can get them for free!).§0\n\nAlso, I've been wanting to talk to you about possibly having a §0§mdate §0dinner party together!§0\n§0\n§0Please send me back your thoughts!§0\n§0\n§0§oCheers,§0\n§0\n§a§lR§0osetta §f§lP§0uddifoot", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Found this beauty a while ago. pretty sure it belonged to your family.§0\n§0~Harold Applefoot", ""),
        StaticBook("Te Rosetta", "§bPetrus Frondor", "Dear Rosetta, §0\n§0\n§0Ah'd love a dinner parteh!§0\n§0\n§0If ye dun' mind Ah'd loike te cook fer ye. Ye've already fed me many a times.§0\n§0\n§0Let me know when yer free and Ah'll start preparing us a meal.\n\nIf ye 'ave any meals ye'd loike te eat, jus' let me know!§0\n§0\n§0Yers Truly,§0\n§0       Petrus Frondor", ""),
        StaticBook("Te Rosetta", "§bPetrus Frondor", "Te Rosetta, §0\n§0\n§0Ah sadly have some Elder stuff Ah must do tonight... But if yer free tomorrow, ah'd be more than excited te prepare ye a picnic. Just go' a ham I've been meanin te make and some fresh veggies from my garden, so let me know what ye\n\nthink.§0\n§0\n§0Got the perfect place to eat it tew! Nice view o' the farms and the rest o' the Vale.§0\n§0\n§0Yours,§0\n§0      Petrus Frondor", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "In the beginning, there was brick. Brick in the infinite nothingness that was space and time before the Great Foundation was lain. Suddenly in the stillness, the brick shattered, but not into choas- But instead thousands of further bricks. The foundation\n\ngave rise to man and from man civilization.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "It was Christmas Eve babe§0\n§0In the drunk tank§0\n§0An old man said to me, won't see another one§0\n§0And then he sang a song§0\n§0The Rare Old Mountain Dew§0\n§0I turned my face away§0\n§0And dreamed about you\n\nGot on a lucky one§0\n§0Came in eighteen to one§0\n§0I've got a feeling§0\n§0This year's for me and you§0\n§0So happy Christmas§0\n§0I love you baby§0\n§0I can see a better time§0\n§0When all our dreams come true\n\nThey've got cars big as bars§0\n§0They've got rivers of gold§0\n§0But the wind goes right through you§0\n§0It's no place for the old§0\n§0When you first took my hand§0\n§0On a cold Christmas Eve§0\n§0You promised me§0\n§0Broadway was waiting for me\n\nYou were handsome§0\n§0You were pretty§0\n§0Queen of New York City§0\n§0When the band finished playing§0\n§0They howled out for more§0\n§0Sinatra was swinging,§0\n§0All the drunks they were singing§0\n§0We kissed on a corner§0\n§0Then danced through the night\n\nThe boys of the NYPD choir§0\n§0Were singing \"Galway Bay\"§0\n§0And the bells were ringing out§0\n§0For Saintsmas day\n\nYou're a bum§0\n§0You're a punk§0\n§0You're an old slut on junk§0\n§0Lying there almost dead on a drip in that bed§0\n§0You scumbag, you maggot§0\n§0You cheap lousy faggot§0\n§0Happy Christmas your arse§0\n§0I pray God it's our last\n\nI could have been someone§0\n§0Well so could anyone§0\n§0You took my dreams from me§0\n§0When I first found you§0\n§0I kept them with me babe§0\n§0I put them with my own§0\n§0Can't make it all alone§0\n§0I've built my dreams around you", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "[!] A letter penned, and sealed with the wax seal of the Reiter Mercenaries.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0\"Hail, good greetings and salutations. I write this letter to inform", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "To Bobby§0\n§0\n§0Here is your wood back along with some drugs I dont need§0\n§0\n§0-Frederick", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Earldon of Skattagon:§0\n§0\n§0The Earldon is a small village across the river from Holm which started from a tent.  They are peaceful and calm.  \n\nHolm:§0\n§0\n§0Holm is a town, ran by two consuls which constantly interchange. Holm is known for its magic, and lack of laws banning it. Quite close to Thania. Its origins are from Ironguts, but dwarves are a minority.\n\nThe Barony of Ihirion,§0\n§0\n§0   Though it is small it is expanding within Atlas. The Barony also has a powerful levy, Commanded by Jacob Altee and Baron Ithilian Enthelor.\n\nThe Kingdom of Sutica, §0\n§0\n§0   Sutica is known for its size and grandeur, not to mention its naval guild and walls. It is open to all races, with a political system of a monarchy. A great place, although riddled with corruption.\n\nThe Kingdom of Haense:§0\n§0\n§0The Kingdom of Haense, or known as The Kingdon of Hanseti-Ruska is the state ckaiming to be in the realm of all Highlanders.  In 1637 Haense borak off from the dualistic state and declared it's independence.\n\nCloud Temple of Atlas:§0\n§0\n§0Cloud Temple is the starting place for all new travilers of Atlas.  There lives the Monks of Atlas.  You can easialy reach resource island from cloud temple.  At Cloud temple, there is a calming armoa and there is no sence of evil.\n\nNorland: §0\n§0\n§0On my travels, I stumbled upon Norland.  Nobody was there.  Dead bodies were everywhere.  It seems to have been conquered by Renatus-Marana.  I have learned that it was enhabited by Norlanders who worshoped the Red Faith.  -Magnus\n\nSenntistan:§0\n§0\n§0The walls of the heart of Renutas-Marna stand tall and proud before you.  Truly there is no greater sight than the one welcoming you to Senntistan, the capital of Man.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "§0\n§0------------------- Santegian Government§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0valid as of the 8th of§0\n§0 the First Seed, 1672§0\n§0\n§0    (July 12, 2018)\n\n       MONARCHS§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0           King§0\n§0     Achilius Horen§0\n§0         (Kukiii)§0\n§0\n§0          Queen§0\n§0    Laelia Kastrovat§0\n§0    (ultraviolentpixi)\n\n         CABINET§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0    Arch Chancellor§0\n§0 Ser Marek Kastrovat§0\n§0 -hand of the crown-§0\n§0        (Gressy)§0\n§0\n§0     Lord Marshal§0\n§0  King Achilius Horen§0\n§0    -military leader-§0\n§0          (Kukiii)\n\n         CABINET§0\n§0-------------------§0\n§0\n§0     Lady Steward§0\n§0    Vera de Castro§0\n§0-", ""),
        StaticBook("SMALL HIATUS", "§bSimon Birchenwald", "I am leaving for approximately 5 days with no activity: 16 July to 20 July. It'd be nice not to be evicted or anything along those lines.\n\nThanks.\n-ashenyx (Simon Birchenwald)\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n    \n\n   \\(-v-)/\nthis page should not be here", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "H | ɦöƈɦֆȶɛToday at 12:48 AM§0\n§0The tale of Hildrebrand I sin. Quiet, while i pluck these strings. Do not, dear children, turn away - I think you will like this, if you stay and hear my song of war and death, and blood and warriors'  last §0breaths.wd\n\n\n§0In wars of old they spared the lives of soldiers young, and weeping wives,  by sending forth a champion to fight alone, just a man to man. One sunny day, across a dell, King dietrich faced lord Haberfel. So dietrichs army first sent out a warrior\n\nwho was fierce and stout but old and creased like ancient elm. He stood out front, with sword and helm. And then from the other side famous Hadubrand came striding wide into the center if the field till the two men stood, shield to shield the\n\nolder man announced i will Know the name of the man I kill. What is your land? Who is your kin? Hadubrand answered, My sire is him, Now long dead, called ildebrand, who died a hero in foreign lands. Staggered, was the older man, His\n\nchain mall shook, he scarce could stand. His lips did ope but no sound came. His ears resound'd with his own name. \"Hildebrand?\" He said, at length, Trying to master his shaken strength.\"And were you born by old cologne? If so, I call you\n\nnow . . . My son.\" The battlefield fell deathly still.. No sheep cried o'er the graying hills. \"My son, My son'\" the old man cried. \"who told you that  your father died?\" He fell upon a greaved knee And to his long - lost son he reached. \"Take my hand,\n\nmy darling boy. Take my hand, my life's last joy But Hadubrand stood off and sneered at the old man shedding old-man tears. \"You expect me to believe such games? You think I was born yesterday?\"  This is how you've grown so old.\" With\n\ncowardly tricks and lies you'vetold My father, Hildebrand, is dead. You've used his§0\n§0name. Now lose your head!\" Hadu brand! My darling boy! This is no game! No trick! No toy! The old man wept and swore it true. But hadubrand would not be\n\nmoved. The ancient bones creaked with pain as Hildebrand stood up again., Thirty years in foreign climes Always placed in the front lines. How many times have I fallen?\" None. But now I may fall -- at the hands of my son. I could kneel down and\n\nforfeit to you. Perhaps you would then believe the truth. But thus you would then beleieve the truth.  ut thus my soldiers would be taken, and then i would deprive their children of fathers, as you were deprived, living an orphan, all of your life. SO\n\nadubrand, my darling child, Who laughed and played so pretty and wild, and bouncded and sang upon m§0\n§0my lap and on my shoulder took his nap-- Now you may kill me. Do your worst\n\n Unless I kill you, my beloved, first. Then they let sai their ashen spears and ran at each other, over years of absence and of childhood misses and fought one another when they should have kissed. They split eachother's bright oak shields,\n\nHacking and hewing, refusing to wield.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0The Tale of Hildebrand  -§0\n§0Shane Cooke", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Fuck me right in the ass senpai§0", ""),
